{"record_id":"D21-B01-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Performance fundamentals","concept":"Performance is multidimensional","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does software performance actually describe?","answer":"Performance is not a single metric. It can include latency, throughput, CPU consumption, memory consumption, I/O cost, startup time, tail behavior, and resource efficiency.","principle":"A performance claim must identify the measured dimension and workload.","rule":"Do not treat lower CPU usage, lower latency, higher throughput, or lower memory as interchangeable objectives.","objective":"Enable correct performance reasoning.","constraints":["The relevant workload must be defined.","The metric must match the requirement."],"expected_behavior":"Performance analysis identifies the resource or user-visible property being optimized.","security_implication":"Optimization must not remove required security controls merely to improve a metric.","performance_implication":"An optimization for one metric can degrade another.","testing_guidance":"Define measurable performance acceptance criteria before optimization.","verification_method":"Review metric definition, workload, baseline, and observed measurement.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["General performance-engineering principle; metric-specific behavior must be established from the target runtime/system documentation."],"related_records":["D21-B01-R002","D21-B02-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B01-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Measurement","concept":"Latency versus throughput","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between latency and throughput?","answer":"Latency measures the time required for an individual operation or request, while throughput measures the amount of work completed per unit time.","rule":"Choose latency or throughput as the primary objective according to the system requirement; measure both when they interact.","objective":"Prevent incorrect performance optimization targets.","input":"A service processes requests.","expected_behavior":"A latency requirement can be expressed as response-time bounds, while throughput can be expressed as completed requests per unit time.","tradeoff":"Batching or concurrency can increase throughput while increasing individual-request latency.","testing_guidance":"Measure both central latency and tail latency when request responsiveness matters, and measure throughput under representative load.","verification_method":"Run a controlled workload and record latency and completed work over time.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance-engineering measurement principle."],"related_records":["D21-B01-R003","D21-B05-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B01-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Measurement","concept":"Wall-clock time versus CPU time","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why must wall-clock time and CPU time be distinguished?","answer":"Wall-clock time includes waiting such as I/O, scheduling, synchronization, and external service delays. CPU time measures processor time attributed to execution and therefore does not represent all elapsed latency.","rule":"Use wall-clock measurements for end-to-end latency and CPU measurements for CPU consumption analysis.","edge_case":"A program may have low CPU time but high wall-clock time because it spends most of its lifetime waiting.","testing_guidance":"Record both metrics when determining whether a workload is compute-bound or wait-bound.","verification_method":"Compare elapsed time with CPU utilization or runtime-specific CPU measurements.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Operating-system performance measurement principle."],"related_records":["D21-B03-R001","D21-B05-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B01-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Measurement","concept":"Baseline before optimization","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"Why establish a baseline before optimizing code?","answer":"A baseline provides the reference against which an optimization can be evaluated. Without a baseline, improvement or regression cannot be established reliably.","objective":"Create an evidence-backed optimization workflow.","requirements":["Representative workload","Stable measurement method","Relevant performance metric","Recorded baseline"],"expected_behavior":"The optimized implementation is compared against the same or explicitly controlled workload and measurement procedure.","testing_guidance":"Repeat baseline measurements and record environmental conditions before changing the implementation.","verification_method":"Compare baseline and post-change measurements using the same acceptance criteria.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PROCEDURE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance-testing methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B02-R001","D21-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B01-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Optimization reasoning","concept":"Measure before changing implementation","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is blind optimization an anti-pattern?","answer":"Changing code without evidence can optimize a non-bottleneck, introduce regressions, increase complexity, and make later diagnosis harder.","invalid_example":"Rewrite a function solely because it appears slow without measuring it.","failure_mode":"Optimization effort is spent on a component that does not materially affect the target workload.","root_cause":"No measured bottleneck was established.","solution":"Measure representative behavior, identify the dominant cost, change one relevant factor, and re-measure.","testing_guidance":"Require before/after measurements and regression tests for behavior.","verification_method":"Confirm that the changed component was responsible for the measured bottleneck.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance optimization methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B09-R001","D21-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B02-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Benchmarking","concept":"Benchmark workload representativeness","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What makes a benchmark useful?","answer":"A useful benchmark exercises behavior representative of the real performance requirement, including relevant input sizes, distributions, execution paths, warmup conditions, and environmental constraints.","rule":"Do not generalize benchmark results beyond the workload and environment actually measured.","requirements":["Defined workload","Defined environment","Defined metric","Repeatable procedure"],"validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Benchmarking methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B02-R002","D21-B02-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B02-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Benchmarking","concept":"Warmup effects","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why can warmup matter in managed runtimes and optimized execution environments?","answer":"Startup, compilation, JIT optimization, cache population, allocation behavior, and other runtime effects can make early iterations behave differently from steady-state execution.","rule":"Separate startup performance from steady-state performance when the runtime exhibits warmup behavior.","edge_case":"A short-lived command-line program may care more about startup than steady-state throughput.","testing_guidance":"Measure cold-start and warmed-up behavior separately when both are relevant.","verification_method":"Repeat measurements across explicitly defined cold and warm conditions.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"RUNTIME_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Runtime-specific behavior must be confirmed against the target runtime version."],"related_records":["D21-B02-R003","D21-B10-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B02-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Benchmarking","concept":"Benchmarking versus profiling","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How does benchmarking differ from profiling?","answer":"Benchmarking measures behavior under a defined workload, while profiling provides evidence about where execution time, allocations, blocking, or other resources are spent.","rule":"Use benchmarks to quantify behavior and profiling to investigate causes.","edge_case":"Python documentation explicitly distinguishes cProfile/profile from timeit: the profilers describe execution behavior and are not intended as benchmarking tools.","verification_method":"Use a benchmark for before/after comparison and a profiler for bottleneck localization.","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Python documentation: cProfile/profile and timeit. 1"],"related_records":["D21-B09-R001","D21-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B02-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Benchmarking","concept":"Benchmark overhead","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must measurement overhead be considered?","answer":"Instrumentation and profiling can alter timing, scheduling, allocation, or other runtime behavior. The measurement itself can therefore distort the workload being measured.","rule":"Use an appropriate measurement method and assess instrumentation overhead when it could affect conclusions.","security_implication":"Instrumentation must not expose sensitive production data merely for performance analysis.","testing_guidance":"Compare instrumented and minimally instrumented runs when overhead is material.","verification_method":"Document measurement tooling and known overhead limitations.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance measurement methodology; Java Flight Recorder documentation explicitly discusses profiling overhead. 2"],"related_records":["D21-B09-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B02-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Benchmarking","concept":"Do not report fabricated performance numbers","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence is required for a numerical performance claim?","answer":"A numerical performance claim requires an actual measurement or an explicitly identified estimate. It must not be presented as measured when it was not measured.","rule":"MEASURED, ESTIMATED, INFERRED, and UNKNOWN are distinct evidence states.","verification_method":"Require workload, environment, method, metric, and observed result for MEASURED claims.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D21 dataset truth model."],"related_records":["D21-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B03-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"CPU performance","concept":"CPU-bound workload","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a CPU-bound workload?","answer":"A CPU-bound workload spends a substantial fraction of its elapsed execution performing computation rather than waiting on external resources.","rule":"CPU optimization is relevant only after evidence indicates processor execution is a material bottleneck.","performance_implication":"Algorithmic complexity, instruction count, branch behavior, vectorization, compiler optimization, and parallelism may matter.","testing_guidance":"Profile CPU activity and compare it with I/O and blocking evidence.","verification_method":"Establish that CPU consumption correlates with the observed latency or throughput limitation.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_DEFINITION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance-engineering fundamentals."],"related_records":["D21-B03-R002","D21-B09-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B03-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"CPU performance","concept":"Avoid unnecessary repeated computation","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","objective":"Avoid recomputing an expensive deterministic result within a loop.","incorrect_code":"result = []\nfor item in items:\n    result.append(expensive_calculation(config, item))","corrected_code":"prepared = prepare(config)\nresult = []\nfor item in items:\n    result.append(prepared.calculate(item))","explanation":"The transformation moves reusable preparation outside the loop. It is valid only when prepare(config) has no required per-item side effects and the resulting object preserves the required semantics.","tradeoff":"Preparation may consume memory or startup time and is beneficial only when the prepared state is reused sufficiently.","edge_case":"If config or relevant external state changes per iteration, hoisting the preparation can change behavior.","testing_guidance":"Compare outputs against the original implementation across representative and boundary inputs.","verification_method":"Behavioral regression testing plus measurement of the target workload.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PATTERN","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":["Generic optimization pattern; exact validity depends on function purity and state semantics."],"related_records":["D21-B03-R003","D21-B10-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B03-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"CPU performance","concept":"Python profiling","knowledge_type":"CODE_EXAMPLE","objective":"Profile a Python function to identify execution hotspots.","source_code":"import cProfile\n\n\ndef workload():\n    total = 0\n    for value in range(100000):\n        total += value * value\n    return total\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    cProfile.run('workload()')","expected_behavior":"The profiler reports execution statistics for the workload.","testing_guidance":"Use profiling to identify expensive call paths; use a benchmark method such as timeit for controlled timing comparisons.","verification_method":"Inspect generated profiling statistics and correlate hot paths with source code.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Python documentation: cProfile/profile. 3"],"related_records":["D21-B02-R003","D21-B09-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B03-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"CPU performance","concept":"CPU profiling evidence","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How should CPU profiling be interpreted?","answer":"CPU profiles identify execution paths consuming processor time; they do not by themselves prove that changing the most sampled function will improve the system's primary requirement.","rule":"Connect CPU hotspot evidence to the target metric and workload before optimizing.","failure_mode":"Optimizing a hot function that is not on the critical path for the measured requirement.","root_cause":"Profile evidence was interpreted without workload-level context.","solution":"Correlate profile data with latency, throughput, or CPU budget and re-measure after changes.","verification_method":"Before/after workload measurement plus profile comparison.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance profiling methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B09-R001","D21-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B03-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"CPU performance","concept":"Go CPU profiling","knowledge_type":"CODE_EXAMPLE","objective":"Expose Go runtime profiling data for CPU analysis.","source_code":"package main\n\nimport (\n    _ \"net/http/pprof\"\n    \"net/http\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n    go http.ListenAndServe(\"localhost:6060\", nil)\n    select {}\n}","expected_behavior":"The process exposes pprof endpoints through the HTTP server when the standard pprof package is imported for side effects.","security_implication":"Profiling endpoints must not be exposed to untrusted networks without appropriate access controls.","testing_guidance":"Use a controlled environment and inspect CPU profiles with the Go profiling tools.","verification_method":"Confirm endpoint availability in an authorized local environment and verify profile collection separately.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.x","framework":"Go standard library","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Go diagnostics and net/http/pprof documentation. 4"],"related_records":["D21-B09-R003","D21-B03-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B04-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Memory","concept":"Allocation pressure","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can excessive allocation hurt performance?","answer":"Frequent allocation can increase allocator work, memory traffic, garbage-collection activity in managed runtimes, cache pressure, and memory footprint.","rule":"Optimize allocation only when allocation behavior is demonstrated to affect the target workload.","performance_implication":"Reducing allocation can improve throughput or latency in allocation-heavy workloads, but may increase retained memory if objects are reused incorrectly.","testing_guidance":"Measure allocation behavior using runtime-appropriate profiling tools.","verification_method":"Compare allocation-related measurements and the primary performance metric before and after the change.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Runtime and memory performance principles."],"related_records":["D21-B04-R002","D21-B09-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B04-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Memory","concept":"Reuse versus retention trade-off","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why is object or buffer reuse not automatically an optimization?","answer":"Reuse can reduce allocation overhead but may retain large buffers or objects longer than necessary, increasing memory consumption and potentially worsening cache behavior.","rule":"Evaluate both allocation cost and retained memory.","edge_case":"A reusable buffer may be efficient for repeated large operations but harmful if retained across long idle periods.","testing_guidance":"Measure allocation rate, peak/live memory, and workload latency or throughput.","verification_method":"Compare both resource dimensions under representative workloads.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Memory-performance trade-off principle."],"related_records":["D21-B04-R001","D21-B08-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B04-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Memory","concept":"Go heap profiling","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What can a Go heap profile reveal?","answer":"Go runtime profiling provides heap allocation information that can be used to investigate memory consumption and potential leaks.","rule":"Heap-profile evidence describes allocation behavior and must be interpreted against the application's lifetime and workload.","testing_guidance":"Collect heap profiles under representative conditions and compare them with expected object lifetimes.","verification_method":"Inspect heap profile allocation evidence and correlate it with application ownership/lifetime behavior.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.x","framework":"Go standard library","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Go diagnostics documentation describes heap profiling. 5"],"related_records":["D21-B04-R001","D21-B09-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B04-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Memory","concept":"Memory leak diagnosis","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a suspected memory leak be investigated?","answer":"Establish that retained memory grows unexpectedly under a repeatable workload, identify the retained object or resource classes, trace ownership or references, correct the lifetime problem, and rerun the workload.","requirements":["Repeatable workload","Memory measurements","Retention evidence"],"failure_mode":"Memory usage increases over time because objects or external resources remain reachable or unreleased beyond their intended lifetime.","testing_guidance":"Include long-running regression tests when the failure requires prolonged execution.","verification_method":"Demonstrate that retained memory stabilizes or follows the expected lifecycle after correction.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PROCEDURE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Memory-leak diagnosis methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B04-R003","D21-B10-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B04-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Memory","concept":"Allocation measurement versus memory usage","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why are allocation rate and memory footprint different metrics?","answer":"Allocation rate measures how much allocation activity occurs, while memory footprint measures how much memory is retained or occupied at a point or over time.","rule":"Do not infer a memory leak solely from high allocation rate or infer low allocation from a low footprint.","testing_guidance":"Measure allocation and retained memory separately when diagnosing memory performance.","verification_method":"Use runtime-specific allocation and heap measurements.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_FACT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Memory-performance measurement principle."],"related_records":["D21-B04-R001","D21-B04-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B05-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"I/O","concept":"I/O-bound workload","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is an I/O-bound workload?","answer":"An I/O-bound workload spends substantial elapsed time waiting for storage, network, database, filesystem, or other external operations rather than executing CPU instructions.","rule":"Reducing CPU instructions may have little effect when waiting dominates the target metric.","performance_implication":"Latency, batching, concurrency, connection reuse, buffering, and I/O scheduling may be more relevant than micro-optimizing CPU code.","testing_guidance":"Measure wait time and I/O operations alongside CPU usage.","verification_method":"Establish that I/O or external wait dominates the relevant workload metric.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_DEFINITION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance-engineering fundamentals."],"related_records":["D21-B05-R002","D21-B09-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B05-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"I/O","concept":"Batching small writes","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","objective":"Reduce per-operation overhead when many independent writes can safely be combined.","incorrect_code":"for item in items:\n    output.write(item)\n    output.flush()","corrected_code":"for item in items:\n    output.write(item)\noutput.flush()","explanation":"The corrected form avoids forcing a flush after every item. This is valid only when immediate durability/visibility after each item is not a requirement.","tradeoff":"Fewer flushes can improve throughput but can increase the amount of data not yet flushed when a failure occurs.","edge_case":"If the contract requires each item to be flushed before continuing, removing the flush changes semantics.","security_implication":"Durability and audit requirements must be preserved before batching writes.","testing_guidance":"Test data integrity, failure behavior, and durability requirements before and after transformation.","verification_method":"Behavioral tests plus workload-level throughput/latency measurement.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Generic buffered-I/O performance pattern; exact durability semantics depend on the I/O target."],"related_records":["D21-B08-R001","D21-B05-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B05-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"I/O","concept":"Java I/O bottleneck diagnosis","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How can Java Flight Recorder help identify I/O bottlenecks?","answer":"Java Flight Recorder can provide socket and file read/write events that help identify time spent waiting on I/O and the amount of I/O activity.","rule":"I/O evidence should be correlated with the application's latency or throughput requirement rather than optimized in isolation.","testing_guidance":"Record representative workload behavior and inspect relevant file/socket events.","verification_method":"Correlate observed I/O duration and volume with the target performance metric.","language":"Java","language_version":"JDK-specific","framework":"Java Flight Recorder","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Oracle Java Flight Recorder performance troubleshooting documentation. 6"],"related_records":["D21-B05-R001","D21-B09-R006"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B05-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Network latency","concept":"Remote-call amplification","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can repeated remote calls create large latency?","answer":"Each remote call can add network latency, serialization/deserialization, scheduling, server processing, and failure/retry overhead. Repeating calls inside a higher-level operation can multiply these costs.","failure_mode":"A request performs many sequential remote calls that could have been reduced or combined.","root_cause":"Remote operations were modeled as if they had local-call cost.","solution":"Reduce round trips, batch compatible operations, parallelize independent work where safe, or redesign the interface.","edge_case":"Parallelizing calls may increase downstream load and contention and therefore must be bounded.","testing_guidance":"Measure request count, latency distribution, downstream load, and error behavior.","verification_method":"Compare end-to-end performance and downstream resource impact before and after the change.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_FAILURE_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Distributed-systems performance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B05-R002","D21-B07-R003","D21-B08-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B05-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Timeouts","concept":"Timeouts as performance and reliability boundaries","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why are timeouts relevant to performance engineering?","answer":"An operation without an effective timeout can retain resources indefinitely, allowing slow dependencies to consume concurrency capacity and amplify latency.","rule":"Every externally bounded operation should have an appropriate timeout where the underlying API permits it.","security_implication":"Unbounded waits can contribute to resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Timeouts bound resource occupancy but do not make a slow dependency faster.","testing_guidance":"Test normal completion, timeout expiration, cancellation, and cleanup.","verification_method":"Verify that timeout failure releases or transitions resources correctly.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Reliability and performance engineering principle."],"related_records":["D21-B07-R004","D21-B09-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B06-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Algorithms","concept":"Asymptotic complexity as a performance model","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is algorithmic complexity important?","answer":"Asymptotic complexity describes how resource requirements scale with input size and can reveal growth behavior that fixed-size benchmarks may not expose.","rule":"Use complexity analysis to reason about scaling, then validate real behavior with measurements.","tradeoff":"An asymptotically better algorithm may be slower for small inputs because of larger constants, memory costs, or implementation overhead.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark representative input sizes rather than relying solely on Big-O notation.","verification_method":"Compare measured scaling behavior with the expected complexity model.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Algorithm analysis principle."],"related_records":["D21-B06-R002","D21-B02-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B06-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Algorithms","concept":"Linear search versus indexed lookup","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","objective":"Avoid repeated linear scans when repeated key lookup is required.","incorrect_code":"def find_user(users, target_id):\n    for user in users:\n        if user['id'] == target_id:\n            return user\n    return None","corrected_code":"def build_index(users):\n    return {user['id']: user for user in users}\n\n\ndef find_user(index, target_id):\n    return index.get(target_id)","explanation":"The indexed approach performs preprocessing once and then supports direct dictionary lookup under normal hash-table assumptions.","tradeoff":"Index construction consumes time and memory and becomes beneficial when repeated lookups justify that cost.","edge_case":"Duplicate IDs require an explicit policy; silently overwriting one record may be incorrect.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate keys, missing keys, empty collections, and repeated lookup workloads.","verification_method":"Behavioral equivalence plus measured workload comparison.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Standard algorithm/data-structure pattern."],"related_records":["D21-B06-R001","D21-B08-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B06-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Algorithms","concept":"Sort once versus repeated sorting","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can repeated sorting become a performance failure?","answer":"Sorting the same or substantially unchanged collection repeatedly can add unnecessary O(n log n)-class work. If sorted order can be reused safely, sorting once and reusing the result may reduce total work.","rule":"Avoid repeated computation when the underlying data and ordering requirements permit reuse.","edge_case":"If the collection changes between operations, cached ordering may become stale.","testing_guidance":"Measure total workload cost and verify ordering remains semantically correct after updates.","verification_method":"Compare total operations and outputs under representative mutation patterns.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Algorithmic performance pattern."],"related_records":["D21-B03-R002","D21-B06-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B06-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Algorithms","concept":"Constant-factor optimization versus complexity change","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why can changing the algorithm matter more than micro-optimizing code?","answer":"An algorithmic change can alter how cost scales with input size, whereas a micro-optimization typically changes a constant factor without changing asymptotic growth.","rule":"Investigate algorithmic scaling before investing heavily in low-level micro-optimization when scaling is the dominant problem.","testing_guidance":"Use both complexity analysis and workload measurements.","verification_method":"Demonstrate the changed scaling behavior or measured benefit under representative sizes.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ALGORITHMIC_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Algorithm analysis and optimization methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B06-R001","D21-B03-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B06-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Algorithms","concept":"Data-structure choice","knowledge_type":"DECISION_RULE","question":"How should data structures be selected for performance?","answer":"Select based on required operations, expected workload distribution, memory constraints, ordering requirements, mutation patterns, and correctness semantics.","rule":"Do not choose a data structure solely because it has a favorable average complexity for one operation.","constraints":["Required operations must be identified.","Workload frequency must be understood.","Semantic requirements must be preserved."],"performance_implication":"The best choice depends on the operation mix and workload rather than one isolated operation.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark representative operation mixes when performance is material.","verification_method":"Confirm semantic correctness and compare workload-level performance.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Data-structure selection principle."],"related_records":["D21-B06-R002","D21-B06-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B07-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Concurrency","concept":"Concurrency does not guarantee speedup","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can concurrency fail to improve performance?","answer":"Concurrency can be limited by shared resources, synchronization, I/O bottlenecks, scheduling overhead, contention, serial sections, or insufficient independent work.","rule":"Measure concurrency scaling rather than assuming additional workers improve throughput.","performance_implication":"Beyond the useful concurrency level, overhead or contention can increase latency and reduce throughput.","testing_guidance":"Measure throughput, latency, CPU utilization, and contention across controlled concurrency levels.","verification_method":"Identify the point at which additional concurrency ceases to improve the target metric.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"CONCURRENCY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Concurrency performance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B07-R002","D21-B07-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B07-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Concurrency","concept":"Lock contention","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can lock contention reduce performance?","answer":"When many workers compete for a shared lock, workers spend time waiting rather than doing useful work. Increasing worker count can therefore increase contention instead of throughput.","failure_mode":"Throughput stops increasing or latency rises as concurrency increases.","candidate_causes":["Large critical section","High lock acquisition frequency","Shared hot resource","Excessive worker count"],"root_cause":"The critical resource serializes a substantial portion of the workload.","solution":"Reduce critical-section duration, reduce shared-state frequency, partition state where safe, or choose a different concurrency design.","testing_guidance":"Measure lock wait time and workload throughput at multiple concurrency levels.","verification_method":"Demonstrate reduced contention and improved target metric without violating synchronization correctness.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"CONCURRENCY_FAILURE_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Concurrency performance methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B07-R001","D21-B09-R007"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B07-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Concurrency","concept":"Go blocking and mutex profiling","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What concurrency-related profiles does Go expose?","answer":"Go's profiling facilities include blocking and mutex-related profiling in addition to CPU and heap profiling, enabling investigation of synchronization contention.","rule":"Use concurrency-specific profiling evidence when contention is suspected rather than inferring contention from CPU utilization alone.","testing_guidance":"Collect the relevant profile under representative concurrent workload.","verification_method":"Correlate blocking or mutex evidence with latency and throughput behavior.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.x","framework":"runtime/pprof and net/http/pprof","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Go pprof documentation describes block and mutex profiles. 7"],"related_records":["D21-B07-R002","D21-B09-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B07-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Concurrency","concept":"Bounded concurrency","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why is bounded concurrency often preferable to unlimited concurrency?","answer":"A bound limits resource occupancy and prevents an increasing number of concurrent tasks from overwhelming CPU, memory, connections, downstream services, or queues.","rule":"Concurrency limits should be chosen from measured capacity and service requirements, not arbitrary optimism.","security_implication":"Bounds can reduce resource-exhaustion exposure.","performance_implication":"Too low a bound can underutilize resources; too high a bound can create contention and overload.","testing_guidance":"Load-test several bounded levels and observe latency, throughput, errors, and resource saturation.","verification_method":"Select a limit only after measuring the target workload and failure behavior.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Concurrency and resource-management principle."],"related_records":["D21-B07-R001","D21-B05-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B07-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Concurrency","concept":"False sharing and shared-memory locality","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why can independent variables still interfere in low-level concurrent code?","answer":"On hardware with cache-coherent shared memory, independently updated data located in the same cache line can cause cache-line invalidation traffic and reduce scalability.","rule":"Treat low-level memory-layout optimization as architecture-specific and evidence-driven.","constraints":["CPU architecture","Compiler behavior","Memory layout","Concurrency pattern"],"testing_guidance":"Only investigate after profiling indicates a relevant scalability problem.","verification_method":"Use architecture-appropriate measurements and compare controlled layouts.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"HARDWARE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":["Computer architecture performance principle; hardware-specific validation required."],"related_records":["D21-B07-R001","D21-B08-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B08-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Caching and batching","concept":"Batching reduces fixed per-operation overhead","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can batching improve performance?","answer":"Batching can amortize fixed costs such as system calls, network round trips, serialization, transaction setup, or scheduling across multiple logical operations.","rule":"Batch only operations whose semantics permit delayed grouping.","tradeoff":"Larger batches can increase memory use and per-item latency and may complicate failure handling.","testing_guidance":"Measure throughput and latency as batch size changes.","verification_method":"Identify a batch size that satisfies correctness and service-level constraints using actual measurements.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Systems performance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B05-R002","D21-B08-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B08-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Caching","concept":"Cache effectiveness depends on workload locality","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"When is caching likely to help?","answer":"Caching helps when reused values are expensive to obtain and the workload exhibits sufficient temporal or spatial locality for cached data to be reused before eviction or invalidation.","rule":"A cache must be evaluated by hit behavior, lookup cost, memory cost, freshness requirements, and invalidation semantics.","failure_mode":"A cache adds overhead and memory consumption while rarely serving reusable data.","root_cause":"The workload lacks sufficient locality or cache policy is inappropriate.","solution":"Measure hit/miss behavior and remove or redesign the cache if it does not improve the target metric.","testing_guidance":"Test hit, miss, expiration, invalidation, and stale-data behavior.","verification_method":"Measure target performance with and without caching under representative workloads.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"CACHE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Caching performance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B08-R001","D21-B10-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B08-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Locality","concept":"Data locality","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can data layout affect performance?","answer":"Data layout affects memory access patterns, cache utilization, memory bandwidth, and sometimes vectorization opportunities. Better locality can reduce the cost of repeatedly accessing related data.","rule":"Do not assume a layout change is beneficial without measuring the actual workload and target architecture.","performance_implication":"Locality optimizations are particularly relevant to memory-intensive loops.","testing_guidance":"Measure under representative data sizes and access patterns.","verification_method":"Compare workload performance and memory behavior before and after the layout change.","language":"C++","language_version":"C++11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"SYSTEM_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":["Computer architecture and memory hierarchy principle."],"related_records":["D21-B07-R005","D21-B04-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B08-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Caching","concept":"Cache invalidation correctness","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can caching create a correctness failure?","answer":"A cache can return stale or invalid data when the invalidation, expiration, versioning, or consistency rules do not match the underlying data's semantics.","failure_mode":"The system returns an old value after the authoritative state has changed.","root_cause":"Cache freshness rules are weaker or inconsistent with the data contract.","solution":"Define explicit freshness and invalidation semantics and test them as part of the feature contract.","security_implication":"Stale authorization or identity information can become a security issue when security state is cached incorrectly.","testing_guidance":"Test update, invalidation, expiration, concurrent update, and failure scenarios.","verification_method":"Demonstrate that cache behavior satisfies the declared consistency contract.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"CACHE_FAILURE_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Caching correctness and consistency principle."],"related_records":["D21-B08-R002","D21-B10-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B08-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Caching","concept":"Negative caching","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is negative caching?","answer":"Negative caching temporarily stores a result indicating that a requested item or operation produced a known negative outcome, reducing repeated expensive lookups.","constraints":["Negative result must have a safe validity period.","The underlying state must tolerate delayed discovery of a newly available item."],"tradeoff":"Long negative-cache lifetimes reduce repeated work but can delay visibility of newly created resources.","testing_guidance":"Test creation after a negative result, expiration, invalidation, and repeated misses.","verification_method":"Confirm that the negative result never violates the underlying freshness contract.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"CACHE_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["General caching pattern."],"related_records":["D21-B08-R002","D21-B08-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B09-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Profiling","concept":"Profiling localizes bottlenecks","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What is the basic profiling workflow?","answer":"Define the performance symptom, capture a representative workload, collect profile evidence using an appropriate tool, identify dominant costs, form a causal hypothesis, change the implementation, and re-measure.","rule":"A profiler identifies evidence; it does not automatically prove the correct fix.","testing_guidance":"Keep workload and measurement conditions comparable across iterations.","verification_method":"Require a measurable improvement in the target metric without unacceptable regressions.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PROCEDURE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance profiling methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B02-R003","D21-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B09-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Profiling","concept":"Profiling overhead","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why should profiler overhead be recorded as part of performance evidence?","answer":"Profiling changes the execution environment to some degree. Java documentation specifically notes that profiling overhead varies and can affect measurements, particularly for configurations that enable more detailed events.","rule":"Document profiler configuration and relevant overhead limitations when interpreting results.","testing_guidance":"Avoid treating profiler measurements as identical to uninstrumented production behavior.","verification_method":"Compare or otherwise bound the measurement impact when it materially affects the conclusion.","language":"Java","language_version":"JDK-specific","framework":"Java Flight Recorder","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Oracle Java Flight Recorder troubleshooting documentation. 8"],"related_records":["D21-B02-R004","D21-B09-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B09-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Profiling","concept":"Go pprof profile types","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Which major Go pprof profiles are useful for performance diagnosis?","answer":"Go's runtime profiling facilities include CPU and heap profiles, and the HTTP pprof integration also exposes blocking, mutex, and execution-trace related facilities.","rule":"Select the profile according to the suspected resource or failure mode.","testing_guidance":"Do not collect every profile simultaneously if their interaction reduces diagnostic precision.","verification_method":"Collect the smallest profile set that can answer the diagnostic question.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.x","framework":"runtime/pprof and net/http/pprof","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Go diagnostics and pprof documentation. 9"],"related_records":["D21-B04-R003","D21-B07-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B09-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Profiling","concept":"Allocation profiling diagnosis","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should excessive allocation be diagnosed?","answer":"Measure allocation behavior, identify hot allocation sites, determine whether allocations are required by semantics, then reduce avoidable allocations only where evidence shows they materially affect performance.","rule":"Do not optimize allocation counts without considering retained memory and behavior.","failure_mode":"A code change reduces allocations but increases memory retention or complexity without improving the target metric.","root_cause":"Allocation was treated as the objective instead of a contributing cost.","solution":"Use allocation evidence together with latency, throughput, and memory measurements.","verification_method":"Confirm the target performance improvement and absence of unacceptable memory or correctness regressions.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PROCEDURE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Memory profiling methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B04-R001","D21-B04-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B09-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Profiling","concept":"Wait-state diagnosis","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How can waiting become the dominant bottleneck?","answer":"An application can spend most elapsed time waiting for network, file, database, locks, scheduling, or external services even when CPU utilization is low.","rule":"Low CPU utilization does not imply good performance; inspect waiting resources before optimizing CPU code.","testing_guidance":"Capture I/O, synchronization, and external dependency timing where supported.","verification_method":"Identify the dominant waiting source and correlate it with the target latency or throughput metric.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance bottleneck diagnosis principle; Java Flight Recorder documents I/O and monitor-wait analysis. 10"],"related_records":["D21-B01-R003","D21-B05-R001","D21-B07-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B09-R006","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Profiling","concept":"Java hot-method analysis","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What can Java Flight Recorder method profiling reveal?","answer":"Method profiling can show methods that account for substantial sampled execution activity, helping identify candidate CPU hotspots.","rule":"Sampling evidence should be interpreted statistically and correlated with workload behavior rather than treated as an exact instruction-level accounting.","testing_guidance":"Use representative workloads and sufficient recording duration for the diagnostic question.","verification_method":"Correlate sampled hot methods with the observed performance symptom and re-measure after changes.","language":"Java","language_version":"JDK-specific","framework":"Java Flight Recorder","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Oracle JFR troubleshooting documentation. 11"],"related_records":["D21-B09-R001","D21-B03-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B09-R007","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Profiling","concept":"Profile isolation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why can multiple simultaneous profiling mechanisms interfere with one another?","answer":"Some diagnostic mechanisms add overhead or alter runtime behavior, potentially skewing another profile. Go's diagnostics documentation explicitly notes interference between some profiling modes.","rule":"Prefer isolated measurements when diagnostic interaction can distort the evidence.","testing_guidance":"Collect one high-priority profile at a time when the tool documentation identifies interference.","verification_method":"Document which profiling modes were active for each measurement.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.x","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Go diagnostics documentation notes interference among profiling tools. 12"],"related_records":["D21-B09-R003","D21-B02-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B10-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Regression","concept":"Performance regression test","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a performance regression test establish?","answer":"It should establish that a defined workload continues to satisfy an explicitly defined performance requirement or remains within an accepted regression threshold.","requirements":["Stable workload definition","Defined metric","Acceptance threshold","Comparable environment or controlled variance"],"test_case":"Run the same representative workload against baseline and candidate implementations and compare the selected performance metric.","expected_test_result":"The candidate remains within the declared acceptance boundary while preserving functional correctness.","expected_test_result_type":"THRESHOLD_COMPARISON","testing_guidance":"Avoid arbitrary thresholds unsupported by workload requirements or historical measurement evidence.","verification_method":"Store measurement context and compare results using the declared criterion.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance regression testing methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B01-R004","D21-B02-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B10-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Regression","concept":"Cold-start versus steady-state regression","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"When should cold-start and steady-state performance be tested separately?","answer":"They should be separated when initialization, compilation, cache population, or runtime warmup materially affects the application's user-visible behavior.","test_case":"Measure startup latency independently, then measure steady-state behavior after the workload reaches the defined warm condition.","expected_test_result":"Each metric satisfies its own requirement rather than allowing a good steady-state result to hide a startup regression.","testing_guidance":"Define exactly what constitutes cold and warm state for the target application.","verification_method":"Compare each condition against its corresponding baseline.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"PERFORMANCE_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Runtime performance testing principle."],"related_records":["D21-B02-R002","D21-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B10-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Regression","concept":"Memory regression test","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a memory regression test validate?","answer":"It should validate that memory consumption or retention remains within an explicitly defined requirement under a representative workload.","requirements":["Representative workload","Memory metric","Expected lifecycle","Acceptance criterion"],"test_case":"Execute a repeatable workload and observe memory behavior over the relevant lifecycle.","expected_test_result":"Memory behavior remains within the declared bound and does not show unexplained growth across repeated cycles.","testing_guidance":"Distinguish legitimate caching or retained state from unintended retention.","verification_method":"Compare repeated-run memory behavior with a validated baseline and inspect retained objects when necessary.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"PERFORMANCE_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Memory regression testing methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B04-R004","D21-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B10-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Regression","concept":"Cache regression test","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should caching tests verify beyond hit rate?","answer":"Caching tests must verify correctness, freshness, invalidation, expiration, miss behavior, resource usage, and the target performance effect where relevant.","test_case":"Exercise hit, miss, update, invalidation, expiration, and concurrent access scenarios.","expected_test_result":"Returned values satisfy the consistency contract and the cache provides the intended performance benefit under representative workload.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive cached state must respect authorization and freshness requirements.","verification_method":"Combine behavioral tests with workload-level measurements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"CACHE_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Caching correctness and performance methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B08-R002","D21-B08-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B10-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Regression","concept":"Performance optimization acceptance","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"When should an optimization be accepted?","answer":"An optimization should be accepted only when the target performance requirement is improved or preserved according to defined evidence, functional behavior remains correct, and unacceptable resource, security, maintainability, or reliability regressions are absent.","rule":"Performance improvement alone is insufficient if correctness or required system properties regress.","requirements":["Functional correctness","Performance evidence","Relevant resource evidence","Security review where applicable","Regression validation"],"verification_method":"Review before/after measurements, functional tests, relevant profiling evidence, and declared acceptance criteria.","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D21 performance verification methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B01-R004","D21-B02-R005","D21-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B10-R006","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Regression","concept":"Performance evidence record","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What information should accompany a performance result?","answer":"A reusable performance result should record workload, input characteristics, implementation version, environment, runtime versions, measurement method, metric, observed result, variance information where applicable, and limitations.","objective":"Make performance evidence reproducible and retrievable.","requirements":["Workload definition","Environment","Implementation identity","Measurement method","Observed result","Limitations"],"expected_behavior":"A later engineer can determine what was actually measured and avoid treating the result as universal.","verification_method":"Check that every numerical performance claim can be traced to an identifiable measurement context.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D21 evidence-governance methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B02-R005","D21-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B10-R007","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Regression","concept":"Go profile-guided optimization","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What is Go profile-guided optimization?","answer":"Go supports profile-guided optimization in which representative profiling information can be fed into compilation to guide optimization decisions.","rule":"PGO profiles must represent relevant production-like workloads; measured gains must not be generalized beyond the tested environment and workload.","performance_implication":"PGO can change generated code based on profile evidence, but benefit depends on workload representativeness and compiler/runtime version.","version_range":"Go 1.20+ supports PGO according to official Go documentation.","testing_guidance":"Compare candidate and baseline builds under representative workloads and record the compiler/runtime versions.","verification_method":"Measure the target workload and retain the profile/build provenance.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.20+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Go official Profile-guided optimization documentation. 13"],"related_records":["D21-B02-R001","D21-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B10-R008","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Regression","concept":"Rust benchmark optimization barrier","knowledge_type":"CODE_EXAMPLE","question":"Why can compiler optimization complicate microbenchmarking?","answer":"If benchmarked work has no observable effect, an optimizer may transform or remove work in ways that make naive timing misleading. Rust provides optimization-related hints such as std::hint::black_box for this class of benchmarking concern.","source_code":"use std::hint::black_box;\n\nfn compute(values: &[u64]) -> u64 {\n    values.iter().copied().sum()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let values = vec![1_u64, 2, 3, 4];\n    let result = compute(black_box(&values));\n    black_box(result);\n}","expected_behavior":"The benchmarked values are passed through compiler optimization barriers intended to reduce unrealistic elimination or simplification of benchmark inputs/results.","constraints":["The exact benchmarking methodology remains dependent on the benchmark harness and compiler version.","black_box is not a proof of benchmark correctness."],"testing_guidance":"Use an established benchmark methodology and repeat measurements rather than relying on a single timing.","verification_method":"Validate benchmark methodology against the target Rust version and benchmark harness.","language":"Rust","language_version":"1.27+ for std::hint::black_box","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Rust std::hint documentation describes black_box as an optimization-related compiler hint. 14"],"related_records":["D21-B02-R003","D21-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B10-R009","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Regression","concept":"Optimization rollback criterion","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"When should a performance optimization be rolled back?","answer":"Rollback is appropriate when the change fails the declared performance objective, introduces unacceptable functional/security/reliability regressions, or increases complexity without a demonstrated compensating benefit.","rule":"Optimization changes must remain reversible until their evidence and regression impact are accepted.","testing_guidance":"Retain the previous implementation or an equivalent reproducible baseline until validation completes.","verification_method":"Compare against the pre-change baseline using the original acceptance criteria.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PROCEDURE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance change-management principle."],"related_records":["D21-B10-R005","D21-B01-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B10-R010","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Regression","concept":"Performance claim confidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should confidence in a performance result be represented?","answer":"Confidence should reflect the quality of the workload, measurement method, repeatability, environment control, instrumentation effects, and applicability of the evidence to the intended use case.","rule":"A single successful timing is weak evidence for a general performance claim.","evidence_level":"Measured results with controlled methodology provide stronger evidence than estimates or informal observations.","limitations":["Results may vary across hardware, runtime versions, workloads, system load, compiler settings, and data distributions."],"verification_method":"Review methodology and repeatability before promoting a result to reusable engineering knowledge.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D21 performance evidence model."],"related_records":["D21-B02-R005","D21-B10-R006"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B11-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Asynchronous performance","concept":"Event-loop blocking","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can blocking work destroy asynchronous application performance?","answer":"An event loop executes tasks cooperatively. If a task performs blocking CPU or I/O work without yielding, other tasks sharing that event loop cannot progress during the blocking interval.","failure_mode":"One coroutine performs a blocking operation directly on the event-loop thread.","observable_symptom":"Unrelated asynchronous requests experience increased latency and reduced concurrency.","root_cause":"The event-loop thread is occupied by non-yielding work.","solution":"Use an asynchronous API or explicitly offload blocking work to an appropriate executor/thread/process mechanism.","testing_guidance":"Run concurrent requests while injecting controlled blocking work and measure latency impact.","verification_method":"Confirm that unrelated tasks continue making progress while the blocking operation is isolated.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Python asyncio development documentation states that blocking CPU-bound code directly in an event loop delays concurrent tasks and I/O operations. 1"],"related_records":["D21-B05-R001","D21-B07-R001","D21-B11-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B11-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Asynchronous performance","concept":"Offloading blocking I/O","knowledge_type":"CODE_EXAMPLE","objective":"Prevent blocking synchronous I/O from occupying an asyncio event-loop thread.","requirements":["Blocking function is safe to execute outside the event-loop thread.","Result can be awaited asynchronously."],"source_code":"import asyncio\nimport time\n\n\ndef blocking_io():\n    time.sleep(1)\n    return \"done\"\n\n\nasync def main():\n    result = await asyncio.to_thread(blocking_io)\n    return result\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    print(asyncio.run(main()))","expected_behavior":"The blocking function executes in a separate thread while the event loop remains available for other tasks.","tradeoff":"Thread offloading introduces scheduling and thread-pool overhead and does not make the underlying blocking operation intrinsically faster.","edge_case":"CPU-bound work may require a process or another suitable execution strategy depending on the Python runtime and workload.","testing_guidance":"Run the function concurrently with another asynchronous task and verify that the second task continues progressing.","verification_method":"Functional test plus workload-level latency measurement.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.9+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Python asyncio documentation documents asyncio.to_thread for executing blocking I/O-bound functions without blocking the event loop. 2"],"related_records":["D21-B11-R001","D21-B11-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B11-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Asynchronous performance","concept":"Async does not make CPU-bound work asynchronous","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When is asyncio a poor solution for CPU-bound work?","answer":"A CPU-intensive function executed directly by an event-loop task can occupy the event-loop thread and delay other tasks. Async concurrency primarily improves utilization of waiting periods; CPU-bound work requires an appropriate CPU execution strategy.","rule":"Do not convert CPU-bound work to async syntax and assume that CPU execution becomes concurrent.","failure_mode":"CPU-heavy computation is placed directly inside an async handler.","root_cause":"Concurrency model was selected based on syntax rather than workload characteristics.","solution":"Measure the workload and consider process-based or runtime-specific parallel execution where appropriate.","testing_guidance":"Compare event-loop responsiveness with and without isolated CPU work.","verification_method":"Verify that the event loop meets its latency requirement under representative concurrent CPU load.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Python asyncio documentation explains that blocking CPU-bound code delays concurrent asyncio tasks. 3"],"related_records":["D21-B03-R001","D21-B07-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B11-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Asynchronous performance","concept":"Slow callback diagnosis","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How can slow callbacks be detected in Python asyncio?","answer":"Asyncio debug facilities can report callbacks that exceed the configured slow-callback threshold, helping identify event-loop work that blocks progress.","rule":"Slow-callback evidence identifies a timing symptom and should be correlated with workload latency and call-path evidence.","testing_guidance":"Enable asyncio debug facilities in a controlled environment and inspect slow callbacks.","verification_method":"Identify the blocking callback, correct the workload design, and re-measure event-loop responsiveness.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Python asyncio development documentation describes debug mode and slow callback logging. 4"],"related_records":["D21-B11-R001","D21-B09-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B11-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Asynchronous performance","concept":"Async task lifecycle overhead","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why should excessive asynchronous task creation be avoided?","answer":"Each task introduces scheduling, bookkeeping, memory, and synchronization overhead. Creating very large numbers of short-lived tasks can make coordination itself a significant cost.","rule":"Use concurrency appropriate to the workload instead of creating unlimited tasks.","performance_implication":"Task overhead can dominate when individual operations are extremely small.","testing_guidance":"Measure throughput and memory while varying task concurrency and granularity.","verification_method":"Identify a workload-specific concurrency level that satisfies the performance requirement.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"CONCURRENCY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Async concurrency engineering principle."],"related_records":["D21-B07-R004","D21-B11-R006"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B12-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Database performance","concept":"Database query performance begins with workload understanding","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should database performance optimization start with query and workload analysis?","answer":"Database performance depends on query shape, cardinality, indexes, data distribution, concurrency, transaction behavior, storage characteristics, and result volume. Optimizing one factor without understanding the workload can produce ineffective or harmful changes.","rule":"Analyze actual query workload and execution behavior before changing indexes or schema.","testing_guidance":"Capture representative queries, parameters, execution plans, latency, throughput, and resource behavior.","verification_method":"Compare the same workload before and after the change.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"DATABASE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Database performance engineering principle."],"related_records":["D21-B12-R002","D21-B12-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B12-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Database performance","concept":"Index usefulness depends on query workload","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should indexes not be added indiscriminately?","answer":"Indexes can accelerate suitable access patterns but consume storage and impose maintenance work on writes. An index that does not support a meaningful workload can add cost without improving the target operation.","rule":"Create indexes based on actual query predicates, joins, ordering requirements, and workload characteristics.","tradeoff":"Read performance may improve while insert/update/delete cost and storage increase.","testing_guidance":"Measure target query latency and write workload impact before and after adding an index.","verification_method":"Confirm that the index changes the relevant execution behavior and improves the workload without unacceptable write regressions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"DATABASE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Relational database indexing principle."],"related_records":["D21-B12-R001","D21-B12-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B12-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Database performance","concept":"Execution plans as diagnostic evidence","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"Why inspect a database execution plan?","answer":"An execution plan provides evidence about how the database intends to execute a query, including access paths and operations relevant to query cost.","rule":"Use the actual database's plan tooling rather than inferring query behavior solely from SQL text.","testing_guidance":"Inspect plans using representative parameters and data distributions.","verification_method":"Compare plan behavior and measured query performance before and after optimization.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"DATABASE_DIAGNOSTIC_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Relational database query-planning principle."],"related_records":["D21-B12-R002","D21-B12-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B12-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Database performance","concept":"Over-indexing","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is over-indexing?","answer":"Over-indexing occurs when a database maintains indexes that provide insufficient workload benefit relative to their storage and write-maintenance cost.","failure_mode":"Write-heavy workloads become slower and storage consumption increases without meaningful read improvement.","root_cause":"Indexes were added without workload-based evidence.","solution":"Measure index usage and workload impact, then remove indexes that do not provide sufficient value subject to operational constraints.","testing_guidance":"Evaluate read latency, write throughput, storage, and query-plan changes.","verification_method":"Confirm that removal does not regress required queries.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"DATABASE_ANTIPATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Database indexing engineering principle."],"related_records":["D21-B12-R002","D21-B12-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B12-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Database performance","concept":"N+1 query pattern","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is the N+1 query performance problem?","answer":"An application retrieves a primary collection with one query and then performs an additional query for each item, producing one initial query plus N dependent queries.","failure_mode":"Database round trips grow with result size.","root_cause":"Related data access was implemented as repeated per-item database operations.","solution":"Use a workload-appropriate set-based query, join, prefetch, batching, or other mechanism that reduces unnecessary round trips while preserving semantics.","tradeoff":"Larger queries can increase result size or server-side work and therefore require measurement.","testing_guidance":"Test empty, small, and large collections and measure query count and end-to-end latency.","verification_method":"Confirm semantic equivalence and reduced round trips under representative workloads.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"DATABASE_FAILURE_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Database application-performance pattern."],"related_records":["D21-B05-R004","D21-B12-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B13-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Serialization","concept":"Serialization cost","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can serialization become a performance bottleneck?","answer":"Serialization and deserialization consume CPU, memory, and often I/O bandwidth. Large or deeply nested representations can increase both processing time and transmitted bytes.","rule":"Treat serialization as part of the end-to-end cost rather than assuming it is negligible.","performance_implication":"Reducing payload size or serialization complexity can improve latency and throughput when serialization is material.","testing_guidance":"Measure serialization time, payload size, allocation behavior, and end-to-end latency.","verification_method":"Compare equivalent workloads before and after representation changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"SYSTEMS_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Serialization performance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B13-R002","D21-B13-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B13-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Serialization","concept":"Avoid unnecessary serialization cycles","knowledge_type":"CODE_TRANSFORMATION","objective":"Avoid converting an object to an intermediate representation and back when the operation can use the original representation safely.","incorrect_code":"payload = encode(obj)\nintermediate = decode(payload)\nresult = process(intermediate)","corrected_code":"result = process(obj)","explanation":"The transformation removes two serialization operations when process() can directly consume obj without changing semantics.","tradeoff":"Direct object handling may increase coupling between components if the serialization boundary was intentionally enforcing isolation or schema validation.","security_implication":"Removing a serialization boundary must not bypass required validation or trust-boundary controls.","testing_guidance":"Verify schema validation, ownership, mutation, and compatibility assumptions before removing the boundary.","verification_method":"Functional regression tests plus measurement of serialization cost.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Generic systems-performance transformation; validity depends on architectural boundary semantics."],"related_records":["D21-B13-R001","D21-B19-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B13-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Serialization","concept":"Payload size and network performance","knowledge_type":"RELATIONSHIP","question":"How can payload size affect network performance?","answer":"Larger payloads increase transmission work and may increase serialization, buffering, memory, and transfer time. The impact depends on bandwidth, latency, protocol behavior, compression, and workload concurrency.","rule":"Measure end-to-end performance rather than assuming payload size alone determines latency.","tradeoff":"Compression can reduce transferred bytes but add CPU cost and may not improve performance for already compressed or small payloads.","testing_guidance":"Measure payload size, compression ratio, CPU cost, network transfer time, and end-to-end latency.","verification_method":"Compare complete request/response workloads under representative network conditions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"NETWORK_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Network and serialization performance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B05-R004","D21-B13-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B13-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Serialization","concept":"Compression trade-off","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When can compression improve performance?","answer":"Compression can reduce bytes transferred and therefore improve network-bound workloads when the CPU cost of compression and decompression is smaller than the saved transfer cost.","rule":"Evaluate compression as an end-to-end trade-off.","edge_case":"Highly compressible large payloads and bandwidth-constrained links can behave differently from small or already compressed payloads.","testing_guidance":"Measure CPU time, compressed size, transfer time, decompression cost, and total latency.","verification_method":"Compare end-to-end workload performance rather than only compression ratio.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"PERFORMANCE_TRADEOFF","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Compression/network performance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B13-R003","D21-B03-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B13-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Serialization","concept":"Schema evolution performance boundary","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why must serialization optimization preserve schema compatibility?","answer":"Changing representation can improve performance while breaking consumers, migration paths, or compatibility contracts.","rule":"Performance changes to serialized formats must preserve or deliberately version the compatibility contract.","security_implication":"Schema changes must not weaken validation or permit ambiguous parsing.","testing_guidance":"Test old/new producer-consumer combinations that the compatibility policy requires.","verification_method":"Compatibility tests plus performance measurements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURAL_CONSTRAINT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["API and data-contract engineering principle."],"related_records":["D21-B13-R001","D21-B10-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B14-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Runtime behavior","concept":"Garbage collection as a performance dimension","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can garbage collection affect application performance?","answer":"Managed runtimes may perform garbage-collection work that consumes CPU and can affect latency, throughput, memory availability, or allocation behavior.","rule":"Analyze garbage collection in the context of the runtime, workload, allocation rate, heap configuration, and target latency requirements.","testing_guidance":"Correlate allocation behavior and GC activity with workload latency and throughput.","verification_method":"Use runtime-specific diagnostics and compare before/after workload measurements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"RUNTIME_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Managed-runtime performance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B04-R001","D21-B14-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B14-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Runtime behavior","concept":"Allocation reduction is not equivalent to GC optimization","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why is reducing allocation count not automatically the same as improving garbage-collection performance?","answer":"Allocation rate, object lifetime, heap occupancy, object graph structure, and collector behavior all influence GC. A change that reduces allocation count can still increase object lifetime or memory retention.","rule":"Measure runtime-specific memory and GC behavior rather than using allocation count as the sole proxy.","testing_guidance":"Record allocation, heap occupancy, GC activity, and target latency/throughput.","verification_method":"Confirm improvement across the relevant runtime metrics and primary workload objective.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"RUNTIME_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Managed-runtime memory-performance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B04-R005","D21-B14-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B14-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Runtime behavior","concept":"JIT warmup and steady state","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why can JIT-based runtimes show different startup and steady-state performance?","answer":"A runtime may initially execute code differently from later execution after profiling, compilation, optimization, or other runtime adaptation has occurred.","rule":"Separate startup, warmup, and steady-state measurements when they represent different product requirements.","testing_guidance":"Define explicit workload phases and report them separately.","verification_method":"Compare each phase with its relevant baseline and acceptance criterion.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"RUNTIME_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Managed-runtime optimization principle."],"related_records":["D21-B02-R002","D21-B10-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B14-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Runtime behavior","concept":"Runtime version affects performance behavior","knowledge_type":"VERSION_RULE","question":"Why must runtime version be part of performance evidence?","answer":"Compiler, interpreter, JIT, garbage collector, standard-library, and runtime implementation changes can alter performance behavior.","rule":"A performance measurement must identify the runtime version when runtime behavior materially contributes to the result.","version_range":"Target-specific","verification_method":"Record exact runtime/compiler versions and compare equivalent builds when evaluating a version change.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"VERSION_AWARENESS_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D21 version-awareness specification."],"related_records":["D21-B02-R005","D21-B10-R006"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B14-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Runtime behavior","concept":"Python memory allocation profiling","knowledge_type":"CODE_EXAMPLE","objective":"Trace Python memory allocations for diagnosis.","source_code":"import tracemalloc\n\ntracemalloc.start()\n\n# Run the workload here.\nvalues = [i * i for i in range(10000)]\n\ncurrent, peak = tracemalloc.get_traced_memory()\nprint(current, peak)\ntracemalloc.stop()","expected_behavior":"The program reports traced current and peak allocation sizes for the portion observed by tracemalloc.","limitations":"The result represents allocations tracked by tracemalloc and is not automatically equivalent to total process resident memory.","testing_guidance":"Use snapshots and differences when identifying allocation sources; compare results under a controlled workload.","verification_method":"Correlate traced allocations with the suspected code path and independent process-level memory evidence when necessary.","language":"Python","language_version":"3.x","dependencies":["Python standard library"],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STATIC_REVIEW","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Python debugging/profiling documentation describes tracemalloc and allocation tracing. 5"],"related_records":["D21-B04-R005","D21-B09-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B15-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Tail latency","concept":"Tail latency","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is tail latency?","answer":"Tail latency describes high-percentile response times, such as a selected upper percentile, rather than the average or median response time.","rule":"Use tail-latency measurements when slow requests materially affect users or dependent systems.","performance_implication":"A system can have acceptable average latency while a meaningful fraction of requests experience severe delays.","testing_guidance":"Define the required percentile and measurement population before testing.","verification_method":"Measure the declared percentile under representative load and analyze outliers.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"PERFORMANCE_DEFINITION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Distributed and service performance measurement principle."],"related_records":["D21-B15-R002","D21-B15-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B15-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Tail latency","concept":"Average latency can hide failures","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is average latency insufficient for many services?","answer":"Averages can hide a small but operationally important population of very slow requests.","failure_mode":"Average response time remains within target while upper-percentile latency violates the user or service requirement.","root_cause":"Only aggregate central tendency was monitored.","solution":"Measure appropriate latency percentiles and investigate the causes of slow requests.","testing_guidance":"Collect enough samples for stable percentile analysis and segment by workload where needed.","verification_method":"Verify that required latency percentiles satisfy the declared acceptance criteria.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"PERFORMANCE_FAILURE_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Latency measurement principle."],"related_records":["D21-B15-R001","D21-B15-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B15-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Tail latency","concept":"Tail amplification in distributed systems","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can distributed request fan-out increase tail latency risk?","answer":"A higher-level operation that depends on multiple downstream operations can be delayed when one or more dependencies experience slow responses. As the number of dependencies grows, the aggregate operation becomes more exposed to slow-tail behavior.","rule":"Analyze dependency count, parallelism, timeout policy, and downstream latency distributions when diagnosing distributed tail latency.","tradeoff":"Parallel fan-out can reduce dependency critical-path time compared with sequential calls but can increase downstream load.","testing_guidance":"Measure end-to-end latency together with downstream percentile distributions and fan-out width.","verification_method":"Correlate high-level slow requests with downstream slow calls.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Distributed-systems latency principle."],"related_records":["D21-B05-R004","D21-B15-R002","D21-B07-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B15-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Tail latency","concept":"Latency outlier investigation","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a slow-request population be investigated?","answer":"Identify the affected population, correlate slow requests with request type and dependency behavior, inspect resource saturation and queueing, and determine whether the latency is caused by a local hotspot, downstream dependency, contention, retry, timeout, or unusual input.","requirements":["Request correlation identifier","Latency measurement","Relevant dependency/resource telemetry"],"testing_guidance":"Preserve representative slow cases while avoiding sensitive-data exposure.","verification_method":"Trace representative outliers to evidence-backed causal factors and reproduce when practical.","security_implication":"Diagnostic traces must protect sensitive request data.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"DIAGNOSTIC_PROCEDURE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Distributed performance diagnosis methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B09-R005","D21-B15-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B15-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Tail latency","concept":"Timeout and retry amplification","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can retries worsen latency and overload?","answer":"A failed or slow request can generate additional attempts, increasing work and downstream load. Under overload, retries can consume capacity that would otherwise serve new requests.","failure_mode":"Retries increase total downstream request volume during an existing latency or failure event.","root_cause":"Retry policy lacks bounded attempts, backoff, or overload-aware behavior.","solution":"Use bounded retries, appropriate backoff, explicit timeout budgets, and idempotency-aware policies.","security_implication":"Unbounded retries can contribute to resource exhaustion.","testing_guidance":"Inject controlled failures and measure request amplification, latency, and downstream load.","verification_method":"Confirm bounded retry behavior and acceptable recovery performance.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"RELIABILITY_PERFORMANCE_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Distributed-systems retry and overload principle."],"related_records":["D21-B05-R005","D21-B15-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B16-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Scalability","concept":"Vertical versus horizontal scaling","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between vertical and horizontal scaling?","answer":"Vertical scaling increases resources available to an individual execution environment, while horizontal scaling increases the number of execution instances or workers.","rule":"Choose based on workload architecture, state management, resource bottlenecks, cost constraints, and required availability characteristics.","tradeoff":"Horizontal scaling can introduce coordination and distribution costs; vertical scaling can encounter hardware or single-instance limits.","testing_guidance":"Measure scaling behavior across the resource dimension actually being changed.","verification_method":"Confirm that the target workload scales according to the stated architecture and constraints.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"SCALABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Scalability engineering principle."],"related_records":["D21-B16-R002","D21-B16-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B16-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Scalability","concept":"Scaling efficiency","knowledge_type":"METRIC_DEFINITION","question":"What does scaling efficiency describe?","answer":"Scaling efficiency compares observed performance improvement against an idealized scaling expectation for the added resource or parallel capacity.","rule":"Define the ideal scaling model explicitly before calculating efficiency.","limitations":"Real systems contain serial work, contention, coordination overhead, and resource bottlenecks that prevent perfect scaling.","testing_guidance":"Measure the same workload across controlled resource levels.","verification_method":"Calculate efficiency from actual measurements and preserve the workload and environment metadata.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"SCALABILITY_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Parallel and scalability measurement principle."],"related_records":["D21-B07-R001","D21-B16-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B16-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Scalability","concept":"Amdahl-style serial bottleneck reasoning","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can a serial component limit parallel speedup?","answer":"If a fraction of a workload cannot be parallelized, increasing parallel resources cannot accelerate that portion. The serial fraction therefore limits achievable overall speedup.","rule":"Identify serial work before assuming that additional parallel workers can provide proportional improvement.","testing_guidance":"Measure performance across increasing parallelism and identify where scaling saturates.","verification_method":"Compare observed scaling with the serial-work model and inspect the remaining bottleneck.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"PARALLEL_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Classical parallel-performance reasoning."],"related_records":["D21-B07-R001","D21-B16-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B16-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Scalability","concept":"Resource saturation","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"What happens when a system reaches a dominant resource limit?","answer":"As demand approaches a resource's capacity, queueing, latency, contention, and error behavior can increase. Adding demand beyond capacity may reduce useful throughput or destabilize the workload.","rule":"Identify the saturated resource before changing concurrency or capacity.","testing_guidance":"Monitor CPU, memory, storage, network, connection pools, queues, and downstream services as appropriate.","verification_method":"Correlate saturation with changes in latency, throughput, and error rates.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"CAPACITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Queueing and capacity engineering principle."],"related_records":["D21-B07-R004","D21-B15-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B16-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Scalability","concept":"Scalability bottleneck migration","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can optimization expose a new bottleneck?","answer":"Improving one resource or operation can increase pressure on another component, causing the dominant bottleneck to move.","rule":"After each material optimization, reassess the whole workload rather than assuming the original bottleneck remains dominant.","testing_guidance":"Repeat workload-level profiling after significant changes.","verification_method":"Compare resource utilization and target metrics before and after optimization.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"SYSTEMS_PERFORMANCE_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance bottleneck migration principle."],"related_records":["D21-B09-R001","D21-B16-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B17-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Capacity planning","concept":"Capacity planning requires workload assumptions","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What inputs are required for meaningful capacity planning?","answer":"Capacity planning requires expected workload volume, concurrency, resource limits, latency or throughput requirements, workload growth assumptions, failure margins, and measured or justified resource behavior.","rule":"Capacity conclusions are conditional on their workload assumptions.","requirements":["Workload model","Resource model","Performance requirement","Growth assumptions","Safety margin"],"testing_guidance":"Validate assumptions against observed workload data where available.","verification_method":"Compare predicted capacity with controlled load measurements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"CAPACITY_METHOD","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Capacity planning methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B16-R004","D21-B17-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B17-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Capacity planning","concept":"Headroom","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should capacity plans include headroom?","answer":"Operating continuously at theoretical maximum capacity leaves little room for workload variation, failures, maintenance, bursts, or resource contention.","rule":"Define headroom according to actual service requirements and risk tolerance rather than using an arbitrary universal percentage.","testing_guidance":"Stress-test the workload around expected operating levels and identify degradation behavior.","verification_method":"Demonstrate acceptable performance under expected peaks plus the declared operational margin.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"CAPACITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Capacity and reliability engineering principle."],"related_records":["D21-B16-R004","D21-B17-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B17-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Capacity planning","concept":"Load testing versus stress testing","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between load testing and stress testing?","answer":"Load testing evaluates behavior under a defined expected or representative workload, while stress testing intentionally pushes the system beyond normal operating conditions to characterize limits and failure behavior.","rule":"Use both when capacity boundaries and overload behavior matter.","testing_guidance":"Define workload, resource limits, success criteria, degradation criteria, and recovery expectations before testing.","verification_method":"Record observed throughput, latency, errors, saturation, and recovery behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"TESTING_DEFINITION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance-testing methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B17-R004","D21-B16-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B17-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Capacity planning","concept":"Overload behavior as a first-class performance property","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should overload behavior be tested?","answer":"A system can meet normal performance requirements yet fail catastrophically when demand exceeds capacity. Controlled overload testing reveals queue growth, rejection behavior, timeouts, retries, resource exhaustion, and recovery characteristics.","rule":"Define how the system should degrade when capacity is exceeded.","security_implication":"Overload handling is relevant to resource-exhaustion resilience.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled, authorized load and stop criteria.","verification_method":"Verify bounded resource consumption and expected degradation/recovery behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"RESILIENCE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Capacity and resilience engineering principle."],"related_records":["D21-B15-R005","D21-B17-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B17-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Capacity planning","concept":"Capacity estimate versus measured capacity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should estimated capacity be represented?","answer":"An estimated capacity is a model-derived expectation and must not be represented as a measured limit unless it has been validated by controlled testing.","rule":"Separate ESTIMATED capacity from MEASURED capacity.","evidence_level":"ESTIMATED","limitations":["Model assumptions may not capture contention, workload distribution, downstream limits, or nonlinear behavior."],"verification_method":"Validate estimates against controlled load measurements before operational adoption.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D21 performance truth model."],"related_records":["D21-B02-R005","D21-B17-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B18-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Memory and data movement","concept":"Copy amplification","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is copy amplification?","answer":"Copy amplification occurs when the same logical data is copied through multiple intermediate representations or buffers, increasing CPU, memory bandwidth, allocation, and latency costs.","failure_mode":"Large payloads move through several unnecessary copies.","root_cause":"Interfaces or transformations require repeated materialization of equivalent data.","solution":"Reduce unnecessary copies where safe and preserve ownership, lifetime, validation, and isolation requirements.","security_implication":"Removing copies must not create unsafe aliasing or lifetime violations.","testing_guidance":"Measure allocations, memory bandwidth where available, and end-to-end latency.","verification_method":"Confirm behavioral equivalence and measurable reduction in the relevant resource cost.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"SYSTEMS_PERFORMANCE_FAILURE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Systems performance and data-movement principle."],"related_records":["D21-B13-R001","D21-B04-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B18-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Memory and data movement","concept":"Zero-copy as a conditional optimization","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why is zero-copy not automatically superior?","answer":"Avoiding copies can reduce data movement, but zero-copy designs can increase complexity, constrain ownership and lifetime, or create aliasing and synchronization requirements.","rule":"Use zero-copy techniques only when measurements show data movement is material and the resulting ownership model remains correct.","security_implication":"Buffer lifetime and isolation must be preserved.","testing_guidance":"Test ownership, mutation, lifetime, concurrency, and error paths in addition to performance.","verification_method":"Demonstrate the target performance benefit and correctness under realistic workloads.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"SYSTEMS_TRADEOFF","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Systems programming performance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B18-R001","D21-B07-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B18-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Memory and data movement","concept":"Buffer sizing","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can buffer size affect performance?","answer":"Small buffers may increase operation overhead, while very large buffers can increase memory consumption, latency, cache pressure, or wasted capacity.","rule":"Choose buffer sizes from workload characteristics and measurement rather than assuming larger is always faster.","testing_guidance":"Evaluate multiple sizes against throughput, latency, memory, and resource utilization.","verification_method":"Select a size only after representative measurement and correctness testing.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"I/O_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Buffering and I/O performance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B05-R002","D21-B18-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B18-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Memory and data movement","concept":"Memory bandwidth bottleneck","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"How can memory bandwidth become the bottleneck?","answer":"A workload can spend substantial time moving data between memory hierarchy levels rather than performing computational instructions. Increasing CPU parallelism may then provide little benefit.","rule":"When computation is lightweight relative to data movement, investigate locality, data volume, access pattern, and memory bandwidth.","testing_guidance":"Use hardware/runtime profiling where available and vary data size/access patterns.","verification_method":"Correlate memory behavior with workload scaling and target metric.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"HARDWARE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":["Computer architecture performance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B08-R003","D21-B18-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B18-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Memory and data movement","concept":"Data representation trade-off","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can a more compact representation improve performance but increase CPU cost?","answer":"Compact representations reduce memory footprint and transferred bytes but may require encoding, decoding, or conversion work.","rule":"Choose representation based on the dominant resource and end-to-end workload requirements.","testing_guidance":"Measure memory, transfer size, CPU cost, and latency together.","verification_method":"Compare complete workload behavior under representative data distributions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"SYSTEMS_PERFORMANCE_TRADEOFF","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Data representation performance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B13-R004","D21-B18-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B19-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Performance-aware architecture","concept":"Critical path","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What is a performance critical path?","answer":"The critical path is the sequence of dependent work that determines the minimum completion time for a particular operation or workflow under the modeled conditions.","rule":"Optimize work that materially lies on the target critical path rather than unrelated work.","tradeoff":"Removing critical-path work can expose another bottleneck or increase resource consumption elsewhere.","testing_guidance":"Map dependencies and correlate the path with measured timing evidence.","verification_method":"Demonstrate that the changed component affects the end-to-end target metric.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance architecture principle."],"related_records":["D21-B09-R001","D21-B15-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B19-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Performance-aware architecture","concept":"Synchronous versus asynchronous boundary","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When can asynchronous architecture improve responsiveness?","answer":"Asynchronous architecture can allow independent waiting operations to overlap and prevent a single waiting operation from unnecessarily occupying a constrained execution resource.","rule":"Use asynchronous boundaries when workload independence and resource behavior justify them.","tradeoff":"Asynchronous designs add lifecycle, cancellation, error-propagation, and observability complexity.","testing_guidance":"Test cancellation, timeout, ordering, backpressure, and failure behavior.","verification_method":"Compare end-to-end latency, throughput, resource usage, and correctness.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"ARCHITECTURE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Async architecture principle; Python asyncio documentation describes concurrency for I/O-bound workloads. 6"],"related_records":["D21-B11-R001","D21-B07-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B19-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Performance-aware architecture","concept":"Backpressure","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is backpressure?","answer":"Backpressure limits or slows producers when downstream processing capacity is insufficient, preventing uncontrolled queue growth and resource exhaustion.","rule":"Bound queues and define producer behavior when consumers cannot keep up.","security_implication":"Backpressure can reduce resource-exhaustion risk caused by unbounded workload accumulation.","performance_implication":"Backpressure may reduce throughput under overload but can preserve system stability and bounded latency.","testing_guidance":"Overload consumers deliberately and observe queue size, producer behavior, latency, and recovery.","verification_method":"Verify bounded resource consumption and declared degradation behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"SYSTEMS_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Queueing, streaming, and resilience engineering principle."],"related_records":["D21-B17-R004","D21-B19-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B19-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Performance-aware architecture","concept":"Unbounded queue growth","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why are unbounded queues dangerous for performance?","answer":"When producers can continuously add work faster than consumers process it, queued work can consume increasing memory and waiting time until the system becomes unstable or fails.","failure_mode":"Queue length grows without a defined bound.","root_cause":"Producer rate is not constrained by downstream capacity.","solution":"Apply backpressure, bounded queues, admission control, load shedding, or workload-specific flow control.","testing_guidance":"Test producer rates above consumer capacity and measure memory, latency, and recovery.","verification_method":"Verify queue/resource bounds and expected overload behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"SYSTEMS_FAILURE_PATTERN","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Queueing and resilience engineering principle."],"related_records":["D21-B17-R004","D21-B19-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B19-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Performance-aware architecture","concept":"Performance budget","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What is a performance budget?","answer":"A performance budget is an explicit limit assigned to a measurable resource or user-visible property, such as latency, payload size, startup time, CPU, or memory.","rule":"Budgets should be measurable, tied to requirements, and testable.","requirements":["Defined metric","Defined population/workload","Acceptance threshold","Measurement method"],"testing_guidance":"Turn budgets into automated or repeatable validation where practical.","verification_method":"Compare measured behavior against the declared budget.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"PERFORMANCE_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance engineering governance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B10-R001","D21-B17-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B20-R001","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Advanced optimization","concept":"Compiler optimization is not a substitute for algorithmic correctness","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should compiler optimization not be used as a substitute for fixing an inefficient algorithm?","answer":"Compiler optimizations can improve generated code within the semantics and constraints of the implementation, but they generally do not remove fundamentally excessive algorithmic work.","rule":"Correct algorithmic and architectural inefficiencies before relying on compiler-level optimization.","testing_guidance":"Measure algorithmic alternatives under representative workloads.","verification_method":"Compare workload scaling and correctness across implementations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"COMPILER_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Compiler and algorithm performance principle."],"related_records":["D21-B06-R001","D21-B06-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B20-R002","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Advanced optimization","concept":"Microbenchmark validity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why can a microbenchmark produce misleading conclusions?","answer":"A microbenchmark may omit real workload effects such as allocation, cache state, branch behavior, I/O, concurrency, startup, compiler optimization, and interaction with surrounding code.","rule":"Use microbenchmarks to answer narrowly defined questions and validate important conclusions with workload-level tests.","testing_guidance":"Control compiler/runtime effects and benchmark realistic operation sizes where applicable.","verification_method":"Correlate microbenchmark results with representative system-level measurements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"BENCHMARKING_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Benchmarking methodology; Python documentation explicitly distinguishes profiling from benchmarking and provides dedicated timing tooling. 7"],"related_records":["D21-B02-R003","D21-B20-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B20-R003","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Advanced optimization","concept":"Dead-code elimination in benchmarks","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can compiler optimization invalidate a naive benchmark?","answer":"If benchmarked work has no observable effect, a compiler may transform or eliminate parts of the computation. The measured code can therefore differ materially from the intended workload.","failure_mode":"A benchmark reports implausibly low execution time because the compiler removed or simplified the intended work.","root_cause":"Benchmark lacks an appropriate observable use of the result or optimization barrier.","solution":"Use a validated benchmark harness and language-specific mechanisms appropriate for preventing invalid elimination.","testing_guidance":"Inspect generated behavior where necessary and use established benchmark frameworks.","verification_method":"Confirm that the benchmark actually exercises the intended operation.","language":"Rust","language_version":"1.27+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"COMPILER_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Rust std::hint documentation describes black_box as an optimization barrier-style hint for benchmarking and optimization-sensitive code. 8"],"related_records":["D21-B10-R008","D21-B20-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B20-R004","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Advanced optimization","concept":"Profile-guided optimization evidence","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What is required before trusting profile-guided optimization results?","answer":"The profile must represent the intended workload, the build and compiler/runtime versions must be recorded, and the resulting implementation must be tested under representative workloads.","rule":"A PGO result is workload-dependent evidence, not a universal performance guarantee.","requirements":["Representative profile","Known compiler/runtime version","Controlled comparison","Functional regression validation"],"testing_guidance":"Compare baseline and PGO builds using the same workload and measurement methodology.","verification_method":"Record build provenance and actual performance measurements.","language":"Go","language_version":"1.20+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"DOCUMENTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Go official PGO documentation describes profile-guided optimization and workload-derived profiles. ([go.dev](https://go.dev/doc/pgo?utm_source=chatgpt.com))"],"related_records":["D21-B10-R007","D21-B20-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B20-R005","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Advanced optimization","concept":"Performance optimization safety gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be checked before accepting a low-level optimization?","answer":"The implementation must preserve functional semantics, security properties, resource ownership, concurrency correctness, compatibility requirements, and the declared performance objective.","rule":"A faster implementation that changes required behavior is not a valid optimization.","testing_guidance":"Run functional, boundary, concurrency, security, and performance regression tests as applicable.","verification_method":"Require evidence for both semantic correctness and the performance claim.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D21 code-quality and verification methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B10-R005","D21-B18-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B20-R006","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Advanced optimization","concept":"Performance optimization lifecycle","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What is the complete evidence-driven performance optimization loop?","answer":"Define requirement → establish baseline → identify workload → measure → profile when necessary → diagnose bottleneck → formulate hypothesis → implement minimal change → test correctness → benchmark → compare evidence → inspect regressions → accept or revert → record provenance.","objective":"Make performance optimization reproducible and evidence-driven.","relationships":["REQUIREMENT -> BASELINE -> MEASUREMENT -> DIAGNOSIS -> CHANGE -> TEST -> BENCHMARK -> VERIFICATION -> EXPERIENCE"],"testing_guidance":"Do not skip baseline or post-change measurement for claims of improvement.","verification_method":"Require traceable evidence from the original performance requirement through the final result.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_WORKFLOW","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D21 performance methodology."],"related_records":["D21-B01-R004","D21-B09-R001","D21-B10-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D21-B20-R007","dataset_id":"D21","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Performance Engineering","subtopic":"Advanced optimization","concept":"Optimization stopping criterion","knowledge_type":"DECISION_RULE","question":"When should performance optimization stop?","answer":"Optimization should stop when the relevant requirement is satisfied and further changes have insufficient demonstrated value relative to complexity, risk, maintenance cost, or resource trade-offs.","rule":"Do not optimize indefinitely without a measurable requirement or meaningful benefit.","tradeoff":"Additional optimization may still be justified for future scale, cost, or reliability requirements, but that justification must be explicit.","verification_method":"Record the satisfied requirement and evidence supporting the decision to stop.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"STRUCTURED_REVIEW","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_DECISION_RULE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Performance engineering optimization governance."],"related_records":["D21-B20-R006","D21-B10-R005"]}

