{"record_id":"D21-B21-004","batch_id":"D21-B21","domain":"performance","topic":"optimization equivalence","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","language":"multi-language","concept":"An optimization is invalid if it changes required observable behavior unless the changed behavior is explicitly part of the specification.","implementation_pattern":"Treat semantic equivalence as a prerequisite for accepting a performance transformation.","example":"Replacing a stable sort with an unstable sort may improve implementation freedom but is invalid when equal-key ordering is observable and required.","expected_behavior":"Optimized implementation preserves documented outputs, errors, ordering guarantees, side effects, and externally observable timing constraints where specified.","anti_pattern":"Accepting lower latency as sufficient evidence that an optimization is correct.","performance_effect":"Prevents performance improvements from introducing silent semantic regressions.","verification_method":"Run functional regression tests and compare relevant outputs before accepting the optimization.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","version_range":"Language-independent"}
