{"record_id":"D21-B23-001","batch_id":"D21-B23","domain":"performance","topic":"blocking event loop","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","language":"Python","concept":"Blocking CPU or I/O work executed directly on an asyncio event-loop thread can delay unrelated asynchronous tasks.","implementation_pattern":"Move blocking work outside the event loop when the API and workload permit it.","code_example":"async def load(path):\n    return await asyncio.to_thread(read_file, path)","expected_behavior":"The event loop remains available for other asynchronous work while the blocking function executes in a worker thread.","anti_pattern":"Calling a long-running blocking function directly from an async request handler.","performance_effect":"Prevents unrelated coroutines from experiencing avoidable event-loop stalls.","verification_method":"Run concurrent requests while instrumenting event-loop delay and verify that blocking work does not monopolize the loop.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DOCUMENTED","confidence":"HIGH","version_range":"Python >=3.11","provenance":{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Python asyncio development documentation","source_version":"3.14.6"}}
