{"record_id":"D21-B50-004","batch_id":"D21-B50","domain":"performance","topic":"performance optimization replay","knowledge_type":"learning_system_pattern","language":"multi-language","concept":"An optimization experiment should be replayable from its workload, baseline, transformation, and measurement metadata.","implementation_pattern":"Store sufficient experiment metadata for an engineering agent to reconstruct the evaluation without guessing missing conditions.","required_metadata":["source_revision","runtime_version","dependency_versions","hardware_context","workload_identifier","input_scale","concurrency","benchmark_method","warmup_policy","measurement_count","metrics","optimization_change"],"expected_behavior":"A future agent can reproduce or appropriately reject an old performance claim when environmental assumptions no longer hold.","anti_pattern":"Retaining only a benchmark score with no workload or environment information.","performance_effect":"Improves long-term reliability of optimization knowledge.","verification_method":"Attempt replay using stored metadata and mark unavailable dependencies or environment conditions explicitly.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","version_range":"Language-independent"}

{"record_id":"D21-B50-005","batch_id":"D21-B50","domain":"performance","topic":"performance phase boundary","knowledge_type":"dataset_coverage_control","language":"multi-language","concept":"Performance optimization knowledge should distinguish local code optimization from system capacity, architecture, database, distributed, and operational performance concerns while preserving their relationships.","implementation_pattern":"Link records across performance subdomains instead of duplicating the same optimization principle under multiple labels.","relationship_examples":["CPU hotspot -> algorithm choice","allocation pressure -> memory behavior","API payload -> serialization/network cost","database query -> index/query-plan behavior","worker count -> downstream saturation","cache hit rate -> backend load"],"expected_behavior":"The knowledge fabric can traverse from a measured bottleneck to related implementation and verification records without duplicate concepts.","anti_pattern":"Creating independent copies of the same performance rule for every language or subsystem without adding language-specific evidence.","performance_effect":"Improves deduplication, retrieval precision, and cross-domain reasoning.","verification_method":"Run semantic duplicate detection and verify each record contributes a distinct implementation or reasoning dimension.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","version_range":"Language-independent"}

{"record_id":"D21-B51-001","batch_id":"D21-B51","domain":"performance","topic":"tail_latency","knowledge_type":"performance_principle","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Optimize and measure tail latency separately from average latency because a system can have an acceptable mean while a small fraction of requests experience severe delays.","implementation_pattern":"Record latency distributions and evaluate percentile-oriented objectives such as p95, p99, and p99.9 using a workload representative of production request classes.","anti_pattern":"Use only arithmetic mean latency as the acceptance criterion.","performance_effect":"Improves visibility into rare but operationally significant latency spikes and prevents averages from hiding queueing, contention, pauses, or downstream outliers.","verification_method":"Run repeated representative workloads and compare latency distributions, not only mean values; inspect outlier traces for common causes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Latency distribution and tail-latency analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B51-002","batch_id":"D21-B51","domain":"performance","topic":"queueing_delay","knowledge_type":"performance_principle","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"As utilization approaches a resource's practical capacity, queueing delay can increase disproportionately and become a major component of response time.","implementation_pattern":"Measure service time and waiting time separately for bounded resources such as worker pools, database connections, CPU executors, and network clients.","anti_pattern":"Treat increased end-to-end latency as proof that the underlying operation itself became slower.","performance_effect":"Allows optimization to target queueing and saturation rather than unnecessarily optimizing already-fast service work.","verification_method":"Instrument enqueue timestamp, start timestamp, completion timestamp, and resource utilization; compare waiting-time and service-time distributions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Queueing and capacity analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B51-003","batch_id":"D21-B51","domain":"performance","topic":"queue_depth","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Queue depth is a useful saturation signal when interpreted together with arrival rate, service rate, and latency.","implementation_pattern":"Expose queue length, enqueue rate, dequeue rate, oldest-item age, and rejection/drop counts as separate metrics.","anti_pattern":"Monitor queue length alone without distinguishing a temporary burst from sustained overload.","performance_effect":"Improves diagnosis of transient bursts versus persistent capacity deficits.","verification_method":"Inject controlled workload bursts and sustained overload; verify that queue metrics distinguish recovery from continuing saturation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Queueing-system observability"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B51-004","batch_id":"D21-B51","domain":"performance","topic":"admission_control","knowledge_type":"optimization_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Admission control can preserve system responsiveness by refusing, delaying, or degrading work before uncontrolled overload consumes all resources.","implementation_pattern":"Define explicit concurrency or queue limits and return controlled overload behavior when capacity is exhausted.","anti_pattern":"Accept unlimited work and allow memory, queues, threads, or connections to grow without bounds.","performance_effect":"Protects bounded resources and can reduce cascading latency growth during overload.","verification_method":"Run overload tests and verify bounded memory/resource usage, predictable rejection behavior, and recovery after load falls.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Admission control and overload protection"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B51-005","batch_id":"D21-B51","domain":"performance","topic":"load_shedding","knowledge_type":"optimization_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Load shedding can preserve critical functionality by deliberately dropping or degrading lower-priority work during resource saturation.","implementation_pattern":"Classify work by priority and define deterministic shedding rules before resource exhaustion occurs.","anti_pattern":"Allow every request class to compete equally for a saturated resource.","performance_effect":"Can preserve availability and latency for critical workloads under overload.","verification_method":"Simulate resource exhaustion and verify that low-priority work is shed while protected workloads remain within their defined limits.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Overload control and priority-based load shedding"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B52-001","batch_id":"D21-B52","domain":"performance","topic":"backpressure","knowledge_type":"concurrency_pattern","language":"Python,JavaScript,TypeScript,Java,C,C++,Rust,Go","concept":"Backpressure prevents producers from continuously outpacing consumers by propagating capacity constraints toward the source of work.","implementation_pattern":"Use bounded queues, flow-control mechanisms, or awaitable producer operations that stop accepting additional work when downstream capacity is exhausted.","anti_pattern":"Use unbounded producer-side buffering to hide a sustained throughput mismatch.","performance_effect":"Limits memory growth and exposes sustained overload instead of converting it into delayed failure.","verification_method":"Create a producer rate greater than consumer capacity and verify bounded buffering plus observable producer throttling.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Backpressure and flow-control engineering"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B52-002","batch_id":"D21-B52","domain":"performance","topic":"bounded_concurrency","knowledge_type":"resource_control","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Limiting concurrent operations can improve throughput and latency when unrestricted concurrency causes contention or resource exhaustion.","implementation_pattern":"Set a concurrency bound based on the actual bottleneck resource and measure throughput and latency as the bound changes.","anti_pattern":"Assume that maximum concurrency always produces maximum throughput.","performance_effect":"Can reduce context switching, lock contention, connection exhaustion, queueing, and downstream overload.","verification_method":"Sweep concurrency limits under representative load and compare throughput, latency percentiles, CPU utilization, memory, and error rates.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Concurrency-limit and saturation analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B52-003","batch_id":"D21-B52","domain":"performance","topic":"connection_pooling","knowledge_type":"resource_reuse","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Pooling expensive reusable resources can reduce setup overhead, but an oversized pool can increase contention and downstream saturation.","implementation_pattern":"Reuse connections or workers through a bounded pool and tune pool capacity against measured downstream capacity.","anti_pattern":"Create a new expensive connection or worker for every operation or set pool size arbitrarily high.","performance_effect":"Reduces repeated setup cost while keeping resource usage bounded.","verification_method":"Measure acquisition wait time, active resources, utilization, downstream saturation, and end-to-end latency while varying pool capacity.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Resource pooling and capacity management"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B52-004","batch_id":"D21-B52","domain":"performance","topic":"work_stealing_and_scheduling","knowledge_type":"parallelism_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Parallel schedulers can improve utilization by redistributing available work among workers, but scheduling overhead and synchronization can dominate when tasks are too small.","implementation_pattern":"Use task granularity large enough to amortize scheduling and synchronization costs and verify load balance under realistic workload skew.","anti_pattern":"Parallelize every tiny operation without measuring scheduler overhead.","performance_effect":"Can improve processor utilization for sufficiently independent and substantial workloads while avoiding excessive scheduling cost.","verification_method":"Compare serial, coarse-grained parallel, and fine-grained parallel implementations using the same workload and measure useful work versus scheduling overhead.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.96,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"parallel_computing_principle","source":"Task scheduling and work-distribution analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B52-005","batch_id":"D21-B52","domain":"performance","topic":"contention_amplification","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Increasing concurrency can amplify contention on shared locks, memory bandwidth, caches, database connections, or external services.","implementation_pattern":"Identify shared bottleneck resources and measure contention metrics while increasing concurrency.","anti_pattern":"Interpret higher thread or task count as direct evidence of better scalability.","performance_effect":"Prevents false scalability assumptions and helps locate the actual shared bottleneck.","verification_method":"Run a concurrency sweep and correlate throughput, latency, CPU utilization, lock wait, queue wait, and downstream saturation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Scalability and contention analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B53-001","batch_id":"D21-B53","domain":"performance","topic":"serialization_cost","knowledge_type":"data_path_optimization","language":"Python,JavaScript,TypeScript,Java,C,C++,Rust,Go","concept":"Serialization and deserialization can become a significant part of end-to-end latency and CPU consumption when data crosses process, network, or storage boundaries.","implementation_pattern":"Profile encoding and decoding separately from business logic and avoid repeated transformations of the same representation.","anti_pattern":"Optimize application logic while repeatedly serializing large objects between equivalent internal representations.","performance_effect":"Reduces CPU work, allocation pressure, and latency on data-heavy paths.","verification_method":"Measure serialization, deserialization, copying, and transport time independently using representative payload sizes and shapes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Data serialization performance analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B53-002","batch_id":"D21-B53","domain":"performance","topic":"copy_avoidance","knowledge_type":"memory_optimization","language":"Python,JavaScript,TypeScript,Java,C,C++,Rust,Go","concept":"Unnecessary copying of large buffers or collections increases memory bandwidth consumption and may increase allocation and cache pressure.","implementation_pattern":"Prefer ownership transfer, views, slices, references, or in-place transformations when semantics permit and safety guarantees remain explicit.","anti_pattern":"Materialize multiple full-size copies of large data solely for convenience.","performance_effect":"Can reduce memory traffic, allocation frequency, and peak memory consumption.","verification_method":"Profile allocation and copy operations and compare peak memory, memory bandwidth, and execution time before and after the transformation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"memory_performance_principle","source":"Data movement and allocation analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B53-003","batch_id":"D21-B53","domain":"performance","topic":"compression_tradeoff","knowledge_type":"tradeoff_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Compression can reduce network or storage transfer size while increasing CPU work and sometimes latency.","implementation_pattern":"Evaluate compression using payload size, compression ratio, CPU cost, decompression cost, network bandwidth, and latency as separate variables.","anti_pattern":"Enable the most aggressive compression level without measuring end-to-end impact.","performance_effect":"Can improve transfer performance when bandwidth is the bottleneck but can hurt latency when CPU is already saturated.","verification_method":"Benchmark representative payloads across compression levels and compare total end-to-end latency and resource consumption.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_tradeoff_principle","source":"Compression versus CPU/network cost analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B53-004","batch_id":"D21-B53","domain":"performance","topic":"batch_size_optimization","knowledge_type":"throughput_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Batching amortizes fixed per-operation overhead but excessive batch sizes can increase latency, memory use, and failure amplification.","implementation_pattern":"Choose batch size from measured fixed overhead, payload size, service capacity, latency requirements, and failure semantics.","anti_pattern":"Use the largest possible batch because it minimizes the number of operations.","performance_effect":"Can increase throughput while preserving predictable latency when batch size is bounded and workload-aware.","verification_method":"Measure throughput, p95/p99 latency, memory, and failure/retry cost across multiple batch sizes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Batching and amortization analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B53-005","batch_id":"D21-B53","domain":"performance","topic":"data_layout","knowledge_type":"low_level_optimization","language":"C,C++,Rust,Java,Go,Python","concept":"Data layout can materially affect cache locality and memory bandwidth when a hot loop processes large collections.","implementation_pattern":"Choose a representation that places frequently accessed data close together and minimizes unnecessary memory traversal for the dominant access pattern.","anti_pattern":"Choose an object-heavy representation without considering the actual hot-path access pattern.","performance_effect":"May improve cache locality and reduce memory traffic for data-intensive workloads.","verification_method":"Profile cache misses, memory bandwidth, and hot-loop execution time before and after layout changes using representative data.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.95,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"computer_architecture_principle","source":"Cache locality and data-layout analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B54-001","batch_id":"D21-B54","domain":"performance","topic":"garbage_collection","knowledge_type":"runtime_behavior","language":"Java","concept":"Garbage collection overhead can affect throughput and pause behavior, so GC activity should be treated as a measurable component of application performance rather than assumed to be negligible.","implementation_pattern":"Use JVM-supported diagnostics and recordings to correlate allocation behavior, GC activity, pause time, heap behavior, and application throughput.","anti_pattern":"Change GC settings blindly without measuring allocation rate, pause behavior, and workload characteristics.","performance_effect":"Improves evidence-based diagnosis of allocation and GC-related performance problems.","verification_method":"Collect runtime diagnostics under representative load and compare GC pause time, allocation behavior, throughput, and latency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"JDK_26_JDK_27","provenance":{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Oracle Java HotSpot Garbage Collection Tuning Guide","source_version":"JDK 26/27"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B54-002","batch_id":"D21-B54","domain":"performance","topic":"runtime_ergonomics","knowledge_type":"runtime_optimization","language":"Java","concept":"JVM runtime ergonomics provide default choices for garbage collection, heap sizing, and runtime compilation, so explicit tuning should follow workload-specific evidence rather than precede measurement.","implementation_pattern":"Establish a measured baseline using runtime defaults, then change one performance-relevant parameter at a time and compare against the baseline.","anti_pattern":"Apply multiple JVM tuning flags simultaneously and attribute any observed change to a single setting.","performance_effect":"Improves attribution and reduces tuning based on assumptions that may conflict with runtime heuristics.","verification_method":"Record baseline and controlled variants with identical workload, environment, and measurement procedure.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"JDK_26_JDK_27","provenance":{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Oracle Java GC ergonomics documentation","source_version":"JDK 27"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B54-003","batch_id":"D21-B54","domain":"performance","topic":"profiling_overhead","knowledge_type":"measurement_principle","language":"Java","concept":"Profiling configuration can introduce measurement overhead, and diagnostic features with higher overhead should not automatically be enabled during latency-sensitive measurements.","implementation_pattern":"Use a low-overhead profiling configuration for continuous observation and stronger diagnostics only for controlled investigations.","anti_pattern":"Enable every diagnostic feature during a benchmark and treat the resulting timing as an unmodified application baseline.","performance_effect":"Improves measurement validity by reducing instrumentation-induced distortion.","verification_method":"Run the same workload with profiling disabled, low-overhead profiling, and high-detail diagnostics; quantify the measurement delta.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"JDK_23_PLUS","provenance":{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Oracle Java Flight Recorder performance troubleshooting documentation","source_version":"JDK 23"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B54-004","batch_id":"D21-B54","domain":"performance","topic":"profile_guided_optimization","knowledge_type":"compiler_optimization","language":"Rust","concept":"Rust supports profile-guided optimization in which representative execution profiles can inform compiler optimization decisions.","implementation_pattern":"Collect representative execution data, feed it into the compiler's PGO workflow, and compare the resulting binary against a reproducible baseline workload.","anti_pattern":"Use an unrepresentative profile and assume the resulting optimization benefits every workload.","performance_effect":"Can improve generated-code performance for workloads that resemble the profile used for optimization.","verification_method":"Build baseline and PGO variants, execute identical representative workloads, and compare performance metrics with repeated measurements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"Rust_current","provenance":{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"The Rustc Book: Profile-guided Optimization","source_version":"Current documentation"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B54-005","batch_id":"D21-B54","domain":"performance","topic":"optimization_level_tradeoff","knowledge_type":"compiler_configuration","language":"Rust","concept":"Higher Rust optimization levels can change runtime performance and compilation characteristics, and a higher optimization level is not a universal guarantee of better application performance.","implementation_pattern":"Benchmark release profiles and relevant optimization levels using the actual workload and target environment before selecting a production configuration.","anti_pattern":"Assume the numerically highest optimization level must always produce the fastest program.","performance_effect":"Prevents unsupported optimization assumptions and allows runtime speed, binary size, compile time, and debugging requirements to be considered together.","verification_method":"Build controlled variants and compare runtime metrics plus binary size and build characteristics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"Rust_current","provenance":{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Cargo Book: Profiles","source_version":"Current documentation"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B55-001","batch_id":"D21-B55","domain":"performance","topic":"profile_sampling_vs_instrumentation","knowledge_type":"profiling_strategy","language":"Rust","concept":"Sampling and instrumented profiling provide different tradeoffs between runtime overhead and profile detail.","implementation_pattern":"Use sampling for low-overhead broad characterization and instrumentation when detailed execution information is necessary for a targeted investigation.","anti_pattern":"Treat every profiling technique as equivalent in overhead and information density.","performance_effect":"Improves profiler selection according to investigation goals and measurement constraints.","verification_method":"Compare profiler overhead and profile resolution using the same representative workload.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"Rust_current","provenance":{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"The Rustc Book: Profile-guided Optimization","source_version":"Current documentation"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B55-002","batch_id":"D21-B55","domain":"performance","topic":"benchmark_reproducibility","knowledge_type":"benchmarking_principle","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A performance comparison is only useful when workload, environment, build configuration, measurement procedure, and comparison baseline are sufficiently controlled.","implementation_pattern":"Persist benchmark configuration, workload parameters, software revision, compiler/runtime configuration, machine characteristics, and measurement output with each result.","anti_pattern":"Compare two timing numbers produced under unknown or materially different environments.","performance_effect":"Improves confidence that observed changes are attributable to the implementation rather than uncontrolled environmental differences.","verification_method":"Repeat measurements under controlled conditions and inspect variance before declaring a regression or improvement.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Reproducible benchmarking methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B55-003","batch_id":"D21-B55","domain":"performance","topic":"warmup_effects","knowledge_type":"benchmarking_principle","language":"Java,JavaScript,TypeScript","concept":"Runtime systems with adaptive compilation or caching behavior may exhibit different startup and steady-state performance characteristics.","implementation_pattern":"Define whether a benchmark measures cold start, warm execution, or both and apply an explicit warmup policy when measuring steady state.","anti_pattern":"Mix cold-start and steady-state measurements into one undifferentiated timing metric.","performance_effect":"Prevents startup behavior from being incorrectly interpreted as steady-state execution performance.","verification_method":"Measure cold and warmed runs separately and report the measurement protocol with results.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RUNTIME_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"runtime_performance_principle","source":"Adaptive-runtime benchmarking methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B55-004","batch_id":"D21-B55","domain":"performance","topic":"regression_thresholds","knowledge_type":"verification_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance regression gates should use workload-specific thresholds and statistical interpretation rather than arbitrary single-run timing differences.","implementation_pattern":"Define an acceptable change range for each benchmark and compare repeated measurements against the established baseline distribution.","anti_pattern":"Fail a build because one timing measurement is slightly slower than one previous run.","performance_effect":"Reduces false performance regressions caused by measurement noise while still detecting meaningful degradation.","verification_method":"Use repeated benchmark samples, stable workload definitions, and documented threshold policies.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Performance regression testing methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B55-005","batch_id":"D21-B55","domain":"performance","topic":"instruction_count","knowledge_type":"benchmark_metric","language":"Rust","concept":"Instruction count can provide a reproducible low-level comparison metric in compiler performance work, although it is not a complete representation of wall-clock application performance.","implementation_pattern":"Use instruction-level metrics as a complementary signal alongside runtime and workload-level measurements.","anti_pattern":"Treat instruction count as a universal substitute for end-to-end latency or throughput.","performance_effect":"Provides a relatively stable low-level signal for controlled comparisons while preserving higher-level performance measurements.","verification_method":"Compare instruction counts and end-to-end timings under identical workloads and investigate cases where they diverge.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.96,"version_range":"Rust_current","provenance":{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Rust Clippy benchmarking documentation","source_version":"Current documentation"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B56-001","batch_id":"D21-B56","domain":"performance","topic":"flamegraph_interpretation","knowledge_type":"profiling_analysis","language_agnostic","concept":"A flame graph is useful for locating where sampled execution time accumulates, but its visual width represents sampled presence rather than a direct causal explanation.","implementation_pattern":"Use flame graphs to identify candidate hot paths, then validate causality with targeted measurements and controlled changes.","anti_pattern":"Optimize the visually largest frame without confirming that changing it affects the target performance metric.","performance_effect":"Improves profiler-driven optimization by separating hotspot discovery from causal verification.","verification_method":"Record a baseline profile, modify a suspected hotspot, repeat the profile, and compare the target metric and profile structure.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PROFILING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.96,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"profiling_methodology","source":"Sampling-profiler and flame-graph analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B56-002","batch_id":"D21-B56","domain":"performance","topic":"profile_data_provenance","knowledge_type":"measurement_integrity","language_agnostic","concept":"Performance profiles require provenance linking the profile to the exact binary, source revision, configuration, workload, and environment that produced it.","implementation_pattern":"Store immutable benchmark/profile metadata alongside raw profile artifacts.","anti_pattern":"Compare profiles without confirming that they were generated from compatible binaries and workloads.","performance_effect":"Prevents incorrect conclusions from mismatched profiling artifacts.","verification_method":"Validate profile metadata against build identifiers, workload identifiers, runtime configuration, and benchmark environment before comparison.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_measurement_principle","source":"Profile provenance and reproducibility methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B56-003","batch_id":"D21-B56","domain":"performance","topic":"measurement_confounding","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_principle","language_agnostic","concept":"Multiple simultaneous changes make it difficult to attribute a performance difference to one optimization.","implementation_pattern":"Prefer isolated changes or explicitly modeled experimental groups when evaluating performance-sensitive modifications.","anti_pattern":"Change algorithm, data representation, runtime settings, hardware, and workload simultaneously and attribute the result to one code change.","performance_effect":"Improves causal attribution and reduces invalid optimization conclusions.","verification_method":"Use controlled experiments with a stable baseline and record all changed variables.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_EXPERIMENTAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"experimental_design_principle","source":"Controlled performance experimentation"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B56-004","batch_id":"D21-B56","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_budget","knowledge_type":"architecture_constraint","language_agnostic","concept":"A performance budget converts an abstract requirement into explicit resource limits for components or request paths.","implementation_pattern":"Allocate latency, CPU, memory, I/O, and dependency budgets to major stages of a critical path and verify them independently.","anti_pattern":"Set only an end-to-end latency target without identifying which components are allowed to consume it.","performance_effect":"Makes performance ownership and regression diagnosis more actionable.","verification_method":"Measure each stage of the critical path and verify that aggregate resource consumption remains within its allocated budget.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_architecture_principle","source":"Performance budgeting methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B56-005","batch_id":"D21-B56","domain":"performance","topic":"critical_path_analysis","knowledge_type":"optimization_method","language_agnostic","concept":"Optimization effort should prioritize components that materially contribute to the target end-to-end metric rather than components that are merely expensive in isolation.","implementation_pattern":"Decompose the request path into measured stages and prioritize work according to contribution to the constrained metric.","anti_pattern":"Optimize an expensive background operation that has negligible effect on the user-visible critical path.","performance_effect":"Improves optimization efficiency and reduces low-value tuning work.","verification_method":"Trace the critical path and compare stage-level contribution against the target metric before and after optimization.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_analysis_principle","source":"Critical-path and bottleneck analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B57-001","batch_id":"D21-B57","domain":"performance","topic":"cache_stampede","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","language_agnostic","concept":"Concurrent expiration of a shared cached item can cause many workers to regenerate the same value simultaneously, creating a load spike against the origin.","implementation_pattern":"Coordinate refreshes using single-flight ownership, refresh-ahead, bounded regeneration, or equivalent mechanisms appropriate to the consistency requirements.","anti_pattern":"Allow every concurrent miss to independently recompute an expensive value.","performance_effect":"Reduces duplicate work and protects the origin during cache expiration events.","verification_method":"Expire a popular cache entry under concurrent load and measure origin request amplification and regeneration concurrency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"distributed_systems_principle","source":"Cache stampede and request-coalescing analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B57-002","batch_id":"D21-B57","domain":"performance","topic":"cache_locality","knowledge_type":"optimization_pattern","language_agnostic","concept":"A cache hierarchy can improve performance only when reuse, locality, and cache-management overhead justify retaining the data at a given layer.","implementation_pattern":"Measure hit rate, miss cost, entry size, eviction behavior, invalidation cost, and access locality for each cache layer.","anti_pattern":"Add caching layers without measuring whether they eliminate expensive work or introduce synchronization and memory costs.","performance_effect":"Helps ensure that caching reduces total cost rather than merely shifting it into memory and cache-management overhead.","verification_method":"Compare cache-enabled and cache-disabled workloads while measuring hit rate, latency, CPU, memory, and downstream load.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"caching_performance_principle","source":"Cache locality and cost analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B57-003","batch_id":"D21-B57","domain":"performance","topic":"negative_caching","knowledge_type":"caching_pattern","language_agnostic","concept":"Caching known negative results can prevent repeated expensive lookups for absent or invalid resources when the negative state has an appropriate lifetime.","implementation_pattern":"Cache negative results with explicit expiration and invalidation semantics appropriate to the underlying data's change frequency.","anti_pattern":"Cache negative results indefinitely when resources may appear later.","performance_effect":"Can reduce repeated misses against expensive backends while avoiding permanent stale absence information when expiration is controlled.","verification_method":"Measure repeated-miss backend load and verify that newly valid resources become discoverable after the configured invalidation or expiration boundary.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CACHING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.95,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"caching_principle","source":"Negative-result caching analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B57-004","batch_id":"D21-B57","domain":"performance","topic":"cache_invalidation_cost","knowledge_type":"tradeoff_pattern","language_agnostic","concept":"A cache can reduce read cost while increasing invalidation complexity, synchronization traffic, memory usage, and stale-data risk.","implementation_pattern":"Evaluate cache effectiveness together with invalidation frequency, update fan-out, consistency requirements, and memory cost.","anti_pattern":"Measure only cache hit rate as proof that a cache improves system performance.","performance_effect":"Prevents high-hit-rate caches from being accepted when their invalidation or maintenance costs dominate the saved work.","verification_method":"Measure hit rate, saved backend work, invalidation operations, update latency, memory usage, and stale-data behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SYSTEM_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"caching_systems_principle","source":"Cache invalidation and consistency cost analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B57-005","batch_id":"D21-B57","domain":"performance","topic":"cache_key_design","knowledge_type":"data_modeling","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Cache-key cardinality and composition directly affect hit rate, memory consumption, invalidation scope, and duplicate storage.","implementation_pattern":"Construct keys from the minimal set of semantics required to distinguish valid results and normalize equivalent requests when safe.","anti_pattern":"Include volatile or irrelevant request fields that unnecessarily fragment the cache.","performance_effect":"Can increase useful reuse and reduce memory fragmentation caused by semantically equivalent keys.","verification_method":"Analyze key cardinality, duplicate-value frequency, hit rate, memory use, and correctness across representative requests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CACHING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"caching_systems_principle","source":"Cache-key and cardinality analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B58-001","batch_id":"D21-B58","domain":"performance","topic":"database_connection_saturation","knowledge_type":"database_performance","language_agnostic","concept":"A database connection pool can become a queueing bottleneck when application concurrency exceeds available database connection capacity.","implementation_pattern":"Measure pool acquisition wait time separately from query execution time and correlate both with database-side capacity.","anti_pattern":"Increase application worker count without considering database connection capacity.","performance_effect":"Prevents application concurrency from creating avoidable database queueing and latency amplification.","verification_method":"Measure pool wait, active connections, query latency, database CPU/I/O, and application latency under controlled concurrency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"database_performance_principle","source":"Connection-pool and database-capacity analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B58-002","batch_id":"D21-B58","domain":"performance","topic":"query_result_materialization","knowledge_type":"database_application_boundary","language_agnostic","concept":"Fetching or materializing more database data than the application actually needs increases database work, network transfer, memory use, and application processing.","implementation_pattern":"Select only required columns and rows and stream or paginate large result sets when complete materialization is unnecessary.","anti_pattern":"Fetch entire rows or datasets and discard most of the data in application code.","performance_effect":"Reduces transfer volume, application memory pressure, and unnecessary database result processing.","verification_method":"Compare database execution statistics, transferred bytes, application memory, and latency for minimal versus oversized result sets.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"database_performance_principle","source":"Result-set minimization and data-transfer analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B58-003","batch_id":"D21-B58","domain":"performance","topic":"pagination_cost","knowledge_type":"database_performance","language_agnostic","concept":"Pagination strategy affects database work and latency, particularly when later pages require increasingly large amounts of skipped data.","implementation_pattern":"Choose pagination semantics based on access pattern, ordering requirements, consistency requirements, and database capabilities; evaluate cursor or keyset approaches when suitable.","anti_pattern":"Assume that all pagination methods have equivalent cost regardless of page depth and ordering.","performance_effect":"Can reduce unnecessary scanning for workloads that repeatedly access later portions of ordered datasets.","verification_method":"Benchmark shallow and deep pagination against realistic table sizes and indexes and compare execution work and latency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"database_performance_principle","source":"Pagination and ordered-access analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B58-004","batch_id":"D21-B58","domain":"performance","topic":"write_amortization","knowledge_type":"database_optimization","language_agnostic","concept":"Grouping compatible writes can amortize transaction, network, logging, and synchronization overhead, but batch size must remain compatible with latency and failure requirements.","implementation_pattern":"Use bounded write batches and measure transaction duration, lock behavior, throughput, memory, and retry scope.","anti_pattern":"Perform every independent write as a separate round trip when the workload permits safe batching.","performance_effect":"Can improve write throughput by reducing fixed per-operation overhead.","verification_method":"Compare controlled batch sizes under representative write loads while measuring throughput, latency, lock contention, and failure behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"database_performance_principle","source":"Database write batching and transaction-cost analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B58-005","batch_id":"D21-B58","domain":"performance","topic":"index_tradeoff","knowledge_type":"database_tradeoff","language_agnostic","concept":"Indexes can accelerate qualifying reads while increasing storage use and write maintenance cost.","implementation_pattern":"Add indexes based on measured query patterns and verify both read improvement and write-side cost.","anti_pattern":"Create indexes for every queried column without considering selectivity, workload frequency, storage, and write overhead.","performance_effect":"Balances read latency against insert/update/delete overhead and storage consumption.","verification_method":"Compare query plans and latency before and after indexing while measuring write throughput, storage, and maintenance cost.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"database_performance_principle","source":"Index design and workload analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B59-001","batch_id":"D21-B59","domain":"performance","topic":"false_optimization","knowledge_type":"anti_pattern","language_agnostic","concept":"An optimization that improves an isolated microbenchmark but does not improve the target workload is not a validated system-level optimization.","implementation_pattern":"Require optimization changes to map to an explicit target metric and validate them using representative end-to-end or component-level workloads.","anti_pattern":"Accept a faster microbenchmark as sufficient evidence of production performance improvement.","performance_effect":"Prevents engineering effort from being spent on optimizations with no meaningful target impact.","verification_method":"Compare microbenchmark and representative workload results and document whether the optimized path contributes materially to the target metric.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Microbenchmark versus workload validation"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B59-002","batch_id":"D21-B59","domain":"performance","topic":"optimization_correctness","knowledge_type":"verification_constraint","language_agnostic","concept":"A performance optimization is invalid if it changes required program semantics even when it improves measured runtime.","implementation_pattern":"Run functional, invariant, concurrency, and boundary tests against optimized implementations before accepting performance measurements.","anti_pattern":"Treat faster execution as evidence that an optimization is correct.","performance_effect":"Prevents incorrect results, weakened guarantees, and hidden data corruption from being accepted as performance improvements.","verification_method":"Compare optimized behavior against the specification and a trusted reference implementation across normal and adversarial cases.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"software_verification_principle","source":"Optimization correctness and differential testing"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B59-003","batch_id":"D21-B59","domain":"performance","topic":"optimization_reversibility","knowledge_type":"engineering_process","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance changes should remain attributable and reversible so that regressions or unexpected resource effects can be isolated quickly.","implementation_pattern":"Keep performance changes small, versioned, benchmarked, and independently revertible when practical.","anti_pattern":"Combine many unrelated optimizations into one opaque change.","performance_effect":"Reduces diagnostic time when a performance change causes regressions in latency, memory, correctness, or maintainability.","verification_method":"Review change-level benchmark evidence and verify that individual performance transformations can be isolated or reverted.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PROCESS","confidence":0.97,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"engineering_methodology","source":"Controlled optimization and change isolation"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B59-004","batch_id":"D21-B59","domain":"performance","topic":"optimization_tradeoff_record","knowledge_type":"knowledge_representation","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance knowledge should represent the costs and benefits of an optimization rather than storing an unconditional instruction that an optimization is always better.","implementation_pattern":"Represent prerequisite conditions, target workload, expected benefit, known costs, correctness constraints, and verification evidence as structured fields.","anti_pattern":"Store rules such as 'X is always faster than Y' without workload or version context.","performance_effect":"Improves retrieval and reasoning quality by allowing the engineering system to match optimizations to applicable conditions.","verification_method":"Check that each optimization record has explicit applicability conditions, tradeoffs, and validation requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"knowledge_engineering_principle","source":"Evidence-aware performance knowledge representation"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B59-005","batch_id":"D21-B59","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_claim_expiration","knowledge_type":"version_awareness","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance claims can become invalid when compiler versions, runtimes, libraries, hardware, workloads, or implementation strategies change.","implementation_pattern":"Attach version ranges, environment assumptions, workload identifiers, and evidence timestamps to performance-sensitive knowledge.","anti_pattern":"Treat an old benchmark or optimization result as universally valid for future runtime and hardware versions.","performance_effect":"Reduces stale optimization recommendations and supports version-aware retrieval.","verification_method":"Revalidate performance-sensitive records when relevant runtime, compiler, dependency, hardware, or workload assumptions change.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"version_aware_engineering_principle","source":"Performance knowledge lifecycle management"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B60-001","batch_id":"D21-B60","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_knowledge_graph","knowledge_type":"knowledge_representation","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance knowledge becomes more useful when relationships connect bottlenecks, measurements, optimizations, prerequisites, tradeoffs, and verification methods.","implementation_pattern":"Represent relations such as OPTIMIZES, CAUSED_BY, MEASURED_BY, CONSTRAINED_BY, REGRESSES, REQUIRES, ALTERNATIVE_TO, and INVALIDATED_BY.","anti_pattern":"Store performance facts as isolated text fragments without relationships or applicability conditions.","performance_effect":"Improves reasoning over optimization chains and helps prevent incompatible recommendations.","verification_method":"Validate that important optimization records resolve to at least one measurable target, applicability condition, and verification strategy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"knowledge_engineering_principle","source":"Performance knowledge graph modeling"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B60-002","batch_id":"D21-B60","domain":"performance","topic":"contradictory_optimization_evidence","knowledge_type":"contradiction_handling","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Conflicting performance observations should be retained when their workload or environment differs instead of being incorrectly deduplicated into a single universal claim.","implementation_pattern":"Store both observations with workload, environment, version, measurement protocol, and evidence metadata and model the conditions under which each result was observed.","anti_pattern":"Delete one apparently conflicting result solely because the numerical conclusions differ.","performance_effect":"Preserves valuable conditional knowledge and reduces overgeneralization.","verification_method":"Check whether apparent contradictions can be explained by workload, hardware, runtime, compiler, configuration, or measurement differences before declaring one record invalid.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"evidence_management_principle","source":"Contradiction-aware performance knowledge modeling"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B60-003","batch_id":"D21-B60","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_replay","knowledge_type":"verification_architecture","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A performance claim is more reproducible when the system can reconstruct the workload, configuration, implementation revision, and measurement protocol used to produce it.","implementation_pattern":"Persist deterministic workload parameters, source revision, build/runtime configuration, environment metadata, benchmark procedure, and raw measurement artifacts.","anti_pattern":"Store only a final performance number without the conditions that generated it.","performance_effect":"Enables later replay, regression investigation, and evidence revalidation.","verification_method":"Attempt to reconstruct the original measurement from stored metadata and identify any missing reproducibility inputs.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"reproducible_performance_engineering","source":"Performance replay and evidence provenance methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B60-004","batch_id":"D21-B60","domain":"performance","topic":"optimization_stop_condition","knowledge_type":"decision_rule","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance optimization should stop when the target requirement is satisfied and additional optimization has insufficient measured benefit relative to complexity, risk, and maintenance cost.","implementation_pattern":"Define target performance requirements and a documented stopping criterion before extended optimization work.","anti_pattern":"Continue optimizing indefinitely because a benchmark can always be made smaller or faster.","performance_effect":"Controls complexity and focuses engineering effort on unmet requirements with measurable impact.","verification_method":"Compare current performance against explicit requirements and document expected benefit and risk for proposed additional changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Optimization economics and stopping criteria"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B60-005","batch_id":"D21-B60","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_phase_boundary","knowledge_type":"dataset_governance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A performance knowledge record should distinguish established principles, implementation patterns, measured observations, hypotheses, and unverified claims so downstream systems do not treat them as equivalent evidence.","implementation_pattern":"Use explicit evidence_level, execution_status, validation_status, provenance, version_range, workload_scope, and confidence fields.","anti_pattern":"Store measured, documented, illustrative, and hypothetical performance knowledge under the same undifferentiated fact type.","performance_effect":"Improves safe retrieval and prevents unsupported performance claims from being used as verified engineering facts.","verification_method":"Run schema validation ensuring every performance-sensitive record has evidence classification, provenance status, version scope, and verification state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"dataset_governance_principle","source":"Evidence-aware performance dataset schema"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B61-001","batch_id":"D21-B61","domain":"performance","topic":"throughput_latency_tradeoff","knowledge_type":"performance_principle","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Increasing throughput and reducing latency are related but distinct objectives and an optimization for one can worsen the other.","implementation_pattern":"Define whether the target is per-operation latency, aggregate throughput, resource efficiency, or a constrained combination before optimizing.","anti_pattern":"Use throughput improvement alone as evidence that interactive latency also improved.","performance_effect":"Prevents optimization decisions from improving the wrong performance objective.","verification_method":"Measure throughput and latency distributions simultaneously under representative workloads.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_principle","source":"Throughput and latency tradeoff analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B61-002","batch_id":"D21-B61","domain":"performance","topic":"utilization_targeting","knowledge_type":"capacity_analysis","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Maximum resource utilization is not automatically the desired operating point because near-saturation conditions can increase queueing and reduce resilience to workload bursts.","implementation_pattern":"Define operating capacity using measured latency, queue depth, error rate, and recovery requirements rather than utilization alone.","anti_pattern":"Target 100 percent CPU, worker, connection, or storage utilization as the default performance goal.","performance_effect":"Preserves headroom for workload variability and reduces saturation-induced latency.","verification_method":"Increase load progressively and identify the point where latency, queueing, or errors become unacceptable.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CAPACITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"capacity_engineering_principle","source":"Resource saturation and queueing analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B61-003","batch_id":"D21-B61","domain":"performance","topic":"burst_capacity","knowledge_type":"capacity_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A system can require temporary burst capacity even when its long-term average workload is below nominal capacity.","implementation_pattern":"Model burst arrival rate, burst duration, queue capacity, recovery rate, and acceptable queueing delay explicitly.","anti_pattern":"Size resources only from long-term average request rate.","performance_effect":"Improves resilience to short workload spikes without requiring every transient increase to become an incident.","verification_method":"Inject workloads with identical averages but different burst distributions and compare queue depth, latency, and recovery time.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CAPACITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"capacity_engineering_principle","source":"Burst and recovery analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B61-004","batch_id":"D21-B61","domain":"performance","topic":"headroom","knowledge_type":"capacity_management","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Capacity headroom provides space for workload variability, background tasks, failures, and temporary inefficiencies.","implementation_pattern":"Track available capacity as a measurable margin rather than assuming unused resources are irrelevant.","anti_pattern":"Consume all available capacity with steady-state workload because current benchmarks show no immediate failure.","performance_effect":"Reduces the probability that small workload or resource changes cause sudden saturation.","verification_method":"Measure performance while progressively reducing headroom and identify the transition into unstable or unacceptable behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CAPACITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"capacity_engineering_principle","source":"Capacity headroom analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B61-005","batch_id":"D21-B61","domain":"performance","topic":"capacity_model","knowledge_type":"performance_model","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A useful capacity model relates workload arrival rate, service capacity, concurrency, resource limits, and target latency.","implementation_pattern":"Represent each major bottleneck with measurable arrival, service, concurrency, and resource parameters and validate the model against observed behavior.","anti_pattern":"Estimate capacity using a single historical throughput number without recording workload characteristics.","performance_effect":"Improves capacity planning and makes scaling limits more explainable.","verification_method":"Compare predicted and observed behavior across multiple controlled workload levels.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_MODELING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_modeling_principle","source":"Capacity and queueing model validation"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B62-001","batch_id":"D21-B62","domain":"performance","topic":"workload_characterization","knowledge_type":"benchmark_design","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance tests should characterize workload dimensions such as request mix, payload size, concurrency, locality, read/write ratio, and temporal distribution.","implementation_pattern":"Encode workload dimensions as explicit benchmark parameters rather than relying on one fixed synthetic case.","anti_pattern":"Use a single average request as the representative workload for a heterogeneous system.","performance_effect":"Improves the relevance of performance measurements to actual operating conditions.","verification_method":"Run benchmark scenarios across meaningful workload dimensions and compare sensitivity of the target metric.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_BENCHMARKING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"benchmarking_methodology","source":"Workload characterization and performance testing"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B62-002","batch_id":"D21-B62","domain":"performance","topic":"representative_dataset","knowledge_type":"benchmark_design","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Dataset shape can materially affect performance through cardinality, skew, locality, payload size, and distribution of values.","implementation_pattern":"Benchmark with datasets that preserve important structural characteristics of the target workload rather than only matching row or item count.","anti_pattern":"Use a uniformly distributed toy dataset when the real workload contains strong skew or locality.","performance_effect":"Improves detection of performance behavior caused by realistic data distribution.","verification_method":"Compare synthetic and representative datasets across the same implementation and measure execution, memory, and I/O characteristics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_testing_principle","source":"Dataset-shape and workload-distribution analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B62-003","batch_id":"D21-B62","domain":"performance","topic":"skew_effects","knowledge_type":"performance_failure_mode","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Highly skewed workloads can create hot keys, uneven partition utilization, cache concentration, or overloaded workers even when average workload volume appears balanced.","implementation_pattern":"Measure per-key, per-partition, or per-worker distribution rather than relying only on global averages.","anti_pattern":"Declare workload balance from aggregate request counts alone.","performance_effect":"Exposes localized bottlenecks hidden by global averages.","verification_method":"Analyze workload distribution percentiles and identify concentration around keys, partitions, resources, or workers.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"distributed_systems_performance_principle","source":"Workload skew and hotspot analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B62-004","batch_id":"D21-B62","domain":"performance","topic":"scenario_matrix","knowledge_type":"benchmark_architecture","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A performance benchmark suite should represent multiple meaningful operating scenarios instead of optimizing against one benchmark case.","implementation_pattern":"Define a scenario matrix covering normal, peak, burst, large-payload, small-payload, cache-cold, cache-warm, and degraded-dependency conditions where applicable.","anti_pattern":"Use one benchmark scenario as the sole performance gate for a complex system.","performance_effect":"Improves coverage of performance behavior across operating modes.","verification_method":"Require each declared performance requirement to map to at least one benchmark scenario and validation metric.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_BENCHMARKING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_testing_methodology","source":"Scenario-based performance validation"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B62-005","batch_id":"D21-B62","domain":"performance","topic":"benchmark_data_lifecycle","knowledge_type":"benchmark_governance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Benchmark inputs should be versioned or reproducibly generated so that performance changes can be attributed to implementation changes rather than silent workload changes.","implementation_pattern":"Assign stable workload identifiers and persist dataset generation parameters or immutable benchmark fixtures.","anti_pattern":"Replace benchmark data over time without preserving its previous form or generation procedure.","performance_effect":"Preserves comparability across performance measurements.","verification_method":"Verify that historical benchmark runs can identify the exact workload version or generation parameters used.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_BENCHMARKING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"benchmark_governance_principle","source":"Reproducible workload management"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B63-001","batch_id":"D21-B63","domain":"performance","topic":"cold_start","knowledge_type":"startup_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Cold-start performance includes initialization, loading, parsing, compilation, connection establishment, and other work absent from a warmed execution path.","implementation_pattern":"Measure cold-start latency independently and identify initialization stages using startup profiling or timestamps.","anti_pattern":"Report warmed request latency as the application's complete startup performance.","performance_effect":"Improves understanding of systems where startup or scale-out latency is operationally important.","verification_method":"Measure process or instance startup from a clean state across repeated runs and record stage-level timings.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RUNTIME_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"runtime_performance_principle","source":"Startup and initialization performance analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B63-002","batch_id":"D21-B63","domain":"performance","topic":"lazy_initialization_tradeoff","knowledge_type":"startup_optimization","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Lazy initialization can reduce startup work but transfers some cost to the first operation that requires the deferred resource.","implementation_pattern":"Defer initialization only for resources whose startup cost is significant and whose first-use latency is acceptable or can be prewarmed.","anti_pattern":"Move every initialization step to first use without considering first-request latency.","performance_effect":"Can reduce startup time while preserving acceptable first-use behavior when applied selectively.","verification_method":"Measure startup, first-use, and steady-state latency before and after deferral.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_STARTUP_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"startup_performance_principle","source":"Initialization timing and first-use analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B63-003","batch_id":"D21-B63","domain":"performance","topic":"startup_work_elimination","knowledge_type":"optimization_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"The safest startup optimization often removes unnecessary initialization work rather than merely making the same work faster.","implementation_pattern":"Classify initialization tasks as required, deferrable, redundant, or environment-dependent and eliminate work that is not required for readiness.","anti_pattern":"Optimize initialization code that should not execute at startup at all.","performance_effect":"Can reduce startup latency and startup resource consumption without changing steady-state semantics.","verification_method":"Trace startup operations and verify that removed work is not required for readiness or correctness.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_STARTUP_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"startup_optimization_principle","source":"Initialization work analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B63-004","batch_id":"D21-B63","domain":"performance","topic":"startup_parallelism","knowledge_type":"startup_optimization","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Independent initialization tasks may overlap to reduce elapsed startup time, but parallel startup can increase peak resource usage and contention.","implementation_pattern":"Parallelize only independent initialization tasks and bound concurrency according to startup resource constraints.","anti_pattern":"Run every startup task concurrently without dependency or resource analysis.","performance_effect":"Can reduce startup wall-clock time while avoiding uncontrolled startup contention when concurrency is bounded.","verification_method":"Measure critical-path startup time, peak CPU/memory/I/O, and initialization failures under controlled concurrency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_STARTUP_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"startup_concurrency_principle","source":"Initialization dependency and resource analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B63-005","batch_id":"D21-B63","domain":"performance","topic":"readiness_boundary","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Readiness should represent the minimum state required to safely serve the intended workload rather than completion of every optional initialization task.","implementation_pattern":"Define explicit readiness criteria and separate them from optional background initialization.","anti_pattern":"Delay readiness until every noncritical cache, report, index, or background component has completed initialization.","performance_effect":"Can reduce time to useful service while preserving correctness when readiness dependencies are explicitly defined.","verification_method":"Test requests immediately after readiness and verify that all required dependencies are available and optional components fail safely.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SYSTEM_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"system_readiness_principle","source":"Readiness and initialization boundary design"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B64-001","batch_id":"D21-B64","domain":"performance","topic":"network_round_trip","knowledge_type":"distributed_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Network round trips introduce latency independent of payload processing time, making excessive request-response exchanges costly even when each individual operation is fast.","implementation_pattern":"Measure round-trip count and latency separately from server processing time and consolidate compatible operations where semantics permit.","anti_pattern":"Optimize server computation while leaving unnecessary sequential network round trips unchanged.","performance_effect":"Can reduce latency dominated by network interaction overhead.","verification_method":"Compare round-trip count, network wait, server processing time, and end-to-end latency before and after request consolidation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_NETWORK_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"network_performance_principle","source":"Round-trip latency analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B64-002","batch_id":"D21-B64","domain":"performance","topic":"parallel_dependency_fetch","knowledge_type":"distributed_optimization","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Independent remote operations can often execute concurrently, reducing critical-path latency when downstream capacity and concurrency limits permit.","implementation_pattern":"Identify independent dependency calls and schedule them concurrently while preserving bounded concurrency and failure semantics.","anti_pattern":"Execute independent remote requests sequentially by default.","performance_effect":"Can reduce end-to-end latency toward the longest dependency latency instead of the sum of all independent dependency latencies.","verification_method":"Compare sequential and bounded-concurrent execution using identical dependency behavior and measure latency plus downstream resource usage.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"distributed_systems_performance_principle","source":"Critical-path reduction through bounded concurrency"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B64-003","batch_id":"D21-B64","domain":"performance","topic":"timeout_budget","knowledge_type":"latency_control","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Independent downstream timeouts can consume more time than the end-to-end latency budget when nested sequentially.","implementation_pattern":"Propagate a remaining time budget through dependency calls instead of assigning unrelated full-duration timeouts at every layer.","anti_pattern":"Give every nested dependency the same full request timeout.","performance_effect":"Prevents downstream waits from exceeding the overall latency objective.","verification_method":"Inject delayed dependencies at multiple layers and verify that total request duration remains bounded by the intended budget.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"distributed_latency_principle","source":"End-to-end timeout budget analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B64-004","batch_id":"D21-B64","domain":"performance","topic":"retry_amplification","knowledge_type":"failure_performance_interaction","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Retries can multiply downstream workload during failure or overload and therefore can increase rather than decrease system-level performance.","implementation_pattern":"Bound retries, apply appropriate backoff, propagate deadlines, and account for retry traffic in capacity calculations.","anti_pattern":"Retry every failure immediately and independently at multiple layers.","performance_effect":"Reduces retry storms and protects already-degraded dependencies.","verification_method":"Simulate dependency failures and measure original requests, retry requests, downstream load, latency, and recovery behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"distributed_systems_performance_principle","source":"Retry amplification and overload analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B64-005","batch_id":"D21-B64","domain":"performance","topic":"payload_size","knowledge_type":"network_optimization","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Reducing unnecessary network payload size can improve transfer time, bandwidth consumption, serialization cost, and downstream processing.","implementation_pattern":"Send only fields required by the consumer and avoid repeated or redundant representations across network boundaries.","anti_pattern":"Transmit full internal objects when the remote consumer requires only a small subset.","performance_effect":"Reduces transfer and processing cost, especially for bandwidth-constrained or high-latency links.","verification_method":"Measure payload bytes, serialization time, network transfer time, and end-to-end latency across representative payload sizes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_NETWORK_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"network_performance_principle","source":"Payload and transfer-cost analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B65-001","batch_id":"D21-B65","domain":"performance","topic":"lock_contention","knowledge_type":"concurrency_performance","language":"C,C++,Rust,Java,Go,Python","concept":"Lock contention can cause workers to spend significant time waiting rather than performing useful work.","implementation_pattern":"Measure lock wait and critical-section duration before changing synchronization strategy.","anti_pattern":"Replace synchronization primitives or remove locks without measuring whether contention is actually a bottleneck.","performance_effect":"Focuses optimization on measured synchronization cost while preserving correctness.","verification_method":"Profile lock wait, ownership duration, contention frequency, and useful CPU time under representative concurrency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCURRENCY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"concurrency_performance_principle","source":"Lock contention and critical-section analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B65-002","batch_id":"D21-B65","domain":"performance","topic":"critical_section_size","knowledge_type":"concurrency_optimization","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Long critical sections increase the amount of work serialized behind synchronization.","implementation_pattern":"Keep protected regions limited to the state transitions that require synchronization and move independent computation outside the critical section when correctness permits.","anti_pattern":"Perform network calls, expensive computation, logging, or unrelated processing while holding a shared lock.","performance_effect":"Can reduce lock wait and increase useful concurrency.","verification_method":"Measure critical-section duration and contention before and after moving independent work outside the protected region.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCURRENCY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"concurrency_performance_principle","source":"Critical-section and lock-contention analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B65-003","batch_id":"D21-B65","domain":"performance","topic":"lock_granularity","knowledge_type":"concurrency_tradeoff","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Finer-grained locking can increase concurrency but also increases synchronization complexity and lock-management overhead.","implementation_pattern":"Choose lock granularity based on measured contention and correctness boundaries rather than assuming finer granularity is always faster.","anti_pattern":"Split one lock into many locks without evaluating contention, deadlock risk, and management overhead.","performance_effect":"Balances concurrency gains against synchronization complexity and overhead.","verification_method":"Compare contention, throughput, latency, and correctness across controlled synchronization designs.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCURRENCY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"concurrency_performance_principle","source":"Lock granularity and contention analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B65-004","batch_id":"D21-B65","domain":"performance","topic":"false_sharing","knowledge_type":"low_level_concurrency","language":"C,C++,Rust,Java","concept":"Independent frequently modified data located on the same cache line can cause cache-coherence traffic between processors and reduce parallel performance.","implementation_pattern":"Use profiling or architecture-aware measurement before changing data layout to separate heavily contended independent fields.","anti_pattern":"Add padding or restructure data solely from theoretical suspicion without confirming the workload exhibits the relevant access pattern.","performance_effect":"Can reduce coherence-related contention in genuinely parallel hot paths.","verification_method":"Measure hardware counters or controlled parallel performance before and after layout changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_COMPUTER_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.96,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"computer_architecture_principle","source":"Cache coherence and false-sharing analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B65-005","batch_id":"D21-B65","domain":"performance","topic":"synchronization_avoidance","knowledge_type":"concurrency_optimization","language_agnostic","concept":"Reducing shared mutable state can reduce synchronization requirements and contention when the resulting design preserves required consistency semantics.","implementation_pattern":"Prefer ownership transfer, immutable data, partitioned state, message passing, or local accumulation when they fit the workload and correctness model.","anti_pattern":"Introduce shared mutable state for convenience and then compensate with increasingly complex synchronization.","performance_effect":"Can reduce contention and simplify concurrent execution paths.","verification_method":"Compare synchronization events, contention, throughput, latency, and correctness under representative concurrent workloads.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCURRENCY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"concurrency_design_principle","source":"Shared-state reduction and synchronization analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B66-001","batch_id":"D21-B66","domain":"performance","topic":"memory_bandwidth_bottleneck","knowledge_type":"hardware_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A workload can become memory-bandwidth bound when processors spend substantial time waiting for data movement rather than performing computation.","implementation_pattern":"Measure memory traffic, cache behavior, working-set size, and arithmetic work before applying CPU-oriented optimizations.","anti_pattern":"Increase computational parallelism indefinitely when memory bandwidth is already saturated.","performance_effect":"Prevents additional CPU parallelism from worsening contention without increasing useful throughput.","verification_method":"Measure throughput while varying parallelism and correlate results with memory bandwidth and cache metrics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_COMPUTER_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"computer_architecture_principle","source":"Memory-bandwidth bottleneck analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B66-002","batch_id":"D21-B66","domain":"performance","topic":"compute_bound","knowledge_type":"hardware_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A compute-bound workload is constrained primarily by arithmetic or instruction execution rather than memory or external I/O.","implementation_pattern":"Confirm processor utilization and instruction-level characteristics before applying memory or I/O optimizations.","anti_pattern":"Optimize storage or network access when measured execution is dominated by CPU computation.","performance_effect":"Improves optimization targeting by distinguishing computation from data-transfer bottlenecks.","verification_method":"Profile CPU time and correlate it with memory, I/O, and external dependency activity.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_COMPUTER_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"computer_architecture_principle","source":"Compute-bound workload analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B66-003","batch_id":"D21-B66","domain":"performance","topic":"io_bound","knowledge_type":"performance_classification","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"An I/O-bound workload spends substantial elapsed time waiting for external resources rather than consuming processor capacity.","implementation_pattern":"Measure CPU utilization alongside I/O wait, network wait, storage latency, and dependency latency before increasing compute resources.","anti_pattern":"Scale CPU to solve a bottleneck dominated by external waiting.","performance_effect":"Improves resource selection and prevents ineffective scaling.","verification_method":"Correlate end-to-end latency with CPU time and external wait components under representative workloads.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_classification_principle","source":"CPU versus I/O wait analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B66-004","batch_id":"D21-B66","domain":"performance","topic":"mixed_bottleneck","knowledge_type":"performance_diagnosis","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Real workloads can transition between CPU, memory, I/O, synchronization, and downstream bottlenecks as concurrency or workload shape changes.","implementation_pattern":"Classify bottlenecks dynamically using multiple measurements rather than assigning one permanent bottleneck label to the application.","anti_pattern":"Optimize the historically dominant bottleneck without checking whether the current workload is constrained by the same resource.","performance_effect":"Improves diagnosis across workload regimes and prevents stale optimization assumptions.","verification_method":"Repeat profiling across representative load levels and compare dominant resource signals.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_DIAGNOSTIC_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_diagnostics_principle","source":"Multi-resource bottleneck analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B66-005","batch_id":"D21-B66","domain":"performance","topic":"roofline_reasoning","knowledge_type":"performance_model","language":"C,C++,Rust,Java,Go","concept":"A roofline-style model distinguishes computational intensity from available compute and memory bandwidth and can help reason about whether optimization should target computation or data movement.","implementation_pattern":"Estimate arithmetic work and memory traffic for a measured kernel and compare its operational intensity against hardware limits.","anti_pattern":"Use a theoretical hardware peak as the expected application throughput without accounting for memory traffic or implementation efficiency.","performance_effect":"Provides a structured model for identifying likely compute or bandwidth constraints.","verification_method":"Compare model predictions with measured kernel throughput and memory bandwidth under representative inputs.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_MODEL","confidence":0.94,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"computer_architecture_model","source":"Roofline performance modeling methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B67-001","batch_id":"D21-B67","domain":"performance","topic":"algorithmic_complexity","knowledge_type":"algorithm_selection","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Asymptotic complexity provides a scalability signal, but constant factors, memory behavior, data distribution, and workload size also affect practical performance.","implementation_pattern":"Use complexity analysis to eliminate clearly unsuitable algorithms and then benchmark realistic implementations when multiple candidates remain plausible.","anti_pattern":"Select an algorithm solely from its Big-O notation without considering actual workload and implementation costs.","performance_effect":"Combines scalability reasoning with empirical validation.","verification_method":"Analyze asymptotic behavior and benchmark candidate algorithms across relevant input sizes and distributions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHM_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"algorithm_analysis_principle","source":"Asymptotic and empirical performance analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B67-002","batch_id":"D21-B67","domain":"performance","topic":"constant_factor","knowledge_type":"algorithmic_tradeoff","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"An algorithm with worse asymptotic complexity can outperform another for sufficiently small inputs when constant factors and setup costs dominate.","implementation_pattern":"Use size-aware algorithm selection when workload sizes span ranges where different algorithms have different practical crossover points.","anti_pattern":"Assume asymptotically superior algorithms always execute faster for every input size.","performance_effect":"Can reduce overhead on small workloads while preserving scalable algorithms for larger inputs.","verification_method":"Benchmark candidate implementations across input-size boundaries and determine empirically where the preferred strategy changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHM_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"algorithm_performance_principle","source":"Algorithm crossover and constant-factor analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B67-003","batch_id":"D21-B67","domain":"performance","topic":"early_exit","knowledge_type":"algorithm_optimization","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"When a computation has a valid condition that determines the result before all candidates are processed, an early exit can reduce expected work.","implementation_pattern":"Terminate iteration as soon as the specification guarantees that further processing cannot change the result.","anti_pattern":"Continue scanning the complete dataset after the required result is already determined.","performance_effect":"Can reduce average execution work without changing worst-case complexity.","verification_method":"Test boundary cases and measure workload distributions where early termination occurs at different positions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHM_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"algorithm_optimization_principle","source":"Early-termination analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B67-004","batch_id":"D21-B67","domain":"performance","topic":"incremental_computation","knowledge_type":"algorithm_optimization","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Incremental computation can avoid recomputing unchanged portions of a result when dependencies and invalidation boundaries are known.","implementation_pattern":"Track affected inputs and update only dependent results rather than rebuilding the complete computation.","anti_pattern":"Recompute the entire result after every small input change when dependency structure permits incremental updates.","performance_effect":"Can reduce repeated computational work for workloads dominated by small changes.","verification_method":"Compare full recomputation and incremental execution under controlled update patterns while verifying identical results.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHM_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"incremental_computation_principle","source":"Dependency-aware recomputation analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B67-005","batch_id":"D21-B67","domain":"performance","topic":"memoization_scope","knowledge_type":"algorithm_optimization","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Memoization can reduce repeated computation when inputs recur, but cache size, key construction, invalidation, and memory cost determine whether it is beneficial.","implementation_pattern":"Memoize expensive deterministic computations with bounded storage or an explicit lifecycle when repeated inputs are sufficiently common.","anti_pattern":"Memoize every function regardless of reuse rate or memory cost.","performance_effect":"Can trade memory for reduced computation while avoiding unnecessary caching overhead when applied selectively.","verification_method":"Measure cache hit rate, lookup cost, memory growth, computation saved, and correctness under changing inputs.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ALGORITHM_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"algorithm_optimization_principle","source":"Memoization and repeated-work analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B68-001","batch_id":"D21-B68","domain":"performance","topic":"memory_pooling","knowledge_type":"allocation_optimization","language":"C,C++,Rust,Java,Go","concept":"Pooling reusable objects or buffers can reduce repeated allocation overhead when allocation and reclamation are demonstrably significant.","implementation_pattern":"Reuse bounded resources with explicit ownership and lifecycle rules and verify that pool management does not introduce contention or retention.","anti_pattern":"Introduce a global object pool without measuring allocation cost or controlling retained memory.","performance_effect":"May reduce allocation frequency and allocator overhead for high-churn workloads.","verification_method":"Compare allocation count, runtime, memory retention, contention, and throughput with and without pooling.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_MEMORY_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.96,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"memory_performance_principle","source":"Allocation and object-reuse analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B68-002","batch_id":"D21-B68","domain":"performance","topic":"buffer_reuse","knowledge_type":"memory_optimization","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Reusable buffers can reduce allocation and initialization costs in repeated data-processing loops when ownership and concurrency are controlled.","implementation_pattern":"Allocate reusable buffers at an appropriate scope and reset or overwrite only the required region between operations.","anti_pattern":"Reuse buffers across concurrent operations without enforcing exclusive ownership.","performance_effect":"Can reduce allocation churn and temporary memory pressure.","verification_method":"Compare allocation rate, memory usage, throughput, and correctness under sequential and concurrent workloads.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_MEMORY_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"memory_performance_principle","source":"Buffer reuse and allocation analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B68-003","batch_id":"D21-B68","domain":"performance","topic":"memory_retention","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Reducing allocation rate does not necessarily reduce memory usage if objects remain reachable longer than required.","implementation_pattern":"Measure live-set size and object lifetime in addition to allocation rate when diagnosing memory growth.","anti_pattern":"Assume high allocation rate is the only cause of high memory usage.","performance_effect":"Distinguishes allocation churn from retention and leak-like behavior.","verification_method":"Track live memory over time and identify retained references or collections responsible for sustained growth.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_MEMORY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"memory_diagnostics_principle","source":"Allocation, lifetime, and retention analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B68-004","batch_id":"D21-B68","domain":"performance","topic":"object_overhead","knowledge_type":"memory_optimization","language":"Python,JavaScript,TypeScript,Java,C++,Rust,Go","concept":"Per-object metadata and indirection can become significant when a workload contains very large numbers of small objects.","implementation_pattern":"Measure object count, object size, pointer/reference overhead, and access patterns before considering compact representations.","anti_pattern":"Optimize only payload size while ignoring metadata and container overhead.","performance_effect":"Can reduce memory footprint and improve locality for object-dense workloads.","verification_method":"Measure total memory by object category and compare equivalent compact and object-oriented representations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_MEMORY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.96,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"runtime_memory_principle","source":"Object representation and memory-overhead analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B68-005","batch_id":"D21-B68","domain":"performance","topic":"memory_budget_enforcement","knowledge_type":"resource_governance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Memory-sensitive systems should enforce or monitor explicit memory budgets instead of relying on eventual failure after unrestricted growth.","implementation_pattern":"Define memory budgets for major caches, queues, buffers, request state, and temporary computation and attach controlled degradation behavior to budget exhaustion.","anti_pattern":"Allow internal collections to grow until the runtime or operating system terminates the process.","performance_effect":"Improves predictability and prevents one workload component from consuming all available memory.","verification_method":"Inject memory-heavy workloads and verify bounded growth, explicit degradation, and recovery behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESOURCE_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"resource_governance_principle","source":"Memory budget and bounded-resource design"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B69-001","batch_id":"D21-B69","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_canary","knowledge_type":"regression_detection","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A representative performance canary can detect significant regressions before a broad workload or production deployment.","implementation_pattern":"Run a stable, bounded workload against a controlled environment and compare predefined latency, throughput, memory, and CPU metrics against a versioned baseline.","anti_pattern":"Use an unstable workload or changing environment as a strict regression gate.","performance_effect":"Provides an early warning mechanism for major performance degradation.","verification_method":"Repeat canary measurements and require reproducible evidence before classifying a change as a regression.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_REGRESSION_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_regression_methodology","source":"Controlled performance canary testing"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B69-002","batch_id":"D21-B69","domain":"performance","topic":"baseline_drift","knowledge_type":"regression_management","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A performance baseline can drift as legitimate implementation, dependency, workload, or environment changes accumulate.","implementation_pattern":"Version baselines and document intentional changes rather than silently replacing historical measurements.","anti_pattern":"Continuously overwrite the baseline with the latest result and lose the ability to detect long-term degradation.","performance_effect":"Preserves historical performance context and makes gradual regressions easier to identify.","verification_method":"Compare current measurements against both the immediate baseline and selected historical baselines.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_regression_methodology","source":"Baseline lifecycle and historical comparison"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B69-003","batch_id":"D21-B69","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_error_budget","knowledge_type":"reliability_performance_interaction","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance requirements can be represented as bounded error budgets describing how much latency, throughput, or resource degradation is acceptable before corrective action is required.","implementation_pattern":"Define measurable performance objectives and track deviations against an explicit tolerance budget.","anti_pattern":"Treat every tiny performance fluctuation as a regression requiring immediate optimization.","performance_effect":"Balances optimization effort against measurement noise and engineering cost.","verification_method":"Compare repeated measurements against documented tolerance ranges and classify changes as acceptable variation or actionable regression.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_governance_principle","source":"Performance objective and tolerance management"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B69-004","batch_id":"D21-B69","domain":"performance","topic":"dependency_regression","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"An application performance regression can originate from a changed runtime, library, database, network dependency, compiler, or external service rather than application source code.","implementation_pattern":"Correlate performance changes with dependency and environment versions before modifying application logic.","anti_pattern":"Immediately rewrite application code when an end-to-end metric regresses.","performance_effect":"Reduces unnecessary application changes and improves root-cause attribution.","verification_method":"Compare controlled versions or isolate dependencies while holding application workload constant.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SYSTEM_DIAGNOSTIC_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"system_performance_diagnostics","source":"Dependency-aware regression analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B69-005","batch_id":"D21-B69","domain":"performance","topic":"regression_localization","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance regression investigation should progressively narrow the affected layer from end-to-end behavior to subsystem, operation, and code-path evidence.","implementation_pattern":"Use hierarchical measurements such as request latency, dependency timing, subsystem metrics, profiling, and targeted microbenchmarks.","anti_pattern":"Jump directly from an end-to-end regression to low-level code optimization without localization evidence.","performance_effect":"Reduces wasted optimization work and improves root-cause identification.","verification_method":"Trace the regression through progressively finer measurement layers until a reproducible bottleneck is identified.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DIAGNOSTIC_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_diagnostics_methodology","source":"Hierarchical performance localization"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B70-001","batch_id":"D21-B70","domain":"performance","topic":"optimization_dependency_chain","knowledge_type":"reasoning_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Some optimizations are only useful when prerequisite bottlenecks or resource constraints have first been addressed.","implementation_pattern":"Represent optimization prerequisites explicitly and evaluate them in dependency order.","anti_pattern":"Apply a downstream optimization whose limiting upstream resource remains saturated.","performance_effect":"Improves optimization sequencing and prevents ineffective changes.","verification_method":"Validate prerequisite conditions before applying dependent optimization records.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_REASONING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_knowledge_reasoning","source":"Optimization dependency modeling"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B70-002","batch_id":"D21-B70","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_hypothesis_testing","knowledge_type":"verification_method","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A performance hypothesis should state the expected bottleneck, predicted effect, affected metric, and conditions under which the prediction should hold.","implementation_pattern":"Record hypothesis, baseline, intervention, expected direction, measured result, and interpretation as separate fields.","anti_pattern":"Begin optimization without recording what measurable behavior the change is expected to affect.","performance_effect":"Improves experimental clarity and makes unsuccessful optimizations useful as knowledge rather than discarded effort.","verification_method":"Compare the predicted and observed effects and classify the hypothesis as supported, unsupported, or conditionally supported.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_REASONING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_experimentation_principle","source":"Hypothesis-driven optimization"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B70-003","batch_id":"D21-B70","domain":"performance","topic":"negative_performance_result","knowledge_type":"learning_record","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A measured optimization that fails to improve the target workload is valuable evidence when its workload, environment, and measurement procedure are preserved.","implementation_pattern":"Store unsuccessful optimization attempts with conditions, expected effect, observed effect, and reason for rejection when known.","anti_pattern":"Delete failed experiments and retain only successful optimizations.","performance_effect":"Reduces repeated experimentation and teaches the engineering system where an optimization is not applicable.","verification_method":"Require failed optimization records to contain sufficient evidence to distinguish true ineffectiveness from invalid measurement.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_LEARNING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_learning_principle","source":"Negative-result preservation and experiment provenance"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B70-004","batch_id":"D21-B70","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_optimization_confidence","knowledge_type":"evidence_scoring","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Confidence in a performance recommendation should depend on evidence quality, workload representativeness, measurement repeatability, version stability, and independent confirmation.","implementation_pattern":"Compute or classify confidence from explicit evidence dimensions rather than assigning confidence from the apparent plausibility of the optimization.","anti_pattern":"Assign high confidence to a performance claim supported only by one unverified benchmark.","performance_effect":"Improves downstream decision quality and reduces overconfident optimization recommendations.","verification_method":"Audit confidence metadata against available measurements, provenance, workload scope, version range, and replication status.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"evidence_management_principle","source":"Performance evidence and confidence modeling"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B70-005","batch_id":"D21-B70","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_dataset_lock","knowledge_type":"dataset_governance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A performance dataset should be locked only after records have been deduplicated, contradiction relationships preserved, evidence states validated, schema consistency checked, and important performance domains covered.","implementation_pattern":"Run structural validation, duplicate detection, contradiction analysis, provenance validation, version checks, and coverage audits before assigning a locked dataset state.","anti_pattern":"Lock a performance dataset because the planned record count or batch count has been reached.","performance_effect":"Protects the knowledge fabric from incomplete, contradictory, duplicated, or unsupported performance knowledge.","verification_method":"Require zero unresolved structural errors and document remaining evidence gaps or explicitly out-of-scope areas before lock.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"dataset_quality_principle","source":"Evidence-aware performance dataset governance"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B71-001","batch_id":"D21-B71","domain":"performance","topic":"load_testing","knowledge_type":"verification_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Load testing evaluates system behavior under defined workload levels and should distinguish steady load, ramp load, burst load, and sustained saturation.","implementation_pattern":"Define workload intensity, duration, request mix, concurrency, dataset state, success criteria, and monitored resources before executing a load test.","anti_pattern":"Generate arbitrary high traffic and interpret the resulting failure point as the system's general capacity.","performance_effect":"Produces more useful capacity and scalability evidence by tying results to explicit workload conditions.","verification_method":"Execute controlled load profiles and record throughput, latency percentiles, errors, resource utilization, queue depth, and recovery behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_testing_methodology","source":"Controlled load-testing methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B71-002","batch_id":"D21-B71","domain":"performance","topic":"stress_testing","knowledge_type":"verification_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Stress testing intentionally pushes a system beyond normal operating conditions to characterize failure behavior, resource exhaustion, and recovery.","implementation_pattern":"Increase workload progressively beyond the expected operating envelope while recording which resource or invariant fails first.","anti_pattern":"Treat the maximum observed throughput during uncontrolled overload as a stable production capacity.","performance_effect":"Reveals saturation boundaries and failure modes that normal-load tests may not expose.","verification_method":"Run controlled overload scenarios and verify bounded failure behavior, resource recovery, and absence of unrecoverable resource leakage.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_testing_methodology","source":"Stress and overload testing"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B71-003","batch_id":"D21-B71","domain":"performance","topic":"soak_testing","knowledge_type":"verification_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Soak tests evaluate whether performance and resource behavior remain stable over extended execution rather than only during short benchmark windows.","implementation_pattern":"Run representative workloads for sufficient duration to expose memory growth, resource leakage, cache instability, connection accumulation, or gradual latency drift.","anti_pattern":"Assume a short successful benchmark proves long-running performance stability.","performance_effect":"Improves detection of time-dependent performance degradation.","verification_method":"Track memory, file descriptors, connections, queue depth, latency distributions, throughput, and resource utilization throughout the test.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_testing_methodology","source":"Long-duration soak testing"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B71-004","batch_id":"D21-B71","domain":"performance","topic":"ramp_testing","knowledge_type":"benchmark_design","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Gradually increasing workload can reveal nonlinear transitions in latency, queueing, resource utilization, and error rates.","implementation_pattern":"Increase workload in controlled stages while collecting identical metrics at each stage.","anti_pattern":"Jump directly from idle to extreme load and lose visibility into the transition point.","performance_effect":"Improves identification of saturation thresholds and capacity inflection points.","verification_method":"Plot workload intensity against throughput, latency percentiles, queue depth, and resource utilization and identify regime changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LOAD_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_testing_methodology","source":"Controlled ramp-load testing"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B71-005","batch_id":"D21-B71","domain":"performance","topic":"recovery_testing","knowledge_type":"resilience_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance validation should include recovery after overload or dependency degradation because a system that performs well before overload may recover slowly or remain degraded afterward.","implementation_pattern":"Measure recovery time, residual queues, resource reclamation, latency normalization, and throughput restoration after load or failure conditions end.","anti_pattern":"Stop a performance test immediately when the overload stimulus ends and ignore recovery behavior.","performance_effect":"Exposes persistent resource exhaustion and delayed recovery effects.","verification_method":"Apply controlled overload, remove the stimulus, and verify that resource usage and service metrics return toward the expected baseline.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESILIENCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"resilience_performance_methodology","source":"Performance recovery testing"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B72-001","batch_id":"D21-B72","domain":"performance","topic":"concurrency_sweep","knowledge_type":"benchmark_method","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A concurrency sweep identifies the relationship between concurrent work, throughput, latency, resource utilization, and saturation.","implementation_pattern":"Execute the same workload at multiple controlled concurrency levels and preserve all other major variables.","anti_pattern":"Benchmark only one concurrency level and infer scalability from that single result.","performance_effect":"Reveals the point where additional concurrency stops increasing useful throughput or starts increasing latency disproportionately.","verification_method":"Record throughput, p50, p95, p99, CPU, memory, queueing, and downstream resource utilization for each concurrency level.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_BENCHMARKING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_benchmarking_methodology","source":"Concurrency scaling analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B72-002","batch_id":"D21-B72","domain":"performance","topic":"scalability_curve","knowledge_type":"performance_model","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Scalability should be evaluated from the shape of performance as resources or concurrency increase rather than from one absolute throughput measurement.","implementation_pattern":"Construct scaling curves for throughput, latency, and resource efficiency across increasing resource levels.","anti_pattern":"Call a system scalable because throughput increased once after adding resources.","performance_effect":"Distinguishes useful scaling from diminishing returns and saturation.","verification_method":"Measure performance across multiple resource configurations and calculate incremental throughput or latency benefit.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SCALABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"scalability_analysis","source":"Resource-scaling curve methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B72-003","batch_id":"D21-B72","domain":"performance","topic":"linear_scalability","knowledge_type":"performance_model","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Linear scalability requires useful capacity to increase approximately proportionally with the scaling resource under the specified workload and constraints.","implementation_pattern":"Compare normalized throughput or useful work per resource unit across controlled scale levels.","anti_pattern":"Assume doubling workers must double throughput regardless of synchronization, memory, I/O, or dependency bottlenecks.","performance_effect":"Provides a quantitative basis for identifying scaling efficiency.","verification_method":"Measure throughput at multiple resource levels and compare actual growth against the resource increase.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SCALABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"scalability_methodology","source":"Scaling efficiency analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B72-004","batch_id":"D21-B72","domain":"performance","topic":"horizontal_scaling","knowledge_type":"distributed_scalability","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Horizontal scaling can increase capacity when work can be distributed with acceptable coordination, state, and dependency overhead.","implementation_pattern":"Measure per-instance useful throughput together with coordination traffic, shared-resource contention, load-distribution quality, and downstream capacity.","anti_pattern":"Assume adding instances automatically increases system capacity without checking shared bottlenecks.","performance_effect":"Identifies whether additional instances provide useful capacity or simply move the bottleneck elsewhere.","verification_method":"Scale instance count while maintaining representative workload and measure total throughput, per-instance utilization, coordination overhead, and downstream saturation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SCALABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"distributed_performance_principle","source":"Horizontal scaling and shared-bottleneck analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B72-005","batch_id":"D21-B72","domain":"performance","topic":"scaling_efficiency","knowledge_type":"performance_metric","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Scaling efficiency compares achieved useful performance improvement with the theoretical improvement expected from the added resources.","implementation_pattern":"Track normalized throughput or useful work per unit of compute, worker, instance, or other scaling resource.","anti_pattern":"Report total throughput growth without accounting for the amount of additional resource consumed.","performance_effect":"Makes inefficient scaling visible and supports capacity decisions based on resource efficiency.","verification_method":"Calculate performance per resource unit across scale levels and investigate significant efficiency declines.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SCALABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"scalability_analysis","source":"Scaling efficiency measurement"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B73-001","batch_id":"D21-B73","domain":"performance","topic":"profiling_hotspot_validation","knowledge_type":"profiling_method","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A profiler identifies candidate hotspots but optimization should be accepted only after a controlled change demonstrates improvement in the target metric.","implementation_pattern":"Profile, select a candidate hotspot, change one relevant factor, rerun the same workload, and compare target metrics.","anti_pattern":"Treat profiler percentage alone as proof that optimizing the corresponding function will improve end-to-end performance.","performance_effect":"Reduces false optimization caused by confusing sampled execution presence with causal impact.","verification_method":"Compare before-and-after target metrics under the same workload and confirm that the suspected hotspot changed as expected.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PROFILING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"profiling_methodology","source":"Hotspot identification and controlled validation"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B73-002","batch_id":"D21-B73","domain":"performance","topic":"instrumentation_placement","knowledge_type":"profiling_method","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Instrumentation should be placed at boundaries that answer a specific performance question while avoiding excessive measurement noise or overhead.","implementation_pattern":"Instrument request stages, critical operations, resource waits, and selected hot paths based on the hypothesis being tested.","anti_pattern":"Instrument every function uniformly without a diagnostic objective.","performance_effect":"Improves signal-to-noise ratio and limits measurement overhead.","verification_method":"Compare observed overhead and diagnostic usefulness across progressively more targeted instrumentation configurations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PROFILING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_observability_principle","source":"Targeted instrumentation methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B73-003","batch_id":"D21-B73","domain":"performance","topic":"distributed_trace_sampling","knowledge_type":"observability_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Tracing every request may impose storage, processing, and transmission costs, so sampling strategy should reflect diagnostic requirements.","implementation_pattern":"Use adaptive or policy-driven trace sampling that preserves high-value requests such as errors, slow operations, and unusual execution paths.","anti_pattern":"Collect complete traces indefinitely without accounting for instrumentation and storage overhead.","performance_effect":"Preserves useful distributed-performance evidence while controlling observability cost.","verification_method":"Measure trace volume, instrumentation overhead, storage consumption, and diagnostic coverage under representative load.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OBSERVABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"observability_performance_principle","source":"Trace sampling and diagnostic-cost analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B73-004","batch_id":"D21-B73","domain":"performance","topic":"cardinality_control","knowledge_type":"observability_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"High-cardinality telemetry dimensions can increase memory, processing, storage, and query costs without proportional diagnostic value.","implementation_pattern":"Use bounded dimensions and separate high-detail diagnostic data from low-cardinality aggregate metrics.","anti_pattern":"Attach arbitrary request identifiers, URLs, payload values, or unbounded user-generated values to every aggregate metric label.","performance_effect":"Controls telemetry overhead and protects monitoring systems from excessive series growth.","verification_method":"Measure active series count, telemetry volume, ingestion cost, query latency, and diagnostic coverage after dimension changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OBSERVABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"observability_performance_principle","source":"Telemetry cardinality management"}}

