{"record_id":"D21-B80-005","batch_id":"D21-B80","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_phase_completion_criteria","knowledge_type":"dataset_governance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance knowledge is complete only when required performance domains, implementation patterns, failure modes, measurement methods, verification procedures, version constraints, tradeoffs, and evidence states have been covered sufficiently for downstream engineering use.","implementation_pattern":"Evaluate coverage by knowledge dimensions rather than record count and require unresolved gaps to be explicitly identified before phase lock.","anti_pattern":"Declare the performance phase complete because a predetermined number of records has been generated.","performance_effect":"Prevents quantity-driven dataset inflation and preserves practical knowledge completeness.","verification_method":"Run coverage, schema, duplicate, contradiction, provenance, version, and evidence-state audits before locking the phase.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"dataset_quality_principle","source":"Coverage-driven performance dataset completion"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B81-001","batch_id":"D21-B81","domain":"performance","topic":"tail_latency","knowledge_type":"latency_engineering","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Tail latency represents the slower portion of a latency distribution and can dominate user-visible reliability when requests depend on multiple sequential or parallel operations.","implementation_pattern":"Track percentile distributions such as p50, p95, p99, and higher percentiles where operationally justified instead of relying on averages alone.","anti_pattern":"Use average latency as the sole acceptance criterion for latency-sensitive systems.","performance_effect":"Improves detection of sporadic slow paths, queueing, contention, and dependency-induced latency spikes.","verification_method":"Run representative workloads and compare latency distributions before and after changes; investigate percentile shifts rather than only mean values.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_LATENCY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"latency_engineering_methodology","source":"Percentile and tail-latency analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B81-002","batch_id":"D21-B81","domain":"performance","topic":"percentile_instability","knowledge_type":"measurement_principle","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"High-percentile measurements can be statistically unstable when the sample population is too small to represent the tail reliably.","implementation_pattern":"Use sufficiently large and representative samples and report sample size alongside percentile measurements.","anti_pattern":"Treat a p99 value calculated from a very small sample as precise evidence of production tail behavior.","performance_effect":"Reduces false conclusions caused by insufficient tail samples.","verification_method":"Repeat measurements with increasing sample sizes and verify that relevant percentiles converge within an acceptable range.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_STATISTICAL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_statistics","source":"Latency percentile sampling methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B81-003","batch_id":"D21-B81","domain":"performance","topic":"coordinated_omission","knowledge_type":"benchmark_failure_mode","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A workload generator can under-report latency when it stops issuing new work while the system is overloaded, thereby failing to represent the waiting time experienced by an open workload.","implementation_pattern":"Choose workload generation semantics that match the real system, including open-loop or closed-loop behavior as appropriate, and account for delayed work explicitly.","anti_pattern":"Assume a closed-loop benchmark always measures the latency distribution experienced by an independently arriving workload.","performance_effect":"Improves accuracy of overload and tail-latency measurements.","verification_method":"Compare workload-generation models and inspect whether delayed requests continue to contribute to the measured latency distribution.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_BENCHMARKING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"benchmarking_methodology","source":"Open-loop versus closed-loop workload analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B81-004","batch_id":"D21-B81","domain":"performance","topic":"queueing_delay","knowledge_type":"latency_model","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Observed request latency can include time spent waiting for CPU, worker capacity, locks, connections, storage, network, or downstream resources before useful processing begins.","implementation_pattern":"Separate service time from queueing and waiting time when diagnosing latency growth.","anti_pattern":"Optimize computation when most observed latency is actually waiting for a constrained resource.","performance_effect":"Improves bottleneck localization and prevents optimization of non-dominant work.","verification_method":"Instrument queue and wait durations and compare them with actual processing time under increasing load.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_QUEUEING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"queueing_performance_methodology","source":"Queueing and service-time decomposition"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B81-005","batch_id":"D21-B81","domain":"performance","topic":"latency_budget","knowledge_type":"architecture_constraint","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"An end-to-end latency target can be decomposed into budgets for application processing, dependencies, serialization, transport, queueing, and other required stages.","implementation_pattern":"Allocate explicit latency budgets to major stages and verify that their combined behavior remains within the end-to-end objective.","anti_pattern":"Assign the entire latency target to application code while ignoring dependency and transport costs.","performance_effect":"Creates actionable ownership boundaries for latency optimization.","verification_method":"Measure stage-level latency and verify that the aggregate remains consistent with the end-to-end budget.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"latency_budgeting_methodology","source":"End-to-end latency decomposition"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B82-001","batch_id":"D21-B82","domain":"performance","topic":"throughput_saturation","knowledge_type":"capacity_model","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Throughput often increases with offered load only until a bottleneck becomes saturated; additional demand can then increase waiting and latency without increasing useful throughput.","implementation_pattern":"Measure offered load, completed work, queue depth, latency, and resource utilization together to identify saturation.","anti_pattern":"Interpret increasing resource utilization and latency under overload as evidence that the system is still scaling normally.","performance_effect":"Improves capacity-boundary identification and overload diagnosis.","verification_method":"Sweep offered load beyond expected operating levels and identify the point where useful throughput flattens while queueing or latency rises.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_QUEUEING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"capacity_analysis","source":"Throughput saturation and queueing behavior"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B82-002","batch_id":"D21-B82","domain":"performance","topic":"Little_law","knowledge_type":"queueing_model","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"For a stable system under applicable assumptions, average number of items in the system relates to throughput and average time in the system through L = λW.","implementation_pattern":"Use measured throughput and latency to sanity-check queue or in-flight-work measurements when the workload satisfies the model assumptions.","anti_pattern":"Apply the relationship blindly to unstable, time-varying, or semantically incompatible measurements.","performance_effect":"Provides a useful consistency check between concurrency, throughput, and latency measurements.","verification_method":"Compare measured in-flight work with the product of throughput and average system time and investigate substantial discrepancies.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"FORMAL_QUEUEING_RELATIONSHIP","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"queueing_theory","source":"Little's Law"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B82-003","batch_id":"D21-B82","domain":"performance","topic":"backpressure","knowledge_type":"flow_control","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Backpressure limits upstream work when downstream capacity is constrained, preventing unbounded queues and uncontrolled resource growth.","implementation_pattern":"Bound queues, propagate capacity signals, reject or defer excess work according to policy, and expose overload state to upstream components.","anti_pattern":"Allow unbounded producer speed to determine queue growth while assuming downstream capacity will eventually catch up.","performance_effect":"Controls memory growth, queueing latency, and cascading overload.","verification_method":"Apply sustained overload and verify bounded queue growth, explicit overload behavior, and eventual recovery after demand falls.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"flow_control_methodology","source":"Backpressure and overload control"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B82-004","batch_id":"D21-B82","domain":"performance","topic":"admission_control","knowledge_type":"capacity_protection","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Admission control protects finite resources by deciding whether new work can be accepted under current capacity and policy constraints.","implementation_pattern":"Define explicit admission criteria using resource availability, queue state, priority, deadlines, and workload class.","anti_pattern":"Accept unlimited work and rely solely on downstream failures to regulate demand.","performance_effect":"Prevents uncontrolled overload and protects latency for accepted work.","verification_method":"Test resource exhaustion and verify that admission decisions remain bounded, observable, and consistent with configured policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CAPACITY_PROTECTION_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"capacity_management","source":"Admission control methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B82-005","batch_id":"D21-B82","domain":"performance","topic":"load_shedding","knowledge_type":"overload_control","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Load shedding intentionally declines or reduces lower-priority work when capacity is insufficient to preserve critical service behavior.","implementation_pattern":"Define workload priorities and deterministic shedding policies before overload occurs.","anti_pattern":"Allow resource exhaustion to choose which requests fail implicitly.","performance_effect":"Can preserve critical-path availability and bound overload propagation.","verification_method":"Apply controlled overload and verify that lower-priority work is shed according to policy while protected work remains within its target envelope.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OVERLOAD_CONTROL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"overload_management","source":"Load-shedding methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B83-001","batch_id":"D21-B83","domain":"performance","topic":"cache_hit_ratio","knowledge_type":"cache_analysis","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Cache hit ratio alone does not determine cache effectiveness because hit cost, miss cost, item value, object size, invalidation behavior, and request distribution also affect end-to-end performance.","implementation_pattern":"Evaluate cache effectiveness using hit ratio together with hit latency, miss latency, downstream load, memory usage, and workload distribution.","anti_pattern":"Increase cache size solely to maximize hit ratio without measuring end-to-end benefit.","performance_effect":"Prevents memory consumption from being optimized toward a metric that may not represent actual performance value.","verification_method":"Compare end-to-end latency, downstream work, memory usage, and cache statistics across controlled cache configurations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CACHE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"cache_performance_methodology","source":"Cache effectiveness analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B83-002","batch_id":"D21-B83","domain":"performance","topic":"cache_stampede","knowledge_type":"cache_failure_mode","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"When many requests simultaneously miss or invalidate the same cached item, they can duplicate expensive downstream work and create a burst of load.","implementation_pattern":"Use request coalescing, controlled refresh, jittered expiration, or equivalent coordination when appropriate to the workload and correctness model.","anti_pattern":"Allow every concurrent miss to independently regenerate the same expensive value.","performance_effect":"Reduces synchronized downstream load spikes and duplicate computation.","verification_method":"Expire or invalidate a hot item under concurrent demand and measure regeneration concurrency, downstream load, and latency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CACHE_FAILURE_MODE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"cache_systems_principle","source":"Cache stampede and request-coalescing analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B83-003","batch_id":"D21-B83","domain":"performance","topic":"negative_caching","knowledge_type":"cache_strategy","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Caching certain negative lookup results can prevent repeated expensive misses, but the cache lifetime must respect correctness and data-change semantics.","implementation_pattern":"Cache safely cacheable absence results with explicit expiration and invalidation rules.","anti_pattern":"Cache all negative results indefinitely and assume absence remains permanently true.","performance_effect":"Can reduce repeated downstream work for stable negative lookups.","verification_method":"Measure downstream request reduction and verify that newly available data becomes visible within the required freshness bound.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CACHE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"cache_strategy_methodology","source":"Negative-result caching"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B83-004","batch_id":"D21-B83","domain":"performance","topic":"cache_memory_fragmentation","knowledge_type":"cache_memory","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A cache can consume substantially more memory than the nominal payload size because of metadata, object representation, allocator overhead, fragmentation, and duplicated data.","implementation_pattern":"Measure resident memory and per-entry overhead rather than estimating cache cost from payload bytes alone.","anti_pattern":"Allocate cache capacity solely from the sum of stored value sizes.","performance_effect":"Improves cache capacity planning and reduces memory-pressure surprises.","verification_method":"Measure process memory before and after controlled cache population and estimate effective bytes per entry.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_MEMORY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"cache_memory_methodology","source":"Cache memory overhead analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B83-005","batch_id":"D21-B83","domain":"performance","topic":"cache_warming","knowledge_type":"startup_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Cache warming can reduce early request misses but can also increase startup work and compete for resources with live traffic.","implementation_pattern":"Warm only high-value data supported by measured access patterns and explicitly budget warming work.","anti_pattern":"Precompute or load the entire cache during startup without evidence that the data is useful.","performance_effect":"Balances warm-start latency against startup resource consumption.","verification_method":"Compare cold-start and warmed-start behavior while measuring startup duration, cache hit behavior, and resource contention.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CACHE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"cache_performance_methodology","source":"Cache warming tradeoff analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B84-001","batch_id":"D21-B84","domain":"performance","topic":"serialization_cost","knowledge_type":"data_path_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Serialization can consume CPU, allocate temporary memory, copy data, and increase payload size; its cost should be separated from transport and business processing.","implementation_pattern":"Measure serialization and deserialization independently for representative payload sizes and structures.","anti_pattern":"Attribute end-to-end transfer latency entirely to the network when serialization dominates CPU or allocation cost.","performance_effect":"Improves localization of data-transfer bottlenecks.","verification_method":"Measure encode/decode CPU time, allocation behavior, payload size, and end-to-end latency across representative payloads.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_PATH_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"serialization_performance_methodology","source":"Serialization cost decomposition"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B84-002","batch_id":"D21-B84","domain":"performance","topic":"serialization_format_selection","knowledge_type":"architecture_tradeoff","language":"Python,JavaScript,TypeScript,Java,C,C++,Rust,Go","concept":"Serialization formats trade off size, encoding speed, decoding speed, schema flexibility, compatibility, and implementation complexity.","implementation_pattern":"Select a format using measured workload characteristics and compatibility requirements rather than assuming one format is universally fastest.","anti_pattern":"Choose a serialization format from a generic benchmark without matching payload shape, language, transport, and compatibility requirements.","performance_effect":"Improves workload-specific data-transfer efficiency.","verification_method":"Benchmark representative payloads using equivalent semantics and measure encode/decode cost, size, and compatibility behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SERIALIZATION_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"serialization_benchmarking_methodology","source":"Format tradeoff analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B84-003","batch_id":"D21-B84","domain":"performance","topic":"copy_elision_and_zero_copy","knowledge_type":"data_movement_optimization","language":"C,C++,Rust,Java,Go,Python","concept":"Avoiding unnecessary data copies can reduce CPU and memory-bandwidth consumption, but zero-copy techniques introduce ownership, lifetime, alignment, and API constraints.","implementation_pattern":"Identify measured copy hotspots and preserve buffer ownership and lifetime invariants when reducing copies.","anti_pattern":"Introduce complex zero-copy mechanisms where copying is not a measured bottleneck.","performance_effect":"Can reduce memory bandwidth and CPU cost in high-volume data paths.","verification_method":"Measure copy counts or memory traffic where possible and compare end-to-end performance and correctness.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_MOVEMENT_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.96,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"systems_performance_principle","source":"Data-copy and memory-bandwidth analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B84-004","batch_id":"D21-B84","domain":"performance","topic":"payload_size_reduction","knowledge_type":"network_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Reducing unnecessary payload fields can lower serialization, network transfer, parsing, memory, and downstream processing costs.","implementation_pattern":"Define purpose-specific response projections and avoid transferring data that the consumer does not require.","anti_pattern":"Return complete domain objects for every API operation regardless of consumer requirements.","performance_effect":"Reduces multiple forms of data-path work and can improve latency and throughput.","verification_method":"Compare payload size, encode/decode cost, network transfer, memory usage, and end-to-end latency before and after projection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATA_PATH_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"API_performance_methodology","source":"Payload minimization"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B84-005","batch_id":"D21-B84","domain":"performance","topic":"compression_tradeoff","knowledge_type":"network_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Compression trades CPU cost for reduced data volume and can improve end-to-end performance when transfer cost dominates but degrade it when CPU is already constrained.","implementation_pattern":"Evaluate compression using representative payloads, compression levels, CPU availability, bandwidth, and latency objectives.","anti_pattern":"Enable maximum compression globally because smaller payloads are assumed to be faster.","performance_effect":"Makes compression decisions workload-dependent rather than based on payload size alone.","verification_method":"Measure compression CPU time, compressed size, transfer time, decompression cost, and total latency across representative payloads.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_NETWORK_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"network_performance_methodology","source":"Compression cost-benefit analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B85-001","batch_id":"D21-B85","domain":"performance","topic":"connection_pooling","knowledge_type":"resource_reuse","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Connection establishment can be substantially more expensive than reusing an existing connection, making pooling useful when the underlying protocol and workload support reuse.","implementation_pattern":"Use bounded connection pools with lifecycle, timeout, health, and capacity policies appropriate to the dependency.","anti_pattern":"Create a new connection for every operation when persistent reuse is supported and safe.","performance_effect":"Can reduce connection setup overhead and improve latency under repeated access.","verification_method":"Compare pooled and non-pooled behavior while measuring connection creation, request latency, throughput, and resource usage.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESOURCE_REUSE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"connection_management_methodology","source":"Connection reuse and pooling"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B85-002","batch_id":"D21-B85","domain":"performance","topic":"pool_sizing","knowledge_type":"capacity_tuning","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"An oversized resource pool can increase contention and downstream load, while an undersized pool can create unnecessary queueing.","implementation_pattern":"Tune pool size from measured concurrency, dependency capacity, service time, and target latency rather than from CPU count alone.","anti_pattern":"Set every connection or worker pool to a very large value to avoid waiting.","performance_effect":"Balances resource utilization against queueing and dependency saturation.","verification_method":"Sweep pool sizes under representative load and observe throughput, latency, queueing, downstream utilization, and errors.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_POOLING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"resource_pool_performance_methodology","source":"Pool capacity tuning"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B85-003","batch_id":"D21-B85","domain":"performance","topic":"connection_leak","knowledge_type":"performance_failure_mode","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Failure to release pooled resources can progressively reduce available capacity and eventually produce queueing, timeouts, and cascading latency.","implementation_pattern":"Use deterministic resource lifecycle management and monitor active, idle, leased, and exhausted pool states.","anti_pattern":"Rely on eventual garbage collection or process termination to release resources.","performance_effect":"Prevents gradual capacity loss caused by resource retention.","verification_method":"Run repeated success and failure paths and verify that pool occupancy returns to the expected baseline after operations complete.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESOURCE_LIFECYCLE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"resource_pool_methodology","source":"Resource leak and pool exhaustion analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B85-004","batch_id":"D21-B85","domain":"performance","topic":"pool_timeout","knowledge_type":"overload_behavior","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A bounded resource pool should define what happens when no resource is immediately available rather than allowing indefinite waiting.","implementation_pattern":"Use explicit acquisition timeouts or cancellation policies appropriate to workload priority and service-level requirements.","anti_pattern":"Allow requests to wait indefinitely for a scarce pooled resource.","performance_effect":"Bounds queueing and prevents hidden resource waits from consuming unbounded request lifetime.","verification_method":"Exhaust the pool under controlled load and verify acquisition behavior, timeout latency, resource release, and recovery.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESOURCE_CONTROL_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"resource_pool_performance_methodology","source":"Bounded pool acquisition"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B85-005","batch_id":"D21-B85","domain":"performance","topic":"pool_contention","knowledge_type":"resource_contention","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A shared resource pool can become a contention point even when the underlying resource itself is not fully utilized.","implementation_pattern":"Measure wait time, occupancy, service time, and contention separately when diagnosing pool-related latency.","anti_pattern":"Increase pool size without determining whether the downstream resource or the pool synchronization mechanism is the actual bottleneck.","performance_effect":"Improves diagnosis of contention and avoids moving overload into downstream systems.","verification_method":"Measure resource wait distributions and downstream utilization across controlled pool sizes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESOURCE_CONTENTION_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"resource_pool_analysis","source":"Pool contention and downstream capacity analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B86-001","batch_id":"D21-B86","domain":"performance","topic":"database_connection_pooling","knowledge_type":"database_performance","language":"Python,Java,JavaScript,TypeScript,Go,Rust","concept":"Database connection pools can reduce connection setup overhead but excessive pool capacity can increase database concurrency, contention, memory usage, and queueing elsewhere.","implementation_pattern":"Size database pools according to measured application concurrency and actual database capacity, not merely application thread count.","anti_pattern":"Give every application instance a large database pool without accounting for aggregate connections across instances.","performance_effect":"Improves database stability and reduces cross-layer resource contention.","verification_method":"Measure aggregate connections, query latency, database CPU, lock waits, and application pool wait time as pool capacity changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"database_performance_methodology","source":"Connection-pool and database-capacity analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B86-002","batch_id":"D21-B86","domain":"performance","topic":"database_lock_contention","knowledge_type":"database_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Concurrent database operations can spend substantial time waiting on locks or equivalent concurrency-control mechanisms even when CPU utilization is low.","implementation_pattern":"Measure lock waits and transaction durations separately from query execution time.","anti_pattern":"Add application workers when the database is already constrained by lock contention.","performance_effect":"Prevents concurrency increases from amplifying serialization and waiting.","verification_method":"Capture lock-wait metrics and transaction timing under representative concurrency and compare with application latency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"database_performance_methodology","source":"Lock contention analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B86-003","batch_id":"D21-B86","domain":"performance","topic":"transaction_scope","knowledge_type":"database_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Long transactions can retain locks and other resources longer than necessary and increase contention and rollback cost.","implementation_pattern":"Keep transaction scope aligned with the atomic correctness requirement and avoid unrelated computation or external I/O inside database transactions.","anti_pattern":"Open a transaction before expensive application work and keep it active until the entire request completes.","performance_effect":"Reduces lock duration and improves concurrency where transaction semantics permit.","verification_method":"Measure transaction duration, lock waits, throughput, and correctness under equivalent workloads with different transaction scopes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"database_performance_methodology","source":"Transaction-scope optimization"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B86-004","batch_id":"D21-B86","domain":"performance","topic":"database_n_plus_one","knowledge_type":"query_efficiency","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Repeated dependent queries for each item in a collection can multiply database round trips and dominate request latency.","implementation_pattern":"Detect repeated query patterns and replace them with appropriate set-based retrieval, batching, joins, or prefetching when semantics permit.","anti_pattern":"Issue one database request per collection item when the same information can be retrieved safely in fewer operations.","performance_effect":"Can reduce network round trips, parsing, planning, and database execution overhead.","verification_method":"Count database operations per request and compare end-to-end latency and database load before and after the change.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"database_query_performance","source":"N+1 query pattern analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B86-005","batch_id":"D21-B86","domain":"performance","topic":"database_result_set_size","knowledge_type":"database_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Returning more database rows or columns than required increases database work, network transfer, application parsing, memory usage, and downstream processing.","implementation_pattern":"Select only required columns and constrain result sets according to the actual operation semantics.","anti_pattern":"Fetch complete records and discard most fields in application code.","performance_effect":"Reduces data movement and unnecessary processing across the database boundary.","verification_method":"Compare query execution, result size, network volume, application memory, and end-to-end latency before and after projection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"database_query_performance","source":"Result-set minimization"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B87-001","batch_id":"D21-B87","domain":"performance","topic":"benchmark_reproducibility","knowledge_type":"benchmark_governance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A performance result is more useful when workload, dataset, environment, software version, configuration, measurement procedure, and resource conditions are recorded.","implementation_pattern":"Store benchmark metadata alongside measurements and preserve the exact test configuration needed for reproduction.","anti_pattern":"Store only a throughput number without workload or environment metadata.","performance_effect":"Improves comparability across revisions and reduces ambiguous benchmark interpretation.","verification_method":"Re-run the benchmark from recorded metadata and compare result distributions rather than expecting identical single-run values.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_BENCHMARK_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"benchmark_reproducibility_methodology","source":"Reproducible performance measurement"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B87-002","batch_id":"D21-B87","domain":"performance","topic":"environment_drift","knowledge_type":"benchmark_integrity","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Changes in hardware, operating-system configuration, compiler/runtime versions, background activity, dependency versions, or dataset characteristics can alter performance independently of the code change being evaluated.","implementation_pattern":"Record relevant environment state and isolate or quantify meaningful environmental changes between benchmark runs.","anti_pattern":"Compare results across materially different environments as though the measurements were directly equivalent.","performance_effect":"Reduces false regression and improvement claims.","verification_method":"Compare environment metadata and rerun representative baselines when material environment changes are detected.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_BENCHMARKING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"benchmark_integrity_methodology","source":"Environment-drift analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B87-003","batch_id":"D21-B87","domain":"performance","topic":"benchmark_noise","knowledge_type":"statistical_measurement","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance measurements contain variability from scheduling, caches, thermal behavior, background processes, runtime state, and other environmental factors.","implementation_pattern":"Use repeated measurements and summarize distributions or uncertainty rather than relying on one run.","anti_pattern":"Treat the fastest observed run as the true performance of the implementation.","performance_effect":"Reduces sensitivity to transient measurement noise.","verification_method":"Repeat equivalent trials and inspect variance, outliers, and central tendency before comparing implementations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_BENCHMARKING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_statistics","source":"Repeated-measurement methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B87-004","batch_id":"D21-B87","domain":"performance","topic":"benchmark_dataset_representativeness","knowledge_type":"benchmark_design","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A benchmark dataset should represent the distribution of inputs, sizes, locality, skew, and exceptional cases that matter to the target workload.","implementation_pattern":"Construct benchmark datasets from documented workload characteristics and include boundary and adversarial-but-valid distributions where relevant.","anti_pattern":"Use a tiny convenient dataset that has different size or distribution characteristics from the production workload.","performance_effect":"Improves transferability of benchmark conclusions to real workloads.","verification_method":"Compare benchmark input distributions with target workload distributions and quantify important differences.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_BENCHMARK_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"benchmark_design_methodology","source":"Workload representativeness analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B87-005","batch_id":"D21-B87","domain":"performance","topic":"benchmark_contamination","knowledge_type":"benchmark_integrity","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Benchmark results can become misleading when caching, warm state, generated data, compiler artifacts, or test-specific shortcuts make the benchmark materially different from the intended workload.","implementation_pattern":"Define and control warm/cold state, cache state, data preparation, and benchmark-specific behavior explicitly.","anti_pattern":"Allow benchmark setup artifacts to provide performance benefits unavailable in the target workload.","performance_effect":"Improves validity of performance comparisons.","verification_method":"Run controlled cold and warm scenarios and inspect whether benchmark-only state materially changes the target metric.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_BENCHMARK_INTEGRITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"benchmark_integrity_methodology","source":"Benchmark contamination analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B88-001","batch_id":"D21-B88","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_regression_threshold","knowledge_type":"acceptance_policy","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A performance regression policy should distinguish meaningful regressions from normal measurement variability and define thresholds relative to the target metric.","implementation_pattern":"Set metric-specific acceptance thresholds based on historical variance, workload importance, and operational impact.","anti_pattern":"Fail every build on any numerical difference regardless of measurement noise.","performance_effect":"Reduces false alarms while preserving detection of meaningful degradation.","verification_method":"Evaluate repeated baseline measurements and calibrate thresholds against observed variance and known-impact changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_regression_methodology","source":"Regression threshold calibration"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B88-002","batch_id":"D21-B88","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_ci","knowledge_type":"continuous_verification","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance checks in continuous integration should use stable workloads and focused metrics because broad noisy benchmarks can create unreliable gates.","implementation_pattern":"Use small deterministic microbenchmarks for local regressions and targeted representative benchmarks for higher-level validation.","anti_pattern":"Run one large variable benchmark on every change and treat its raw output as a deterministic pass/fail signal.","performance_effect":"Makes automated performance regression detection more actionable and less noisy.","verification_method":"Validate CI benchmark stability and measure false-positive behavior across repeated baseline runs.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_CI_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_ci_methodology","source":"Continuous performance verification"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B88-003","batch_id":"D21-B88","domain":"performance","topic":"microbenchmark_scope","knowledge_type":"benchmark_design","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Microbenchmarks are useful for isolated operations but may omit allocation, I/O, scheduling, integration, and system effects that dominate real workloads.","implementation_pattern":"Use microbenchmarks to answer narrow implementation questions and combine them with workload-level validation.","anti_pattern":"Use an isolated microbenchmark as proof of end-to-end application improvement.","performance_effect":"Reduces the risk of optimizing an isolated operation that has negligible system-level impact.","verification_method":"Correlate microbenchmark changes with representative end-to-end workload measurements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_BENCHMARKING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"benchmarking_methodology","source":"Microbenchmark versus system-benchmark analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B88-004","batch_id":"D21-B88","domain":"performance","topic":"benchmark_overfitting","knowledge_type":"benchmark_failure_mode","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"An implementation can become highly optimized for a narrow benchmark while performing poorly on broader valid workloads.","implementation_pattern":"Maintain multiple workload classes and vary input sizes, distributions, and execution paths during validation.","anti_pattern":"Optimize directly against one fixed benchmark until its score improves without testing workload diversity.","performance_effect":"Improves generality of performance improvements.","verification_method":"Evaluate the change across independent workload distributions and reject optimizations that regress important target classes without justified tradeoffs.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_BENCHMARK_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"benchmark_quality_methodology","source":"Benchmark overfitting analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B88-005","batch_id":"D21-B88","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_budget_enforcement","knowledge_type":"quality_gate","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance budgets become useful engineering constraints when they are expressed as measurable limits for latency, memory, CPU, payload size, or other target metrics.","implementation_pattern":"Associate each budget with a defined workload, measurement procedure, threshold, owner, and response when exceeded.","anti_pattern":"State performance goals qualitatively without a measurable validation mechanism.","performance_effect":"Turns performance requirements into testable engineering constraints.","verification_method":"Execute the defined workload and automatically compare measured metrics with the approved budget.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_budgeting_methodology","source":"Measurable performance budgets"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B89-001","batch_id":"D21-B89","domain":"performance","topic":"optimization_order","knowledge_type":"optimization_strategy","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Optimization effort should generally prioritize bottlenecks with significant contribution to the target metric and feasible improvement potential rather than optimizing code solely because it is technically interesting.","implementation_pattern":"Rank candidates using measured cost, user or system impact, confidence in diagnosis, expected improvement, implementation risk, and validation cost.","anti_pattern":"Optimize the most complex function or the function with the largest source-code size without performance evidence.","performance_effect":"Improves engineering return on optimization effort.","verification_method":"Compare predicted and observed benefit for prioritized optimizations and retain evidence for rejected alternatives.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_OPTIMIZATION_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_optimization_methodology","source":"Evidence-driven optimization prioritization"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B89-002","batch_id":"D21-B89","domain":"performance","topic":"optimization_interaction","knowledge_type":"optimization_system_effect","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance optimizations can interact nonlinearly, so the benefit of a change in isolation may differ after other changes modify the bottleneck structure.","implementation_pattern":"Revalidate important end-to-end metrics after composing multiple performance changes.","anti_pattern":"Assume the sum of isolated benchmark improvements equals the final system improvement.","performance_effect":"Reduces incorrect performance forecasts caused by independent-change assumptions.","verification_method":"Measure individual changes and the composed implementation under the same representative workload.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SYSTEM_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_optimization_methodology","source":"Optimization interaction analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B89-003","batch_id":"D21-B89","domain":"performance","topic":"bottleneck_migration","knowledge_type":"optimization_reasoning","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Removing one bottleneck can expose another previously hidden bottleneck, causing the dominant performance constraint to migrate.","implementation_pattern":"Reprofile and remeasure the system after each material optimization rather than assuming the original bottleneck remains dominant.","anti_pattern":"Continue optimizing the original component after its contribution has become small.","performance_effect":"Keeps optimization aligned with the current system bottleneck.","verification_method":"Compare profiling and resource-utilization evidence before and after each significant optimization.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"bottleneck_analysis","source":"Bottleneck migration methodology"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B89-004","batch_id":"D21-B89","domain":"performance","topic":"optimization_reversibility","knowledge_type":"engineering_risk","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Optimizations that substantially increase implementation complexity should justify their maintenance and correctness cost with measured performance benefit.","implementation_pattern":"Record complexity impact, correctness risk, benchmark evidence, and rollback strategy for high-risk optimizations.","anti_pattern":"Accept substantial complexity solely because an optimization is theoretically faster.","performance_effect":"Balances runtime performance against engineering and maintenance cost.","verification_method":"Review measured benefit, regression coverage, maintainability impact, and rollback readiness before adoption.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_TRADEOFF","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_engineering_methodology","source":"Optimization risk-benefit analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B89-005","batch_id":"D21-B89","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_knowledge_reuse","knowledge_type":"knowledge_fabric_pattern","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance knowledge becomes more reusable when each optimization records applicability conditions, observed evidence, limitations, alternatives, and known failure modes.","implementation_pattern":"Store performance records as structured evidence units linked to workloads, implementations, metrics, versions, and verification states.","anti_pattern":"Store optimization advice as isolated prose without applicability or evidence metadata.","performance_effect":"Improves retrieval accuracy and reduces inappropriate reuse of context-specific optimizations.","verification_method":"Validate that retrieved recommendations expose their prerequisites, evidence state, and applicability boundaries.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"knowledge_fabric_design","source":"Structured performance knowledge representation"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B90-001","batch_id":"D21-B90","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_anti_pattern_detection","knowledge_type":"automated_reasoning","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance anti-pattern detection can be expressed as machine-readable predicates over code structure, runtime evidence, workload characteristics, and resource behavior.","implementation_pattern":"Represent anti-patterns with detection signals, confidence conditions, exceptions, remediation candidates, and verification requirements.","anti_pattern":"Encode an anti-pattern as a simple keyword match without workload or semantic context.","performance_effect":"Enables automated analysis while reducing false positives from context-free pattern matching.","verification_method":"Test detection rules against positive, negative, boundary, and exception cases.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_DESIGN_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_reasoning_methodology","source":"Machine-readable anti-pattern detection"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B90-002","batch_id":"D21-B90","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_root_cause_chain","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_reasoning","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A performance diagnosis should preserve a causal chain from observed symptom through measurements and suspected bottleneck to intervention and verified outcome.","implementation_pattern":"Represent symptom, evidence, hypothesis, intervention, expected effect, observed effect, and residual bottleneck as linked records.","anti_pattern":"Store only the final optimization recommendation without the evidence and reasoning that produced it.","performance_effect":"Improves future diagnosis and prevents unsupported optimization reuse.","verification_method":"Require each accepted optimization record to reference supporting measurements and post-change evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_REASONING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"performance_reasoning_methodology","source":"Evidence-to-outcome causal chain"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B90-003","batch_id":"D21-B90","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_contradiction_handling","knowledge_type":"knowledge_governance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance recommendations may conflict because different workloads, architectures, versions, resource constraints, or measurement methods produce different outcomes.","implementation_pattern":"Represent contradictions explicitly with scope conditions, evidence references, workload definitions, and resolution status instead of deleting conflicting records.","anti_pattern":"Select one optimization rule as universally correct and discard context-dependent evidence.","performance_effect":"Preserves useful conditional knowledge and reduces unsafe generalization.","verification_method":"Check that conflicting records identify the conditions under which each recommendation applies.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"contradiction_aware_knowledge_design","source":"Context-dependent performance evidence"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B90-004","batch_id":"D21-B90","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_evidence_levels","knowledge_type":"knowledge_governance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance knowledge should distinguish theoretical reasoning, official documentation, controlled benchmark evidence, production observation, and independently reproduced results.","implementation_pattern":"Assign an explicit evidence level and verification state to each performance record and prevent stronger claims from being inferred from weaker evidence.","anti_pattern":"Treat an illustrative optimization example as an experimentally verified benchmark result.","performance_effect":"Improves trustworthiness of autonomous performance reasoning.","verification_method":"Audit evidence metadata against the actual provenance and execution state of each record.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"evidence_governance","source":"Performance evidence classification"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B90-005","batch_id":"D21-B90","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_phase_lock_validation","knowledge_type":"dataset_validation","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Before a performance dataset phase is locked, records should be checked for schema validity, duplicate concepts, contradictory claims, unsupported provenance, version ambiguity, missing verification states, and coverage gaps.","implementation_pattern":"Run structural validation, semantic deduplication, contradiction analysis, provenance checks, version checks, relationship integrity checks, and coverage analysis before phase lock.","anti_pattern":"Lock the phase solely because the planned batch count has been reached.","performance_effect":"Preserves dataset reliability and prevents incomplete or misleading performance knowledge from becoming foundational training data.","verification_method":"Require all validation categories to pass or have explicitly documented unresolved exceptions before declaring the phase locked.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"dataset_quality_methodology","source":"Performance dataset validation and phase-lock criteria"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B91-001","batch_id":"D21-B91","domain":"performance","topic":"network_latency_decomposition","knowledge_type":"network_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"End-to-end network latency can contain DNS resolution, connection establishment, protocol negotiation, queueing, transmission, server processing, response transmission, and client-side processing.","implementation_pattern":"Measure distinct network-path stages when diagnosing latency instead of treating total request duration as a single network cost.","anti_pattern":"Optimize server computation when connection establishment, transport, or downstream network waiting dominates total latency.","performance_effect":"Improves network bottleneck localization and prevents optimization of non-dominant stages.","verification_method":"Capture stage-level timings across representative requests and compare their contribution to total latency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_NETWORK_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"network_performance_methodology","source":"Network latency decomposition"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B91-002","batch_id":"D21-B91","domain":"performance","topic":"connection_reuse_network","knowledge_type":"network_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Reusing suitable network connections can avoid repeated connection setup and protocol negotiation costs.","implementation_pattern":"Use persistent or pooled connections when protocol semantics, failure handling, and workload characteristics support safe reuse.","anti_pattern":"Repeatedly establish connections for short-lived operations without measuring the setup overhead.","performance_effect":"Can reduce latency and connection-management CPU overhead for repeated communication.","verification_method":"Compare connection-reuse and connection-establishment behavior under representative request rates and payload sizes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_NETWORK_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"network_performance_methodology","source":"Connection reuse analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B91-003","batch_id":"D21-B91","domain":"performance","topic":"network_payload_amplification","knowledge_type":"network_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Protocol metadata, framing, retries, duplicated fields, and inefficient application payloads can make transferred bytes substantially larger than the logical business data.","implementation_pattern":"Measure wire-level bytes separately from logical payload size when network efficiency matters.","anti_pattern":"Estimate network cost exclusively from serialized business payload size.","performance_effect":"Improves bandwidth planning and identifies hidden transfer overhead.","verification_method":"Measure logical payload size, encoded size, wire bytes, retransmissions, and request frequency under representative traffic.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_NETWORK_ANALYSIS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"network_performance_methodology","source":"Wire-size and payload-amplification analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B91-004","batch_id":"D21-B91","domain":"performance","topic":"network_retry_amplification","knowledge_type":"distributed_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Retries increase offered load and can amplify congestion or overload when a dependency is already slow or unavailable.","implementation_pattern":"Use bounded retries with appropriate timeout, backoff, jitter, idempotency, and retry-budget policies.","anti_pattern":"Retry every failure immediately and indefinitely.","performance_effect":"Reduces retry storms and secondary overload during degraded dependency conditions.","verification_method":"Inject controlled latency and failures and measure retry volume, total request load, latency, and recovery behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"distributed_systems_performance","source":"Retry amplification and overload control"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B91-005","batch_id":"D21-B91","domain":"performance","topic":"timeout_budget_propagation","knowledge_type":"distributed_performance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Independent downstream timeouts can cause nested operations to consume more time than the caller's actual deadline permits.","implementation_pattern":"Propagate remaining deadline or timeout budget through dependent calls where the architecture supports it.","anti_pattern":"Give every downstream operation a fresh full timeout unrelated to the caller's remaining deadline.","performance_effect":"Reduces wasted work after the original request can no longer meet its deadline.","verification_method":"Inject downstream delay and verify that propagated deadlines bound total request lifetime and cancellation behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"distributed_systems_methodology","source":"Deadline and timeout propagation"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B92-001","batch_id":"D21-B92","domain":"performance","topic":"lock_contention","knowledge_type":"concurrency_performance","language":"Python,Java,JavaScript,TypeScript,C,C++,Rust,Go","concept":"A lock can become a performance bottleneck when many workers compete for a critical section whose serialized work limits useful concurrency.","implementation_pattern":"Measure lock wait time and critical-section duration before changing synchronization strategy.","anti_pattern":"Replace synchronization blindly with more threads or processes without identifying contention.","performance_effect":"Improves identification of serialized portions of concurrent workloads.","verification_method":"Measure contention, wait time, hold time, and throughput at increasing concurrency levels.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCURRENCY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"concurrency_performance_methodology","source":"Lock contention analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B92-002","batch_id":"D21-B92","domain":"performance","topic":"critical_section_minimization","knowledge_type":"concurrency_optimization","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Reducing non-essential work inside a serialized critical section can increase effective concurrency without removing required synchronization.","implementation_pattern":"Move independent computation, I/O, formatting, and other non-critical work outside the protected region when correctness permits.","anti_pattern":"Perform expensive unrelated work while holding a shared lock.","performance_effect":"Reduces lock hold time and increases potential parallel progress.","verification_method":"Measure critical-section duration, contention, and throughput before and after the change while testing concurrency correctness.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCURRENCY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"concurrency_performance_methodology","source":"Critical-section minimization"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B92-003","batch_id":"D21-B92","domain":"performance","topic":"false_sharing","knowledge_type":"memory_concurrency","language":"C,C++,Rust,Java,Go","concept":"Independent variables modified by different workers can interfere through shared cache-line ownership when they occupy the same cache line, increasing coherence traffic.","implementation_pattern":"Identify measured cache-coherence contention and consider data layout changes that separate heavily updated independent state.","anti_pattern":"Add padding or restructure memory solely from theoretical assumptions without profiling or representative workload evidence.","performance_effect":"Can reduce cache-coherence traffic in highly concurrent workloads.","verification_method":"Measure scalability and hardware-level contention indicators where available before and after controlled data-layout changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_HARDWARE_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.96,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"computer_architecture","source":"Cache coherence and false-sharing analysis"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B92-004","batch_id":"D21-B92","domain":"performance","topic":"atomic_contention","knowledge_type":"concurrency_performance","language":"C,C++,Rust,Java,Go","concept":"Highly contended atomic operations can serialize cache ownership and become a scalability bottleneck even though they avoid traditional mutex locking.","implementation_pattern":"Measure contention before replacing synchronization and consider sharding, batching, local aggregation, or reduced update frequency when semantics allow.","anti_pattern":"Assume atomic operations are effectively free because they do not use explicit locks.","performance_effect":"Can improve scalability of heavily updated shared counters or state.","verification_method":"Measure throughput as worker count increases and compare shared versus partitioned update strategies.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCURRENCY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"computer_architecture","source":"Atomic contention and cache-coherence behavior"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B92-005","batch_id":"D21-B92","domain":"performance","topic":"work_stealing","knowledge_type":"parallel_scheduling","language":"Java,C++,Rust,Go","concept":"Work-stealing schedulers can improve load balance by allowing workers with less work to acquire tasks from workers with more work, subject to synchronization and scheduling overhead.","implementation_pattern":"Use work-stealing or equivalent dynamic scheduling when task execution times are sufficiently variable and balancing benefits exceed scheduling overhead.","anti_pattern":"Assume dynamic scheduling is always faster than static partitioning.","performance_effect":"Can improve parallel utilization for irregular workloads.","verification_method":"Compare worker utilization, scheduling overhead, throughput, and tail task completion time against static scheduling.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PARALLEL_PERFORMANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.97,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"parallel_computing","source":"Dynamic scheduling and work-stealing"}}

