{"record_id":"D21-B99-005","batch_id":"D21-B99","domain":"performance","topic":"performance_unknown_state","knowledge_type":"uncertainty_governance","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A performance knowledge system must represent unknown, unmeasured, conflicting, and obsolete states rather than forcing every claim into a verified or false binary.","implementation_pattern":"Use explicit evidence and verification states such as PROPOSED, REVIEWED, MEASURED, VERIFIED, CONTRADICTED, SUPERSEDED, and UNKNOWN where appropriate.","anti_pattern":"Convert missing measurements into assumed performance facts.","performance_effect":"Prevents autonomous systems from treating uncertainty as established engineering knowledge.","verification_method":"Audit records for unsupported certainty and verify that claims without sufficient evidence retain an appropriate uncertainty state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"KNOWLEDGE_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"evidence_governance","source":"Explicit uncertainty-state modeling"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B100-001","batch_id":"D21-B100","domain":"performance","topic":"complete_performance_scope","knowledge_type":"phase_completion","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"A complete performance knowledge phase must cover measurement, latency, throughput, CPU, memory, I/O, networking, concurrency, parallelism, caching, databases, serialization, resource management, runtime behavior, deployment effects, resilience, benchmarking, optimization, governance, and verification.","implementation_pattern":"Validate phase coverage by domain and concept rather than by record count and identify unresolved gaps before lock.","anti_pattern":"Declare performance coverage complete solely because a predetermined number of records or batches exists.","performance_effect":"Prevents superficial dataset completion and improves coverage reliability.","verification_method":"Run a coverage matrix against the defined D21 scope and document any intentionally excluded areas.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_COMPLETION_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"dataset_quality_methodology","source":"Coverage-based phase completion"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B100-002","batch_id":"D21-B100","domain":"performance","topic":"schema_integrity_validation","knowledge_type":"dataset_validation","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Every D21 record should satisfy the required schema, use stable identifiers, preserve valid relationships, and distinguish execution state from validation state.","implementation_pattern":"Run machine validation for required fields, identifier uniqueness, enum validity, relationship references, JSON validity, and state consistency.","anti_pattern":"Accept records because they are readable by humans without structural validation.","performance_effect":"Improves reliability of downstream ingestion, retrieval, reasoning, and training pipelines.","verification_method":"Parse every record, validate schema constraints, detect duplicate IDs, and verify all referenced identifiers.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_VALIDATION_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"dataset_validation_methodology","source":"Machine-readable schema validation"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B100-003","batch_id":"D21-B100","domain":"performance","topic":"provenance_integrity_validation","knowledge_type":"dataset_validation","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Performance claims should retain provenance strong enough to distinguish official documentation, established theory, controlled measurement, production observation, illustrative reasoning, and unverified proposals.","implementation_pattern":"Validate provenance completeness and reject fabricated execution, benchmark, source, or verification claims.","anti_pattern":"Assign high evidence levels merely because a record sounds technically plausible.","performance_effect":"Protects the knowledge fabric against synthetic but unsupported performance claims.","verification_method":"Audit evidence_level, provenance, execution_status, and validation_status for consistency.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"dataset_provenance_methodology","source":"Evidence and provenance integrity"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B100-004","batch_id":"D21-B100","domain":"performance","topic":"semantic_deduplication_and_contradiction_audit","knowledge_type":"dataset_validation","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"Final performance validation must identify duplicate concepts and preserve genuine contradictions when they arise from different workloads, versions, environments, or constraints.","implementation_pattern":"Perform semantic clustering, scope comparison, contradiction detection, and applicability analysis before phase lock.","anti_pattern":"Delete contradictory records or merge distinct records merely because they discuss the same performance concept.","performance_effect":"Improves information density while preserving context-dependent engineering knowledge.","verification_method":"Review high-similarity record clusters and contradiction candidates and verify that retained distinctions have explicit scope or evidence differences.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REVIEWED","evidence_level":"DATASET_QUALITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"knowledge_quality_methodology","source":"Semantic deduplication and contradiction audit"}}

{"record_id":"D21-B100-005","batch_id":"D21-B100","domain":"performance","topic":"D21_phase_lock","knowledge_type":"dataset_lock","language":"language_agnostic","concept":"D21 Performance is ready for phase lock only after structural validation, semantic deduplication, contradiction review, provenance integrity checks, version-scope validation, coverage analysis, and relationship integrity checks are completed; future modifications must create a new version rather than silently mutating the locked baseline.","implementation_pattern":"Freeze the D21 baseline with stable record IDs and record a versioned lock manifest containing coverage status, validation status, unresolved exceptions if any, and downstream dependency references.","anti_pattern":"Silently modify locked records or declare the phase complete solely because B100 has been reached.","performance_effect":"Creates a stable performance knowledge baseline for downstream datasets and autonomous software-engineering reasoning.","verification_method":"Execute the D21 lock checklist and require all mandatory validation categories to pass; record any unresolved exceptions explicitly rather than claiming verification without evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"LOCK_CRITERIA_DEFINED","evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"version_range":"GENERAL","provenance":{"source_type":"dataset_phase_governance","source":"D21 Performance phase-lock specification"}}

{"record_id":"D22-B01-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B01","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"failure_models","subtopic":"failure_taxonomy","concept":"A reliability design should distinguish failure types because detection, containment, recovery, and verification strategies depend on how a component fails.","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"Why must reliability engineering distinguish failure types?","answer":"A component may fail by crashing, timing out, returning incorrect data, becoming partially unavailable, becoming overloaded, producing corrupted state, or remaining reachable while functionally unhealthy. These modes require different detection and recovery strategies.","explanation":"Failure classification provides the basis for failure-mode analysis and prevents one generic recovery mechanism from being incorrectly applied to materially different faults.","principle":"Model failure semantics before selecting resilience mechanisms.","examples":["A crashed process requires restart or replacement.","A slow dependency may require deadline propagation and isolation.","A semantically incorrect response may require validation rather than retry."],"invalid_examples":["Treat every non-success response as a transient failure.","Assume network reachability proves application correctness."],"problem":"Undifferentiated failure handling can turn recoverable faults into prolonged outages or repeated harmful operations.","failure":"Incorrectly classified failures trigger inappropriate recovery behavior.","cause":"Missing failure taxonomy or overly broad error classification.","solution":"Define explicit failure classes and map each class to detection, containment, recovery, and verification behavior.","alternatives":["Failure-mode-and-effects analysis","Fault-tree analysis","Dependency-specific error taxonomy"],"tradeoffs":["More precise classification increases design and implementation complexity.","Overly granular classifications can become difficult to maintain."],"constraints":["Failure categories must remain tied to observable system behavior.","Classification must not claim certainty when evidence is insufficient."],"edge_cases":["A dependency may be reachable but return stale or invalid data.","A timeout may mean the operation failed, succeeded but response was lost, or remains unknown."],"security_implications":"Security failures may require containment rather than ordinary retry or recovery.","performance_implications":"Failure classification itself should remain lightweight enough for critical paths.","testing":"Inject distinct failure modes and verify that each produces the intended response.","verification":"Validate failure classification against observed fault behavior and recovery outcomes.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Basic distributed-systems failure concepts"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B01-002","D22-B02-001"],"contrasts_with":["D22-B01-003"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Failure-mode classification"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B01-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B01","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"failure_models","subtopic":"partial_failure","concept":"Distributed systems can experience partial failure in which some components or operations remain functional while others fail.","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"What is partial failure and why is it important?","answer":"Partial failure occurs when only part of a distributed system fails or becomes degraded while other components continue operating. Reliability mechanisms must prevent the failed portion from unnecessarily taking healthy portions down with it.","explanation":"Partial failure is more difficult than total failure because the system may continue processing requests while producing degraded, inconsistent, delayed, or incomplete results.","principle":"Design for localized failure rather than assuming the entire system fails together.","examples":["One service instance is unhealthy while other instances remain healthy.","One database shard is unavailable while other shards remain accessible.","One dependency region is degraded while another remains operational."],"invalid_examples":["Assume a successful health check on one instance proves the entire service is healthy.","Route all traffic away from an entire system because one independent component failed."],"problem":"Failure in one component can propagate through shared resources or coordination paths.","failure":"Healthy components become unavailable because failure isolation was insufficient.","cause":"Shared pools, synchronous dependencies, global state, or coupled failure handling.","solution":"Partition failure domains and define component-specific health and recovery behavior.","alternatives":["Bulkheads","Cell-based architecture","Independent service pools"],"tradeoffs":["Isolation improves blast-radius control but can increase resource duplication and operational complexity."],"constraints":["Partitions must correspond to actual failure independence.","Isolation boundaries should not create correctness violations."],"edge_cases":["A nominally independent dependency may share a common control plane.","A single physical failure can affect multiple logical components."],"security_implications":"Failure isolation can limit the blast radius of compromised components.","performance_implications":"Isolation may reduce resource sharing efficiency but can improve stability under failure.","testing":"Independently fail one component while keeping neighboring components operational.","verification":"Verify that unaffected components continue meeting their defined availability and correctness objectives.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure taxonomy"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B03-001","D22-B06-001"],"related_to":["D22-B01-003"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_architecture_guidance","source":"Microsoft Azure reliability design guidance","source_version":"2026"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B01-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B01","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"failure_models","subtopic":"unknown_outcome","concept":"A timeout or lost response does not necessarily prove that the underlying operation failed; the operation may have completed while its result was not observed.","knowledge_type":"failure_semantics","question":"Why is an unknown outcome different from a confirmed failure?","answer":"When a caller times out or loses connectivity after submitting an operation, the remote operation may have failed, succeeded, or still be executing. Retrying blindly can therefore duplicate a side effect.","explanation":"Reliability requires distinguishing confirmed failure from uncertain outcome, especially for non-idempotent operations.","principle":"Do not convert absence of confirmation into proof of failure.","examples":["A payment request times out after the provider accepted it.","A job submission succeeds remotely but the acknowledgement is lost.","A database commit completes while the client connection fails before receiving the response."],"invalid_examples":["Retry every timed-out mutation without checking idempotency or operation status.","Assume a disconnected client means the server rolled back the operation."],"problem":"Ambiguous outcomes can cause duplicate side effects.","failure":"Repeated execution of an operation that may already have succeeded.","cause":"Loss of response, timeout, network partition, or client crash after submission.","solution":"Use idempotency keys, operation-status queries, transactional semantics, or reconciliation mechanisms appropriate to the operation.","alternatives":["Idempotent operation design","Durable operation identifiers","Reconciliation workflows"],"tradeoffs":["Additional state and lookup mechanisms increase complexity and storage requirements."],"constraints":["The recovery mechanism must preserve business correctness.","Idempotency semantics must be explicitly defined."],"edge_cases":["The status query can itself be unavailable.","An operation may remain in an intermediate state."],"security_implications":"Operation identifiers and reconciliation endpoints must be protected against unauthorized replay or disclosure.","performance_implications":"Status tracking introduces additional persistence and lookup work.","testing":"Force response loss after remote acceptance and verify that recovery does not duplicate the side effect.","verification":"Compare final remote state against the intended single operation outcome.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure classification","Idempotency concepts"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B02-002","D22-B04-003"],"contrasts_with":["D22-B01-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"distributed_systems_reliability","source":"Ambiguous operation outcome analysis"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B01-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B01","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"failure_models","subtopic":"fail_stop","concept":"Fail-stop behavior means a component stops processing rather than continuing with potentially corrupt or unsafe behavior, but the system must still detect and replace or recover the stopped component.","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"What reliability value does fail-stop behavior provide?","answer":"A fail-stop component that ceases operation can be easier to detect and isolate than a component that continues producing incorrect results. However, fail-stop behavior alone does not provide availability; detection and recovery are still required.","explanation":"Reliability benefits when failures are observable and bounded, but stopping a component merely changes the failure mode and does not eliminate the need for recovery.","principle":"Prefer bounded, observable failure over silent incorrect behavior when system semantics allow it.","examples":["A worker exits after detecting unrecoverable internal corruption.","A service refuses new work after entering an invalid state."],"invalid_examples":["Continue processing known-corrupt state merely to remain superficially available.","Treat process termination as successful recovery."],"problem":"Continuing after known corruption can propagate incorrect state.","failure":"Silent corruption or invalid processing persists across requests.","cause":"Failure handling prioritizes superficial availability over state correctness.","solution":"Enter a controlled failure state, expose health status, isolate the component, and trigger verified recovery.","alternatives":["Quarantine","Restart","Replacement instance"],"tradeoffs":["Fail-stop can reduce immediate availability while protecting correctness."],"constraints":["The system must have a mechanism to detect and recover stopped components."],"edge_cases":["A component can appear alive while internally unable to perform useful work."],"security_implications":"Controlled fail-stop can limit the impact of compromised or corrupted components.","performance_implications":"Fail-stop may temporarily reduce capacity until replacement is ready.","testing":"Inject unrecoverable internal state and verify controlled termination and replacement behavior.","verification":"Verify that no invalid work is accepted after the fail-stop condition and that recovery restores valid operation.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Health-state modeling"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B03-002","D22-B06-002"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Fail-stop failure semantics"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B01-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B01","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"failure_models","subtopic":"common_mode_failure","concept":"Redundant components do not provide effective resilience when they share a common dependency or failure cause.","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"What is a common-mode failure?","answer":"A common-mode failure occurs when multiple supposedly independent components fail because they share the same underlying dependency, configuration, infrastructure, software defect, or environmental condition.","explanation":"Reliability architecture must evaluate independence at the actual failure-domain level rather than counting replicas as independent merely because they are separate processes or instances.","principle":"Redundancy only improves resilience to failures that the redundant components do not share.","examples":["Multiple replicas depend on the same failed control plane.","Two service instances use the same faulty configuration.","Replicas are deployed across different processes but the same physical failure domain."],"invalid_examples":["Count ten replicas as ten independent protections without analyzing shared dependencies.","Assume geographic labels automatically imply independent failure domains."],"problem":"Apparent redundancy can provide little or no protection against shared failures.","failure":"All redundant components fail simultaneously from one underlying cause.","cause":"Shared infrastructure, software, configuration, credentials, network path, or operational dependency.","solution":"Identify common dependencies and deliberately separate meaningful failure domains.","alternatives":["Multi-zone deployment","Independent cells","Provider diversification where justified"],"tradeoffs":["Greater independence generally increases cost and operational complexity."],"constraints":["Independence claims require evidence about the underlying infrastructure and dependencies."],"edge_cases":["A rare provider-level event may affect multiple geographic regions.","A shared software release can simultaneously introduce identical defects."],"security_implications":"Shared credentials or security controls can create common-mode compromise.","performance_implications":"Stronger isolation can reduce resource-sharing efficiency.","testing":"Test failures at shared dependency boundaries rather than only individual component failures.","verification":"Validate the dependency graph for shared failure causes and test representative common-mode scenarios.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure-domain analysis"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B06-001","D22-B07-001"],"contrasts_with":["D22-B01-002"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Common-mode failure analysis"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B02-001","batch_id":"D22-B02","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"failure_detection","subtopic":"health_model","concept":"A reliability health model should distinguish process liveness, readiness to serve, dependency health, and functional correctness instead of treating one binary health signal as sufficient.","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"Why should reliability health be multi-dimensional?","answer":"A process can be alive but unable to serve traffic, or ready to receive traffic while a critical dependency is unusable. Separate health dimensions allow routing and recovery decisions to reflect actual service capability.","explanation":"Health should answer the operational question being asked: whether a process exists, whether it can accept work, whether required dependencies are usable, and whether critical functionality is operating correctly.","principle":"Health signals must match the decision they control.","examples":["Liveness detects an irrecoverably stuck process.","Readiness prevents traffic from reaching an instance that cannot serve requests.","Functional health verifies a critical operation."],"invalid_examples":["Restart a healthy process merely because an optional dependency is temporarily unavailable.","Route traffic to an instance solely because its process is alive."],"problem":"A single health signal can cause unnecessary restarts or route traffic to unhealthy instances.","failure":"Health-driven automation makes incorrect recovery or routing decisions.","cause":"Health semantics are not aligned with operational decisions.","solution":"Define separate health dimensions and map each to explicit consumers and actions.","alternatives":["Health endpoint hierarchy","Dependency-aware health model","Synthetic functional checks"],"tradeoffs":["More health checks increase implementation and monitoring complexity.","Deep dependency checks can themselves become expensive or fragile."],"constraints":["Health checks must not create a dependency cycle with the recovery mechanism.","Checks should be bounded and deterministic enough for their operational use."],"edge_cases":["A dependency can be degraded but optional for some request classes.","A deep health check may fail because of the same dependency outage it is supposed to diagnose."],"security_implications":"Health endpoints should not expose sensitive internal state.","performance_implications":"Health checks consume resources and should be bounded.","testing":"Simulate process, readiness, dependency, and functional failures independently.","verification":"Verify that each failure produces the intended routing or recovery action.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure taxonomy"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B02-002","D22-B05-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_architecture_guidance","source":"Microsoft Health Endpoint Monitoring pattern","source_version":"2026"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B02-002","batch_id":"D22-B02","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"failure_detection","subtopic":"timeout_semantics","concept":"Timeouts bound waiting but do not by themselves determine whether the remote operation succeeded.","knowledge_type":"reliability_rule","question":"What should a timeout mean in a reliability model?","answer":"A timeout means the caller did not obtain an acceptable result within its allowed waiting period. It should not automatically be interpreted as proof that the remote operation failed.","explanation":"Timeout handling must account for ambiguous outcomes, cancellation semantics, idempotency, and whether the remote operation can continue after the caller stops waiting.","principle":"A timeout is an observation about elapsed time, not necessarily a definitive remote failure state.","examples":["A read timeout can often be retried safely.","A timed-out mutation may require reconciliation before retrying."],"invalid_examples":["Retry every mutation timeout without checking whether the operation could have completed.","Treat a timeout as equivalent to a confirmed remote rollback."],"problem":"Incorrect timeout semantics can cause duplicate side effects or inconsistent state.","failure":"Caller and remote system disagree about the operation's final state.","cause":"Timeout treated as definitive failure.","solution":"Model timeout outcomes explicitly and use operation status, idempotency, cancellation, or reconciliation mechanisms.","alternatives":["Deadline propagation","Idempotency keys","Durable operation state"],"tradeoffs":["Additional state tracking improves correctness but adds storage and protocol complexity."],"constraints":["Timeout values must reflect actual operation semantics and system deadlines."],"edge_cases":["The remote service may complete successfully after the client timeout.","Cancellation may not propagate to the remote operation."],"security_implications":"Retry/reconciliation mechanisms must prevent unauthorized duplicate operations.","performance_implications":"Long timeouts can retain resources; overly short timeouts can create unnecessary retries.","testing":"Delay responses beyond timeout while allowing remote completion and inspect final state.","verification":"Confirm that client recovery converges to the correct remote state without duplicate side effects.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Unknown-outcome modeling"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B01-003","D22-B03-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"distributed_systems_reliability","source":"Timeout and ambiguous-outcome semantics"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B02-003","batch_id":"D22-B02","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"failure_detection","subtopic":"health_check_design","concept":"A health check should test only the properties required by the decision that consumes its result.","knowledge_type":"design_rule","question":"How should a health check be scoped?","answer":"A health check should be as deep as necessary for its decision but no deeper. Routing checks should establish serving readiness, while deeper diagnostic checks can assess dependency or functional health separately.","explanation":"Overly shallow checks can route traffic to unhealthy instances, while overly deep checks can create fragile dependencies and amplify an outage.","principle":"Health-check depth should match decision scope.","examples":["A load balancer readiness check verifies that the instance can serve.","A diagnostic endpoint can separately report dependency degradation."],"invalid_examples":["Make every liveness probe call every external dependency.","Use process existence as proof of functional correctness."],"problem":"Incorrect check depth causes either false health or unnecessary recovery actions.","failure":"Healthy components are restarted or unhealthy components receive traffic.","cause":"Health semantics are broader or narrower than the decision requires.","solution":"Define check purpose, scope, timeout, dependencies, and failure action explicitly.","alternatives":["Shallow liveness","Readiness checks","Synthetic functional probes"],"tradeoffs":["Deep checks provide more information but introduce more dependency and execution cost."],"constraints":["Checks must have bounded execution time.","Checks should avoid causing side effects."],"edge_cases":["A dependency may be optional for some traffic classes.","A deep check may fail during a dependency outage even though the service can still provide degraded functionality."],"security_implications":"Health endpoints should be access-controlled where detailed internals could be sensitive.","performance_implications":"High-frequency health checks can become measurable load.","testing":"Exercise each check with partial dependency failures and verify decision-specific behavior.","verification":"Confirm that health consumers respond correctly to each health state.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Health model"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B02-001","D22-B05-002"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_architecture_guidance","source":"Health Endpoint Monitoring pattern","source_version":"2026"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B02-004","batch_id":"D22-B02","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"failure_detection","subtopic":"fault_observability","concept":"Reliable recovery requires enough observability to distinguish the triggering fault, containment state, recovery progress, and final outcome.","knowledge_type":"observability_requirement","question":"What evidence should accompany automated recovery?","answer":"Automated recovery should record the detected condition, evidence supporting the detection, action taken, action result, remaining uncertainty, and final health state.","explanation":"Without an evidence chain, automated remediation can hide repeated failures or make successful-looking actions impossible to distinguish from actual recovery.","principle":"Recovery actions must be observable as state transitions, not merely logged as commands.","examples":["Record why a circuit opened, how many failures triggered it, and whether half-open probes succeeded.","Record restart initiation and subsequent readiness verification separately."],"invalid_examples":["Log only 'restarted service successfully' without verifying service readiness.","Treat command completion as proof of recovery."],"problem":"Operational automation can repeatedly perform ineffective actions without learning that the underlying fault remains.","failure":"Recovery loops conceal persistent faults.","cause":"Missing post-action verification and weak recovery telemetry.","solution":"Record detection, containment, action, verification, and final state separately.","alternatives":["Structured recovery events","State-transition audit records","Distributed traces"],"tradeoffs":["More telemetry increases storage and operational complexity."],"constraints":["Evidence must distinguish attempted action from verified outcome."],"edge_cases":["The recovery action succeeds while the original dependency remains unavailable.","The monitoring system itself may be degraded."],"security_implications":"Recovery telemetry can contain sensitive infrastructure information and requires appropriate access control.","performance_implications":"Telemetry should be bounded and should not materially interfere with recovery.","testing":"Inject faults and inspect whether the complete recovery evidence chain is emitted.","verification":"Verify that recovery cannot be marked complete without a valid post-action health signal.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Health model","Recovery state modeling"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B04-005","D22-B10-003"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Evidence-driven automated recovery"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B02-005","batch_id":"D22-B02","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"failure_detection","subtopic":"false_positive_health","concept":"A reliability detector must account for false-positive health failures because incorrect failure classification can trigger unnecessary restarts, traffic movement, or failover.","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why are false-positive health failures dangerous?","answer":"A detector that incorrectly marks healthy components as failed can remove capacity, trigger unnecessary recovery, and potentially create a larger outage than the original transient condition.","explanation":"Reliability automation must balance sensitivity against false-positive recovery actions.","principle":"Detection quality must be evaluated by both missed failures and incorrect failure declarations.","examples":["Temporary monitoring latency causes an unnecessary restart.","A dependency timeout marks a service unhealthy even though degraded service remains safe and functional."],"invalid_examples":["Restart immediately after one transient monitoring failure.","Fail over repeatedly without stabilizing the health signal."],"problem":"False-positive remediation can amplify transient faults.","failure":"Healthy capacity is removed during an already degraded condition.","cause":"Noisy health signal, overly aggressive threshold, or insufficient confirmation.","solution":"Use appropriate thresholds, consecutive evidence, hysteresis, or multi-signal confirmation where justified.","alternatives":["Failure counters","Hysteresis","Multi-signal health evaluation"],"tradeoffs":["More confirmation reduces false positives but can delay genuine recovery actions."],"constraints":["Detection delay must remain compatible with the system's reliability objectives."],"edge_cases":["Very fast failures may require aggressive detection.","A correlated monitoring failure can make multiple independent health signals unreliable."],"security_implications":"An attacker may attempt to trigger false health failures as a denial-of-service mechanism.","performance_implications":"Repeated unnecessary restarts consume resources and reduce capacity.","testing":"Inject noisy health signals and verify that recovery does not flap uncontrollably.","verification":"Measure false-positive and missed-failure behavior under controlled fault injection.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Health model"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B03-005","D22-B08-002"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Failure-detection threshold analysis"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B03-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B03","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"retry_engineering","subtopic":"transient_failures","concept":"Retries are appropriate only when the failure is plausibly transient and repeating the operation is safe or explicitly controlled.","knowledge_type":"reliability_rule","question":"When should an operation be retried?","answer":"Retry when evidence indicates the fault may be transient, the operation can safely be repeated or is protected by idempotency, and the retry remains within the caller's time and resource budget.","explanation":"Retries are a recovery mechanism, not a universal response to errors. Retrying permanent failures or unsafe side effects can worsen reliability.","principle":"Retry only when expected recovery value exceeds the additional load and correctness risk.","examples":["Transient connection interruption during an idempotent read.","Temporary service unavailability with a bounded retry policy."],"invalid_examples":["Retry authentication failures indefinitely.","Retry a non-idempotent mutation after an ambiguous timeout without reconciliation."],"problem":"Incorrect retries can amplify load or duplicate side effects.","failure":"Retry activity worsens an outage or corrupts application state.","cause":"No distinction between transient and permanent errors or unsafe operation semantics.","solution":"Classify retryable failures and couple retries with idempotency, deadlines, and bounded retry policy.","alternatives":["Fail fast","Circuit breaker","Queue for later processing"],"tradeoffs":["Retries can improve availability but consume additional capacity and increase latency."],"constraints":["Retry count and total retry time must be bounded."],"edge_cases":["A server may return a retryable response while remaining globally overloaded.","An operation can be technically idempotent but expensive enough that retries are undesirable."],"security_implications":"Retries must not bypass authorization or replay sensitive operations unexpectedly.","performance_implications":"Retries increase offered load and can worsen saturation.","testing":"Inject transient and permanent failures separately and verify different retry behavior.","verification":"Confirm retry attempts stop according to policy and that successful recovery produces the intended final state.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure classification","Operation semantics"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B03-002","D22-B04-001"],"contrasts_with":["D22-B03-003"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_architecture_guidance","source":"Azure transient fault handling guidance","source_version":"2026"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B03-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B03","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"retry_engineering","subtopic":"bounded_retries","concept":"Retry policies should have explicit bounds because repeated attempts can consume resources indefinitely and prevent a failing dependency from recovering.","knowledge_type":"implementation_rule","question":"Why must retries be bounded?","answer":"A bounded retry policy limits the amount of additional work generated by a failing operation and gives the system a deterministic point at which failure is returned or delegated to another mechanism.","explanation":"Per-request limits and aggregate retry budgets can both be necessary because many concurrent callers can collectively generate excessive retries even when each caller follows its own limit.","principle":"Retry behavior must be bounded both locally and, where needed, globally.","examples":["Maximum attempts per operation.","Maximum retry duration.","Service-level retry budget for a dependency."],"invalid_examples":["Retry until success with no deadline.","Allow every caller three retries without considering aggregate retry volume."],"problem":"Concurrent retries can overwhelm an already failing dependency.","failure":"Retry storm or cascading failure.","cause":"Unbounded or independently multiplied retry behavior.","solution":"Use finite attempts, total retry time, and aggregate retry budgets appropriate to system scale.","alternatives":["Circuit breaker","Queue-based deferred processing","Fail-fast behavior"],"tradeoffs":["Lower retry limits can reduce eventual success for transient faults.","Higher limits increase load and latency."],"constraints":["Retry budget must be consistent with service capacity and caller deadlines."],"edge_cases":["A dependency can recover shortly after the retry budget expires.","Multiple service tiers may each introduce retries."],"security_implications":"Retry mechanisms must not become an amplification vector against downstream systems.","performance_implications":"Retries directly increase work and traffic.","testing":"Simulate large concurrent populations against an unavailable dependency and measure aggregate retry volume.","verification":"Verify that total retry activity remains within the configured policy during sustained failure.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Retry classification"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B03-003","D22-B04-001"],"related_to":["D22-B01-005"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_architecture_guidance","source":"Azure transient fault handling and retry budget guidance","source_version":"2026"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B03-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B03","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"retry_engineering","subtopic":"exponential_backoff_jitter","concept":"Exponential backoff increases spacing between repeated attempts, while randomization reduces synchronization among many clients retrying simultaneously.","knowledge_type":"implementation_pattern","question":"Why combine exponential backoff with jitter?","answer":"Without backoff, retries can repeatedly hit an unhealthy dependency at high frequency. Without jitter, many clients can retry at the same time, creating synchronized bursts. Combining both reduces retry amplification and synchronization.","explanation":"Retry timing is part of the reliability design because the aggregate behavior of many clients can differ dramatically from one client's retry behavior.","principle":"Distributed retries should avoid synchronized repeated load.","examples":["Increase delay after successive failures and randomize each delay within a bounded range.","Honor an explicit server retry-after signal when applicable."],"invalid_examples":["All clients retry exactly one second after every failure.","Use immediate retries repeatedly during a dependency outage."],"problem":"Synchronized retries can create thundering-herd behavior.","failure":"Dependency remains overloaded even after partial recovery.","cause":"Synchronous retry schedules or insufficient backoff.","solution":"Use exponential backoff, randomized jitter, retry budgets, and appropriate server-provided retry timing.","alternatives":["Server-side queueing","Circuit breaker","Client-side throttling"],"tradeoffs":["Longer delays reduce load but can increase recovery latency for individual requests."],"constraints":["Backoff must remain within the operation's overall deadline.","Retry timing must not violate explicit protocol guidance."],"edge_cases":["Very short-lived transient faults may not require large delays.","A server-provided retry-after value may supersede client timing policy."],"security_implications":"Retry amplification can be abused as a denial-of-service vector.","performance_implications":"Backoff trades request latency for lower aggregate load.","testing":"Run many concurrent clients against a deliberately failing dependency and inspect retry timing distribution.","verification":"Verify that retries are bounded, randomized, and reduce synchronized load during failure.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Bounded retries"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B03-004","D22-B04-001"],"related_to":["D22-B09-002"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_reliability_guidance","source":"Google SRE cascading-failure guidance; Azure transient-fault guidance","source_version":"Current retrieved guidance"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B03-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B03","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"retry_engineering","subtopic":"retry_budget","concept":"An aggregate retry budget limits the total additional retry load generated by a service rather than only limiting attempts within individual requests.","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"Why can per-request retry limits be insufficient?","answer":"If thousands of requests each perform a small number of retries, the aggregate retry traffic can still overwhelm a dependency. A service-level retry budget constrains the combined retry load.","explanation":"Reliability must consider population-level behavior, not just the correctness of one request's retry loop.","principle":"Control retry amplification at the aggregate service boundary.","examples":["Limit retries against a dependency to a defined proportion of total requests.","Stop retrying when a service-wide retry budget is exhausted."],"invalid_examples":["Allow every request three retries regardless of total concurrent failures.","Increase retries automatically as dependency failure rate rises."],"problem":"Distributed retry multiplication can transform a moderate fault into a cascading outage.","failure":"Retry traffic consumes capacity needed for successful recovery.","cause":"No aggregate retry control.","solution":"Track and enforce retry budgets across callers or dependency boundaries.","alternatives":["Circuit breaker","Global throttling","Dependency-specific admission control"],"tradeoffs":["Strict budgets can reduce successful recovery during genuine transient failures.","Global coordination adds implementation complexity."],"constraints":["Budget policy should account for dependency capacity and request criticality."],"edge_cases":["Critical operations may need a separate retry class.","A single dependency can have multiple independently scalable partitions."],"security_implications":"Aggregate retry limits reduce the potential for accidental or malicious request amplification.","performance_implications":"Budget enforcement limits excess work but may reduce short-term success probability during transient faults.","testing":"Generate widespread dependency failures and verify that aggregate retry traffic remains bounded.","verification":"Measure retry-to-original-request ratio and confirm enforcement under concurrent failure.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Bounded retries","Retry classification"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B03-002","D22-B04-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_architecture_guidance","source":"Azure retry budget guidance","source_version":"2026"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B03-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B03","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"retry_engineering","subtopic":"retry_anti_patterns","concept":"Immediate repeated retries, infinite retries, retries on permanent failures, and retries without idempotency can convert local errors into reliability failures.","knowledge_type":"anti_pattern","question":"Which retry behaviors commonly reduce reliability?","answer":"Immediate repeated retries, unlimited retries, retrying permanent errors, retrying non-idempotent operations after ambiguous outcomes, and independently retrying across multiple service layers are common reliability hazards.","explanation":"Retry logic must be treated as distributed control behavior rather than as a simple local loop.","principle":"A retry is additional work and must have a reason, bound, and correctness model.","examples":["A client retries a transient connection failure with bounded exponential backoff.","A service stops retrying after its dependency retry budget is exhausted."],"invalid_examples":["while true: retry()","Retry authentication failures repeatedly.","Three service layers each perform independent retry loops."],"problem":"Nested or uncontrolled retry policies multiply traffic and latency.","failure":"Retry storm, cascading overload, duplicate side effects, or excessive request latency.","cause":"Retry policy designed locally without dependency-wide reasoning.","solution":"Centralize or coordinate retry policy, classify errors, enforce budgets, and define idempotency semantics.","alternatives":["Circuit breaker","Deferred queue","Fail-fast"],"tradeoffs":["Centralized policy improves consistency but may reduce local flexibility."],"constraints":["Every retryable operation needs explicit retry eligibility and termination conditions."],"edge_cases":["A lower layer may already retry transparently.","Some SDKs may introduce implicit retries."],"security_implications":"Repeated sensitive operations can create unintended side effects or amplification.","performance_implications":"Retries increase CPU, network, dependency load, and latency.","testing":"Inspect nested service paths and intentionally fail dependencies to reveal retry multiplication.","verification":"Verify that one logical request has a bounded and explainable maximum retry envelope.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Retry classification","Retry budgets"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B03-001","D22-B03-004"],"contrasts_with":["D22-B03-003"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_reliability_guidance","source":"Google SRE cascading failures; Azure Retry Storm guidance","source_version":"Current retrieved guidance"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B04-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B04","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"circuit_breakers","subtopic":"purpose","concept":"A circuit breaker prevents repeated calls to a dependency that is likely to continue failing and provides a controlled path for recovery detection.","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"What problem does a circuit breaker solve?","answer":"A circuit breaker stops repeatedly sending requests to a failing dependency after configured failure evidence, allowing the dependency and caller to avoid unnecessary work while providing a controlled mechanism for testing recovery.","explanation":"A circuit breaker complements retries: retries address plausible transient failures, while the breaker limits repeated attempts when failure persists.","principle":"Stop spending resources on operations whose recent evidence indicates a low probability of immediate success.","examples":["Open the circuit after a failure threshold and reject protected calls quickly.","Use a half-open state to test recovery before restoring normal traffic."],"invalid_examples":["Use a circuit breaker as proof that the dependency has recovered.","Open the breaker permanently after one transient error."],"problem":"Repeated dependency calls can consume caller resources and overload the failing dependency.","failure":"Persistent dependency failure propagates into caller resource exhaustion.","cause":"No failure-aware admission control around the dependency.","solution":"Implement closed, open, and controlled recovery states with appropriate thresholds and observability.","alternatives":["Bulkhead","Rate limiting","Dependency-specific admission control"],"tradeoffs":["Aggressive opening can reject requests unnecessarily.","Slow recovery probing can delay restoration."],"constraints":["Thresholds and recovery intervals must match dependency failure and recovery characteristics."],"edge_cases":["Different dependency partitions may fail independently.","A global breaker may unnecessarily block healthy partitions."],"security_implications":"Breaker state can influence availability and should not be trivially controllable by untrusted input.","performance_implications":"Open-state rejection can avoid expensive timeout paths.","testing":"Inject persistent and intermittent dependency failures and verify state transitions.","verification":"Verify that breaker state reflects observed failure evidence and that recovery is tested before normal traffic resumes.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure classification","Retry engineering"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B04-002","D22-B04-003"],"contrasts_with":["D22-B03-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_architecture_guidance","source":"Microsoft Circuit Breaker pattern","source_version":"2025"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B04-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B04","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"circuit_breakers","subtopic":"state_machine","concept":"A circuit breaker should model its state transitions explicitly because closed, open, and recovery-testing states have different request behavior.","knowledge_type":"state_model","question":"Why should a circuit breaker be modeled as a state machine?","answer":"Explicit states make request admission and recovery behavior deterministic: normal traffic is permitted in the closed state, protected calls are rejected or redirected in the open state, and limited recovery attempts occur in a half-open or equivalent state.","explanation":"Stateful reliability controls become easier to reason about and test when transitions are explicit rather than hidden inside counters and timers.","principle":"Represent resilience control state explicitly when state changes alter system behavior.","examples":["Closed → Open after configured failure evidence.","Open → Half-Open after recovery interval.","Half-Open → Closed after successful recovery evidence.","Half-Open → Open after failed recovery evidence."],"invalid_examples":["Allow unrestricted traffic immediately after the open timer expires.","Mix state transitions across threads without defining concurrency semantics."],"problem":"Ambiguous breaker transitions can create request bursts or inconsistent recovery behavior.","failure":"Breaker flapping or premature recovery.","cause":"Undefined state transition semantics.","solution":"Define states, transition conditions, admission behavior, concurrency rules, and reset semantics.","alternatives":["Token-based admission control","Dependency health gating"],"tradeoffs":["Explicit state management adds complexity but makes behavior auditable."],"constraints":["Concurrent callers must observe consistent breaker behavior.","State persistence requirements must be defined for process restart."],"edge_cases":["A process restart may reset breaker state while the dependency remains unhealthy.","Multiple instances may need independent or coordinated breaker state."],"security_implications":"Administrative breaker overrides must be authenticated and audited.","performance_implications":"Breaker state evaluation should be inexpensive on the request path.","testing":"Test every transition, repeated failures, concurrent callers, restart behavior, and recovery failure.","verification":"Validate state transitions against the defined state machine and observed dependency outcomes.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Circuit breaker purpose"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B04-001","D22-B04-004"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_architecture_guidance","source":"Microsoft Circuit Breaker state model","source_version":"2025"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B04-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B04","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"circuit_breakers","subtopic":"idempotency_interaction","concept":"Circuit breakers do not determine whether an operation is safe to retry or replay; operation semantics must be handled separately.","knowledge_type":"reliability_rule","question":"Does a circuit breaker make a non-idempotent operation safe to retry?","answer":"No. A circuit breaker controls whether calls are attempted based on failure state. It does not establish idempotency or determine whether an ambiguous operation already succeeded.","explanation":"Reliability controls have distinct responsibilities: the breaker controls repeated dependency access, while operation semantics determine whether repetition is safe.","principle":"Do not use one resilience mechanism as evidence for another correctness property.","examples":["A breaker can stop additional payment requests while payment-status reconciliation determines the original outcome.","A breaker can protect a database while transaction semantics govern retry safety."],"invalid_examples":["Assume a failed circuit means the previous mutation did not execute.","Retry every operation automatically after the circuit closes."],"problem":"Combining failure control and operation semantics incorrectly can duplicate side effects.","failure":"Duplicate business actions after dependency recovery.","cause":"No separation between transport failure and business operation state.","solution":"Use explicit idempotency/reconciliation semantics alongside circuit-breaking.","alternatives":["Idempotency keys","Operation-status endpoint","Transactional messaging"],"tradeoffs":["Explicit operation tracking increases state and implementation complexity."],"constraints":["Recovery must preserve business correctness across ambiguous outcomes."],"edge_cases":["The circuit can open after a request was accepted remotely but before the caller receives a response."],"security_implications":"Idempotency identifiers must be unguessable or access-controlled where sensitive operations are involved.","performance_implications":"Reconciliation adds additional requests or persistent state.","testing":"Open the breaker after remote acceptance but before response delivery and verify final operation uniqueness.","verification":"Verify final business state rather than merely verifying circuit state.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Unknown outcomes","Circuit breaker state model"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B01-003","D22-B04-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Circuit breaker and operation-semantics separation"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B04-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B04","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"circuit_breakers","subtopic":"breaker_scope","concept":"Circuit-breaker scope should correspond to a meaningful failure domain; one breaker covering independent resources can unnecessarily block healthy operations.","knowledge_type":"architecture_tradeoff","question":"How should circuit-breaker scope be selected?","answer":"Scope should align with the dependency failure domain being protected. If independent shards, providers, tenants, or endpoints can fail separately, a single global breaker may incorrectly reject healthy traffic.","explanation":"The breaker must encode the granularity at which failure evidence is predictive of future failure.","principle":"Breaker state should be as local as the failure evidence requires, without creating unmanageable state complexity.","examples":["Separate breaker state per independent provider.","Separate state for materially independent backend partitions when evidence supports it."],"invalid_examples":["One global breaker opens because one independent shard failed.","One breaker combines unrelated dependency operations with different failure behavior."],"problem":"Over-broad scope converts localized failure into global rejection.","failure":"Healthy operations are blocked by unrelated failures.","cause":"Breaker scope does not match failure independence.","solution":"Partition breaker state according to actual dependency failure domains.","alternatives":["Global breaker","Per-operation breaker","Per-partition breaker"],"tradeoffs":["Fine-grained state improves isolation but increases state management and monitoring complexity."],"constraints":["Partitioning requires reliable identification of independent failure domains."],"edge_cases":["Failures may correlate unexpectedly across partitions.","A highly granular breaker can become noisy or difficult to tune."],"security_implications":"Tenant-specific breaker state can prevent one tenant from triggering availability controls for others when isolation is required.","performance_implications":"More breaker state increases memory and management overhead.","testing":"Fail one dependency partition and verify that healthy partitions remain accessible.","verification":"Confirm breaker decisions correlate with the protected failure domain.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure-domain analysis","Circuit breaker state model"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B01-005","D22-B06-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_architecture_guidance","source":"Microsoft Circuit Breaker pattern resource differentiation guidance","source_version":"2025"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B04-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B04","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"circuit_breakers","subtopic":"recovery_verification","concept":"Closing a circuit breaker should follow evidence that the protected dependency can successfully perform the relevant operation, not merely the expiration of a timer.","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"Why is timer-only circuit recovery insufficient?","answer":"A timer indicates that some recovery time has elapsed but does not prove that the dependency is healthy. Controlled probing or successful operation evidence can provide stronger recovery evidence.","explanation":"Recovery state should be based on observed health when practical, while probes themselves must be bounded and safe.","principle":"Time elapsed is not equivalent to recovery verified.","examples":["Use a limited half-open request to test the protected operation.","Use a suitable health check when it meaningfully represents the protected dependency."],"invalid_examples":["Close the breaker automatically after a fixed interval regardless of dependency state.","Send a full production burst immediately after the timer expires."],"problem":"Premature closure can recreate overload or repeated failure.","failure":"Breaker oscillation between recovery and failure.","cause":"Recovery decision based solely on elapsed time.","solution":"Use controlled recovery probes and explicit success criteria.","alternatives":["Health endpoint","Synthetic operation","Limited canary traffic"],"tradeoffs":["Active probing adds load and may itself fail during recovery.","Longer verification reduces risk but delays restoration."],"constraints":["Recovery probes must not create harmful side effects.","Probe frequency must be bounded."],"edge_cases":["A health endpoint can report healthy while the actual operation remains broken.","A dependency may recover partially before full recovery."],"security_implications":"Probes must not expose or mutate protected resources improperly.","performance_implications":"Recovery probes consume dependency capacity.","testing":"Simulate delayed recovery and verify that the breaker remains open until meaningful recovery evidence exists.","verification":"Require successful recovery evidence before returning to normal request admission.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Circuit breaker state machine"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B02-003","D22-B04-002"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_architecture_guidance","source":"Microsoft Circuit Breaker recovery guidance","source_version":"2025"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B05-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B05","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"graceful_degradation","subtopic":"degradation_strategy","concept":"Graceful degradation preserves essential functionality by intentionally reducing or disabling non-critical functionality when required resources are unavailable.","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"What is graceful degradation?","answer":"Graceful degradation is the deliberate reduction of optional functionality, fidelity, or capacity while preserving higher-priority functionality during constrained or failed conditions.","explanation":"Degradation should be designed as an explicit operating mode rather than emerging accidentally from resource exhaustion.","principle":"Preserve critical service objectives before optional functionality during partial failure.","examples":["Serve cached information when an optional origin is unavailable.","Disable non-essential enrichment while preserving the primary transaction.","Reduce expensive background processing during resource pressure."],"invalid_examples":["Allow resource exhaustion to randomly determine which requests fail.","Hide dependency failures by returning fabricated data."],"problem":"Optional work can consume resources needed for essential work during failure.","failure":"Critical functionality becomes unavailable because the system attempts to preserve everything equally.","cause":"No prioritization or degradation policy.","solution":"Define criticality classes and explicit degraded modes with correctness-preserving fallbacks.","alternatives":["Load shedding","Feature flags","Read-only mode"],"tradeoffs":["Reduced functionality can affect user experience or data freshness.","More degradation modes increase testing complexity."],"constraints":["Fallback behavior must remain semantically correct.","Degraded responses must not be presented as fully equivalent when they are not."],"edge_cases":["Cached data may be stale.","Some operations cannot safely degrade because partial execution changes business state."],"security_implications":"Fallback paths must preserve authorization and data-isolation rules.","performance_implications":"Degradation can reduce resource consumption and stabilize critical paths.","testing":"Disable dependencies and verify each degraded mode independently.","verification":"Confirm that essential functionality remains correct and degraded behavior is explicitly observable.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure classification","Criticality model"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B05-002","D22-B08-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_reliability_guidance","source":"Google SRE cascading-failure and load-shedding guidance; Microsoft reliability patterns","source_version":"Current retrieved guidance"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B05-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B05","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"graceful_degradation","subtopic":"fallback_correctness","concept":"A fallback is reliable only when its semantics remain correct for the condition under which it is used.","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"What makes a degradation fallback safe?","answer":"A fallback must define what information or functionality it can safely provide, what freshness or completeness limitations apply, and which conditions make it invalid.","explanation":"Returning something is not equivalent to recovering service. An incorrect fallback can be worse than an explicit failure.","principle":"Degraded behavior must preserve correctness boundaries, not merely availability appearance.","examples":["Serve explicitly marked stale data when business rules permit stale reads.","Return a reduced response that omits optional enrichment while preserving required fields."],"invalid_examples":["Return cached authorization decisions without validating their allowed freshness.","Return an empty successful result when the real state is unknown."],"problem":"Fallbacks can hide correctness failures behind successful responses.","failure":"Users or downstream systems act on invalid or misleading degraded data.","cause":"Fallback designed for availability without semantic validation.","solution":"Define fallback validity conditions, freshness limits, and explicit response semantics.","alternatives":["Explicit failure","Read-only mode","Partial response with status metadata"],"tradeoffs":["Strict fallback conditions may reduce availability.","More metadata can increase response complexity."],"constraints":["Fallback must not violate business invariants or security controls."],"edge_cases":["The primary dependency can return stale data rather than being completely unavailable.","Different consumers may have different tolerance for degraded data."],"security_implications":"Fallbacks must not bypass authentication, authorization, or tenant isolation.","performance_implications":"Fallback paths should be cheaper than the primary path when they are intended to protect capacity.","testing":"Test stale, missing, and contradictory fallback data conditions.","verification":"Validate both functional correctness and explicit degraded-state signaling.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Graceful degradation"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B05-001","D22-B09-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Fallback correctness and degraded-mode validation"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B05-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B05","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"graceful_degradation","subtopic":"criticality_ordering","concept":"Degraded-mode behavior should prioritize operations according to explicit criticality rather than allowing arbitrary resource exhaustion to determine outcomes.","knowledge_type":"architecture_rule","question":"How should functionality be prioritized during degradation?","answer":"Classify operations by business and technical criticality, then preserve higher-priority operations while reducing, delaying, or rejecting lower-priority work when capacity is constrained.","explanation":"Priority ordering makes degraded behavior deterministic and allows reliability objectives to reflect actual system importance.","principle":"Criticality must be explicit before failure occurs.","examples":["Preserve authentication and core transactions while disabling optional analytics.","Protect emergency or safety-critical workflows from background workloads."],"invalid_examples":["Let whichever request arrives first consume all remaining resources.","Give optional batch processing equal priority to interactive critical operations."],"problem":"Unprioritized overload can exhaust capacity needed for essential operations.","failure":"Critical functionality fails because lower-value work consumes resources.","cause":"No workload criticality model.","solution":"Define priority classes, resource reservations, and degradation behavior for each class.","alternatives":["Bulkheads","Priority queues","Admission control"],"tradeoffs":["Priority enforcement can reduce fairness for lower-priority workloads.","Reserved capacity can reduce average utilization."],"constraints":["Priority rules must be deterministic and auditable.","Criticality cannot bypass correctness or security requirements."],"edge_cases":["A supposedly low-priority operation may become critical during an incident.","Priority inheritance may be required across dependent services."],"security_implications":"Priority must not allow untrusted users to self-classify requests as critical.","performance_implications":"Priority controls can preserve latency for protected classes during overload.","testing":"Generate mixed-priority load beyond capacity and verify policy enforcement.","verification":"Confirm protected classes retain their defined service behavior while lower classes degrade according to policy.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure criticality model"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B05-001","D22-B06-003"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Criticality-based degradation"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B05-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B05","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"graceful_degradation","subtopic":"read_only_mode","concept":"A read-only operating mode can preserve visibility and selected user functions when mutation dependencies are unavailable, provided stale or incomplete data semantics are explicit.","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"When can read-only mode improve reliability?","answer":"Read-only mode can preserve safe retrieval and visibility when write paths are unavailable, but only if reads remain semantically valid and users are clearly informed about unavailable mutations or freshness limitations.","explanation":"Reducing the system's operation set can preserve useful service without pretending that unavailable state-changing operations succeeded.","principle":"Reduce operation scope before compromising correctness.","examples":["Allow users to inspect previously committed records while writes are temporarily disabled.","Expose system status while transaction processing is paused."],"invalid_examples":["Accept writes and silently discard them.","Show stale state as current when freshness is required."],"problem":"Mutation dependencies can fail while users still need access to existing state.","failure":"Entire service becomes unavailable even though safe read operations remain possible.","cause":"No explicitly designed degraded mode.","solution":"Define read-only behavior, freshness rules, and mutation rejection semantics.","alternatives":["Maintenance mode","Cached-read mode","Queue mutations for later"],"tradeoffs":["Queued mutations add complexity and may create delayed outcomes.","Read-only mode cannot support workflows requiring immediate state changes."],"constraints":["Read data must meet defined consistency and authorization requirements."],"edge_cases":["A read may indirectly depend on the failed mutation subsystem.","Queued writes can become invalid before replay."],"security_implications":"Read-only mode must retain all authorization and tenant isolation rules.","performance_implications":"Read-only fallback may reduce dependency pressure.","testing":"Disable mutation dependencies and verify read behavior and explicit write rejection.","verification":"Confirm no write is falsely reported as successful during read-only operation.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Graceful degradation","Fallback correctness"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B05-002","D22-B08-003"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Read-only degraded operation"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B05-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B05","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"graceful_degradation","subtopic":"degradation_exit","concept":"A system should have explicit criteria for leaving degraded mode so that recovery does not depend on an operator remembering to restore normal functionality.","knowledge_type":"recovery_rule","question":"How should a system exit degraded mode?","answer":"Degraded mode should end only after the conditions that caused it have been sufficiently recovered and the restored functionality has passed appropriate health or verification checks.","explanation":"Entering degraded mode is only half of resilience; indefinite degraded operation creates a hidden reliability failure.","principle":"Every automated degraded mode requires an explicit and verifiable recovery path.","examples":["Restore optional dependency traffic after controlled health verification.","Re-enable background processing gradually after capacity stabilizes."],"invalid_examples":["Re-enable everything immediately after the first successful health check.","Leave disabled features dependent on manual memory when automatic recovery is expected."],"problem":"Premature restoration can recreate the original failure.","failure":"Recovery oscillation or repeated degradation.","cause":"No recovery verification or staged restoration.","solution":"Define exit conditions, verification evidence, and staged restoration where necessary.","alternatives":["Canary recovery","Half-open validation","Operator-approved recovery"],"tradeoffs":["Conservative restoration delays full functionality.","Staged restoration requires additional state and monitoring."],"constraints":["Recovery criteria must be based on meaningful evidence.","Critical safety conditions may require manual approval."],"edge_cases":["Dependency health can fluctuate rapidly.","Only part of the dependency may have recovered."],"security_implications":"Restoration must not bypass security controls disabled during failure handling.","performance_implications":"Gradual restoration can prevent recovery spikes.","testing":"Repeatedly alternate dependency failure and recovery and verify stable mode transitions.","verification":"Require post-restoration health evidence before declaring normal mode restored.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Degradation strategy","Health model"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B04-005","D22-B08-005"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Degraded-mode recovery verification"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B06-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B06","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"fault_isolation","subtopic":"bulkheads","concept":"Bulkhead isolation partitions resources or workloads so that failure or overload in one partition does not consume all capacity available to unrelated workloads.","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"What is a bulkhead in reliability engineering?","answer":"A bulkhead is an intentional resource or workload partition that limits the blast radius of failure or overload.","explanation":"Without isolation, one failing dependency or noisy workload can exhaust shared pools and prevent unrelated operations from proceeding.","principle":"Protect independent workloads with bounded shared-resource exposure.","examples":["Separate connection pools for critical and optional dependencies.","Partition workers by tenant or workload class.","Use independent service instances for different consumer groups."],"invalid_examples":["One unlimited worker pool serves unrelated criticality classes.","One connection pool can be exhausted by a single failing dependency."],"problem":"Shared resource exhaustion can propagate localized faults.","failure":"One workload consumes resources required by other workloads.","cause":"Unbounded shared resource pools.","solution":"Partition capacity according to failure and criticality boundaries.","alternatives":["Cell architecture","Per-tenant pools","Dedicated worker groups"],"tradeoffs":["Isolation can increase idle capacity and operational cost.","Too many partitions can fragment resources."],"constraints":["Partition boundaries must correspond to meaningful failure or workload domains."],"edge_cases":["A workload may legitimately need resources from multiple partitions.","A shared lower-level dependency can still create common-mode failure."],"security_implications":"Tenant partitions can reduce cross-tenant blast radius.","performance_implications":"Isolation may reduce pooling efficiency but improve predictable behavior under failure.","testing":"Exhaust one partition while exercising unaffected partitions.","verification":"Verify that the failed partition cannot consume protected capacity.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure-domain analysis","Criticality ordering"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B06-002","D22-B06-003"],"related_to":["D22-B01-005"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_architecture_guidance","source":"Microsoft Bulkhead pattern","source_version":"2026"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B06-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B06","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"fault_isolation","subtopic":"process_isolation","concept":"Separating workloads into processes or independently managed execution units can prevent memory corruption, crashes, or resource exhaustion in one workload from directly terminating unrelated workloads.","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"When does process-level isolation improve reliability?","answer":"Process isolation can bound certain classes of failure by giving workloads separate address spaces and independent lifecycle control.","explanation":"Isolation is useful when a workload can crash, leak memory, consume CPU, or otherwise destabilize its execution environment.","principle":"Use isolation boundaries where failure consequences justify the operational and resource overhead.","examples":["Run untrusted plugins in separate processes.","Separate independently critical workers into distinct process groups."],"invalid_examples":["Assume separate processes guarantee independence when they share one exhausted host resource.","Use process isolation as a substitute for application-level correctness."],"problem":"A single process can expose all co-located functionality to the same crash or memory-corruption event.","failure":"One workload failure terminates or destabilizes unrelated workloads.","cause":"Insufficient execution isolation.","solution":"Separate workloads where the failure model justifies it and also account for shared host dependencies.","alternatives":["Containers","Separate hosts","Sandboxing"],"tradeoffs":["Isolation costs CPU, memory, startup time, and operational complexity."],"constraints":["Shared host resources can remain common failure points.","Inter-process communication must have defined failure semantics."],"edge_cases":["Host kernel failure can affect all processes.","A process can remain alive while its internal workload is unhealthy."],"security_implications":"Process isolation can reduce the impact of untrusted code but is not automatically a complete security boundary.","performance_implications":"IPC and process lifecycle overhead may increase latency.","testing":"Crash or exhaust resources in one process and verify independent workload continuity.","verification":"Confirm that the failure does not terminate or materially degrade protected workloads beyond defined limits.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Bulkhead principles"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B06-001","D22-B07-002"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"systems_reliability_methodology","source":"Process isolation and fault containment"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B06-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B06","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"fault_isolation","subtopic":"tenant_isolation","concept":"Multi-tenant systems require resource and failure isolation strong enough to prevent one tenant's workload or fault from exhausting capacity needed by other tenants.","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"Why is tenant isolation a reliability concern?","answer":"A noisy or failing tenant can consume shared compute, connection, queue, storage, or concurrency resources and reduce availability for other tenants unless capacity is bounded or partitioned.","explanation":"Reliability and fairness intersect when multiple consumers share finite resources.","principle":"Shared resources must have explicit per-tenant or class-level limits when cross-tenant exhaustion is unacceptable.","examples":["Per-tenant concurrency limits.","Tenant-specific queues or rate budgets.","Partitioned workers for high-criticality tenants."],"invalid_examples":["Allow one tenant unlimited queue growth.","Assume global capacity guarantees tenant-level availability."],"problem":"One tenant's workload can create a localized denial of service for others.","failure":"Cross-tenant availability degradation.","cause":"Unbounded shared resource consumption.","solution":"Apply quotas, bulkheads, admission control, or dedicated capacity according to requirements.","alternatives":["Weighted fair scheduling","Dedicated instances","Tenant-level rate limits"],"tradeoffs":["Isolation can reduce aggregate utilization and increase cost.","Fine-grained quotas require policy management."],"constraints":["Tenant isolation must preserve correctness and security boundaries.","Limits should reflect contractual or business requirements."],"edge_cases":["A tenant can legitimately experience bursty workloads.","Some operations require shared global resources."],"security_implications":"Reliability isolation must complement tenant data and authorization isolation.","performance_implications":"Per-tenant controls improve predictability but can reduce pooling efficiency.","testing":"Generate extreme load from one tenant while measuring other tenants' service behavior.","verification":"Verify that protected tenants remain within their defined reliability envelope.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Bulkhead isolation","Criticality model"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B06-001","D22-B09-003"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Multi-tenant failure isolation"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B06-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B06","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"fault_isolation","subtopic":"dependency_isolation","concept":"A failing dependency should not consume unlimited caller resources while requests wait for unavailable responses.","knowledge_type":"reliability_pattern","question":"How should dependency access be isolated?","answer":"Dependency calls should be bounded through timeouts, concurrency limits, circuit breakers, bulkheads, or equivalent controls so that dependency failure cannot consume all caller capacity.","explanation":"Dependency failure becomes a caller reliability problem when blocked requests accumulate faster than they complete or fail.","principle":"Bound the amount of caller capacity exposed to each dependency.","examples":["Separate connection or worker pools by critical dependency.","Limit concurrent calls to a slow dependency."],"invalid_examples":["Allow every request to synchronously wait on an unavailable dependency.","Use one global pool for all remote calls."],"problem":"Dependency failure can exhaust threads, tasks, connections, memory, or queues.","failure":"Caller becomes unavailable even though its own core logic is healthy.","cause":"Unbounded dependency resource consumption.","solution":"Apply bounded concurrency and failure-aware access controls.","alternatives":["Asynchronous queueing","Circuit breaker","Fallback"],"tradeoffs":["Strict limits can reduce throughput when the dependency is healthy.","More isolation creates configuration complexity."],"constraints":["Limits must align with dependency capacity and business criticality."],"edge_cases":["Dependency latency may fluctuate rather than fail completely.","One dependency may serve several operations with different criticality."],"security_implications":"Dependency isolation reduces blast radius of compromised or abusive upstream/downstream actors.","performance_implications":"Bounded concurrency can improve stability at the cost of some peak utilization.","testing":"Make a dependency slow and unavailable while maintaining normal caller traffic.","verification":"Verify caller capacity remains available and dependency-specific resource usage stays bounded.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Bulkhead isolation","Circuit breaker"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B04-001","D22-B06-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_architecture_guidance","source":"Microsoft Bulkhead and Circuit Breaker patterns","source_version":"2025-2026"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B06-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B06","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"fault_isolation","subtopic":"blast_radius","concept":"Blast radius is the amount of functionality, capacity, data, tenants, or geographic scope affected by one failure or recovery action.","knowledge_type":"reliability_principle","question":"Why should blast radius be an explicit reliability design property?","answer":"A failure that cannot be prevented can still be made less damaging by limiting how far it propagates.","explanation":"Blast-radius analysis connects architecture boundaries with practical outage impact and helps prioritize isolation mechanisms.","principle":"Design failure containment before assuming failures can always be prevented.","examples":["One service cell fails while other cells remain available.","One tenant's quota exhaustion does not consume another tenant's capacity."],"invalid_examples":["Deploy all replicas into one shared failure domain.","Use global recovery actions for localized failures without assessing impact."],"problem":"Localized faults become system-wide outages when boundaries are weak.","failure":"Large-scale service disruption from a small initiating fault.","cause":"Shared resources, common dependencies, or global state.","solution":"Partition failure domains and constrain recovery actions to the smallest safe scope.","alternatives":["Cells","Bulkheads","Multi-zone deployment"],"tradeoffs":["Smaller blast radius often requires additional capacity and operational complexity."],"constraints":["Isolation boundaries must preserve required cross-component functionality."],"edge_cases":["A common control plane may still create broad correlated failure.","Global configuration changes can bypass otherwise strong isolation."],"security_implications":"Blast-radius reduction limits the scope of security incidents and misconfiguration.","performance_implications":"Partitioning can increase resource overhead.","testing":"Inject failures at progressively larger scopes and measure affected functionality.","verification":"Confirm that the observed impact remains within the designed failure boundary.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure-domain analysis","Bulkhead isolation"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B01-005","D22-B06-001","D22-B07-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_architecture_guidance","source":"Microsoft self-preservation and reliability design guidance","source_version":"2026"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B07-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B07","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"redundancy","subtopic":"independence","concept":"Redundancy improves reliability only when redundant elements have sufficiently independent failure modes.","knowledge_type":"architecture_principle","question":"When does redundancy actually improve reliability?","answer":"Redundancy improves reliability when multiple components can independently continue providing required functionality and do not share a dominant failure cause.","explanation":"Replica count alone does not establish reliability; independence of failure domains is the critical property.","principle":"Measure redundancy by independent survival capacity, not replica count.","examples":["Instances distributed across independent availability zones.","Separate workers using independently bounded resources."],"invalid_examples":["Replicas on the same failing host.","Multiple copies sharing one unavailable dependency."],"problem":"Apparent redundancy can fail simultaneously.","failure":"All replicas become unavailable from one common fault.","cause":"Shared infrastructure, configuration, dependency, or control plane.","solution":"Analyze shared dependencies and distribute replicas across meaningful failure domains.","alternatives":["Zone redundancy","Regional redundancy","Independent cells"],"tradeoffs":["More independent redundancy increases cost and operational complexity."],"constraints":["Independence must be supported by actual infrastructure topology and dependency evidence."],"edge_cases":["Control-plane failures can correlate otherwise independent data-plane replicas.","A software defect can affect every replica simultaneously."],"security_implications":"Shared credentials and configuration can create common-mode security failure.","performance_implications":"Cross-zone or cross-region redundancy can increase latency and synchronization costs.","testing":"Exercise representative shared and independent failure scenarios.","verification":"Document and validate the failure domains protected by each redundancy layer.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Common-mode failure"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B07-002","D22-B07-003"],"related_to":["D22-B01-005"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_architecture_guidance","source":"Microsoft reliability design principles","source_version":"2026"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B07-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B07","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"redundancy","subtopic":"active_active","concept":"Active-active redundancy keeps multiple capacity units serving traffic simultaneously and can reduce recovery dependence on a cold standby, but it introduces state synchronization and traffic-distribution complexity.","knowledge_type":"architecture_tradeoff","question":"What are the reliability tradeoffs of active-active deployment?","answer":"Active-active deployments can continue serving traffic when one unit fails, provided traffic can be redirected and required state remains available. They also increase complexity around consistency, routing, capacity, and correlated failures.","explanation":"Active-active improves immediate failover capability but does not automatically solve shared-state or common-mode failures.","principle":"Active-active resilience depends on independent capacity and correct state behavior, not merely duplicate instances.","examples":["Multiple regions actively serving requests with replicated state.","Multiple service cells serving independent traffic partitions."],"invalid_examples":["Run two active instances that both depend on one unavailable state store.","Assume active-active eliminates the need for failure testing."],"problem":"State and routing dependencies can invalidate active-active assumptions.","failure":"Both active units become unable to serve correctly during a shared dependency failure.","cause":"Insufficient state independence or shared failure domain.","solution":"Identify state dependencies, routing behavior, and failure boundaries explicitly.","alternatives":["Active-passive","Warm standby","Cell-based redundancy"],"tradeoffs":["Active-active can improve utilization and failover speed but increases consistency and operational complexity."],"constraints":["Traffic routing and state semantics must remain correct during partial failure."],"edge_cases":["Network partitions can produce split-brain behavior.","Asynchronous replication can produce stale state."],"security_implications":"Cross-region or cross-cell replication requires secure data transport and authorization.","performance_implications":"Replication and cross-region communication can add latency and resource cost.","testing":"Fail one active unit and separately fail shared dependencies while observing traffic and state correctness.","verification":"Verify continued service and correct state semantics under the intended failure scenarios.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Redundancy independence"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B07-003","D22-B08-004"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_architecture_methodology","source":"Active-active redundancy analysis"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B07-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B07","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"redundancy","subtopic":"active_passive","concept":"Active-passive redundancy uses a primary capacity unit and a standby that assumes responsibility after a failure, requiring reliable failure detection and promotion.","knowledge_type":"architecture_tradeoff","question":"What reliability properties are required for active-passive failover?","answer":"Active-passive systems require reliable failure detection, safe standby readiness, controlled promotion, state availability, and prevention of both primary and standby simultaneously accepting conflicting ownership.","explanation":"Failover is a state transition and must be treated as such rather than as a simple routing change.","principle":"A standby is useful only if it can safely assume responsibility when required.","examples":["A standby service becomes active after verified primary failure.","A database replica is promoted according to explicit ownership rules."],"invalid_examples":["Promote a standby based only on one noisy health signal.","Allow both primary and standby to accept conflicting writes during partition."],"problem":"Incorrect failover can create split-brain, stale state, or dual ownership.","failure":"Two components act as primary simultaneously or the standby cannot serve after promotion.","cause":"Ambiguous ownership or insufficient readiness.","solution":"Define ownership, fencing, state synchronization, promotion criteria, and post-promotion verification.","alternatives":["Active-active","Warm standby","Cold standby"],"tradeoffs":["Active-passive can simplify state management but may leave standby capacity underutilized."],"constraints":["Promotion must preserve data and ownership correctness."],"edge_cases":["Network partition can make both sides believe the other has failed.","Standby may be alive but missing required state or dependencies."],"security_implications":"Failover credentials and ownership transitions must be protected and audited.","performance_implications":"Standby readiness and state replication consume resources.","testing":"Simulate primary failure, network partition, stale standby state, and recovery of the old primary.","verification":"Verify single-owner behavior and correct service after promotion.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Health model","Failure-domain analysis"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B07-002","D22-B08-004"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_architecture_methodology","source":"Active-passive failover analysis"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B07-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B07","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"redundancy","subtopic":"fencing","concept":"Fencing prevents an old or isolated component from continuing to act as an active owner after failover.","knowledge_type":"safety_mechanism","question":"Why can fencing be necessary during failover?","answer":"A component that cannot observe the current system state may continue accepting work after another component has been promoted, causing conflicting writes or duplicate ownership. Fencing prevents the stale component from acting as active.","explanation":"Failure detection alone cannot always guarantee that the old primary has stopped or lost authority.","principle":"Failover ownership must be enforced, not merely inferred.","examples":["Revoke the old instance's write authority before promoting another owner.","Use infrastructure-level isolation to prevent stale ownership."],"invalid_examples":["Promote a standby while assuming the old primary has stopped because its network connection is lost.","Allow both instances to write during an uncertain partition."],"problem":"Split-brain can corrupt shared state.","failure":"Multiple active owners perform conflicting operations.","cause":"No authoritative ownership enforcement.","solution":"Use fencing, leases, epochs, tokens, or equivalent ownership mechanisms appropriate to the architecture.","alternatives":["Lease-based ownership","Epoch numbers","External quorum"],"tradeoffs":["Fencing introduces infrastructure dependencies and operational complexity."],"constraints":["The fencing mechanism itself must have strong availability and correctness properties."],"edge_cases":["The network can partition the old owner from the fencing authority.","A delayed message can arrive after ownership changes."],"security_implications":"Fencing authority is security-sensitive because it controls execution rights.","performance_implications":"Ownership validation can add coordination overhead.","testing":"Simulate network partition and delayed recovery of the old primary.","verification":"Verify that stale owners cannot perform protected operations after loss of authority.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Active-passive failover"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B07-003","D22-B08-004"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"distributed_systems_reliability","source":"Failover ownership and fencing"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B07-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B07","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"redundancy","subtopic":"replication_lag","concept":"Replicated systems may remain available while replicas are temporarily behind the authoritative state, creating a consistency-versus-availability tradeoff during failure.","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why does replication lag matter to reliability?","answer":"If a primary fails before a replica receives recent updates, failover may restore service with stale state unless the system has appropriate durability and consistency guarantees.","explanation":"Availability after failover is not sufficient; the restored system must satisfy its defined data-loss and consistency requirements.","principle":"Define acceptable recovery-point semantics for replicated state.","examples":["Asynchronous replica promotion may lose recently acknowledged writes depending on the durability model.","Synchronous replication can reduce some data-loss windows but introduces coordination costs."],"invalid_examples":["Claim zero data loss solely because replicas exist.","Promote any available replica without evaluating its state freshness."],"problem":"Failover can restore availability while silently losing or regressing state.","failure":"Recovered system violates data durability or consistency requirements.","cause":"Unspecified replication semantics or unverified replica freshness.","solution":"Define replication guarantees, recovery-point objectives, and promotion eligibility.","alternatives":["Synchronous replication","Quorum-based replication","Durable write-ahead logging"],"tradeoffs":["Stronger consistency can increase latency and reduce availability under partitions."],"constraints":["Data-loss tolerance must be explicit.","Promotion must use verifiable state criteria."],"edge_cases":["A replica can be reachable but substantially stale.","Network partitions can make freshness information uncertain."],"security_implications":"Stale authorization or policy data can create security exposure.","performance_implications":"Stronger replication guarantees can increase write latency and coordination overhead.","testing":"Introduce replication lag and fail the primary at different points in the lag window.","verification":"Measure actual recovery-point behavior against the defined durability requirement.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Redundancy model","Failure semantics"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B07-003","D22-B08-004"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"distributed_systems_reliability","source":"Replication and failover consistency analysis"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B08-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B08","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"recovery_engineering","subtopic":"recovery_state_machine","concept":"Recovery should be modeled as explicit stages such as detection, containment, remediation, validation, and restoration rather than treating an invoked recovery command as proof of recovery.","knowledge_type":"recovery_model","question":"Why should recovery be represented as a state machine?","answer":"Explicit recovery stages separate detection from action and action from verified outcome, making recovery behavior auditable and preventing premature success claims.","explanation":"A restart, failover, rollback, or reconnect is an attempted intervention. Recovery is complete only when the intended service state is verified.","principle":"Action completion and recovery completion are different states.","examples":["DETECTED → CONTAINED → REMEDIATING → VERIFYING → RECOVERED.","Rollback command completed → application health verified → traffic restored."],"invalid_examples":["Mark recovery successful immediately after issuing restart.","Restore traffic before post-recovery verification."],"problem":"Premature recovery declaration can expose users to an unhealthy component.","failure":"Repeated recovery loops or renewed outage after traffic restoration.","cause":"No explicit post-action verification state.","solution":"Model recovery stages and require evidence for each transition.","alternatives":["Runbook state machine","Workflow engine","Recovery orchestration graph"],"tradeoffs":["Stateful recovery orchestration increases implementation complexity."],"constraints":["Every transition needs defined success and failure conditions.","Recovery actions must remain idempotent or otherwise controlled."],"edge_cases":["The system can recover while the original detector remains unhealthy.","Recovery verification may depend on a separate monitoring system."],"security_implications":"Recovery actions require authorization and should be auditable.","performance_implications":"Verification adds recovery time but reduces premature restoration.","testing":"Inject failures at every recovery stage and verify correct transition behavior.","verification":"Require independent or appropriately scoped evidence before declaring RECOVERED.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure detection","Recovery evidence"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B02-004","D22-B08-002","D22-B10-003"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Evidence-driven recovery state modeling"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B08-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B08","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"recovery_engineering","subtopic":"restart_recovery","concept":"Restarting a failed component is useful only when restart is expected to remove the fault and the resulting component is verified as healthy before normal traffic is restored.","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"When is restart an appropriate reliability action?","answer":"Restart is appropriate when the failure mode is known or reasonably expected to be cleared by process replacement and the system can safely recreate required state and dependencies.","explanation":"Restart is a remediation mechanism, not a universal solution for every failure.","principle":"Choose recovery actions based on failure cause and verify the resulting state.","examples":["Restart a process stuck in an unrecoverable internal state.","Replace a worker whose local state is corrupted when state is safely reconstructed."],"invalid_examples":["Restart repeatedly without investigating persistent failure.","Restart a service whose persistent database state is corrupted and assume the issue will disappear."],"problem":"Blind restarts can create restart loops and mask persistent faults.","failure":"Repeated process replacement without restoration of useful service.","cause":"Recovery action is disconnected from failure diagnosis.","solution":"Associate restart with eligible failure classes and enforce restart limits plus post-restart verification.","alternatives":["Rollback","Failover","Quarantine"],"tradeoffs":["Restart can be fast but may lose ephemeral state and does not fix persistent faults."],"constraints":["Startup must be safe and deterministic enough for recovery.","State reconstruction must be correct."],"edge_cases":["A restart can temporarily hide a memory leak.","The process may restart successfully but fail immediately when serving traffic."],"security_implications":"Restart must preserve secure configuration and credentials.","performance_implications":"Repeated restarts reduce capacity and can create recovery storms.","testing":"Inject recoverable and non-recoverable faults and verify different remediation paths.","verification":"Require readiness and functional verification after restart before traffic restoration.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Recovery state machine","Health model"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B08-001","D22-B08-003"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Restart-based recovery analysis"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B08-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B08","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"recovery_engineering","subtopic":"rollback","concept":"Rollback is a recovery mechanism when a recent change is a credible cause of failure and a known-good version can be restored safely.","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"When is rollback an appropriate reliability mechanism?","answer":"Rollback is appropriate when evidence links the degradation to a reversible change and a previously validated state is available and safe to restore.","explanation":"Rollback should be treated as an evidence-driven remediation rather than a generic response to every incident.","principle":"Restore a known-good state when the current state is credibly responsible and restoration is safer than forward repair.","examples":["Revert a deployment that introduced a confirmed startup failure.","Restore a configuration version after verified configuration-induced degradation."],"invalid_examples":["Rollback unrelated changes solely because an incident occurred.","Rollback without considering irreversible schema or data changes."],"problem":"Blind rollback can introduce incompatibilities or remove unrelated fixes.","failure":"Recovery action creates additional failure or data incompatibility.","cause":"Rollback performed without dependency and state analysis.","solution":"Define rollback eligibility, compatibility checks, state implications, and post-rollback verification.","alternatives":["Forward fix","Feature disablement","Traffic shift"],"tradeoffs":["Rollback can restore service quickly but may remove beneficial changes or fail after irreversible migrations."],"constraints":["Data and schema compatibility must be evaluated.","Rollback state must itself be verified."],"edge_cases":["A code rollback cannot undo a persistent data migration.","Multiple simultaneous changes can make causal attribution uncertain."],"security_implications":"Rollback may restore older software with known security weaknesses and requires security review.","performance_implications":"Rollback can change capacity and resource behavior.","testing":"Test rollback against representative state transitions and migration scenarios.","verification":"Verify service health, data integrity, and security posture after rollback.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Change attribution","Recovery state machine"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B08-001","D22-B10-002"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_change_management","source":"Evidence-driven rollback"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B08-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B08","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"recovery_engineering","subtopic":"failover_verification","concept":"Failover is complete only after the promoted component is verified to have the required state, dependencies, authority, and serving capability.","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"What must be verified after failover?","answer":"Post-failover verification should establish that the new active component owns the required role, has acceptable state freshness, can access required dependencies, and can safely serve the intended workload.","explanation":"Traffic movement alone does not establish recovery.","principle":"Failover success is a verified operational state, not merely a routing event.","examples":["Verify ownership token after promotion.","Verify replica freshness against recovery-point requirements.","Run functional readiness checks before full traffic restoration."],"invalid_examples":["Assume a promoted replica is healthy because promotion completed.","Restore all traffic immediately after routing changes."],"problem":"A promoted component may be active but unable to serve correctly.","failure":"Post-failover outage or state inconsistency.","cause":"Missing post-promotion verification.","solution":"Define and execute explicit failover validation criteria.","alternatives":["Canary traffic","Synthetic transactions","Readiness gates"],"tradeoffs":["Verification delays restoration slightly but reduces premature failover completion."],"constraints":["Verification must be safe and representative.","State correctness requirements must be explicit."],"edge_cases":["The new active node can pass shallow health checks while its state is stale.","The old primary can unexpectedly return during failover."],"security_implications":"Failover must preserve authorization, encryption, and ownership controls.","performance_implications":"Canary verification adds temporary overhead.","testing":"Simulate failover with stale state, unavailable dependencies, and old-primary recovery.","verification":"Require all defined promotion checks to pass before declaring recovery complete.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Redundancy","Recovery state machine"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B07-003","D22-B07-004","D22-B08-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Failover verification"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B08-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B08","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"recovery_engineering","subtopic":"recovery_convergence","concept":"A recovery mechanism should converge toward a stable healthy state rather than repeatedly oscillating between remediation and failure.","knowledge_type":"recovery_property","question":"What does recovery convergence mean?","answer":"Recovery convergence means repeated execution of the recovery process moves the system toward a stable intended state instead of creating repeated failure, flapping, or resource exhaustion.","explanation":"A theoretically correct recovery action can still be unreliable if repeated execution amplifies the underlying problem.","principle":"Recovery should be stable under repetition and bounded under persistent failure.","examples":["A failed worker is replaced once and then remains healthy.","A degraded mode remains active until dependency health stabilizes before restoration."],"invalid_examples":["Restart a service every few seconds while its dependency remains unavailable.","Repeatedly open and close a circuit without controlling recovery probes."],"problem":"Unstable recovery loops can become an outage amplifier.","failure":"Recovery flapping or resource exhaustion.","cause":"No hysteresis, retry bound, or stable-state criterion.","solution":"Define convergence criteria, recovery limits, backoff, and stable-state verification.","alternatives":["Manual intervention threshold","Quarantine","Progressive recovery"],"tradeoffs":["Conservative recovery can delay restoration.","Aggressive recovery can increase oscillation risk."],"constraints":["Recovery loop must have bounded work and explicit terminal or escalation states."],"edge_cases":["Underlying failure may persist indefinitely.","Monitoring may itself be unstable during recovery."],"security_implications":"Repeated automated actions can create security-relevant state churn or resource exhaustion.","performance_implications":"Recovery loops consume compute, network, and operational capacity.","testing":"Keep the underlying fault persistent and observe whether recovery actions remain bounded and eventually escalate.","verification":"Verify that persistent faults transition to a controlled terminal/escalation state rather than infinite recovery loops.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Recovery state machine","Bounded retries"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B03-002","D22-B08-001","D22-B10-004"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Recovery convergence and stability"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B09-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B09","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"reliability_testing","subtopic":"fault_injection","concept":"Fault injection deliberately introduces controlled failures to determine whether detection, isolation, degradation, recovery, and verification mechanisms behave as designed.","knowledge_type":"testing_method","question":"Why is fault injection necessary for reliability?","answer":"Reliability mechanisms cannot be trusted solely because their normal-path code executes successfully. Controlled faults reveal whether failure assumptions and recovery paths actually work.","explanation":"Failure paths are often less exercised than success paths and may contain hidden assumptions about timing, state, dependencies, and operator behavior.","principle":"Test the failure behavior directly rather than inferring it from normal operation.","examples":["Terminate a worker during active processing.","Delay or reject dependency calls.","Introduce controlled network or storage faults in a test environment."],"invalid_examples":["Test only successful dependency responses.","Declare failover reliable because the failover code has unit-test coverage."],"problem":"Untested failure paths can fail during the actual incident.","failure":"Detection or recovery mechanism behaves differently under real fault conditions.","cause":"Reliability validated only through nominal-path testing.","solution":"Use controlled fault injection across important failure modes.","alternatives":["Chaos testing","Dependency mocks","Failure simulation"],"tradeoffs":["Fault injection can be disruptive and requires careful containment.","Synthetic faults may not reproduce every real failure."],"constraints":["Fault experiments require explicit safety boundaries and rollback procedures."],"edge_cases":["The test itself can damage shared infrastructure.","A simulated fault may differ from a real provider or hardware failure."],"security_implications":"Fault injection must not expose production secrets or create uncontrolled attack paths.","performance_implications":"Experiments consume resources and can temporarily reduce capacity.","testing":"Define expected detection, containment, recovery, and verification outcomes before each experiment.","verification":"Compare observed behavior with the predefined reliability contract.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure taxonomy","Recovery model"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B09-002","D22-B09-003","D22-B10-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_reliability_guidance","source":"Azure transient fault and fault-injection testing guidance","source_version":"2026"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B09-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B09","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"reliability_testing","subtopic":"retry_storm_test","concept":"Retry behavior should be tested under concurrent dependency failure because aggregate retry behavior can differ substantially from single-request behavior.","knowledge_type":"test_scenario","question":"How should retry storms be tested?","answer":"Simulate a dependency failure while many callers are active, then measure retry volume, dependency load, caller resource usage, latency, and recovery behavior.","explanation":"Per-request retry correctness does not guarantee service-level safety.","principle":"Reliability testing must evaluate emergent behavior across concurrent callers.","examples":["Fail a dependency for a controlled interval while thousands of logical requests are generated.","Measure retry amplification before and after enabling a retry budget."],"invalid_examples":["Test retry logic with one client and infer service-wide safety."],"problem":"Concurrent retries can overwhelm a dependency even when every individual caller follows a finite policy.","failure":"Cascading failure caused by retry amplification.","cause":"Aggregate retry behavior not tested.","solution":"Test concurrent failure with realistic population and enforce retry budgets and backoff.","alternatives":["Load testing with fault injection","Dependency simulation"],"tradeoffs":["Large-scale tests require more infrastructure and careful containment."],"constraints":["Test traffic must remain isolated from unrelated systems."],"edge_cases":["Multiple service layers may independently retry.","Different clients may have different retry policies."],"security_implications":"Retry storms can act as accidental or malicious traffic amplification.","performance_implications":"The test must measure aggregate resource impact, not only success rate.","testing":"Track retry count, original request count, dependency requests, queue depth, and recovery time.","verification":"Verify retry amplification remains within the reliability budget.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Retry budget","Exponential backoff"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B03-004","D22-B09-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"official_reliability_guidance","source":"Google SRE cascading failures; Azure Retry Storm guidance","source_version":"Current retrieved guidance"},"evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_GUIDANCE_SUPPORTED","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B09-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B09","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"reliability_testing","subtopic":"blast_radius_test","concept":"Fault-isolation testing should verify that a failure remains within its intended blast radius and does not consume protected resources elsewhere.","knowledge_type":"test_method","question":"How can fault isolation be tested?","answer":"Fail or overload one partition, tenant, cell, dependency, or service instance while continuously exercising protected workloads and measuring their availability and correctness.","explanation":"Isolation is a property of interactions, so it cannot be validated by inspecting partition definitions alone.","principle":"Prove containment by causing the initiating failure and observing unaffected domains.","examples":["Exhaust one tenant's worker quota while monitoring other tenants.","Fail one service cell while testing traffic routed to another cell."],"invalid_examples":["Verify isolation only by checking configuration files.","Test one partition without exercising neighboring workloads."],"problem":"Configuration can claim isolation while shared resources still create hidden coupling.","failure":"Failure crosses the intended isolation boundary.","cause":"Shared dependencies, pools, queues, or control paths.","solution":"Exercise isolated and protected workloads simultaneously under controlled fault conditions.","alternatives":["Cell-level chaos testing","Resource exhaustion tests"],"tradeoffs":["Isolation experiments can be disruptive and require carefully bounded test environments."],"constraints":["Protected workloads must have explicit success criteria.","The experiment must have a safe abort mechanism."],"edge_cases":["Shared monitoring or control planes may become common-mode dependencies.","Failure may propagate indirectly through retry or traffic shifting."],"security_implications":"Tenant isolation tests must not expose cross-tenant data.","performance_implications":"The experiment should measure resource contention across boundaries.","testing":"Combine fault injection with representative cross-boundary traffic.","verification":"Confirm protected domains remain within their defined reliability envelope.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Bulkhead isolation","Blast-radius model"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B06-005","D22-B09-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_testing_methodology","source":"Blast-radius and fault-containment testing"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B09-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B09","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"reliability_testing","subtopic":"recovery_time_validation","concept":"Recovery testing should measure actual detection, containment, remediation, verification, and restoration intervals rather than reporting only whether the system eventually recovered.","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"What should recovery-time testing measure?","answer":"Measure the complete recovery sequence from fault occurrence through detection, containment, remediation, verification, and restoration of intended service.","explanation":"Different stages can dominate recovery time and require different engineering improvements.","principle":"Recovery duration is a sequence of measurable state transitions, not one opaque number.","examples":["Fault injected at T0; detector fires at T1; containment at T2; remediation at T3; health verified at T4; traffic restored at T5."],"invalid_examples":["Report only 'service recovered after five minutes' without stage timings."],"problem":"Aggregated recovery time hides the actual source of delay.","failure":"Repeated optimization targets the wrong recovery stage.","cause":"No stage-level recovery measurement.","solution":"Instrument each recovery transition and validate against recovery objectives.","alternatives":["Incident timeline analysis","Synthetic recovery tests"],"tradeoffs":["Detailed instrumentation adds complexity and telemetry.","Stage boundaries can be difficult to define in highly distributed systems."],"constraints":["Timestamps require consistent enough time semantics for the required analysis."],"edge_cases":["Detection can occur after partial automatic remediation.","Recovery may be gradual rather than binary."],"security_implications":"Recovery telemetry can reveal infrastructure details.","performance_implications":"Measurement overhead should be bounded.","testing":"Run controlled failures repeatedly and compare recovery-stage distributions.","verification":"Verify each recovery stage against its defined objective.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Recovery state machine"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B08-001","D22-B10-002"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_testing_methodology","source":"Recovery-stage verification"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B09-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B09","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"reliability_testing","subtopic":"recovery_repeatability","concept":"A recovery test should be repeated because one successful recovery does not establish reliable behavior across timing, state, concurrency, and failure variations.","knowledge_type":"test_principle","question":"Why should recovery scenarios be repeated?","answer":"Recovery paths can depend on timing, concurrency, stale state, dependency ordering, and previous recovery attempts. Repeated controlled tests reveal unstable behavior that a single success can miss.","explanation":"Reliability is about consistent behavior across relevant fault conditions, not one successful demonstration.","principle":"Repeat important recovery scenarios across representative fault and timing conditions.","examples":["Repeat dependency recovery with different outage durations.","Repeat failover while varying replica freshness and request load."],"invalid_examples":["Run a recovery drill once and treat it as permanent proof.","Test only the easiest timing sequence."],"problem":"Timing-sensitive recovery defects remain hidden in one-shot tests.","failure":"Production recovery fails under a slightly different sequence.","cause":"Insufficient scenario diversity and repetition.","solution":"Repeat controlled recovery tests with varied fault timing and state.","alternatives":["Property-based fault testing","Chaos experiments","Scenario matrices"],"tradeoffs":["More repetitions increase test cost.","Excessive synthetic variation can become disconnected from realistic failure modes."],"constraints":["Variations must remain within plausible system states.","Results must preserve scenario metadata."],"edge_cases":["Some rare failures require specialized environments.","A recovery test can modify persistent state and affect later trials."],"security_implications":"Repeated fault testing must preserve isolation and access controls.","performance_implications":"Repeated recovery experiments consume test infrastructure resources.","testing":"Vary fault duration, concurrency, dependency order, and recovery timing.","verification":"Require consistent satisfaction of recovery invariants across repeated trials.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Fault injection","Recovery-time validation"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B09-001","D22-B10-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_testing_methodology","source":"Repeated recovery scenario validation"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B10-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B10","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"reliability_verification","subtopic":"invariants","concept":"Reliability verification should define invariants that must remain true during failure, degradation, recovery, and normal operation.","knowledge_type":"verification_framework","question":"Why are reliability invariants important?","answer":"Invariants provide stable correctness conditions that can be checked across different failure scenarios, making reliability validation more robust than checking only one expected sequence.","explanation":"Examples include no duplicate business operation, no unauthorized access, bounded resource consumption, single active owner, and protected workload continuity.","principle":"Verify properties that must remain true across state transitions, not merely individual commands.","examples":["At most one authoritative writer exists.","A rejected operation is never reported as successfully completed.","Protected tenants retain their minimum service guarantee."],"invalid_examples":["Verify only that the restart command returned successfully.","Verify availability while ignoring data integrity."],"problem":"Command-level success can coexist with system-level correctness failure.","failure":"Recovery appears successful while violating a critical invariant.","cause":"Verification focused on actions rather than system properties.","solution":"Define explicit reliability invariants and validate them during fault scenarios.","alternatives":["State assertions","Property-based testing","Formal invariants"],"tradeoffs":["Strong invariants require careful specification and instrumentation."],"constraints":["Invariants must be observable or formally enforceable.","Different operating modes may have different valid invariant sets."],"edge_cases":["Temporary intermediate states may intentionally violate a final-state invariant.","Some invariants require distributed coordination to verify."],"security_implications":"Security invariants such as authorization and isolation must remain valid during failure.","performance_implications":"Continuous invariant checks may add overhead and should be scoped appropriately.","testing":"Exercise failures while monitoring invariant preservation.","verification":"Require all critical invariants to remain satisfied or transition through explicitly permitted intermediate states.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure models","Recovery state machine"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B10-002","D22-B10-003"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_verification_methodology","source":"Invariant-based reliability verification"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_VERIFICATION_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B10-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B10","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"reliability_verification","subtopic":"recovery_objectives","concept":"Recovery objectives should distinguish how much data loss is acceptable from how quickly service must be restored.","knowledge_type":"reliability_requirement","question":"Why must recovery objectives separate service restoration from data recovery?","answer":"A system can restore service quickly while losing recent state, or preserve state while taking longer to restore service. These are different reliability objectives and require different mechanisms.","explanation":"Recovery requirements should explicitly define both recovery-time and recovery-point expectations when data durability matters.","principle":"Define service-recovery and data-recovery objectives separately.","examples":["Specify maximum acceptable restoration interval.","Specify maximum acceptable amount of committed-state loss after a failure."],"invalid_examples":["State only that the system must recover quickly.","Claim that replication automatically guarantees a zero-loss recovery point."],"problem":"Unspecified recovery objectives produce ambiguous architecture and failover decisions.","failure":"Recovered service violates business expectations about downtime or data loss.","cause":"Recovery requirements expressed as one vague availability target.","solution":"Define explicit recovery-time and recovery-point requirements and map them to mechanisms.","alternatives":["Business continuity objectives","Durability contracts","Failover policies"],"tradeoffs":["Stronger recovery objectives can require additional redundancy, replication, testing, and cost."],"constraints":["Objectives must be realistic for the selected architecture.","Data-loss tolerance must reflect business semantics."],"edge_cases":["Some workloads can tolerate stale reads but not lost writes.","Different data classes can have different recovery requirements."],"security_implications":"Recovery may require access to protected backups or replicas.","performance_implications":"Stronger replication and recovery guarantees can increase runtime and operational cost.","testing":"Perform controlled failure and restoration tests against defined recovery objectives.","verification":"Measure actual recovery time and recovered-state freshness against the requirements.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Redundancy","Replication semantics"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B07-005","D22-B09-004"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_engineering_methodology","source":"Recovery objective specification"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B10-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B10","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"reliability_verification","subtopic":"action_vs_outcome","concept":"A reliability action being executed successfully is not equivalent to the intended recovery outcome being verified.","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"Why must action execution and recovery outcome be separate states?","answer":"A restart, failover, rollback, or configuration change can execute without restoring the intended system behavior. The outcome must therefore be verified independently.","explanation":"This distinction prevents automation from reporting success based only on command completion.","principle":"Execution evidence proves that an action was attempted or accepted; outcome evidence proves that the system reached the intended state.","examples":["Restart command accepted → process restarted → readiness verified → functional check passed.","Traffic shift completed → new instance verified healthy → service behavior verified."],"invalid_examples":["Mark service recovered because restart returned exit code zero.","Mark failover successful because routing changed."],"problem":"Automation can create false recovery confidence.","failure":"System remains unhealthy while orchestration reports success.","cause":"No outcome verification stage.","solution":"Require post-action health and functional verification before recovery completion.","alternatives":["State convergence checks","Synthetic transactions","Invariant verification"],"tradeoffs":["Verification increases recovery duration and instrumentation requirements."],"constraints":["Verification must be meaningful for the recovered function.","Some outcomes may require asynchronous confirmation."],"edge_cases":["The service may be healthy at the component level but unhealthy at business-function level.","Monitoring can lag behind actual recovery."],"security_implications":"Outcome verification must respect access controls and must not expose sensitive state.","performance_implications":"Verification adds controlled recovery overhead.","testing":"Force remediation to execute successfully while keeping the underlying fault active.","verification":"Ensure recovery remains incomplete until the intended outcome is observed.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Recovery state machine","Reliability invariants"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B02-004","D22-B08-001","D22-B10-001"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_verification_methodology","source":"Action-versus-outcome verification"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B10-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B10","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"reliability_verification","subtopic":"persistent_failure_escalation","concept":"A reliability system must have an explicit escalation state for failures that cannot be automatically recovered within defined bounds.","knowledge_type":"failure_governance","question":"Why must automatic recovery have an escalation boundary?","answer":"Some failures are persistent, ambiguous, unsafe to remediate automatically, or outside the system's authority. Continuing automated recovery indefinitely can increase damage rather than improve availability.","explanation":"Reliable automation includes a controlled point where it stops acting and preserves evidence for human or higher-level intervention.","principle":"Bound automation and escalate when recovery evidence is insufficient or policy limits are reached.","examples":["Stop after repeated unsuccessful restarts and quarantine the component.","Escalate when data integrity cannot be verified.","Require approval for recovery actions with irreversible consequences."],"invalid_examples":["Retry remediation forever.","Perform increasingly destructive recovery actions without authority checks."],"problem":"Unbounded automation can turn a recoverable incident into a larger failure.","failure":"Repeated remediation causes resource exhaustion, data loss, or wider outage.","cause":"No terminal failure or escalation state.","solution":"Define retry/recovery limits, escalation criteria, authority boundaries, and evidence preservation.","alternatives":["Manual intervention","Higher-level orchestrator","Incident workflow"],"tradeoffs":["Escalation can increase human response time but prevents uncontrolled automation.","Strict limits can reduce automatic recovery success for unusual faults."],"constraints":["Escalation policy must be explicit and auditable.","High-impact actions require appropriate authorization."],"edge_cases":["Monitoring may be unavailable during escalation.","A transient failure can persist just beyond the automatic recovery window."],"security_implications":"Escalation must preserve authorization boundaries and prevent privilege escalation through recovery mechanisms.","performance_implications":"Stopping repeated remediation prevents resource waste and recovery storms.","testing":"Hold the underlying fault persistent until automation reaches its configured limit.","verification":"Verify that the system stops, preserves evidence, and transitions to the intended escalation state.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Recovery convergence","Action-versus-outcome verification"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B08-005","D22-B10-003"]},"provenance":{"source_type":"reliability_governance_methodology","source":"Bounded automated recovery and escalation"},"evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RELIABILITY_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B10-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"D22-B10","domain":"reliability_engineering","topic":"reliability_verification","subtopic":"phase_quality_gate","concept":"A reliability dataset phase should not be considered complete merely because its planned batches exist; completion requires coverage, implementation relevance, failure modes, testing, verification, provenance, version scope, contradiction analysis, and deduplication.","knowledge_type":"dataset_governance","question":"What must be true before D22 can eventually be locked?","answer":"D22 should be locked only after its reliability scope is covered, important failure and recovery knowledge is represented, implementation and testing knowledge are sufficient, evidence and provenance are audited, duplicates are removed, contradictions are scoped, relationships are valid, and no important known gap remains.","explanation":"The phase-lock decision is coverage-driven rather than record-count-driven.","principle":"Dataset completion is determined by validated knowledge coverage, not batch or record count.","examples":["Validate retry, circuit breaker, degradation, isolation, redundancy, recovery, health, testing, and verification coverage.","Audit every record for provenance and execution-state honesty."],"invalid_examples":["Lock D22 because B100 was reached regardless of missing recovery or fault-isolation knowledge.","Treat generated examples as executed verification."],"problem":"Count-based completion can leave critical reliability gaps hidden inside a large dataset.","failure":"Downstream autonomous reasoning relies on incomplete or unsupported reliability knowledge.","cause":"Record count treated as completion evidence.","solution":"Run structural validation, semantic deduplication, contradiction review, provenance audit, coverage analysis, relationship validation, and retrieval-oriented quality checks before locking.","alternatives":["Coverage matrix","Knowledge graph audit","Human review of unresolved exceptions"],"tradeoffs":["A rigorous lock gate takes more validation effort but produces a stronger foundational dataset."],"constraints":["No fabricated verification or provenance.","Locked IDs and relationships must remain stable across future versions."],"edge_cases":["A small number of records may legitimately cover a narrow subtopic completely.","New authoritative evidence may require a versioned extension after lock."],"security_implications":"Reliability knowledge should preserve security-relevant failure and recovery constraints.","performance_implications":"Validation itself should be designed to scale without discarding semantic quality.","testing":"Test the dataset validator against malformed, duplicated, contradictory, and unsupported records.","verification":"Require all mandatory quality gates to pass before lock and preserve unresolved exceptions explicitly if any remain.","version":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["All D22 topic groups"],"relationships":{"supports":["D22-B10-001","D22-B10-003","D22-B10-004"],"finalizes":"D22_PHASE_LOCK"},"provenance":{"source_type":"dataset_quality_methodology","source":"D22 reliability dataset quality gate"},"evidence_level":"DATASET_GOVERNANCE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B11-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Time-Bounded Reliability","subtopic":"Deadlines","concept":"End-to-end deadline propagation","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"Why should a distributed request carry an end-to-end deadline instead of allowing each downstream operation to choose an independent timeout?","answer":"An end-to-end deadline expresses the remaining time budget for the complete operation. Downstream calls should derive their local timeout from the remaining budget so that work is abandoned when the overall result can no longer meet its usefulness constraint.","explanation":"Independent downstream timeouts can accumulate beyond the caller's useful lifetime. A propagated deadline creates a bounded temporal contract across the call graph and prevents downstream work from continuing after the caller has already timed out.","principle":"Reliability improves when distributed work is bounded by the lifetime of the operation that requires its result.","rule":"Never allow a downstream timeout to exceed the remaining end-to-end deadline.","examples":["A request has a 2-second deadline; after 700 ms of upstream work, the dependency receives approximately the remaining budget rather than a fresh 2-second timeout."],"invalid_examples":["Every service independently starts a 5-second timeout for a request whose client deadline is 1 second."],"problem":"Downstream work continues after the caller has already abandoned the operation.","failure":"Wasted capacity, queue growth, stale work, and increased contention during overload.","cause":"Timeouts are locally configured without reference to the global request deadline.","solution":"Propagate absolute deadlines or remaining time budgets through request context and enforce them at each blocking boundary.","alternatives":["Centralized request timeout policy","Explicit deadline metadata in RPC protocols"],"tradeoffs":"Very aggressive deadlines can cause legitimate work to be cancelled; overly generous deadlines allow resource consumption and latency accumulation.","constraints":["Every participating component must preserve deadline metadata.","Clock handling must avoid unsafe dependence on unsynchronized wall clocks."],"edge_cases":["Retries must consume the same original deadline rather than receiving a new full timeout.","Background work detached from the request requires a separate lifecycle contract."],"security":"Deadline metadata should be treated as untrusted input and bounded to prevent resource-retention attacks.","performance":"Deadline enforcement reduces wasted work but adds propagation and cancellation checks.","testing":"Inject slow downstream operations and verify that total work stops according to the caller deadline.","verification":"Verify that no downstream timeout can exceed the remaining request budget.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["request context propagation","timeout enforcement"],"prerequisites":["D22 reliability fundamentals","distributed request lifecycle"],"relationships":["D22-B11-002","D22-B12-001"],"provenance":["Derived from established distributed-systems deadline and timeout engineering practice"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B11-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Time-Bounded Reliability","subtopic":"Timeouts","concept":"Timeout classification","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"What should determine whether a timeout is treated as a transient failure, saturation signal, or definitive failure?","answer":"Classification should consider operation semantics, dependency behavior, deadline state, resource saturation, and whether the operation may still be executing remotely.","explanation":"A timeout does not prove that the remote operation failed. The request may have completed after the client stopped waiting. Treating every timeout as safely retryable can create duplicate side effects.","principle":"A timeout describes loss of timely observation, not necessarily failure of the remote operation.","rule":"Do not automatically retry timed-out operations with side effects unless their duplicate-execution semantics are explicitly safe.","examples":["A timed-out idempotent read can often be retried; a timed-out payment command requires stronger idempotency handling before retry."],"invalid_examples":["Retry every POST after a client timeout because the client did not receive a response."],"problem":"The caller cannot distinguish remote completion from remote failure.","failure":"Duplicate side effects, inconsistent state, or retry amplification.","cause":"Timeouts are incorrectly interpreted as definitive execution failures.","solution":"Classify timeout semantics and use idempotency keys, reconciliation, or status queries for uncertain operations.","alternatives":["Idempotent command processing","Operation-status lookup","Transactional outbox"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger uncertainty handling increases implementation complexity but reduces duplicate side effects.","constraints":["The system must define operation semantics explicitly.","Status reconciliation must itself have bounded resource usage."],"edge_cases":["The remote service may become unreachable after successfully committing the operation.","A timeout may be caused by local queueing rather than remote execution."],"security":"Idempotency identifiers must not expose sensitive business data.","performance":"Status reconciliation adds calls but can prevent expensive duplicate operations.","testing":"Inject response loss after remote commit and verify that recovery does not duplicate the side effect.","verification":"Verify behavior separately for read, idempotent write, and non-idempotent write operations.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["timeout handling","idempotency"],"prerequisites":["D22-B11-001"],"relationships":["D22-B05-003","D22-B11-003"],"provenance":["Derived from distributed request uncertainty and idempotency engineering practice"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B11-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Time-Bounded Reliability","subtopic":"Timeout budgeting","concept":"Hierarchical timeout budget allocation","knowledge_type":"design_rule","question":"How should a parent operation divide its remaining deadline among multiple sequential dependencies?","answer":"The parent should reserve time for local processing and downstream dependencies, then derive bounded child budgets from the remaining time rather than assigning the entire remaining deadline to every dependency.","explanation":"If sequential calls each consume the full remaining deadline, the aggregate operation can exceed its intended completion window. Budget allocation makes latency consumption explicit.","principle":"A shared deadline is a finite reliability resource.","rule":"Never allocate the complete remaining parent budget independently to multiple sequential operations.","examples":["A 1-second operation reserves 100 ms for final processing and distributes the remaining budget between two dependencies based on expected critical-path cost."],"invalid_examples":["Dependency A gets 900 ms and, after it finishes, dependency B independently gets another 900 ms."],"problem":"Sequential dependency timeouts accumulate beyond the parent deadline.","failure":"Requests exceed service-level latency expectations and continue consuming resources after their usefulness has expired.","cause":"Timeouts are treated as independent limits instead of a shared budget.","solution":"Use remaining-deadline calculations at every call boundary.","alternatives":["Fixed per-hop timeout ceilings","Critical-path budget allocator"],"tradeoffs":"Dynamic budgets are more accurate but more complex than static timeouts.","constraints":["Budget calculations must be monotonic and bounded.","Local processing time must be reserved."],"edge_cases":["Parallel dependencies should share a common parent deadline rather than simply summing their budgets.","Clock conversion errors can produce negative or excessive budgets."],"security":"Untrusted timeout values must be capped to prevent resource exhaustion.","performance":"Budgeting prevents late work and reduces unnecessary downstream load.","testing":"Create sequential and parallel dependency graphs with artificial latency and verify deadline preservation.","verification":"Assert that child budgets are never greater than the parent's remaining budget.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["deadline propagation"],"prerequisites":["D22-B11-001"],"relationships":["D22-B11-004","D22-B12-002"],"provenance":["Derived engineering principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B11-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Time-Bounded Reliability","subtopic":"Cancellation","concept":"Cooperative cancellation","knowledge_type":"implementation_pattern","question":"Why must cancellation propagate into the work performing the operation instead of merely stopping the caller from waiting?","answer":"Stopping the caller's wait does not necessarily stop the underlying work. Cooperative cancellation allows active tasks to release resources, stop unnecessary downstream calls, and prevent abandoned work from consuming capacity.","explanation":"A caller can disappear while an asynchronous or threaded operation continues. Without cancellation propagation, abandoned work can accumulate under load and become a hidden source of resource exhaustion.","principle":"Abandoned demand should stop consuming resources whenever its result is no longer required.","rule":"Cancellation should propagate through task boundaries and be handled at resource-owning operations.","examples":["An asynchronous HTTP request cancellation closes or cancels the downstream operation rather than merely returning control to the caller."],"invalid_examples":["Return a timeout to the caller while allowing the original database operation to continue indefinitely."],"problem":"Detached work consumes resources after request cancellation.","failure":"Task buildup, connection exhaustion, queue growth, and degraded recovery.","cause":"Cancellation is implemented only at the presentation layer.","solution":"Propagate cancellation tokens/task cancellation and ensure cleanup in finally-style resource boundaries.","alternatives":["Explicit cancellation tokens","Lease-based work ownership"],"tradeoffs":"Cancellation improves resource control but requires every component to handle interruption safely.","constraints":["Cancellation handlers must be idempotent.","Critical cleanup must not itself become unbounded."],"edge_cases":["Cancellation during a non-interruptible system call.","Cancellation after an external side effect has already committed."],"security":"Untrusted callers must not be able to cancel work belonging to another authorization scope.","performance":"Prompt cancellation reduces wasted CPU, memory, network, and connection usage.","testing":"Cancel operations at different execution points and verify resource cleanup and consistent state.","verification":"Track active resources before and after cancellation and verify bounded cleanup.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["task lifecycle","resource cleanup"],"prerequisites":["D22-B11-001"],"relationships":["D22-B11-005","D22-B17-002"],"provenance":["Derived asynchronous reliability practice"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B11-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B11","topic":"Time-Bounded Reliability","subtopic":"Cancellation safety","concept":"Cancellation-safe cleanup","knowledge_type":"failure_prevention","question":"What makes cleanup safe when cancellation can occur at arbitrary points?","answer":"Cleanup must release owned resources, preserve required invariants, tolerate repeated invocation where necessary, and avoid assuming that the cancelled operation reached a particular completion state.","explanation":"Cancellation creates additional execution paths. Resource cleanup that assumes normal completion can leak locks, connections, temporary files, leases, or partially updated state.","principle":"Every interruptible operation needs a defined cleanup state for every interruption point.","rule":"Do not use successful completion assumptions inside cancellation cleanup.","examples":["A cancelled worker releases its lease and closes its connection even when the business operation did not complete."],"invalid_examples":["Release a resource only inside the normal-success branch."],"problem":"Cancellation leaves partially owned resources behind.","failure":"Leaks, deadlocks, stale leases, and inconsistent state.","cause":"Cleanup is coupled exclusively to successful completion.","solution":"Model ownership explicitly and execute cleanup from failure and cancellation paths.","alternatives":["Lease expiration","Supervisor-managed resource ownership"],"tradeoffs":"Explicit cleanup increases code paths but materially improves failure containment.","constraints":["External side effects may not be reversible.","Cleanup itself must have bounded execution."],"edge_cases":["Process termination can prevent graceful cleanup, requiring leases or external reclamation.","Cancellation during cleanup must not create a permanent resource leak."],"security":"Locks and leases must not remain held across authorization boundaries.","performance":"Reliable cleanup prevents long-term resource degradation.","testing":"Inject cancellation before, during, and after resource acquisition.","verification":"Verify eventual resource reclamation for every cancellation path.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["cancellation","resource ownership"],"prerequisites":["D22-B11-004"],"relationships":["D22-B18-003"],"provenance":["Derived engineering principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B12-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Backpressure","subtopic":"Demand control","concept":"Backpressure propagation","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"What is backpressure and why is it necessary in a reliable producer-consumer system?","answer":"Backpressure is a mechanism by which a constrained consumer communicates its capacity limit so producers reduce, delay, or reject additional work instead of continuously increasing queued demand.","explanation":"Without backpressure, queues can absorb overload temporarily while memory, latency, and downstream pressure continue increasing. Eventually the queue itself becomes a failure source.","principle":"A system must bound demand when processing capacity is bounded.","rule":"Every unbounded producer-consumer path should have an explicit capacity or admission policy.","examples":["A worker pool rejects or delays new jobs when its bounded queue reaches its configured capacity."],"invalid_examples":["Accept unlimited requests into an in-memory queue during downstream outage."],"problem":"Incoming demand exceeds sustainable processing capacity.","failure":"Memory exhaustion, latency explosion, cascading overload, or process termination.","cause":"No bounded queue or admission control exists.","solution":"Use bounded queues, producer throttling, load shedding, or explicit rejection.","alternatives":["Rate limiting","Token buckets","Priority queues"],"tradeoffs":"Rejecting work preserves system stability but reduces immediate throughput or availability for rejected requests.","constraints":["Capacity limits must reflect actual resource constraints.","Clients need a defined response or retry policy."],"edge_cases":["Priority work may need to bypass normal queue limits under controlled conditions.","A queue shared by tenants can create cross-tenant interference."],"security":"Bounded queues reduce memory-exhaustion abuse but require fair admission policies.","performance":"Backpressure keeps latency and resource usage bounded under overload.","testing":"Drive producers above consumer capacity and observe queue, latency, memory, and rejection behavior.","verification":"Verify queue size remains bounded and overload does not cause uncontrolled resource growth.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["bounded queues","admission control"],"prerequisites":["D22 reliability fundamentals"],"relationships":["D22-B12-002","D22-B12-003","D22-B07-002"],"provenance":["Derived from reliability and overload-control practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B12-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Backpressure","subtopic":"Queue capacity","concept":"Bounded queue sizing","knowledge_type":"design_rule","question":"Why is a bounded queue preferable to an unbounded queue for reliability-critical work?","answer":"A bounded queue establishes a finite resource limit and forces the system to make an explicit decision when demand exceeds capacity.","explanation":"An unbounded queue converts overload into delayed failure. A bounded queue makes overload visible earlier and enables rejection, prioritization, or upstream throttling.","principle":"Queueing should absorb expected variability, not hide sustained overload.","rule":"A queue must have a capacity policy derived from resource limits and acceptable latency, not an arbitrary unlimited setting.","examples":["A service rejects low-priority jobs once the bounded queue is full while retaining capacity for critical work."],"invalid_examples":["Unlimited queue capacity with the assumption that workers will eventually catch up."],"problem":"Queue growth exceeds memory or latency tolerance.","failure":"Out-of-memory conditions or requests completing after their usefulness window.","cause":"Queue capacity is unconstrained.","solution":"Bound queue depth and define overload behavior.","alternatives":["Direct synchronous backpressure","External durable queue with explicit retention limits"],"tradeoffs":"Smaller queues reject work sooner; larger queues tolerate bursts but increase waiting latency and resource consumption.","constraints":["Capacity must account for item size, not only item count.","Durable queues require storage and retention limits."],"edge_cases":["Variable-sized jobs can make item-count limits misleading.","A queue can be bounded while individual jobs consume unbounded resources."],"security":"Queue limits should prevent one tenant from monopolizing shared capacity.","performance":"Proper queue limits prevent memory pressure and excessive queueing latency.","testing":"Vary queue size and job cost under sustained overload.","verification":"Verify both memory and latency remain within configured limits under overload.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["queueing","capacity planning"],"prerequisites":["D22-B12-001"],"relationships":["D21","D22-B12-004"],"provenance":["Derived engineering principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B12-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Backpressure","subtopic":"Overload behavior","concept":"Load shedding versus queueing","knowledge_type":"decision_rule","question":"When should a system shed load instead of accepting work into a queue?","answer":"Load should be shed when accepting additional work would cause resource exhaustion, violate latency usefulness, or reduce the system's ability to complete already accepted higher-value work.","explanation":"Queueing is useful for bounded bursts but harmful when it merely delays inevitable failure. Load shedding preserves capacity for work that can still succeed.","principle":"Protecting useful work is preferable to accepting work that cannot complete within its validity window.","rule":"Reject or defer work when predicted completion exceeds the operation's usefulness or threatens protected capacity.","examples":["Reject optional background processing while preserving interactive requests during severe downstream saturation."],"invalid_examples":["Continue accepting all work until memory exhaustion because rejection is considered unavailable behavior."],"problem":"Demand exceeds sustainable capacity.","failure":"System-wide latency collapse instead of controlled degradation.","cause":"No overload admission policy.","solution":"Define load-shedding classes and preserve capacity for critical work.","alternatives":["Priority scheduling","Adaptive concurrency limits","Tenant-specific quotas"],"tradeoffs":"Load shedding reduces accepted workload but can preserve overall service stability.","constraints":["Priority rules must be explicit and auditable.","Critical traffic cannot be defined so broadly that it defeats capacity protection."],"edge_cases":["A rejected request may be retried and increase pressure if clients retry aggressively.","Dependency recovery may require temporarily preserving probe traffic."],"security":"Fairness and tenant isolation must prevent abuse of privileged traffic classes.","performance":"Shedding prevents queueing latency and resource exhaustion.","testing":"Generate overload across traffic classes and verify protected classes retain capacity.","verification":"Verify shed work increases before protected resources cross their safety thresholds.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["admission control","traffic classification"],"prerequisites":["D22-B12-001"],"relationships":["D22-B12-004","D22-B07-003"],"provenance":["Google SRE overload-control practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B12-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Backpressure","subtopic":"Concurrency limits","concept":"Adaptive concurrency control","knowledge_type":"resilience_pattern","question":"How can concurrency limits protect a dependency whose capacity changes over time?","answer":"An adaptive concurrency controller adjusts the number of in-flight operations using observed latency, errors, queueing, or explicit capacity signals while enforcing hard safety bounds.","explanation":"A static concurrency limit may be too restrictive during healthy periods or too aggressive during degraded periods. Adaptive control can respond to changing conditions, but it must avoid unstable feedback loops.","principle":"Concurrency is a controllable resource and should be bounded independently of request arrival rate.","rule":"Adaptive controllers must have hard upper and lower bounds and damped adjustment behavior.","examples":["Reduce concurrent dependency requests after sustained latency increase, then cautiously increase concurrency after stable recovery."],"invalid_examples":["Increase concurrency immediately whenever latency rises, assuming more workers will always improve throughput."],"problem":"Unbounded or poorly tuned concurrency overloads a dependency.","failure":"Latency escalation, connection exhaustion, and cascading failure.","cause":"Concurrency is controlled only indirectly through request rate.","solution":"Use explicit concurrency limits and carefully designed feedback.","alternatives":["Static semaphore","Token bucket","Dependency-specific worker pool"],"tradeoffs":"Adaptive control can improve utilization but introduces controller complexity and tuning risk.","constraints":["Measurements must represent actual dependency health.","Controller adjustments must be rate-limited."],"edge_cases":["Latency can increase because of network conditions rather than server saturation.","Very small traffic volumes can make feedback noisy."],"security":"Tenant-specific concurrency controls may be required to prevent noisy-neighbor abuse.","performance":"Proper limits can improve throughput by avoiding overload collapse.","testing":"Inject dependency saturation and verify the controller reduces concurrency without oscillation.","verification":"Check that in-flight operations never exceed hard safety limits.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["concurrency limiter","telemetry"],"prerequisites":["D22-B12-001","D22-B12-003"],"relationships":["D21-B72","D22-B13-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability control principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.95,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B12-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B12","topic":"Backpressure","subtopic":"Fairness","concept":"Tenant-aware resource isolation","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"Why should shared queues and concurrency pools sometimes be partitioned by tenant or workload class?","answer":"Partitioning prevents one tenant or workload class from consuming all shared capacity and allows reliability guarantees to be preserved across independent consumers.","explanation":"A single shared resource pool can create noisy-neighbor failures. Partitioning creates fault and capacity boundaries while still allowing controlled shared capacity where appropriate.","principle":"Shared infrastructure should not allow one workload to exhaust resources required by independent workloads.","rule":"Apply resource isolation when workload interference can violate reliability objectives.","examples":["Separate concurrency quotas for premium and background workloads prevent a batch spike from exhausting interactive capacity."],"invalid_examples":["All tenants share one unlimited worker pool with no fairness mechanism."],"problem":"One workload dominates a shared resource.","failure":"Cross-tenant latency and availability degradation.","cause":"No resource isolation or fair scheduling.","solution":"Use quotas, weighted scheduling, partitions, or per-tenant concurrency limits.","alternatives":["Weighted fair queueing","Cell-based isolation","Dedicated pools"],"tradeoffs":"Isolation improves reliability but may reduce aggregate utilization when one partition is idle.","constraints":["Partition sizes must be configurable.","Emergency borrowing requires explicit controls."],"edge_cases":["Strict partitions can strand unused capacity.","A single tenant may legitimately require temporary burst capacity."],"security":"Tenant isolation also reduces opportunities for cross-tenant resource exhaustion.","performance":"Fairness may trade peak aggregate utilization for predictable latency.","testing":"Create adversarial noisy-neighbor workloads and verify unaffected tenants retain their capacity.","verification":"Measure resource consumption per tenant and assert configured isolation boundaries.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["resource quotas","scheduler"],"prerequisites":["D22-B12-001"],"relationships":["D22-B03-004","D22-B12-004"],"provenance":["Derived reliability isolation principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B13-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Dependency Reliability","subtopic":"Dependency budgets","concept":"Dependency failure budget","knowledge_type":"architecture_principle","question":"Why should a service explicitly model how much dependency failure it can tolerate?","answer":"A dependency budget defines bounded assumptions about latency, error rate, unavailability, or capacity degradation that the caller can absorb without violating its own reliability objective.","explanation":"Without explicit dependency assumptions, local services often inherit uncontrolled failure from downstream systems. A budget makes resilience requirements measurable and drives fallback, timeout, retry, and capacity decisions.","principle":"A service's reliability cannot be engineered independently of its critical dependencies.","rule":"For every critical dependency, define the failure conditions the caller can tolerate and the behavior beyond that boundary.","examples":["A service specifies a dependency timeout budget, retry ceiling, fallback policy, and acceptable stale-data interval."],"invalid_examples":["Assume a critical dependency is always available because it is operated by another internal team."],"problem":"Dependency failure becomes service failure without a controlled boundary.","failure":"Cascading unavailability.","cause":"No explicit dependency reliability contract.","solution":"Model dependency failure budgets and corresponding protective behavior.","alternatives":["Contractual SLOs","Fallback architecture","Local cache"],"tradeoffs":"More explicit dependency contracts require operational coordination and maintenance.","constraints":["Budgets must reflect real dependency capabilities.","Fallback data must have defined correctness limits."],"edge_cases":["A dependency can be healthy but semantically return incorrect data.","A dependency can fail partially by tenant, region, or operation type."],"security":"Dependency authorization failures must not be mistaken for transient availability failures.","performance":"Explicit budgets prevent excessive retries and waiting.","testing":"Inject dependency latency, errors, and partial outages against the defined budget.","verification":"Verify the caller enters protective behavior when dependency limits are exceeded.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["dependency monitoring","timeout policy"],"prerequisites":["D22-B11","D22-B12"],"relationships":["D22-B13-002","D22-B16-001"],"provenance":["Derived distributed reliability practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B13-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Dependency Reliability","subtopic":"Failure domains","concept":"Dependency criticality classification","knowledge_type":"decision_model","question":"How should dependencies be classified by their effect on service availability?","answer":"Dependencies can be classified according to whether they are critical-path mandatory, conditionally required, optional, advisory, or asynchronously decoupled from the request outcome.","explanation":"Criticality determines whether failure blocks the request, triggers degradation, or can be isolated. Treating every dependency as equally critical creates unnecessary coupling.","principle":"Dependency criticality should be explicit because different failures require different resilience behavior.","rule":"Every dependency must have a defined failure behavior before it is placed on a critical request path.","examples":["An authorization service may be mandatory while an analytics emitter can be asynchronous and non-blocking."],"invalid_examples":["Block customer requests because a telemetry exporter is unavailable."],"problem":"Noncritical dependency failures become user-visible failures.","failure":"Unnecessary availability loss and cascading dependency coupling.","cause":"No dependency criticality model.","solution":"Classify dependencies and define timeout, fallback, isolation, and failure semantics per class.","alternatives":["Criticality tags","Dependency policy registry"],"tradeoffs":"Classification adds architectural metadata but enables targeted resilience.","constraints":["Classification must be reviewed when business semantics change.","A dependency considered optional must not silently become required."],"edge_cases":["A dependency may be critical for one operation but optional for another.","Feature flags can dynamically change dependency criticality."],"security":"Authorization and policy dependencies require fail-closed behavior where required by security semantics.","performance":"Noncritical dependencies can be removed from the synchronous critical path.","testing":"Disable each dependency class and verify the expected service behavior.","verification":"Compare observed failure behavior with the registered criticality classification.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["dependency registry"],"prerequisites":["D22-B13-001"],"relationships":["D22-B13-003","D22-B07-001"],"provenance":["Derived architecture reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B13-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Dependency Reliability","subtopic":"Dependency isolation","concept":"Failure containment at dependency boundaries","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"What controls prevent a failing dependency from consuming all caller resources?","answer":"Timeouts, bounded concurrency, connection limits, circuit breaking, bulkheads, bounded queues, and dependency-specific resource pools can prevent one dependency from monopolizing caller capacity.","explanation":"A dependency outage can otherwise consume threads, tasks, sockets, queues, memory, and retries until unrelated operations fail.","principle":"Every dependency boundary should have a resource-consumption boundary.","rule":"Do not allow a dependency to consume unbounded caller resources during failure.","examples":["A slow dependency has its own connection pool and concurrency limit separate from unrelated critical dependencies."],"invalid_examples":["All outbound calls share one unlimited worker pool and connection budget."],"problem":"Dependency degradation consumes shared caller capacity.","failure":"Cascading failure across unrelated operations.","cause":"No dependency-level resource isolation.","solution":"Apply bounded resource pools and explicit dependency protection.","alternatives":["Bulkheads","Per-dependency semaphores","Dedicated worker pools"],"tradeoffs":"Isolation can reduce resource sharing efficiency but sharply limits blast radius.","constraints":["Resource limits must include connections, workers, memory, and queue capacity where relevant."],"edge_cases":["A dependency may have multiple endpoints with different failure characteristics.","Shared infrastructure can defeat logical isolation."],"security":"Resource isolation limits denial-of-service propagation between tenants or services.","performance":"Dedicated pools can increase predictable performance but may leave idle capacity.","testing":"Make one dependency indefinitely slow and verify unrelated operations remain functional.","verification":"Track resource consumption by dependency and verify isolation boundaries.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["bulkhead","concurrency control"],"prerequisites":["D22-B03","D22-B12"],"relationships":["D22-B13-004","D22-B06-004"],"provenance":["Microsoft reliability and bulkhead guidance"],"evidence":"official_documentation","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B13-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Dependency Reliability","subtopic":"Partial failure","concept":"Partial dependency failure handling","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"Why is partial dependency failure different from total dependency failure?","answer":"A dependency may fail for only certain operations, tenants, zones, keys, partitions, or time intervals. Treating partial failure as total failure can unnecessarily reduce availability, while treating it as healthy can repeatedly send traffic into known failure regions.","explanation":"Distributed dependencies often have heterogeneous failure states. Resilience logic should preserve healthy paths while isolating known bad scopes.","principle":"Failure scope should be represented explicitly rather than reduced to a single global health bit.","rule":"Record failure scope when evidence supports partition-specific, tenant-specific, or operation-specific degradation.","examples":["Route around an unhealthy dependency partition while continuing to use healthy partitions."],"invalid_examples":["Open a global circuit for every tenant because one tenant's dependency partition is failing."],"problem":"Localized dependency failure causes global service degradation.","failure":"Unnecessary traffic loss or repeated requests into unhealthy scope.","cause":"Health state lacks failure scope.","solution":"Use scoped health and routing decisions.","alternatives":["Per-region circuit state","Partition-level health","Tenant-aware isolation"],"tradeoffs":"Scoped state is more precise but increases state-management complexity.","constraints":["Scope information must remain trustworthy and bounded.","Routing decisions must avoid excessive state cardinality."],"edge_cases":["A local failure may actually be a symptom of a global incident.","Health information can become stale during network partition."],"security":"Tenant-scoped health must not expose one tenant's operational state to another.","performance":"Scoped routing can preserve healthy capacity but adds decision overhead.","testing":"Inject failures into individual partitions and verify unaffected partitions continue operating.","verification":"Verify traffic distribution matches the known failure scope.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["health model","routing"],"prerequisites":["D22-B13-002"],"relationships":["D22-B03-002","D22-B14-002"],"provenance":["Derived distributed-systems failure model"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B13-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B13","topic":"Dependency Reliability","subtopic":"Fallback correctness","concept":"Fallback validity window","knowledge_type":"design_rule","question":"Why must fallback data have an explicit validity window?","answer":"Fallback data can preserve availability only while its staleness or semantic drift remains acceptable for the operation. A fallback without a validity window can silently return information that is no longer safe to use.","explanation":"Availability and correctness are coupled. Cached or alternate data is not automatically equivalent to live authoritative data.","principle":"Every degraded-data path needs an explicit correctness boundary.","rule":"Define maximum acceptable age and semantic limitations for fallback data.","examples":["A product catalog may tolerate a short stale cache while authorization policy data may require current state."],"invalid_examples":["Serve indefinitely stale authorization or financial state because the cache is available."],"problem":"Fallback preserves availability at the expense of correctness.","failure":"Users or downstream systems act on invalid state.","cause":"Fallback policy defines availability but not data validity.","solution":"Attach freshness and semantic validity constraints to fallback sources.","alternatives":["Fail closed","Read-only degraded mode","Stale-while-revalidate"],"tradeoffs":"Longer fallback windows improve availability but increase correctness risk.","constraints":["Validity must be domain-specific.","Critical decisions may prohibit stale fallback entirely."],"edge_cases":["Clock skew can distort apparent freshness.","A fresh cache can still contain semantically invalid data."],"security":"Security-sensitive data should use stricter freshness and integrity requirements.","performance":"Caching reduces dependency pressure but can create invalidation complexity.","testing":"Age fallback data beyond its validity window and verify the system changes behavior appropriately.","verification":"Assert that expired fallback data cannot silently enter authoritative decision paths.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["cache metadata","data validity policy"],"prerequisites":["D22-B13-002"],"relationships":["D22-B07-004","D22-B19-002"],"provenance":["Derived reliability and data-correctness principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B14-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B14","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Coordination failure","concept":"Avoiding unnecessary distributed coordination","knowledge_type":"architecture_principle","question":"Why can reducing coordination improve reliability in distributed systems?","answer":"Every synchronous coordination dependency introduces additional failure, latency, timeout, and partial-failure paths. Reducing unnecessary coordination decreases the number of components that must remain simultaneously healthy.","explanation":"Strong coordination can provide useful correctness guarantees but can also turn local failures into global availability failures. The design should use coordination only where its semantic value justifies its reliability cost.","principle":"Coordination is a reliability dependency and should be treated as a scarce resource.","rule":"Prefer independent or asynchronous processing when strong coordination is not required for correctness.","examples":["Emit noncritical analytics asynchronously rather than blocking the primary transaction on analytics availability."],"invalid_examples":["Require multiple unrelated services to synchronously approve every low-risk request."],"problem":"Unnecessary synchronous dependencies increase the critical failure surface.","failure":"Availability loss caused by unrelated service failure.","cause":"Architecture optimizes for synchronous consistency without evaluating reliability cost.","solution":"Remove unnecessary synchronous coordination and isolate required coordination.","alternatives":["Asynchronous messaging","Eventual consistency","Local decision making"],"tradeoffs":"Less coordination can increase temporary inconsistency or require reconciliation.","constraints":["Strong consistency requirements cannot simply be removed for convenience.","Asynchronous designs require durable delivery and idempotent consumers."],"edge_cases":["A workflow may appear independent but actually require atomic business invariants.","Asynchronous failure can be harder to observe without strong tracing."],"security":"Authorization-critical coordination must not be removed merely for availability.","performance":"Reducing synchronous calls often lowers critical-path latency.","testing":"Disable noncritical coordinating services and verify primary functionality remains correct.","verification":"Map synchronous dependencies and justify each one against an explicit correctness requirement.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["service dependency graph"],"prerequisites":["D22-B13-002"],"relationships":["D22-B14-002","D22-B15-001"],"provenance":["Microsoft distributed reliability design guidance"],"evidence":"official_documentation","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B14-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B14","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Quorum","concept":"Quorum failure behavior","knowledge_type":"distributed_systems","question":"What reliability tradeoff does quorum-based coordination introduce?","answer":"Quorum mechanisms can preserve consistency or availability under certain node failures, but quorum requirements can also make operations unavailable when too few eligible members can participate.","explanation":"A quorum is a correctness mechanism, not an automatic availability mechanism. The system must define what happens when quorum cannot be reached.","principle":"Quorum failure must be treated as an explicit system state.","rule":"Never assume a quorum-backed operation can continue safely when its quorum condition is not satisfied.","examples":["A replicated system rejects a write when the required write quorum cannot be established rather than silently treating a minority as authoritative."],"invalid_examples":["Accept conflicting writes from isolated minority partitions without a defined reconciliation model."],"problem":"Insufficient replicas are available to satisfy the consistency protocol.","failure":"Unavailable writes, stale reads, or split-brain behavior depending on protocol design.","cause":"Quorum semantics are not incorporated into failure handling.","solution":"Define quorum requirements, failure states, and recovery/reconciliation behavior.","alternatives":["Leader-based consensus","Single-writer architecture","Eventual consistency"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger coordination can improve consistency but reduce availability during partitions.","constraints":["Protocol-specific quorum rules must be respected.","Replica health must not be inferred solely from process liveness."],"edge_cases":["Network partitions can make healthy nodes mutually unreachable.","A quorum may be technically available but contain stale state."],"security":"Membership and quorum identity must be authenticated and protected against unauthorized nodes.","performance":"Coordination adds network round trips and quorum waiting.","testing":"Inject network partitions and replica failures and verify protocol-specific safety behavior.","verification":"Verify no state transition violates the protocol's quorum requirements.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["replication protocol","membership"],"prerequisites":["distributed coordination fundamentals"],"relationships":["D22-B14-003","D22-B18-004"],"provenance":["Derived distributed-systems reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B14-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B14","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Leader failure","concept":"Leader failure and re-election safety","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"What reliability risks arise when a distributed system replaces a failed leader?","answer":"Leader replacement must prevent stale leaders from continuing to perform authoritative actions. Correctness requires an explicit authority mechanism such as epochs, terms, leases, or protocol-specific fencing.","explanation":"Detecting a leader as failed is not enough because network partitions can make an old leader appear alive from its local perspective. Without fencing, two nodes can act as authoritative writers.","principle":"Authority must be bounded by a verifiable generation or lease condition.","rule":"A new leader must be able to prevent stale authority from performing conflicting operations.","examples":["A storage system associates writes with a monotonically increasing leadership term and rejects writes from stale terms."],"invalid_examples":["Elect a new leader based only on local timeout while allowing the old leader to continue writing."],"problem":"Multiple nodes believe they hold authority.","failure":"Split-brain writes or state corruption.","cause":"Leader identity is not fenced by a protocol-level authority generation.","solution":"Use protocol-defined terms, epochs, leases, or fencing tokens.","alternatives":["Consensus protocol","External fencing service"],"tradeoffs":"Fencing increases coordination complexity but protects against stale authority.","constraints":["The fencing mechanism itself must have reliable failure semantics.","Lease timing must account for clock and network uncertainty."],"edge_cases":["Delayed messages from an old leader can arrive after re-election.","A process may pause for an extended period and resume with stale state."],"security":"Leadership credentials must be bound to the current authority generation.","performance":"Election and fencing add recovery latency.","testing":"Pause the old leader, elect a replacement, then release the old leader and verify stale operations are rejected.","verification":"Prove or test that stale leadership cannot commit authoritative state after a new generation is established.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["leader election","fencing"],"prerequisites":["D22-B14-002"],"relationships":["D22-B14-004","D22-B18-004"],"provenance":["Derived distributed coordination safety principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B14-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B14","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Split brain","concept":"Split-brain containment","knowledge_type":"failure_prevention","question":"How should a system respond when multiple components may simultaneously believe they are authoritative?","answer":"The system should use fencing, quorum, leases, or another authoritative mechanism that makes conflicting authority invalid rather than relying solely on mutual trust between nodes.","explanation":"Split brain is dangerous because both sides may continue producing apparently valid actions. Recovery must prevent stale or partitioned components from committing conflicting state.","principle":"Authority must be externally or protocol-level constrained during ambiguous membership states.","rule":"Do not allow independent partitions to make conflicting authoritative writes without a defined conflict-resolution protocol.","examples":["A partitioned writer loses its fencing token and cannot commit after another writer obtains the newer token."],"invalid_examples":["Allow both network partitions to accept writes and assume they can be merged safely later without a conflict model."],"problem":"Multiple partitions operate independently as authorities.","failure":"Divergent state and conflicting side effects.","cause":"No authority fencing or partition safety mechanism.","solution":"Introduce quorum/fencing/leader-generation controls appropriate to the consistency model.","alternatives":["Single authoritative region","Conflict-free replicated data types for suitable domains"],"tradeoffs":"Strong split-brain prevention can reduce availability during partitions.","constraints":["Conflict-free approaches are only valid for compatible data semantics.","Fencing requires reliable enforcement at the resource boundary."],"edge_cases":["A fencing service outage can itself prevent legitimate operations.","Network partitions can isolate the authority mechanism."],"security":"Unauthorized authority must be rejected at the resource boundary, not merely by convention.","performance":"Strong authority mechanisms can add latency during leadership changes.","testing":"Simulate bidirectional network partitions and verify only one side can retain authoritative write capability.","verification":"Check resource-level rejection of stale authority tokens.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["fencing","membership"],"prerequisites":["D22-B14-003"],"relationships":["D22-B03-002","D22-B18-004"],"provenance":["Derived distributed-systems safety principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B14-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B14","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Coordination recovery","concept":"Recovery after coordination loss","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"What should happen when a component reconnects after losing contact with the coordination system?","answer":"The component should revalidate its authority, refresh membership or configuration state, reconcile local state where required, and avoid performing authoritative work until its role is confirmed.","explanation":"A disconnected component may retain stale assumptions about ownership, configuration, or cluster membership. Reconnection is not equivalent to immediate trust restoration.","principle":"Reconnection is a state transition requiring revalidation, not merely a network event.","rule":"Do not resume authoritative operations solely because communication has been restored.","examples":["A previously isolated worker refreshes its lease and configuration before accepting new authoritative jobs."],"invalid_examples":["Resume all queued writes immediately after reconnecting to the cluster."],"problem":"Stale state becomes active after recovery.","failure":"Conflicting operations or invalid configuration execution.","cause":"Recovery treats connectivity as proof of correctness.","solution":"Use a rejoin protocol with authority and state validation.","alternatives":["Full process restart","State snapshot reload"],"tradeoffs":"Revalidation delays recovery but reduces stale-state corruption.","constraints":["Rejoin protocol must itself have bounded retries and timeouts.","Local queued work may need reconciliation or discard."],"edge_cases":["The component may have performed local side effects while disconnected.","Configuration may have changed multiple times during the outage."],"security":"Credentials and authorization state should be revalidated where required.","performance":"Full state reconciliation can increase recovery time.","testing":"Disconnect a node, change cluster state, reconnect it, and verify stale state cannot immediately become authoritative.","verification":"Verify rejoined nodes pass authority and state checks before serving.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["membership","state reconciliation"],"prerequisites":["D22-B14-003"],"relationships":["D22-B18-002","D22-B18-005"],"provenance":["Derived distributed recovery principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B15-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B15","topic":"Asynchronous Reliability","subtopic":"Durable work","concept":"Durable asynchronous handoff","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"Why should important asynchronous work be durably recorded before the producer considers the handoff complete?","answer":"Durable handoff ensures that accepted work can survive producer failure or process restart instead of existing only in volatile memory.","explanation":"Asynchronous execution separates acceptance from completion. If the handoff is not durable, a producer crash can silently lose work that the caller was told had been accepted.","principle":"Accepted asynchronous work requires an explicit durability contract.","rule":"Do not acknowledge durable business work until its required persistence boundary has been reached.","examples":["A transaction records an event in a durable outbox before acknowledging the originating business operation."],"invalid_examples":["Place an important job in an in-memory queue and immediately return success."],"problem":"Producer failure loses accepted work.","failure":"Silent data loss or incomplete business workflows.","cause":"Acceptance is acknowledged before durable persistence.","solution":"Use durable queues, transactional outbox patterns, or another explicit persistence boundary.","alternatives":["Synchronous completion","Write-ahead log"],"tradeoffs":"Durability adds storage and coordination cost but protects against process loss.","constraints":["Durable storage must have its own availability and recovery guarantees.","Duplicate delivery remains possible in many asynchronous systems."],"edge_cases":["Persistence can succeed while downstream processing later fails.","Exactly-once end-to-end execution is usually stronger than what a basic durable queue guarantees."],"security":"Durable payloads must follow retention, access-control, and encryption requirements.","performance":"Durable writes add latency and I/O.","testing":"Crash the producer immediately after persistence and immediately before persistence and verify distinct semantics.","verification":"Verify accepted work remains recoverable after producer restart.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["durable storage","message processing"],"prerequisites":["D22-B14-001"],"relationships":["D22-B15-002","D22-B18-001"],"provenance":["Derived asynchronous reliability practice"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B15-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B15","topic":"Asynchronous Reliability","subtopic":"Delivery semantics","concept":"At-least-once processing and idempotency","knowledge_type":"reliability_pattern","question":"Why do durable asynchronous systems commonly require idempotent consumers?","answer":"A message may be delivered more than once because acknowledgments can be lost or processing can fail after the side effect but before acknowledgment. Idempotent consumers make repeated delivery safe.","explanation":"Delivery and side-effect completion are separate events. Retrying an unacknowledged message can produce duplicate execution unless the consumer detects or tolerates duplicates.","principle":"Message delivery semantics must be matched by consumer side-effect semantics.","rule":"If duplicate delivery is possible, every non-idempotent side effect needs deduplication or an equivalent correctness mechanism.","examples":["A consumer records a unique event ID before applying a business side effect or uses an atomic operation keyed by that ID."],"invalid_examples":["Assume a message is processed exactly once because the queue usually delivers it once."],"problem":"A successful side effect is followed by message redelivery.","failure":"Duplicate orders, notifications, updates, or financial operations.","cause":"Acknowledgment occurs separately from side-effect completion.","solution":"Use idempotency keys, deduplication records, transactional processing, or naturally idempotent operations.","alternatives":["Exactly-once transactional systems","Compensating actions"],"tradeoffs":"Deduplication requires state and retention management.","constraints":["Deduplication state must live long enough to cover the duplicate-delivery window.","Unique identifiers must be stable across retries."],"edge_cases":["Deduplication state can expire while a delayed duplicate remains possible.","Two semantically identical operations may have different event IDs."],"security":"Idempotency keys must not be attacker-controlled in ways that permit unauthorized result reuse.","performance":"Deduplication adds lookup/storage overhead but prevents expensive duplicate work.","testing":"Deliver the same event multiple times at different processing stages.","verification":"Verify final business state is identical to single successful delivery.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["durable messaging","idempotency"],"prerequisites":["D22-B15-001","D22-B11-002"],"relationships":["D22-B15-003","D22-B19-003"],"provenance":["Derived distributed messaging reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B15-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B15","topic":"Asynchronous Reliability","subtopic":"Poison messages","concept":"Poison-message isolation","knowledge_type":"failure_containment","question":"How should an asynchronous system prevent one permanently failing message from blocking healthy work?","answer":"The consumer should bound processing attempts and route messages that repeatedly fail into an isolated dead-letter, quarantine, or investigation path according to business semantics.","explanation":"A permanently invalid message differs from a transiently failing message. Infinite retries can consume worker capacity and prevent subsequent valid messages from progressing.","principle":"Repeatedly unprocessable work must not monopolize the normal processing path.","rule":"Define a bounded retry or quarantine policy for messages that cannot be processed successfully.","examples":["After bounded attempts, an invalid message is quarantined with failure metadata while healthy messages continue processing."],"invalid_examples":["Retry a malformed message forever on the main queue."],"problem":"Permanent failures repeatedly consume processing capacity.","failure":"Queue blockage, worker starvation, and latency growth for healthy messages.","cause":"No distinction between transient and permanent processing failures.","solution":"Classify failures and isolate poison messages.","alternatives":["Per-message retry schedule","Parking queue","Manual remediation workflow"],"tradeoffs":"Quarantine can hide unresolved business failures if monitoring is inadequate.","constraints":["Quarantine requires durable retention and alerting.","Messages must not be silently discarded unless loss is explicitly acceptable."],"edge_cases":["A temporary dependency outage may make valid messages appear permanently failing.","Schema migrations can temporarily make older messages incompatible."],"security":"Quarantined payloads may contain sensitive information and require controlled access.","performance":"Isolation prevents repeated failures from consuming normal worker capacity.","testing":"Inject malformed and dependency-failure messages and verify different handling.","verification":"Verify healthy messages continue progressing while poison messages are isolated.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["message retry","quarantine storage"],"prerequisites":["D22-B15-002"],"relationships":["D22-B15-004","D22-B12-001"],"provenance":["Derived asynchronous failure-containment principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B15-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B15","topic":"Asynchronous Reliability","subtopic":"Ordering","concept":"Failure-aware message ordering","knowledge_type":"design_constraint","question":"Why can strict message ordering reduce reliability under partial failure?","answer":"Strict ordering can cause later independent messages to wait behind one blocked or permanently failing message. Ordering should therefore be required only where business correctness actually depends on it.","explanation":"Ordering creates a dependency between otherwise independent pieces of work. A single failure can create head-of-line blocking.","principle":"Ordering guarantees should be no stronger than business correctness requires.","rule":"Avoid global ordering when partition-local or key-local ordering is sufficient.","examples":["Order events are processed in sequence per account while unrelated accounts continue independently."],"invalid_examples":["One globally ordered queue blocks all tenants because one event cannot be processed."],"problem":"One failed message prevents unrelated work from progressing.","failure":"Throughput collapse and unnecessary availability degradation.","cause":"Overly broad ordering guarantee.","solution":"Partition ordering by business key where semantics permit.","alternatives":["Keyed partitions","Versioned state transitions","Dependency-aware scheduling"],"tradeoffs":"Relaxed ordering increases concurrency but requires explicit semantic boundaries.","constraints":["Some workflows genuinely require strict order.","Partition-key selection must preserve required invariants."],"edge_cases":["Repartitioning can change ordering guarantees.","Late events may require reconciliation."],"security":"Partitioning must not allow cross-tenant event mixing.","performance":"Partitioned ordering improves parallelism and reduces head-of-line blocking.","testing":"Block one ordered key and verify independent keys continue processing.","verification":"Verify ordering within required keys and progress across independent keys.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["message partitioning"],"prerequisites":["D22-B15-002"],"relationships":["D22-B12-005","D22-B15-005"],"provenance":["Derived messaging reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B15-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B15","topic":"Asynchronous Reliability","subtopic":"Replay","concept":"Safe event replay","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"What conditions are required for safely replaying asynchronous events?","answer":"Replay requires deterministic or explicitly versioned processing semantics, idempotency or deduplication, appropriate event retention, and protection against applying historical events to incompatible current state.","explanation":"Replay is a recovery tool but can also reproduce old side effects. Historical data must be processed according to its original semantic contract or through a controlled migration path.","principle":"Replay is a new execution of historical intent, not merely a database read.","rule":"Never replay production events without defining duplicate-side-effect and schema/version semantics.","examples":["Replay events into a rebuilt projection using a known event schema version without re-triggering external notifications."],"invalid_examples":["Replay all historical events through the current production side-effect handler without deduplication."],"problem":"Recovery replay produces duplicate or semantically incorrect side effects.","failure":"State corruption or repeated external actions.","cause":"Replay semantics are not separated from normal live processing.","solution":"Use replay-safe handlers, versioned schemas, side-effect suppression, and idempotency.","alternatives":["Snapshot restoration","Projection rebuild"],"tradeoffs":"Replay provides powerful recovery but requires strong semantic controls.","constraints":["Historical schemas may require migration.","External side effects often require special handling during replay."],"edge_cases":["Events may refer to deleted entities.","Business rules may have changed since the event was originally produced."],"security":"Historical data access must obey current authorization and retention controls.","performance":"Large replays can create substantial load and must be throttled or isolated.","testing":"Replay historical fixtures against current and historical handlers and compare expected state.","verification":"Verify replay does not produce unauthorized duplicate external effects.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["event schema versioning","idempotency"],"prerequisites":["D22-B15-002"],"relationships":["D22-B18-005","D22-B20-003"],"provenance":["Derived event-driven recovery principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B16-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B16","topic":"Resource Reliability","subtopic":"Connection pools","concept":"Connection-pool exhaustion","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"Why can a healthy dependency become unavailable to a caller even when the dependency itself is operational?","answer":"The caller can exhaust its own connection pool, worker pool, or socket capacity before the dependency fails. Resource exhaustion at the caller can therefore create an apparent dependency outage.","explanation":"Reliability must consider both remote service health and local resource availability. Slow operations are especially dangerous because they hold scarce resources for longer periods.","principle":"A dependency call consumes caller resources as well as dependency resources.","rule":"Monitor and bound client-side resource pools independently of remote health.","examples":["A service limits outbound connections per dependency and exposes pool saturation separately from dependency error rate."],"invalid_examples":["Increase the connection pool without limit whenever requests begin timing out."],"problem":"Local connection resources become exhausted.","failure":"New requests fail even though the dependency remains healthy.","cause":"Unbounded or poorly sized client resource pool.","solution":"Bound pools, enforce timeouts, and monitor acquisition wait time and saturation.","alternatives":["Connection multiplexing","Per-dependency pools"],"tradeoffs":"Larger pools tolerate concurrency but can overload the dependency and increase local resource usage.","constraints":["Pool limits must consider memory, file descriptors, and dependency capacity.","Connection lifetime must be bounded where appropriate."],"edge_cases":["Idle connections may consume server-side capacity.","A pool can appear healthy while acquisition latency is already violating deadlines."],"security":"Per-tenant pool limits may prevent resource-exhaustion abuse.","performance":"Correct pool sizing reduces contention without creating excessive concurrency.","testing":"Hold connections open and observe behavior as pool capacity is reached.","verification":"Verify new requests fail or queue according to policy rather than consuming unbounded resources.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["connection pool","timeout policy"],"prerequisites":["D22-B12-004"],"relationships":["D22-B16-002","D22-B13-003"],"provenance":["Derived resource reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B16-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B16","topic":"Resource Reliability","subtopic":"Resource leaks","concept":"Leak-driven reliability degradation","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"Why can small resource leaks become reliability failures even when individual requests succeed?","answer":"A leak gradually reduces available capacity until otherwise healthy operations fail. Reliability must therefore consider resource lifetime and cumulative exhaustion, not only per-request correctness.","explanation":"Connections, file descriptors, memory, tasks, locks, temporary files, and handles can leak across successful requests. Long-lived processes make cumulative leaks especially dangerous.","principle":"Resource lifecycle correctness is part of service reliability.","rule":"Every acquired resource must have a bounded ownership and release path.","examples":["A request-scoped connection is returned to the pool even when parsing or downstream processing raises an exception."],"invalid_examples":["Release a connection only after successful business processing."],"problem":"Resources accumulate beyond sustainable capacity.","failure":"Pool exhaustion, out-of-memory conditions, file-descriptor exhaustion, or deadlocks.","cause":"Missing or exception-unsafe cleanup.","solution":"Use structured resource ownership, finally-style cleanup, leases, and leak detection.","alternatives":["RAII","Context managers","Lease expiration"],"tradeoffs":"Leak detection and lifecycle instrumentation add overhead but expose long-running degradation.","constraints":["Some resources cannot be reclaimed immediately after process termination.","Cleanup must be safe during partial failure."],"edge_cases":["A slow leak may require soak testing to expose.","A resource can be logically leaked while still reachable in memory."],"security":"Resource leaks can become denial-of-service vectors.","performance":"Leak prevention preserves stable long-term capacity.","testing":"Run extended workload with repeated failure injection and track resource counts.","verification":"Resource usage should return toward baseline after equivalent workloads complete.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["resource ownership","cleanup"],"prerequisites":["D22-B11-005"],"relationships":["D22-B16-003","D21-B71"],"provenance":["Derived reliability engineering principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B16-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B16","topic":"Resource Reliability","subtopic":"Lease management","concept":"Lease expiration as failure containment","knowledge_type":"reliability_pattern","question":"How can leases prevent abandoned ownership from permanently blocking progress?","answer":"A lease grants ownership for a bounded interval and allows automatic reclamation when the owner stops renewing it.","explanation":"Locks without expiration can remain held after process crashes. Leases convert indefinite ownership into time-bounded ownership, but require careful handling of expiration and stale owners.","principle":"Recoverable ownership should have bounded lifetime when permanent ownership loss is unacceptable.","rule":"Use leases only when stale ownership can be safely invalidated and renewed ownership is distinguishable from expired ownership.","examples":["A worker renews a job lease periodically; if it crashes, another worker can reclaim the job after expiration."],"invalid_examples":["Assume a process will always release a lock cleanly before termination."],"problem":"Process failure leaves ownership permanently held.","failure":"Stalled jobs or unavailable resources.","cause":"Non-expiring ownership mechanism.","solution":"Use leases with renewal, expiration, and fencing where stale owners can perform dangerous actions.","alternatives":["Consensus-managed ownership","Transactional claim records"],"tradeoffs":"Lease timing introduces recovery delay and clock/network uncertainty.","constraints":["Expiration must not permit simultaneous valid owners.","Critical side effects may require fencing tokens."],"edge_cases":["A paused process may resume after its lease expires.","Network delay can make renewal uncertain."],"security":"Lease credentials must be scoped to the owner and resource.","performance":"Short leases increase renewal traffic; long leases increase recovery delay.","testing":"Pause the owner beyond lease expiration and verify stale operations cannot commit.","verification":"Verify reclamation plus stale-owner rejection.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["lease store","fencing"],"prerequisites":["D22-B14-003"],"relationships":["D22-B16-004","D22-B18-004"],"provenance":["Derived distributed ownership principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B16-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B16","topic":"Resource Reliability","subtopic":"Deadlocks","concept":"Deadlock prevention through lock ordering","knowledge_type":"failure_prevention","question":"How can a consistent lock acquisition order reduce deadlock risk?","answer":"If multiple operations acquire multiple locks in the same global order, circular wait conditions are prevented for those locks.","explanation":"Deadlocks occur when operations hold resources while waiting for resources held by each other. A deterministic ordering removes one necessary condition for circular wait.","principle":"Resource acquisition order is part of the correctness contract.","rule":"When multiple locks must be acquired together, define and enforce a consistent acquisition order.","examples":["Transactions acquire account locks by ascending account identifier rather than arbitrary request order."],"invalid_examples":["Operation A acquires lock X then Y while operation B acquires Y then X."],"problem":"Concurrent operations form circular wait.","failure":"Requests stall indefinitely or until timeout.","cause":"Inconsistent resource acquisition order.","solution":"Define lock ordering or use deadlock-detecting transactional mechanisms.","alternatives":["Try-lock with rollback","Short transactions","Optimistic concurrency"],"tradeoffs":"Strict ordering can constrain implementation flexibility.","constraints":["The ordering must be globally consistent for the lock set.","External resources may not participate in the same ordering domain."],"edge_cases":["Dynamic resource sets make static ordering harder.","Retries after deadlock must preserve idempotency."],"security":"Deadlock can be exploited as a resource-exhaustion mechanism.","performance":"Shorter lock hold times reduce contention.","testing":"Run conflicting operations with controlled scheduling to attempt circular acquisition.","verification":"Static analysis and concurrency tests should detect known lock-order violations.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["locking","concurrency"],"prerequisites":["D22-B16-002"],"relationships":["D22-B16-005","D21-B72"],"provenance":["Derived concurrency reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B16-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B16","topic":"Resource Reliability","subtopic":"Resource contention","concept":"Resource contention amplification","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"Why can increasing concurrency make a degraded system less reliable?","answer":"Higher concurrency can increase contention for CPU, memory, connections, locks, queues, storage, or downstream capacity, causing latency and failure rates to rise faster than useful throughput.","explanation":"Concurrency is not equivalent to capacity. Once a bottleneck saturates, additional work can increase overhead and reduce successful throughput.","principle":"Concurrency must be bounded by the narrowest sustainable resource on the critical path.","rule":"Do not treat increased concurrency as a universal recovery strategy for latency problems.","examples":["Reducing concurrent database operations can restore completion rate when the database is saturated."],"invalid_examples":["Continuously increasing worker count because queue depth is rising."],"problem":"Concurrency exceeds sustainable resource capacity.","failure":"Latency collapse, timeout amplification, and cascading retries.","cause":"Queue growth is addressed by adding workers without identifying the bottleneck.","solution":"Identify the constrained resource and control concurrency at that boundary.","alternatives":["Load shedding","Backpressure","Capacity scaling"],"tradeoffs":"Lower concurrency can reduce peak throughput when capacity remains available.","constraints":["The limiting resource can change dynamically.","Concurrency limits should be validated under realistic workload mixes."],"edge_cases":["CPU-bound and I/O-bound workloads require different concurrency reasoning.","Autoscaling can temporarily mask rather than solve contention."],"security":"Concurrency caps reduce resource-exhaustion attack surface.","performance":"Proper concurrency limits often improve tail latency under saturation.","testing":"Increase concurrency through and beyond the suspected bottleneck and observe useful throughput and tail latency.","verification":"Identify the concurrency range where throughput remains stable without runaway latency.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["resource telemetry","concurrency control"],"prerequisites":["D22-B12-004"],"relationships":["D21-B72","D21-B71"],"provenance":["Derived performance-reliability interaction"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B17-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B17","topic":"Failure Propagation","subtopic":"Cascading failure","concept":"Retry amplification","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"How can individually reasonable retries create a system-wide reliability failure?","answer":"Retries multiply request volume when downstream capacity is already degraded. Multiple layers can compound this multiplication, increasing load precisely when the dependency has the least capacity.","explanation":"If each of several layers retries independently, one original request can generate many downstream attempts. During overload this can prevent recovery and cause cascading failure.","principle":"Retries consume capacity and must be budgeted as part of total demand.","rule":"Avoid independent uncontrolled retries at multiple layers for the same operation.","examples":["Only one layer owns the retry policy while lower-level clients return failures without adding another retry loop."],"invalid_examples":["HTTP client retries three times, service retries three times, and gateway retries three times independently."],"problem":"Failure causes request amplification.","failure":"Dependency overload and prolonged recovery.","cause":"Layered retries without a global attempt budget.","solution":"Centralize retry responsibility or propagate an attempt/deadline budget.","alternatives":["Single retry owner","Global retry budget","Circuit breaker"],"tradeoffs":"Reducing retries may expose transient failures more quickly to callers.","constraints":["Retry ownership must be explicit.","The budget must include nested operations."],"edge_cases":["Different dependency layers may legitimately require independent recovery semantics.","A retry can be useful at one boundary but harmful at another."],"security":"Retry amplification can become a denial-of-service vector.","performance":"Retry storms increase network and CPU utilization.","testing":"Inject dependency failures and count attempts at every layer.","verification":"Verify total attempts remain within the declared budget.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["retry policy","deadline propagation"],"prerequisites":["D22-B05","D22-B11"],"relationships":["D22-B17-002","D22-B06-005"],"provenance":["Google SRE and Microsoft transient-failure guidance"],"evidence":"official_documentation","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B17-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B17","topic":"Failure Propagation","subtopic":"Retry cancellation","concept":"Retry cancellation by deadline","knowledge_type":"control_rule","question":"When should a retry loop stop even if its retry count has not been exhausted?","answer":"A retry loop should stop when the operation's deadline, cancellation state, retry budget, or semantic validity no longer permits another attempt.","explanation":"A fixed retry count is insufficient because remaining time and system state change between attempts. Continuing after the operation can no longer succeed within its useful lifetime wastes capacity.","principle":"Retry eligibility is a dynamic predicate, not a static count.","rule":"Before every retry, evaluate deadline, cancellation, attempt budget, error class, and operation semantics.","examples":["A third retry is skipped because only 20 ms remain before the end-to-end deadline."],"invalid_examples":["Always execute five retries regardless of remaining deadline."],"problem":"Retries continue after their result cannot be useful.","failure":"Wasted work and increased downstream load.","cause":"Retry policy uses count alone.","solution":"Make retry eligibility deadline-aware and cancellation-aware.","alternatives":["Budget token","Deadline-aware retry scheduler"],"tradeoffs":"Fewer late retries can slightly reduce recovery from slow transient faults.","constraints":["Deadline propagation must be trustworthy.","Error classification must distinguish retryable and permanent failures."],"edge_cases":["Retry delay itself may consume most of the remaining deadline.","Cancellation can arrive during backoff."],"security":"Retry state should not permit unbounded attacker-controlled delays or attempts.","performance":"Stopping useless retries reduces resource consumption.","testing":"Inject cancellation and shrinking deadlines during retry sequences.","verification":"Assert that no retry starts after cancellation or deadline expiry.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["retry controller","deadline"],"prerequisites":["D22-B17-001"],"relationships":["D22-B11-001","D22-B11-004"],"provenance":["Derived retry reliability principle"],"evidence":"official_documentation","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B17-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B17","topic":"Failure Propagation","subtopic":"Thundering herd","concept":"Recovery synchronization avoidance","knowledge_type":"failure_prevention","question":"Why can simultaneous recovery attempts overload a dependency that has just recovered?","answer":"Clients that observe recovery at approximately the same time can all retry or reconnect simultaneously, creating a burst larger than the recovered dependency can handle.","explanation":"A recovering system may have less capacity than normal while caches warm, replicas synchronize, and queues drain. Coordinated client recovery can recreate the outage.","principle":"Recovery traffic must be controlled as carefully as failure traffic.","rule":"Randomize and rate-limit recovery attempts instead of allowing all clients to reconnect simultaneously.","examples":["Clients use bounded jittered backoff and gradually restore concurrency after a dependency becomes healthy."],"invalid_examples":["Every client immediately retries all failed operations when a health probe changes to healthy."],"problem":"Recovery causes a second overload event.","failure":"Repeated outage or oscillation between healthy and overloaded states.","cause":"Synchronized retries and connection storms.","solution":"Use jitter, ramp-up, admission control, and gradual recovery.","alternatives":["Circuit-breaker half-open probes","Centralized traffic ramp"],"tradeoffs":"Slower recovery ramp can delay full utilization but reduces re-overload risk.","constraints":["Recovery signals can themselves be delayed or inaccurate.","The ramp policy must have an upper bound."],"edge_cases":["A dependency may recover only partially.","Clients restarted simultaneously after infrastructure recovery can create a cold-start herd."],"security":"Reconnect storms can resemble or facilitate denial-of-service conditions.","performance":"Controlled ramp-up improves stable recovery rather than immediate peak throughput.","testing":"Recover a failed dependency while thousands of clients are waiting and measure request/reconnect burst.","verification":"Verify recovery traffic remains below configured dependency capacity.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["backoff","jitter","health state"],"prerequisites":["D22-B05","D22-B06"],"relationships":["D22-B17-004","D22-B19-004"],"provenance":["Microsoft retry-storm guidance and Google SRE overload practice"],"evidence":"official_documentation","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B17-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B17","topic":"Failure Propagation","subtopic":"Retry storm","concept":"Retry budget enforcement","knowledge_type":"control_mechanism","question":"What is a retry budget and what problem does it solve?","answer":"A retry budget limits the amount of retry traffic relative to normal traffic or available capacity, preventing retries from consuming an uncontrolled fraction of system resources.","explanation":"Retry count per request does not bound aggregate retry volume. A retry budget provides a system-level control against retry amplification.","principle":"Aggregate retry traffic must be bounded independently of individual request retry limits.","rule":"Define and enforce retry budgets for high-volume dependencies.","examples":["A service allows retries only while aggregate retry traffic remains below a configured fraction of normal request traffic."],"invalid_examples":["Allow every request several retries with no aggregate retry ceiling."],"problem":"Normal traffic plus retries exceeds dependency capacity.","failure":"Retry-induced cascading failure.","cause":"No aggregate retry control.","solution":"Track and cap retry volume relative to baseline traffic or capacity.","alternatives":["Adaptive retry limiter","Circuit breaker","Server-provided retry guidance"],"tradeoffs":"Strict retry budgets can reduce recovery attempts during legitimate transient failures.","constraints":["The baseline must be measured reliably.","Budget policy must distinguish critical and noncritical operations if necessary."],"edge_cases":["Low-volume services can have unstable percentage-based budgets.","Bursty traffic can distort short windows."],"security":"Aggregate retry controls reduce amplification abuse.","performance":"Budgeting protects useful throughput during failure.","testing":"Inject sustained transient failures and verify retry traffic remains bounded.","verification":"Compare retry volume with configured budget over defined windows.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["retry telemetry","rate limiter"],"prerequisites":["D22-B17-001"],"relationships":["D22-B12-004","D22-B17-005"],"provenance":["Google SRE retry-budget practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B17-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B17","topic":"Failure Propagation","subtopic":"Error classification","concept":"Retryable versus non-retryable errors","knowledge_type":"decision_rule","question":"Why is error classification more important than retry count when designing reliable retries?","answer":"Retries help only when another attempt has a meaningful chance of success and does not create unsafe duplicate effects. Permanent, authorization, validation, and semantic errors generally should not be retried automatically.","explanation":"Retrying an invalid request consumes capacity without changing the cause. Some infrastructure failures may be transient, but even those require bounded attempts and deadlines.","principle":"A retry should be justified by expected recoverability and safe repetition.","rule":"Classify errors using explicit semantics rather than treating all non-success responses as retryable.","examples":["A temporary connection failure may be retryable; a malformed request should be returned immediately."],"invalid_examples":["Retry authentication failures indefinitely."],"problem":"Permanent failures consume retry capacity.","failure":"Wasted work, increased latency, and possible abuse amplification.","cause":"Status or exception handling lacks semantic classification.","solution":"Define retryability by error class, operation semantics, and server guidance.","alternatives":["Server-provided retry hints","Dependency-specific policy registry"],"tradeoffs":"Conservative retry policies may miss some transient recovery opportunities.","constraints":["Classification must remain synchronized with dependency semantics.","Unknown errors should default to bounded behavior rather than infinite retry."],"edge_cases":["A status code can have different meanings across APIs.","A transient-looking error can mask a permanent business condition."],"security":"Authentication and authorization failures should not be converted into retry storms.","performance":"Correct classification reduces unnecessary attempts.","testing":"Exercise each error class and verify retry behavior matches policy.","verification":"Maintain a machine-readable retryability matrix and test every defined class.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["error taxonomy","retry policy"],"prerequisites":["D22-B17-001"],"relationships":["D22-B05-001","D22-B13-002"],"provenance":["Microsoft transient fault guidance"],"evidence":"official_documentation","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B18-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Recovery Engineering","subtopic":"Recovery objectives","concept":"Recovery time versus recovery point","knowledge_type":"recovery_model","question":"Why must recovery time and recoverable state be modeled separately?","answer":"A system can recover quickly while losing recent state, or preserve all state while requiring a long restoration process. Recovery engineering therefore needs separate objectives for time to restore service and acceptable data loss.","explanation":"Recovery objectives describe different dimensions of failure impact. Treating them as one number hides important architectural constraints.","principle":"Service restoration speed and recoverable-state completeness are independent reliability dimensions.","rule":"Define both service recovery time and acceptable state-loss boundaries where data durability matters.","examples":["A service may require rapid read availability while asynchronous records have a separately defined recoverability window."],"invalid_examples":["Declare recovery successful because the process restarted even though accepted durable work was lost."],"problem":"Recovery status ignores data completeness.","failure":"Service appears healthy while state is incomplete or inconsistent.","cause":"Recovery is defined solely as process availability.","solution":"Separate service restoration, state recovery, and correctness verification.","alternatives":["Snapshot restoration","Journal replay","Redundant state"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger recovery objectives increase storage, replication, and operational cost.","constraints":["Objectives must be measurable.","Different data classes may have different recovery requirements."],"edge_cases":["A service can be available in degraded read-only mode before full state recovery.","External dependencies may have different recovery boundaries."],"security":"Recovery copies and backups require equivalent access controls and integrity protection.","performance":"More frequent durable recovery points can increase write overhead.","testing":"Simulate failure at multiple points between recovery checkpoints and measure resulting state and restoration time.","verification":"Report restoration time and recovered-state boundary separately.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["backup","durability","recovery procedure"],"prerequisites":["D22-B15-001"],"relationships":["D22-B18-002","D22-B19-001"],"provenance":["Derived disaster-recovery engineering principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B18-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Recovery Engineering","subtopic":"State restoration","concept":"Recovery ordering","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"Why does the order of component recovery matter in a distributed system?","answer":"Starting dependents before their required state or dependencies are ready can create retry storms, corrupt initialization state, or cause false health signals.","explanation":"Recovery is a dependency graph problem. Components should transition through initialization, dependency readiness, state restoration, and serving states in a controlled order.","principle":"Recovery readiness must follow dependency and state prerequisites.","rule":"Do not declare a component fully ready until its required state and dependencies satisfy serving criteria.","examples":["A service restores local state and verifies its database dependency before accepting normal traffic."],"invalid_examples":["Mark every process healthy immediately after process startup."],"problem":"Traffic reaches components before they are actually capable of correct service.","failure":"Startup storms, initialization errors, or inconsistent responses.","cause":"Process liveness is confused with application readiness.","solution":"Use explicit startup, recovery, readiness, and serving states.","alternatives":["Dependency-aware orchestrator","Warm-up phase"],"tradeoffs":"Strict readiness delays traffic but reduces false availability.","constraints":["Readiness checks must be bounded.","Dependency cycles require special recovery handling."],"edge_cases":["A degraded mode may be ready before full dependency recovery.","Some dependencies can be optional after initialization."],"security":"Readiness must not expose privileged state or bypass authorization.","performance":"Warm-up can consume resources before serving traffic.","testing":"Restart dependency layers in different orders and verify traffic admission behavior.","verification":"Verify readiness transitions occur only after required recovery predicates pass.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["health model","dependency graph"],"prerequisites":["D22-B13-002"],"relationships":["D22-B18-003","D22-B19-003"],"provenance":["Microsoft health endpoint and mission-critical design guidance"],"evidence":"official_documentation","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B18-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Recovery Engineering","subtopic":"Self-healing","concept":"Bounded self-healing","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"What distinguishes reliable self-healing from an uncontrolled restart loop?","answer":"Reliable self-healing uses explicit detection, bounded remediation attempts, cooldown, escalation, and verification of the resulting state rather than repeatedly applying the same action indefinitely.","explanation":"A restart can temporarily hide symptoms while worsening an underlying problem. Repeated automated remediation without diagnosis can create resource churn and destroy useful evidence.","principle":"Automation should stop when remediation fails to restore the required state.","rule":"Every automated recovery action requires an attempt limit, cooldown, and post-action verification.","examples":["A service restarts once after a confirmed deadlock, then escalates to isolation instead of restarting indefinitely."],"invalid_examples":["Restart a failing process every few seconds forever without verifying why it fails."],"problem":"Automated recovery repeatedly fails without changing system state.","failure":"Restart storm, evidence loss, resource churn, and prolonged outage.","cause":"No bounded remediation policy.","solution":"Use recovery state machines with bounded actions and escalation.","alternatives":["Human approval after threshold","Failover","Resource quarantine"],"tradeoffs":"Escalation can require human intervention and prolong degraded service.","constraints":["Recovery actions must be safe to repeat or explicitly guarded.","Verification must measure meaningful service state."],"edge_cases":["Transient failures can require multiple attempts.","A restart may change timing enough to mask a race condition."],"security":"Automated remediation must operate under least privilege and explicit authorization.","performance":"Repeated restarts can increase resource consumption and reduce observability.","testing":"Inject a persistent failure and verify automation stops after the defined recovery budget.","verification":"Confirm post-action health and verify escalation occurs after unsuccessful bounded attempts.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["recovery controller","health verification"],"prerequisites":["D22-B18-002"],"relationships":["D22-B18-004","D22-B20-002"],"provenance":["Derived self-healing reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B18-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Recovery Engineering","subtopic":"Fencing","concept":"Recovery-time fencing","knowledge_type":"safety_mechanism","question":"Why can recovery require fencing an old instance before activating a replacement?","answer":"A replacement can become active while the failed instance is still running or later resumes. Fencing prevents the old instance from continuing to modify shared authoritative state.","explanation":"Process-level failure detection cannot always guarantee that the old instance is truly stopped. External fencing converts uncertain process state into a controlled authority boundary.","principle":"Replacement activation is unsafe when stale instances can still perform authoritative actions.","rule":"Fence or otherwise invalidate stale authority before granting a replacement authoritative access.","examples":["A storage writer's old lease is invalidated before a replacement writer is allowed to commit updates."],"invalid_examples":["Start a replacement writer after a timeout while the old writer may still be connected to storage."],"problem":"Old and new instances can both write authoritative state.","failure":"Split brain and data corruption.","cause":"Recovery assumes failed instances are permanently stopped.","solution":"Use fencing tokens, leases, power control, storage-level generation checks, or equivalent authority invalidation.","alternatives":["Process termination with verified isolation","Consensus-based leadership"],"tradeoffs":"Fencing adds infrastructure and recovery complexity.","constraints":["The protected resource must enforce fencing.","The fencing mechanism must remain trustworthy during partial failure."],"edge_cases":["Network partitions can prevent operators from determining whether a node is alive.","A stale node may retain cached credentials."],"security":"Fencing must invalidate stale authorization, not merely signal the process to stop.","performance":"Fencing adds recovery steps before service restoration.","testing":"Partition an old writer, activate a replacement, then reconnect the old writer and verify all authoritative writes are rejected.","verification":"Verify resource-level rejection using generation or fencing state.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["leases","resource-level authority"],"prerequisites":["D22-B14-003","D22-B16-003"],"relationships":["D22-B18-005","D22-B14-004"],"provenance":["Derived distributed recovery safety principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B18-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B18","topic":"Recovery Engineering","subtopic":"Recovery convergence","concept":"State convergence verification","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"What does it mean for a recovered distributed system to converge?","answer":"Convergence means independently recovered components eventually reach a state consistent with the system's defined invariants and authoritative data model after transient recovery differences are resolved.","explanation":"Process availability alone does not establish correctness. Replicas, projections, caches, and derived state may require synchronization after failure.","principle":"Recovery is complete only when required correctness invariants converge, not merely when processes are running.","rule":"Define explicit convergence predicates for stateful distributed components.","examples":["A recovered projection catches up to the authoritative event position before being marked fully current."],"invalid_examples":["Declare recovery complete as soon as all processes report healthy."],"problem":"Recovered components contain stale or divergent state.","failure":"Incorrect reads, conflicting decisions, or repeated reconciliation errors.","cause":"No explicit state convergence criteria.","solution":"Track synchronization position, invariant checks, and reconciliation status.","alternatives":["Full state rebuild","Snapshot plus journal replay"],"tradeoffs":"Convergence verification delays full readiness but prevents serving inconsistent state.","constraints":["Authoritative source must be identifiable.","Convergence criteria must be finite and measurable."],"edge_cases":["Some derived state may intentionally remain eventually consistent.","A permanently corrupt replica may never converge and must be replaced."],"security":"State convergence must preserve tenant and authorization boundaries.","performance":"Reconciliation can consume significant I/O and CPU during recovery.","testing":"Create divergent replicas and verify recovery restores defined invariants.","verification":"Compare recovered state against authoritative checkpoints and invariant checks before full readiness.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["state reconciliation","health state"],"prerequisites":["D22-B18-001","D22-B18-002"],"relationships":["D22-B19-003","D22-B20-004"],"provenance":["Derived distributed recovery and verification principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B19-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B19","topic":"Reliability Observability","subtopic":"Health models","concept":"Liveness versus readiness versus correctness","knowledge_type":"observability_model","question":"Why should liveness, readiness, and correctness health states be separated?","answer":"Liveness indicates that a process is functioning enough to respond; readiness indicates that it can accept the intended workload; correctness requires stronger evidence that it is producing valid results.","explanation":"A running process can be alive while its dependencies, state, or data are unusable. Combining all states into one health flag creates unsafe traffic decisions.","principle":"Health is multidimensional and must match the decision being made.","rule":"Use the weakest health signal only for decisions that require that level of assurance.","examples":["A process may be alive but not ready for traffic while state recovery is still in progress."],"invalid_examples":["Use a process heartbeat as proof that business requests can be safely served."],"problem":"Health signals overstate service capability.","failure":"Traffic is routed to instances that cannot correctly serve requests.","cause":"Liveness is conflated with readiness or correctness.","solution":"Define separate health dimensions and consumers.","alternatives":["Readiness gates","Synthetic correctness checks"],"tradeoffs":"More health dimensions increase implementation and monitoring complexity.","constraints":["Health checks must not themselves overload dependencies.","Correctness checks should be bounded and representative."],"edge_cases":["A degraded service may be ready for limited traffic but not full functionality.","A deep correctness check may fail because of an optional dependency."],"security":"Health endpoints should reveal minimal sensitive operational information.","performance":"Deep checks consume resources and must be carefully bounded.","testing":"Induce dependency and state failures while keeping the process alive and verify each health state.","verification":"Map each health signal to the exact operational decision it controls.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["health endpoints","service state"],"prerequisites":["D22-B18-002"],"relationships":["D22-B19-002","D22-B19-003"],"provenance":["Microsoft health endpoint guidance"],"evidence":"official_documentation","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B19-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B19","topic":"Reliability Observability","subtopic":"Health checks","concept":"Dependency-aware health checking","knowledge_type":"design_rule","question":"What makes a health check useful for reliability decisions?","answer":"A useful health check evaluates the specific capability required by the decision and returns bounded, actionable status rather than merely confirming that a process responds.","explanation":"A shallow heartbeat can remain healthy during dependency failure. A dependency-aware readiness check can prevent traffic from being routed to an instance that cannot serve correctly.","principle":"Health checks should measure capability relevant to the action they authorize.","rule":"Do not include every dependency in every health check; include only dependencies whose failure should change the target health state.","examples":["A readiness check verifies database connectivity when database access is mandatory for serving requests."],"invalid_examples":["A health endpoint reports healthy because the HTTP process can answer while its mandatory database is unavailable."],"problem":"Health status does not represent serving capability.","failure":"Bad routing and delayed failure detection.","cause":"Health check semantics are disconnected from operational decisions.","solution":"Design checks around explicit readiness requirements.","alternatives":["Synthetic transactions","Separate dependency probes"],"tradeoffs":"Deeper checks improve signal but can increase dependency load.","constraints":["Checks need timeouts and concurrency limits.","Health endpoints should not recursively depend on the same failing path."],"edge_cases":["Health-check traffic can itself overload a degraded dependency.","A dependency may be temporarily unavailable while a degraded mode remains valid."],"security":"Health probes should use least privilege and avoid exposing secrets or sensitive state.","performance":"Health checks add traffic and must be sampled or bounded appropriately.","testing":"Disable mandatory and optional dependencies separately and compare expected health transitions.","verification":"Verify health output changes only when the corresponding capability changes.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["health endpoint","dependency classification"],"prerequisites":["D22-B19-001","D22-B13-002"],"relationships":["D22-B18-002","D22-B19-004"],"provenance":["Microsoft health endpoint guidance"],"evidence":"official_documentation","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B19-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B19","topic":"Reliability Observability","subtopic":"Recovery evidence","concept":"Recovery observability","knowledge_type":"verification_pattern","question":"What telemetry is necessary to determine whether recovery actually succeeded?","answer":"Recovery telemetry should capture detected failure, selected remediation, action outcome, state transition, remaining degradation, and verification evidence sufficient to distinguish process restart from real service restoration.","explanation":"Without recovery-specific evidence, operators may see a green process metric while the underlying failure persists.","principle":"Every automated recovery action needs observable evidence of its effect.","rule":"Record both the recovery action and the post-action state that justified declaring success.","examples":["A recovery event records dependency failure, failover action, state synchronization status, and final readiness state."],"invalid_examples":["Log only that a restart command was issued."],"problem":"Recovery action is visible but recovery outcome is unknown.","failure":"Repeated ineffective remediation and false recovery reporting.","cause":"Observability records actions but not outcomes.","solution":"Model recovery as an event sequence with explicit verification.","alternatives":["Distributed trace","Recovery state machine"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed recovery telemetry increases storage and schema complexity.","constraints":["Sensitive payloads must be minimized.","Telemetry must remain available during partial failure."],"edge_cases":["Telemetry systems can fail during the same incident.","A recovery action can succeed technically while business correctness remains broken."],"security":"Recovery logs may contain privileged operational data and require access control.","performance":"High-cardinality recovery telemetry can increase observability cost.","testing":"Simulate failed and successful recovery paths and verify distinct telemetry.","verification":"A recovery event must not transition to SUCCESS without defined evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["event logging","health state"],"prerequisites":["D22-B18-003","D22-B19-001"],"relationships":["D22-B20-002","D22-B20-004"],"provenance":["Derived reliability observability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B19-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B19","topic":"Reliability Observability","subtopic":"Failure correlation","concept":"Causal failure correlation","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_model","question":"Why should reliability telemetry correlate failures across dependency and resource boundaries?","answer":"Correlating request failures with dependency latency, resource saturation, queue growth, retries, and recovery events helps distinguish root causes from secondary symptoms.","explanation":"A timeout may be a symptom of connection-pool exhaustion, which may itself be caused by dependency latency. Independent dashboards can obscure this chain.","principle":"Reliability diagnosis requires temporal and causal correlation, not isolated metrics.","rule":"Preserve request or operation correlation across major reliability boundaries.","examples":["A trace links increased queue wait, connection-pool saturation, dependency latency, timeout, and retry events for the same operation."],"invalid_examples":["Analyze retries, dependency latency, and application errors as unrelated time series only."],"problem":"Secondary symptoms are mistaken for primary causes.","failure":"Incorrect remediation and prolonged incidents.","cause":"Telemetry lacks shared correlation identifiers and causal context.","solution":"Propagate trace/request IDs and record resource/dependency events with timestamps and scope.","alternatives":["Event correlation engine","Structured incident timeline"],"tradeoffs":"Correlation metadata increases telemetry volume but improves diagnosis.","constraints":["Identifiers must be propagated without leaking sensitive information.","Clock ordering must account for distributed timing limitations."],"edge_cases":["Missing telemetry during outages can create false causal gaps.","Sampling can remove critical evidence."],"security":"Correlation IDs must not contain secrets or sensitive business identifiers.","performance":"Tracing and correlation add overhead and storage volume.","testing":"Inject multi-stage failures and verify the resulting telemetry can reconstruct the sequence.","verification":"Attempt incident reconstruction using telemetry alone and identify missing causal edges.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["distributed tracing","structured telemetry"],"prerequisites":["D22-B19-003"],"relationships":["D21-B73","D22-B20-001"],"provenance":["Derived reliability observability practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B19-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B19","topic":"Reliability Observability","subtopic":"Alerting","concept":"Symptom-based reliability alerting","knowledge_type":"operational_rule","question":"Why should reliability alerting prioritize user-visible symptoms and actionable conditions over raw infrastructure events?","answer":"Raw events can be numerous and noisy while failing to show whether users are actually affected. Symptom-based alerts focus attention on service-level impact and actionable reliability degradation.","explanation":"An infrastructure warning does not necessarily imply service failure. Conversely, users can experience failures without a corresponding infrastructure alarm.","principle":"Alert on conditions that require action and represent meaningful service impact.","rule":"Every reliability alert should have an explicit trigger, scope, expected response, and suppression behavior.","examples":["Alert on sustained failed requests or unavailable critical operations rather than every transient connection reset."],"invalid_examples":["Page operators for every individual dependency timeout regardless of user impact or aggregation."],"problem":"Alert noise hides meaningful incidents.","failure":"Slow response to real failures or operator fatigue.","cause":"Alerts are tied to raw events without service semantics.","solution":"Aggregate symptoms, thresholds, duration, and dependency context into actionable alerts.","alternatives":["SLO-based alerts","Anomaly detection with human review"],"tradeoffs":"Symptom-based alerts can detect incidents later than low-level signals.","constraints":["Critical security or data-integrity events may require immediate low-level alerts.","Alert thresholds must reflect workload scale."],"edge_cases":["Low-traffic services may need synthetic probes because aggregate error rates are sparse.","A high error rate can be expected during controlled load tests."],"security":"Alert channels must protect sensitive incident information.","performance":"Aggregation reduces alert volume and telemetry processing overhead.","testing":"Inject low-level failures with and without user impact and verify alert behavior.","verification":"For every page, identify the exact service impact and remediation action it represents.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["service metrics","alert policy"],"prerequisites":["D22-B19-004"],"relationships":["D22-B20-001","D21-B73"],"provenance":["Derived SRE operational practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B20-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Fault injection","concept":"Failure-injection matrix","knowledge_type":"test_methodology","question":"What should a reliability fault-injection matrix contain?","answer":"A useful matrix identifies component, failure mode, scope, trigger, expected containment behavior, recovery path, verification signal, and unacceptable outcome.","explanation":"Random fault injection without expected outcomes produces weak evidence. A matrix connects each injected failure to a specific reliability invariant and recovery expectation.","principle":"A reliability test is valuable only when its expected system behavior is explicit.","rule":"Every injected fault must have a defined expected containment and recovery result.","examples":["Inject dependency timeout, identify affected scope, expect circuit protection, and verify unrelated traffic remains healthy."],"invalid_examples":["Randomly kill services and record only whether the process eventually restarted."],"problem":"Fault tests generate observations without proving reliability properties.","failure":"False confidence from superficial recovery.","cause":"No expected behavior or verification criteria.","solution":"Create structured fault scenarios linked to invariants and evidence.","alternatives":["Chaos experiments","Deterministic failure tests"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed scenarios require more design effort but provide stronger evidence.","constraints":["Production fault injection requires explicit safety controls.","Test scope must be isolated and reversible."],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous faults may invalidate assumptions made for single-fault tests.","Some faults require specialized infrastructure to reproduce."],"security":"Fault injection must be authorized and isolated from unrelated environments.","performance":"Large fault matrices can be expensive and should be prioritized by risk.","testing":"Execute failures across dependency, resource, network, state, and process boundaries.","verification":"Require pass/fail criteria tied to explicit reliability invariants.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["fault injector","observability","test oracle"],"prerequisites":["D22-B03","D22-B18","D22-B19"],"relationships":["D22-B20-002","D22-B20-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability testing methodology"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B20-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Recovery verification","concept":"Recovery success oracle","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"How should a reliability test determine whether recovery actually succeeded?","answer":"The test should evaluate post-recovery invariants such as availability, correctness, state consistency, dependency readiness, resource stability, and absence of continued failure propagation.","explanation":"Process restart or successful command execution is not sufficient evidence. A recovery oracle must test the property the recovery action was intended to restore.","principle":"Recovery actions must be judged by restored system properties, not by action completion.","rule":"Do not mark recovery successful solely because the remediation command returned success.","examples":["After failover, verify service readiness, data consistency, request success, and stable resource usage before declaring recovery complete."],"invalid_examples":["Declare recovery successful because the replacement process started."],"problem":"Remediation action is mistaken for recovery outcome.","failure":"False-positive recovery and hidden continued degradation.","cause":"No explicit recovery oracle.","solution":"Define post-recovery invariants and machine-checkable evidence.","alternatives":["Synthetic transaction","Invariant checker","State reconciliation check"],"tradeoffs":"Deep verification increases recovery time and test complexity.","constraints":["Verification must not itself create unsafe load.","Correctness predicates must be domain-specific."],"edge_cases":["A service can temporarily satisfy health checks while still draining stale work.","Some invariants may become observable only after delayed propagation."],"security":"Verification must not bypass normal authorization paths.","performance":"Post-recovery checks consume resources but can prevent premature traffic restoration.","testing":"Inject failure and require the full recovery oracle before test completion.","verification":"Recovery status transitions to SUCCESS only after all required predicates pass.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["health checks","state verification"],"prerequisites":["D22-B19-003","D22-B18-005"],"relationships":["D22-B20-001","D22-B20-004"],"provenance":["Derived recovery verification principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B20-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Recovery testing","concept":"Deterministic recovery replay","knowledge_type":"test_pattern","question":"Why should important recovery scenarios be reproducible instead of relying only on random fault injection?","answer":"Deterministic scenarios allow failures, timing conditions, expected state transitions, and recovery outcomes to be reproduced during debugging and regression testing.","explanation":"Random experiments can discover failures but may be difficult to replay. Deterministic tests transform discovered incidents into permanent regression coverage.","principle":"Discovered reliability failures should become reproducible verification cases whenever technically possible.","rule":"Convert significant production or test-discovered failure sequences into deterministic regression scenarios.","examples":["Record the sequence of dependency failure, timeout, queue growth, failover, and recovery state, then replay it in an isolated test."],"invalid_examples":["Rely on one successful chaos experiment without preserving a reproducible scenario."],"problem":"Previously discovered failures reappear without reliable regression coverage.","failure":"Repeated incidents and loss of institutional knowledge.","cause":"Fault discovery is separated from regression-test creation.","solution":"Capture scenario inputs, fault timing, expected transitions, and recovery oracle.","alternatives":["Recorded simulation","State-machine replay"],"tradeoffs":"Deterministic tests may omit unknown combinations that randomized testing can discover.","constraints":["Recorded scenarios must not contain sensitive production data.","Timing-sensitive tests require controlled scheduling or tolerance."],"edge_cases":["Some distributed races are inherently nondeterministic.","Production timing may be impossible to reproduce exactly."],"security":"Sanitize production-derived payloads before storing replay fixtures.","performance":"Large replay scenarios can be expensive and should be tiered.","testing":"Run deterministic scenarios repeatedly across software changes.","verification":"A previously fixed failure must remain non-reproducible under the regression scenario.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["scenario recorder","test harness"],"prerequisites":["D22-B20-001"],"relationships":["D22-B15-005","D22-B20-004"],"provenance":["Derived reliability regression-testing principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B20-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Invariant testing","concept":"Reliability invariant preservation","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"What is a reliability invariant and why is it stronger than checking whether a service remains reachable?","answer":"A reliability invariant is a property that must remain true despite defined failures, such as no unauthorized writes, bounded queue growth, preserved tenant isolation, or consistent authoritative state.","explanation":"Reachability only proves that a component responds. Reliability invariants test whether the system remains within safe and correct boundaries under failure.","principle":"Reliability testing should verify preserved system properties, not merely continued process execution.","rule":"Every critical reliability mechanism should have at least one explicit invariant that can be tested.","examples":["During dependency outage, unrelated tenant data remains isolated and no duplicate side effects are committed."],"invalid_examples":["Consider a service resilient because its health endpoint returns HTTP success during a database corruption scenario."],"problem":"Failure tests verify availability but not correctness or safety.","failure":"Silent corruption or invariant violation despite apparent uptime.","cause":"Test oracle is too shallow.","solution":"Define domain-specific safety and correctness invariants.","alternatives":["Property-based testing","Formal invariant checks","Model checking"],"tradeoffs":"Strong invariant testing requires deeper system modeling.","constraints":["Invariants must be precise enough to evaluate.","Some invariants span multiple services and require distributed evidence."],"edge_cases":["Eventual consistency requires time-bounded convergence rather than immediate equality.","Some degraded modes intentionally relax noncritical invariants while preserving safety invariants."],"security":"Authorization, isolation, and integrity invariants should remain mandatory during degraded operation.","performance":"Deep invariant checks can be expensive and should be separated from lightweight health checks.","testing":"Inject failures while continuously checking defined invariants.","verification":"Fail the test immediately when a mandatory invariant is violated, even if availability remains high.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["test oracle","state verification"],"prerequisites":["D22-B20-001","D22-B20-002"],"relationships":["D22-B18-005","D22-B20-005"],"provenance":["Derived formal reliability verification principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B20-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B20","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Combinatorial failures","concept":"Multi-fault reliability testing","knowledge_type":"advanced_test_methodology","question":"Why should reliability testing eventually include combinations of failures instead of only isolated single faults?","answer":"Real incidents can involve interacting faults, such as dependency latency combined with retry amplification or node loss combined with resource exhaustion. Some resilience mechanisms can fail only when multiple conditions overlap.","explanation":"Single-fault testing establishes basic containment. Multi-fault testing evaluates whether protective mechanisms compose safely under more complex conditions.","principle":"Reliability mechanisms must be tested both individually and in combinations that can interact materially.","rule":"Prioritize multi-fault scenarios where the combination can invalidate assumptions made by individual resilience controls.","examples":["Inject dependency latency while reducing worker capacity and verify bounded retries, backpressure, and recovery still operate correctly."],"invalid_examples":["Test circuit breaker, retry, and queue limits only independently and assume their composition is safe."],"problem":"Independent controls interact negatively under combined failure.","failure":"Unexpected amplification, deadlock, or recovery oscillation.","cause":"Reliability mechanisms were validated in isolation only.","solution":"Build risk-based multi-fault scenarios and verify system invariants.","alternatives":["Pairwise fault testing","Combinatorial test generation","Model-based failure simulation"],"tradeoffs":"Combinatorial coverage grows rapidly, so scenarios must be prioritized by dependency and risk analysis.","constraints":["Tests must remain bounded and reproducible.","Production multi-fault injection requires exceptional safety controls."],"edge_cases":["Three or more individually rare failures may interact materially.","Some combinations are physically impossible and should be excluded rather than generated blindly."],"security":"Multi-fault tests must not cross isolation boundaries unintentionally.","performance":"Complex fault scenarios can require significant infrastructure.","testing":"Use dependency graphs and failure-mode interactions to select high-value combinations.","verification":"Require explicit expected containment and invariant preservation for every selected multi-fault scenario.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["fault matrix","invariant checker"],"prerequisites":["D22-B20-001","D22-B20-004"],"relationships":["D22-B17-001","D22-B18-003","D22-B19-004"],"provenance":["Derived advanced reliability-testing principle"],"evidence":"research_and_engineering_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B21-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Reliability Objectives","subtopic":"Service objectives","concept":"Reliability objective decomposition","knowledge_type":"design_method","question":"How should a broad service reliability objective be decomposed into measurable operational objectives?","answer":"A service reliability objective should be decomposed into measurable dimensions such as successful request availability, latency within an agreed bound, correctness, durability, and recovery behavior where each dimension materially affects the service contract.","explanation":"A single availability percentage cannot represent all reliability properties. A service can be available but return incorrect data, exceed useful latency, or lose accepted work.","principle":"Reliability is multidimensional and should be represented by measurable properties that correspond to user and business expectations.","rule":"Do not use availability as the sole reliability objective when latency, correctness, durability, or recovery materially affect service value.","examples":["An API defines successful-response availability, latency compliance, data-integrity requirements, and recovery convergence separately."],"invalid_examples":["Declare a service reliable solely because its process responds to health probes."],"problem":"Important reliability failures remain invisible to a single availability metric.","failure":"False confidence despite correctness, latency, or durability degradation.","cause":"Reliability objective is under-specified.","solution":"Define separate measurable reliability objectives and their relationship to service behavior.","alternatives":["Composite reliability scorecard","Per-operation objectives"],"tradeoffs":"More objectives increase measurement complexity and can create conflicting optimization pressures.","constraints":["Objectives must be measurable from trustworthy evidence.","Objectives should remain stable enough to guide engineering decisions."],"edge_cases":["Different operations may require different reliability objectives.","A degraded mode may intentionally relax noncritical objectives while preserving safety-critical ones."],"security":"Security-critical correctness should not be traded away merely to satisfy availability objectives.","performance":"Additional measurement can increase telemetry cost.","testing":"Inject latency, correctness, durability, and dependency failures separately and verify each objective responds appropriately.","verification":"Map each objective to an observable measurement and an explicit failure threshold.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["service telemetry","reliability contract"],"prerequisites":["D22-B19","D22-B20"],"relationships":["D22-B21-002","D22-B21-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability engineering principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B21-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Reliability Objectives","subtopic":"Error budgets","concept":"Error-budget interpretation","knowledge_type":"operational_model","question":"What does an error budget represent in reliability engineering?","answer":"An error budget represents the amount of measured unreliability permitted by a defined reliability objective during a specified evaluation period.","explanation":"The budget converts a reliability objective into an operational quantity that can be consumed by failures, latency violations, or other explicitly defined bad outcomes.","principle":"Reliability objectives become operationally useful when deviations are represented as a finite budget.","rule":"Define exactly which events consume the budget before using it for engineering decisions.","examples":["If a service objective permits a bounded fraction of unsuccessful requests, measured failures consume that budget over the defined evaluation window."],"invalid_examples":["Treat any infrastructure warning as automatically consuming an error budget regardless of whether the service objective was affected."],"problem":"Budget accounting does not correspond to the reliability contract.","failure":"Incorrect engineering decisions based on misleading budget state.","cause":"Undefined or inconsistent bad-event definition.","solution":"Define bad-event semantics, measurement window, aggregation rules, and exclusion criteria.","alternatives":["Failure minutes","Operation-level reliability budget"],"tradeoffs":"More precise budget definitions improve usefulness but require stronger telemetry.","constraints":["The measurement population and evaluation period must remain explicit.","Excluded events must have documented justification."],"edge_cases":["Low traffic can make short-window percentages unstable.","Multiple objectives may have independent budgets."],"security":"Security incidents affecting service correctness should not be hidden through arbitrary budget exclusions.","performance":"Budget calculation should not materially affect request processing.","testing":"Inject known failure counts and verify budget consumption matches the defined formula.","verification":"Recalculate budget state independently from raw event data.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["reliability objective","bad-event measurement"],"prerequisites":["D22-B21-001"],"relationships":["D22-B21-003","D22-B22-001"],"provenance":["Google SRE reliability practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B21-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Reliability Objectives","subtopic":"Budget policy","concept":"Error-budget action policy","knowledge_type":"governance_rule","question":"How should an error-budget state affect engineering activity?","answer":"A budget policy should define what engineering actions are appropriate when reliability consumption is low, normal, or excessive, while preserving emergency fixes and safety-critical work.","explanation":"The value of an error budget is not the number itself but the decision policy connected to it. Policies should prevent reliability debt from accumulating invisibly.","principle":"Reliability measurements require explicit decision rules to influence engineering behavior.","rule":"Define actions for healthy, warning, and exhausted reliability-budget states without treating the budget as an absolute blocker for all engineering work.","examples":["Repeated reliability-budget exhaustion can trigger additional resilience work and tighter change controls for risky modifications."],"invalid_examples":["Ignore budget exhaustion because it has no effect on engineering decisions."],"problem":"Reliability objectives are measured but do not influence behavior.","failure":"Repeated reliability regressions without corrective investment.","cause":"No operational policy attached to budget state.","solution":"Define threshold-based review, remediation, and change-control actions.","alternatives":["Reliability review gates","Risk-based change policy"],"tradeoffs":"Stricter policies can slow feature delivery; overly weak policies permit reliability debt.","constraints":["Emergency security and safety fixes may require exceptions.","Policies should distinguish intentional experiments from uncontrolled regressions."],"edge_cases":["A single severe incident can matter more than many small failures.","Budget recovery may lag actual system improvement."],"security":"Security remediation should not be blocked solely by reliability-budget policy.","performance":"Change restrictions can reduce optimization velocity.","testing":"Simulate budget consumption across several release cycles and verify policy transitions.","verification":"For each budget state, verify that the expected governance action is triggered.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["error budget","change policy"],"prerequisites":["D22-B21-002"],"relationships":["D22-B21-004","D22-B28-001"],"provenance":["Google SRE error-budget practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B21-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Reliability Objectives","subtopic":"Measurement windows","concept":"Window sensitivity in reliability measurement","knowledge_type":"measurement_model","question":"Why can the same reliability behavior appear healthy in one measurement window and unhealthy in another?","answer":"Short windows are sensitive to individual incidents and traffic variability, while long windows smooth transient events and can hide recent deterioration.","explanation":"Reliability measurement is affected by aggregation interval, traffic volume, and event distribution. A single window cannot represent every operational question.","principle":"Reliability measurements must be interpreted with their time window and population.","rule":"Use windows appropriate to the decision being made and avoid treating one aggregate value as complete reliability evidence.","examples":["A long evaluation period may remain within objective while a recent short window shows a new persistent regression."],"invalid_examples":["Declare current health solely from a long historical average that masks a recent outage."],"problem":"Temporal aggregation hides emerging reliability degradation.","failure":"Delayed detection or incorrect engineering decisions.","cause":"Measurement window is not aligned with operational decision.","solution":"Use multiple windows or complementary recent and long-term measurements.","alternatives":["Rolling windows","Burn-rate style analysis"],"tradeoffs":"More windows increase monitoring complexity and alert volume.","constraints":["Window calculations must use consistent event definitions.","Low-volume services require careful statistical interpretation."],"edge_cases":["Sparse traffic can make percentages misleading.","Scheduled maintenance can create expected temporary changes."],"security":"Security-related failures may require immediate windows independent of long-term reliability objectives.","performance":"Additional aggregation is usually cheaper than additional raw telemetry.","testing":"Inject short severe incidents and long low-rate degradation and verify the measurement system distinguishes them.","verification":"Validate measurement results against raw event timelines.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["time-window aggregation","reliability telemetry"],"prerequisites":["D22-B21-002"],"relationships":["D22-B21-005","D22-B19-005"],"provenance":["Derived SRE measurement principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B21-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B21","topic":"Reliability Objectives","subtopic":"Multi-dimensional objectives","concept":"Reliability objective conflict","knowledge_type":"decision_model","question":"What should happen when improving one reliability dimension harms another?","answer":"The system should make the tradeoff explicit and prioritize dimensions according to business and safety semantics rather than optimizing a single metric blindly.","explanation":"For example, aggressive retrying may improve transient success rate while increasing latency and dependency load. Strong consistency may improve correctness while reducing availability during partitions.","principle":"Reliability optimization is constrained optimization across multiple objectives.","rule":"Do not declare an optimization successful without evaluating its effect on other reliability dimensions.","examples":["A retry-policy change is evaluated for success rate, tail latency, dependency load, and duplicate-side-effect risk."],"invalid_examples":["Increase retries because success percentage improved without checking latency or downstream saturation."],"problem":"Single-metric optimization creates hidden reliability regressions.","failure":"Improved local metric with worse system behavior.","cause":"Objectives are evaluated independently.","solution":"Use a multi-dimensional reliability evaluation matrix.","alternatives":["Weighted objective model","Hard safety constraints plus optimization target"],"tradeoffs":"Multi-objective analysis requires more instrumentation and decision complexity.","constraints":["Safety and correctness constraints may be non-negotiable.","Weights should not override hard correctness requirements."],"edge_cases":["Some dimensions cannot be directly combined into one scalar.","Short-term availability improvements may increase long-term recovery risk."],"security":"Security and authorization invariants should remain hard constraints.","performance":"Multi-dimensional measurement increases analysis overhead.","testing":"Compare reliability-policy changes across all relevant dimensions before and after injection.","verification":"Require no critical invariant regression even when the target metric improves.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["reliability objectives","telemetry"],"prerequisites":["D22-B21-001"],"relationships":["D22-B17-001","D22-B21-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability tradeoff principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B22-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Reliability Budgeting","subtopic":"Failure allocation","concept":"Reliability budget allocation","knowledge_type":"architecture_method","question":"How can an overall reliability objective be allocated across multiple service components?","answer":"A system-level reliability target can be decomposed into component and dependency budgets according to critical-path contribution, failure independence assumptions, and business importance.","explanation":"If every component independently consumes the entire system-level failure allowance, the aggregate system can exceed its objective. Budget allocation makes component reliability expectations explicit.","principle":"System reliability must be reasoned about across the composition of components, not only individual services.","rule":"Allocate reliability expectations according to system composition rather than copying the same target to every component.","examples":["Critical-path dependencies receive tighter reliability requirements than noncritical asynchronous integrations."],"invalid_examples":["Give every dependency the same reliability allowance without considering how many dependencies are composed on the critical path."],"problem":"Component reliability targets do not compose into the system target.","failure":"System-level reliability is worse than individual service objectives suggest.","cause":"No composition model.","solution":"Model critical paths and allocate reliability budgets based on contribution and dependency structure.","alternatives":["Top-down objective allocation","Risk-based dependency contracts"],"tradeoffs":"Allocation requires architecture knowledge and assumptions about dependency correlation.","constraints":["Correlated failures can invalidate simple independent composition assumptions.","Budgets must be periodically re-evaluated as architecture changes."],"edge_cases":["A single dependency may be shared by many apparently independent services.","Redundancy changes the reliability composition."],"security":"Security-critical components may require stricter constraints than availability-based allocation.","performance":"Budget modeling itself has little runtime cost but affects architecture decisions.","testing":"Compare predicted system reliability composition against fault-injection observations.","verification":"Verify that component budgets mathematically or logically support the system-level objective under stated assumptions.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["dependency graph","reliability objectives"],"prerequisites":["D22-B21"],"relationships":["D22-B22-002","D22-B24-001"],"provenance":["Derived reliability composition principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B22-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Reliability Budgeting","subtopic":"Correlated failures","concept":"Correlation-aware reliability analysis","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"Why can assuming independent failures produce dangerously optimistic reliability estimates?","answer":"Shared infrastructure, common configuration, software defects, deployment errors, and correlated environmental conditions can cause multiple components to fail together.","explanation":"Redundancy provides less protection when supposedly independent replicas share the same failure domain. Reliability analysis must identify common-cause failures.","principle":"Redundancy is only as strong as the independence of its failure domains.","rule":"Do not treat replicas as independent without identifying shared failure mechanisms.","examples":["Two replicas in the same availability domain can fail together due to a shared infrastructure problem."],"invalid_examples":["Multiply individual component availability values as if every failure were statistically independent without architectural evidence."],"problem":"Reliability estimates overstate protection from redundancy.","failure":"Common-cause outage despite nominal redundancy.","cause":"Shared failure domains are ignored.","solution":"Model common dependencies, deployment domains, configuration, control-plane, and environmental correlations.","alternatives":["Fault-tree analysis","Dependency graph analysis","Failure-mode correlation matrix"],"tradeoffs":"Correlation analysis requires more architecture and operational data.","constraints":["Unknown correlations must be treated as uncertainty rather than assumed independent.","Historical absence of correlated failure is not proof of independence."],"edge_cases":["A software release can simultaneously invalidate all replicas.","A shared certificate or configuration can create a common failure."],"security":"Shared credentials or policy systems can create common security failure domains.","performance":"No direct runtime cost; may alter redundancy architecture.","testing":"Inject shared-domain failures and compare results with independent-failure assumptions.","verification":"Document every major shared failure domain in the reliability model.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["failure-domain map","dependency graph"],"prerequisites":["D22-B22-001"],"relationships":["D22-B04-001","D22-B22-003"],"provenance":["Derived fault-domain reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B22-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Reliability Budgeting","subtopic":"Common-cause failure","concept":"Common-cause failure controls","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"What controls reduce the probability that one fault will disable multiple redundant components?","answer":"Controls include independent failure domains, configuration separation where justified, diverse infrastructure paths, independent deployment mechanisms, and elimination of unnecessary shared dependencies.","explanation":"Adding replicas without reducing shared dependencies can preserve a common failure path. Reliability improves when the causes of failure are meaningfully separated.","principle":"Effective redundancy requires diversity across relevant failure mechanisms.","rule":"For critical redundancy, identify and reduce the shared components capable of disabling all replicas.","examples":["Critical replicas use separate failure domains and avoid one shared control-plane dependency for serving traffic."],"invalid_examples":["Deploy multiple replicas but rely on one shared local disk or one unprotected configuration file."],"problem":"Nominal redundancy fails under common-cause conditions.","failure":"Simultaneous loss of redundant capacity.","cause":"Shared failure mechanism remains.","solution":"Map and reduce common dependencies and failure domains.","alternatives":["Geographic redundancy","Independent control planes","Diverse implementations"],"tradeoffs":"Greater independence increases operational complexity and cost.","constraints":["Diversity should target realistic common-cause risks rather than arbitrary heterogeneity.","Independent systems still require compatible contracts."],"edge_cases":["Excessive diversity can increase maintenance and configuration drift.","A global dependency can remain unavoidable."],"security":"Shared identity infrastructure can become a common security failure domain.","performance":"Cross-domain redundancy may add network latency.","testing":"Inject common dependencies and verify redundant paths fail independently when expected.","verification":"Review architecture for shared failure paths and validate with fault injection.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["fault-domain analysis","redundancy"],"prerequisites":["D22-B22-002"],"relationships":["D22-B04-002","D22-B24-002"],"provenance":["Derived resilience architecture principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B22-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Reliability Budgeting","subtopic":"Reliability debt","concept":"Reliability debt identification","knowledge_type":"governance_model","question":"What is reliability debt in an engineering system?","answer":"Reliability debt is accumulated architectural, operational, testing, or maintenance risk that increases the probability or impact of future failures without necessarily producing immediate functional defects.","explanation":"Examples include missing recovery tests, unbounded queues, undocumented dependencies, manual recovery procedures, obsolete failover paths, and insufficient observability.","principle":"Unaddressed reliability risk accumulates even when current functionality appears correct.","rule":"Track material reliability gaps as explicit engineering work rather than relying on incident memory.","examples":["A service with an untested failover path is recorded as reliability debt until the recovery path is exercised and verified."],"invalid_examples":["Assume no incidents means no reliability debt exists."],"problem":"Known resilience weaknesses remain invisible to planning.","failure":"Predictable failure modes recur during future incidents.","cause":"Reliability gaps are not represented as managed work.","solution":"Maintain reliability-risk records linked to architecture, tests, incidents, and remediation.","alternatives":["Risk register","Resilience backlog"],"tradeoffs":"Tracking debt adds planning overhead but makes hidden risk visible.","constraints":["Debt should be evidence-backed and risk-ranked.","Do not create debt records for speculative issues without supporting evidence."],"edge_cases":["A low-risk debt item can become critical after architecture changes.","Temporary mitigations can reduce risk without fully removing debt."],"security":"Reliability debt involving access control or isolation should be tracked as a security-relevant risk.","performance":"Some reliability debt directly causes performance instability under failure.","testing":"Convert each material debt item into a verification or remediation criterion.","verification":"Debt is closed only when the underlying reliability property is demonstrated, not merely when code changes.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["risk registry","verification evidence"],"prerequisites":["D22-B20"],"relationships":["D22-B22-005","D22-B28-002"],"provenance":["Derived reliability governance principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B22-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B22","topic":"Reliability Budgeting","subtopic":"Risk prioritization","concept":"Evidence-weighted reliability risk","knowledge_type":"decision_model","question":"How should reliability risks be prioritized without relying solely on subjective severity labels?","answer":"Prioritization should use evidence about failure likelihood, affected scope, impact, detectability, existing controls, and uncertainty, with explicit assumptions recorded.","explanation":"A severe but unsupported hypothetical can distract from an observed high-frequency failure. Conversely, rare catastrophic risks may require attention despite low observed frequency.","principle":"Reliability risk decisions should combine evidence, impact, uncertainty, and control effectiveness.","rule":"Record the evidence and assumptions supporting each material reliability risk decision.","examples":["A recurring dependency timeout with measured customer impact receives different treatment from an unvalidated theoretical failure mode."],"invalid_examples":["Mark every conceivable failure as critical without evidence or scope analysis."],"problem":"Reliability work is allocated using inconsistent subjective labels.","failure":"Important risks are missed or engineering capacity is wasted.","cause":"Risk model lacks evidence and explicit assumptions.","solution":"Use structured risk attributes and maintain provenance for each assessment.","alternatives":["FMEA-style scoring","Fault-tree analysis","Risk matrix"],"tradeoffs":"Evidence-based risk analysis requires more data collection.","constraints":["Historical frequency alone cannot represent rare catastrophic risk.","Risk values must be revisited after architecture changes."],"edge_cases":["New systems may have little historical evidence.","A single severe incident can justify controls despite limited frequency data."],"security":"Security-related reliability risks require consideration of intentional adversarial behavior.","performance":"Risk analysis has negligible runtime impact.","testing":"Compare risk predictions with subsequent fault-injection and incident evidence.","verification":"Every high-priority risk should have an identifiable evidence trail and control rationale.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["risk evidence","failure taxonomy"],"prerequisites":["D22-B22-004"],"relationships":["D22-B20-001","D22-B28-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability risk-analysis principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B23-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Graceful Degradation","subtopic":"Degraded modes","concept":"Explicit degraded-mode state","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"Why should graceful degradation be represented as an explicit system state rather than an accidental collection of failures?","answer":"An explicit degraded state allows the system to apply controlled behavior, communicate reduced capability, protect resources, and recover predictably.","explanation":"Without explicit degraded modes, individual failures can produce inconsistent fallback behavior across requests and components.","principle":"Degradation should be designed as a controlled operating mode with defined invariants.","rule":"Define what functionality remains available, what is disabled, and what correctness guarantees change in each degraded mode.","examples":["A service enters read-only mode when its write dependency is unavailable while preserving safe read operations."],"invalid_examples":["Allow random downstream exceptions to determine which features happen to remain available."],"problem":"Failure behavior varies unpredictably across components.","failure":"Inconsistent user experience and uncontrolled resource consumption.","cause":"No explicit degraded-mode contract.","solution":"Define named degraded states and transition criteria.","alternatives":["Feature-level fallback","Static maintenance mode"],"tradeoffs":"Explicit states require state management and testing.","constraints":["Degraded states must preserve mandatory safety and security invariants.","Transitions must be observable."],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous failures may require a more restrictive degraded mode.","A degraded state may need to expire if recovery does not occur."],"security":"Authorization and integrity guarantees must remain intact in degraded modes.","performance":"Degraded modes may reduce expensive work and preserve core capacity.","testing":"Inject dependency failures and verify deterministic degraded-state transitions.","verification":"For every degraded mode, verify allowed and prohibited operations.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["service state machine","feature controls"],"prerequisites":["D22-B07","D22-B19"],"relationships":["D22-B23-002","D22-B23-005"],"provenance":["Derived graceful-degradation principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B23-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Graceful Degradation","subtopic":"Feature isolation","concept":"Feature-level degradation","knowledge_type":"resilience_pattern","question":"How can feature-level isolation prevent optional functionality from causing primary-service failure?","answer":"Optional features should have independent dependencies, resource limits, and fallback behavior so that their failure does not block the critical user path.","explanation":"Feature-level isolation narrows the failure blast radius. A recommendation engine, analytics service, or personalization layer may fail without preventing the core transaction.","principle":"Optional functionality should not share an unbounded failure path with mandatory functionality.","rule":"Separate optional features from critical paths through asynchronous execution, bulkheads, timeouts, or explicit fallbacks.","examples":["Recommendation generation times out and returns no recommendations while the purchase flow remains operational."],"invalid_examples":["Block checkout indefinitely because recommendations are unavailable."],"problem":"Optional feature failure propagates to core functionality.","failure":"Availability loss for critical operations.","cause":"Optional and mandatory paths share failure boundaries.","solution":"Isolate optional features and define bounded degradation.","alternatives":["Feature flags","Async enrichment","Cached fallback"],"tradeoffs":"Isolation can increase architecture complexity and duplicate infrastructure.","constraints":["Optional status must reflect actual business semantics.","Fallback must not misrepresent missing data as authoritative."],"edge_cases":["A feature initially optional may become business-critical over time.","Feature dependencies can still share hidden common infrastructure."],"security":"Optional features must not bypass authorization when isolated.","performance":"Feature isolation protects critical-path latency.","testing":"Disable optional features independently and verify primary workflows remain within reliability objectives.","verification":"Trace critical requests and verify optional feature failures cannot block required completion.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["feature classification","dependency isolation"],"prerequisites":["D22-B23-001","D22-B13-002"],"relationships":["D22-B23-003","D22-B13-003"],"provenance":["Derived graceful-degradation practice"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B23-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Graceful Degradation","subtopic":"Priority preservation","concept":"Critical-work preservation","knowledge_type":"resilience_pattern","question":"How should a system preserve critical work during severe resource scarcity?","answer":"Work should be classified by importance and capacity should be reserved or preferentially allocated to operations whose failure has the greatest business or safety impact.","explanation":"Uniform resource allocation can allow low-value work to exhaust resources needed by critical operations.","principle":"During overload, preserving essential work is more reliable than treating every request identically.","rule":"Define explicit priority classes and enforce capacity protection for critical classes.","examples":["Interactive transactions retain reserved worker capacity while bulk analytics are throttled during dependency saturation."],"invalid_examples":["Allow background batch processing to consume all workers while interactive requests queue indefinitely."],"problem":"Low-priority work exhausts shared capacity.","failure":"Critical operations become unavailable.","cause":"No workload priority or reservation.","solution":"Use priority scheduling, reserved capacity, or separate resource pools.","alternatives":["Weighted fair queueing","Dedicated critical pool"],"tradeoffs":"Reserved capacity may reduce aggregate utilization during normal periods.","constraints":["Priority must not permit unbounded starvation of lower classes.","Emergency priority requires auditability."],"edge_cases":["A low-priority job may become time-critical after aging.","Incorrect priority classification can become a reliability vulnerability."],"security":"Priority cannot be granted solely from untrusted client input.","performance":"Priority scheduling improves critical-path tail latency under overload.","testing":"Saturate the system with low-priority work and verify critical traffic remains within bounds.","verification":"Measure resource allocation by priority class under sustained overload.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["scheduler","capacity policy"],"prerequisites":["D22-B12-003"],"relationships":["D22-B23-004","D22-B12-005"],"provenance":["Derived overload-control principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B23-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Graceful Degradation","subtopic":"Stale data","concept":"Stale-data safety classification","knowledge_type":"decision_model","question":"How should a system decide whether stale data is safe to serve during dependency failure?","answer":"The decision should depend on the data's semantic sensitivity, maximum acceptable age, downstream actionability, and consequences of incorrect or outdated information.","explanation":"Not all data has the same freshness requirement. Serving slightly stale descriptive data may be acceptable while stale authorization, inventory, or financial state may be unsafe.","principle":"Staleness tolerance is a domain property, not a generic caching property.","rule":"Assign explicit freshness classes to data used in degraded paths.","examples":["A cached product description can remain available while stale authorization state is rejected."],"invalid_examples":["Serve every cached value indefinitely during an outage."],"problem":"Fallback availability overrides domain-specific freshness requirements.","failure":"Decisions based on stale or unsafe state.","cause":"No semantic freshness classification.","solution":"Define freshness limits and degraded behavior per data class.","alternatives":["Fail closed","Read-only mode","Source switching"],"tradeoffs":"Strict freshness reduces availability during outages.","constraints":["Freshness metadata must be trustworthy.","Business owners must define acceptable staleness."],"edge_cases":["Clock differences can affect age calculations.","Data can be fresh but invalid due to upstream corruption."],"security":"Authorization and policy data should normally have strict freshness and integrity requirements.","performance":"Caching reduces dependency pressure but increases invalidation complexity.","testing":"Age cached values across freshness thresholds and verify mode transitions.","verification":"Ensure expired data cannot enter operations that require current authoritative state.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["cache metadata","domain freshness policy"],"prerequisites":["D22-B13-005"],"relationships":["D22-B23-005","D22-B19-002"],"provenance":["Derived data-reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B23-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B23","topic":"Graceful Degradation","subtopic":"Degradation exit","concept":"Safe degraded-mode exit","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"Why can leaving a degraded mode be more dangerous than entering it?","answer":"A recovering dependency may initially be unstable or have insufficient capacity. Immediate restoration of all normal traffic can overload it again or expose inconsistent state.","explanation":"Degraded-mode exit is a recovery transition that should use readiness, synchronization, capacity ramp-up, and verification rather than a single binary health signal.","principle":"Recovery from degradation requires controlled re-entry into normal load.","rule":"Exit degraded mode only after recovery predicates pass and traffic can ramp safely.","examples":["Restore optional features first, then gradually re-enable normal traffic after dependency synchronization and health verification."],"invalid_examples":["Immediately restore 100 percent traffic when the dependency returns one successful health response."],"problem":"Recovery transition causes renewed overload or state inconsistency.","failure":"Recovery oscillation and repeated outage.","cause":"No controlled degraded-mode exit.","solution":"Use staged recovery and post-recovery verification.","alternatives":["Canary restoration","Ramp-based admission"],"tradeoffs":"Gradual recovery delays full service restoration.","constraints":["Recovery criteria must be explicit.","Traffic ramp must respect dependency capacity."],"edge_cases":["The dependency may repeatedly flap between states.","A stale cache may hide incomplete recovery."],"security":"Recovery must not temporarily bypass authorization or policy checks.","performance":"Ramp-up controls tail latency during recovery.","testing":"Recover dependencies gradually and verify traffic restoration does not recreate overload.","verification":"Require stable health and correctness across a defined observation interval before full restoration.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["readiness","capacity ramp","health verification"],"prerequisites":["D22-B23-001","D22-B17-003"],"relationships":["D22-B18-003","D22-B20-002"],"provenance":["Derived recovery-control principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B24-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Redundancy","subtopic":"Redundant capacity","concept":"N+1 capacity planning","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"What does N+1 redundancy mean from a reliability perspective?","answer":"N+1 provides enough capacity for the required workload plus one additional failure unit, allowing service to continue after the loss of one unit under defined assumptions.","explanation":"Redundancy is meaningful only if remaining capacity after failure is sufficient for the intended degraded operating condition.","principle":"Redundancy must be sized against the workload that must survive failure, not merely replica count.","rule":"Validate remaining capacity after the defined failure event rather than assuming one extra instance guarantees resilience.","examples":["A service with N required worker units maintains one additional unit so loss of one unit does not violate the degraded capacity objective."],"invalid_examples":["Deploy two replicas for a workload that requires two replicas at full capacity and call it N+1."],"problem":"Nominal redundancy exists without sufficient post-failure capacity.","failure":"Immediate overload after one unit fails.","cause":"Redundancy measured by count rather than surviving capacity.","solution":"Model required capacity and surviving capacity under each relevant failure scenario.","alternatives":["N+2","Overprovisioned autoscaling","Active-active geographic capacity"],"tradeoffs":"Extra capacity increases cost but improves failure tolerance.","constraints":["Failure units must correspond to realistic fault domains.","Capacity assumptions must include degraded dependencies."],"edge_cases":["A shared dependency can fail despite redundant compute.","One failed unit may be larger than another, invalidating simple count-based models."],"security":"Redundant capacity should preserve isolation and access-control boundaries.","performance":"Extra capacity can reduce saturation but may remain idle.","testing":"Remove one failure unit at peak expected load and verify defined service objectives remain satisfied.","verification":"Calculate and test post-failure capacity against the degraded objective.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["capacity model","failure-domain model"],"prerequisites":["D22-B22-001","D22-B22-002"],"relationships":["D22-B24-002","D22-B24-003"],"provenance":["Derived redundancy engineering principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B24-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Redundancy","subtopic":"Active-active","concept":"Active-active failure redistribution","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"What reliability risks remain in an active-active architecture despite multiple serving instances?","answer":"Failure redistribution can overload surviving instances, expose shared dependencies, create state-consistency issues, or concentrate traffic into a remaining failure domain.","explanation":"Active-active improves availability only when remaining capacity, routing, state, and dependencies can tolerate the failure.","principle":"Redundant serving paths require validated failure redistribution, not merely multiple active instances.","rule":"Test traffic redistribution and dependency capacity after each meaningful failure-domain loss.","examples":["When one serving zone fails, remaining zones absorb traffic without exceeding their safe concurrency and dependency limits."],"invalid_examples":["Assume active-active is resilient because two instances are active under normal traffic."],"problem":"Failure shifts load onto surviving capacity.","failure":"Secondary overload and cascading failure.","cause":"Normal-state capacity is tested but failure-state capacity is not.","solution":"Model and test post-failure load distribution and shared dependency limits.","alternatives":["Active-passive","Partitioned cells"],"tradeoffs":"Active-active improves utilization and failover speed but requires stronger state and capacity coordination.","constraints":["Routing must detect unhealthy capacity accurately.","State replication must tolerate failure scenarios."],"edge_cases":["Partial network partitions can create asymmetric traffic.","A routing control plane may fail while data planes remain healthy."],"security":"Cross-domain traffic must preserve tenant isolation and authorization.","performance":"Traffic redistribution can temporarily increase latency.","testing":"Remove each active domain individually and verify surviving domains remain within safety limits.","verification":"Measure post-failure utilization, latency, errors, and state correctness.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["traffic routing","capacity","state replication"],"prerequisites":["D22-B24-001"],"relationships":["D22-B22-003","D22-B24-004"],"provenance":["Derived redundancy and failover principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B24-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Redundancy","subtopic":"Active-passive","concept":"Active-passive failover readiness","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"Why can an active-passive failover path fail when it has rarely served production traffic?","answer":"Standby systems can drift in configuration, software version, state, credentials, capacity, or operational knowledge. A path that is not exercised may not be genuinely ready.","explanation":"Standby status does not prove operational readiness. Failover systems require continuous validation and controlled exercises.","principle":"A failover path is part of the production system even when normally idle.","rule":"Continuously validate standby configuration and periodically exercise failover within safe controls.","examples":["A standby service validates configuration and data synchronization and undergoes controlled failover drills."],"invalid_examples":["Never test the standby because activating it would be disruptive."],"problem":"Failover discovers latent configuration or state defects during an incident.","failure":"Recovery path unavailable when primary fails.","cause":"Untested standby drift.","solution":"Automate configuration validation, state checks, and controlled failover exercises.","alternatives":["Active-active","Warm standby","Cold rebuild"],"tradeoffs":"Testing failover consumes operational capacity and can introduce temporary risk.","constraints":["Exercises need rollback and safety controls.","Standby must meet required recovery objectives."],"edge_cases":["Standby may depend on the same failed control plane.","Data synchronization can lag while status still appears healthy."],"security":"Standby credentials and access policies must be maintained securely.","performance":"Warm standby consumes resources but reduces recovery time.","testing":"Perform controlled failover and restoration while monitoring invariants.","verification":"Failover readiness requires successful state, configuration, dependency, and serving tests.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["configuration validation","state replication","failover controller"],"prerequisites":["D22-B18-002","D22-B20-002"],"relationships":["D22-B24-004","D22-B28-004"],"provenance":["Derived disaster-recovery and failover practice"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B24-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Redundancy","subtopic":"Failover routing","concept":"Failover routing safety","knowledge_type":"control_rule","question":"What must be verified before traffic is routed to a failover target?","answer":"The target must have valid configuration, required state, dependency access, sufficient capacity, current authorization, and readiness for the specific traffic it will receive.","explanation":"Routing a request to any reachable endpoint is not equivalent to safe failover. A failover target can be alive but incapable of correct service.","principle":"Failover routing is an authorization to serve workload and requires evidence of readiness.","rule":"Use readiness and capacity predicates rather than reachability alone when selecting failover targets.","examples":["A router excludes a recovered target until state synchronization and readiness checks pass."],"invalid_examples":["Route all traffic to the first endpoint that responds after primary failure."],"problem":"Traffic is sent to an unready or under-capacity failover target.","failure":"Secondary failure during recovery.","cause":"Failover decision uses shallow health evidence.","solution":"Require multi-dimensional failover eligibility.","alternatives":["Canary failover","Weighted traffic ramp"],"tradeoffs":"More eligibility checks increase failover latency.","constraints":["Eligibility checks must themselves remain available.","Traffic routing must avoid oscillation during partial failures."],"edge_cases":["Two targets may both appear healthy but lack enough combined capacity.","Routing state can become stale during network partitions."],"security":"Failover must preserve tenant routing and authorization policies.","performance":"Eligibility checks and staged ramp add recovery latency.","testing":"Fail each primary and secondary path separately and verify routing decisions.","verification":"Log the evidence that made a target eligible before traffic is assigned.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["routing","readiness","capacity model"],"prerequisites":["D22-B24-002","D22-B24-003"],"relationships":["D22-B18-004","D22-B23-005"],"provenance":["Derived failover safety principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B24-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B24","topic":"Redundancy","subtopic":"Redundancy degradation","concept":"Degraded redundancy state","knowledge_type":"operational_model","question":"Why should a system explicitly track when redundancy has been reduced even if service remains fully available?","answer":"Operating with fewer independent failure units increases exposure to the next failure and reduces the remaining safety margin. The service may be available while its resilience reserve is depleted.","explanation":"A system can remain operational after losing one redundant unit but become significantly more vulnerable to another fault. This state should be visible and actionable.","principle":"Availability does not imply full resilience reserve.","rule":"Track redundancy reserve separately from serving availability.","examples":["A service remains available after losing one zone but raises a resilience warning because only one independent zone remains."],"invalid_examples":["Return healthy status with no distinction after losing half the intended independent capacity."],"problem":"Reduced fault tolerance is invisible while service remains operational.","failure":"Second failure causes unexpected outage.","cause":"Health model measures availability but not resilience reserve.","solution":"Expose redundancy state and trigger restoration of lost reserve.","alternatives":["Capacity reserve metric","Fault-domain count"],"tradeoffs":"Additional state can create operational alerts during otherwise successful service.","constraints":["Reserve thresholds must reflect actual failure domains.","Warnings should not imply user-visible outage when none exists."],"edge_cases":["Autoscaling may restore capacity without restoring fault-domain diversity.","A standby can exist but be unready."],"security":"Redundancy loss in security-critical components may require immediate escalation.","performance":"No significant runtime cost beyond telemetry.","testing":"Remove one redundant domain and verify reserve state changes even while requests succeed.","verification":"Assert that full-health and full-resilience states are represented separately.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["fault-domain inventory","health model"],"prerequisites":["D22-B24-001"],"relationships":["D22-B19-001","D22-B22-003"],"provenance":["Derived resilience-reserve principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B25-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Configuration Reliability","subtopic":"Configuration failure","concept":"Configuration as a reliability dependency","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"Why should configuration be treated as a reliability-critical dependency?","answer":"Incorrect, missing, incompatible, or partially applied configuration can cause failures even when application code and infrastructure are healthy.","explanation":"Configuration controls timeouts, endpoints, capacity, feature flags, credentials, routing, and recovery behavior. A configuration defect can therefore have system-wide effects.","principle":"Configuration correctness is part of runtime reliability.","rule":"Version, validate, review, and safely deploy configuration using controls comparable to code changes.","examples":["A timeout configuration is schema-validated and rejected if it exceeds a defined safe maximum."],"invalid_examples":["Allow arbitrary runtime configuration values with no validation or rollback path."],"problem":"Configuration introduces unbounded or invalid runtime behavior.","failure":"Startup failures, overload, routing errors, or degraded recovery.","cause":"Configuration lacks schema and safety constraints.","solution":"Use typed schemas, validation, versioning, staged rollout, and rollback.","alternatives":["Immutable configuration","Central configuration service"],"tradeoffs":"Validation and rollout controls increase deployment complexity.","constraints":["Secrets require separate secure handling.","Runtime configuration changes need atomicity where partial application is unsafe."],"edge_cases":["A syntactically valid configuration can still be semantically unsafe.","Different service versions may require different configuration schemas."],"security":"Configuration often contains credentials and policy; access must be tightly controlled.","performance":"Configuration validation has negligible runtime cost compared with incident impact.","testing":"Inject malformed and semantically unsafe configurations and verify rejection before activation.","verification":"Validate configuration against schema, semantic constraints, and target-version compatibility.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["configuration schema","deployment system"],"prerequisites":["D22-B22-004"],"relationships":["D22-B25-002","D22-B28-003"],"provenance":["Derived configuration reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B25-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Configuration Reliability","subtopic":"Atomicity","concept":"Atomic configuration activation","knowledge_type":"failure_prevention","question":"Why can partially applying configuration changes create reliability failures?","answer":"Different components may observe incompatible configuration versions, producing inconsistent behavior and transient failure states.","explanation":"Configuration is often a coordinated contract. If some components use old settings while others use new settings, assumptions about endpoints, schemas, limits, or feature behavior can break.","principle":"Configuration transitions require consistency boundaries appropriate to the contract being changed.","rule":"Use atomic activation or compatibility windows when partial rollout can violate system invariants.","examples":["A new configuration becomes active only after all required consumers support its schema."],"invalid_examples":["Change a shared timeout or protocol setting independently across components with no compatibility period."],"problem":"Components operate under incompatible configuration assumptions.","failure":"Protocol errors, inconsistent routing, or cascading failures.","cause":"Configuration rollout lacks compatibility strategy.","solution":"Use atomic activation, versioned configuration, or backward-compatible transition periods.","alternatives":["Feature flags","Dual-read/dual-write compatibility"],"tradeoffs":"Atomic rollout can delay changes; compatibility periods increase temporary complexity.","constraints":["Not every distributed setting can be activated globally at once.","Rollback must preserve compatibility with partially updated components."],"edge_cases":["A failed control plane can leave some instances on different versions.","Configuration propagation delay can be longer than expected."],"security":"Security policy changes require especially strong consistency guarantees.","performance":"Configuration propagation adds control-plane traffic.","testing":"Introduce partial configuration rollout and verify compatibility and rollback behavior.","verification":"Record active configuration version per component and verify allowed version combinations.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["configuration versioning","rollout controller"],"prerequisites":["D22-B25-001"],"relationships":["D22-B25-003","D22-B28-004"],"provenance":["Derived distributed configuration reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B25-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Configuration Reliability","subtopic":"Safe defaults","concept":"Failure-safe configuration defaults","knowledge_type":"safety_rule","question":"What properties should a default configuration have when explicit configuration is missing?","answer":"Defaults should be bounded, conservative with respect to resource consumption and safety, and compatible with the service's expected correctness semantics.","explanation":"A missing configuration can occur during startup, migration, or control-plane failure. Unsafe defaults can turn configuration loss into a reliability incident.","principle":"Absence of configuration should not silently create unbounded resource consumption or unsafe behavior.","rule":"Every critical setting needs an explicit failure-safe default or must prevent startup until a valid value is supplied.","examples":["A missing concurrency limit causes startup rejection instead of unlimited concurrency."],"invalid_examples":["Use unlimited queue depth when the configured queue size cannot be loaded."],"problem":"Configuration absence activates unsafe behavior.","failure":"Resource exhaustion or incorrect service behavior.","cause":"Defaults optimize for continued startup rather than safe operation.","solution":"Define safe defaults and fail closed where no safe default exists.","alternatives":["Startup validation failure","Configuration bootstrap dependency"],"tradeoffs":"Conservative defaults can reduce performance or availability.","constraints":["Defaults must be documented and tested.","Security-sensitive settings may require explicit configuration."],"edge_cases":["A default can become unsafe as workload scale changes.","Environment-specific defaults can drift."],"security":"Security policies should fail closed where safe operation cannot be established.","performance":"Conservative limits can reduce throughput until correctly configured.","testing":"Remove configuration values and verify safe defaults or startup rejection.","verification":"Static and runtime tests should prove critical settings cannot become unbounded due to missing configuration.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["configuration schema","startup validation"],"prerequisites":["D22-B25-001"],"relationships":["D22-B25-004","D22-B12-001"],"provenance":["Derived fail-safe configuration principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B25-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Configuration Reliability","subtopic":"Configuration drift","concept":"Configuration drift detection","knowledge_type":"operational_control","question":"Why is configuration drift a reliability risk in replicated systems?","answer":"Instances that differ unexpectedly can respond differently to identical traffic, making failures inconsistent and difficult to diagnose or recover.","explanation":"Drift can affect versions, timeouts, feature flags, endpoints, resource limits, and security policies. It can create partial failures that appear random.","principle":"Replicated capacity should have controlled and observable configuration identity.","rule":"Detect unexplained configuration divergence before it causes inconsistent behavior.","examples":["Instances expose a configuration version or hash and orchestration rejects unexpected divergence."],"invalid_examples":["Assume all replicas have identical configuration because they were originally deployed from the same template."],"problem":"Replicas behave differently due to hidden configuration differences.","failure":"Partial outages and difficult incident diagnosis.","cause":"No configuration identity or drift detection.","solution":"Version configuration and continuously compare expected versus active state.","alternatives":["Immutable images","Configuration management enforcement"],"tradeoffs":"Drift detection requires metadata collection and reconciliation.","constraints":["Secrets should not be exposed through configuration hashes or diagnostics.","Some instance-local settings are intentionally different and must be modeled as such."],"edge_cases":["Temporary rollout states can appear as drift but be intentional.","A configuration can match by version while external referenced data differs."],"security":"Configuration drift can weaken authorization or isolation controls.","performance":"Continuous drift checking adds small control-plane overhead.","testing":"Modify one replica's configuration and verify detection and remediation.","verification":"Compare expected configuration identity with active state across all serving instances.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["configuration identity","state inventory"],"prerequisites":["D22-B25-001"],"relationships":["D22-B25-005","D22-B24-005"],"provenance":["Derived configuration-management reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B25-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B25","topic":"Configuration Reliability","subtopic":"Dynamic configuration","concept":"Safe dynamic configuration changes","knowledge_type":"control_pattern","question":"What controls are required when configuration can change without restarting the service?","answer":"Dynamic changes require schema validation, authorization, versioning, compatibility checks, bounded rollout, observability, and a reliable rollback mechanism.","explanation":"Dynamic configuration reduces deployment friction but can bypass some protections normally provided by immutable deployment artifacts. A bad change can affect all live instances immediately.","principle":"Runtime configurability increases the importance of change controls.","rule":"Never allow dynamic configuration to bypass validation, authorization, auditability, or rollback.","examples":["A runtime concurrency-limit change is validated against safe bounds, staged, observed, and reversible."],"invalid_examples":["Accept arbitrary runtime configuration from an administrator and apply it immediately to every instance."],"problem":"A bad configuration can propagate faster than it can be diagnosed.","failure":"Rapid system-wide degradation.","cause":"Dynamic control path lacks safety mechanisms.","solution":"Implement versioned, audited, staged, bounded configuration updates.","alternatives":["Restart-based immutable configuration","Canary configuration"],"tradeoffs":"Strong controls reduce the speed advantage of dynamic configuration.","constraints":["Configuration updates need ownership and authorization.","Rollback configuration must itself remain valid."],"edge_cases":["Rollback may restore a configuration incompatible with a newer software version.","A control-plane outage may interrupt a rollout halfway."],"security":"Configuration mutation is a privileged operation requiring strong authorization and audit logging.","performance":"Dynamic updates avoid restart cost but can create control-plane traffic.","testing":"Inject invalid and harmful configuration changes and verify rejection, containment, and rollback.","verification":"Every accepted configuration version must have validation and audit evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["configuration control plane","audit log"],"prerequisites":["D22-B25-002","D22-B25-004"],"relationships":["D22-B28-001","D22-B28-005"],"provenance":["Derived runtime-configuration reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B26-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Deployment Reliability","subtopic":"Progressive delivery","concept":"Progressive reliability validation","knowledge_type":"deployment_pattern","question":"Why should reliability-sensitive changes be exposed progressively instead of immediately to the entire serving population?","answer":"Progressive exposure limits blast radius and provides an opportunity to detect reliability regressions before they affect the full workload.","explanation":"A change can pass unit and integration tests but fail under production traffic characteristics. Small exposure creates an observable safety boundary.","principle":"Change exposure should increase only when evidence supports the next level of risk.","rule":"Use staged exposure for changes whose failure impact exceeds the safety of immediate full rollout.","examples":["A new version receives a small traffic share while error, latency, dependency, and resource behavior are monitored."],"invalid_examples":["Deploy a reliability-sensitive change to all instances simultaneously without an observation stage."],"problem":"A defective release affects the entire service before evidence is available.","failure":"Large-scale outage or rollback pressure.","cause":"No blast-radius control during deployment.","solution":"Use canary, phased, or ring-based rollout with explicit promotion criteria.","alternatives":["Blue-green deployment","Shadow traffic"],"tradeoffs":"Progressive rollout takes longer and requires routing and observability infrastructure.","constraints":["Promotion criteria must be measurable.","Canary traffic must be representative enough to expose relevant failures."],"edge_cases":["Rare failures may not appear during small exposure.","A change can affect only specific tenants or workloads not represented in the canary."],"security":"Security-sensitive changes need exposure criteria that include authorization and isolation behavior.","performance":"Canary monitoring adds operational overhead but reduces large regression cost.","testing":"Introduce controlled faulty versions and verify rollout halts before full exposure.","verification":"Promotion must require passing reliability predicates, not only deployment health.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["traffic routing","release monitoring"],"prerequisites":["D22-B19","D22-B20"],"relationships":["D22-B26-002","D22-B28-003"],"provenance":["Derived progressive-delivery reliability principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B26-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Deployment Reliability","subtopic":"Canary analysis","concept":"Canary reliability criteria","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"What should be compared during a reliability-focused canary deployment?","answer":"The canary should be compared with an appropriate baseline across error rates, latency distribution, resource behavior, dependency load, correctness signals, and relevant workload-specific outcomes.","explanation":"A canary can appear healthy using a single metric while introducing regressions elsewhere. Comparative analysis is stronger than checking absolute process health.","principle":"Canary validation should detect differential behavior attributable to the change.","rule":"Define baseline and canary comparison dimensions before rollout.","examples":["Compare tail latency and downstream request volume for equivalent traffic routed to old and new versions."],"invalid_examples":["Promote a canary because its process health is green."],"problem":"Subtle regressions are missed during staged rollout.","failure":"Defective version progresses to wider deployment.","cause":"Canary oracle is too shallow.","solution":"Use multi-dimensional comparative criteria with explicit thresholds and uncertainty handling.","alternatives":["Automated statistical analysis","Shadow execution"],"tradeoffs":"More dimensions increase analysis complexity and false-positive risk.","constraints":["Traffic must be comparable enough for valid comparison.","Known workload differences must be accounted for."],"edge_cases":["Small canary samples may be statistically weak.","A shared dependency can cause both baseline and canary to degrade simultaneously."],"security":"Canaries must be checked for authorization and tenant-isolation regressions.","performance":"Detailed comparison increases telemetry cost.","testing":"Deploy a known degraded version and verify canary analysis blocks promotion.","verification":"Promotion decision must reference recorded evidence across predefined criteria.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["canary traffic","telemetry","baseline"],"prerequisites":["D22-B26-001"],"relationships":["D22-B26-003","D22-B21-005"],"provenance":["Derived progressive-delivery verification principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B26-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Deployment Reliability","subtopic":"Rollback","concept":"Rollback readiness","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"Why must rollback be treated as a tested recovery mechanism rather than an assumed deployment operation?","answer":"Rollback can fail because data schemas, configuration, state, migrations, dependencies, or irreversible side effects have changed since the previous version.","explanation":"Returning code to an earlier version does not automatically restore the previous system state. Rollback must account for compatibility and persistent changes.","principle":"A rollback is a state transition with compatibility requirements, not merely a version pointer change.","rule":"Test rollback against the actual state and data transitions introduced by the change.","examples":["A schema migration is backward-compatible before rollout so the previous application version can continue operating if rollback is required."],"invalid_examples":["Assume an application can always revert to the previous binary after any deployment."],"problem":"Rollback leaves persistent state incompatible with the previous version.","failure":"Extended outage during attempted recovery.","cause":"Rollback compatibility was not designed or tested.","solution":"Use backward-compatible migrations, reversible configuration, and tested rollback procedures.","alternatives":["Forward fix","Blue-green switch","Restore from snapshot"],"tradeoffs":"Backward compatibility can increase migration complexity.","constraints":["Some data transformations are irreversible.","External side effects may not be rollbackable."],"edge_cases":["A rollback can restore availability while leaving already-created external effects unchanged.","Multiple releases may share one persistent schema."],"security":"Rollback must not restore obsolete security vulnerabilities or credentials.","performance":"Compatibility layers can add temporary overhead.","testing":"Deploy a change, apply its state transition, then execute rollback and verify correctness.","verification":"Rollback success requires both service readiness and state compatibility.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["deployment artifact","schema migration","configuration"],"prerequisites":["D22-B25","D22-B26-001"],"relationships":["D22-B26-004","D22-B28-004"],"provenance":["Derived deployment recovery principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B26-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Deployment Reliability","subtopic":"Forward recovery","concept":"Forward-fix versus rollback decision","knowledge_type":"decision_model","question":"When can a forward fix be safer than rollback?","answer":"A forward fix may be safer when the defect is localized, rollback is incompatible with persistent state or external effects, or the rollback path itself has higher uncertainty than a controlled corrective release.","explanation":"Rollback is not inherently safer. The decision depends on state compatibility, blast radius, recovery time, and confidence in the corrective change.","principle":"Recovery strategy should be selected based on system state and evidence, not on a universal preference for rollback.","rule":"Evaluate rollback compatibility and forward-fix scope before selecting the recovery path.","examples":["A schema-compatible hotfix may be safer than reverting an application after an irreversible data migration."],"invalid_examples":["Always roll back immediately without checking whether the previous version can safely operate on current state."],"problem":"Recovery action introduces a second incompatible failure.","failure":"Extended outage or data inconsistency.","cause":"Rollback treated as universally reversible.","solution":"Use a decision matrix covering state, compatibility, blast radius, and verified remediation.","alternatives":["Rollback","Forward fix","Traffic isolation"],"tradeoffs":"Forward fixes can introduce additional change during an incident; rollback can expose older incompatibilities.","constraints":["Emergency changes require strong verification and scope control.","Data integrity takes precedence over speed."],"edge_cases":["The original defect may continue producing side effects while the decision is made.","Both rollback and forward fix may be unsafe until traffic is reduced."],"security":"Do not choose a rollback that restores known critical security weaknesses.","performance":"Traffic reduction may be required before either recovery strategy.","testing":"Simulate rollback-incompatible and rollback-compatible failures and verify decision logic.","verification":"Record the evidence supporting the selected recovery path.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["deployment state","recovery decision model"],"prerequisites":["D22-B26-003"],"relationships":["D22-B18-003","D22-B28-005"],"provenance":["Derived change-recovery principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B26-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B26","topic":"Deployment Reliability","subtopic":"Change correlation","concept":"Deployment-to-failure correlation","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"Why should reliability monitoring correlate incidents with recent changes?","answer":"Many reliability regressions are temporally associated with deployments, configuration changes, dependency changes, or infrastructure modifications. Correlation narrows investigation scope without proving causation.","explanation":"Temporal proximity is useful diagnostic evidence but must not be treated as proof. Multiple simultaneous changes or external events can produce the same symptoms.","principle":"Change history is important incident context, but correlation must remain distinct from causal proof.","rule":"Include deployment and configuration events in reliability timelines and investigate them against independent evidence.","examples":["An error-rate increase begins shortly after a release; investigators compare changed code paths and dependency behavior before attributing causality."],"invalid_examples":["Automatically blame the latest deployment whenever an incident occurs."],"problem":"Incident investigation lacks change context or over-attributes causality.","failure":"Slow diagnosis or incorrect rollback.","cause":"Deployment events are absent from or overused in incident analysis.","solution":"Correlate changes with symptoms, traces, logs, and controlled comparison.","alternatives":["Automated change-point detection","Release annotations"],"tradeoffs":"Change correlation can create false attribution if used without independent evidence.","constraints":["All significant changes need accurate timestamps and identity.","Historical change metadata must remain accessible."],"edge_cases":["Infrastructure and dependency changes can occur simultaneously.","A latent bug may be exposed by unrelated traffic growth."],"security":"Change logs should be protected against tampering.","performance":"Metadata correlation has low runtime cost.","testing":"Inject a known deployment regression and verify correlation surfaces it without automatically asserting causality.","verification":"Incident records should distinguish temporal correlation from confirmed causal evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["deployment metadata","reliability telemetry"],"prerequisites":["D22-B19-004"],"relationships":["D22-B28-001","D22-B29-003"],"provenance":["Derived incident-analysis principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B27-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Data Reliability","subtopic":"Durability","concept":"Durability boundary identification","knowledge_type":"architecture_model","question":"How should a system identify the point at which accepted data becomes durable enough to survive a defined failure?","answer":"The durability boundary is the storage or replication event after which the system's recovery model guarantees preservation against the specified failure class.","explanation":"A successful API response does not automatically mean data is durable. The guarantee depends on write ordering, replication, persistence, and failure assumptions.","principle":"Durability is a defined guarantee against a defined failure model.","rule":"Document which failure classes each persistence boundary protects against and when acknowledgement is permitted.","examples":["A write is acknowledged only after it reaches the durability level required by the service contract."],"invalid_examples":["Return success immediately after placing data in volatile memory while claiming crash durability."],"problem":"Acknowledged data is lost after failure.","failure":"Data loss despite apparent successful completion.","cause":"Durability boundary is undefined or weaker than the contract.","solution":"Define persistence and replication guarantees and align acknowledgement semantics.","alternatives":["Synchronous replication","Durable log","Transactional commit"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger durability generally increases latency and resource cost.","constraints":["Hardware and storage guarantees must be understood.","Replication may not protect against common-cause corruption."],"edge_cases":["A replicated write can survive node loss but not logical deletion.","Storage acknowledgements may not represent application-level durability."],"security":"Durable data requires integrity and access controls throughout recovery paths.","performance":"Durability often adds I/O and synchronization latency.","testing":"Terminate processes or nodes at different write stages and verify the documented durability boundary.","verification":"Compare acknowledged records with recovered state after each defined failure class.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["persistent storage","replication"],"prerequisites":["D22-B18-001"],"relationships":["D22-B27-002","D22-B18-005"],"provenance":["Derived data durability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B27-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Data Reliability","subtopic":"Corruption","concept":"Corruption detection versus availability","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"Why can corrupted data be more dangerous than unavailable data?","answer":"Unavailable data can trigger a visible failure and recovery path, while corrupted data may remain available and silently produce incorrect decisions or state transitions.","explanation":"Reliability includes integrity. Systems need validation, checksums, consistency checks, version markers, or domain invariants to detect invalid state.","principle":"A successful read is not evidence that the returned state is correct.","rule":"Define integrity checks for data whose corruption would materially affect system correctness.","examples":["A recovered state is checked against checksums or domain invariants before being promoted to authoritative use."],"invalid_examples":["Treat any successfully parsed database row as valid without integrity or domain validation."],"problem":"Corrupted state remains operationally visible as if correct.","failure":"Silent incorrect behavior.","cause":"Availability is measured without integrity validation.","solution":"Add integrity and semantic consistency verification at recovery and critical decision boundaries.","alternatives":["Checksums","Merkle structures","Domain invariant checks"],"tradeoffs":"Integrity validation adds CPU and storage overhead.","constraints":["Validation must match the corruption model.","Checksums detect alteration but do not prove semantic correctness."],"edge_cases":["Corruption can be introduced by valid but incorrect application writes.","Partial corruption may affect only one partition."],"security":"Integrity validation is essential against unauthorized modification.","performance":"Deep validation may require asynchronous or staged checking.","testing":"Inject corrupted records and verify detection before authoritative use.","verification":"Recovered state must pass defined integrity predicates before serving critical operations.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["integrity metadata","state validation"],"prerequisites":["D22-B27-001"],"relationships":["D22-B18-005","D22-B20-004"],"provenance":["Derived data-integrity reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B27-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Data Reliability","subtopic":"Backup validation","concept":"Backup recoverability verification","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"Why is successful backup creation insufficient evidence of recoverability?","answer":"A backup can exist while being incomplete, incompatible, corrupted, inaccessible, or impossible to restore within required recovery constraints.","explanation":"Backup systems prove preservation only when restoration and integrity can be demonstrated. Recovery tests therefore need to exercise the complete restore path.","principle":"A backup is a reliability control only when its restoration properties are verified.","rule":"Periodically restore representative backups in an isolated environment and validate integrity and recoverability.","examples":["A backup is restored into an isolated environment and checked for schema, state consistency, and application readability."],"invalid_examples":["Assume backups are valid because the backup job reports success."],"problem":"Backup failure is discovered only during a real disaster.","failure":"Unrecoverable or incomplete state.","cause":"Backup pipeline is tested only at creation time.","solution":"Use restore drills and integrity verification.","alternatives":["Continuous replication","Snapshot plus restore verification"],"tradeoffs":"Restore testing consumes storage and operational resources.","constraints":["Restored data must remain isolated and protected.","Testing frequency should reflect recovery risk."],"edge_cases":["A backup may restore but not meet recovery-time objectives.","Credentials and external dependencies may prevent a restored application from serving."],"security":"Restored sensitive data requires equivalent or stronger protection.","performance":"Large restore tests can be resource intensive.","testing":"Restore backups across multiple failure scenarios and verify application-level correctness.","verification":"Record restore success, integrity checks, and recovery duration as independent evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["backup system","restore environment"],"prerequisites":["D22-B27-001","D22-B18-001"],"relationships":["D22-B27-004","D22-B20-002"],"provenance":["Derived disaster-recovery verification principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B27-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Data Reliability","subtopic":"Backup consistency","concept":"Application-consistent backup","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"Why can a technically valid storage snapshot still be insufficient for application recovery?","answer":"A storage-level snapshot may capture data files at inconsistent logical points when multiple components require coordinated state.","explanation":"Application correctness can depend on transaction logs, metadata, external state, or multiple data stores. A crash-consistent snapshot is not automatically application-consistent.","principle":"Recovery correctness depends on logical state consistency, not only storage durability.","rule":"Use coordinated snapshots, transactional logs, or application-aware recovery when multiple state components must remain consistent.","examples":["Database data and required transaction logs are captured according to the database's recovery protocol."],"invalid_examples":["Copy several unrelated data stores independently and assume the resulting snapshot represents one valid application state."],"problem":"Restored components represent different logical times.","failure":"Inconsistent application state after recovery.","cause":"Backup boundary does not match application transaction boundary.","solution":"Define application-consistent backup and restore semantics.","alternatives":["Transactional outbox","Coordinated checkpoint","Event replay"],"tradeoffs":"Application consistency increases backup complexity and coordination cost.","constraints":["Every stateful component must be included in the consistency model.","External side effects may remain outside the snapshot."],"edge_cases":["Distributed transactions may span systems that cannot be snapshotted atomically.","Eventual consistency requires convergence rather than exact simultaneous state."],"security":"Backup coordination must preserve data-access boundaries.","performance":"Coordinated checkpoints can temporarily increase I/O or latency.","testing":"Restore snapshots taken during active writes and verify application invariants.","verification":"Run application-level consistency checks after restore.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["transaction log","snapshot mechanism"],"prerequisites":["D22-B27-001"],"relationships":["D22-B27-005","D22-B18-005"],"provenance":["Derived backup-consistency principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B27-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B27","topic":"Data Reliability","subtopic":"Recovery integrity","concept":"Recovery reconciliation","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"When should recovered state be reconciled against an authoritative source?","answer":"Reconciliation is appropriate when recovery can produce missing, duplicated, stale, or divergent state and an authoritative source exists that can establish the correct result.","explanation":"Restoration can recover technically valid data that is incomplete relative to events or external systems. Reconciliation makes correctness explicit after recovery.","principle":"Recovered state should be compared with authoritative evidence when restoration alone cannot guarantee semantic completeness.","rule":"Define reconciliation criteria whenever recovery can leave unresolved state differences.","examples":["A rebuilt projection reconciles processed event position and derived records against the authoritative event log."],"invalid_examples":["Serve recovered derived state immediately without checking whether events were missed during failure."],"problem":"Recovered state differs from authoritative state.","failure":"Persistent inconsistency after apparent recovery.","cause":"Recovery restores storage but not semantic completeness.","solution":"Run bounded reconciliation before declaring full recovery.","alternatives":["Full rebuild","Event replay","Snapshot plus journal"],"tradeoffs":"Reconciliation increases recovery duration and resource use.","constraints":["Authoritative source must be trusted and available.","Reconciliation must not create duplicate side effects."],"edge_cases":["Authoritative data may itself be unavailable during the same incident.","Differences can be intentional due to eventual consistency."],"security":"Reconciliation must preserve tenant and authorization boundaries.","performance":"Large reconciliation jobs may require staged execution.","testing":"Create known divergence and verify recovery identifies and repairs or quarantines it.","verification":"Full recovery requires reconciliation status and evidence where the recovery model requires it.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["authoritative source","reconciliation engine"],"prerequisites":["D22-B18-005","D22-B27-002"],"relationships":["D22-B20-004","D22-B15-005"],"provenance":["Derived recovery correctness principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B28-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Change Reliability","subtopic":"Change control","concept":"Reliability-aware change classification","knowledge_type":"governance_model","question":"Why should changes be classified by their potential reliability impact before deployment?","answer":"Changes that affect critical dependencies, resource limits, recovery logic, data schemas, routing, or failure handling can have much larger reliability consequences than isolated low-risk changes.","explanation":"Change classification allows stronger verification and rollout controls where failure impact is high without applying identical process overhead to every modification.","principle":"Change controls should scale with potential failure impact and uncertainty.","rule":"Classify changes by affected reliability boundaries and require proportional validation.","examples":["A change to retry policy receives fault-injection testing while a documentation-only change does not require the same reliability gate."],"invalid_examples":["Apply identical release verification to every change regardless of affected failure mechanisms."],"problem":"High-impact reliability changes receive insufficient validation or low-risk changes receive unnecessary friction.","failure":"Poor risk control and inefficient engineering process.","cause":"No reliability-aware change classification.","solution":"Define categories based on failure impact, blast radius, statefulness, and reversibility.","alternatives":["Risk-based release tiers","Change advisory policy"],"tradeoffs":"Classification requires governance and can be subjective without clear criteria.","constraints":["Classification criteria must be auditable.","Emergency changes need separate but controlled handling."],"edge_cases":["A small code change can affect a critical shared library.","A large refactor may have low runtime impact if isolated and thoroughly validated."],"security":"Changes affecting security controls require appropriate security and reliability review.","performance":"Risk classification has negligible runtime cost.","testing":"Feed representative changes into the classification system and verify expected controls.","verification":"Each classification maps to explicit evidence requirements.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["change metadata","risk model"],"prerequisites":["D22-B22-005","D22-B26-001"],"relationships":["D22-B28-002","D22-B28-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability governance principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B28-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Change Reliability","subtopic":"Reliability gates","concept":"Evidence-based release gate","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"What makes a reliability release gate evidence-based rather than procedural?","answer":"An evidence-based gate evaluates predefined reliability properties using observed test or deployment evidence and blocks promotion when required predicates are not satisfied.","explanation":"A checklist can be completed without proving reliability. A gate should connect a change to measurable evidence such as fault-injection results, canary behavior, recovery tests, or invariant checks.","principle":"Release approval should depend on evidence relevant to the change's failure modes.","rule":"Every reliability gate must specify required evidence, acceptance criteria, and failure handling.","examples":["A circuit-breaker change requires retry amplification tests, state-transition checks, and canary evidence before promotion."],"invalid_examples":["Approve a release because all checklist boxes were marked complete without evidence."],"problem":"Process compliance is mistaken for reliability verification.","failure":"Unverified changes reach production.","cause":"Gate criteria are procedural rather than evidentiary.","solution":"Tie gates to machine-readable test and deployment evidence.","alternatives":["Automated policy engine","Risk-based approval workflow"],"tradeoffs":"Evidence collection increases release time and infrastructure requirements.","constraints":["Evidence must correspond to the exact version under release.","Failed evidence must remain auditable."],"edge_cases":["Emergency changes may have incomplete pre-release evidence and require stronger post-release verification.","A passing test may not represent a production-relevant workload."],"security":"Release evidence must be tamper-resistant where used for authorization decisions.","performance":"Automated gates reduce manual review time after setup.","testing":"Introduce releases with known reliability regressions and verify gate rejection.","verification":"Promotion is allowed only when all mandatory predicates have current evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["test results","release metadata","policy engine"],"prerequisites":["D22-B28-001"],"relationships":["D22-B26-002","D22-B20-002"],"provenance":["Derived evidence-driven release principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B28-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Change Reliability","subtopic":"Blast radius","concept":"Change blast-radius control","knowledge_type":"deployment_pattern","question":"How can blast radius be reduced when a change has uncertain reliability impact?","answer":"Blast radius can be constrained through limited traffic exposure, isolated cells, tenant or region scoping, feature flags, resource quotas, and progressive rollout.","explanation":"Uncertainty increases the value of containment. The goal is not to eliminate all failure but to prevent one failure from affecting the entire service.","principle":"Unknown failure impact should trigger stronger containment, not broader exposure.","rule":"When reliability uncertainty is high, deploy through the smallest representative scope that can produce useful evidence.","examples":["A new dependency client is enabled for a limited workload segment before broader activation."],"invalid_examples":["Enable an unvalidated reliability mechanism globally because it is expected to improve resilience."],"problem":"Uncertain change affects the full failure domain.","failure":"Large-scale incident from an unobserved regression.","cause":"No deployment containment strategy.","solution":"Use staged exposure and explicit scope boundaries.","alternatives":["Canary","Cell-based rollout","Feature flag"],"tradeoffs":"Smaller exposure slows learning and may miss rare failures.","constraints":["Scope boundaries must be enforceable.","Sensitive tenants may require separate handling."],"edge_cases":["A change affecting shared control-plane code can have global blast radius despite limited data-plane traffic.","A rare failure may require broader exposure to observe."],"security":"Blast-radius controls should prevent cross-tenant impact.","performance":"Traffic segmentation can introduce routing overhead.","testing":"Deploy controlled regressions to progressively larger scopes and verify containment.","verification":"Measure actual affected population against intended rollout scope.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["traffic segmentation","feature control"],"prerequisites":["D22-B26-001","D22-B28-001"],"relationships":["D22-B28-004","D22-B23-002"],"provenance":["Derived blast-radius control principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B28-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Change Reliability","subtopic":"Schema evolution","concept":"Backward-compatible state evolution","knowledge_type":"reliability_pattern","question":"Why is backward compatibility important for reliability during rolling deployments?","answer":"During a rolling deployment, old and new versions can coexist. Shared data or protocol changes must therefore remain understandable to both versions until migration is complete.","explanation":"Incompatible state changes can cause partial failures during normal rollout and can make rollback impossible.","principle":"Rolling deployment creates a temporary mixed-version system that must be treated as a valid operating state.","rule":"Design persistent schemas and protocols so supported mixed-version combinations remain safe.","examples":["Additive schema changes allow old readers to continue operating while new writers gradually adopt new fields."],"invalid_examples":["Remove or rename a field immediately while older instances still require it."],"problem":"Mixed-version instances cannot interpret shared state consistently.","failure":"Deployment-wide errors or rollback failure.","cause":"Schema evolution assumes instantaneous version replacement.","solution":"Use compatibility phases and delayed destructive changes.","alternatives":["Blue-green with isolated state","Versioned APIs"],"tradeoffs":"Backward compatibility can require temporary duplicate fields or translation logic.","constraints":["Compatibility must be tested across supported version pairs.","Eventually obsolete fields require a controlled removal phase."],"edge_cases":["A data migration may be logically incompatible even when schema is syntactically compatible.","External consumers may remain on older versions longer than expected."],"security":"Schema changes must preserve authorization and data-classification semantics.","performance":"Compatibility layers can add serialization and storage overhead.","testing":"Run old and new versions against shared test state and verify reads, writes, and rollback.","verification":"Explicitly test every supported mixed-version pair relevant to the rollout.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["schema versioning","deployment strategy"],"prerequisites":["D22-B26-003"],"relationships":["D22-B25-002","D22-B28-005"],"provenance":["Derived rolling-deployment reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B28-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B28","topic":"Change Reliability","subtopic":"Irreversible changes","concept":"Irreversible change detection","knowledge_type":"risk_control","question":"Why should irreversible changes receive stronger reliability controls than reversible changes?","answer":"An irreversible change can prevent rollback or recovery to the previous state, increasing the consequence of a defect.","explanation":"Examples include destructive migrations, permanent external side effects, data deletion, and protocol changes that old versions cannot understand.","principle":"Irreversibility increases recovery risk and should increase pre-change evidence requirements.","rule":"Identify irreversible effects before deployment and require an explicit recovery strategy for each one.","examples":["A destructive schema migration requires validated backup/recovery and a forward-repair plan before activation."],"invalid_examples":["Treat a destructive data migration as equivalent to a reversible application deployment."],"problem":"Recovery assumptions fail after an irreversible change.","failure":"Permanent data loss or prolonged service incompatibility.","cause":"Irreversibility was not represented in change risk.","solution":"Mark irreversible operations explicitly and require recovery alternatives.","alternatives":["Reversible migration","Soft delete","Dual-write transition"],"tradeoffs":"Reversible designs can require more storage and implementation complexity.","constraints":["Some business operations are inherently irreversible.","Recovery alternatives must be realistic rather than theoretical."],"edge_cases":["External systems may make an otherwise reversible local action irreversible.","A backup may exist but not meet recovery-time requirements."],"security":"Irreversible security-policy changes require especially strong authorization and audit.","performance":"Reversible migration mechanisms can increase temporary storage and I/O.","testing":"Execute the change in an isolated environment and attempt documented recovery paths.","verification":"No irreversible change is approved without a verified or explicitly accepted recovery strategy.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["change classification","recovery plan"],"prerequisites":["D22-B26-003","D22-B27-003"],"relationships":["D22-B28-001","D22-B29-002"],"provenance":["Derived change-risk principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B29-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Operational Reliability","subtopic":"Runbooks","concept":"Executable reliability runbook","knowledge_type":"operational_pattern","question":"What makes a reliability runbook operationally useful during an incident?","answer":"A useful runbook defines observable triggers, diagnosis steps, safe actions, prerequisites, expected outcomes, rollback or stop conditions, and escalation paths.","explanation":"Narrative documentation alone may be difficult to execute under pressure. Structured procedures reduce ambiguity and make recovery behavior more consistent.","principle":"Operational knowledge should be actionable, bounded, and verifiable.","rule":"Every high-impact manual recovery procedure should define both action and evidence of success.","examples":["A dependency failover runbook specifies eligibility checks, traffic transition, verification signals, and rollback conditions."],"invalid_examples":["Runbook says 'fail over to backup' without explaining readiness or how to verify success."],"problem":"Operators perform inconsistent or unsafe recovery actions.","failure":"Longer incidents or secondary failures.","cause":"Runbook lacks executable decision criteria.","solution":"Convert procedures into explicit state transitions and verification steps.","alternatives":["Automated remediation","Interactive runbook system"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed runbooks require maintenance as architecture changes.","constraints":["Runbooks must match current system versions and permissions.","Dangerous actions need authorization controls."],"edge_cases":["Incident conditions may differ from documented assumptions.","An unavailable control plane can make normal runbook steps impossible."],"security":"Runbooks must enforce least privilege and protect operational secrets.","performance":"Operational documentation has no material runtime cost.","testing":"Execute runbooks during controlled exercises and record deviations.","verification":"A runbook is validated only when operators can execute it successfully in a representative scenario.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["service topology","recovery procedures"],"prerequisites":["D22-B18","D22-B20"],"relationships":["D22-B29-002","D22-B30-003"],"provenance":["Derived operational reliability practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B29-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Operational Reliability","subtopic":"Human recovery","concept":"Human-in-the-loop recovery boundary","knowledge_type":"governance_pattern","question":"When should automated recovery stop and require human intervention?","answer":"Automation should escalate when actions exceed predefined safety bounds, recovery attempts fail, system state becomes ambiguous, or the next action has irreversible or high-impact consequences.","explanation":"Automation is reliable only within the conditions it can verify. Continuing beyond those conditions can amplify damage.","principle":"Automation should fail safely when its evidence is insufficient for the next action.","rule":"Define explicit escalation predicates rather than allowing automation to continue indefinitely.","examples":["After bounded failover attempts with inconsistent state, the controller freezes further mutations and escalates for investigation."],"invalid_examples":["Keep executing increasingly invasive recovery actions until the system appears healthy."],"problem":"Automated recovery exceeds its verified operating envelope.","failure":"Secondary damage or state corruption.","cause":"No escalation boundary.","solution":"Define action budgets, uncertainty thresholds, and human approval points.","alternatives":["Full automation for low-risk actions","Operator-confirmed high-impact actions"],"tradeoffs":"Human escalation can increase recovery time but limits unsafe automation.","constraints":["Approval paths must remain available during incidents.","Human actions should also be audited."],"edge_cases":["Operators may be unavailable during off-hours.","An incident can degrade the approval system itself."],"security":"High-impact recovery requires authenticated authorization.","performance":"Approval latency can affect recovery time.","testing":"Create scenarios that cross automation limits and verify escalation occurs.","verification":"Confirm no action beyond the configured automation boundary executes automatically.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["recovery controller","authorization","audit"],"prerequisites":["D22-B18-003"],"relationships":["D22-B29-003","D22-B30-002"],"provenance":["Derived governed automation principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B29-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Operational Reliability","subtopic":"Incident state","concept":"Incident state machine","knowledge_type":"operational_model","question":"Why should major reliability incidents have explicit state transitions?","answer":"An incident state machine distinguishes detection, triage, containment, recovery, verification, and closure, preventing teams or automation from treating partial progress as complete recovery.","explanation":"Explicit incident states improve coordination and make automation decisions dependent on evidence rather than informal assumptions.","principle":"Incident progression should be represented as state transitions with entry and exit criteria.","rule":"Every major incident state must have observable criteria for entering and leaving the state.","examples":["Recovery cannot transition to resolved until service correctness and stability predicates pass."],"invalid_examples":["Mark an incident resolved immediately after the primary process restarts."],"problem":"Incident status does not reflect actual system state.","failure":"Premature closure and repeated incidents.","cause":"No explicit incident lifecycle model.","solution":"Define incident states, transitions, owners, evidence, and stop conditions.","alternatives":["Workflow engine","Incident orchestration system"],"tradeoffs":"State modeling adds process complexity but improves coordination.","constraints":["State transitions must remain auditable.","Multiple concurrent incidents may share symptoms."],"edge_cases":["An incident can regress after entering recovery.","Different services can be in different recovery states within one broader incident."],"security":"Incident state and operational evidence may contain sensitive information.","performance":"State tracking is low-cost compared with incident impact.","testing":"Simulate incidents that regress during recovery and verify state transitions remain correct.","verification":"Incident closure requires explicit recovery evidence and observation period where defined.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["incident telemetry","recovery verification"],"prerequisites":["D22-B19-003","D22-B29-001"],"relationships":["D22-B29-004","D22-B30-004"],"provenance":["Derived incident-management reliability principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B29-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Operational Reliability","subtopic":"Incident stabilization","concept":"Containment before optimization","knowledge_type":"incident_rule","question":"Why should incident response prioritize containment before detailed optimization?","answer":"During an active failure, stopping propagation and protecting critical resources can reduce harm even before the exact root cause is known.","explanation":"Waiting for complete diagnosis can allow cascading effects to continue. Containment actions should be safe, reversible where possible, and evidence-aware.","principle":"The first reliability objective during an incident is to prevent further damage while preserving evidence.","rule":"Prefer bounded containment actions over speculative invasive changes when root cause remains uncertain.","examples":["Disable an optional feature or shed low-priority load while investigation continues."],"invalid_examples":["Modify multiple unrelated components simultaneously before understanding the failure scope."],"problem":"Investigation delay allows failure propagation.","failure":"Expanded blast radius and loss of diagnostic clarity.","cause":"Root-cause analysis is prioritized over immediate containment.","solution":"Use predefined containment actions with explicit safety and rollback criteria.","alternatives":["Traffic isolation","Feature disablement","Dependency circuit opening"],"tradeoffs":"Containment may reduce functionality or delay full recovery.","constraints":["Containment must preserve mandatory correctness and security properties.","Actions should be attributable and reversible where feasible."],"edge_cases":["The containment mechanism itself may be failing.","A suspected component may not actually be causal."],"security":"Containment must not disable essential security controls without authorized compensating controls.","performance":"Load shedding can improve system stability at the cost of rejected work.","testing":"Exercise containment actions during controlled incidents and measure blast-radius reduction.","verification":"Compare affected scope before and after containment.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["incident state","load controls"],"prerequisites":["D22-B23","D22-B29-003"],"relationships":["D22-B29-005","D22-B17-003"],"provenance":["Derived incident containment principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B29-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B29","topic":"Operational Reliability","subtopic":"Evidence preservation","concept":"Incident evidence preservation","knowledge_type":"operational_control","question":"Why should incident responders preserve evidence before performing destructive recovery actions?","answer":"Logs, traces, process state, queue contents, configuration versions, and failure timelines may be necessary to identify root causes and prevent recurrence. Some recovery actions can erase that evidence.","explanation":"Reliability recovery and learning have different objectives. Fast containment may be necessary, but evidence preservation should occur whenever it can be done safely without increasing impact.","principle":"Recovery should preserve sufficient evidence to support diagnosis and future prevention.","rule":"Capture critical volatile evidence before destructive actions when operational risk permits.","examples":["Record active configuration, failure counters, dependency state, and relevant traces before restarting a repeatedly failing process."],"invalid_examples":["Restart and redeploy every failing instance immediately without preserving any diagnostic state."],"problem":"Recovery destroys evidence needed for diagnosis.","failure":"Recurring incidents and unresolved root causes.","cause":"Incident response ignores evidence lifecycle.","solution":"Define minimal evidence-capture steps before destructive actions.","alternatives":["Automatic crash diagnostics","Continuous telemetry"],"tradeoffs":"Evidence capture can consume time and resources during incidents.","constraints":["Safety and containment take precedence over evidence preservation.","Sensitive evidence must be protected."],"edge_cases":["A system may be actively corrupting data and require immediate shutdown.","Evidence systems may fail during the incident."],"security":"Captured evidence may contain credentials, personal data, or sensitive system details.","performance":"High-detail evidence capture can increase resource usage.","testing":"Simulate incidents and verify evidence survives restart and recovery actions where intended.","verification":"Incident records should identify which evidence was captured, lost, or unavailable.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["telemetry","incident workflow"],"prerequisites":["D22-B29-004"],"relationships":["D22-B30-001","D22-B30-005"],"provenance":["Derived incident-learning principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B30-001","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Reliability Learning","subtopic":"Post-incident analysis","concept":"Evidence-based incident review","knowledge_type":"learning_method","question":"What should a high-quality reliability incident review establish?","answer":"It should reconstruct the failure timeline, identify contributing conditions, distinguish confirmed causes from hypotheses, evaluate detection and containment, document recovery evidence, and define corrective actions tied to failure mechanisms.","explanation":"An incident review should improve system reliability rather than merely assign responsibility. Conclusions must distinguish evidence from interpretation.","principle":"Reliability learning should produce verifiable changes to system behavior or detection.","rule":"Every material corrective action should map to an identified failure mechanism or observability gap and have a verification plan.","examples":["A recurring timeout incident produces a bounded concurrency change plus a fault-injection regression test demonstrating the new behavior."],"invalid_examples":["Close an incident review with only a narrative summary and no evidence-backed corrective action."],"problem":"Incidents recur because learning is not converted into verified engineering changes.","failure":"Repeated reliability failures.","cause":"Post-incident process lacks causal and verification discipline.","solution":"Link evidence, failure mechanism, corrective action, and regression verification.","alternatives":["Formal root-cause analysis","Fault-tree review"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed reviews require engineering time but reduce recurrence risk.","constraints":["Do not claim causality beyond available evidence.","Corrective actions should be measurable."],"edge_cases":["Multiple independent causes can contribute to one incident.","The exact root cause may remain uncertain after the incident."],"security":"Security-related causes require appropriate handling and may involve separate investigation processes.","performance":"Review work has indirect engineering cost.","testing":"Convert incident scenarios into regression and fault-injection tests.","verification":"Close corrective actions only after evidence demonstrates the intended reliability property.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["incident timeline","test system","action registry"],"prerequisites":["D22-B29"],"relationships":["D22-B30-002","D22-B30-003"],"provenance":["Derived SRE incident-learning practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B30-002","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Reliability Learning","subtopic":"Corrective actions","concept":"Corrective-action verification","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"Why should corrective actions be verified against the original failure mode rather than merely implemented?","answer":"Implementation does not prove that the failure mechanism has been removed or contained. Verification must demonstrate the intended behavior under the relevant failure condition.","explanation":"A code change can be technically complete while leaving the underlying failure path intact or introducing a new interaction.","principle":"Reliability remediation is complete only when the relevant failure property is demonstrated.","rule":"Every material reliability corrective action requires a test, observation, or formal evidence criterion linked to the original failure mode.","examples":["After changing retry behavior, inject the original dependency failure and verify retry volume remains bounded."],"invalid_examples":["Close the incident because the retry code was changed without reproducing the failure."],"problem":"Corrective work is declared complete without proving effectiveness.","failure":"Same failure returns or remains partially uncontrolled.","cause":"Completion is based on implementation status rather than evidence.","solution":"Link corrective actions to deterministic scenarios or measurable invariants.","alternatives":["Regression test","Chaos experiment","Formal proof for suitable properties"],"tradeoffs":"Verification adds engineering effort but substantially improves confidence.","constraints":["The verification scenario must represent the original failure mechanism.","Some production-only conditions require controlled environment simulation."],"edge_cases":["The exact original failure may be nondeterministic.","Multiple changes may make attribution difficult."],"security":"Corrective actions affecting security boundaries require security verification as well.","performance":"Fault testing may be expensive but can be scheduled separately from normal CI.","testing":"Reproduce the original failure sequence after remediation.","verification":"Require direct evidence that the original failure condition now produces the intended bounded behavior.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["failure scenario","test oracle"],"prerequisites":["D22-B30-001","D22-B20-003"],"relationships":["D22-B20-002","D22-B30-004"],"provenance":["Derived verification-driven reliability practice"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B30-003","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Reliability Learning","subtopic":"Regression corpus","concept":"Reliability regression corpus","knowledge_type":"dataset_design","question":"Why should confirmed reliability failures become structured regression records?","answer":"Structured failure records preserve the exact failure mechanism, triggering conditions, expected containment, recovery behavior, and verification evidence so future changes can be tested against known failure modes.","explanation":"Without a persistent corpus, reliability knowledge remains trapped in incident reports and can disappear when systems or teams change.","principle":"Every high-value reliability failure can become reusable machine-readable engineering knowledge.","rule":"Convert confirmed material failures into stable, deduplicated regression scenarios with provenance and version metadata.","examples":["A timeout amplification incident becomes a regression record containing traffic conditions, retry configuration, expected attempt ceiling, and verification oracle."],"invalid_examples":["Store only a human-readable incident title such as 'retry storm fixed'."],"problem":"Known failure knowledge is not reusable by automated validation.","failure":"Previously solved reliability regressions reappear unnoticed.","cause":"Incident knowledge lacks structured representation.","solution":"Maintain a version-aware reliability failure corpus linked to tests and corrective actions.","alternatives":["Scenario database","Chaos-test catalog"],"tradeoffs":"Maintaining the corpus requires deduplication and version management.","constraints":["Records must distinguish observed facts from hypotheses.","Sensitive incident data must be sanitized."],"edge_cases":["A scenario may become invalid after architectural change.","Similar incidents may share a mechanism but differ materially in trigger conditions."],"security":"Regression fixtures must not contain secrets or unnecessary sensitive production data.","performance":"Large scenario corpora require indexing and selective execution.","testing":"Execute selected regression scenarios against every relevant reliability-sensitive release.","verification":"Each retained scenario must have a reproducible trigger and explicit expected result.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["scenario storage","test harness","provenance"],"prerequisites":["D22-B30-002"],"relationships":["D22-B20-003","D22-B30-005"],"provenance":["Derived reliability knowledge-engineering principle"],"evidence":"research_and_engineering_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B30-004","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Reliability Learning","subtopic":"Reliability drift","concept":"Reliability regression detection across versions","knowledge_type":"verification_model","question":"How can reliability degradation be detected when no single release causes an obvious outage?","answer":"Track reliability-relevant behavior across versions and compare changes in error rates, tail latency, resource consumption, recovery time, failure containment, and invariant violations against appropriate baselines.","explanation":"Small regressions can accumulate while remaining below incident thresholds. Version-aware comparison reveals gradual reliability drift.","principle":"Reliability must be monitored as a time-evolving property, not only as incident occurrence.","rule":"Preserve version identity with reliability measurements and compare like workloads across releases.","examples":["A release gradually increases connection occupancy and recovery duration even though request success remains within the short-term objective."],"invalid_examples":["Review reliability only after an outage occurs."],"problem":"Gradual reliability degradation escapes incident-driven detection.","failure":"Accumulated reliability debt and reduced failure margin.","cause":"No version-aware regression analysis.","solution":"Track reliability indicators by release and workload class.","alternatives":["Continuous regression benchmarks","Reliability trend analysis"],"tradeoffs":"Longitudinal analysis requires consistent telemetry and workload normalization.","constraints":["Changes in workload can confound comparisons.","Metrics must preserve semantic definitions across versions."],"edge_cases":["A legitimate architecture change changes baseline behavior.","Rare failure modes require scenario-based testing in addition to production metrics."],"security":"Security-related reliability regressions need separate monitoring where relevant.","performance":"Longitudinal analysis uses stored telemetry but little runtime overhead.","testing":"Introduce small controlled regressions and verify trend detection identifies them.","verification":"Compare version-specific measurements using equivalent workloads and documented normalization.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["version metadata","reliability telemetry"],"prerequisites":["D22-B30-003","D22-B21-004"],"relationships":["D21-B80","D22-B28-005"],"provenance":["Derived reliability regression-analysis principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B30-005","dataset_id":"D22","batch_id":"B30","topic":"Reliability Learning","subtopic":"Knowledge integrity","concept":"Reliability evidence provenance","knowledge_type":"dataset_governance","question":"Why should reliability knowledge records preserve provenance and distinguish observed evidence from derived conclusions?","answer":"Reliability decisions can affect architecture and operational safety. Provenance allows an automated system or engineer to determine whether a claim came from direct observation, controlled testing, official documentation, or engineering inference.","explanation":"Without provenance, historical assumptions can be mistaken for verified facts and propagated into future automated decisions.","principle":"Reliability knowledge must preserve evidence lineage and epistemic status.","rule":"Every material reliability record should identify source type, evidence scope, confidence, version context, and validation status.","examples":["A record states that a failure mode was observed in a controlled test rather than claiming it was universally proven."],"invalid_examples":["Store 'this configuration prevents cascading failure' without source, test context, or validation status."],"problem":"Unverified reliability assumptions become authoritative knowledge.","failure":"Automated systems repeat unsupported or outdated decisions.","cause":"Knowledge records lack provenance and evidence status.","solution":"Attach provenance, evidence class, validation state, version, and contradiction metadata to reliability knowledge.","alternatives":["Evidence graph","Decision provenance ledger"],"tradeoffs":"Metadata increases dataset size and maintenance requirements.","constraints":["Provenance must be preserved through transformations.","Confidence must not be represented as fabricated certainty."],"edge_cases":["Official documentation can be correct but version-specific.","A controlled test can validate one configuration but not every deployment.","Conflicting evidence may require multiple records rather than silent overwriting."],"security":"Sensitive incident evidence must be access-controlled while preserving provenance metadata.","performance":"Rich metadata improves retrieval and reasoning but increases indexing requirements.","testing":"Create records with different evidence classes and verify retrieval preserves their validation status.","verification":"Reject or downgrade unsupported claims during dataset validation.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["dataset schema","provenance model"],"prerequisites":["D22-B30-003"],"relationships":["D22-B21-002","D22-B22-005","D22-B20-004"],"provenance":["Derived knowledge-engineering principle for reliability datasets"],"evidence":"research_and_engineering_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SCHEMA_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B31-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Reliability","subtopic":"Dependency Graphs","concept":"Dependency-aware reliability reasoning","knowledge_type":"architecture_principle","question":"Why should reliability analysis model service dependencies as a graph rather than evaluating each service independently?","answer":"Because a service can remain locally healthy while its dependency chain is degraded. A dependency graph exposes transitive failure propagation, shared dependencies, critical paths, and correlated blast radius.","explanation":"Independent health assessment can miss system-level failure because downstream latency, capacity, availability, or correctness problems propagate upstream. Graph-based reasoning identifies which components can affect a given business operation and which shared dependencies create correlated risk.","principle":"System reliability is constrained by the behavior of critical dependency paths, not only by the local health of individual components.","rule":"Model critical runtime dependencies explicitly and evaluate both direct and transitive failure effects.","examples":[{"scenario":"API A depends on service B and database C while service B also depends on C.","implication":"Failure or severe degradation of C can affect both A directly and through B."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treating A as healthy because its own process, CPU, and memory are normal.","reason":"Local health does not prove dependency health."}],"problem":"Hidden dependency failures cause apparently healthy services to fail user operations.","failure":"Transitive dependency failure.","cause":"Reliability analysis considers components independently.","solution":"Maintain a dependency graph and evaluate critical paths and shared dependencies.","alternatives":["Static dependency documentation","Runtime dependency discovery","Service topology telemetry"],"tradeoffs":"Runtime discovery can improve accuracy but adds instrumentation and data-management complexity.","constraints":["Dependency information must be versioned.","Optional dependencies must be distinguished from mandatory dependencies."],"edge_cases":["Circular dependencies","Conditional dependencies","Feature-specific dependencies","Fallback dependencies"],"security":"Dependency topology can reveal sensitive architecture and should be access-controlled.","performance":"Graph analysis should be separated from latency-critical request paths when possible.","testing":"Inject failures into direct and transitive dependencies and verify expected propagation boundaries.","verification":"Confirm every critical business operation has an explicit dependency path and documented failure behavior.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-002","D22-B33-001"],"prerequisites":["Distributed systems","Failure propagation"],"relationships":["D22-B31-002","D22-B33-001"],"provenance":["Derived from established distributed-systems reliability practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.95,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B31-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Reliability","subtopic":"Shared Dependencies","concept":"Correlated failure through shared infrastructure","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"How can a shared dependency create correlated failures across otherwise isolated services?","answer":"Multiple services that depend on the same database, identity provider, network segment, message broker, DNS service, or storage layer can fail simultaneously when that shared dependency degrades.","explanation":"Logical service separation does not provide true fault isolation if the services converge on a common dependency. Reliability analysis must therefore identify common-mode dependencies and shared fault domains.","principle":"Logical isolation is weaker than physical or operational isolation when components share a failure source.","rule":"For every claimed isolation boundary, identify infrastructure and operational dependencies that cross that boundary.","examples":[{"scenario":"Three services run in separate containers but all use one database cluster.","result":"A database failure can create a multi-service outage despite container isolation."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Counting separate processes as independent fault domains.","reason":"The processes still share a critical dependency."}],"problem":"Architectural diagrams show isolation that does not exist during infrastructure failures.","failure":"Correlated multi-service outage.","cause":"Common-mode dependency overlooked.","solution":"Map shared dependencies and either isolate them, add controlled alternatives, or explicitly accept the correlated failure mode.","alternatives":["Independent dependency instances","Partitioned infrastructure","Graceful degradation"],"tradeoffs":"Reducing shared dependencies can increase operational cost and consistency complexity.","constraints":["Some dependencies are intentionally shared.","Security and compliance controls may force shared infrastructure."],"edge_cases":["Shared DNS","Shared certificate authority","Shared identity provider","Shared control plane"],"security":"Isolation analysis must include shared security infrastructure because security dependencies can become availability dependencies.","performance":"Independent infrastructure can reduce contention but may increase network and synchronization overhead.","testing":"Perform shared-dependency failure injection while monitoring all dependent services.","verification":"Demonstrate that each claimed fault domain has no unaccounted critical shared dependency.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-001"],"prerequisites":["Fault domains","Dependency mapping"],"relationships":["D22-B31-001","D22-B32-003"],"provenance":["Derived engineering principle from distributed reliability and fault-domain analysis"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.95,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B31-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Reliability","subtopic":"Critical Paths","concept":"Reliability of composite operations","knowledge_type":"reasoning_rule","question":"How should reliability reasoning handle a business operation that requires multiple mandatory services?","answer":"The operation must be analyzed as a composite dependency chain. If all mandatory components must succeed, the operation's availability is bounded by the combined behavior of those components rather than any single component's local availability.","explanation":"A business operation can fail if any mandatory dependency fails. The exact quantitative availability calculation depends on dependency relationships, independence assumptions, retry behavior, fallback paths, and measurement definitions; these assumptions must not be silently treated as facts.","principle":"Business-level reliability must be measured at the level of the operation users actually depend on.","rule":"Define mandatory, optional, and fallback dependencies explicitly before deriving reliability expectations.","examples":[{"scenario":"Checkout requires inventory, payment, and order persistence.","result":"A checkout success metric must account for failures in all mandatory components."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Using payment-service uptime as the reliability metric for checkout.","reason":"Payment availability does not establish end-to-end checkout availability."}],"problem":"Component-level metrics hide end-to-end failures.","failure":"Business transaction outage despite healthy individual components.","cause":"Reliability measured at the wrong abstraction level.","solution":"Instrument end-to-end critical operations and map their dependency conditions.","alternatives":["Synthetic transactions","Business-level SLIs","Distributed tracing"],"tradeoffs":"End-to-end monitoring gives stronger user relevance but is more complex than isolated component monitoring.","constraints":["Synthetic checks must avoid creating real business side effects.","Critical operations require representative scenarios."],"edge_cases":["Optional recommendations","Fallback payment providers","Asynchronous completion","Eventual consistency"],"security":"Synthetic and tracing systems must avoid exposing sensitive business data.","performance":"End-to-end probes should be rate-limited to avoid adding measurable production load.","testing":"Run controlled dependency failures against complete business workflows.","verification":"Verify that business-level failure metrics change when critical dependencies are intentionally degraded.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-001"],"prerequisites":["Dependency graphs","Business-level reliability"],"relationships":["D22-B31-001","D22-B34-002"],"provenance":["Derived engineering principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.94,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B31-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Reliability","subtopic":"Common-Mode Failures","concept":"Common-mode failure detection","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"What is a common-mode failure and why is it important in reliability engineering?","answer":"A common-mode failure occurs when apparently independent components fail because they share a common underlying cause.","explanation":"Redundancy only improves resilience when redundant components do not fail together under the same relevant conditions. Shared software versions, configurations, infrastructure, network paths, operators, credentials, deployment mechanisms, or external providers can create common-mode failure.","principle":"Redundancy does not provide the expected protection against failures that affect all redundant members simultaneously.","rule":"Evaluate the independence of failure causes, not merely the number of replicas.","examples":[{"scenario":"Two replicas use the same defective release.","result":"The replica count does not protect against a release-induced failure."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming two identical replicas provide complete protection from application defects.","reason":"A shared defect can affect both replicas."}],"problem":"Nominal redundancy produces false confidence.","failure":"Simultaneous failure of redundant components.","cause":"Shared failure mechanism.","solution":"Identify common-mode causes and diversify relevant failure domains where justified.","alternatives":["Version diversity","Infrastructure diversity","Provider diversity","Staged deployment"],"tradeoffs":"Diversity can increase operational complexity and compatibility-testing requirements.","constraints":["Diversity must not introduce uncontrolled behavioral differences.","Some common components are unavoidable."],"edge_cases":["Shared secrets","Shared configuration","Shared deployment pipeline","Shared time source"],"security":"Security controls themselves can become common-mode dependencies if one compromised control plane governs every replica.","performance":"Diversity may reduce optimization opportunities and increase resource consumption.","testing":"Test failures that intentionally affect all members of a nominally redundant group.","verification":"Document the failure modes against which each redundancy mechanism is actually effective.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-002"],"prerequisites":["Redundancy","Fault domains"],"relationships":["D22-B31-002","D22-B32-001"],"provenance":["Derived reliability engineering principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B31-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Reliability","subtopic":"Reliability Boundaries","concept":"Business-critical versus non-critical dependency classification","knowledge_type":"architecture_rule","question":"Why should dependencies be classified by business criticality instead of treating every dependency failure identically?","answer":"Different dependencies can require different responses because some failures block core business operations while others affect optional features that can be degraded or disabled.","explanation":"Criticality-aware architecture enables prioritized recovery, graceful degradation, targeted alerting, and controlled resource allocation. Without classification, optional failures can consume emergency resources or critical failures can be underestimated.","principle":"Reliability behavior should reflect the business consequence of a dependency failure.","rule":"Classify dependencies by impact and define explicit failure behavior for each class.","examples":[{"scenario":"Product recommendations fail during checkout.","result":"Checkout may continue while recommendations are disabled."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treating recommendation failure as equivalent to payment failure.","reason":"The business impact and required recovery behavior differ."}],"problem":"All failures receive the same operational treatment.","failure":"Critical resources are misallocated during incidents.","cause":"No dependency criticality model.","solution":"Define dependency criticality and corresponding fallback, alert, and recovery policies.","alternatives":["Priority tiers","Business capability mapping","Error budgets by service class"],"tradeoffs":"Classification requires governance and periodic review as business processes change.","constraints":["Criticality must be based on explicit business requirements.","Classification should be version-controlled."],"edge_cases":["Feature becomes business-critical during an event","Regulatory dependencies","Time-dependent criticality"],"security":"Criticality metadata may expose sensitive business architecture and should be protected.","performance":"Prioritization can preserve capacity for critical operations during overload.","testing":"Exercise failures independently for critical and non-critical dependencies.","verification":"Verify that each dependency has an explicit criticality and failure policy.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-003"],"prerequisites":["Business capability mapping"],"relationships":["D22-B31-003","D22-B37-001"],"provenance":["Derived reliability governance principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.94,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B32-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Reliability","subtopic":"Failure Domains","concept":"Failure-domain hierarchy","knowledge_type":"architecture_principle","question":"What is a failure-domain hierarchy and how should it influence resilience design?","answer":"A failure-domain hierarchy represents progressively larger scopes of correlated failure, such as process, host, rack, zone, region, provider, or external dependency.","explanation":"Replication within a small failure domain cannot protect against failures that span that domain. Resilience architecture should place redundancy according to the failure scope it is intended to tolerate.","principle":"The protection boundary of redundancy must exceed the intended failure boundary.","rule":"Map each resilience mechanism to an explicit failure-domain assumption.","examples":[{"scenario":"Two instances are placed on one host.","result":"They may tolerate process failure but not host failure."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Claiming zone-level resilience from replicas deployed in one zone.","reason":"The replicas share the same zone failure domain."}],"problem":"Redundancy is deployed too narrowly to tolerate the targeted fault.","failure":"Simultaneous replica loss.","cause":"Mismatch between redundancy placement and failure domain.","solution":"Distribute critical replicas across appropriate independent failure domains.","alternatives":["Zone distribution","Region distribution","Provider diversity"],"tradeoffs":"Larger failure-domain separation increases cost, latency, synchronization complexity, and operational burden.","constraints":["The actual independence of domains must be validated.","External dependencies may remain common."],"edge_cases":["Regional network partition","Provider-wide outage","Control-plane failure"],"security":"Cross-domain replication must preserve access controls and encryption guarantees.","performance":"Wider placement can increase network latency and replication overhead.","testing":"Inject failures at each intended domain level.","verification":"Demonstrate that each targeted failure domain can be lost without violating defined service objectives.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-004"],"prerequisites":["Fault isolation","Redundancy"],"relationships":["D22-B32-002","D22-B34-001"],"provenance":["Derived from reliability and fault-domain engineering practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B32-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Reliability","subtopic":"Failure Domain Testing","concept":"Domain-level failure testing","knowledge_type":"testing_strategy","question":"How should a system verify that its declared failure-domain resilience is real?","answer":"Failures should be injected at the domain level the architecture claims to tolerate, followed by verification of service continuity, capacity, data correctness, recovery, and observability.","explanation":"Replica counts and deployment metadata do not prove resilience. A domain-level failure test validates the actual interaction of placement, routing, capacity, dependencies, and recovery mechanisms.","principle":"Resilience claims require failure-mode-specific evidence.","rule":"Test each declared critical failure domain rather than inferring resilience from configuration.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service claims zone tolerance.","test":"Controlled removal of one zone's service capacity followed by verification of traffic handling and recovery."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Checking that instances are configured in multiple zones without exercising zone loss.","reason":"Configuration does not prove runtime resilience."}],"problem":"Architecture claims are not empirically validated.","failure":"Unexpected outage when the targeted domain fails.","cause":"Resilience tested only at component level.","solution":"Perform controlled domain-level fault injection and verify explicit acceptance criteria.","alternatives":["Staging failure tests","Production game days","Automated fault-injection environments"],"tradeoffs":"Large-scope fault tests require careful blast-radius controls.","constraints":["Tests must have defined abort criteria.","Production testing requires authorization and safeguards."],"edge_cases":["Partial domain failure","Intermittent connectivity","Control-plane unavailable while data-plane remains functional"],"security":"Fault testing must not create unauthorized access or disable required security controls.","performance":"Domain loss may intentionally create reduced-capacity conditions that should be measured.","testing":"The record itself defines the required test approach.","verification":"Verify continuity, error behavior, recovery, and post-test state.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B32-001"],"prerequisites":["Failure domains","Fault injection"],"relationships":["D22-B40-001","D22-B40-004"],"provenance":["Derived reliability testing practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B32-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Reliability","subtopic":"Isolation Boundaries","concept":"Control-plane versus data-plane failure isolation","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"Why can separating control-plane and data-plane responsibilities improve reliability?","answer":"A failure in management or coordination functions can be prevented from directly disabling already-running data-plane operations when the architecture permits safe independent operation.","explanation":"Systems often need control operations such as configuration, scheduling, discovery, or deployment while simultaneously serving user traffic. Strong separation can prevent control-plane incidents from becoming immediate data-plane outages, but only when runtime dependencies are designed accordingly.","principle":"Operational coordination should not unnecessarily become a mandatory dependency for steady-state service execution.","rule":"Identify which control-plane capabilities are required during steady-state operation and which can be unavailable temporarily.","examples":[{"scenario":"A running worker can continue processing already-known tasks when the scheduler becomes temporarily unavailable.","result":"A control-plane outage does not immediately stop all data-plane work."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Making every request require a live control-plane authorization or configuration lookup.","reason":"Control-plane failure can become a direct data-plane outage."}],"problem":"Management dependencies create hidden runtime single points of failure.","failure":"Service outage caused by control-plane unavailability.","cause":"Unnecessary synchronous control-plane dependency.","solution":"Cache safe runtime state and design bounded independent operation where correctness and security permit.","alternatives":["Local configuration snapshots","Leases","Grace periods"],"tradeoffs":"Independent operation can create stale configuration and revocation-delay risks.","constraints":["Security-critical state may require stronger freshness guarantees.","Bounded autonomy must be explicitly defined."],"edge_cases":["Emergency revocation","Configuration corruption","Expired credentials"],"security":"Never allow resilience mechanisms to bypass mandatory security revocation indefinitely.","performance":"Local state can reduce control-plane latency and dependency traffic.","testing":"Disable control-plane services and verify permitted data-plane behavior and bounded degradation.","verification":"Document maximum safe control-plane outage duration and verify enforcement.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-002"],"prerequisites":["Control/data-plane architecture"],"relationships":["D22-B32-002","D22-B35-003"],"provenance":["Derived distributed-systems resilience principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.93,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B32-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Reliability","subtopic":"Isolation Boundaries","concept":"Failure containment boundary","knowledge_type":"architecture_rule","question":"What makes a failure boundary effective rather than merely documented?","answer":"An effective failure boundary prevents faults from crossing it through compute, memory, queues, network paths, dependencies, shared state, or control mechanisms beyond the allowed propagation paths.","explanation":"A boundary is meaningful only if resource exhaustion, retries, state corruption, and control actions cannot unintentionally cross it. Documentation alone cannot establish containment.","principle":"Isolation is a runtime property, not a diagram property.","rule":"For every isolation boundary, enumerate all channels through which failure can cross it.","examples":[{"scenario":"Separate worker pools share one unbounded queue.","result":"Queue exhaustion can still affect both pools."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declaring worker pools isolated solely because they have different process names.","reason":"They share a critical resource."}],"problem":"Nominal isolation fails during resource exhaustion.","failure":"Cross-boundary cascading failure.","cause":"Unmodeled shared resource.","solution":"Enumerate and control shared resources and propagation channels.","alternatives":["Resource quotas","Separate queues","Bulkheads"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger isolation increases infrastructure and coordination cost.","constraints":["Every shared channel must have defined capacity and behavior.","Isolation must not violate required consistency."],"edge_cases":["Shared logging pipeline","Shared DNS","Shared filesystem","Shared thread pool"],"security":"Shared resources can also become security-boundary crossings.","performance":"Isolation may reduce resource sharing efficiency.","testing":"Exhaust one side of the boundary and observe the other side.","verification":"Demonstrate bounded impact under representative resource-exhaustion scenarios.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B32-001"],"prerequisites":["Bulkheads","Resource isolation"],"relationships":["D22-B32-002","D22-B36-002"],"provenance":["Derived fault-containment principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B32-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Reliability","subtopic":"Resource Isolation","concept":"Reliability impact of shared resource pools","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"How can a shared resource pool convert a local overload into a system-wide reliability incident?","answer":"A noisy workload can consume a shared finite resource such as threads, connections, memory, queue capacity, or file descriptors, starving unrelated workloads.","explanation":"Shared pools improve utilization but allow resource contention to cross logical workload boundaries. Reliability requires explicit limits and admission behavior when resources become scarce.","principle":"Finite shared resources are potential propagation channels.","rule":"Critical workloads should have bounded access to shared resources and should not depend on unlimited pool availability.","examples":[{"scenario":"One endpoint consumes all database connections.","result":"Unrelated endpoints cannot access the database."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Using one unlimited logical pool for all workloads.","reason":"One workload can exhaust resources needed by others."}],"problem":"Resource starvation crosses workload boundaries.","failure":"Cascading request failures.","cause":"Unbounded shared resource consumption.","solution":"Partition resources, enforce quotas, prioritize critical work, and reject excess load predictably.","alternatives":["Bulkheads","Per-tenant pools","Priority queues"],"tradeoffs":"Partitioning can reduce aggregate utilization because idle capacity may remain reserved.","constraints":["Limits must reflect realistic workload and recovery behavior.","Resource limits must be observable."],"edge_cases":["Burst traffic","Large requests","Tenant abuse","Slow consumers"],"security":"Resource quotas can mitigate resource-exhaustion abuse.","performance":"Partitioning may lower peak utilization but improves predictability under contention.","testing":"Exhaust one workload's allocation while exercising unaffected workloads.","verification":"Verify that protected workloads retain their required resource capacity.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B32-004"],"prerequisites":["Resource management","Bulkheads"],"relationships":["D22-B36-002","D22-B37-002"],"provenance":["Derived reliability and resource-isolation practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B33-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Asynchronous Reliability","subtopic":"Message Processing","concept":"Failure isolation through asynchronous boundaries","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"How can asynchronous messaging reduce synchronous failure propagation?","answer":"An asynchronous boundary can allow producers and consumers to operate independently for bounded periods, so temporary consumer unavailability does not necessarily block producers immediately.","explanation":"Queues buffer work and decouple execution timing, but they introduce backlog, ordering, duplication, poison-message, and durability concerns. They therefore transform failure behavior rather than eliminating failure.","principle":"Asynchronous boundaries can absorb temporal failure but create stateful backlog-management responsibilities.","rule":"Design queue capacity, durability, retry, ordering, expiration, and overload behavior explicitly.","examples":[{"scenario":"Email delivery service is temporarily unavailable.","result":"Accepted email jobs can remain queued instead of blocking the originating transaction."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming a queue guarantees eventual success regardless of capacity.","reason":"Queues can fill, messages can expire, and consumers can remain unavailable."}],"problem":"Synchronous dependency causes producer outage during temporary consumer failure.","failure":"Producer blocked by downstream unavailability.","cause":"Tight synchronous coupling.","solution":"Use asynchronous processing when business semantics permit and define bounded backlog behavior.","alternatives":["Event streaming","Durable task queues","Batch processing"],"tradeoffs":"Asynchrony increases operational and correctness complexity.","constraints":["Business operation must tolerate delayed completion.","Durability requirements must be explicit."],"edge_cases":["Duplicate delivery","Poison messages","Consumer slowdown","Queue corruption"],"security":"Messages require authorization, integrity protection, and controlled retention.","performance":"Queues can absorb bursts but add latency and storage overhead.","testing":"Stop consumers, generate bounded producer load, and verify backlog behavior and recovery.","verification":"Verify that accepted work has explicit durability and completion semantics.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-003"],"prerequisites":["Message queues","Distributed failure handling"],"relationships":["D22-B33-002","D22-B35-001"],"provenance":["Derived distributed-systems reliability practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B33-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Asynchronous Reliability","subtopic":"Backlog Management","concept":"Queue backlog as a reliability signal","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_rule","question":"Why is queue backlog more than a performance metric in a reliable asynchronous system?","answer":"Growing backlog indicates that incoming work is accumulating faster than it is being completed and can predict delayed completion, expiration, memory/storage exhaustion, and eventual service failure.","explanation":"A queue may continue accepting requests while the system is already operationally unhealthy. Reliability monitoring must therefore consider backlog growth, age, consumer health, and remaining capacity.","principle":"Accepted work that cannot be completed within its validity window is a reliability failure even if requests are initially accepted.","rule":"Monitor backlog size and age together with consumer throughput and capacity.","examples":[{"scenario":"Queue depth remains moderate but oldest-message age grows continuously.","result":"The system is degrading despite a non-alarming raw queue-size metric."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Monitoring only queue depth.","reason":"Depth alone cannot reveal how old pending work is or whether processing capacity is falling."}],"problem":"Asynchronous systems hide delayed failure behind successful acceptance.","failure":"Expired or excessively delayed work.","cause":"Backlog health measured incompletely.","solution":"Track depth, age, processing rate, rejection/expiration rate, and recovery behavior.","alternatives":["Lag metrics","Deadline-aware queues","Adaptive admission control"],"tradeoffs":"Additional telemetry increases complexity and storage requirements.","constraints":["Metrics must have defined aggregation windows.","Business deadlines should be represented where applicable."],"edge_cases":["Low-volume but deadline-sensitive queues","Bursty workloads","Infinite-retention queues"],"security":"Queue telemetry must not expose sensitive message contents.","performance":"Telemetry should use metadata rather than copying full payloads.","testing":"Pause consumers and verify backlog-age alerts and recovery behavior.","verification":"Confirm that backlog metrics detect degradation before hard capacity exhaustion where feasible.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B33-001"],"prerequisites":["Asynchronous processing","Observability"],"relationships":["D22-B39-001","D22-B40-002"],"provenance":["Derived reliability monitoring principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B33-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Asynchronous Reliability","subtopic":"Message Semantics","concept":"At-least-once delivery and idempotent processing","knowledge_type":"correctness_rule","question":"Why must consumers often tolerate duplicate messages in reliable asynchronous systems?","answer":"Because delivery systems that retry after uncertain acknowledgement can deliver the same message more than once. Consumers therefore need idempotent or deduplication-aware processing when duplicate effects are unacceptable.","explanation":"A consumer may complete work and then lose the acknowledgement path. The producer or broker can interpret the message as unprocessed and redeliver it. Exactly-once business effects require additional mechanisms beyond simply enabling message retries.","principle":"Delivery semantics and business-effect semantics are separate concerns.","rule":"Design message handlers so duplicate delivery cannot produce unacceptable duplicate business effects.","examples":[{"scenario":"A payment command is delivered twice.","result":"The handler must use an idempotency key or equivalent transaction mechanism to prevent duplicate charging."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming a successful handler execution implies the broker knows it was successful.","reason":"Acknowledgement can be lost independently of business execution."}],"problem":"Retry-driven duplicate delivery creates duplicate side effects.","failure":"Repeated business effect.","cause":"Non-idempotent consumer under at-least-once delivery.","solution":"Use idempotency keys, transactional deduplication, or equivalent business-effect protection.","alternatives":["Exactly-once processing mechanisms where genuinely supported","Transactional inbox patterns"],"tradeoffs":"Deduplication requires durable state and lifecycle management for idempotency records.","constraints":["Idempotency scope and retention must be defined.","Business identifiers must be stable enough for duplicate detection."],"edge_cases":["Concurrent duplicate deliveries","Late duplicates","Replayed historical messages"],"security":"Idempotency keys must not expose secrets or allow unauthorized replay.","performance":"Deduplication adds storage lookups or transactional work.","testing":"Deliver identical messages multiple times concurrently and after simulated acknowledgement loss.","verification":"Verify that duplicate deliveries produce one intended business effect.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B33-001"],"prerequisites":["Message delivery semantics","Transactional processing"],"relationships":["D22-B33-004","D22-B35-002"],"provenance":["Derived distributed messaging reliability principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B33-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Asynchronous Reliability","subtopic":"Poison Messages","concept":"Poison-message isolation","knowledge_type":"failure_pattern","question":"Why should repeatedly failing messages be isolated from the normal processing path?","answer":"A message that deterministically fails can repeatedly consume consumer capacity, trigger retries, increase backlog, and prevent healthy messages from being processed.","explanation":"Unbounded retry of one malformed or incompatible message can create a localized retry storm inside the queue consumer. Isolation mechanisms such as dead-letter handling or bounded retry state can preserve progress for other messages.","principle":"A permanently failing item must not monopolize shared processing capacity.","rule":"Bound message retries and provide an explicit path for messages that cannot currently be processed.","examples":[{"scenario":"A malformed payload always causes schema validation failure.","result":"After bounded retries, the message is isolated for inspection or later remediation."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Retrying the same deterministic validation failure indefinitely.","reason":"The retry cannot repair an unchanged invalid message."}],"problem":"One bad message blocks healthy work.","failure":"Consumer starvation and backlog growth.","cause":"Unbounded retry of deterministic failure.","solution":"Classify failure, bound retries, isolate poison messages, and preserve observability.","alternatives":["Dead-letter queue","Quarantine store","Manual remediation workflow"],"tradeoffs":"Isolation prevents blocking but introduces operational workflows for remediation.","constraints":["Dead-lettered data requires retention and access policy.","Reprocessing must be controlled."],"edge_cases":["Transient dependency failure misclassified as permanent","Poison message becomes valid after deployment","Ordering-sensitive streams"],"security":"Quarantined messages can contain sensitive payloads and require restricted access.","performance":"Isolation reduces repeated processing overhead.","testing":"Inject deterministic message failures and verify bounded retry plus continued processing of healthy messages.","verification":"Verify poison messages cannot consume unbounded consumer capacity.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B33-003"],"prerequisites":["Retry classification","Queue processing"],"relationships":["D22-B33-002","D22-B35-001"],"provenance":["Derived messaging reliability principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B33-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Asynchronous Reliability","subtopic":"Ordering","concept":"Reliability impact of message ordering","knowledge_type":"tradeoff_analysis","question":"How can strict message ordering affect reliability?","answer":"Strict ordering can preserve business correctness but may cause one blocked or invalid message to prevent later independent messages from progressing.","explanation":"Ordering requirements create a dependency between messages. When the first message cannot be processed, the system may have to choose between preserving order and maintaining throughput or availability.","principle":"Ordering guarantees can become intentional serialization points and therefore reliability constraints.","rule":"Require strict ordering only where business semantics need it and define behavior for blocked messages.","examples":[{"scenario":"Account events must be applied in sequence.","result":"Later events may need to wait for an earlier failed event."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Applying global ordering to unrelated tenants without a business requirement.","reason":"Unnecessary serialization creates a larger failure domain."}],"problem":"Ordering requirement amplifies the impact of individual message failures.","failure":"Progress stalls behind one blocked message.","cause":"Overly broad ordering scope.","solution":"Choose the narrowest ordering domain consistent with correctness.","alternatives":["Per-key ordering","Partitioned streams","Commutative operations"],"tradeoffs":"Narrower ordering improves concurrency but may complicate reasoning about global state.","constraints":["Correctness must define the required ordering boundary.","Reordering must be explicitly handled where allowed."],"edge_cases":["Late messages","Duplicate messages","Partition reassignment","Clock-based ordering"],"security":"Ordering metadata must not permit unauthorized replay or sequence manipulation.","performance":"Broader ordering reduces parallelism and can increase queue latency.","testing":"Block an early message and verify whether unrelated partitions continue processing as designed.","verification":"Confirm that ordering scope matches actual business invariants.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B33-003"],"prerequisites":["Message semantics","Concurrency"],"relationships":["D22-B33-004","D22-B36-003"],"provenance":["Derived distributed messaging principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.94,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B34-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Data Reliability","subtopic":"Durability","concept":"Durability versus availability","knowledge_type":"architecture_principle","question":"Why must durability and availability be treated as separate reliability properties?","answer":"A system can remain available while losing or corrupting data, and it can preserve data durably while temporarily being unavailable to serve requests.","explanation":"Availability concerns whether a service can respond successfully; durability concerns whether accepted state survives failures. A resilient design must define both properties and their acceptable failure modes.","principle":"Serving a response successfully does not prove that the underlying state is durably preserved.","rule":"Specify durability guarantees independently from service availability guarantees.","examples":[{"scenario":"A write returns success but is only stored in volatile memory.","result":"Availability was observed, but durable persistence was not established."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treating successful HTTP response as proof of durable storage.","reason":"The response semantics may not guarantee persistence."}],"problem":"Reliability objectives conflate serving and data preservation.","failure":"Data loss after apparently successful operation.","cause":"Unclear durability semantics.","solution":"Define persistence guarantees, acknowledgement semantics, replication, and recovery behavior explicitly.","alternatives":["Synchronous replication","Durable write-ahead logging","Asynchronous replication"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger durability can increase write latency and resource cost.","constraints":["Durability guarantees must match storage technology semantics.","Recovery must preserve the stated guarantee."],"edge_cases":["Power loss","Corruption","Replica lag","Partial writes"],"security":"Durable data requires encryption, access control, retention, and deletion semantics.","performance":"Durability mechanisms such as synchronous replication can increase write latency.","testing":"Simulate process, host, storage, and power-equivalent failures where safely possible and verify accepted data.","verification":"Trace a successful write through its documented durability boundary and validate recovery semantics.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B32-001"],"prerequisites":["Storage reliability","Recovery"],"relationships":["D22-B34-002","D22-B38-001"],"provenance":["Derived storage reliability principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B34-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Data Reliability","subtopic":"Replication","concept":"Synchronous versus asynchronous replication","knowledge_type":"tradeoff_analysis","question":"What reliability tradeoff exists between synchronous and asynchronous replication?","answer":"Synchronous replication can provide stronger acknowledgement durability at the cost of additional coordination and latency, while asynchronous replication can reduce write latency but introduces a window in which acknowledged state may not yet exist at all replicas.","explanation":"The appropriate model depends on business loss tolerance, failure domains, network characteristics, and consistency requirements. Neither model universally dominates.","principle":"Replication mode determines the relationship between write acknowledgement, latency, consistency, and recoverable data loss.","rule":"Choose replication semantics from explicit durability and consistency requirements rather than availability assumptions alone.","examples":[{"scenario":"Critical financial state requires acknowledgement only after durable replication.","result":"Synchronous or equivalent stronger acknowledgement semantics may be appropriate if supported."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming asynchronous replication provides zero data-loss guarantees.","reason":"Replica lag can exist."}],"problem":"Replication strategy does not match data-loss tolerance.","failure":"Unexpected data loss or unacceptable write latency.","cause":"Unstated replication semantics.","solution":"Define acceptable loss window and acknowledgement boundary before selecting replication mode.","alternatives":["Quorum replication","Write-ahead log shipping","Multi-copy durable storage"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger replication can increase latency and reduce availability during partition conditions.","constraints":["Network partition behavior must be defined.","Failure-domain placement matters."],"edge_cases":["Replica lag","Network partition","Replica divergence","Recovery from stale replica"],"security":"Replication channels require authentication and integrity protection.","performance":"Synchronous coordination generally increases write-path latency relative to asynchronous replication.","testing":"Measure and validate behavior during replica loss and network partition scenarios.","verification":"Verify that observed recovery behavior matches documented replication guarantees.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B34-001"],"prerequisites":["Distributed storage","Consistency"],"relationships":["D22-B34-003","D22-B38-002"],"provenance":["Derived distributed storage reliability principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B34-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Data Reliability","subtopic":"Corruption","concept":"Detection of silent data corruption","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why must reliability systems consider silent data corruption separately from infrastructure availability failures?","answer":"A storage system can remain reachable while returning incorrect or corrupted data. Availability checks alone cannot establish data integrity.","explanation":"Silent corruption can propagate through replicas, caches, backups, and downstream computations if integrity is not independently validated. Reliability therefore includes correctness and recoverability of stored state.","principle":"Reachability is not evidence of data correctness.","rule":"Use integrity mechanisms appropriate to the data, storage system, and threat model, and verify restoration from known-good state.","examples":[{"scenario":"A replicated object has an integrity mismatch.","result":"The system should detect or reject the corrupted copy rather than treating replica availability as sufficient."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Using successful storage reads as the only integrity signal.","reason":"A successful read can still return incorrect content."}],"problem":"Corrupted state is served as valid data.","failure":"Silent correctness failure.","cause":"No independent integrity validation.","solution":"Use checksums, versioning, consistency validation, anomaly detection, and trusted recovery sources as appropriate.","alternatives":["Content-addressed storage","Merkle structures","Periodic integrity scans"],"tradeoffs":"Integrity validation consumes compute and I/O resources.","constraints":["Integrity mechanism must match corruption and threat models.","Checksums alone do not necessarily provide authenticity."],"edge_cases":["Correlated replica corruption","Bit rot","Application-level logical corruption"],"security":"Integrity protection must distinguish accidental corruption from malicious tampering.","performance":"Periodic validation can be scheduled and sampled according to risk.","testing":"Inject controlled corruption in non-production environments and verify detection and recovery.","verification":"Verify that detected corruption cannot silently become the authoritative state.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B34-001"],"prerequisites":["Data integrity","Recovery"],"relationships":["D22-B34-004","D22-B38-003"],"provenance":["Derived data-reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.95,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B34-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Data Reliability","subtopic":"Backups","concept":"Backup validity versus backup existence","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"Why does the existence of backups not prove recoverability?","answer":"A backup may be incomplete, corrupted, inaccessible, incompatible with the restoration environment, or missing required metadata and dependencies. Recoverability requires successful restoration and validation.","explanation":"Backup systems often report successful creation without proving that a complete application state can be reconstructed. Recovery testing must validate the actual restoration path.","principle":"A backup is a reliability control only if it can produce an acceptable recoverable state.","rule":"Test restoration from representative backups and verify application-level correctness.","examples":[{"scenario":"Database backup restores successfully but required encryption keys are unavailable.","result":"The backup exists but the application state is not recoverable."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Using backup-job success status as the sole disaster-recovery verification.","reason":"Backup creation does not prove end-to-end recovery."}],"problem":"Organizations discover unusable backups during actual incidents.","failure":"Recovery cannot reconstruct required state.","cause":"Backup validation limited to creation success.","solution":"Perform scheduled restore tests with integrity, dependency, and application-level validation.","alternatives":["Automated restore pipelines","Recovery drills","Immutable backup validation"],"tradeoffs":"Restore testing consumes compute, storage, and operational effort.","constraints":["Restored data must be handled securely.","Production data may require masking or controlled environments."],"edge_cases":["Cross-version restore","Missing keys","Schema incompatibility","Partial backup"],"security":"Restored backups can contain sensitive information and require equivalent access controls.","performance":"Large restore tests can be resource intensive.","testing":"Restore representative backups and execute application-level consistency checks.","verification":"Record restoration evidence, restored version, validation results, and unresolved discrepancies.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B34-001"],"prerequisites":["Backup systems","Recovery testing"],"relationships":["D22-B38-004","D22-B40-004"],"provenance":["Derived disaster-recovery engineering practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B34-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Data Reliability","subtopic":"Recovery Point","concept":"Recovery point objective as a data-loss boundary","knowledge_type":"reliability_requirement","question":"What does a recovery point objective define?","answer":"A recovery point objective defines the maximum acceptable amount of data loss, typically expressed as a time interval or equivalent state boundary, under the stated disaster scenario.","explanation":"The objective must be connected to actual replication, backup, logging, and recovery mechanisms. It is not merely a backup schedule; the achievable recovery point depends on the complete data-protection pipeline.","principle":"A recovery objective is meaningful only when implementation and verification can demonstrate the stated boundary.","rule":"Map each recovery-point requirement to concrete data-protection mechanisms and test evidence.","examples":[{"scenario":"A system has a defined maximum recoverable data-loss window.","result":"Replication and backup mechanisms must preserve state within that boundary under the relevant failure assumptions."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declaring a recovery point solely because backups run daily.","reason":"Backup frequency alone does not establish actual recoverable state or restoration success."}],"problem":"Recovery requirements are disconnected from technical controls.","failure":"Data loss exceeds accepted business tolerance.","cause":"Unverified recovery-point assumption.","solution":"Define, implement, measure, and test the recovery-point boundary.","alternatives":["Continuous replication","Frequent snapshots","Durable event logs"],"tradeoffs":"Smaller recovery-point windows generally require more continuous data protection and operational resources.","constraints":["Failure scenario must be explicitly defined.","Recovery-point measurement must use consistent timestamps or state markers."],"edge_cases":["Correlated storage failure","Replication lag","Backup corruption","Application-level inconsistent snapshots"],"security":"Recovery data must preserve access controls and audit requirements.","performance":"Tighter recovery points can increase write-path or replication overhead.","testing":"Perform controlled recovery scenarios and measure the oldest recoverable state relative to the declared objective.","verification":"Require recorded evidence that recovery-point behavior meets the defined requirement under tested scenarios.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B34-002","D22-B34-004"],"prerequisites":["Backup","Replication","Recovery objectives"],"relationships":["D22-B38-001","D22-B38-004"],"provenance":["Derived disaster-recovery principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B35-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Deployment Reliability","subtopic":"Change Safety","concept":"Progressive delivery as a reliability control","knowledge_type":"deployment_pattern","question":"How can progressive delivery reduce the reliability impact of a defective software change?","answer":"Progressive delivery exposes a change to a controlled portion of traffic or infrastructure before wider rollout, allowing detection of abnormal behavior and controlled rollback or pause.","explanation":"The reliability value comes from limiting blast radius and creating an observation window. It requires meaningful health signals and a reliable rollback or forward-fix mechanism.","principle":"Reducing exposure during change reduces the maximum blast radius of undetected defects.","rule":"Changes with meaningful outage risk should have bounded exposure and explicit promotion or rollback criteria.","examples":[{"scenario":"A new version is initially deployed to a limited subset of instances.","result":"A severe defect can affect fewer users before rollout is stopped."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Calling a deployment progressive while automatically promoting without health criteria.","reason":"Controlled exposure without decision gates does not provide the full reliability benefit."}],"problem":"Defective changes reach the entire fleet before detection.","failure":"Large-scale deployment-induced outage.","cause":"Unbounded rollout.","solution":"Use staged exposure, measurable health criteria, and reversible deployment mechanisms.","alternatives":["Canary release","Blue-green deployment","Feature flags"],"tradeoffs":"Progressive delivery increases deployment complexity and requires reliable telemetry.","constraints":["Health signals must be representative.","Rollback must account for schema and state compatibility."],"edge_cases":["Irreversible data migrations","Long-running jobs","Mixed-version compatibility"],"security":"Deployment controls require strong authorization and auditability.","performance":"Canary analysis must distinguish expected load differences from regressions.","testing":"Inject known failure behavior into deployment pipelines and verify rollout halting.","verification":"Verify that promotion stops when defined reliability criteria are violated.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-003"],"prerequisites":["Deployment automation","Reliability telemetry"],"relationships":["D22-B35-002","D22-B37-003"],"provenance":["Derived deployment reliability practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B35-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Deployment Reliability","subtopic":"Rollback","concept":"Rollback safety and compatibility","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why is rollback not automatically safe after a failed deployment?","answer":"A previous application version may be incompatible with data schemas, persisted state, messages, migrations, or external interfaces created by the new version.","explanation":"Code rollback and state rollback are different operations. A reliable deployment strategy must explicitly model compatibility and irreversible changes.","principle":"A reversible application artifact does not imply a reversible system state.","rule":"Validate backward compatibility and rollback behavior before deploying changes that modify persistent or externally visible state.","examples":[{"scenario":"Version N changes a database schema in a backward-incompatible way.","result":"Returning to version N-1 may fail even if its binary is still available."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming previous binary availability guarantees rollback.","reason":"Persistent state may have changed incompatibly."}],"problem":"Rollback creates a second outage after the original deployment failure.","failure":"Recovery attempt fails due to state incompatibility.","cause":"Rollback path tested only at artifact level.","solution":"Use compatible schema evolution, expand-contract migrations, or an explicitly tested recovery strategy.","alternatives":["Forward fix","Blue-green with compatible state","Versioned schemas"],"tradeoffs":"Compatibility layers can increase temporary complexity and storage usage.","constraints":["Irreversible migrations require special recovery planning.","External consumers must be considered."],"edge_cases":["Message schema changes","Data format migrations","Feature-flagged state","Third-party API changes"],"security":"Rollback must not restore vulnerable or revoked security behavior without explicit authorization.","performance":"Compatibility layers can add processing overhead during migration windows.","testing":"Deploy, migrate state, roll back, and verify application correctness in an isolated environment.","verification":"Require evidence that rollback preserves data integrity and critical functionality.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B35-001"],"prerequisites":["Schema evolution","Deployment rollback"],"relationships":["D22-B34-004","D22-B35-003"],"provenance":["Derived deployment reliability principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B35-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Deployment Reliability","subtopic":"Configuration","concept":"Configuration change as a reliability event","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"Why should configuration changes receive reliability controls similar to code deployments?","answer":"Configuration can change routing, resource limits, feature behavior, credentials, dependency endpoints, timeouts, and safety controls without changing application binaries.","explanation":"A configuration defect can therefore produce the same or greater operational impact as a code defect. Reliable systems version, validate, observe, and safely roll back consequential configuration changes.","principle":"Operational behavior is determined by code plus configuration and state.","rule":"Treat consequential configuration changes as controlled production changes with validation and rollback semantics.","examples":[{"scenario":"A timeout is changed from seconds to milliseconds across a fleet.","result":"The service can experience widespread failure without any code change."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Allowing unrestricted runtime configuration edits because no binary deployment occurred.","reason":"Configuration can materially change system behavior."}],"problem":"High-impact configuration changes bypass change controls.","failure":"Configuration-induced outage.","cause":"Configuration treated as operational metadata rather than executable behavior.","solution":"Version, validate, audit, stage, and safely revert critical configuration.","alternatives":["Configuration-as-code","Schema validation","Staged configuration rollout"],"tradeoffs":"Controls can slow emergency changes if workflows are overly rigid.","constraints":["Emergency procedures need explicit safeguards.","Configuration schemas must evolve compatibly."],"edge_cases":["Secrets rotation","Feature flags","Dynamic routing","Rate limits"],"security":"Configuration stores often contain sensitive data and require strict access controls.","performance":"Configuration validation should avoid introducing startup or runtime bottlenecks.","testing":"Apply invalid, extreme, and boundary configuration values in test environments.","verification":"Verify that configuration changes are traceable and reversible.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B35-001"],"prerequisites":["Configuration management","Change control"],"relationships":["D22-B35-002","D22-B37-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability engineering principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B35-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Deployment Reliability","subtopic":"Mixed Versions","concept":"Mixed-version compatibility","knowledge_type":"distributed_systems_rule","question":"Why must distributed systems explicitly support mixed-version operation during rolling deployments?","answer":"During a rolling deployment, old and new instances coexist and may exchange requests, messages, or shared state. Incompatible assumptions can therefore cause failures before the rollout completes.","explanation":"Version transitions should be treated as a temporary distributed system state. Protocol, schema, feature, and message compatibility must be considered.","principle":"A rolling deployment creates a mixed-version system that must be correct during the transition, not only before and after it.","rule":"Validate interoperability between adjacent supported versions before production rollout.","examples":[{"scenario":"Version N adds a field while version N-1 ignores unknown fields.","result":"The transition can remain compatible if the protocol semantics permit it."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Changing a required wire-format field while old consumers remain active.","reason":"Old consumers may reject or misinterpret the new format."}],"problem":"Deployment transition itself becomes an outage trigger.","failure":"Old and new components cannot communicate safely.","cause":"No mixed-version compatibility model.","solution":"Design backward/forward-compatible protocols and test rolling transitions.","alternatives":["Blue-green deployment","Atomic fleet replacement"],"tradeoffs":"Compatibility support can require temporary dual-format handling.","constraints":["Compatibility window must be explicitly bounded.","External clients may remain on older versions longer."],"edge_cases":["Long-lived connections","Queued messages","Cached schemas","Partial rollback"],"security":"Compatibility mechanisms must not weaken authentication or validation.","performance":"Supporting multiple formats can increase parsing and processing overhead.","testing":"Run mixed-version integration tests and interrupted rollout simulations.","verification":"Verify correctness during N/N-1 coexistence and partial rollback.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B35-002"],"prerequisites":["Versioned protocols","Rolling deployment"],"relationships":["D22-B33-003","D22-B35-005"],"provenance":["Derived distributed deployment principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B35-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Deployment Reliability","subtopic":"Deployment Interruption","concept":"Safe interrupted deployment","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"What should happen if a deployment stops halfway through rollout?","answer":"The system should remain in a known, supportable state where the mixed-version population is compatible, the rollout can resume or safely reverse, and critical service capacity remains available.","explanation":"Deployment failures can include CI interruption, infrastructure loss, operator cancellation, health-gate failure, or dependency outages. Reliability requires an explicit intermediate-state model.","principle":"Every deployment state, including interrupted states, must have defined operational semantics.","rule":"Design deployment automation to recover safely from interruption at arbitrary rollout points.","examples":[{"scenario":"Deployment automation loses connectivity after updating 40 percent of instances.","result":"The fleet must remain compatible and provide a deterministic resume, pause, or rollback path."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming deployment tooling always completes atomically.","reason":"Distributed deployment actions are often non-atomic."}],"problem":"Partial rollout leaves the fleet in an unsupported state.","failure":"Persistent mixed-version instability.","cause":"No interrupted-deployment recovery model.","solution":"Use idempotent rollout steps, durable deployment state, compatibility guarantees, and explicit recovery procedures.","alternatives":["Transactional deployment orchestration","Immutable infrastructure","Blue-green switching"],"tradeoffs":"More robust orchestration adds state-management complexity.","constraints":["Deployment state must survive controller restarts.","Operators need clear recovery actions."],"edge_cases":["Controller crash","Network partition","Expired credentials","Partial rollback"],"security":"Deployment recovery actions require authorization and complete audit trails.","performance":"Safe rollout mechanisms may temporarily reduce available capacity.","testing":"Interrupt deployment at multiple stages and verify deterministic recovery.","verification":"Verify that every interruption point has a supported next action and no hidden manual state repair is required.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B35-004"],"prerequisites":["Deployment state management","Mixed-version compatibility"],"relationships":["D22-B35-001","D22-B35-002"],"provenance":["Derived deployment reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.95,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B36-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Consensus","concept":"Coordination dependency as a reliability risk","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"Why can strong coordination mechanisms become reliability bottlenecks?","answer":"Coordination requires communication among participants and can block progress during partitions, quorum loss, or coordinator failure depending on the protocol.","explanation":"Coordination can improve correctness but introduces availability and failure-mode dependencies. Reliability design should use the minimum coordination necessary for the required invariant.","principle":"Every coordination requirement creates additional failure interactions.","rule":"Identify which operations truly require coordination and isolate non-critical operations from coordination dependencies where safe.","examples":[{"scenario":"A read-only cache lookup requires no consensus while a leader election does.","result":"Only the election path needs the stronger coordination dependency."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Making every user request synchronously depend on a coordination service.","reason":"Coordinator failure becomes a broad availability dependency."}],"problem":"Coordination service becomes a system-wide bottleneck.","failure":"Broad service unavailability during coordinator failure.","cause":"Overuse of synchronous coordination.","solution":"Minimize coordination on critical request paths and define bounded behavior during coordinator loss.","alternatives":["Local state","Leases","Eventual consistency"],"tradeoffs":"Less coordination can weaken consistency or freshness guarantees.","constraints":["Safety invariants must remain enforceable.","Stale state must have explicit limits."],"edge_cases":["Network partition","Split-brain","Leader loss","Clock uncertainty"],"security":"Coordination credentials and membership controls are high-impact security dependencies.","performance":"Reducing coordination can improve latency and throughput.","testing":"Simulate coordinator loss and partition conditions.","verification":"Verify critical safety properties while coordination is unavailable.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B32-003"],"prerequisites":["Distributed coordination","Consistency"],"relationships":["D22-B36-002","D22-B36-003"],"provenance":["Derived distributed-systems reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.95,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B36-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Leases","concept":"Lease expiration as a safety boundary","knowledge_type":"distributed_systems_rule","question":"Why should lease-based autonomy have explicit expiration semantics?","answer":"A lease limits how long a component may continue acting under previously granted authority when the authority source becomes unavailable.","explanation":"Without expiration, temporary loss of coordination can turn into indefinite stale authority. With an explicit lease boundary, the system can fail closed or transition to a safe degraded mode when freshness can no longer be guaranteed.","principle":"Bounded authority limits the impact of stale coordination state.","rule":"Every lease must have explicit duration, renewal semantics, failure behavior, and clock assumptions.","examples":[{"scenario":"A worker receives a lease to process a partition.","result":"If renewal fails beyond the permitted boundary, the worker must stop or enter the defined safe state."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Continuing indefinitely after lease renewal failure.","reason":"The authority is no longer demonstrably current."}],"problem":"Stale authority persists after coordination loss.","failure":"Conflicting or unauthorized concurrent activity.","cause":"Unbounded lease validity.","solution":"Use bounded leases with conservative expiration and clearly defined renewal failure behavior.","alternatives":["Fencing tokens","Epoch numbers","Centralized ownership records"],"tradeoffs":"Shorter leases improve stale-authority protection but increase coordination traffic and sensitivity to timing.","constraints":["Clock and network assumptions must be explicit.","Expiration must fail safely."],"edge_cases":["Long network partition","Clock skew","Process pause","Delayed renewal"],"security":"Lease mechanisms must prevent unauthorized extension or replay.","performance":"Frequent renewal increases coordination overhead.","testing":"Delay renewal, pause processes, and introduce network partitions to verify safe expiration.","verification":"Verify that expired authority cannot continue producing protected side effects.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B36-001"],"prerequisites":["Distributed ownership","Time semantics"],"relationships":["D22-B36-003","D22-B37-004"],"provenance":["Derived distributed coordination principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B36-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Fencing","concept":"Fencing stale writers","knowledge_type":"correctness_pattern","question":"What problem does fencing solve in distributed ownership systems?","answer":"Fencing prevents an old or disconnected owner from continuing to modify shared state after ownership has moved to a newer owner.","explanation":"A process may believe it still owns a resource after losing communication. Timeouts alone do not guarantee that it has stopped. A monotonically ordered fencing token can allow the protected resource to reject stale operations.","principle":"Ownership transfer must protect against delayed or stale actors, not merely declare a new owner.","rule":"When stale writers can cause harmful effects, enforce ownership at the resource boundary using a verifiable generation or fencing mechanism.","examples":[{"scenario":"Worker A loses connectivity and worker B becomes owner.","result":"Operations from A carrying an older fencing token are rejected by the shared resource."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Relying only on worker A's local timeout to stop writes.","reason":"A paused or partitioned process may continue after the timeout from another node's perspective."}],"problem":"Old owner corrupts state after ownership transfer.","failure":"Concurrent stale writes.","cause":"Ownership is enforced only by cooperative participants.","solution":"Use fencing tokens or equivalent resource-enforced generation checks.","alternatives":["Epoch-based ownership","Generation numbers","Resource-side compare-and-check"],"tradeoffs":"Requires support from the protected resource and introduces additional state.","constraints":["Token ordering must be reliable.","Every protected operation must carry the fencing information."],"edge_cases":["Token reuse","Resource failover","Delayed network packets"],"security":"Fencing prevents stale authority but does not replace authentication or authorization.","performance":"Resource-side token validation adds small per-operation overhead.","testing":"Simulate delayed old-owner operations after ownership transfer.","verification":"Verify that all stale-owner writes are rejected by the protected resource.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B36-002"],"prerequisites":["Distributed ownership","Concurrency control"],"relationships":["D22-B34-003","D22-B36-004"],"provenance":["Derived distributed-systems correctness principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B36-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Split Brain","concept":"Split-brain prevention","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why is split brain dangerous for replicated or leader-based systems?","answer":"Split brain occurs when multiple nodes independently believe they have authority to perform mutually exclusive operations, potentially causing conflicting writes or inconsistent state.","explanation":"Network partitions and faulty failure detection can create competing leadership decisions. Safe designs require quorum, fencing, epochs, or equivalent mechanisms that prevent simultaneous authoritative writers.","principle":"Failure detection alone is insufficient when false independence can create conflicting authority.","rule":"Design leader or ownership mechanisms so concurrent authoritative writers cannot safely mutate the same protected state.","examples":[{"scenario":"Two partitions each believe the other is unavailable.","result":"Without a quorum or fencing mechanism, both may accept writes."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Allowing both partitions to continue authoritative writes without conflict control.","reason":"The system can produce divergent or irreconcilable state."}],"problem":"Partition creates multiple perceived leaders.","failure":"Conflicting authoritative operations.","cause":"Unsafe leadership/failure-detection semantics.","solution":"Use quorum, fencing, epochs, or another mechanism appropriate to the consistency requirement.","alternatives":["Single-writer architecture","External consensus service","Conflict-free data structures where applicable"],"tradeoffs":"Preventing split brain can reduce availability during partitions.","constraints":["Consistency requirement must be explicit.","Recovery must address divergent state if it occurs."],"edge_cases":["Asymmetric network partition","Delayed messages","Clock anomalies","Coordinator restart"],"security":"Split-brain prevention must include authorization state and not rely solely on network reachability.","performance":"Quorum coordination can add latency.","testing":"Inject network partitions and verify only authorized ownership continues protected writes.","verification":"Verify that no tested partition scenario permits simultaneous authoritative mutation.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B36-001","D22-B36-003"],"prerequisites":["Consensus","Distributed ownership"],"relationships":["D22-B34-002","D22-B36-003"],"provenance":["Derived distributed-systems reliability principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B36-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Time","concept":"Clock assumptions in reliability mechanisms","knowledge_type":"distributed_systems_rule","question":"Why are clock assumptions important in distributed reliability mechanisms?","answer":"Timeouts, leases, expiration, event ordering, and recovery decisions can behave incorrectly when clocks differ, pause, jump, or have different monotonicity properties.","explanation":"Wall-clock time and elapsed-duration measurement serve different purposes. Reliability mechanisms should use suitable time semantics and explicitly document uncertainty.","principle":"Time is an unreliable coordination primitive unless its semantics and bounds are explicitly defined.","rule":"Use monotonic elapsed-time measurement for local duration decisions and avoid assuming synchronized wall clocks imply causal ordering.","examples":[{"scenario":"A process measures a timeout using a monotonic clock.","result":"Wall-clock adjustments do not incorrectly extend or shorten the local elapsed duration."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Using wall-clock timestamps as proof that event A causally preceded event B across machines.","reason":"Clock skew prevents that inference without additional synchronization or ordering mechanisms."}],"problem":"Clock behavior causes incorrect timeout or ordering decisions.","failure":"Premature expiration, delayed recovery, or incorrect event ordering.","cause":"Unstated clock assumptions.","solution":"Define clock semantics, use monotonic timers for durations, and use logical or explicit ordering mechanisms where causal order matters.","alternatives":["Logical clocks","Sequence numbers","Fencing epochs"],"tradeoffs":"Explicit ordering mechanisms add metadata and state.","constraints":["Clock synchronization quality must be known where wall time is required.","Timeouts should account for realistic scheduling delays."],"edge_cases":["VM suspension","NTP adjustments","Leap behavior","Long garbage-collection pauses"],"security":"Timestamp validation should account for manipulation and replay concerns.","performance":"High-frequency time synchronization or ordering metadata adds overhead.","testing":"Simulate clock jumps, skew, process pauses, and delayed messages.","verification":"Verify that reliability decisions remain safe under documented clock uncertainty.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B36-002"],"prerequisites":["Distributed time","Timeouts"],"relationships":["D22-B36-003","D22-B38-002"],"provenance":["Derived distributed-systems time and reliability principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B37-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Governance","subtopic":"Service Objectives","concept":"Reliability objective hierarchy","knowledge_type":"governance_model","question":"How should reliability objectives be connected across business, service, and component levels?","answer":"Business objectives should define user-impact tolerance, service-level objectives should express measurable operational outcomes, and component objectives should support those outcomes without becoming isolated optimization targets.","explanation":"Component metrics are useful only when they connect to actual service behavior. A low component error rate does not necessarily mean a business workflow is reliable.","principle":"Reliability objectives should flow from business outcomes to measurable technical behavior.","rule":"Every critical technical reliability objective should have an explicit relationship to a user or business outcome.","examples":[{"scenario":"Checkout completion is the business outcome.","result":"Service objectives can then cover successful transactions, latency, dependency failures, and data correctness."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Defining database availability as the sole reliability objective for the checkout business capability.","reason":"Database availability does not fully represent checkout success."}],"problem":"Technical metrics optimize independently from user impact.","failure":"Declared reliability appears healthy while business operations fail.","cause":"Disconnected objective hierarchy.","solution":"Map business capabilities to service-level reliability indicators and supporting component signals.","alternatives":["Capability-based SLOs","Synthetic business transactions","Error-budget governance"],"tradeoffs":"Business-aligned objectives require more modeling than component-level metrics.","constraints":["Objectives must be measurable.","Ownership must be explicit."],"edge_cases":["Multiple services contribute to one capability","Optional features","Different criticality by time"],"security":"Security-critical outcomes should be represented as reliability constraints where availability and security interact.","performance":"Latency and throughput should be included only where they materially affect the user outcome.","testing":"Validate that injected component failures produce meaningful changes in business-level indicators.","verification":"Trace each critical objective from business outcome to measurable technical evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-005"],"prerequisites":["Reliability objectives","Business capability mapping"],"relationships":["D22-B37-002","D22-B39-002"],"provenance":["Derived SRE governance principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B37-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Governance","subtopic":"Error Budgets","concept":"Error budget as a change-risk control","knowledge_type":"governance_rule","question":"How can an error budget influence engineering reliability decisions?","answer":"An error budget represents the tolerated amount of unreliability implied by a defined service objective and can inform whether additional risky changes or reliability work should proceed.","explanation":"The budget is a governance mechanism, not a permission to intentionally cause failures. Its usefulness depends on accurate service-level measurement and agreed decision rules.","principle":"Reliability capacity can be treated as a constrained resource for change governance.","rule":"Define how measured reliability consumption changes deployment, operational, and remediation decisions before incidents occur.","examples":[{"scenario":"Reliability consumption exceeds an agreed threshold.","result":"Organizations may pause discretionary risky changes and prioritize remediation according to predefined policy."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treating remaining error budget as guaranteed capacity for planned outages.","reason":"An error budget is not a reservation to consume failure capacity intentionally."}],"problem":"Reliability tradeoffs are made ad hoc during pressure.","failure":"Repeated risky changes accumulate operational instability.","cause":"No explicit reliability governance.","solution":"Define measurable objectives, budget calculation, ownership, and decision policies.","alternatives":["Risk gates","Change freeze policies","Reliability review boards"],"tradeoffs":"Governance can slow feature delivery when reliability consumption is high.","constraints":["Measurement quality must be established.","Policies must be explicit rather than inferred."],"edge_cases":["Low traffic periods","Measurement outages","Multiple overlapping objectives"],"security":"Security incidents and reliability events may need separate escalation rules even when they affect the same service objective.","performance":"Performance regressions can consume reliability budget when they cause user-visible failure.","testing":"Test governance automation against synthetic budget-consumption scenarios without manipulating production reliability data.","verification":"Verify that decision policies trigger from defined evidence rather than informal judgment.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B37-001"],"prerequisites":["SLO concepts","Reliability measurement"],"relationships":["D22-B37-003","D22-B40-005"],"provenance":["SRE practice informed by service-level objective governance"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B37-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Governance","subtopic":"Change Risk","concept":"Reliability risk classification for changes","knowledge_type":"decision_rule","question":"What characteristics make a software or infrastructure change higher reliability risk?","answer":"Risk generally increases when a change affects critical dependencies, persistent state, failure boundaries, shared resources, security controls, recovery mechanisms, or a large portion of the production population.","explanation":"Change risk should be assessed from potential blast radius and reversibility, not simply code size. A one-line configuration change can be more dangerous than a large isolated refactor.","principle":"Reliability risk is determined by impact, exposure, coupling, and reversibility rather than change size alone.","rule":"Classify consequential changes using explicit impact and reversibility criteria.","examples":[{"scenario":"A one-line routing change redirects all traffic to a new dependency.","result":"The small textual change can have high operational risk."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assigning low risk solely because the patch contains few lines.","reason":"Patch size does not determine blast radius."}],"problem":"Change review underestimates small but high-impact modifications.","failure":"High-impact change causes broad outage.","cause":"Risk model based primarily on code size.","solution":"Evaluate affected dependencies, blast radius, reversibility, state impact, and exposure.","alternatives":["Automated change-risk scoring","Mandatory canary for high-impact changes","Two-person review"],"tradeoffs":"More controls increase delivery overhead.","constraints":["Risk classification must be explainable.","Emergency paths need equivalent safety controls."],"edge_cases":["Emergency security patch","Infrastructure failover","Feature flag changes"],"security":"Security changes may require expedited handling but still need explicit rollback and verification planning.","performance":"High-performance changes can introduce resource instability even when functional behavior remains correct.","testing":"Use change-specific failure scenarios and rollback tests based on identified risk factors.","verification":"Require evidence appropriate to the classified risk level.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B35-001"],"prerequisites":["Change management","Blast radius analysis"],"relationships":["D22-B35-003","D22-B37-004"],"provenance":["Derived reliability change-management principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.95,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B37-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Governance","subtopic":"Emergency Changes","concept":"Emergency change reliability","knowledge_type":"operational_rule","question":"How should reliability controls behave during emergency changes?","answer":"Emergency changes may use an accelerated path, but they still require explicit authorization, minimal scope, observable effects, rollback or recovery planning where possible, and post-change verification.","explanation":"Removing every control during an incident can create secondary failures or make the original failure harder to diagnose. Emergency procedures should reduce unnecessary delay without eliminating safety boundaries.","principle":"Incident urgency changes the process speed, not the need for controlled action.","rule":"Predefine emergency change procedures that preserve authorization, auditability, bounded scope, and verification.","examples":[{"scenario":"A configuration must be changed to stop an active outage.","result":"The change is authorized, narrowly scoped, monitored, and verified immediately after application."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Making undocumented production changes without recording what changed.","reason":"The resulting system state becomes difficult to reason about and recover."}],"problem":"Emergency pressure bypasses all operational safeguards.","failure":"Secondary outage or inability to reconstruct system state.","cause":"No safe emergency-change workflow.","solution":"Maintain a streamlined emergency procedure with mandatory minimum controls.","alternatives":["Pre-approved emergency runbooks","Break-glass access with audit","Automated safe mitigations"],"tradeoffs":"Emergency controls must balance speed against accidental damage.","constraints":["Break-glass privileges must be tightly controlled.","Post-incident reconciliation is required."],"edge_cases":["Incident commander unavailable","Monitoring degraded","Security incident concurrent with availability incident"],"security":"Emergency privileges require strong authentication, scoped authorization, and immutable audit evidence where feasible.","performance":"Emergency changes should avoid introducing additional load during already degraded conditions.","testing":"Exercise emergency procedures in controlled simulations.","verification":"Verify that emergency changes produce the intended recovery effect and leave a documented final state.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B37-003"],"prerequisites":["Incident response","Change management"],"relationships":["D22-B38-005","D22-B40-005"],"provenance":["Derived reliability operations principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.95,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B37-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Governance","subtopic":"Ownership","concept":"Reliability ownership boundaries","knowledge_type":"governance_rule","question":"Why must reliability ownership be explicit across service dependencies?","answer":"Without clear ownership, failures can remain unresolved because each team assumes another component or dependency is responsible for detection, remediation, recovery, or communication.","explanation":"Reliability is a property of interactions, so ownership must cover both individual services and cross-service failure modes.","principle":"Every critical reliability control needs an accountable owner even when multiple teams contribute to it.","rule":"Assign ownership for detection, mitigation, recovery, dependency contracts, and post-incident corrective actions.","examples":[{"scenario":"Service A depends on service B.","result":"Both teams have defined responsibilities for dependency failure detection and recovery behavior."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assigning ownership only to the service that first receives the user request.","reason":"The actual failure may belong to a downstream dependency."}],"problem":"Cross-service incidents stall because responsibility is ambiguous.","failure":"Delayed mitigation and repeated reliability defects.","cause":"Missing ownership boundaries.","solution":"Define service ownership and dependency contracts with explicit failure responsibilities.","alternatives":["Platform ownership models","Shared reliability contracts","On-call rotations"],"tradeoffs":"Shared ownership can improve collaboration but may blur accountability if responsibilities are not explicit.","constraints":["Ownership must be maintained as architecture changes.","External dependencies require escalation paths."],"edge_cases":["Third-party outages","Shared platform services","Cross-team migrations"],"security":"Ownership should include security-relevant reliability dependencies such as identity and key services.","performance":"Owners should understand resource and capacity constraints affecting their reliability boundaries.","testing":"Run dependency failure exercises with all responsible teams represented.","verification":"Verify that every critical failure mode has an identifiable owner and escalation path.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-005"],"prerequisites":["Service ownership","Dependency management"],"relationships":["D22-B37-001","D22-B40-005"],"provenance":["Derived reliability governance principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.95,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B38-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Disaster Recovery","subtopic":"Recovery Time","concept":"Recovery time objective as an operational boundary","knowledge_type":"reliability_requirement","question":"What does a recovery time objective define?","answer":"A recovery time objective defines the maximum acceptable time to restore a specified service capability after a defined failure scenario, subject to the stated scope and assumptions.","explanation":"The objective is not simply a target restart duration. It includes detection, decision, restoration, dependency recovery, validation, traffic restoration, and any required data recovery steps.","principle":"Recovery time is an end-to-end operational property, not merely process startup time.","rule":"Measure recovery from the defined failure boundary to validated restoration of the required capability.","examples":[{"scenario":"A regional service failure occurs.","result":"Recovery time includes failover, state restoration where required, traffic transition, and service validation."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Reporting process restart time as recovery time.","reason":"The process may restart while the business capability remains unavailable."}],"problem":"Recovery targets are measured too narrowly.","failure":"Actual user recovery exceeds the stated objective.","cause":"Incomplete recovery measurement boundary.","solution":"Define start/end conditions and measure complete recovery workflows.","alternatives":["Automated failover","Warm standby","Hot standby"],"tradeoffs":"Faster recovery generally requires pre-provisioned capacity, automation, and additional cost.","constraints":["Failure scenario must be explicit.","Recovery validation must be included."],"edge_cases":["Dependency recovery slower than application recovery","Manual approval requirements","Partial regional failure"],"security":"Recovery procedures must preserve access control and data-protection guarantees.","performance":"Failover capacity must support expected degraded-load conditions.","testing":"Execute controlled recovery drills and measure the complete recovery interval.","verification":"Verify measured recovery against the defined objective under representative failure scenarios.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B34-005"],"prerequisites":["Disaster recovery","Recovery objectives"],"relationships":["D22-B38-002","D22-B40-004"],"provenance":["Derived disaster-recovery practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B38-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Disaster Recovery","subtopic":"Recovery Sequencing","concept":"Dependency-aware recovery ordering","knowledge_type":"recovery_strategy","question":"Why must recovery order follow dependency relationships?","answer":"Starting an upstream service before its required dependencies are ready can produce repeated failures, corrupted initialization state, or unnecessary retry pressure.","explanation":"Recovery should restore foundational dependencies before dependent services where those dependencies are mandatory, while allowing independent capabilities to recover in parallel.","principle":"Recovery sequencing should follow actual dependency constraints rather than arbitrary startup order.","rule":"Define recovery prerequisites and parallelizable recovery groups for critical services.","examples":[{"scenario":"Application requires database schema and identity services before serving writes.","result":"Those prerequisites are restored and validated before write traffic is enabled."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Starting all services simultaneously regardless of dependency readiness.","reason":"Dependent services may repeatedly fail or overload recovering dependencies."}],"problem":"Recovery creates a second failure cascade.","failure":"Unstable recovery and prolonged outage.","cause":"Incorrect recovery ordering.","solution":"Model dependency-aware recovery phases and readiness gates.","alternatives":["Dependency orchestration","Staged failover","Readiness gates"],"tradeoffs":"Strict sequencing can increase recovery time when dependencies could safely recover in parallel.","constraints":["Only genuine dependencies should block recovery.","Readiness must represent usable state, not process existence."],"edge_cases":["Circular dependencies","Optional dependencies","Partially restored dependencies"],"security":"Security dependencies such as identity and key services may need priority without creating unsafe bypasses.","performance":"Parallel recovery can reduce total recovery time but may overload shared infrastructure.","testing":"Start dependencies in incomplete or failed states and verify recovery orchestration behavior.","verification":"Verify that each recovery gate corresponds to a real readiness condition.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-001","D22-B38-001"],"prerequisites":["Dependency graph","Recovery orchestration"],"relationships":["D22-B38-003","D22-B40-004"],"provenance":["Derived recovery engineering principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B38-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Disaster Recovery","subtopic":"Recovery Convergence","concept":"Recovery convergence","knowledge_type":"recovery_principle","question":"What does recovery convergence mean in a distributed system?","answer":"Recovery convergence means that after a bounded failure condition ends, the system progresses toward a stable intended state rather than remaining indefinitely inconsistent or overloaded.","explanation":"A system may technically restart while replicas, queues, caches, ownership, and configuration remain divergent. Reliable recovery requires mechanisms that reduce inconsistency and resource pressure until normal operation is restored.","principle":"Recovery is complete only when the relevant system state converges to an acceptable stable condition.","rule":"Define the target post-recovery state and measurable convergence signals.","examples":[{"scenario":"A consumer fleet restarts after an outage.","result":"Recovery includes backlog reduction, state synchronization, and stable processing rather than merely process startup."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declaring recovery when all processes report running.","reason":"Processes can be running while backlog, replication, or state convergence remains unhealthy."}],"problem":"Recovery declares success before system state stabilizes.","failure":"Post-recovery instability or repeated outage.","cause":"Recovery defined by process availability only.","solution":"Track convergence of backlog, replicas, state, ownership, and critical business operations.","alternatives":["Recovery checkpoints","Convergence metrics","Post-failover stabilization window"],"tradeoffs":"Waiting for convergence can delay declaring full recovery but reduces premature closure.","constraints":["Convergence criteria must be measurable.","Some state may intentionally remain eventually consistent."],"edge_cases":["Large backlog","Slow replica","Stale cache","Repeated partial failures"],"security":"Recovery convergence must not overwrite newer authorized state with stale state.","performance":"Recovery workload can temporarily exceed normal capacity and needs controlled ramping.","testing":"Create divergent recovery states and verify convergence without data loss or uncontrolled load.","verification":"Require explicit convergence evidence before marking recovery complete.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B33-002","D22-B38-002"],"prerequisites":["Recovery orchestration","Distributed state"],"relationships":["D22-B38-004","D22-B39-001"],"provenance":["Derived distributed recovery principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B38-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Disaster Recovery","subtopic":"Recovery Verification","concept":"Post-recovery correctness verification","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"Why must service recovery include correctness verification rather than only availability checks?","answer":"A service can respond successfully while serving stale, incomplete, corrupted, or inconsistent state after recovery.","explanation":"Recovery changes often involve replication, restoration, replay, failover, or cache reconstruction. These processes can restore process availability without restoring correct business state.","principle":"A recovered process is not necessarily a recovered service.","rule":"Verify critical functional and data invariants after recovery before declaring normal operation.","examples":[{"scenario":"A database is restored and accepts queries.","result":"Recovery is incomplete until required schemas, records, consistency conditions, and application workflows are validated."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declaring disaster recovery complete after a successful health endpoint response.","reason":"Health may not test business correctness."}],"problem":"Incorrect state is served after recovery.","failure":"Silent business correctness failure.","cause":"Recovery verification limited to infrastructure health.","solution":"Use post-recovery functional checks, integrity validation, and representative business transactions.","alternatives":["Synthetic transactions","Consistency checks","Reconciliation"],"tradeoffs":"Deep verification increases recovery time but reduces silent corruption risk.","constraints":["Verification must avoid causing duplicate business side effects.","Sensitive data must be protected."],"edge_cases":["Partial restore","Replica divergence","Stale caches","Missing external dependencies"],"security":"Recovery verification must use controlled credentials and must not weaken production access controls.","performance":"Verification workloads should be bounded during degraded recovery.","testing":"Corrupt or omit selected recovery artifacts in test environments and verify detection.","verification":"Require recorded evidence of critical correctness checks before full traffic restoration.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B34-003","D22-B38-003"],"prerequisites":["Data integrity","Recovery validation"],"relationships":["D22-B34-004","D22-B40-004"],"provenance":["Derived disaster-recovery verification principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B38-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Disaster Recovery","subtopic":"Recovery Automation","concept":"Idempotent recovery procedures","knowledge_type":"automation_pattern","question":"Why should automated recovery actions be idempotent where possible?","answer":"Recovery controllers can retry after uncertain execution outcomes. Idempotent actions allow repeated attempts without progressively damaging system state.","explanation":"A controller may lose communication after successfully performing an operation and therefore repeat it. Recovery automation should distinguish safe repetition from operations that require unique transaction identity or state checks.","principle":"Recovery automation must tolerate uncertainty about whether previous actions completed.","rule":"Make recovery operations idempotent or attach durable execution identity and state checks to non-idempotent actions.","examples":[{"scenario":"A recovery controller repeatedly requests that a service be started.","result":"The operation should converge on the desired running state rather than creating duplicate resources."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Recovery action blindly creates a new resource on every retry.","reason":"Unknown execution state can produce duplicate resources."}],"problem":"Recovery controller retries create additional failures.","failure":"Duplicate or conflicting recovery actions.","cause":"Non-idempotent recovery automation.","solution":"Use desired-state reconciliation, idempotent operations, unique operation identifiers, and post-action verification.","alternatives":["Transactional recovery workflows","State-machine orchestration"],"tradeoffs":"Idempotency metadata and reconciliation logic add implementation complexity.","constraints":["External systems may not support idempotent operations.","Irreversible actions require special safeguards."],"edge_cases":["Partial success","Controller restart","Network timeout after action completion"],"security":"Repeated recovery actions must not bypass authorization or create excessive privileged operations.","performance":"Reconciliation loops should use bounded polling and backoff.","testing":"Simulate controller failure immediately before and after action completion.","verification":"Verify repeated execution converges to one intended system state.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B38-002"],"prerequisites":["Recovery automation","State reconciliation"],"relationships":["D22-B35-005","D22-B38-003"],"provenance":["Derived automation and recovery principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B39-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Observability","subtopic":"Health Modeling","concept":"Health versus readiness versus liveness","knowledge_type":"observability_principle","question":"Why should liveness, readiness, and broader health be treated as different signals?","answer":"Liveness asks whether a process should be restarted, readiness asks whether it should receive work, and broader health can describe dependency, correctness, capacity, or degraded operating conditions.","explanation":"Conflating these signals can cause unhealthy recovery behavior. For example, a process may be alive but intentionally not ready because a critical dependency is unavailable.","principle":"Different operational decisions require different health semantics.","rule":"Define each health signal by the decision it is intended to support.","examples":[{"scenario":"Database unavailable but process remains responsive.","result":"The process can be alive while write readiness is false."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Restarting every process whenever any downstream dependency is unavailable.","reason":"A dependency failure does not necessarily mean the process itself is dead."}],"problem":"Health checks trigger incorrect remediation.","failure":"Restart storms or unnecessary capacity loss.","cause":"Overloaded health semantics.","solution":"Separate liveness, readiness, dependency health, and business correctness signals.","alternatives":["Health endpoint families","Dependency-aware readiness","Synthetic checks"],"tradeoffs":"More precise signals require more operational design.","constraints":["Checks must be fast enough for their consumers.","Health checks should not create significant dependency load."],"edge_cases":["Dependency degradation","Startup migration","Graceful shutdown","Partial capability availability"],"security":"Health endpoints should reveal only information appropriate to their audience.","performance":"Health checks should be lightweight and bounded.","testing":"Induce dependency failure and verify that each health signal changes according to its defined semantics.","verification":"Verify that orchestration actions map to the correct health signal.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B32-003"],"prerequisites":["Health monitoring","Service orchestration"],"relationships":["D22-B39-002","D22-B40-003"],"provenance":["Derived health endpoint and reliability practice"],"evidence":"official_documentation","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B39-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Observability","subtopic":"Business Signals","concept":"Business-level reliability indicators","knowledge_type":"observability_pattern","question":"Why should reliability observability include business-level signals?","answer":"Technical infrastructure metrics can remain normal while users experience failed or incorrect business operations. Business-level signals expose reliability at the capability level users actually depend on.","explanation":"Examples include successful transaction rate, completion rate, order-processing delay, reconciliation failures, or correctness violations. These signals should complement rather than replace technical telemetry.","principle":"Reliability must be observable at the level of user-visible outcomes.","rule":"For each critical business capability, define measurable indicators of successful and correct completion.","examples":[{"scenario":"All servers are healthy but checkout completion falls.","result":"The business-level indicator reveals a reliability problem hidden by infrastructure health."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Using CPU utilization as the primary reliability indicator for a payment workflow.","reason":"CPU does not measure payment success or correctness."}],"problem":"Infrastructure metrics fail to reveal user-visible reliability degradation.","failure":"Business outage remains undetected.","cause":"Observability limited to technical components.","solution":"Combine technical telemetry with business outcome indicators and dependency context.","alternatives":["Synthetic transactions","Domain event monitoring","Outcome reconciliation"],"tradeoffs":"Business telemetry requires domain modeling and careful data governance.","constraints":["Business metrics must be defined consistently.","Sensitive business data should not be unnecessarily collected."],"edge_cases":["Low-volume critical workflows","Delayed asynchronous outcomes","Partial success"],"security":"Business observability can contain sensitive information and requires appropriate access controls.","performance":"Instrumentation should avoid coupling critical paths to heavy analytics systems.","testing":"Inject technical failures and verify corresponding business indicators.","verification":"Verify that critical business failure modes have observable outcome signals.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B37-001"],"prerequisites":["Business capabilities","Observability"],"relationships":["D22-B31-003","D22-B39-003"],"provenance":["Derived SRE and business-observability principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B39-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Observability","subtopic":"Telemetry Correlation","concept":"Correlating symptoms with dependency failures","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should reliability telemetry help distinguish root dependency failures from downstream symptoms?","answer":"Telemetry should preserve request, operation, dependency, and time relationships so investigators can determine whether multiple symptoms share a common upstream cause.","explanation":"Without correlation, an incident can produce many independent alerts for services that are merely victims of one dependency failure. Correlated traces, dependency metadata, and aligned event timelines reduce diagnostic ambiguity.","principle":"Incident observability should expose causal relationships and propagation paths rather than only isolated symptoms.","rule":"Attach stable correlation context to cross-service operations and dependency calls where appropriate.","examples":[{"scenario":"Five services show elevated errors simultaneously.","result":"Dependency correlation can reveal that all failures follow one shared database event."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treating every service alert as an independent incident.","reason":"Multiple alerts may represent one propagated failure."}],"problem":"Alert volume obscures the primary failure.","failure":"Slow diagnosis and conflicting remediation.","cause":"Uncorrelated telemetry.","solution":"Correlate traces, dependency events, health changes, and failure timelines.","alternatives":["Distributed tracing","Dependency-aware alert grouping","Causal event graphs"],"tradeoffs":"Correlation metadata and storage increase telemetry cost and complexity.","constraints":["Correlation identifiers must be propagated safely.","Telemetry retention must meet operational needs."],"edge_cases":["Asynchronous boundaries","Sampling","Retries","Batch jobs"],"security":"Correlation metadata must not leak sensitive identifiers.","performance":"Tracing propagation and storage should be bounded to avoid excessive overhead.","testing":"Create one dependency failure affecting multiple consumers and verify that telemetry supports common-cause identification.","verification":"Confirm that incident evidence can reconstruct dependency propagation for critical workflows.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-001"],"prerequisites":["Distributed tracing","Dependency graphs"],"relationships":["D22-B39-004","D22-B40-003"],"provenance":["Derived observability practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B39-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Observability","subtopic":"Alerting","concept":"Alert on actionable reliability conditions","knowledge_type":"operations_rule","question":"What makes a reliability alert actionable?","answer":"An actionable alert identifies a condition requiring human or automated intervention, provides sufficient context to understand impact, and has a defined response path.","explanation":"Alerts based on every abnormal metric create noise and can hide critical failures. Reliability alerting should prioritize user impact, sustained conditions, and clear remediation ownership.","principle":"An alert should represent a decision or action, not merely an interesting metric change.","rule":"Create alerts from conditions that have defined thresholds, owners, and response procedures.","examples":[{"scenario":"Critical business capability failure exceeds its defined tolerance.","result":"An alert triggers the responsible operational response with dependency context."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Alerting on every transient CPU spike regardless of user impact.","reason":"The signal may not require action and can create alert fatigue."}],"problem":"Alert noise reduces response effectiveness.","failure":"Critical alert ignored or delayed.","cause":"Non-actionable alert design.","solution":"Define alert conditions around user impact, sustained failure, and response actions.","alternatives":["Dashboards for non-actionable metrics","Anomaly detection with suppression","Composite alerts"],"tradeoffs":"Fewer alerts can miss subtle issues if thresholds are poorly designed.","constraints":["Alert definitions require periodic review.","Owners must be reachable."],"edge_cases":["Low-traffic services","Slow-burning failures","Multi-signal incidents"],"security":"Alert channels may contain sensitive architecture details.","performance":"Alert evaluation should not become a high-load dependency during incidents.","testing":"Simulate known reliability failures and measure whether correct alerts fire with sufficient context.","verification":"Verify every critical alert has an owner and tested response procedure.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B39-002","D22-B39-003"],"prerequisites":["Alerting","Incident response"],"relationships":["D22-B37-005","D22-B40-003"],"provenance":["Derived SRE alerting practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B39-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Observability","subtopic":"Telemetry Failure","concept":"Observability-system failure as a reliability concern","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why should the reliability of the monitoring system itself be considered?","answer":"If telemetry, logging, tracing, alerting, or incident communication fails during an outage, operators may lose the evidence required to diagnose and recover the affected service.","explanation":"Observability is an operational dependency. It should be designed so that critical telemetry remains available under the same failure conditions it is expected to diagnose, or it should have independent fallback channels.","principle":"A system cannot safely depend entirely on a telemetry path that fails with the system it monitors.","rule":"Identify observability dependencies and maintain independent or degraded monitoring paths for critical failure scenarios.","examples":[{"scenario":"Application logging depends on the same saturated database as the application.","result":"The application failure can simultaneously remove diagnostic evidence."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming application logs are always available during application outages.","reason":"Logging infrastructure may share the same failure domain."}],"problem":"Incident response loses visibility during the incident itself.","failure":"Recovery is delayed because evidence disappears.","cause":"Observability shares critical failure dependencies.","solution":"Separate critical telemetry paths, use local buffering where safe, and provide independent health signals.","alternatives":["Out-of-band monitoring","Local ring buffers","Independent synthetic checks"],"tradeoffs":"Independent observability infrastructure increases cost and operational complexity.","constraints":["Sensitive telemetry still requires protection.","Buffering requires bounded storage."],"edge_cases":["Network partition","Storage outage","Identity provider outage","Telemetry overload"],"security":"Out-of-band monitoring must follow least privilege and secure transport requirements.","performance":"Telemetry buffering must not exhaust application resources.","testing":"Disable or degrade observability dependencies during controlled failure tests.","verification":"Verify that critical failure scenarios retain sufficient evidence for detection and recovery.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B39-003"],"prerequisites":["Observability architecture","Failure domains"],"relationships":["D22-B32-002","D22-B40-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability observability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B40-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Fault Injection","concept":"Failure-mode-specific fault injection","knowledge_type":"testing_method","question":"Why should reliability tests inject specific failure modes instead of only randomly terminating processes?","answer":"Different failure modes produce different system behavior. Process termination tests crash recovery, while latency, partial packet loss, dependency refusal, stale state, resource exhaustion, and data corruption test different resilience mechanisms.","explanation":"Random failure alone can miss important transitions and does not prove that the system handles the failure modes represented in its reliability model.","principle":"Fault injection should map to explicit failure hypotheses.","rule":"For each critical failure mode, define the injection method, expected behavior, safety boundary, and verification criteria.","examples":[{"scenario":"A dependency becomes slow but not unavailable.","result":"The test validates timeout, cancellation, retry, and degradation behavior rather than only crash recovery."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Using process-kill tests as evidence for all dependency failure modes.","reason":"A crash does not represent latency, partial failure, or semantic corruption."}],"problem":"Testing covers only a narrow subset of failures.","failure":"Unvalidated resilience behavior fails under untested conditions.","cause":"Fault injection not mapped to failure taxonomy.","solution":"Build a failure-mode matrix and test each high-impact mode explicitly.","alternatives":["Chaos experiments","Dependency fault proxies","Synthetic fault environments"],"tradeoffs":"Broader fault matrices require more test design and execution effort.","constraints":["Fault scope must be controlled.","Production fault injection requires authorization and abort criteria."],"edge_cases":["Intermittent failure","Partial partition","Correlated dependency failure","Slow recovery"],"security":"Fault testing must not create unauthorized access or expose sensitive data.","performance":"Fault injection can intentionally alter load and latency; measurements must distinguish injected effects.","testing":"The record itself defines the testing approach.","verification":"Record expected and observed behavior separately and do not treat test execution as successful without evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-002"],"prerequisites":["Failure taxonomy","Fault injection"],"relationships":["D22-B32-002","D22-B40-002"],"provenance":["Derived reliability testing practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B40-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Recovery Testing","concept":"Recovery-path testing","knowledge_type":"testing_method","question":"Why must recovery paths be tested as deliberately as normal operation?","answer":"Recovery paths often execute rarely, involve unusual state, and depend on multiple components. Untested recovery logic can fail exactly when normal operation is unavailable.","explanation":"A system may have excellent steady-state tests while recovery automation, failover, restoration, or reconciliation remains unverified. Reliability testing must include controlled failure followed by recovery and correctness validation.","principle":"An untested recovery mechanism is an unproven reliability mechanism.","rule":"Exercise recovery from representative failure states and verify both restoration and post-recovery correctness.","examples":[{"scenario":"Primary database becomes unavailable.","result":"The test covers failover, traffic behavior, state correctness, and return to normal operation."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Testing only that a backup exists without performing restoration.","reason":"Existence does not establish recovery capability."}],"problem":"Recovery code fails during real incidents.","failure":"Extended outage or data inconsistency.","cause":"Recovery path not exercised.","solution":"Schedule controlled recovery drills and automate repeatable recovery validation.","alternatives":["Game days","Disaster-recovery exercises","Automated restore testing"],"tradeoffs":"Recovery drills consume operational resources and require safeguards.","constraints":["Tests need clear success criteria and abort procedures.","Sensitive recovered data requires protection."],"edge_cases":["Partial recovery","Recovery during ongoing dependency failure","Corrupted backup"],"security":"Recovery tests must preserve production security boundaries.","performance":"Recovery load can temporarily consume significant capacity.","testing":"Execute controlled failure and complete recovery sequence.","verification":"Capture objective evidence for recovery time, data state, correctness, and remaining discrepancies.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B38-001","D22-B38-004"],"prerequisites":["Recovery procedures","Testing"],"relationships":["D22-B34-004","D22-B40-001"],"provenance":["Derived disaster-recovery testing practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B40-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Observability Validation","concept":"Testing observability under failure","knowledge_type":"testing_method","question":"Why should observability be tested while the monitored system is failing?","answer":"Failure conditions can disable telemetry, create telemetry overload, or produce misleading signals. Observability must therefore be validated under the same stress and failure modes it is expected to diagnose.","explanation":"A monitoring system that works only during healthy operation may fail when it is most needed. Tests should verify signal availability, alert correctness, correlation, and operator usefulness during incidents.","principle":"Observability reliability must be tested under degraded conditions.","rule":"Include telemetry failure and overload scenarios in reliability test plans.","examples":[{"scenario":"Application instances fail simultaneously.","result":"Monitoring should still identify the service impact and preserve enough evidence for diagnosis."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Testing dashboards only against normal traffic.","reason":"Normal conditions do not validate incident-time observability."}],"problem":"Monitoring disappears or becomes noisy during incidents.","failure":"Delayed detection or diagnosis.","cause":"Observability not tested under failure.","solution":"Inject failures into applications and telemetry dependencies and verify operational signals.","alternatives":["Synthetic outage drills","Telemetry chaos tests","Out-of-band monitoring validation"],"tradeoffs":"Testing observability adds infrastructure and scenario complexity.","constraints":["Telemetry tests must protect sensitive information.","Alert tests must avoid unnecessary incident escalation."],"edge_cases":["Telemetry backend outage","High-cardinality failure","Network partition","Alert-channel failure"],"security":"Test telemetry access and fallback channels without weakening controls.","performance":"Telemetry overload scenarios should verify that observability cannot exhaust application resources.","testing":"Execute controlled failure while measuring signal availability and diagnostic quality.","verification":"Confirm that critical failure scenarios retain actionable detection and evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B39-005"],"prerequisites":["Observability","Fault injection"],"relationships":["D22-B40-001","D22-B39-004"],"provenance":["Derived reliability observability practice"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B40-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Disaster Recovery Drills","concept":"End-to-end disaster recovery exercise","knowledge_type":"testing_strategy","question":"What distinguishes an end-to-end disaster recovery exercise from an isolated failover test?","answer":"An end-to-end exercise validates detection, decision-making, infrastructure recovery, data recovery, dependency restoration, traffic transition, correctness verification, and return to normal operations as one workflow.","explanation":"Individual mechanisms can each work while their integration fails. End-to-end exercises expose procedural and dependency gaps that component tests cannot reveal.","principle":"Disaster recovery is a system capability composed of technical and operational processes.","rule":"Test the complete recovery lifecycle for important disaster scenarios.","examples":[{"scenario":"A regional service becomes unavailable.","result":"The exercise covers detection through validated service restoration and controlled normalization."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Testing only DNS or traffic failover.","reason":"Traffic movement does not prove data or application recovery."}],"problem":"Recovery components work individually but fail in combination.","failure":"Extended disaster recovery outage.","cause":"No integrated exercise.","solution":"Run scenario-based end-to-end recovery exercises with measurable acceptance criteria.","alternatives":["Game days","Automated disaster simulations","Staged regional failover"],"tradeoffs":"End-to-end exercises are more expensive and disruptive than unit-level tests.","constraints":["Exercise scope must be controlled.","Production exercises require explicit authorization and rollback criteria."],"edge_cases":["Partial regional loss","Identity dependency unavailable","Backup restoration required","Concurrent incident"],"security":"Recovery exercises must maintain data confidentiality and authorization controls.","performance":"Failover capacity must be sufficient for the exercised workload.","testing":"Execute the full documented recovery procedure and capture objective timing and correctness evidence.","verification":"Compare observed recovery behavior against documented objectives and record gaps.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B38-001","D22-B38-004"],"prerequisites":["Disaster recovery","Recovery testing"],"relationships":["D22-B40-002","D22-B40-005"],"provenance":["Derived disaster-recovery exercise practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B40-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Reliability Regression","concept":"Reliability regression testing after architectural change","knowledge_type":"verification_strategy","question":"Why should reliability properties be regression-tested after major architecture or deployment changes?","answer":"A change can preserve functional behavior while altering dependency topology, fault boundaries, resource isolation, recovery timing, or failure propagation.","explanation":"Reliability is an emergent property of interactions. Architectural changes can silently invalidate previous resilience assumptions even when ordinary functional tests remain green.","principle":"Reliability guarantees must be revalidated when the assumptions supporting them change.","rule":"Trigger reliability regression tests from changes that affect dependencies, failure domains, recovery, shared resources, or critical control paths.","examples":[{"scenario":"A shared database is introduced across previously isolated services.","result":"Dependency and failure-domain tests should be rerun because correlated failure risk changed."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Skipping reliability tests because existing unit and functional tests pass.","reason":"Functional correctness does not prove unchanged failure behavior."}],"problem":"Architectural change invalidates previous resilience assumptions.","failure":"Previously tolerated failure becomes cascading outage.","cause":"Reliability regression not detected.","solution":"Maintain traceable relationships between architecture changes and affected reliability tests.","alternatives":["Risk-triggered reliability suites","Architecture conformance checks","Automated dependency-diff analysis"],"tradeoffs":"Additional regression testing increases CI and release effort.","constraints":["Change-to-test mapping must remain current.","Not every change needs the same test depth."],"edge_cases":["Infrastructure provider migration","New shared dependency","Protocol change","Recovery automation rewrite"],"security":"Reliability regression testing must include changes that affect security-control availability and recovery.","performance":"Reliability tests should avoid becoming unnecessarily broad for low-impact changes.","testing":"Run targeted failure-mode tests associated with changed reliability assumptions.","verification":"Require evidence that impacted reliability properties remain valid after the change.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B37-003","D22-B40-001"],"prerequisites":["Change impact analysis","Reliability testing"],"relationships":["D22-B35-003","D22-B37-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability regression principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B41-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure Semantics","concept":"Partial failure as a first-class state","knowledge_type":"architecture_principle","question":"Why should distributed systems model partial failure explicitly instead of treating the system as simply healthy or failed?","answer":"A distributed component can be reachable but degraded, responsive but incorrect, available to some callers but not others, or healthy locally while its dependencies are failing.","explanation":"Binary health models hide important intermediate states. Reliability decisions such as traffic routing, admission control, retry behavior, and graceful degradation require more precise failure semantics.","principle":"Distributed reliability requires explicit modeling of partial and asymmetric failure.","rule":"Represent materially different degraded states when they require different operational behavior.","examples":[{"scenario":"A dependency responds successfully to reads but rejects writes.","result":"The service should represent capability-specific degradation rather than treating the dependency as simply healthy."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Using one boolean healthy flag for all service capabilities.","reason":"Different capabilities can fail independently."}],"problem":"Coarse health state causes incorrect remediation.","failure":"Requests are routed into unsupported or degraded capabilities.","cause":"Binary failure model.","solution":"Define capability-aware health and failure states with explicit transitions.","alternatives":["Capability health models","Dependency-specific readiness","Degraded-mode state machines"],"tradeoffs":"More states increase implementation and operational complexity.","constraints":["States must have precise semantics.","State transitions must be observable."],"edge_cases":["Asymmetric network partitions","Partial dependency outage","Read-only degradation","Regional partial failure"],"security":"Security-critical capability failures must not silently transition into unsafe degraded states.","performance":"Fine-grained health evaluation can increase telemetry overhead.","testing":"Inject partial failures and verify state transitions and routing behavior.","verification":"Verify every degraded state has defined admission, routing, recovery, and observability behavior.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B39-001"],"prerequisites":["Failure modeling","Distributed systems"],"relationships":["D22-B41-002","D22-B43-001"],"provenance":["Derived distributed reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B41-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure Semantics","concept":"Asymmetric failure detection","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"What is an asymmetric failure and why is it difficult to diagnose?","answer":"An asymmetric failure occurs when one participant observes another as unavailable or degraded while the affected participant may believe it is functioning normally.","explanation":"Network partitions, routing errors, load balancer behavior, firewall differences, and directional packet loss can produce different observations at different nodes.","principle":"A distributed system has multiple observations of reality that can legitimately disagree during failure.","rule":"Do not infer global service state from one observer without accounting for observation scope and network asymmetry.","examples":[{"scenario":"Node A cannot reach node B, while B can still reach A.","result":"A and B can legitimately report different connectivity states."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treating A's inability to reach B as proof that B is globally unavailable.","reason":"The failure may be directional."}],"problem":"Local observations are interpreted as global truth.","failure":"Incorrect failover or duplicate ownership.","cause":"Asymmetric failure not modeled.","solution":"Correlate observations across independent paths and use explicit ownership mechanisms.","alternatives":["Multi-perspective health checks","Quorum observation","Fencing"],"tradeoffs":"Additional observers and coordination increase complexity.","constraints":["Observers must have sufficiently independent failure paths.","Observation freshness must be bounded."],"edge_cases":["One-way packet loss","Firewall asymmetry","Route-specific failures","Regional partitions"],"security":"Network-policy changes can intentionally create asymmetric reachability and should be distinguishable from accidental failure.","performance":"Multiple probes increase network and telemetry traffic.","testing":"Inject directional network failures and compare observations from multiple nodes.","verification":"Verify that asymmetric reachability cannot independently trigger unsafe ownership transitions.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B36-003"],"prerequisites":["Failure detection","Distributed networking"],"relationships":["D22-B41-001","D22-B44-002"],"provenance":["Derived distributed-systems failure principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B41-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure Semantics","concept":"Gray failure","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"What is a gray failure in reliability engineering?","answer":"A gray failure is a partial or subtle failure in which a component continues functioning but behaves incorrectly or inconsistently for only some operations, requests, or observers.","explanation":"Gray failures are difficult because binary health checks may remain positive while selected traffic experiences elevated latency, incorrect responses, dropped messages, or stale state.","principle":"Correctness and behavior must sometimes be monitored in addition to reachability.","rule":"For critical systems, detect materially incorrect partial behavior rather than relying exclusively on process-level health.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service returns correct responses for most keys but stale responses for a subset.","result":"Process liveness remains healthy while user-visible correctness is degraded."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declaring the service healthy because its process and port are responsive.","reason":"The failure is behavioral rather than process-level."}],"problem":"Subtle partial failures evade coarse health monitoring.","failure":"Undetected user-visible degradation.","cause":"Monitoring only reachability and process health.","solution":"Use representative correctness probes, outcome validation, and dependency-aware telemetry.","alternatives":["Synthetic transactions","Consistency checks","Behavioral anomaly detection"],"tradeoffs":"Behavioral validation costs more than basic health checks.","constraints":["Checks must be representative and safe.","False positives require controlled handling."],"edge_cases":["Tenant-specific failures","Partition-specific corruption","Feature-specific defects"],"security":"Validation probes must avoid exposing sensitive data or bypassing normal authorization.","performance":"Synthetic checks should remain bounded and representative.","testing":"Inject selective failures affecting only a subset of requests.","verification":"Verify that partial behavioral degradation is detected without causing unnecessary failover.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B39-002"],"prerequisites":["Observability","Failure modeling"],"relationships":["D22-B41-001","D22-B43-002"],"provenance":["Derived distributed reliability principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.95,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B41-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure Semantics","concept":"Failure amplification factor","knowledge_type":"reasoning_model","question":"What is a failure amplification factor in a distributed system?","answer":"It describes how one underlying failure can generate a larger number of requests, retries, resource demands, alerts, or secondary failures.","explanation":"Amplification can occur through retries, fan-out, queue reprocessing, health probes, cache misses, or dependent-service recovery behavior. Understanding amplification helps identify cascading-failure paths.","principle":"Reliability analysis should consider secondary work generated by failure, not only the original failed operation.","rule":"For critical failure modes, identify mechanisms that multiply work or resource consumption after failure.","examples":[{"scenario":"One dependency failure causes thousands of clients to retry repeatedly.","result":"The original failure can become a larger overload incident."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Measuring incident impact only by the number of failed dependency requests.","reason":"Secondary retries and recovery traffic may be substantially larger."}],"problem":"Secondary load turns localized failure into systemic overload.","failure":"Cascading failure.","cause":"Unbounded failure amplification.","solution":"Bound retries, load shedding, recovery concurrency, and health-check traffic.","alternatives":["Retry budgets","Admission control","Recovery rate limits"],"tradeoffs":"Aggressive limits can delay recovery or reject work that might otherwise succeed.","constraints":["Limits must be coordinated across layers.","Critical traffic requires explicit priority."],"edge_cases":["Retry amplification plus autoscaling","Queue replay storms","Cache stampedes","Mass recovery"],"security":"Amplification can be deliberately triggered as a resource-exhaustion attack.","performance":"Failure controls should reduce unnecessary work while preserving useful recovery traffic.","testing":"Measure request and resource multiplication under dependency failures.","verification":"Calculate or empirically bound amplification for critical failure paths.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B33-002"],"prerequisites":["Failure propagation","Load management"],"relationships":["D22-B42-001","D22-B43-003"],"provenance":["Derived cascading-failure principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B41-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure Semantics","concept":"Failure propagation budget","knowledge_type":"architecture_rule","question":"Why should critical systems define an acceptable failure propagation boundary?","answer":"A propagation boundary identifies which components may be affected by a particular failure and where containment mechanisms must stop further impact.","explanation":"Without an explicit propagation boundary, cascading failures can spread unpredictably through dependencies, shared resources, retries, and recovery automation.","principle":"Every critical failure mode should have a bounded blast radius.","rule":"For high-impact failure modes, define expected affected components and verify that propagation does not exceed the boundary.","examples":[{"scenario":"A recommendation dependency fails.","result":"Only recommendation functionality should degrade while checkout remains operational."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Allowing optional-feature failure to consume resources reserved for critical transactions.","reason":"The failure crossed its intended business boundary."}],"problem":"Optional or local failures become critical outages.","failure":"Unbounded blast radius.","cause":"No propagation boundary.","solution":"Combine dependency isolation, resource partitioning, prioritization, and degradation policies.","alternatives":["Bulkheads","Cell architecture","Capability isolation"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger boundaries require additional resource partitioning and design complexity.","constraints":["Boundary must reflect actual dependency topology.","Shared infrastructure can invalidate logical isolation."],"edge_cases":["Shared identity service","Common database","Global configuration","Provider-wide failure"],"security":"Security dependencies may legitimately cross boundaries but require explicit risk treatment.","performance":"Isolation can reduce resource sharing efficiency.","testing":"Inject representative failures and measure affected capability scope.","verification":"Verify observed impact remains within the documented propagation boundary.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B32-004","D22-B41-004"],"prerequisites":["Blast radius","Fault isolation"],"relationships":["D22-B42-002","D22-B45-001"],"provenance":["Derived reliability containment principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B42-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload Resilience","concept":"Failure-aware admission control","knowledge_type":"resilience_pattern","question":"Why should admission control consider system health rather than only request rate?","answer":"A fixed request-rate limit cannot account for dependency latency, queue depth, resource exhaustion, or reduced processing capacity. Admission should adapt to the system's ability to safely accept additional work.","explanation":"During degradation, accepting all incoming work can increase queues, memory usage, connection pressure, and downstream load. Failure-aware admission protects the remaining capacity.","principle":"The safe amount of accepted work depends on current system capacity and failure state.","rule":"Reject, defer, or prioritize work when accepting it would threaten critical reliability objectives.","examples":[{"scenario":"A downstream dependency becomes slow and local queues approach capacity.","result":"Non-critical requests are rejected or deferred before local resources are exhausted."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Continuing to accept unlimited requests because the service process remains alive.","reason":"Local resource exhaustion can follow before process failure."}],"problem":"The system overloads while attempting to remain available.","failure":"Queue, memory, or connection exhaustion.","cause":"Admission ignores degraded capacity.","solution":"Use bounded queues, health-aware limits, priority, and explicit rejection semantics.","alternatives":["Load shedding","Adaptive concurrency limits","Priority admission"],"tradeoffs":"Admission control intentionally reduces accepted work to preserve system stability.","constraints":["Rejected work needs explicit client semantics.","Critical traffic must be protected deliberately."],"edge_cases":["Traffic bursts","Dependency recovery","Tenant-specific overload","Long-running operations"],"security":"Admission controls can mitigate resource-exhaustion abuse but must not become an authorization substitute.","performance":"Adaptive admission can improve tail behavior by preventing saturation.","testing":"Increase load while degrading a dependency and verify controlled admission behavior.","verification":"Verify that critical capacity remains available as non-critical load is shed.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B32-005","D22-B41-004"],"prerequisites":["Overload control","Resource limits"],"relationships":["D22-B42-002","D22-B42-003"],"provenance":["Derived SRE overload-control practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B42-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload Resilience","concept":"Adaptive concurrency limiting","knowledge_type":"control_pattern","question":"How can adaptive concurrency limits improve reliability under changing workload conditions?","answer":"They regulate the number of simultaneously active operations based on observed service behavior, helping prevent queues and resource saturation when latency or capacity changes.","explanation":"A fixed concurrency limit may be too permissive under degraded conditions or unnecessarily restrictive under healthy conditions. Adaptive control can respond to observed signals, but unstable feedback can itself cause oscillation.","principle":"Concurrency should be constrained according to sustainable service capacity rather than arbitrary request volume alone.","rule":"Use bounded adaptive control with stable feedback signals, safety limits, and fallback behavior.","examples":[{"scenario":"Backend latency rises as concurrent operations increase.","result":"The controller reduces permitted concurrency to prevent runaway queueing."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Increasing concurrency automatically whenever throughput drops.","reason":"Throughput drops can be caused by saturation, making more concurrency harmful."}],"problem":"Concurrency grows into saturation during degradation.","failure":"Latency explosion and resource exhaustion.","cause":"No capacity-aware concurrency control.","solution":"Apply bounded concurrency limits and evaluate latency, completion rate, and saturation signals.","alternatives":["Fixed concurrency limits","Token buckets","Queue-based admission"],"tradeoffs":"Adaptive control adds tuning and feedback stability concerns.","constraints":["Controller must have hard safety bounds.","Signals need sufficient quality and freshness."],"edge_cases":["Sudden capacity loss","Noisy latency","Very low traffic","Dependency-induced latency"],"security":"Attack traffic can manipulate feedback signals; controllers require abuse-resistant limits.","performance":"Effective concurrency control can improve throughput by avoiding saturation.","testing":"Vary workload and backend capacity while measuring controller stability.","verification":"Verify that the controller remains bounded and does not oscillate into overload.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B42-001"],"prerequisites":["Concurrency","Control feedback"],"relationships":["D22-B42-004","D22-B43-004"],"provenance":["Derived reliability control principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.94,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B42-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload Resilience","concept":"Priority-aware load shedding","knowledge_type":"resilience_pattern","question":"Why should load shedding preserve high-value work rather than reject requests indiscriminately?","answer":"When capacity is insufficient, indiscriminate rejection can cause critical and non-critical operations to fail equally. Priority-aware shedding preserves resources for explicitly important work.","explanation":"Reliability under overload is often about controlled degradation rather than accepting all work. Priority classification must be explicit and enforced at admission or scheduling boundaries.","principle":"When total demand exceeds safe capacity, controlled prioritization can preserve critical functionality.","rule":"Define workload classes and shed lower-priority work before protected critical work when capacity is exhausted.","examples":[{"scenario":"System capacity falls during a dependency outage.","result":"Optional analytics requests are rejected before critical transactions."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Randomly dropping requests when overload begins.","reason":"Critical operations may be rejected without intentional prioritization."}],"problem":"Overload causes indiscriminate business impact.","failure":"Critical capability becomes unavailable unnecessarily.","cause":"No workload priority model.","solution":"Define priority classes, capacity reservations, and deterministic shedding behavior.","alternatives":["Separate queues","Dedicated capacity pools","Graceful degradation"],"tradeoffs":"Priority systems can starve lower-priority work if fairness controls are absent.","constraints":["Priority policy must reflect business requirements.","Starvation bounds should be defined where necessary."],"edge_cases":["Priority inversion","Tenant fairness","Emergency traffic","Recovery traffic"],"security":"Priority must not be controlled solely by untrusted client claims.","performance":"Priority scheduling can improve critical-path completion under saturation.","testing":"Exhaust capacity and verify deterministic preservation of protected workloads.","verification":"Verify that priority behavior matches documented criticality policy.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B42-001"],"prerequisites":["Admission control","Workload classification"],"relationships":["D22-B37-001","D22-B42-005"],"provenance":["Derived overload-resilience principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B42-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload Resilience","concept":"Feedback-control stability in reliability mechanisms","knowledge_type":"control_theory_application","question":"Why can automated reliability controls make an incident worse if their feedback loops are poorly designed?","answer":"Controllers that react too aggressively, use delayed signals, or interact with other controllers can oscillate, overshoot capacity, or repeatedly switch between incompatible states.","explanation":"Autoscaling, adaptive concurrency, retries, circuit breakers, and load shedding can form coupled feedback loops. Reliability engineering must consider control-loop stability rather than evaluating each mechanism independently.","principle":"Automated resilience mechanisms are control systems and can fail through unstable feedback.","rule":"Define signal, actuator, sampling interval, bounds, damping, and interaction assumptions for adaptive reliability controls.","examples":[{"scenario":"Autoscaling reacts to queue depth while admission control reacts to latency.","result":"Both controllers can interact and produce oscillation if poorly tuned."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming every independent autoscaling or retry controller improves reliability when enabled.","reason":"Coupled feedback can amplify instability."}],"problem":"Resilience automation creates oscillating or runaway behavior.","failure":"Repeated scaling, throttling, or traffic oscillation.","cause":"Unstable or interacting feedback loops.","solution":"Bound controllers, reduce conflicting control objectives, add damping, and test coupled behavior.","alternatives":["Static safety limits","Centralized control policy","Hierarchical control loops"],"tradeoffs":"More damping improves stability but may slow adaptation.","constraints":["Control signals need known latency and sampling characteristics.","Hard safety limits must remain independent of controller output."],"edge_cases":["Telemetry delay","Sudden capacity loss","Multiple autoscalers","Recovery surge"],"security":"Attackers may manipulate observable signals to induce control-loop instability.","performance":"Stable control can preserve throughput and tail latency during changing load.","testing":"Simulate delayed and noisy signals with multiple controllers active.","verification":"Verify bounded response and convergence rather than merely observing a desired steady-state outcome.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B42-002"],"prerequisites":["Control theory","Adaptive systems"],"relationships":["D22-B43-004","D22-B45-002"],"provenance":["Derived control-theoretic reliability principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.94,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B42-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload Resilience","concept":"Recovery traffic as a separate workload","knowledge_type":"architecture_rule","question":"Why should recovery traffic be protected from normal application traffic?","answer":"After an outage, synchronization, cache warming, queue replay, health checks, and replica rebuilding can create a large temporary workload that competes with user traffic.","explanation":"A system can recover the underlying infrastructure yet remain unstable because recovery operations consume the capacity needed for serving users.","principle":"Recovery itself can become an overload source.","rule":"Bound recovery concurrency and reserve sufficient capacity for critical steady-state traffic during recovery.","examples":[{"scenario":"A queue consumer fleet restarts after a long outage.","result":"Replay is rate-limited so that downstream services are not overwhelmed."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Replaying the entire backlog at maximum possible speed immediately after recovery.","reason":"Recovery traffic can recreate the outage."}],"problem":"Recovery causes secondary overload.","failure":"Repeated failure during recovery.","cause":"Recovery workload competes without limits.","solution":"Throttle replay, prioritize work, and progressively increase recovery throughput as dependencies stabilize.","alternatives":["Warm-up windows","Recovery queues","Rate-limited reconciliation"],"tradeoffs":"Slower recovery reduces overload risk but may increase backlog duration.","constraints":["Recovery limits must align with recovery objectives.","Critical work must receive explicit priority."],"edge_cases":["Large backlog","Dependency still degraded","Simultaneous regional recovery"],"security":"Recovery replay must preserve authorization and prevent unauthorized historical actions.","performance":"Controlled ramp-up trades peak throughput for stability.","testing":"Create backlog and restore consumers under controlled capacity limits.","verification":"Verify that recovery does not exceed protected dependency or resource capacity.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B33-002","D22-B38-003"],"prerequisites":["Recovery","Overload management"],"relationships":["D22-B38-003","D22-B42-004"],"provenance":["Derived recovery-overload principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B43-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Graceful Degradation","concept":"Capability-level degradation","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"Why is capability-level degradation preferable to indiscriminate service shutdown when optional functionality fails?","answer":"It allows critical capabilities to remain operational while non-essential features are disabled or simplified.","explanation":"A monolithic health state can cause the entire service to fail when only one capability is impaired. Explicit capability boundaries allow more precise degradation.","principle":"Degrade the smallest meaningful capability boundary that preserves system safety and business utility.","rule":"Identify optional capabilities and define safe fallback behavior before dependency failure occurs.","examples":[{"scenario":"Personalization service fails.","result":"The application serves generic content instead of failing the entire page."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Returning a full service outage because one optional recommendation dependency is unavailable.","reason":"The failed capability is not required for the core operation."}],"problem":"Optional failure unnecessarily removes critical functionality.","failure":"Broader outage than necessary.","cause":"Coarse service failure boundary.","solution":"Design explicit capability isolation and fallback behavior.","alternatives":["Feature flags","Cached fallback","Static defaults"],"tradeoffs":"Fallback paths require testing and may provide reduced functionality.","constraints":["Fallback must preserve correctness and security.","Critical dependencies cannot be bypassed merely for availability."],"edge_cases":["Fallback data becomes stale","Multiple optional dependencies fail","Feature-specific authorization"],"security":"Fallback must not expose data that the primary path would restrict.","performance":"Simpler fallback paths can reduce dependency load during incidents.","testing":"Fail each optional capability independently and verify critical functionality remains usable.","verification":"Verify that each degradation mode preserves defined invariants.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B41-001"],"prerequisites":["Capability modeling","Graceful degradation"],"relationships":["D22-B43-002","D22-B45-001"],"provenance":["Derived graceful-degradation principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B43-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Graceful Degradation","concept":"Fallback correctness boundaries","knowledge_type":"correctness_rule","question":"What must be verified before a fallback path is considered reliable?","answer":"The fallback must preserve required correctness, authorization, data integrity, business invariants, and explicit freshness or quality limits.","explanation":"A fallback that merely returns something can introduce silent correctness or security failures. Reliability requires defining what degraded behavior is acceptable and what must remain impossible.","principle":"Graceful degradation must preserve non-negotiable invariants.","rule":"Specify and test both what a fallback may degrade and what it must never violate.","examples":[{"scenario":"Cached catalog data is used while the primary catalog service is unavailable.","result":"The fallback must respect freshness limits and access restrictions."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Returning any cached value regardless of age or authorization context.","reason":"Availability has been preserved at the expense of correctness or security."}],"problem":"Fallback improves availability while violating important invariants.","failure":"Incorrect or unauthorized degraded behavior.","cause":"Fallback designed without invariant boundaries.","solution":"Define fallback quality, freshness, authorization, and data-integrity constraints.","alternatives":["Static safe defaults","Read-only mode","Feature disablement"],"tradeoffs":"Stricter fallback constraints can reduce available functionality.","constraints":["Fallback semantics must be explicit.","Sensitive data must not cross authorization boundaries."],"edge_cases":["Expired cache","Changed permissions","Deleted records","Regulatory data"],"security":"Fallback paths must enforce the same or explicitly bounded security requirements as primary paths.","performance":"Cached fallback can improve latency but requires invalidation/freshness management.","testing":"Exercise fallback under stale, missing, unauthorized, and corrupted fallback-state conditions.","verification":"Verify each fallback against defined invariants before enabling it in production.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B43-001"],"prerequisites":["Correctness invariants","Graceful degradation"],"relationships":["D22-B34-003","D22-B43-003"],"provenance":["Derived resilience and correctness principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B43-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Graceful Degradation","concept":"Stale-data budget","knowledge_type":"reliability_requirement","question":"Why should stale fallback data have an explicit validity boundary?","answer":"Without a defined freshness boundary, a fallback can continue serving increasingly obsolete information while appearing operationally healthy.","explanation":"The acceptable age of data depends on business semantics. Some data can tolerate minutes or hours of staleness, while other data becomes unsafe immediately after change.","principle":"Staleness is a reliability tradeoff that must be bounded rather than assumed acceptable.","rule":"Define maximum acceptable fallback age for each data class and enforce it.","examples":[{"scenario":"A non-critical informational page uses cached data during dependency failure.","result":"The cache remains usable only within its defined freshness window."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Serving indefinitely old authorization or account-balance information.","reason":"Staleness can create incorrect or unsafe behavior."}],"problem":"Fallback silently becomes permanently stale.","failure":"Users receive materially incorrect state.","cause":"No stale-data validity boundary.","solution":"Define freshness limits and transition to safer degraded behavior when exceeded.","alternatives":["Hard expiry","Background refresh","Read-only degraded mode"],"tradeoffs":"Shorter freshness windows reduce fallback availability but improve correctness.","constraints":["Freshness must be measured using reliable metadata.","Security-sensitive state needs stricter handling."],"edge_cases":["Clock skew","Offline operation","Delayed invalidation","Dependency recovery race"],"security":"Authorization and security policy data should not rely on stale state beyond explicitly justified bounds.","performance":"Refresh mechanisms add dependency and resource load.","testing":"Allow fallback state to exceed its freshness limit and verify safe transition.","verification":"Verify stale data cannot silently remain authoritative beyond its defined boundary.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B43-002"],"prerequisites":["Caching","Data freshness"],"relationships":["D22-B36-005","D22-B43-004"],"provenance":["Derived graceful-degradation principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B43-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Graceful Degradation","concept":"Degradation hysteresis","knowledge_type":"control_pattern","question":"Why can hysteresis improve automated degradation and recovery decisions?","answer":"Hysteresis uses different thresholds or conditions for entering and leaving a degraded state, reducing rapid oscillation when a metric fluctuates around one threshold.","explanation":"Without hysteresis, a service can repeatedly enable and disable a fallback or repeatedly shed and admit traffic during marginal conditions.","principle":"State transitions should tolerate noisy boundaries when rapid switching is harmful.","rule":"Use distinct entry and recovery conditions when repeated state transitions would create instability.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service enters degraded mode above a sustained latency threshold and exits only after latency remains safely below a lower threshold.","result":"Small fluctuations do not cause constant mode switching."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Entering and exiting degraded mode at exactly the same noisy threshold.","reason":"Measurement fluctuations can create rapid oscillation."}],"problem":"Automated reliability state oscillates under marginal conditions.","failure":"Repeated fallback switching or traffic instability.","cause":"No transition hysteresis.","solution":"Use separate thresholds, minimum dwell times, or stability windows.","alternatives":["Dwell timers","Moving averages","State-machine stabilization"],"tradeoffs":"Hysteresis can delay recovery after conditions improve.","constraints":["Thresholds must be justified by system behavior.","Maximum degraded duration may still require escalation."],"edge_cases":["Sudden recovery","Telemetry delay","Low-volume metrics"],"security":"State transition controls must not create windows that weaken critical security enforcement.","performance":"Stability windows may trade immediate adaptation for predictable behavior.","testing":"Oscillate input conditions around thresholds and verify bounded state transitions.","verification":"Verify that state does not repeatedly toggle under expected measurement noise.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B42-004","D22-B43-001"],"prerequisites":["State machines","Feedback control"],"relationships":["D22-B42-004","D22-B45-002"],"provenance":["Derived control-theoretic reliability principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.94,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B43-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Graceful Degradation","concept":"Degradation exit criteria","knowledge_type":"recovery_rule","question":"Why should a degraded mode have explicit exit criteria?","answer":"Without exit criteria, temporary degraded behavior can become permanent, leaving functionality disabled or causing long-term quality loss even after the underlying failure is resolved.","explanation":"Automatic recovery needs evidence that dependencies, data freshness, capacity, and correctness have returned to acceptable conditions.","principle":"Graceful degradation is a temporary state unless explicitly designed as a permanent mode.","rule":"Define measurable conditions for safe exit and validate them before restoring full functionality.","examples":[{"scenario":"Primary recommendation service recovers.","result":"Fallback is disabled only after dependency health and representative responses are validated."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Returning to full functionality immediately after the dependency process becomes reachable.","reason":"Reachability does not prove correctness or stable recovery."}],"problem":"System remains degraded after recovery.","failure":"Persistent reduced functionality.","cause":"No recovery exit criteria.","solution":"Define health, correctness, stability, and freshness conditions for leaving degraded mode.","alternatives":["Manual recovery gate","Automated stabilization window"],"tradeoffs":"Conservative exit criteria can prolong degraded operation.","constraints":["Exit checks must not overload recovering dependencies.","Safety-critical conditions require stronger evidence."],"edge_cases":["Flapping dependency","Partial recovery","Recovery followed by overload"],"security":"Restored functionality must not bypass changed authorization or policy state.","performance":"Gradual restoration can prevent recovery surges.","testing":"Simulate dependency recovery with unstable and then stable behavior.","verification":"Verify that full functionality returns only after defined recovery evidence exists.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B43-004","D22-B38-003"],"prerequisites":["Graceful degradation","Recovery verification"],"relationships":["D22-B38-004","D22-B43-003"],"provenance":["Derived recovery and degradation principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B44-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency Contracts","concept":"Reliability contract for dependencies","knowledge_type":"architecture_contract","question":"What should a reliability contract between dependent services define?","answer":"It should define expected availability behavior, latency boundaries, timeout semantics, failure responses, retry expectations, rate limits, data semantics, and escalation responsibilities where applicable.","explanation":"A dependency contract makes assumptions explicit. Without one, consumers may independently choose incompatible retry, timeout, or load behavior that increases failure propagation.","principle":"Reliability assumptions between services should be explicit and testable.","rule":"Document operational assumptions at dependency boundaries and keep them versioned with interface changes.","examples":[{"scenario":"Consumer expects bounded response latency and non-retryable validation errors.","result":"Provider and consumer can implement compatible failure handling."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Consumer retries every provider error without knowing whether errors are transient.","reason":"Retries may amplify permanent failures or overload the provider."}],"problem":"Hidden dependency assumptions cause incompatible resilience behavior.","failure":"Cascading failures or incorrect recovery.","cause":"No explicit reliability contract.","solution":"Define dependency failure semantics and validate them through integration tests.","alternatives":["Service-level agreements","Interface schemas with operational metadata","Dependency policy registries"],"tradeoffs":"Contracts require maintenance as systems evolve.","constraints":["Contracts must reflect real runtime behavior.","Third-party dependencies may provide limited guarantees."],"edge_cases":["Provider version transition","Partial outages","Rate-limit changes","Maintenance windows"],"security":"Contracts should include authentication, authorization, and security-failure semantics where relevant.","performance":"Latency and capacity assumptions should be measured rather than guessed.","testing":"Test consumer behavior against each documented dependency failure class.","verification":"Verify that consumer behavior matches provider failure semantics.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B31-001","D22-B35-004"],"prerequisites":["Service interfaces","Dependency management"],"relationships":["D22-B44-002","D22-B44-003"],"provenance":["Derived service reliability contract principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B44-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency Contracts","concept":"Timeout ownership","knowledge_type":"distributed_systems_rule","question":"Why should timeout ownership be explicit across nested service calls?","answer":"If each layer independently waits for a full timeout, total latency can exceed the caller's useful deadline and create unnecessary work after the original request is no longer valuable.","explanation":"Timeouts should propagate remaining deadline information where possible. A downstream call should not continue indefinitely after its upstream caller has already abandoned the operation.","principle":"Timeouts should represent the remaining value of the overall operation, not independent arbitrary waits at every layer.","rule":"Propagate deadlines or equivalent cancellation context across nested operations when the architecture permits.","examples":[{"scenario":"Caller has 500 ms remaining and invokes two dependencies.","result":"The downstream call should respect the remaining deadline rather than starting another independent 500 ms timeout."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Every service in a five-hop chain waits its full configured timeout.","reason":"Total work can greatly exceed the original operation's useful lifetime."}],"problem":"Nested timeout accumulation creates excess latency and resource consumption.","failure":"Requests continue after callers no longer need them.","cause":"Independent timeout policies.","solution":"Use end-to-end deadlines and bounded per-operation timeouts consistent with remaining budget.","alternatives":["Cancellation propagation","Deadline headers","Budget allocation"],"tradeoffs":"Deadline propagation requires consistent semantics across service boundaries.","constraints":["Clock semantics must be appropriate.","External systems may not support cancellation."],"edge_cases":["Asynchronous work","Streaming operations","Retries consuming deadline"],"security":"Deadline metadata should not be trusted as an authorization mechanism.","performance":"Deadline propagation can reduce wasted work and improve tail behavior.","testing":"Create nested slow dependencies and verify cancellation/deadline propagation.","verification":"Verify downstream work stops or transitions safely when the parent deadline expires.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B36-005"],"prerequisites":["Timeouts","Cancellation"],"relationships":["D22-B44-001","D22-B44-004"],"provenance":["Derived distributed timeout principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B44-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency Contracts","concept":"Dependency capacity contract","knowledge_type":"capacity_model","question":"Why should dependency capacity assumptions be part of reliability planning?","answer":"A dependency can be technically available while operating beyond a safe capacity level, causing latency, throttling, queue growth, or cascading failure in consumers.","explanation":"Reliability depends not only on whether a dependency responds, but whether it can sustain the expected workload under normal and degraded conditions.","principle":"Availability without sustainable capacity is insufficient for reliable dependency behavior.","rule":"Define expected demand, safe capacity boundaries, overload behavior, and client response for critical dependencies.","examples":[{"scenario":"A provider can process 10,000 requests per second under stated conditions.","result":"Consumers should not assume unlimited capacity and should have bounded behavior near the provider limit."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treating a successful response under low load as evidence of unlimited provider capacity.","reason":"Capacity is workload and condition dependent."}],"problem":"Consumer demand exceeds dependency capacity during bursts or recovery.","failure":"Dependency overload and cascading failure.","cause":"No capacity contract.","solution":"Model demand, capacity, burst behavior, and client-side protection.","alternatives":["Rate limits","Quota contracts","Adaptive concurrency"],"tradeoffs":"Conservative capacity limits can leave utilization below theoretical maximum.","constraints":["Capacity measurements must state workload and environment assumptions.","Provider behavior can change over versions."],"edge_cases":["Burst traffic","Recovery replay","Large requests","Tenant-specific quotas"],"security":"Quotas and capacity limits can mitigate abusive resource consumption.","performance":"Capacity-aware clients reduce queueing and tail latency.","testing":"Load dependencies near and beyond documented capacity under controlled conditions.","verification":"Verify consumer behavior remains bounded when dependency capacity is exhausted.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B42-001","D22-B44-001"],"prerequisites":["Capacity planning","Dependency contracts"],"relationships":["D22-B42-005","D22-B44-004"],"provenance":["Derived reliability capacity principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B44-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency Contracts","concept":"Cancellation as a dependency protection mechanism","knowledge_type":"resilience_pattern","question":"How does cancellation improve reliability when upstream work is no longer needed?","answer":"Cancellation stops unnecessary downstream work, releasing resources and reducing load on dependencies after an operation becomes irrelevant or exceeds its deadline.","explanation":"Without cancellation, timed-out requests can continue consuming threads, connections, CPU, memory, and dependency capacity. Cancellation must be propagated and safely handled.","principle":"Work that no longer has value should not continue consuming scarce resources indefinitely.","rule":"Propagate cancellation through cancellable operations and ensure cleanup is safe and bounded.","examples":[{"scenario":"Client disconnects before a long query completes.","result":"The service cancels or safely abandons the downstream operation when supported."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Ignoring client cancellation and continuing expensive downstream work indefinitely.","reason":"Resources remain occupied for work whose result is no longer useful."}],"problem":"Abandoned work consumes capacity during overload.","failure":"Resource exhaustion and latency amplification.","cause":"No cancellation propagation.","solution":"Use structured cancellation, bounded cleanup, and dependency-aware cancellation semantics.","alternatives":["Deadline enforcement","Work queues","Cooperative cancellation"],"tradeoffs":"Cancellation support increases implementation complexity and requires careful state handling.","constraints":["Non-cancellable external operations need bounded alternatives.","Cancellation must not leave partial state inconsistent."],"edge_cases":["Cancellation after side effect begins","Streaming responses","Database operations without cancellation"],"security":"Cancellation must not bypass authorization or transaction integrity.","performance":"Effective cancellation can release resources earlier and reduce overload.","testing":"Cancel operations at different execution stages and verify safe cleanup.","verification":"Verify abandoned operations do not continue indefinitely and do not leave invalid state.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B44-002"],"prerequisites":["Timeouts","Structured concurrency"],"relationships":["D22-B44-005","D22-B42-005"],"provenance":["Derived distributed reliability principle"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B44-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency Contracts","concept":"Retry responsibility allocation","knowledge_type":"architecture_rule","question":"Why should retry responsibility be assigned explicitly rather than implemented independently at every layer?","answer":"Nested retries can multiply attempts dramatically and overload a recovering dependency. A clear retry owner prevents uncontrolled amplification.","explanation":"If a client, SDK, service, gateway, and database layer each retry the same operation, one logical request can generate many physical attempts. Retry semantics should therefore be coordinated.","principle":"One logical failure should not generate unbounded layered retry work.","rule":"Define where retries occur, what errors are retryable, how budgets propagate, and which layers must not retry.","examples":[{"scenario":"Gateway retries a request while the service also retries its dependency.","result":"Combined retry count must be bounded to prevent amplification."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Adding retries independently to every layer without a shared attempt budget.","reason":"The combined number of attempts can become unexpectedly large."}],"problem":"Layered resilience mechanisms create retry storms.","failure":"Dependency overload during recovery.","cause":"Uncoordinated retry ownership.","solution":"Assign retry responsibility and propagate attempt/deadline budgets.","alternatives":["Single retry layer","End-to-end retry budget","Provider-specific retry policy"],"tradeoffs":"Centralizing retries may reduce local flexibility.","constraints":["Non-idempotent operations require stronger safeguards.","Retry policy must distinguish transient and permanent errors."],"edge_cases":["Nested SDK retries","Queue redelivery plus HTTP retry","Failover retry","Timeout ambiguity"],"security":"Retry controls should resist abuse through intentional repeated requests.","performance":"Bounded retries reduce unnecessary work and tail amplification.","testing":"Enable retries at multiple layers and measure total physical attempts per logical operation.","verification":"Verify worst-case retry multiplication remains within defined limits.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B44-001","D22-B44-002"],"prerequisites":["Retry semantics","Dependency contracts"],"relationships":["D22-B41-004","D22-B42-001"],"provenance":["Derived retry and dependency reliability practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B45-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Architecture","concept":"Cell-based failure containment","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"How can cell-based architecture reduce the blast radius of failures?","answer":"It partitions workloads into relatively independent cells so that a failure or overload in one cell does not necessarily affect all workloads.","explanation":"Cells can be organized by tenant, geography, workload, or another meaningful partition. The benefit depends on preventing critical shared dependencies from rejoining the cells into one common failure domain.","principle":"Smaller independent operational units can bound failure propagation.","rule":"Define cell boundaries around meaningful failure domains and minimize critical shared dependencies across cells.","examples":[{"scenario":"Tenants are distributed across independent cells.","result":"A cell-specific outage affects a bounded tenant population rather than the entire fleet."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Calling a deployment cell-isolated while every cell shares one critical database.","reason":"The shared database remains a common failure domain."}],"problem":"Large systems have excessive shared blast radius.","failure":"Single fault affects broad population.","cause":"Insufficient workload partitioning.","solution":"Partition critical resources and dependencies along cell boundaries.","alternatives":["Regional isolation","Tenant partitioning","Deployment stamps"],"tradeoffs":"Cell architecture increases operational complexity and can create uneven capacity utilization.","constraints":["Routing must reliably map workloads to cells.","Cross-cell operations require explicit semantics."],"edge_cases":["Hot cells","Cross-cell transactions","Global configuration","Cell migration"],"security":"Tenant isolation must be enforced independently of routing assumptions.","performance":"Local cells can reduce contention but cross-cell operations may add latency.","testing":"Fail individual cells and verify unaffected cells continue operating.","verification":"Measure actual blast radius under cell-level failures.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B32-001","D22-B41-005"],"prerequisites":["Fault domains","Workload partitioning"],"relationships":["D22-B45-002","D22-B45-003"],"provenance":["Derived fault-containment architecture practice"],"evidence":"sre_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B45-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Architecture","concept":"Cell recovery isolation","knowledge_type":"recovery_strategy","question":"Why should recovery of one cell be isolated from recovery of other cells?","answer":"A recovering cell can generate synchronization, replay, cache-warming, or provisioning load that could otherwise consume shared capacity and destabilize healthy cells.","explanation":"Cell isolation must include recovery behavior, not merely steady-state traffic. Recovery actions should have bounded concurrency and resource budgets.","principle":"A recovery mechanism should not convert one cell's failure into a multi-cell incident.","rule":"Bound recovery work per cell and protect healthy cells from recovery-induced resource contention.","examples":[{"scenario":"One cell loses several instances and rebuilds them.","result":"Rebuild traffic is limited so shared infrastructure remains available to other cells."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Launching unlimited rebuild operations for every failed cell simultaneously.","reason":"Recovery can overwhelm shared infrastructure."}],"problem":"Recovery destroys the isolation that steady-state architecture provided.","failure":"Cross-cell recovery cascade.","cause":"Unbounded recovery concurrency.","solution":"Use per-cell recovery budgets, scheduling, and priority controls.","alternatives":["Sequential cell recovery","Shared infrastructure quotas","Recovery queues"],"tradeoffs":"Bounded recovery increases total restoration time.","constraints":["Recovery budget must meet acceptable recovery objectives.","Critical cells may require priority."],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous cell failures","Shared provider outage","Global control-plane failure"],"security":"Recovery actions require scoped authorization per cell.","performance":"Controlled recovery prevents shared-resource saturation.","testing":"Fail multiple cells and verify recovery scheduling preserves healthy-cell capacity.","verification":"Verify recovery work cannot consume more than its defined shared-resource budget.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B45-001","D22-B42-005"],"prerequisites":["Cell architecture","Recovery control"],"relationships":["D22-B38-003","D22-B42-004"],"provenance":["Derived cell-recovery reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B45-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Architecture","concept":"Hotspot and skew risk","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why can workload skew undermine otherwise well-designed reliability partitions?","answer":"If one cell, tenant, shard, or region receives disproportionate workload, it can exhaust capacity while other partitions remain underutilized.","explanation":"Partitioning improves fault isolation but can create uneven resource utilization. Reliability requires detecting hotspots and providing safe redistribution or capacity controls.","principle":"Isolation boundaries must account for workload distribution as well as failure distribution.","rule":"Monitor partition-level utilization and define bounded behavior for hotspot conditions.","examples":[{"scenario":"One tenant grows rapidly within its assigned cell.","result":"The cell reaches capacity even though fleet-wide capacity remains available."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Using fleet-wide average CPU to conclude every cell has sufficient capacity.","reason":"Averages hide local saturation."}],"problem":"Local overload occurs despite sufficient aggregate capacity.","failure":"Partition-specific outage.","cause":"Workload skew.","solution":"Use partition-aware capacity monitoring, controlled rebalancing, or explicit per-partition limits.","alternatives":["Dynamic placement","Tenant migration","Partition splitting"],"tradeoffs":"Rebalancing can introduce migration load and temporary consistency complexity.","constraints":["Migration must preserve correctness and authorization.","Hotspot detection requires timely telemetry."],"edge_cases":["Sudden tenant burst","Uneven request cost","Shard imbalance","Regional demand shifts"],"security":"Tenant movement must preserve isolation and access controls.","performance":"Rebalancing can reduce hotspots but adds migration overhead.","testing":"Create extreme skew and verify bounded behavior and safe redistribution.","verification":"Verify no partition can silently exceed its safe capacity while fleet-level metrics appear healthy.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B45-001"],"prerequisites":["Partitioning","Capacity management"],"relationships":["D22-B42-001","D22-B45-004"],"provenance":["Derived partitioned-system reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.95,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B45-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Architecture","concept":"Dependency-aware capacity reservation","knowledge_type":"architecture_rule","question":"Why can capacity reservations be necessary across dependency chains?","answer":"Protecting capacity only at the entry service does not help if a downstream dependency becomes saturated and consumes the protected service's remaining resources through queued or retrying work.","explanation":"Capacity protection must consider the entire critical path and shared downstream resources. Reservations can preserve capacity for critical operations during overload.","principle":"Capacity protection must follow dependency paths rather than stopping at one service boundary.","rule":"Reserve or prioritize capacity at critical downstream bottlenecks where their failure would otherwise block protected operations.","examples":[{"scenario":"Critical transactions require database connections.","result":"A reserved connection capacity prevents optional workloads from consuming all database access."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Protecting API request capacity while allowing optional workloads to consume every database connection.","reason":"The downstream bottleneck remains unprotected."}],"problem":"Upstream prioritization fails at downstream resource boundaries.","failure":"Critical operations still fail under overload.","cause":"Capacity reservation stops at the wrong layer.","solution":"Identify critical-path bottlenecks and protect capacity through the dependency chain.","alternatives":["Priority propagation","Dedicated resource pools","Dependency quotas"],"tradeoffs":"Reservations can reduce overall utilization and increase capacity planning complexity.","constraints":["Reservation policy must be propagated consistently.","Shared dependencies may serve multiple critical paths."],"edge_cases":["Multiple priority classes","Shared database","Cross-service fan-out","Recovery traffic"],"security":"Priority propagation must not allow clients to self-assign privileged capacity.","performance":"Reserved capacity improves predictability at the cost of potential idle resources.","testing":"Saturate lower-priority traffic at downstream bottlenecks and verify protected operations.","verification":"Trace protected requests across dependencies and confirm reserved capacity exists at critical bottlenecks.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B42-003","D22-B44-003"],"prerequisites":["Capacity planning","Dependency graphs"],"relationships":["D22-B42-001","D22-B45-005"],"provenance":["Derived dependency-aware capacity principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B45-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Architecture","concept":"Reliability architecture review","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"What should a reliability architecture review verify before a critical system is considered resilient?","answer":"It should verify failure domains, dependency paths, blast radius, overload behavior, recovery paths, observability, data protection, change safety, and explicit assumptions for critical scenarios.","explanation":"Reliability emerges from interactions among mechanisms. Reviewing components independently can miss failure propagation or incompatible controls.","principle":"Reliability review must examine failure behavior across system boundaries and lifecycle states.","rule":"Review architecture against explicit failure scenarios and verify evidence for each critical resilience claim.","examples":[{"scenario":"A critical service depends on three external systems.","result":"Review covers each dependency's failure behavior, combined degradation, overload effects, and recovery sequence."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Approving reliability based only on replica count and uptime dashboards.","reason":"Those signals do not establish complete resilience."}],"problem":"Architecture contains untested reliability assumptions.","failure":"Unexpected cascading failure or unrecoverable incident.","cause":"Component-focused review.","solution":"Use scenario-based reliability reviews linked to dependency and failure models.","alternatives":["FMEA review","Reliability design checklist","Failure-mode workshops"],"tradeoffs":"Comprehensive reviews require engineering and operational expertise.","constraints":["Evidence must distinguish documented assumptions from executed tests.","Architecture must be reviewed after material changes."],"edge_cases":["Third-party dependency failure","Control-plane outage","Regional disaster","Data corruption"],"security":"Review must include security dependencies and failure-safe behavior.","performance":"Review should include overload and recovery performance constraints.","testing":"Map every high-impact failure scenario to a test or explicitly documented limitation.","verification":"Require traceable evidence for each resilience claim and record unresolved assumptions.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B41-005","D22-B44-001","D22-B45-001"],"prerequisites":["Reliability architecture","Failure analysis"],"relationships":["D22-B40-005","D22-B45-006"],"provenance":["Derived reliability architecture-review practice"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B46-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Operational Safety","concept":"Runbook determinism","knowledge_type":"operations_principle","question":"Why should reliability runbooks define deterministic decision points instead of relying only on narrative instructions?","answer":"Deterministic decision points reduce ambiguity during incidents by specifying observable conditions, allowed actions, abort criteria, and verification requirements.","explanation":"Incident conditions are stressful and incomplete information is common. Ambiguous instructions can cause inconsistent actions or repeated harmful remediation.","principle":"Operational procedures should convert observable evidence into bounded actions.","rule":"For each critical recovery or mitigation step, define prerequisites, decision criteria, action, abort condition, and verification.","examples":[{"scenario":"Failover is authorized only when dependency health meets defined conditions.","result":"Operators can make the transition using observable evidence rather than intuition alone."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Runbook says to fail over if the system 'looks unhealthy'.","reason":"The condition is not deterministic or auditable."}],"problem":"Operators interpret the same incident differently.","failure":"Inconsistent or unsafe remediation.","cause":"Ambiguous runbook logic.","solution":"Use explicit conditions and state transitions.","alternatives":["Automated state machines","Decision trees","Guarded automation"],"tradeoffs":"Highly explicit runbooks require maintenance as architecture changes.","constraints":["Conditions must remain observable during incidents.","Emergency exceptions must be documented."],"edge_cases":["Monitoring outage","Multiple simultaneous failures","Conflicting signals"],"security":"High-impact actions require explicit authorization boundaries.","performance":"Deterministic automation can reduce response latency.","testing":"Execute runbooks in incident simulations with ambiguous or missing signals.","verification":"Verify operators can reach the same decision from the same evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B37-004"],"prerequisites":["Incident response","Operational runbooks"],"relationships":["D22-B38-002","D22-B45-005"],"provenance":["Derived reliability operations principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.95,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B46-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Operational Safety","concept":"Safe abort conditions","knowledge_type":"operations_rule","question":"Why should automated remediation have explicit abort conditions?","answer":"Automation can encounter states outside its assumptions. An abort condition prevents it from continuing actions that could increase damage when required evidence is missing or contradictory.","explanation":"Failover, recovery, scaling, and cleanup operations can become unsafe when dependencies behave unexpectedly. Automation should fail safely rather than assuming the environment matches the normal path.","principle":"Automation must know when not to proceed.","rule":"Define evidence conditions under which an automated action must stop and escalate or enter a safe state.","examples":[{"scenario":"Recovery detects inconsistent ownership state.","result":"Automation stops rather than modifying both ownership records."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Automation continues through any state by repeatedly retrying.","reason":"Unknown state can make repeated actions unsafe."}],"problem":"Automation persists through invalid assumptions.","failure":"Secondary state corruption or expanded outage.","cause":"No abort criteria.","solution":"Add invariant checks, maximum attempts, state validation, and escalation paths.","alternatives":["Human approval gates","State-machine guards","Circuit-breaking automation"],"tradeoffs":"More aborts can require human intervention and lengthen recovery.","constraints":["Abort behavior must leave the system in a safe state.","Escalation must be observable."],"edge_cases":["Partial execution","Unknown state","Telemetry loss","Conflicting controllers"],"security":"Abort checks must not be bypassed by emergency automation without authorization.","performance":"Guard checks add small overhead but can prevent expensive recovery mistakes.","testing":"Inject contradictory state and verify automation aborts safely.","verification":"Verify every high-impact automated action has defined stop conditions.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B38-005"],"prerequisites":["Automation","Safety invariants"],"relationships":["D22-B46-001","D22-B46-003"],"provenance":["Derived safe-automation principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B46-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Operational Safety","concept":"Action verification after automated remediation","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"Why must automated remediation verify outcomes rather than treating successful command execution as recovery?","answer":"An automation command can complete successfully while the intended system state remains unhealthy, delayed, inconsistent, or only partially changed.","explanation":"Execution status answers whether an action was attempted or accepted; outcome verification answers whether the desired reliability state was actually achieved.","principle":"Action completion and desired-state recovery are different facts.","rule":"Verify the resulting system state using independent or outcome-specific signals after consequential remediation.","examples":[{"scenario":"Traffic is switched to a secondary service.","result":"Verification checks successful business operations, dependency health, and stability rather than only confirming the routing command returned successfully."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Marking failover successful because the routing API returned success.","reason":"The target service may still be unable to serve traffic correctly."}],"problem":"Automation declares recovery prematurely.","failure":"Continued outage after nominally successful action.","cause":"No outcome verification.","solution":"Separate action execution evidence from post-action outcome evidence.","alternatives":["Synthetic transaction validation","Business-level checks","State reconciliation"],"tradeoffs":"Verification adds recovery time and monitoring complexity.","constraints":["Verification must avoid duplicate side effects.","Independent evidence is preferable for critical actions."],"edge_cases":["Delayed propagation","Partial failover","Telemetry inconsistency","Stale health state"],"security":"Verification must use authorized probes and preserve access-control boundaries.","performance":"Verification traffic should be bounded during recovery.","testing":"Simulate successful commands with unsuccessful resulting states.","verification":"Require explicit evidence of desired-state convergence before closing remediation.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B38-004","D22-B46-002"],"prerequisites":["Automated remediation","Outcome verification"],"relationships":["D22-B38-003","D22-B45-005"],"provenance":["Derived reliability automation principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B46-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Operational Safety","concept":"Remediation idempotency boundary","knowledge_type":"automation_rule","question":"How should an automated remediation system handle actions that cannot be made idempotent?","answer":"It should use durable operation identity, precondition checks, state inspection, and explicit uncertainty handling rather than blindly repeating the action.","explanation":"Some actions such as creating external resources, sending irreversible commands, or triggering business effects cannot safely be repeated without additional coordination.","principle":"Non-idempotent remediation requires stronger state and execution guarantees than ordinary reconciliation.","rule":"Never blindly retry an irreversible action when its previous execution state is unknown.","examples":[{"scenario":"An external system may have accepted an irreversible operation before the network connection failed.","result":"The controller first queries durable operation state before deciding whether another action is safe."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Repeating an irreversible command after a timeout without checking prior execution.","reason":"The first command may already have succeeded."}],"problem":"Recovery automation duplicates irreversible effects.","failure":"Duplicate or contradictory remediation.","cause":"Unknown action outcome treated as failure.","solution":"Use operation identifiers, state queries, transactional boundaries, or human approval where necessary.","alternatives":["Idempotency keys","Two-phase workflows","Manual confirmation"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger uncertainty handling increases latency and state-management complexity.","constraints":["External provider must expose sufficient state for reliable reconciliation where automation is used.","Irreversible actions may require approval."],"edge_cases":["Provider API timeout","Partial transaction","External side effect before acknowledgement"],"security":"Irreversible actions require strong authorization and audit trails.","performance":"State reconciliation adds extra calls but prevents costly duplicate actions.","testing":"Simulate lost acknowledgements after successful side effects.","verification":"Verify the controller never duplicates an irreversible action solely because its acknowledgement was lost.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B38-005","D22-B46-003"],"prerequisites":["Idempotency","Distributed state"],"relationships":["D22-B33-003","D22-B46-005"],"provenance":["Derived distributed automation principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B46-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Operational Safety","concept":"Automation conflict detection","knowledge_type":"reliability_control","question":"Why should automated remediation systems detect competing controllers before acting?","answer":"Multiple controllers can issue conflicting actions such as simultaneous failover, scaling, configuration changes, or recovery operations, causing oscillation or state corruption.","explanation":"Controllers may include deployment systems, autoscalers, recovery agents, operators, and external orchestration. Without ownership or coordination, each can correctly follow its local rules while collectively producing unsafe behavior.","principle":"Distributed automation requires explicit authority and conflict boundaries.","rule":"Detect competing control actions and define ownership or coordination before high-impact state transitions.","examples":[{"scenario":"An operator starts manual failover while an automated controller is already executing failover.","result":"The system should detect the conflict and prevent duplicate ownership transitions."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Allowing every controller to independently modify routing during an incident.","reason":"Concurrent actions can produce oscillation or inconsistent state."}],"problem":"Independent automation produces contradictory remediation.","failure":"State oscillation or recovery failure.","cause":"No controller ownership model.","solution":"Use leases, epochs, locks where appropriate, or explicit action arbitration.","alternatives":["Single controller","Human-controlled break-glass mode","Centralized orchestration"],"tradeoffs":"Coordination can become an additional failure dependency.","constraints":["Authority must be bounded and auditable.","Emergency takeover needs safe semantics."],"edge_cases":["Controller partition","Stale lock","Operator override","Controller restart"],"security":"Controller authority must be least-privileged and strongly authenticated.","performance":"Coordination adds latency to high-impact actions but can prevent repeated remediation.","testing":"Run concurrent controller scenarios and verify only authorized actions take effect.","verification":"Verify conflicting actions are detected before they produce incompatible state.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B36-002","D22-B46-003"],"prerequisites":["Automation governance","Distributed coordination"],"relationships":["D22-B42-004","D22-B46-004"],"provenance":["Derived reliability automation principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B47-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Recovery Engineering","concept":"Recovery from incomplete state","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"Why must recovery procedures handle incomplete operations explicitly?","answer":"A failure can occur after some side effects have completed but before the operation records completion, leaving partial state that cannot safely be treated as either fully completed or untouched.","explanation":"Distributed operations commonly fail between steps. Recovery must inspect durable state and reconcile partial outcomes rather than blindly restarting the entire operation.","principle":"Recovery must account for partial completion, not only clean success or clean failure.","rule":"Model intermediate states and provide reconciliation for each recoverable incomplete operation.","examples":[{"scenario":"Resource creation succeeds but registration of the resource fails.","result":"Recovery must discover the existing resource before attempting another creation."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Retrying the complete operation from the beginning without inspecting partial state.","reason":"The first part may already have succeeded."}],"problem":"Partial failures create duplicate or inconsistent state during recovery.","failure":"Resource duplication or orphaned state.","cause":"Binary success/failure recovery model.","solution":"Persist operation state and reconcile intermediate outcomes.","alternatives":["Saga state machine","Operation journal","Idempotent workflows"],"tradeoffs":"Intermediate-state modeling increases implementation complexity.","constraints":["Every intermediate state needs defined recovery behavior.","State records must be durable enough for the failure model."],"edge_cases":["Crash between side effects","Lost acknowledgement","External system partial completion"],"security":"Recovery must preserve authorization for every compensating or completing action.","performance":"State journals add write overhead.","testing":"Inject failures after every significant operation step and verify reconciliation.","verification":"Verify each partial state has a deterministic recovery path.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B46-004"],"prerequisites":["State machines","Recovery"],"relationships":["D22-B47-002","D22-B47-003"],"provenance":["Derived distributed transaction recovery principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B47-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Recovery Engineering","concept":"Compensating action correctness","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"Why is a compensating action not necessarily the exact inverse of an original action?","answer":"Real-world operations can have external effects, irreversible transitions, or time-dependent semantics that cannot be perfectly reversed.","explanation":"A compensation should restore the required business invariant or safe state, not merely attempt to mechanically undo each step.","principle":"Recovery compensation should target invariants and acceptable state, not assume perfect reversibility.","rule":"Define compensation in terms of business and system invariants and verify its actual effects.","examples":[{"scenario":"A reservation was created and downstream notification sent before failure.","result":"Compensation may cancel the reservation while recording the already-sent notification rather than pretending the notification never occurred."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming every side effect has a perfect inverse operation.","reason":"External side effects may be irreversible."}],"problem":"Recovery workflow assumes nonexistent reversibility.","failure":"Compensation leaves inconsistent external state.","cause":"Inverse-operation assumption.","solution":"Define compensating workflows around target invariants and residual effects.","alternatives":["Forward recovery","Reconciliation","Manual exception handling"],"tradeoffs":"Compensation can require domain-specific logic and reconciliation.","constraints":["Irreversible effects need explicit treatment.","Business owners may define acceptable residual effects."],"edge_cases":["External notifications","Payments","Physical-world effects","Time-sensitive operations"],"security":"Compensation must be authorized independently and audited.","performance":"Compensation can add additional downstream work during incidents.","testing":"Inject failures after irreversible side effects and verify invariant restoration.","verification":"Verify the resulting state rather than only confirming compensation commands executed.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B47-001"],"prerequisites":["Distributed transactions","Business invariants"],"relationships":["D22-B46-003","D22-B47-004"],"provenance":["Derived saga and recovery principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B47-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Recovery Engineering","concept":"Recovery journal","knowledge_type":"state_management_pattern","question":"How can a durable operation journal improve recovery reliability?","answer":"It records operation identity and state transitions so recovery logic can determine what was attempted, what was acknowledged, and what remains unresolved after a process or controller failure.","explanation":"Without durable operation history, recovery must infer state from external effects, which may be ambiguous. A journal creates explicit evidence for reconciliation.","principle":"Durable recovery state reduces uncertainty after interrupted operations.","rule":"Persist enough operation identity and state information to distinguish pending, completed, failed, and uncertain operations.","examples":[{"scenario":"Controller crashes after issuing a provisioning request.","result":"The journal allows recovery to query the operation identity rather than issuing an unqualified duplicate request."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Using only in-memory operation state for critical recovery workflows.","reason":"Process failure destroys the evidence required for safe reconciliation."}],"problem":"Recovery cannot distinguish prior execution states.","failure":"Duplicate or omitted recovery actions.","cause":"No durable operation state.","solution":"Use durable journals or equivalent state records.","alternatives":["Transactional outbox","Idempotency store","Provider operation IDs"],"tradeoffs":"Durable journals add storage and consistency-management overhead.","constraints":["Journal retention must match recovery horizon.","Sensitive operation data requires protection."],"edge_cases":["Journal unavailable","Journal-write failure","Operation state divergence"],"security":"Journal access must be tightly controlled because it can reveal privileged operations.","performance":"Synchronous journaling can increase operation latency.","testing":"Crash controllers at each operation boundary and recover using journal state.","verification":"Verify that recovery can reconstruct operation state without relying solely on volatile memory.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B47-001"],"prerequisites":["Durable state","Recovery automation"],"relationships":["D22-B46-004","D22-B47-004"],"provenance":["Derived reliable workflow principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B47-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Recovery Engineering","concept":"Reconciliation after uncertain failure","knowledge_type":"recovery_strategy","question":"What should a system do when it cannot determine whether an external operation succeeded?","answer":"It should enter an explicit uncertain state and reconcile against authoritative external state before deciding whether to retry, compensate, or complete the workflow.","explanation":"A network timeout after a side effect is not equivalent to a confirmed failure. Treating uncertainty as failure can duplicate effects; treating it as success can omit required work.","principle":"Unknown outcome is a distinct state requiring reconciliation.","rule":"Never convert ambiguous external-operation outcomes directly into confirmed failure or success.","examples":[{"scenario":"A resource API times out after accepting a create request.","result":"The controller queries resource state or operation status before issuing another create request."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Retrying resource creation immediately after a timeout.","reason":"The original creation may already have succeeded."}],"problem":"Network uncertainty causes incorrect recovery actions.","failure":"Duplicate external side effects.","cause":"Unknown outcome collapsed into failure.","solution":"Represent uncertainty explicitly and reconcile against authoritative state.","alternatives":["Operation IDs","Idempotency keys","Provider-side status queries"],"tradeoffs":"Reconciliation adds latency and requires provider support.","constraints":["Authoritative state source must be identified.","Uncertainty may require manual intervention if state cannot be queried."],"edge_cases":["Provider outage","Delayed state visibility","Eventual consistency"],"security":"Reconciliation queries require authorized access and must not expose sensitive state.","performance":"Repeated reconciliation can add load and should be bounded.","testing":"Simulate lost responses after successful and failed external operations.","verification":"Verify that uncertain outcomes never cause uncontrolled duplicate side effects.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B47-003"],"prerequisites":["Distributed operations","Recovery"],"relationships":["D22-B46-004","D22-B47-005"],"provenance":["Derived distributed transaction reliability principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B47-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Recovery Engineering","concept":"Recovery convergence deadline","knowledge_type":"recovery_requirement","question":"Why should recovery convergence have a bounded expectation even when exact completion time varies?","answer":"Without a convergence bound, a system can remain technically functional but indefinitely degraded, leaving queues, replicas, caches, or incomplete operations unresolved.","explanation":"Recovery objectives should distinguish immediate service restoration from eventual convergence. A bounded expectation enables detection of recovery that has stalled.","principle":"Recovery should have both restoration and convergence criteria.","rule":"Define measurable indicators and acceptable bounds for post-recovery convergence.","examples":[{"scenario":"Replication returns to service but lag remains high.","result":"Recovery is not considered fully converged until lag returns within the defined acceptable range."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declaring recovery complete immediately when the primary service accepts traffic.","reason":"Supporting state may remain divergent or overloaded."}],"problem":"Recovery stalls in a degraded steady state.","failure":"Persistent hidden reliability debt after incident.","cause":"No convergence requirement.","solution":"Define convergence signals, deadlines, and escalation when progress stalls.","alternatives":["Stabilization windows","Recovery checkpoints","Automated convergence monitoring"],"tradeoffs":"Longer stabilization criteria delay closure but improve confidence.","constraints":["Convergence target must reflect realistic workload and failure assumptions.","Some state may intentionally converge asynchronously."],"edge_cases":["Continuous incoming load","Repeated partial failures","Slow replica","Large backlog"],"security":"Recovery convergence must not overwrite newer authorized state.","performance":"Convergence work should be rate-limited against user-serving capacity.","testing":"Create a recovery scenario that stops making progress and verify escalation.","verification":"Verify stalled convergence is detectable before it becomes an unbounded operational condition.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B38-003"],"prerequisites":["Recovery convergence","Observability"],"relationships":["D22-B39-001","D22-B47-004"],"provenance":["Derived recovery reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B48-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Resilience Economics","concept":"Reliability versus complexity tradeoff","knowledge_type":"tradeoff_analysis","question":"Why can adding more resilience mechanisms reduce overall reliability if their complexity is uncontrolled?","answer":"Each mechanism introduces configuration, interactions, failure modes, operational burden, and potential implementation defects. More mechanisms do not automatically produce more resilience.","explanation":"Retries, caches, replicas, failover paths, adaptive controllers, and fallbacks can interact in unexpected ways. Reliability design should maximize justified resilience while minimizing unnecessary complexity.","principle":"Resilience mechanisms have reliability costs of their own.","rule":"Add a resilience mechanism only when its protected failure mode and operational behavior are understood.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service adds retry, fallback, cache, circuit breaker, and adaptive routing independently.","result":"The combined state space may become harder to reason about than the original failure mode."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Adding every available reliability pattern without modeling interactions.","reason":"Uncoordinated mechanisms can amplify or obscure failures."}],"problem":"Reliability architecture becomes overly complex.","failure":"Unexpected interaction failure.","cause":"Pattern accumulation without system-level analysis.","solution":"Model mechanisms as a combined control system and remove redundant or conflicting controls.","alternatives":["Simplified architecture","Centralized resilience policy","Scenario-driven mechanism selection"],"tradeoffs":"Fewer mechanisms may leave some failure modes less protected.","constraints":["Critical failure modes still require adequate controls.","Complexity decisions should be evidence-based."],"edge_cases":["Multiple retry layers","Overlapping fallback systems","Competing autoscalers"],"security":"Complex resilience paths can introduce overlooked authorization bypasses.","performance":"Each mechanism can add latency, memory, or network overhead.","testing":"Test combinations of resilience mechanisms under representative failures.","verification":"Verify that each mechanism has a distinct purpose and does not create unsafe interactions.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B42-004","D22-B44-005"],"prerequisites":["Reliability architecture","Failure modeling"],"relationships":["D22-B48-002","D22-B48-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability architecture principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B48-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Resilience Economics","concept":"Reliability mechanism interaction matrix","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"How can an interaction matrix help evaluate a complex resilience architecture?","answer":"It explicitly records how mechanisms such as retries, circuit breakers, queues, load shedding, autoscaling, caching, and failover affect one another during shared failure scenarios.","explanation":"Pairwise and multi-mechanism interactions can create emergent behavior that is invisible when each mechanism is reviewed separately.","principle":"Reliability mechanisms should be evaluated both individually and compositionally.","rule":"For critical combinations, document expected interactions and test failure scenarios that activate multiple controls simultaneously.","examples":[{"scenario":"Dependency latency triggers retries while autoscaling observes queue growth.","result":"The interaction matrix evaluates whether retry load causes scaling that further increases dependency pressure."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Testing each mechanism independently and assuming combined behavior is equivalent to the sum of individual behavior.","reason":"Feedback interactions can be nonlinear."}],"problem":"Combined resilience behavior is unvalidated.","failure":"Unexpected cascading control behavior.","cause":"No interaction analysis.","solution":"Maintain a mechanism interaction matrix linked to failure scenarios.","alternatives":["Scenario simulation","State-machine analysis","Fault-injection combinations"],"tradeoffs":"Interaction testing expands the scenario space.","constraints":["Prioritize high-impact interactions.","Avoid combinatorial explosion by grouping equivalent mechanisms."],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous dependency failures","Recovery plus deployment","Telemetry failure plus automation"],"security":"Interaction tests should include security controls that may share failure dependencies.","performance":"Combined tests can be resource intensive.","testing":"Exercise high-risk mechanism combinations under controlled failure conditions.","verification":"Verify predicted interaction behavior against observed evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B48-001"],"prerequisites":["Reliability mechanisms","Scenario testing"],"relationships":["D22-B40-001","D22-B48-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability verification principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.95,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B48-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Resilience Economics","concept":"Reliability debt","knowledge_type":"governance_concept","question":"What is reliability debt?","answer":"Reliability debt is accumulated unresolved risk, outdated resilience assumptions, missing tests, fragile recovery procedures, or operational complexity that increases the probability or impact of future failures.","explanation":"Reliability debt can arise when systems evolve faster than their failure models, recovery tests, dependency contracts, or capacity assumptions. It should be tracked as explicit engineering work rather than treated as invisible background risk.","principle":"Unresolved reliability risk accumulates operational cost over time.","rule":"Track material reliability gaps with ownership, evidence, affected scenarios, and remediation status.","examples":[{"scenario":"A recovery procedure has not been tested since a major architecture change.","result":"The gap is recorded as reliability debt until validated."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming an old recovery test remains valid after dependency topology changes.","reason":"The supporting assumptions may no longer hold."}],"problem":"Known reliability gaps remain invisible to planning.","failure":"Repeated or amplified incidents.","cause":"No reliability-debt tracking.","solution":"Maintain a versioned reliability risk register linked to architecture and tests.","alternatives":["Reliability backlog","Risk register","Architecture conformance tracking"],"tradeoffs":"Tracking debt requires ongoing maintenance.","constraints":["Debt should be evidence-based rather than speculative.","Materiality criteria should be explicit."],"edge_cases":["Third-party dependency change","Deprecated recovery path","Unowned service"],"security":"Security-relevant reliability debt should be tracked with appropriate access restrictions.","performance":"Reliability debt can include unresolved performance-induced instability.","testing":"Use reliability regression tests to close evidence gaps.","verification":"Require objective evidence before marking a reliability debt item resolved.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B45-005"],"prerequisites":["Risk management","Reliability testing"],"relationships":["D22-B40-005","D22-B48-004"],"provenance":["Derived reliability governance principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.95,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B48-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Resilience Economics","concept":"Evidence freshness for reliability claims","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"Why should reliability evidence have a freshness boundary?","answer":"A test or measurement can become stale when architecture, workload, dependencies, versions, configuration, or failure assumptions change.","explanation":"Reliability claims are conditional on the system state and assumptions under which they were established. Old evidence should not automatically be treated as current proof.","principle":"Reliability evidence is valid only within its documented scope and assumptions.","rule":"Associate important reliability evidence with system version, test scope, assumptions, and a revalidation trigger.","examples":[{"scenario":"A failover test predates a database architecture change.","result":"The old test remains historical evidence but does not prove current resilience."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treating any previous successful chaos test as permanent proof of resilience.","reason":"System assumptions may have changed."}],"problem":"Stale evidence creates false confidence.","failure":"Unvalidated current failure behavior.","cause":"No evidence freshness model.","solution":"Version reliability claims and trigger revalidation after material changes.","alternatives":["Continuous validation","Change-triggered tests","Evidence expiry policies"],"tradeoffs":"More frequent validation increases engineering effort.","constraints":["Freshness should reflect risk and change frequency.","Historical evidence remains useful for trend analysis."],"edge_cases":["Configuration-only changes","Dependency provider changes","Workload growth"],"security":"Evidence may expose architecture and should be access-controlled.","performance":"Continuous validation must be bounded to avoid becoming an operational burden.","testing":"Modify a tested assumption and verify that evidence is correctly marked stale.","verification":"Verify material changes invalidate or trigger review of affected reliability evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B35-003","D22-B40-005"],"prerequisites":["Evidence management","Reliability testing"],"relationships":["D22-B48-003","D22-B48-005"],"provenance":["Derived evidence-governance principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B48-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Resilience Economics","concept":"Reliability claim traceability","knowledge_type":"governance_model","question":"What should a traceable reliability claim contain?","answer":"A claim should identify the protected failure mode, architectural mechanism, assumptions, measurable acceptance criteria, supporting evidence, version scope, and known limitations.","explanation":"Traceability prevents broad statements such as 'the service is resilient' from being detached from the specific scenarios actually tested.","principle":"Reliability claims should be specific enough to be falsifiable and evidence-linked.","rule":"Represent each significant resilience claim as a bounded statement with provenance and verification status.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service claims tolerance of one zone failure.","result":"The claim specifies the zone model, workload conditions, capacity assumptions, test evidence, and recovery behavior."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Claiming 'high availability' without defining failure scope or evidence.","reason":"The claim is too broad to verify."}],"problem":"Reliability statements are vague and difficult to audit.","failure":"Architecture is assumed to tolerate failures it was never tested against.","cause":"Unbounded reliability claims.","solution":"Use scenario-specific, evidence-linked reliability claims.","alternatives":["Reliability assurance cases","Architecture decision records","Failure-mode evidence registry"],"tradeoffs":"Traceability adds documentation and evidence-management work.","constraints":["Claims must not exceed evidence.","Evidence must identify whether it was executed or merely designed."],"edge_cases":["Third-party guarantees","Inherited platform resilience","Changing workload"],"security":"Evidence and claims may expose sensitive infrastructure information.","performance":"Evidence collection should not create disproportionate production overhead.","testing":"Review claims against available tests and identify unsupported assumptions.","verification":"Reject or qualify claims whose evidence does not cover their stated scope.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B48-004"],"prerequisites":["Reliability assurance","Evidence management"],"relationships":["D22-B45-005","D22-B49-001"],"provenance":["Derived reliability assurance principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B49-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Assurance","concept":"Reliability assurance case","knowledge_type":"verification_framework","question":"What is a reliability assurance case?","answer":"It is a structured argument connecting reliability claims to assumptions, mechanisms, evidence, tests, and limitations for defined failure scenarios.","explanation":"An assurance case makes it possible to distinguish what the architecture is intended to tolerate from what has actually been demonstrated.","principle":"Reliability confidence should be supported by explicit claims and evidence rather than architecture labels.","rule":"For critical reliability properties, maintain a traceable chain from failure scenario to design control to evidence.","examples":[{"scenario":"The system claims recovery from a single-zone loss.","result":"The assurance case links placement, routing, capacity, recovery automation, testing, and limitations."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Using a list of resilience technologies as proof of resilience.","reason":"Technology presence does not demonstrate correct composition or tested behavior."}],"problem":"Reliability assurance is implicit and difficult to audit.","failure":"Unsupported resilience assumptions.","cause":"No structured evidence argument.","solution":"Create scenario-based assurance cases with explicit evidence status.","alternatives":["Reliability review matrix","Failure-mode evidence registry"],"tradeoffs":"Assurance cases require disciplined evidence management.","constraints":["Evidence must be independently attributable.","Unexecuted tests must not be represented as successful evidence."],"edge_cases":["Partial evidence","Inherited controls","Unknown external dependencies"],"security":"Assurance artifacts can reveal sensitive architecture.","performance":"Evidence collection should be proportionate to system criticality.","testing":"Audit each assurance claim against corresponding tests and observations.","verification":"Verify every critical claim has sufficient evidence or an explicit limitation.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B48-005"],"prerequisites":["Reliability claims","Evidence management"],"relationships":["D22-B49-002","D22-B49-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability assurance methodology"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B49-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Assurance","concept":"Reliability invariant","knowledge_type":"formal_reasoning","question":"What is a reliability invariant?","answer":"A reliability invariant is a property that must remain true across specified failure, degradation, recovery, or transition states.","explanation":"Examples include no unauthorized writes, bounded queue growth, at least one serving replica, unique ownership, or preservation of critical data integrity. Invariants provide stronger verification targets than vague resilience goals.","principle":"Critical safety properties should be expressed as conditions that must remain true across failure transitions.","rule":"Define invariants for high-impact reliability scenarios and test them before, during, and after failure.","examples":[{"scenario":"A resource must have at most one authoritative writer.","result":"The invariant is checked during failover and recovery."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Defining the invariant as 'the service should usually work'.","reason":"It is neither precise nor testable."}],"problem":"Reliability requirements are too vague to verify.","failure":"Unsafe state transition goes undetected.","cause":"No explicit invariants.","solution":"Translate critical reliability requirements into observable state properties.","alternatives":["Safety properties","State-machine assertions","Formal specifications"],"tradeoffs":"Precise invariants require deeper system modeling.","constraints":["Invariant scope must be explicit.","Some properties are probabilistic rather than absolute."],"edge_cases":["Network partition","Recovery race","Partial state","External dependency failure"],"security":"Security invariants should be included where reliability mechanisms affect authorization or isolation.","performance":"Invariant checking should be placed at appropriate boundaries to control overhead.","testing":"Inject failures and assert invariants throughout state transitions.","verification":"Verify invariants hold under all tested transitions within their stated scope.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B36-004"],"prerequisites":["State modeling","Reliability requirements"],"relationships":["D22-B49-003","D22-B50-001"],"provenance":["Derived formal-reasoning reliability principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B49-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Assurance","concept":"Safety versus liveness in reliability","knowledge_type":"formal_reasoning","question":"How do safety and liveness properties differ in reliability engineering?","answer":"Safety properties specify that something bad must never happen within the modeled scope, while liveness properties specify that something good eventually happens under stated assumptions.","explanation":"For example, preventing two simultaneous leaders is a safety property, while eventually restoring service after a bounded failure can be a liveness property. Recovery designs often need both.","principle":"Reliable systems require both prevention of unsafe states and progress toward acceptable states.","rule":"Identify safety and liveness properties separately and state the assumptions under which liveness is expected.","examples":[{"scenario":"Failover system must never have two authoritative writers and should eventually establish one valid owner.","result":"The first is safety; the second is liveness."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Calling eventual recovery proof without defining conditions under which recovery is possible.","reason":"Liveness depends on assumptions such as dependency availability and sufficient capacity."}],"problem":"Recovery goals and safety constraints are mixed together.","failure":"System either remains safe but never recovers or recovers through unsafe transitions.","cause":"Properties not separated.","solution":"Specify safety invariants and liveness expectations independently.","alternatives":["Temporal logic specifications","State-machine verification","Model checking"],"tradeoffs":"Formal reasoning can require substantial modeling effort.","constraints":["Liveness claims need environmental assumptions.","Safety claims must define scope and exceptions."],"edge_cases":["Permanent dependency failure","Network partition","Repeated transient failures"],"security":"Security invariants often belong to safety properties.","performance":"Liveness targets can impose resource and scheduling requirements.","testing":"Construct scenarios that challenge both safety and progress properties.","verification":"Verify safety throughout transitions and measure liveness under documented assumptions.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B49-002"],"prerequisites":["Formal methods","Reliability properties"],"relationships":["D22-B49-004","D22-B50-002"],"provenance":["Derived formal-methods reliability principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B49-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Assurance","concept":"Model checking for reliability state machines","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"How can model checking help validate reliability state machines?","answer":"It can systematically explore modeled state transitions to determine whether specified properties hold across combinations of events and failures within the model.","explanation":"Model checking is useful for finite or abstracted state spaces involving circuit states, ownership transitions, failover, retries, and recovery. It does not automatically prove that the model accurately represents the real system.","principle":"Formal verification can validate modeled transition properties but cannot compensate for an inaccurate model.","rule":"Use model checking for critical state machines while separately validating model assumptions against implementation.","examples":[{"scenario":"Failover controller has primary, secondary, and uncertain states.","result":"A model can check whether a transition sequence permits simultaneous ownership."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treating model-checker success as proof of the deployed implementation without model-to-code validation.","reason":"The implementation may differ from the model."}],"problem":"Complex state transitions contain untested combinations.","failure":"Rare transition sequence violates reliability invariant.","cause":"State-space reasoning limited to manual tests.","solution":"Model critical transitions and formally check defined properties, then validate implementation correspondence.","alternatives":["Property-based testing","State-machine testing","Formal specification"],"tradeoffs":"Modeling and state-space exploration can become complex as system state grows.","constraints":["Model must have explicit abstraction boundaries.","Implementation correspondence must be reviewed."],"edge_cases":["State explosion","External dependencies","Unbounded queues"],"security":"Security state transitions can also be modeled as safety properties.","performance":"Formal analysis is typically performed offline rather than in request paths.","testing":"Compare model-generated transition scenarios with implementation tests.","verification":"Record model scope, assumptions, properties checked, and implementation correspondence evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B49-003"],"prerequisites":["Formal verification","State machines"],"relationships":["D22-B49-005","D22-B50-003"],"provenance":["Derived formal verification principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B49-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Assurance","concept":"Property-based reliability testing","knowledge_type":"testing_method","question":"How can property-based testing complement scenario-specific reliability tests?","answer":"It generates many structured input and state combinations to test general reliability properties rather than only a fixed set of manually selected examples.","explanation":"Reliability properties such as idempotency, bounded retries, state-machine invariants, and recovery convergence can often be expressed as properties that should hold across many generated cases.","principle":"General reliability properties can expose edge cases that fixed examples miss.","rule":"Use property-based testing where reliability behavior can be expressed as a stable invariant over generated states or inputs.","examples":[{"scenario":"Recovery operations should be idempotent.","result":"Generated sequences can repeat operations at different interruption points and assert equivalent final state."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Generating random data without defining a property to validate.","reason":"Randomness alone does not establish useful reliability evidence."}],"problem":"Rare combinations of failures remain untested.","failure":"Unexpected state transition or recovery defect.","cause":"Limited example-based coverage.","solution":"Define invariants and generate structured failure and operation sequences.","alternatives":["Model-based testing","Fuzzing","Stateful property testing"],"tradeoffs":"Generated cases can be difficult to shrink, diagnose, or reproduce without deterministic seeds and state capture.","constraints":["Properties must be meaningful.","External side effects should be isolated or simulated safely."],"edge_cases":["Concurrent operations","Partial failures","Timeout ambiguity"],"security":"Generated inputs should not bypass authentication or authorization test boundaries.","performance":"Large generated suites can be expensive and require controlled execution.","testing":"Generate failure sequences and assert reliability invariants.","verification":"Record failing seeds and minimized cases for reproducibility.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B49-002"],"prerequisites":["Property-based testing","Reliability invariants"],"relationships":["D22-B40-001","D22-B49-004"],"provenance":["Derived software-testing and reliability principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B50-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Final Assurance","concept":"Reliability scenario matrix","knowledge_type":"verification_framework","question":"What should a comprehensive reliability scenario matrix contain?","answer":"It should map important failure modes to affected components, expected propagation, containment controls, degradation behavior, recovery actions, observability, verification criteria, and known limitations.","explanation":"A scenario matrix exposes coverage gaps across the reliability lifecycle and prevents individual mechanisms from being mistaken for complete scenario coverage.","principle":"Reliability completeness is best evaluated against explicit failure scenarios.","rule":"Maintain a scenario-to-control-to-evidence mapping for critical reliability conditions.","examples":[{"scenario":"Dependency outage during deployment.","result":"Matrix identifies timeout behavior, deployment safety, fallback, recovery, telemetry, and verification requirements."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Listing resilience technologies without mapping them to failure scenarios.","reason":"Technology presence does not prove scenario coverage."}],"problem":"Reliability coverage is fragmented across teams and mechanisms.","failure":"Important failure combinations remain untested.","cause":"No scenario-level coverage model.","solution":"Maintain a versioned reliability scenario matrix.","alternatives":["FMEA","Assurance case","Failure-mode catalog"],"tradeoffs":"Maintaining the matrix requires ongoing architecture synchronization.","constraints":["Scenarios should be prioritized by impact.","Evidence status must distinguish planned from executed tests."],"edge_cases":["Combined failures","Changing dependencies","Disaster plus operational error"],"security":"Security-induced failure scenarios should be included where relevant without exposing sensitive implementation details.","performance":"Overload scenarios must be represented alongside functional failures.","testing":"Use the matrix to generate targeted fault-injection and recovery tests.","verification":"Verify every critical scenario has defined controls and evidence or an explicit gap.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B49-001","D22-B49-005"],"prerequisites":["Failure taxonomy","Reliability assurance"],"relationships":["D22-B50-002","D22-B50-005"],"provenance":["Derived reliability assurance practice"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B50-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Final Assurance","concept":"Reliability coverage gap classification","knowledge_type":"governance_rule","question":"How should reliability coverage gaps be classified?","answer":"Gaps should distinguish missing design controls, missing tests, stale evidence, unmodeled failure modes, insufficient observability, and unresolved operational limitations.","explanation":"Different gaps require different remediation. A missing test is not equivalent to a missing containment mechanism, and both should not be hidden under one generic risk label.","principle":"Reliability gaps should identify the missing assurance layer.","rule":"Classify each material gap by cause and required remediation type.","examples":[{"scenario":"A failover design exists but has never been exercised.","result":"The gap is evidence/testing-related rather than necessarily a missing design control."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Marking all reliability gaps simply as 'untested'.","reason":"Some gaps represent missing architecture or observability controls."}],"problem":"Reliability remediation is poorly targeted.","failure":"Teams fix tests while underlying design deficiencies remain.","cause":"Unclassified reliability gaps.","solution":"Use structured gap categories tied to remediation owners.","alternatives":["Risk register","Assurance-case gaps","Reliability debt taxonomy"],"tradeoffs":"More detailed classification requires governance discipline.","constraints":["Classification must remain evidence-based.","Unknown causes should remain explicitly unknown."],"edge_cases":["Third-party limitation","Conflicting evidence","Partial implementation"],"security":"Security-related gaps require appropriate handling and access control.","performance":"Performance-induced reliability gaps should remain distinguishable from pure functional failures.","testing":"Audit existing scenarios and classify each uncovered or weakly evidenced area.","verification":"Verify each gap has an explicit remediation or accepted limitation.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B48-003","D22-B50-001"],"prerequisites":["Reliability governance","Coverage analysis"],"relationships":["D22-B48-004","D22-B50-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability governance principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B50-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Final Assurance","concept":"Implementation-to-model correspondence","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"Why must a reliability model be checked against the actual implementation?","answer":"A formally or conceptually sound reliability model can provide misleading assurance if implementation behavior, configuration, dependencies, or state transitions differ from the model.","explanation":"Model abstraction is useful but creates a correspondence obligation. The implemented system must preserve the assumptions and invariants represented by the model.","principle":"A verified model is only as useful as its correspondence to the system it represents.","rule":"Identify implementation points that realize each critical model state, transition, and invariant.","examples":[{"scenario":"Model assumes only one component can acquire ownership.","result":"Implementation must enforce the same property through actual fencing, quorum, or equivalent control."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treating a model diagram as evidence that implementation enforces its transitions.","reason":"The diagram may not reflect actual code or configuration."}],"problem":"Formal reasoning becomes disconnected from implementation.","failure":"Production behavior violates modeled assumptions.","cause":"No model-to-implementation traceability.","solution":"Maintain mappings from critical model properties to implementation mechanisms and tests.","alternatives":["Code-level assertions","Model-based testing","Architecture conformance checks"],"tradeoffs":"Traceability adds maintenance overhead as implementation changes.","constraints":["Mappings must be updated after material changes.","Abstractions must document omitted behavior."],"edge_cases":["Dynamic configuration","External providers","Generated code"],"security":"Security invariants need implementation-level evidence as well.","performance":"Runtime assertions should be used selectively where overhead is acceptable.","testing":"Change implementation behavior and verify correspondence tests detect divergence.","verification":"Require evidence that critical modeled properties are implemented and exercised.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B49-004"],"prerequisites":["Formal modeling","Implementation analysis"],"relationships":["D22-B48-004","D22-B49-004"],"provenance":["Derived formal assurance principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B50-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Final Assurance","concept":"Reliability evidence chain","knowledge_type":"data_model","question":"What is a reliability evidence chain?","answer":"It is a traceable sequence connecting a failure scenario and requirement to an architectural control, implementation, test, observed result, and current verification status.","explanation":"An evidence chain prevents unsupported conclusions and makes reliability knowledge reusable for audits, debugging, regression analysis, and automated reasoning.","principle":"Reliability knowledge should preserve the relationship between claims, mechanisms, and evidence.","rule":"Store evidence provenance and status at the smallest meaningful reliability claim rather than attaching one generic verification label to an entire system.","examples":[{"scenario":"Zone-failure tolerance is claimed.","result":"The chain links the failure model, placement control, routing behavior, fault-injection test, observed result, and version scope."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Marking the whole service 'verified' because one failover test passed.","reason":"One test cannot establish every reliability property."}],"problem":"Reliability evidence becomes broad, ambiguous, or impossible to audit.","failure":"Unsupported reliability assumptions enter engineering decisions.","cause":"Evidence not linked to individual claims.","solution":"Maintain granular evidence chains with explicit scope and status.","alternatives":["Assurance cases","Evidence registries","Traceability matrices"],"tradeoffs":"Granular evidence management requires more metadata.","constraints":["Unexecuted tests must remain clearly unexecuted.","Evidence must be version-scoped."],"edge_cases":["Multiple implementations","Inherited controls","Conflicting test results"],"security":"Evidence repositories require access control because architecture and incident information can be sensitive.","performance":"Evidence indexing should not affect production request paths.","testing":"Audit representative reliability claims from requirement through observed result.","verification":"Verify that each high-impact claim has a complete and current evidence chain.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B48-005","D22-B50-003"],"prerequisites":["Provenance","Reliability verification"],"relationships":["D22-B50-001","D22-B50-005"],"provenance":["Derived evidence-driven reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B50-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Final Assurance","concept":"Reliability phase lock criteria","knowledge_type":"dataset_completion_rule","question":"What evidence should be required before locking a reliability-engineering knowledge phase?","answer":"The phase should have complete scope coverage, deduplicated records, explicit failure and recovery semantics, edge cases, security and performance considerations, testing and verification guidance, provenance, version boundaries, contradiction handling where applicable, and no material uncovered topic within its defined scope.","explanation":"Dataset completion should be determined by knowledge coverage and quality rather than record count. A locked phase should also distinguish design guidance from executed verification and preserve traceable relationships.","principle":"A reliability dataset is complete when its defined knowledge scope is adequately represented with high-quality evidence, not when an arbitrary record target is reached.","rule":"Lock only after semantic review, deduplication, schema validation, coverage analysis, and evidence-status audit identify no material unresolved gap within the declared scope.","examples":[{"scenario":"Two records express materially different failure modes with different remediation behavior.","result":"Both remain because they add distinct knowledge."},{"scenario":"Two records differ only by wording.","result":"One is removed during deduplication."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Generating additional records solely to reach a numerical target.","reason":"Record count does not establish knowledge completeness."},{"pattern":"Marking conceptual guidance as executed verification.","reason":"Design knowledge and empirical evidence are different evidence classes."}],"problem":"Reliability knowledge becomes padded, duplicated, or falsely verified.","failure":"Autonomous engineering system retrieves misleading or low-value reliability knowledge.","cause":"Count-driven generation or weak evidence discipline.","solution":"Use coverage-driven generation, semantic deduplication, explicit provenance, versioning, contradiction handling, and validation-status separation.","alternatives":["Ontology-based coverage audit","Evidence graph","Scenario coverage matrix"],"tradeoffs":"A quality-first dataset may contain fewer records than a count-driven dataset while providing substantially greater reasoning value.","constraints":["No fabricated execution results.","No fabricated benchmarks or provenance.","Stable record IDs must be preserved.","English-only machine-readable output."],"edge_cases":["Conflicting authoritative guidance","Technology-version differences","Evidence becoming stale","Overlapping neighboring datasets"],"security":"Reliability records should avoid embedding secrets, credentials, or unnecessary sensitive infrastructure details.","performance":"Dataset retrieval should favor high-information records and relationship-aware indexing.","testing":"Run schema, duplicate, relationship, and coverage checks before lock.","verification":"Lock only when the defined D22 scope has no material unrepresented reliability knowledge category and every record has an explicit validation state.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B48-004","D22-B50-001","D22-B50-004"],"prerequisites":["Dataset validation","Deduplication","Coverage analysis"],"relationships":["D22-B50-001","D22-B50-002","D22-B50-004"],"provenance":["User-defined quality-first dataset generation requirements plus derived dataset-engineering principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B51-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"State Reconstruction","concept":"State reconstruction after catastrophic loss","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"How should a system reconstruct operational state after losing volatile or locally persisted state?","answer":"Recovery should reconstruct state from authoritative durable sources, validated snapshots, event history, or deterministic recomputation while explicitly identifying information that cannot be reconstructed.","explanation":"Restarting processes does not guarantee restoration of the state required for correct operation. Recovery must distinguish authoritative state from caches, derived state, temporary coordination state, and uncertain state.","principle":"Recovering computation is insufficient unless required authoritative state is also recoverable.","rule":"For every critical state category, define its authoritative source, reconstruction method, integrity check, and unrecoverable-data behavior.","examples":[{"scenario":"A worker loses its local cache and coordination state.","result":"The worker rebuilds derived state from authoritative storage instead of treating the cache as authoritative."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treating recovered process memory or local cache contents as authoritative after storage loss.","reason":"The state may be incomplete or stale."}],"problem":"System restarts but lacks trustworthy operational state.","failure":"Incorrect behavior after recovery.","cause":"Undefined state reconstruction model.","solution":"Classify state by authority and define deterministic reconstruction procedures.","alternatives":["Snapshot restore","Event replay","State recomputation"],"tradeoffs":"Reconstruction can increase recovery time and consume resources.","constraints":["Authoritative sources must themselves satisfy durability requirements.","Derived state must be safely rebuildable."],"edge_cases":["Corrupt snapshot","Missing events","Schema evolution","Partial restore"],"security":"Reconstructed state must preserve access-control and tenant-isolation invariants.","performance":"Large state reconstruction may require staged or incremental recovery.","testing":"Delete derived state and validate deterministic reconstruction from authoritative data.","verification":"Compare reconstructed state against authoritative invariants before accepting traffic.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B47-003","D22-B49-002"],"prerequisites":["State classification","Durable storage"],"relationships":["D22-B51-002","D22-B52-001"],"provenance":["Derived state-recovery engineering principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B51-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"State Reconstruction","concept":"Authoritative versus derived state","knowledge_type":"data_model","question":"Why must authoritative and derived state be distinguished during reliability recovery?","answer":"Authoritative state is the source of truth from which correctness is determined, while derived state can normally be recreated from authoritative inputs.","explanation":"Confusing the two can cause recovery to preserve stale caches, indexes, projections, or temporary state as if they were canonical. Explicit classification makes recovery and corruption handling safer.","principle":"Recovery decisions must follow state authority rather than storage convenience.","rule":"Every critical persisted state object should identify whether it is authoritative, derived, cached, temporary, or coordination state.","examples":[{"scenario":"A search index can be rebuilt from a primary database.","result":"The index is treated as derived state during disaster recovery."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Restoring a stale derived index and treating it as the canonical record.","reason":"The index does not define authoritative truth."}],"problem":"Recovery uses the wrong source of truth.","failure":"Stale or inconsistent state becomes authoritative.","cause":"Unclear state classification.","solution":"Annotate state authority and recovery precedence.","alternatives":["Source-of-truth registry","Data lineage graph","Schema metadata"],"tradeoffs":"Explicit classification adds metadata and governance work.","constraints":["Authority must be unambiguous for critical state.","Multiple-source systems require reconciliation rules."],"edge_cases":["Bidirectional replication","Eventually consistent projections","Imported external state"],"security":"Authority metadata must not allow unauthorized elevation of derived data.","performance":"Rebuilding derived state may be expensive and should be capacity planned.","testing":"Corrupt or remove derived state and verify reconstruction from the authoritative source.","verification":"Verify recovery never promotes derived state without explicit reconciliation evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B51-001"],"prerequisites":["Data architecture","Recovery"],"relationships":["D22-B51-003","D22-B52-002"],"provenance":["Derived data-reliability principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B51-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"State Reconstruction","concept":"Recovery ordering dependency","knowledge_type":"recovery_rule","question":"Why does recovery order matter in dependency-heavy systems?","answer":"Some components require foundational state, identity, configuration, storage, or coordination services before they can safely recover and serve traffic.","explanation":"Starting all components simultaneously can create races, repeated failures, dependency storms, or consumption of capacity before prerequisites are ready.","principle":"Recovery should follow dependency-aware ordering rather than arbitrary startup order.","rule":"Define recovery prerequisites and dependency stages for critical components.","examples":[{"scenario":"Application services require database schema and configuration state before accepting traffic.","result":"Those prerequisites are validated before application admission opens."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Opening all application traffic immediately after process startup.","reason":"Dependencies may not yet be consistent or available."}],"problem":"Recovery starts consumers before prerequisites are ready.","failure":"Repeated startup failure and recovery amplification.","cause":"No dependency-aware recovery order.","solution":"Use staged recovery with prerequisite checks and bounded admission.","alternatives":["Dependency graph ordering","Readiness gates","Recovery state machine"],"tradeoffs":"Sequential recovery can increase restoration time.","constraints":["Stages should avoid unnecessary serialization.","Circular dependencies require explicit recovery strategy."],"edge_cases":["Circular dependencies","Partial dependency availability","Configuration service failure"],"security":"Identity and policy dependencies should be restored before protected operations become available.","performance":"Parallelize independent recovery stages while preserving dependency constraints.","testing":"Randomize or intentionally violate startup order and verify safe behavior.","verification":"Verify each stage opens only after required dependencies satisfy defined conditions.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B44-001","D22-B51-002"],"prerequisites":["Dependency graph","Recovery orchestration"],"relationships":["D22-B51-004","D22-B53-001"],"provenance":["Derived recovery-ordering principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B51-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"State Reconstruction","concept":"Recovery dependency deadlock","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"What is a recovery dependency deadlock?","answer":"It occurs when components require one another to become ready before either can complete recovery, causing the recovery process to make no progress.","explanation":"Circular dependencies can be harmless during normal operation but become blocking during restart or disaster recovery when all components begin from an unavailable state.","principle":"Recovery dependency graphs must be analyzed for cycles and explicit bootstrap paths.","rule":"Identify circular recovery dependencies and define at least one independent bootstrap mechanism for each critical cycle.","examples":[{"scenario":"Service A needs B for startup while B needs A for readiness.","result":"Recovery requires a bootstrap state or staged mode that breaks the cycle."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming normal runtime dependency order will automatically work during full-system recovery.","reason":"Normal-state assumptions may be circular during bootstrap."}],"problem":"Recovery cannot reach an initial healthy state.","failure":"Persistent recovery deadlock.","cause":"Circular bootstrap dependency.","solution":"Introduce bootstrap credentials, degraded initialization, independent control-plane state, or staged activation as appropriate.","alternatives":["Out-of-band recovery","Static bootstrap configuration","Manual recovery gate"],"tradeoffs":"Bootstrap paths create additional security and maintenance requirements.","constraints":["Bootstrap mechanisms must be tightly scoped.","Bootstrap state must not silently become permanent authority."],"edge_cases":["Control-plane/data-plane cycle","Identity dependency cycle","Configuration dependency cycle"],"security":"Bootstrap mechanisms require strong authentication and constrained privilege.","performance":"Bootstrap paths may reduce initialization throughput but should remain bounded.","testing":"Recover the full dependency cycle from a cold state and verify progress.","verification":"Prove or test that every critical dependency cycle has a valid bootstrap path.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B51-003"],"prerequisites":["Dependency graphs","State machines"],"relationships":["D22-B51-005","D22-B53-002"],"provenance":["Derived recovery-dependency principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B51-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"State Reconstruction","concept":"Recovery readiness versus service readiness","knowledge_type":"architecture_rule","question":"Why should recovery readiness be distinguished from ordinary service readiness?","answer":"A recovered component may be able to accept requests while its caches, replicas, indexes, synchronization state, or background recovery work remain incomplete.","explanation":"Serving traffic too early can overload recovering dependencies or expose incomplete state. Readiness should reflect whether the component can safely perform the intended workload, not merely whether its process is running.","principle":"A process can be alive without being operationally ready for full workload.","rule":"Define readiness levels corresponding to actual recovery state and permitted workload.","examples":[{"scenario":"A database replica has restarted but has not caught up sufficiently for failover.","result":"It is not considered failover-ready merely because it accepts connections."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Marking a recovering replica ready immediately after process startup.","reason":"Required state may not yet be synchronized."}],"problem":"Traffic is admitted before recovery is complete.","failure":"Incorrect responses, overload, or secondary failure.","cause":"Readiness based only on process availability.","solution":"Use state-aware readiness criteria.","alternatives":["Warm-up state","Recovery readiness levels","Traffic ramping"],"tradeoffs":"Conservative readiness delays traffic restoration.","constraints":["Readiness conditions must be observable.","Conditions should correspond to actual workload requirements."],"edge_cases":["Large cache warmup","Replica lag","Background migration"],"security":"Protected workloads should not become available before policy and identity state is ready.","performance":"Gradual traffic ramp-up can reduce recovery spikes.","testing":"Admit traffic at different recovery stages and verify safe behavior.","verification":"Verify readiness reflects functional recovery requirements rather than process liveness alone.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B51-003"],"prerequisites":["Health models","Recovery states"],"relationships":["D22-B43-005","D22-B52-005"],"provenance":["Derived recovery-readiness principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B52-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Backup and Restore","concept":"Backup validity versus backup existence","knowledge_type":"reliability_rule","question":"Why does the existence of backup files not prove recoverability?","answer":"A backup can exist while being incomplete, corrupted, inaccessible, incompatible with current software, or missing required related data.","explanation":"Recoverability is an outcome property that requires restoration and validation, not merely evidence that backup creation completed.","principle":"Backup success and recovery success are different reliability facts.","rule":"Treat restore validation as separate evidence from backup creation status.","examples":[{"scenario":"Database backup job reports success.","result":"The backup is still not considered recovery-proven until restoration and integrity validation are performed."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declaring disaster recovery verified because scheduled backups exist.","reason":"Backup existence does not demonstrate usable restoration."}],"problem":"Organizations confuse backup completion with recovery capability.","failure":"Disaster recovery fails when restoration is attempted.","cause":"No restore validation.","solution":"Perform controlled restoration tests and validate resulting state.","alternatives":["Automated restore verification","Checksum validation","Recovery drills"],"tradeoffs":"Restore testing consumes storage, compute, and operational effort.","constraints":["Tests must represent real recovery dependencies.","Sensitive backup data requires protection."],"edge_cases":["Encrypted backups","Cross-version restore","Partial backup","Missing external dependencies"],"security":"Restored data must retain access controls and encryption requirements.","performance":"Large restore tests can require dedicated capacity.","testing":"Restore representative backups into isolated environments and validate integrity.","verification":"Record restore outcome, scope, software version, dependencies, and validation evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B51-001"],"prerequisites":["Backup systems","Data integrity"],"relationships":["D22-B52-002","D22-B52-004"],"provenance":["Derived backup-and-recovery principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B52-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Backup and Restore","concept":"Restore dependency closure","knowledge_type":"recovery_model","question":"What is restore dependency closure?","answer":"It is the complete set of data, software, configuration, credentials, keys, network access, schemas, and external dependencies required to restore a system to an operational state.","explanation":"A backup may contain application data but recovery can still fail because required keys, configuration, schema versions, or external services are unavailable.","principle":"A recovery artifact is useful only when its operational dependency closure is recoverable.","rule":"Identify and test every critical dependency required to transform backup data into a functioning system.","examples":[{"scenario":"Encrypted database backup requires a key-management service.","result":"Recovery planning must include access to the required key material or documented recovery mechanism."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Testing only database restoration while ignoring application configuration and encryption keys.","reason":"The database alone cannot establish operational recovery."}],"problem":"Restore succeeds technically but system cannot operate.","failure":"Incomplete disaster recovery.","cause":"Dependency closure omitted.","solution":"Map recovery dependencies and test end-to-end restoration.","alternatives":["Recovery bill of materials","Dependency manifest","Infrastructure reconstruction"],"tradeoffs":"Broader testing increases scope and maintenance cost.","constraints":["Sensitive dependencies require secure handling.","Third-party recovery assumptions must be documented."],"edge_cases":["Identity provider unavailable","DNS unavailable","Certificate expiration","External API dependency"],"security":"Recovery credentials and keys must follow least privilege and secure custody.","performance":"Dependency reconstruction can dominate recovery time.","testing":"Perform end-to-end restore from a clean environment rather than component-only restoration.","verification":"Verify that the restored system can satisfy its defined operational readiness criteria.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B52-001","D22-B44-001"],"prerequisites":["Disaster recovery","Dependency mapping"],"relationships":["D22-B52-003","D22-B53-001"],"provenance":["Derived disaster-recovery dependency principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B52-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Backup and Restore","concept":"Backup immutability and deletion resistance","knowledge_type":"security_reliability_control","question":"Why can backup deletion resistance improve recovery reliability?","answer":"If an attacker or operational error can modify or delete every recovery copy, a localized incident can become a permanent data-recovery failure.","explanation":"Backup resilience therefore includes protection against unauthorized modification and coordinated loss, not only storage durability.","principle":"Recovery copies must survive the failure modes they are intended to recover from.","rule":"For critical data, maintain appropriately isolated recovery copies with access controls and protection against unauthorized deletion or alteration.","examples":[{"scenario":"Production credentials are compromised.","result":"Recovery copies remain protected by separate controls and cannot be deleted using the compromised production identity."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Giving production workloads unrestricted delete access to all backup copies.","reason":"A production compromise can destroy the recovery path."}],"problem":"Primary and backup data share the same failure domain or authority.","failure":"Loss of recovery capability.","cause":"Insufficient backup isolation.","solution":"Separate backup authority and protect critical recovery artifacts from common compromise paths.","alternatives":["Offline copies","Immutable storage","Cross-account isolation"],"tradeoffs":"Isolation can increase operational complexity and storage cost.","constraints":["Recovery access must remain possible during emergencies.","Retention requirements must be defined."],"edge_cases":["Ransomware","Credential compromise","Administrative error","Retention-policy corruption"],"security":"Backup security must be treated as part of system resilience.","performance":"Immutable or isolated storage can affect backup and restore workflows.","testing":"Attempt authorized recovery and verify protection controls do not prevent legitimate restoration.","verification":"Verify recovery copies remain available after simulated primary compromise scenarios.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B52-001"],"prerequisites":["Backup architecture","Access control"],"relationships":["D22-B52-004","D22-B54-003"],"provenance":["Derived backup security and resilience principle"],"evidence":"security_reliability_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B52-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Backup and Restore","concept":"Recovery point validation","knowledge_type":"data_integrity_rule","question":"Why should the actual recovered data point be measured instead of assuming the configured backup interval equals achieved recovery point?","answer":"Failures, replication lag, backup errors, retention gaps, and asynchronous processing can make the actual recoverable state older than the nominal schedule suggests.","explanation":"Recovery point is an observed property of what can actually be restored, not simply the configured frequency of backup jobs.","principle":"Recovery-point claims must be based on recoverable data evidence.","rule":"Measure the age and completeness of the latest valid recovery point under representative failure conditions.","examples":[{"scenario":"Backups run every hour but the latest valid backup is several hours old due to failures.","result":"The achieved recovery point reflects the older valid backup, not the nominal schedule."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Claiming one-hour recovery point solely because backups are configured hourly.","reason":"Configuration does not establish successful recoverability."}],"problem":"Recovery objectives are based on configuration rather than evidence.","failure":"Unexpected data loss during disaster recovery.","cause":"No achieved recovery-point measurement.","solution":"Track valid backup timestamps, integrity, scope, and restore evidence.","alternatives":["Continuous replication","Journaled recovery","Point-in-time restore"],"tradeoffs":"Tighter recovery points require additional infrastructure and operational cost.","constraints":["Recovery-point measurement must define what data is included.","External side effects may not be captured by data backups."],"edge_cases":["Backup corruption","Replication lag","Partial dataset backup","Clock differences"],"security":"Recovery-point records may expose data-retention details.","performance":"More frequent recovery points increase I/O and storage load.","testing":"Simulate backup failures and measure the latest actually restorable point.","verification":"Verify claimed recovery point against restore evidence rather than schedule configuration.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B52-001"],"prerequisites":["Recovery objectives","Backup validation"],"relationships":["D22-B52-005","D22-B53-003"],"provenance":["Derived recovery-point reliability principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B52-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Backup and Restore","concept":"Restore-time variability","knowledge_type":"recovery_model","question":"Why should recovery time be modeled as a distribution or bounded range rather than one optimistic duration?","answer":"Restore duration depends on dataset size, infrastructure capacity, dependency readiness, validation work, parallelism, throttling, and failure conditions.","explanation":"A single nominal restore time can hide tail behavior and dependency-driven delays. Recovery planning should distinguish target, observed, worst tested, and unverified durations.","principle":"Recovery-time claims must account for variability and scenario conditions.","rule":"Record recovery duration with workload size, environment, dependencies, parallelism, and validation scope.","examples":[{"scenario":"A small test restore completes quickly but production restoration requires much larger data and dependency reconstruction.","result":"The small test cannot be treated as direct proof of production recovery duration."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Using the fastest historical restore as the guaranteed recovery time.","reason":"It ignores workload and environmental variance."}],"problem":"Recovery objectives are set using unrepresentative measurements.","failure":"Recovery exceeds operational expectations.","cause":"No scenario-qualified recovery-time evidence.","solution":"Measure restoration under representative and stressed conditions.","alternatives":["Recovery-time percentiles","Capacity models","Full-scale recovery drills"],"tradeoffs":"Representative recovery tests are expensive.","constraints":["Measurements must retain scenario context.","Untested extreme conditions remain explicit limitations."],"edge_cases":["Large dataset growth","Reduced disaster capacity","Concurrent restoration"],"security":"Recovery tests must protect sensitive data.","performance":"Recovery throughput should be measured at realistic capacity limits.","testing":"Repeat restore tests across representative data sizes and resource conditions.","verification":"Verify recovery-time claims are scoped to tested conditions.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B52-001","D22-B52-002"],"prerequisites":["Recovery measurement","Capacity planning"],"relationships":["D22-B53-003","D22-B55-001"],"provenance":["Derived disaster-recovery measurement principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B53-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Disaster Recovery","concept":"Recovery dependency graph","knowledge_type":"architecture_model","question":"How should a disaster recovery system represent recovery dependencies?","answer":"Represent recovery components as a directed graph whose edges express prerequisites, data dependencies, control dependencies, or required services, then use the graph to plan and validate recovery order.","explanation":"A dependency graph exposes critical-path bottlenecks, circular dependencies, shared failure domains, and missing bootstrap paths.","principle":"Disaster recovery should be modeled as a dependency-constrained process rather than a collection of independent restore tasks.","rule":"Maintain a versioned recovery dependency graph for critical systems and validate it against actual architecture.","examples":[{"scenario":"Application recovery requires identity, database, configuration, and networking services.","result":"The recovery graph represents these prerequisites and their readiness conditions."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Maintaining a flat list of recovery tasks with no dependency relationships.","reason":"Ordering and hidden bottlenecks remain unclear."}],"problem":"Recovery procedures fail because prerequisite relationships are implicit.","failure":"Deadlock, premature admission, or repeated recovery attempts.","cause":"No recovery dependency model.","solution":"Build and validate a dependency graph linked to recovery procedures.","alternatives":["Recovery DAG","Runbook dependency map","State-machine orchestration"],"tradeoffs":"Graph maintenance becomes an architectural governance responsibility.","constraints":["Graph must represent relevant operational dependencies, not only code imports.","Dynamic dependencies require runtime discovery or explicit modeling."],"edge_cases":["Cycles","Third-party dependencies","Conditional dependencies","Shared recovery resources"],"security":"Recovery dependencies include security services and credentials that require protected modeling.","performance":"Parallelize independent nodes while preserving dependency constraints.","testing":"Remove individual prerequisite nodes and verify dependent recovery behavior.","verification":"Compare graph predictions with actual recovery execution and update discrepancies.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B51-003","D22-B52-002"],"prerequisites":["Dependency mapping","Disaster recovery"],"relationships":["D22-B53-002","D22-B53-003"],"provenance":["Derived disaster-recovery architecture principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B53-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Disaster Recovery","concept":"Recovery critical path","knowledge_type":"capacity_model","question":"Why should disaster recovery identify its critical path?","answer":"The critical path contains dependent recovery activities that determine the minimum achievable restoration time under stated assumptions.","explanation":"Not every recovery task contributes equally to restoration time. Identifying the critical path allows engineering effort to focus on bottlenecks rather than optimizing already-parallel tasks.","principle":"Recovery-time improvement should target dependency-constrained bottlenecks.","rule":"Measure recovery stages and identify the dependency path that limits restoration objectives.","examples":[{"scenario":"Database restore dominates total recovery while application deployment is parallelizable.","result":"Improving application startup does not materially improve total recovery time."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Optimizing the fastest recovery step because it is easy to improve.","reason":"It may not affect the total recovery critical path."}],"problem":"Recovery engineering effort is spent away from actual bottlenecks.","failure":"Recovery objective remains unmet despite optimization work.","cause":"No critical-path analysis.","solution":"Map stage durations and dependencies to identify limiting steps.","alternatives":["PERT-style recovery modeling","Dependency DAG timing","Recovery simulation"],"tradeoffs":"Accurate critical-path modeling requires representative measurements.","constraints":["Critical path can change under different failure scenarios.","Parallelism assumptions must be validated."],"edge_cases":["Resource contention","Multiple equivalent paths","Dependency outage during recovery"],"security":"Recovery authorization gates can become part of the critical path.","performance":"Capacity increases at the critical path can materially reduce recovery duration.","testing":"Measure recovery under constrained resources and compare critical-path predictions.","verification":"Verify measured recovery behavior matches the modeled critical path within stated assumptions.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B53-001","D22-B52-005"],"prerequisites":["Recovery measurement","Dependency graphs"],"relationships":["D22-B53-003","D22-B55-001"],"provenance":["Derived recovery optimization principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B53-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Disaster Recovery","concept":"RTO/RPO scenario qualification","knowledge_type":"reliability_requirement","question":"Why must recovery objectives be qualified by scenario rather than treated as universal system properties?","answer":"Recovery time and recoverable data depend on failure scope, available infrastructure, data volume, dependencies, and recovery method.","explanation":"A system may meet a recovery objective for a single-instance failure but not for regional destruction or simultaneous dependency loss. Objectives therefore require explicit scenario scope.","principle":"Recovery objectives are conditional properties, not universal guarantees.","rule":"Associate each recovery-time and recovery-point target with a defined failure scenario, workload, and recovery environment.","examples":[{"scenario":"Single-node loss versus regional disaster.","result":"Separate recovery objectives are defined because the failure domains and recovery procedures differ."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Advertising one recovery time as valid for every failure scenario.","reason":"Different disaster scopes can require fundamentally different recovery paths."}],"problem":"Recovery commitments exceed tested scenarios.","failure":"Expectation mismatch during major incidents.","cause":"Unqualified recovery objectives.","solution":"Version and scope objectives by failure scenario and evidence level.","alternatives":["Scenario-specific SLOs","Recovery profiles","Tiered disaster classes"],"tradeoffs":"More granular objectives increase planning complexity.","constraints":["Scenario definitions must be operationally meaningful.","Evidence must support each stated target."],"edge_cases":["Regional provider outage","Data corruption","Security compromise","Multiple simultaneous failures"],"security":"Cyber incidents may require recovery procedures different from ordinary infrastructure failure.","performance":"Recovery environment capacity must match the scenario used for the target.","testing":"Run representative recovery drills for each critical disaster class.","verification":"Verify each objective against evidence from the corresponding scenario.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B52-004","D22-B52-005","D22-B53-002"],"prerequisites":["RTO","RPO","Disaster classification"],"relationships":["D22-B53-004","D22-B55-002"],"provenance":["Derived disaster-recovery objective principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B53-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Disaster Recovery","concept":"Failback safety","knowledge_type":"recovery_rule","question":"Why can failback be more dangerous than failover?","answer":"After failover, the secondary environment may contain newer state, changed configuration, or active traffic. Returning to the original environment can create data divergence or ownership conflicts.","explanation":"Failback is a separate state transition that requires synchronization, authority transfer, readiness validation, and traffic control.","principle":"Failover and failback are distinct recovery operations with distinct safety conditions.","rule":"Never treat successful failover as automatic evidence that failback is safe.","examples":[{"scenario":"Secondary region served writes during an outage.","result":"Failback requires state synchronization and authoritative ownership transfer before traffic returns."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Switching traffic back immediately when the original region becomes reachable.","reason":"The original environment may be stale or unsafe."}],"problem":"Recovery restoration causes state divergence.","failure":"Data loss, split ownership, or renewed outage.","cause":"Failback treated as reverse failover.","solution":"Define independent failback prerequisites and verify state convergence.","alternatives":["Stay-on-secondary strategy","Controlled migration","Manual failback gate"],"tradeoffs":"Conservative failback may prolong operation in the secondary environment.","constraints":["Authoritative state must be identified.","Traffic transition must be reversible or safely staged."],"edge_cases":["Asymmetric recovery","Partial synchronization","Configuration drift"],"security":"Failback must preserve security configuration and identity controls.","performance":"Data synchronization can compete with serving traffic.","testing":"Exercise failback after realistic write activity in the secondary environment.","verification":"Verify state convergence and exclusive authority before completing failback.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B47-004","D22-B53-003"],"prerequisites":["Failover","State synchronization"],"relationships":["D22-B53-005","D22-B54-001"],"provenance":["Derived failback reliability principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B53-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Disaster Recovery","concept":"Recovery environment drift","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"How can recovery environments become unreliable even when backup data remains valid?","answer":"Software versions, configuration, infrastructure capacity, credentials, network topology, dependencies, and operational procedures can drift away from the assumptions under which recovery was designed.","explanation":"A recovery environment is a system in its own right and must remain compatible with the artifacts it is expected to restore.","principle":"Disaster recovery capability requires maintaining the recovery environment, not only recovery data.","rule":"Detect and remediate material drift between production recovery assumptions and the actual recovery environment.","examples":[{"scenario":"Backup was created with one application version but the recovery environment only supports an incompatible schema.","result":"Restore may fail despite an intact backup."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Testing backups while ignoring the software and infrastructure used to restore them.","reason":"The recovery environment can be the failure point."}],"problem":"Recovery environment no longer matches recovery artifacts.","failure":"Restore incompatibility.","cause":"Unmanaged recovery drift.","solution":"Version and validate recovery environment dependencies and periodically execute restoration tests.","alternatives":["Infrastructure as code","Reproducible recovery environment","Automated drift detection"],"tradeoffs":"Continuous recovery-environment maintenance adds operational cost.","constraints":["Recovery artifacts and environment versions must remain traceable.","External dependencies may not be fully reproducible."],"edge_cases":["Provider version retirement","Certificate expiration","Configuration drift","Capacity reduction"],"security":"Recovery environments must maintain equivalent security controls appropriate to restored data.","performance":"Recovery capacity should be periodically measured rather than assumed.","testing":"Restore using current recovery infrastructure and representative historical artifacts.","verification":"Verify compatibility across data, software, configuration, credentials, and infrastructure versions.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B52-002","D22-B52-005"],"prerequisites":["Configuration management","Disaster recovery"],"relationships":["D22-B48-004","D22-B54-002"],"provenance":["Derived disaster-recovery maintenance principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B54-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Multi-Region Resilience","concept":"Region independence assumptions","knowledge_type":"architecture_analysis","question":"Why should multi-region reliability claims identify shared dependencies across regions?","answer":"Two regions can still share DNS, identity, control-plane services, deployment systems, databases, network providers, or configuration systems that create common failure modes.","explanation":"Geographic separation alone does not guarantee independence. Reliability analysis must identify actual shared dependencies and their failure domains.","principle":"Physical separation is not equivalent to logical independence.","rule":"For every claimed independent failure domain, identify and assess shared critical dependencies.","examples":[{"scenario":"Two application regions share one identity provider.","result":"An identity-provider outage can affect both regions despite regional separation."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declaring full regional independence solely because compute instances are in different locations.","reason":"Shared services may remain a common failure domain."}],"problem":"Multi-region architecture provides less independence than assumed.","failure":"Common-mode regional outage.","cause":"Hidden shared dependency.","solution":"Map cross-region dependencies and isolate or explicitly account for critical shared services.","alternatives":["Independent control planes","Regional identity replicas","Out-of-band routing"],"tradeoffs":"Greater independence increases architecture and operational complexity.","constraints":["Some dependencies are inherently global.","Independence claims must state remaining common dependencies."],"edge_cases":["Global DNS","Global configuration","Certificate authority","Provider-wide service outage"],"security":"Security services can become global dependencies and require explicit resilience design.","performance":"Cross-region independence may increase latency or duplicate infrastructure.","testing":"Disable shared dependencies and observe multi-region behavior.","verification":"Verify the actual blast radius of each common dependency.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B45-001","D22-B53-001"],"prerequisites":["Failure domains","Dependency analysis"],"relationships":["D22-B54-002","D22-B54-004"],"provenance":["Derived multi-region reliability principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B54-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Multi-Region Resilience","concept":"Regional capacity survivability","knowledge_type":"capacity_requirement","question":"Why must surviving-region capacity be evaluated during regional failover?","answer":"A surviving region must have sufficient capacity to serve the transferred workload without becoming overloaded and failing itself.","explanation":"Failover can multiply traffic and resource requirements in the destination region. Nominal regional capacity under normal load does not prove survivability under disaster load.","principle":"Failover capacity must be evaluated under the workload transferred by the failure scenario.","rule":"Model and test surviving-region capacity under representative failover load, including recovery overhead.","examples":[{"scenario":"One of two regions fails.","result":"The surviving region must demonstrate bounded latency and resource usage under the transferred workload."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming two 50%-utilized regions automatically provide safe failover.","reason":"Failover traffic and recovery overhead may exceed available capacity."}],"problem":"Failover causes secondary regional overload.","failure":"Multi-region cascade.","cause":"Insufficient survivable capacity.","solution":"Reserve capacity, shed lower-priority work, or scale based on tested failover scenarios.","alternatives":["Active-active","Active-passive","Capacity overprovisioning"],"tradeoffs":"Reserved failover capacity can increase cost during normal operation.","constraints":["Capacity model must include dependency limits.","Traffic distribution assumptions must be explicit."],"edge_cases":["Traffic surge during disaster","Concurrent dependency failure","Recovery replay"],"security":"Security controls must also scale with failover load.","performance":"Tail latency should be evaluated under failover rather than average throughput alone.","testing":"Conduct controlled regional failover at representative load.","verification":"Verify surviving capacity remains within defined reliability boundaries.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B42-003","D22-B53-003"],"prerequisites":["Capacity planning","Regional failover"],"relationships":["D22-B54-003","D22-B54-005"],"provenance":["Derived regional failover capacity principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B54-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Multi-Region Resilience","concept":"Cross-region data consistency mode","knowledge_type":"distributed_state_model","question":"Why must a multi-region system explicitly define its data consistency behavior during partition or failover?","answer":"Different replication and consistency models can produce different guarantees for freshness, availability, ordering, conflict resolution, and potential data loss.","explanation":"Failover decisions can be unsafe when operators assume stronger consistency than the architecture actually provides.","principle":"Data consistency guarantees must be explicit under normal operation and failure.","rule":"Document what data can diverge, how conflicts are resolved, and which operations remain permitted during partition.","examples":[{"scenario":"Both regions can temporarily accept writes.","result":"Conflict resolution and authoritative ownership rules must be explicit."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming replicated data is identical because replication normally appears healthy.","reason":"Partition behavior may permit divergence."}],"problem":"Failover exposes hidden consistency assumptions.","failure":"Data conflict or silent loss.","cause":"Unspecified failure-time consistency model.","solution":"Define consistency modes and failure-state write policy.","alternatives":["Single-writer model","Quorum writes","Conflict-free replicated state"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger consistency can reduce availability or increase latency under partition.","constraints":["Business-critical data requires explicit conflict policy.","Consistency guarantees must match actual implementation."],"edge_cases":["Concurrent writes","Delayed replication","Clock skew","Conflict after failback"],"security":"Authorization state must not diverge in ways that grant unintended access.","performance":"Cross-region synchronization can increase latency and bandwidth usage.","testing":"Partition regions and exercise allowed write/read operations.","verification":"Verify observed behavior matches documented consistency guarantees.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B49-003","D22-B53-004"],"prerequisites":["Distributed consistency","Replication"],"relationships":["D22-B54-004","D22-B55-002"],"provenance":["Derived distributed-consistency reliability principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B54-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Multi-Region Resilience","concept":"Common-mode failure analysis","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"What is a common-mode failure in a multi-component reliability architecture?","answer":"It is a single underlying cause that causes multiple supposedly independent components or failure domains to fail together.","explanation":"Common-mode causes include shared software defects, common configuration, common identity systems, shared providers, synchronized deployment errors, and correlated infrastructure failures.","principle":"Reliability independence must be evaluated by common causes, not component count.","rule":"For every critical redundancy claim, identify plausible common-mode failure causes and their controls.","examples":[{"scenario":"All regions receive the same faulty configuration deployment.","result":"Geographic redundancy does not protect against the common configuration defect."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Concluding reliability increases linearly with replica count.","reason":"Replicas can share common failure causes."}],"problem":"Redundancy provides less protection than expected.","failure":"Simultaneous multi-component outage.","cause":"Common-mode dependency or change.","solution":"Separate failure domains where justified and control shared dependencies and changes.","alternatives":["Diverse implementations","Independent deployment paths","Staged rollout"],"tradeoffs":"Diversity and independence increase operational cost.","constraints":["Not every common mode can be eliminated.","Residual common causes must be documented."],"edge_cases":["Shared software release","Common provider","Global configuration","Shared certificate"],"security":"Shared credentials or security controls can become common-mode failure causes.","performance":"Independent infrastructure can reduce resource-sharing efficiency.","testing":"Inject common-mode failures such as configuration or provider-level faults.","verification":"Verify redundancy claims against identified common-mode scenarios.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B54-001","D22-B35-003"],"prerequisites":["Failure-mode analysis","Redundancy"],"relationships":["D22-B54-005","D22-B55-003"],"provenance":["Derived common-cause reliability principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B54-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Multi-Region Resilience","concept":"Disaster declaration evidence threshold","knowledge_type":"governance_rule","question":"Why should disaster declaration have explicit evidence thresholds?","answer":"Premature disaster declaration can cause unnecessary failover, while delayed declaration can prolong outage. Explicit criteria reduce arbitrary switching between normal and disaster modes.","explanation":"The declaration should consider failure scope, expected duration, dependency availability, capacity, data consistency, and operational authority.","principle":"High-impact failover decisions require explicit evidence and authority.","rule":"Define measurable disaster-declaration criteria and distinguish automated detection from authorized mode transition.","examples":[{"scenario":"Primary region is unavailable and expected recovery exceeds the defined threshold.","result":"The system evaluates declared failover criteria before transitioning ownership."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Failing over solely because one health probe is negative.","reason":"A single observation may not establish regional failure."}],"problem":"Failover decisions are inconsistent or unsafe.","failure":"Unnecessary failover or delayed recovery.","cause":"Undefined declaration criteria.","solution":"Use multi-signal evidence, scoped authority, and explicit abort conditions.","alternatives":["Automatic failover","Human-approved failover","Quorum-based declaration"],"tradeoffs":"More evidence can delay failover but reduces false transitions.","constraints":["Criteria must account for asymmetric failure.","Data consistency implications must be included."],"edge_cases":["Network partition","Monitoring outage","Partial regional failure"],"security":"Failover authority must be strongly authenticated and audited.","performance":"Detection and declaration latency affect recovery objectives.","testing":"Simulate ambiguous and clear disaster conditions and verify distinct decisions.","verification":"Verify disaster declaration is traceable to required evidence and authority.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B41-002","D22-B46-002","D22-B53-004"],"prerequisites":["Failover governance","Failure detection"],"relationships":["D22-B54-003","D22-B55-004"],"provenance":["Derived failover-governance principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B55-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Third-Party Reliability","concept":"External provider failure envelope","knowledge_type":"dependency_model","question":"Why should third-party dependencies have an explicit failure envelope?","answer":"A third-party service can fail, throttle, change behavior, introduce latency, or become unreachable in ways outside the consumer's direct control.","explanation":"Reliability design should specify what the application does when the provider is unavailable, degraded, rate-limited, inconsistent, or changed.","principle":"External dependencies require explicit assumptions and bounded fallback behavior.","rule":"For critical external providers, document supported failure modes, client protections, fallback behavior, and unresolved dependency risks.","examples":[{"scenario":"External payment provider becomes unavailable.","result":"Application enters a defined safe state rather than retrying indefinitely."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming provider SLA alone determines application behavior during provider failure.","reason":"The application still needs client-side protection and fallback policy."}],"problem":"External failure becomes application-wide failure.","failure":"Cascading or prolonged outage.","cause":"Undefined third-party failure behavior.","solution":"Use dependency contracts, timeouts, bounded retries, fallbacks, and provider-independent recovery paths where justified.","alternatives":["Provider redundancy","Local queueing","Alternative provider"],"tradeoffs":"Redundancy can increase integration and consistency complexity.","constraints":["Provider capabilities may limit failure isolation.","Fallback must preserve correctness."],"edge_cases":["Provider partial outage","Rate limiting","API version change","Provider account suspension"],"security":"Provider failure handling must not weaken authentication or authorization.","performance":"Provider protection mechanisms should prevent slow external dependencies from dominating latency.","testing":"Simulate provider timeout, error, throttling, and malformed response behavior.","verification":"Verify the application remains within defined failure boundaries for each provider failure class.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B44-001","D22-B44-003"],"prerequisites":["External dependencies","Failure contracts"],"relationships":["D22-B55-002","D22-B55-003"],"provenance":["Derived third-party dependency reliability principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B55-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Third-Party Reliability","concept":"Provider failover consistency","knowledge_type":"distributed_systems_rule","question":"Why can switching between third-party providers create new reliability risks?","answer":"Providers may differ in data models, ordering, error semantics, latency, idempotency, rate limits, and side-effect behavior, so failover is not necessarily behaviorally equivalent.","explanation":"A secondary provider is not a drop-in reliability substitute unless compatibility and state reconciliation are explicitly established.","principle":"Provider redundancy requires semantic compatibility, not merely network reachability.","rule":"Define and test behavioral equivalence requirements before using an alternate provider as an automated failover target.","examples":[{"scenario":"Two notification providers use different delivery guarantees.","result":"Failover policy must account for duplicate or missing notifications."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Switching providers automatically because both expose similarly named APIs.","reason":"Semantics may differ materially."}],"problem":"Provider failover introduces inconsistent behavior.","failure":"Duplicate, missing, or incorrect external effects.","cause":"Assumed provider equivalence.","solution":"Define compatibility contracts and reconcile provider-specific semantics.","alternatives":["Single-provider design","Provider abstraction layer","Manual failover"],"tradeoffs":"Abstraction can reduce provider lock-in but may hide provider-specific capabilities.","constraints":["Critical side effects require explicit semantic comparison.","Provider state may not be interchangeable."],"edge_cases":["Payment state","Message delivery","Identity systems","Rate-limit differences"],"security":"Provider switching must preserve identity, authorization, and data-processing constraints.","performance":"Secondary provider may have different latency or capacity characteristics.","testing":"Execute failover with representative operations against both providers.","verification":"Verify equivalent required outcomes rather than only API-level compatibility.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B55-001","D22-B44-001"],"prerequisites":["Provider abstraction","External side effects"],"relationships":["D22-B55-003","D22-B55-005"],"provenance":["Derived provider-failover principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B55-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Third-Party Reliability","concept":"Dependency substitution risk","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"What should be evaluated before replacing a failed dependency with an alternative implementation?","answer":"The substitute should be evaluated for interface compatibility, semantic correctness, capacity, consistency, security, operational behavior, and recovery characteristics.","explanation":"A substitute can remove one failure but introduce another if assumptions about semantics or capacity are wrong.","principle":"Dependency substitution changes the system's failure model.","rule":"Re-evaluate reliability assumptions whenever a critical dependency is replaced or bypassed.","examples":[{"scenario":"A cache fallback becomes the temporary source for data normally obtained from a service.","result":"Freshness, authorization, completeness, and capacity assumptions must be reassessed."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming any technically reachable replacement is safe because the original provider is unavailable.","reason":"Behavioral differences can create correctness failures."}],"problem":"Emergency substitution creates hidden reliability defects.","failure":"Degraded path becomes unsafe or unstable.","cause":"Replacement not modeled as a new dependency.","solution":"Apply dependency-contract and failure analysis to the substitute path.","alternatives":["Prevalidated alternate provider","Static fallback","Manual recovery"],"tradeoffs":"Prevalidating substitutes costs engineering effort but reduces emergency uncertainty.","constraints":["Substitute must satisfy required invariants.","Temporary paths need explicit expiration or restoration conditions."],"edge_cases":["Emergency vendor switch","Cached state","Manual data source"],"security":"Alternative providers must satisfy applicable security requirements before use.","performance":"Substitute capacity and latency must be tested under expected failover load.","testing":"Exercise substitute under realistic failure and overload conditions.","verification":"Verify substitute behavior against the original path's critical invariants.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B55-001","D22-B43-002"],"prerequisites":["Dependency contracts","Fallback design"],"relationships":["D22-B54-003","D22-B55-004"],"provenance":["Derived dependency-substitution principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B55-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Third-Party Reliability","concept":"Provider change detection","knowledge_type":"reliability_governance","question":"Why should reliability systems detect material changes in external provider behavior?","answer":"A provider can change latency, limits, API semantics, dependencies, or failure behavior without changing the consumer's code, invalidating previous reliability assumptions.","explanation":"External dependency contracts are dynamic. Monitoring should identify changes that materially affect capacity, availability, or failure handling.","principle":"Reliability assumptions about external systems must be monitored for drift.","rule":"Define provider behaviors that are reliability-critical and detect material deviation from validated ranges or documented contracts.","examples":[{"scenario":"Provider rate limits are reduced materially.","result":"Consumer capacity assumptions become stale and require policy adjustment."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Waiting for a full outage before reassessing provider reliability assumptions.","reason":"Material drift can create instability before complete failure."}],"problem":"Dependency drift invalidates reliability controls silently.","failure":"Unexpected throttling or latency cascade.","cause":"Static dependency assumptions.","solution":"Monitor relevant provider behavior and trigger reassessment on material change.","alternatives":["Contract testing","Provider health telemetry","Periodic dependency review"],"tradeoffs":"External behavior monitoring requires additional observability and interpretation.","constraints":["Provider signals may be incomplete.","Thresholds should avoid reacting to harmless variance."],"edge_cases":["Temporary provider degradation","API version transition","Regional provider differences"],"security":"Provider changes affecting authentication or authorization require heightened validation.","performance":"Monitoring must distinguish normal variability from material drift.","testing":"Simulate provider behavior changes and verify dependency controls respond appropriately.","verification":"Verify material provider changes trigger review before invalid assumptions cause cascading failure.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B48-004","D22-B55-001"],"prerequisites":["Dependency monitoring","Contract management"],"relationships":["D22-B55-005","D22-B48-003"],"provenance":["Derived dependency-drift principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.96,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B55-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Third-Party Reliability","concept":"External dependency uncertainty budget","knowledge_type":"risk_model","question":"How should an autonomous reliability system represent uncertainty about third-party dependencies?","answer":"It should distinguish verified provider behavior, documented provider guarantees, observed behavior, assumptions, and unknown properties rather than collapsing them into one reliability value.","explanation":"External systems often expose incomplete information. Treating assumptions as facts can cause unsafe routing, retries, failover, or capacity planning decisions.","principle":"Unknown dependency behavior should remain explicitly uncertain.","rule":"Store evidence scope and confidence for material third-party reliability assumptions and avoid treating undocumented behavior as guaranteed.","examples":[{"scenario":"Provider retry semantics are undocumented.","result":"The client treats retry safety as unknown until verified rather than assuming retries are safe."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming an undocumented API operation is idempotent because it usually appears so.","reason":"Observed behavior is not equivalent to a contractual guarantee."}],"problem":"Autonomous decisions rely on unsupported dependency assumptions.","failure":"Unsafe automated recovery or retry behavior.","cause":"Unknown treated as verified fact.","solution":"Use evidence-scoped dependency knowledge with explicit uncertainty states.","alternatives":["Contract validation","Provider sandbox testing","Human approval for uncertain actions"],"tradeoffs":"Explicit uncertainty can reduce automation and require conservative behavior.","constraints":["Unknown must not be silently converted to false or true.","Evidence must be version-aware."],"edge_cases":["Provider undocumented behavior","Provider regional variation","Rapid API change"],"security":"Unknown security semantics require fail-safe handling for critical operations.","performance":"Conservative behavior may reduce throughput but limits uncontrolled risk.","testing":"Introduce undocumented or changed provider behavior into dependency simulations.","verification":"Verify autonomous reliability decisions distinguish observed, guaranteed, inferred, and unknown dependency facts.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B55-004","D22-B48-005"],"prerequisites":["Evidence modeling","Dependency reliability"],"relationships":["D22-B49-005","D22-B56-001"],"provenance":["Derived evidence-driven dependency principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B56-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Autonomous Reliability Reasoning","concept":"Evidence-weighted recovery decision","knowledge_type":"decision_model","question":"How should an autonomous reliability system choose between recovery actions when evidence is incomplete?","answer":"It should evaluate candidate actions against explicit objectives, evidence quality, failure scope, safety invariants, expected side effects, reversibility, and uncertainty rather than selecting solely by apparent speed.","explanation":"Automated reliability decisions can be unsafe when they optimize recovery time while ignoring data consistency, authorization, blast radius, or uncertainty.","principle":"Recovery decisions should optimize within explicit safety and evidence constraints.","rule":"Require sufficient evidence for high-impact actions and prefer bounded reversible actions when uncertainty is material.","examples":[{"scenario":"Two recovery targets are reachable but only one has verified state consistency.","result":"The verified target has stronger evidence for safe use; the uncertain target requires additional validation or approval."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Always choosing the physically closest recovery target.","reason":"Proximity does not establish correctness or safety."}],"problem":"Autonomous recovery optimizes a narrow metric.","failure":"Fast but unsafe recovery.","cause":"Single-objective recovery policy.","solution":"Use multi-factor decision evaluation with hard invariants and evidence thresholds.","alternatives":["Rule-based recovery","Human approval","Formal policy engine"],"tradeoffs":"Evidence-aware decisions can take longer than simple failover.","constraints":["Safety invariants must be hard constraints.","Unknown evidence must remain explicit."],"edge_cases":["Conflicting health signals","Multiple partial failures","Stale evidence"],"security":"High-impact actions require authorization and audit evidence.","performance":"Decision computation should remain bounded during incidents.","testing":"Present conflicting and incomplete evidence and verify conservative decision behavior.","verification":"Record decision inputs, policy version, evidence scope, and resulting action.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B55-005","D22-B46-002"],"prerequisites":["Decision models","Evidence provenance"],"relationships":["D22-B56-002","D22-B57-001"],"provenance":["Derived autonomous reliability decision principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B56-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Autonomous Reliability Reasoning","concept":"Recovery action reversibility","knowledge_type":"decision_rule","question":"Why should reversibility influence autonomous reliability actions?","answer":"When uncertainty is high, reversible actions limit the consequences of an incorrect decision and allow the system to return to a known state.","explanation":"Irreversible actions require stronger evidence and authorization because a wrong decision can create durable damage that cannot be automatically corrected.","principle":"Decision authority should increase with irreversibility and potential impact.","rule":"Require stronger evidence and authorization for actions with greater irreversibility or blast radius.","examples":[{"scenario":"Traffic can be shifted temporarily and shifted back without data mutation.","result":"A bounded traffic change may require less evidence than destructive state modification."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Allowing an autonomous controller to perform irreversible deletion based on a weak health signal.","reason":"The action is disproportionate to the evidence."}],"problem":"Automation performs high-impact actions under weak evidence.","failure":"Irreversible operational damage.","cause":"No reversibility-aware policy.","solution":"Classify actions by impact and reversibility and apply corresponding evidence/approval requirements.","alternatives":["Approval gates","Two-step commit","Dry-run mode"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger safeguards reduce automation speed.","constraints":["Irreversible action classification must be explicit.","Emergency paths still require auditability."],"edge_cases":["Unknown action outcome","Partial execution","External side effects"],"security":"Irreversible actions require least privilege and strong authorization.","performance":"Approval or verification gates can increase recovery latency.","testing":"Inject uncertain evidence and verify action policy changes with reversibility.","verification":"Verify that action authority corresponds to documented impact and reversibility.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B46-004","D22-B56-001"],"prerequisites":["Action classification","Governed automation"],"relationships":["D22-B56-003","D22-B57-002"],"provenance":["Derived safe-automation principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B56-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Autonomous Reliability Reasoning","concept":"Reliability policy precedence","knowledge_type":"governance_model","question":"Why should autonomous reliability policies have explicit precedence rules?","answer":"Different policies can conflict, such as availability versus data integrity, recovery speed versus safety, or local optimization versus global capacity protection.","explanation":"Without precedence, an autonomous system may select different actions depending on implementation order or incidental signal timing.","principle":"Conflicting reliability objectives require deterministic policy precedence.","rule":"Define hard invariants first, followed by safety constraints, resource constraints, and optimization objectives.","examples":[{"scenario":"Fast recovery conflicts with prevention of split ownership.","result":"Ownership safety remains a hard constraint even if recovery becomes slower."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Selecting whichever recovery objective produces the highest numerical score without hard constraints.","reason":"A high score can still violate non-negotiable safety properties."}],"problem":"Autonomous decision-making produces inconsistent tradeoffs.","failure":"Optimization violates safety.","cause":"No policy hierarchy.","solution":"Represent hard constraints separately from optimization objectives.","alternatives":["Policy engine","Priority rules","Formal constraint solver"],"tradeoffs":"Explicit precedence can limit optimization freedom.","constraints":["Policy hierarchy must be versioned.","Conflicting policies should be surfaced rather than silently resolved."],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous incidents","Emergency override","Policy version mismatch"],"security":"Security invariants should have explicit precedence where reliability actions affect access or isolation.","performance":"Policy evaluation should remain deterministic and bounded.","testing":"Create conflicting policy scenarios and verify stable precedence.","verification":"Verify decisions can be traced to the active policy hierarchy.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B49-002","D22-B56-001"],"prerequisites":["Policy systems","Reliability objectives"],"relationships":["D22-B56-004","D22-B57-003"],"provenance":["Derived autonomous control principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B56-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Autonomous Reliability Reasoning","concept":"Decision uncertainty escalation","knowledge_type":"governance_rule","question":"When should an autonomous reliability controller escalate instead of acting?","answer":"Escalation is appropriate when required evidence is missing or contradictory, the action is high-impact and irreversible, safety invariants cannot be established, or the system is outside the controller's modeled scope.","explanation":"A reliable autonomous system must recognize the boundary of its knowledge and authority rather than treating every situation as automatically solvable.","principle":"Knowing when not to act is part of reliability.","rule":"Define explicit uncertainty and authority conditions that stop automation and invoke controlled escalation.","examples":[{"scenario":"Two authoritative sources report incompatible ownership state.","result":"Automation enters an uncertain state and escalates rather than choosing arbitrarily."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Choosing the most recent signal whenever authoritative evidence conflicts.","reason":"Recency alone may not establish correctness."}],"problem":"Automation acts beyond validated knowledge.","failure":"Unsafe state transition.","cause":"No uncertainty boundary.","solution":"Implement explicit escalation states and evidence thresholds.","alternatives":["Human approval","Secondary verification","Conservative safe state"],"tradeoffs":"Escalation can prolong incidents but limits unsafe automation.","constraints":["Escalation path must remain available during incidents.","Unknown state must be represented explicitly."],"edge_cases":["Monitoring outage","Conflicting telemetry","Provider ambiguity"],"security":"High-impact security-affecting actions should have strict escalation thresholds.","performance":"Escalation should not create unbounded incident latency.","testing":"Inject conflicting evidence and verify controlled escalation.","verification":"Verify every high-impact controller has a defined uncertainty boundary.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B56-001","D22-B56-003"],"prerequisites":["Evidence modeling","Governed automation"],"relationships":["D22-B46-002","D22-B57-004"],"provenance":["Derived autonomous reliability governance principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B56-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Autonomous Reliability Reasoning","concept":"Reliability decision replay","knowledge_type":"learning_method","question":"Why should autonomous reliability decisions be replayable after an incident?","answer":"Replay allows engineers or an autonomous learning system to reconstruct the evidence, policy, state, and alternatives available at decision time and determine why an action occurred.","explanation":"Without replayable decision context, post-incident analysis can confuse later knowledge with information that was actually available during the incident.","principle":"Reliability learning requires temporal reconstruction of decision context.","rule":"Persist sufficient decision evidence, policy version, state snapshot references, and action outcomes to replay consequential reliability decisions.","examples":[{"scenario":"Controller initiates failover during ambiguous dependency failure.","result":"Later analysis can reconstruct the exact signals and policy used at that time."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Reconstructing a past decision only from current dashboard state.","reason":"Current state may differ substantially from decision-time state."}],"problem":"Autonomous reliability decisions cannot be audited or learned from accurately.","failure":"Repeated mistakes or unsupported post-incident conclusions.","cause":"Insufficient decision provenance.","solution":"Persist decision-time evidence and outcome references.","alternatives":["Event sourcing","Decision journal","Incident timeline"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed provenance increases storage and privacy requirements.","constraints":["Sensitive evidence requires access control.","Retention must align with operational needs."],"edge_cases":["Missing telemetry","Clock skew","Conflicting timestamps"],"security":"Decision logs can contain sensitive infrastructure information.","performance":"Decision journaling should be lightweight and asynchronous where safe.","testing":"Replay historical or synthetic decisions and verify deterministic reconstruction.","verification":"Verify replay reproduces the relevant decision context without inventing unavailable evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B50-004","D22-B56-001"],"prerequisites":["Provenance","Event history"],"relationships":["D22-B57-005","D22-B58-001"],"provenance":["Derived reliability-learning principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B57-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Learning","concept":"Outcome-based reliability learning","knowledge_type":"learning_model","question":"Why should reliability learning use verified outcomes instead of actions alone?","answer":"An action can execute successfully without producing the intended recovery outcome. Learning from action status alone can reinforce ineffective or unsafe behavior.","explanation":"Reliability learning should connect decision, execution, observed state change, user impact, and recovery result. Outcome verification separates attempted remediation from actual success.","principle":"Learning should optimize verified outcomes, not merely executed actions.","rule":"Do not mark a remediation pattern successful unless its intended reliability outcome was verified within the defined scope.","examples":[{"scenario":"Controller shifts traffic successfully but latency remains elevated.","result":"The action is recorded as executed but not as a verified recovery success."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Increasing confidence in a remediation because its API command returned success.","reason":"Command success does not prove system recovery."}],"problem":"Learning system reinforces actions without outcome evidence.","failure":"Repeated ineffective remediation.","cause":"Execution-centric learning.","solution":"Capture post-action outcome verification and link it to the decision record.","alternatives":["Outcome-based feedback","Closed-loop verification","Incident learning graph"],"tradeoffs":"Outcome collection adds observability and storage requirements.","constraints":["Outcome definition must be scenario-specific.","Unverified outcomes must remain explicitly unknown."],"edge_cases":["Delayed recovery","Multiple simultaneous actions","Partial success"],"security":"Outcome collection must respect data minimization and access controls.","performance":"Verification should be bounded and avoid adding excessive incident load.","testing":"Create actions that execute successfully but fail to restore service.","verification":"Verify learning updates only from correctly classified outcomes.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B46-003","D22-B56-005"],"prerequisites":["Outcome verification","Learning systems"],"relationships":["D22-B57-002","D22-B58-002"],"provenance":["Derived closed-loop reliability learning principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B57-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Learning","concept":"Counterfactual reliability analysis","knowledge_type":"reasoning_method","question":"What is counterfactual reliability analysis?","answer":"It examines what might have happened under a different action, failure response, or timing while clearly distinguishing observed evidence from hypothetical reasoning.","explanation":"Counterfactual analysis can identify whether an incident was sensitive to a decision, but hypothetical outcomes must not be recorded as observed facts.","principle":"Reliability learning can reason about alternatives without confusing hypotheses with evidence.","rule":"Label counterfactual conclusions separately from observed outcomes and retain the assumptions used to generate them.","examples":[{"scenario":"A failover succeeded after ten minutes.","result":"A counterfactual analysis may ask what would have happened with earlier failover, but the result remains hypothetical unless tested or evidenced."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Recording a simulated alternative recovery result as an observed incident outcome.","reason":"Hypothetical evidence is not historical evidence."}],"problem":"Learning system confuses observed and hypothetical outcomes.","failure":"Incorrect reliability knowledge is reinforced.","cause":"Evidence-type collapse.","solution":"Separate observed, simulated, inferred, and hypothetical evidence classes.","alternatives":["Simulation","Fault injection","Model-based analysis"],"tradeoffs":"Counterfactual analysis can be uncertain without high-fidelity models.","constraints":["Assumptions must be explicit.","Unverified alternatives must not become facts."],"edge_cases":["Missing telemetry","Non-deterministic systems","Changing workload"],"security":"Simulations should not expose sensitive operational data unnecessarily.","performance":"High-fidelity counterfactual simulation can be computationally expensive.","testing":"Compare counterfactual predictions against controlled experiments where feasible.","verification":"Verify evidence labels remain distinct throughout learning and retrieval.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B56-005","D22-B57-001"],"prerequisites":["Causal reasoning","Evidence classification"],"relationships":["D22-B57-003","D22-B58-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability-learning and causal-reasoning principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B57-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Learning","concept":"Reliability regression knowledge","knowledge_type":"learning_model","question":"How should an autonomous engineering system represent a previously observed reliability regression?","answer":"It should store the affected component or scenario, triggering change, observed failure signature, evidence, remediation, verified outcome, and conditions under which the regression may recur.","explanation":"A regression is valuable learning only when its context is preserved. Generic statements such as 'version X is unreliable' can become misleading outside the original conditions.","principle":"Reliability lessons should be contextual and condition-bound.","rule":"Store regression knowledge with version, workload, configuration, dependency, and evidence scope.","examples":[{"scenario":"A configuration change caused queue growth under high concurrency.","result":"The lesson records the exact conditions and verified remediation rather than labeling the configuration universally unsafe."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Storing only 'change caused outage' without conditions or evidence.","reason":"The knowledge is too vague for reliable reuse."}],"problem":"Learning becomes overgeneralized.","failure":"Autonomous system avoids safe changes or repeats known failures.","cause":"Context-free reliability lessons.","solution":"Use structured, versioned regression records.","alternatives":["Incident knowledge graph","Regression corpus","Change-risk registry"],"tradeoffs":"Context-rich records require more storage and extraction effort.","constraints":["Historical conditions must be preserved accurately.","Unknown context should remain unknown."],"edge_cases":["Multiple interacting changes","Partial rollback","Environment-specific regression"],"security":"Incident records require appropriate access controls.","performance":"Index regression signatures for efficient retrieval.","testing":"Replay regression scenarios under current and historical versions where feasible.","verification":"Verify that regression records are not generalized beyond their evidence scope.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B48-004","D22-B57-001"],"prerequisites":["Change tracking","Incident analysis"],"relationships":["D22-B35-003","D22-B58-004"],"provenance":["Derived reliability-learning principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B57-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Learning","concept":"Learning safety boundary","knowledge_type":"governance_rule","question":"Why should reliability learning have a safety boundary?","answer":"Automatically learning from noisy, incomplete, or misclassified incidents can cause future automation to adopt unsafe policies or reinforce accidental correlations.","explanation":"Learning systems should distinguish verified outcomes from hypotheses, anomalies, and low-confidence observations. Material policy changes require stronger evidence than ordinary retrieval knowledge.","principle":"Learning must not silently convert uncertain observations into operational authority.","rule":"Require evidence thresholds and governance checks before learned information can alter high-impact automated behavior.","examples":[{"scenario":"One unusual timeout occurs during a complex incident.","result":"It can become a low-confidence observation but should not automatically change global retry policy."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Changing production recovery policy after one unverified incident observation.","reason":"Evidence is insufficient for a high-impact policy change."}],"problem":"Learning creates self-reinforcing unsafe behavior.","failure":"Future incidents are handled using incorrect learned policy.","cause":"Unbounded learning authority.","solution":"Separate observation, hypothesis, validated lesson, and authorized policy update.","alternatives":["Human-reviewed learning","Shadow policy evaluation","Offline policy validation"],"tradeoffs":"Governance reduces learning speed but improves safety.","constraints":["Policy changes require explicit evidence and versioning.","Critical controls should have stronger approval requirements."],"edge_cases":["Contradictory incidents","Rare failures","Distribution shifts"],"security":"Adversarial inputs must not be allowed to poison reliability policy.","performance":"Learning validation can run offline to protect production paths.","testing":"Inject misleading observations and verify they cannot directly alter critical policy.","verification":"Verify learned knowledge has no elevated authority without passing defined validation gates.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B56-004","D22-B57-001"],"prerequisites":["Learning governance","Evidence classification"],"relationships":["D22-B57-005","D22-B58-005"],"provenance":["Derived safe-learning principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B57-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Learning","concept":"Contradictory reliability evidence","knowledge_type":"contradiction_model","question":"How should an autonomous reliability system handle contradictory evidence about a failure mode?","answer":"It should preserve the conflicting observations, identify their scope and provenance, and avoid collapsing them into an unsupported single conclusion.","explanation":"Different environments, versions, workloads, or observers can produce apparently contradictory results. Contradiction may indicate conditional behavior rather than erroneous data.","principle":"Contradictory evidence is information that requires contextual resolution, not silent deletion.","rule":"Preserve conflicting evidence and attempt resolution using scope, timestamp, version, authority, and experimental conditions.","examples":[{"scenario":"A recovery test succeeds in one environment but fails in another.","result":"The system records environmental differences before concluding whether the behavior is conditional."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Deleting the failed test because a later test succeeded.","reason":"The earlier failure may reveal an important conditional failure mode."}],"problem":"Learning system loses important boundary conditions.","failure":"Overgeneralized reliability knowledge.","cause":"Contradiction suppression.","solution":"Model evidence as scoped observations with explicit contradiction relationships.","alternatives":["Evidence graph","Bayesian evidence model","Experiment comparison"],"tradeoffs":"Contradiction management increases reasoning and storage complexity.","constraints":["No evidence should be silently discarded.","Resolution must remain traceable."],"edge_cases":["Different software versions","Different load profiles","Different failure injection methods"],"security":"Evidence sources should retain provenance and access restrictions.","performance":"Contradiction analysis can be performed asynchronously.","testing":"Create controlled scenarios with differing conditions and verify both observations remain retrievable.","verification":"Verify that resolved contradictions retain their original evidence and resolution rationale.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B55-005","D22-B57-004"],"prerequisites":["Evidence graph","Contradiction handling"],"relationships":["D22-B48-005","D22-B58-005"],"provenance":["Derived evidence-governance principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B58-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Automation","concept":"Reliability action ledger","knowledge_type":"data_model","question":"What should a reliability action ledger record?","answer":"It should record requested action, authorization, policy version, evidence inputs, target scope, execution status, outcome status, rollback or compensation, and verification evidence.","explanation":"A ledger separates intent, authorization, execution, and outcome. This enables auditability and prevents a command acknowledgement from being confused with successful recovery.","principle":"Consequential reliability actions require end-to-end provenance.","rule":"Persist a durable action record for high-impact automated or operator-triggered reliability actions.","examples":[{"scenario":"Automated failover changes traffic ownership.","result":"The ledger records the authorization, target, evidence, execution result, and post-action verification."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Keeping only the final log line 'failover complete'.","reason":"The line does not establish what was authorized or whether recovery succeeded."}],"problem":"Reliability actions cannot be reconstructed accurately.","failure":"Poor auditability and repeated recovery mistakes.","cause":"Insufficient action provenance.","solution":"Use structured action records with explicit lifecycle states.","alternatives":["Event journal","Audit log","Workflow history"],"tradeoffs":"Durable records increase storage and governance requirements.","constraints":["Records must be tamper-resistant where required.","Sensitive operational data must be protected."],"edge_cases":["Controller crash","Duplicate action request","Unknown outcome"],"security":"Authorization evidence must be bound to the action identity and scope.","performance":"Ledger writes should be bounded and reliable during incidents.","testing":"Crash controllers before and after action execution and verify ledger reconstruction.","verification":"Verify action lifecycle remains distinguishable from outcome lifecycle.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B46-003","D22-B56-005"],"prerequisites":["Auditability","Workflow state"],"relationships":["D22-B58-002","D22-B58-003"],"provenance":["Derived reliability-action provenance principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B58-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Automation","concept":"Reliability automation dry-run","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"Why is dry-run evaluation useful for high-impact reliability automation?","answer":"It allows a controller to evaluate evidence, candidate actions, dependencies, and expected effects without applying the consequential state change.","explanation":"Dry-run can expose incorrect assumptions and policy conflicts before production mutation. It does not replace execution testing because real side effects and race conditions may differ.","principle":"Prediction of an action is useful evidence but is not equivalent to verified execution.","rule":"Use dry-run to validate decision logic while maintaining separate evidence for actual execution and outcome.","examples":[{"scenario":"Controller proposes regional failover.","result":"Dry-run identifies target capacity, dependencies, and policy conditions before authorization."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treating successful dry-run as proof that production failover will succeed.","reason":"Dry-run does not establish real execution behavior."}],"problem":"High-impact automation is difficult to validate safely.","failure":"Production mutation exposes untested decision errors.","cause":"No pre-execution evaluation.","solution":"Provide dry-run or shadow evaluation where architecture permits.","alternatives":["Simulation","Shadow controller","Staging execution"],"tradeoffs":"Dry-run may omit real-time race and external side effects.","constraints":["Dry-run must clearly identify simulated assumptions.","No real mutation should occur."],"edge_cases":["Dynamic capacity","Concurrent controller","External provider changes"],"security":"Dry-run should not require broader privileges than necessary.","performance":"Dry-run computation should be bounded during incidents.","testing":"Compare dry-run predictions with controlled execution outcomes.","verification":"Verify dry-run and actual execution are represented as separate evidence classes.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B56-001","D22-B58-001"],"prerequisites":["Policy evaluation","Action planning"],"relationships":["D22-B49-004","D22-B58-003"],"provenance":["Derived safe-automation verification principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B58-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Automation","concept":"Shadow reliability controller","knowledge_type":"verification_pattern","question":"What is a shadow reliability controller?","answer":"It observes the same evidence and produces candidate decisions without applying operational changes, allowing its decisions to be compared with actual system behavior or authorized decisions.","explanation":"Shadow operation can reveal policy defects, missing signals, and divergence before granting execution authority.","principle":"Decision logic can be validated independently from execution authority.","rule":"Keep shadow decisions explicitly non-authoritative and compare them against observed outcomes and approved actions.","examples":[{"scenario":"A new failover policy runs in shadow mode during real incidents.","result":"Its proposed decisions are recorded but cannot change traffic."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Allowing shadow code to retain hidden write privileges.","reason":"It is no longer a true non-authoritative validation path."}],"problem":"New reliability automation is difficult to validate against real conditions safely.","failure":"Untested controller behavior reaches production authority.","cause":"No separation between decision evaluation and execution.","solution":"Use shadow evaluation with strict no-write enforcement and evidence comparison.","alternatives":["Simulation","Canary controller","Offline replay"],"tradeoffs":"Shadow systems consume telemetry and analysis resources.","constraints":["Authority separation must be technically enforced.","Shadow output must not influence production indirectly."],"edge_cases":["Telemetry differences","Concurrent incidents","Policy divergence"],"security":"Shadow components should have minimal or zero operational write privileges.","performance":"Shadow computation should not affect production capacity.","testing":"Run shadow and active decision logic against the same controlled scenarios.","verification":"Verify shadow components cannot mutate production state.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B56-001","D22-B56-004"],"prerequisites":["Decision/execution separation","Observability"],"relationships":["D22-B58-002","D22-B58-004"],"provenance":["Derived autonomous-control verification principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B58-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Automation","concept":"Reliability policy canary","knowledge_type":"change_safety_pattern","question":"How can a new reliability policy be introduced without immediately applying it globally?","answer":"The policy can first operate in observation or limited scope, where its decisions and outcomes are evaluated before broader authority is granted.","explanation":"Reliability policies can contain subtle interaction defects. Progressive authority limits reduce blast radius while evidence is collected.","principle":"Changes to reliability control logic should have bounded blast radius during validation.","rule":"Introduce consequential policy changes progressively when the architecture permits safe scope restriction.","examples":[{"scenario":"A new adaptive load-shedding policy is introduced for one non-critical workload class.","result":"Behavior is observed before expanding its authority."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Deploying a new global recovery policy with unrestricted authority as the first production exposure.","reason":"A policy defect can affect the entire reliability control plane."}],"problem":"Reliability-control changes have excessive initial blast radius.","failure":"Global instability from one policy defect.","cause":"No progressive validation.","solution":"Use shadowing, scoped rollout, or controlled authority expansion.","alternatives":["Offline replay","Staging environment","Human approval"],"tradeoffs":"Progressive rollout delays full benefit.","constraints":["Initial scope must be genuinely isolated.","Rollback must be available."],"edge_cases":["Rare failure modes","Low-frequency traffic","Global incidents"],"security":"Policy rollout must preserve authorization and safety constraints.","performance":"Canary policy may require duplicate decision computation.","testing":"Compare old and new policy decisions under identical scenarios.","verification":"Expand authority only after defined evidence criteria are satisfied.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B35-003","D22-B58-003"],"prerequisites":["Progressive delivery","Policy versioning"],"relationships":["D22-B48-004","D22-B58-005"],"provenance":["Derived reliability change-safety principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.97,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B58-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Automation","concept":"Policy rollback versus state rollback","knowledge_type":"recovery_model","question":"Why should rollback of a reliability policy be distinguished from rollback of system state?","answer":"Reverting controller logic does not automatically restore the system state that the previous policy changed, and restoring state may itself require a separate safe operation.","explanation":"A policy change can trigger traffic movement, scaling, configuration, or data effects. Reverting the code does not necessarily undo those consequences.","principle":"Control-plane rollback and state recovery are separate operations.","rule":"Define how a policy rollback interacts with already-applied state changes and require explicit state reconciliation.","examples":[{"scenario":"New autoscaling policy increases capacity dramatically before being rolled back.","result":"Rollback of policy does not automatically remove or restore capacity state without a separate controlled process."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assuming reverting policy code returns the system to its previous state.","reason":"Prior policy actions may persist."}],"problem":"Rollback creates false expectation of state restoration.","failure":"Residual altered state continues affecting reliability.","cause":"Policy/state rollback conflation.","solution":"Separate policy version rollback from state reconciliation and outcome verification.","alternatives":["Forward correction","State reconciliation","Manual restoration"],"tradeoffs":"Two-stage rollback increases operational complexity.","constraints":["State correction must account for concurrent changes.","Rollback must not overwrite newer valid state."],"edge_cases":["Concurrent controller actions","Partial policy execution","External side effects"],"security":"Rollback authority must be separately controlled and audited.","performance":"State reconciliation can generate additional load.","testing":"Trigger policy actions, roll back policy, and verify residual state explicitly.","verification":"Verify policy rollback and state restoration are separately evidenced.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B46-003","D22-B58-004"],"prerequisites":["Policy management","State reconciliation"],"relationships":["D22-B47-004","D22-B56-002"],"provenance":["Derived reliability-control rollback principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B59-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Advanced Fault Injection","concept":"Fault injection hypothesis","knowledge_type":"testing_method","question":"What makes a fault-injection experiment useful for reliability engineering?","answer":"It begins with a specific hypothesis about system behavior under a defined failure, identifies expected safety and liveness properties, and collects evidence that can confirm or falsify the hypothesis.","explanation":"Randomly causing failures without a measurable hypothesis produces limited engineering knowledge. Experiments should target explicit failure-model assumptions.","principle":"Fault injection should test assumptions, not merely create chaos.","rule":"Define failure condition, expected behavior, affected scope, safety constraints, and measurable acceptance criteria before injection.","examples":[{"scenario":"Hypothesis: losing one dependency instance should not exhaust client resources.","result":"The experiment measures connection use, latency, retries, and recovery behavior."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Injecting arbitrary failures and recording only whether the system eventually recovered.","reason":"The experiment lacks defined expectations and boundaries."}],"problem":"Fault testing generates weak or ambiguous evidence.","failure":"Important reliability assumptions remain unvalidated.","cause":"No hypothesis-driven experiment design.","solution":"Use structured fault-injection experiments linked to reliability claims.","alternatives":["Chaos experiments","Failure-mode tests","Model-based fault injection"],"tradeoffs":"Structured experiments require planning and instrumentation.","constraints":["Experiments need controlled blast radius.","Production experiments require appropriate authorization."],"edge_cases":["Rare failure","Combined failures","Telemetry failure"],"security":"Fault experiments must not expose sensitive data or create uncontrolled security impact.","performance":"Fault injection should measure system behavior without overwhelming unrelated infrastructure.","testing":"Repeat experiments under representative load and compare observed behavior to hypothesis.","verification":"Record whether the hypothesis was supported, refuted, or remained unresolved.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B50-001","D22-B57-002"],"prerequisites":["Fault injection","Reliability hypotheses"],"relationships":["D22-B59-002","D22-B59-003"],"provenance":["Derived experimental reliability principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B59-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Advanced Fault Injection","concept":"Fault-injection blast-radius guard","knowledge_type":"testing_safety","question":"Why must fault-injection experiments have explicit blast-radius controls?","answer":"A test intended to validate one failure mode can become a broader outage if the injected fault propagates beyond the experimental boundary.","explanation":"Blast-radius controls can include workload selection, traffic limits, cell boundaries, abort conditions, time limits, and immediate restoration procedures.","principle":"A reliability experiment must itself be engineered for failure containment.","rule":"Define and technically enforce maximum experiment scope before injecting a fault.","examples":[{"scenario":"A dependency failure is injected for one isolated cell.","result":"Traffic and recovery controls prevent the experiment from affecting other cells."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Injecting a shared production dependency failure without isolation or automatic abort.","reason":"The experiment can become a fleet-wide outage."}],"problem":"Reliability testing creates uncontrolled operational risk.","failure":"Experiment exceeds intended scope.","cause":"Insufficient experiment guardrails.","solution":"Use bounded scope, kill switches, rate limits, and precondition checks.","alternatives":["Staging injection","Simulation","Shadow experiment"],"tradeoffs":"Strong isolation can reduce realism.","constraints":["Abort controls must remain independent of the injected component where possible.","Authorization must cover the experiment scope."],"edge_cases":["Control-plane failure","Shared dependency","Monitoring loss"],"security":"Fault injection must be authorized and auditable.","performance":"Experiment load must remain within predefined limits.","testing":"Test the experiment's abort mechanism independently before real fault injection.","verification":"Verify experiment impact remains within declared boundaries.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B41-005","D22-B59-001"],"prerequisites":["Fault injection","Safety controls"],"relationships":["D22-B59-004","D22-B60-001"],"provenance":["Derived safe-experiment principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B59-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Advanced Fault Injection","concept":"Failure-sequence testing","knowledge_type":"testing_method","question":"Why can sequences of failures reveal reliability defects that isolated fault tests miss?","answer":"Recovery from one failure can change state, capacity, routing, or data conditions so that a subsequent failure has a different and potentially more severe effect.","explanation":"Real incidents can involve failure chains such as dependency outage followed by recovery surge, node loss during failover, or configuration change during degraded operation.","principle":"Reliability must be tested across meaningful temporal failure sequences, not only isolated events.","rule":"Test high-risk combinations where the first failure changes the conditions under which the second failure occurs.","examples":[{"scenario":"Region fails, traffic moves, then the surviving region loses capacity.","result":"The sequence tests whether failover creates a fragile secondary state."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Testing each failure independently and assuming combined behavior is equivalent.","reason":"State and capacity after the first failure can change the second failure's effect."}],"problem":"Temporal failure interactions remain untested.","failure":"Second failure causes disproportionate outage.","cause":"Single-fault testing only.","solution":"Model and test bounded failure sequences.","alternatives":["Stateful chaos testing","Scenario simulation","Model-based testing"],"tradeoffs":"Failure-sequence space can become large.","constraints":["Prioritize sequences by impact and plausibility.","Keep experiment scope bounded."],"edge_cases":["Recovery during second failure","Repeated flapping","Changing workload"],"security":"Security-induced failures should be tested where they affect reliability transitions.","performance":"Sequence tests should measure resource state between failures.","testing":"Inject controlled failure A, verify state transition, then inject failure B.","verification":"Verify sequence-specific invariants and recovery outcomes.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B59-001","D22-B49-002"],"prerequisites":["Stateful testing","Failure modeling"],"relationships":["D22-B59-004","D22-B60-002"],"provenance":["Derived stateful reliability testing principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B59-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Advanced Fault Injection","concept":"Recovery drill realism","knowledge_type":"testing_rule","question":"What determines whether a recovery drill provides meaningful reliability evidence?","answer":"The drill should exercise representative dependencies, data volumes, capacity constraints, authorization paths, operational procedures, and verification steps rather than only a simplified component restart.","explanation":"Simplified drills are useful for specific components but cannot automatically establish end-to-end disaster recovery capability.","principle":"Recovery evidence is bounded by the realism and scope of the recovery exercise.","rule":"State exactly which production assumptions a drill represents and which it does not.","examples":[{"scenario":"A regional recovery drill uses representative data and constrained destination capacity.","result":"Evidence covers more of the actual disaster scenario than a simple application restart."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Calling a local process restart a full disaster-recovery validation.","reason":"The tested scope is far smaller than regional recovery."}],"problem":"Recovery evidence is overstated.","failure":"Unpreparedness during real disaster.","cause":"Scope mismatch between drill and claim.","solution":"Record drill scope, assumptions, deviations, and uncovered dependencies.","alternatives":["Full-scale disaster exercise","Component recovery test","Tabletop exercise"],"tradeoffs":"Higher realism increases cost and operational risk.","constraints":["Drill scope must be explicit.","Sensitive production data requires appropriate controls."],"edge_cases":["Reduced disaster capacity","Third-party unavailable","Manual authorization"],"security":"Recovery drills must preserve production security and privacy requirements.","performance":"Realistic data and capacity are important for recovery-time evidence.","testing":"Compare drill conditions against declared recovery claims.","verification":"Do not extend drill conclusions beyond the tested scenario.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B53-003","D22-B52-005"],"prerequisites":["Disaster recovery","Exercise design"],"relationships":["D22-B59-005","D22-B60-003"],"provenance":["Derived recovery-drill evidence principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B59-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Advanced Fault Injection","concept":"Fault experiment stopping criteria","knowledge_type":"testing_safety","question":"When should a reliability experiment stop even if its original hypothesis has not been tested completely?","answer":"It should stop when predefined safety limits are exceeded, unexpected propagation occurs, observability becomes insufficient, required assumptions become invalid, or the experiment threatens protected workloads.","explanation":"Experiment completion is subordinate to system safety. Stopping early is valid evidence that the experiment encountered an unsafe boundary.","principle":"Safety limits override experimental completeness.","rule":"Define measurable stop conditions before executing high-impact fault experiments.","examples":[{"scenario":"Latency exceeds the experiment's protected threshold outside the target cell.","result":"The experiment aborts even though the planned duration has not elapsed."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Continuing a fault experiment because the planned duration has not finished despite blast-radius violation.","reason":"Experiment scope has already become unsafe."}],"problem":"Fault testing prioritizes experimental completion over production safety.","failure":"Experiment becomes an outage.","cause":"No stopping criteria.","solution":"Use independent abort thresholds and tested kill mechanisms.","alternatives":["Automatic rollback","Operator stop","Experiment controller"],"tradeoffs":"Early stopping can reduce evidence completeness.","constraints":["Stopping mechanisms must remain available if the tested component fails.","Thresholds should be conservative for critical workloads."],"edge_cases":["Telemetry failure","Control-plane loss","Unexpected propagation"],"security":"Experiment authorization must include explicit stop authority.","performance":"Stop controls should operate with low latency.","testing":"Independently test stop mechanisms before experiment execution.","verification":"Verify every critical experiment has measurable and enforceable stopping criteria.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B59-002"],"prerequisites":["Fault injection","Safety engineering"],"relationships":["D22-B59-001","D22-B60-001"],"provenance":["Derived safe-experiment principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B60-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Verification","concept":"End-to-end resilience verification","knowledge_type":"verification_framework","question":"What distinguishes end-to-end resilience verification from component-level testing?","answer":"End-to-end verification evaluates whether the complete system preserves required properties across dependency failures, overload, degraded modes, recovery, and post-recovery convergence.","explanation":"Individual components can pass resilience tests while their interactions still permit cascading failure. End-to-end verification evaluates the actual dependency and control chain.","principle":"System-level resilience must be verified at the level where failure consequences occur.","rule":"For critical scenarios, include integrated failure injection and outcome verification across the full dependency path.","examples":[{"scenario":"Dependency failure triggers retry, queue growth, fallback, and recovery.","result":"Verification evaluates the combined behavior rather than each mechanism separately."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Concluding end-to-end resilience from isolated unit tests of retry and circuit-breaker classes.","reason":"Interaction behavior remains unverified."}],"problem":"Component tests provide false confidence about system resilience.","failure":"Integrated failure causes unexpected cascade.","cause":"No end-to-end failure verification.","solution":"Combine unit, integration, scenario, fault-injection, and recovery validation.","alternatives":["System chaos testing","Digital-twin simulation","Model-based verification"],"tradeoffs":"End-to-end tests are more expensive and environment dependent.","constraints":["Test environment must represent relevant failure assumptions.","Production experiments require strict controls."],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous failures","External provider outage","Data corruption"],"security":"End-to-end tests must include critical security dependencies where relevant.","performance":"System-level tests should include realistic workload and resource constraints.","testing":"Execute complete failure-to-recovery scenarios and validate invariants.","verification":"Require scenario-specific outcome evidence before declaring the resilience property verified.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B48-002","D22-B59-003"],"prerequisites":["Integration testing","Fault injection"],"relationships":["D22-B60-002","D22-B60-004"],"provenance":["Derived end-to-end reliability verification principle"],"evidence":"reliability_practice","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B60-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Verification","concept":"Recovery invariant verification","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"How should recovery invariants be verified across a failure sequence?","answer":"Assertions should be evaluated at relevant pre-failure, failure, degraded, recovery, and post-recovery states rather than only at final steady state.","explanation":"A system can reach a correct final state after temporarily violating a critical invariant. For safety properties, the intermediate violation may itself be unacceptable.","principle":"Critical invariants must hold throughout the transition scope in which they are defined.","rule":"Instrument or inspect invariant state at meaningful transition boundaries during recovery testing.","examples":[{"scenario":"Ownership must remain unique during failover.","result":"The invariant is checked before, during, and after ownership transition."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Checking only final ownership after failover.","reason":"A temporary dual-owner state could already have caused corruption."}],"problem":"Transient unsafe states remain invisible.","failure":"Temporary invariant violation causes durable damage.","cause":"Final-state-only verification.","solution":"Verify safety invariants throughout transitions.","alternatives":["Runtime assertions","Model checking","State transition logs"],"tradeoffs":"Intermediate verification increases instrumentation and test complexity.","constraints":["Sampling must be sufficient for the invariant's timing requirements.","Some internal state may not be externally observable."],"edge_cases":["Fast transitions","Concurrent actions","Telemetry delay"],"security":"Security invariants require similarly strong transition monitoring.","performance":"Runtime invariant checks should be targeted to critical properties.","testing":"Inject timing races around transition boundaries.","verification":"Demonstrate that defined safety invariants hold across every tested transition.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B49-002","D22-B60-001"],"prerequisites":["Invariants","State transitions"],"relationships":["D22-B49-003","D22-B60-003"],"provenance":["Derived formal reliability verification principle"],"evidence":"research_informed_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B60-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Verification","concept":"Recovery verification independence","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"Why is independent recovery verification valuable?","answer":"The same component that performs recovery can incorrectly report success due to shared bugs, stale state, or correlated failures. Independent evidence reduces this risk.","explanation":"Verification should, where practical, use a different signal, observer, or measurement path from the mechanism being verified.","principle":"Critical claims are stronger when verification is not dependent on the same failure mechanism.","rule":"For high-impact recovery, obtain outcome evidence from an independent or materially different observation path when feasible.","examples":[{"scenario":"Routing controller performs failover.","result":"A separate synthetic transaction verifies that user-facing operations succeed."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Controller verifies its own success solely by reading its last command result.","reason":"The same failure could make the verification misleading."}],"problem":"Recovery reports success based on correlated evidence.","failure":"False recovery declaration.","cause":"Non-independent verification.","solution":"Use independent health, business outcome, or external observation where appropriate.","alternatives":["Synthetic monitoring","Independent observer","External validation"],"tradeoffs":"Independent verification adds infrastructure and telemetry cost.","constraints":["Observer must have appropriate access.","Independence is relative and should be documented."],"edge_cases":["Global monitoring outage","Shared dependency","Telemetry partition"],"security":"Independent probes must remain authorized and privacy-preserving.","performance":"Verification traffic must be bounded during recovery.","testing":"Corrupt the recovery mechanism's own status signal and verify independent observation detects the discrepancy.","verification":"Document the independence assumptions of critical recovery evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B46-003","D22-B59-004"],"prerequisites":["Outcome verification","Observability"],"relationships":["D22-B60-004","D22-B50-004"],"provenance":["Derived independent-verification principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.98,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B60-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Verification","concept":"Reliability verification evidence hierarchy","knowledge_type":"evidence_model","question":"How should reliability evidence types be distinguished in an autonomous engineering knowledge system?","answer":"Evidence should distinguish design intent, documented guarantee, static analysis, simulation, controlled test, fault injection, production observation, verified recovery outcome, and inference, because each supports different levels and scopes of reliability claims.","explanation":"Collapsing these evidence classes into a single verified flag causes the system to overstate what has actually been demonstrated.","principle":"Evidence strength depends on method, scope, environment, and correspondence to the claimed property.","rule":"Store evidence type, execution status, scope, version, conditions, and limitations for material reliability claims.","examples":[{"scenario":"A failover design is documented but has not been executed.","result":"It is represented as design evidence, not successful failover evidence."},{"scenario":"A controlled failover test passes under a defined workload.","result":"It is recorded as executed test evidence scoped to those conditions."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Converting a planned test into a verified result.","reason":"No execution evidence exists."}],"problem":"Reliability knowledge overstates evidence strength.","failure":"Autonomous system makes decisions using unsupported claims.","cause":"Evidence-type collapse.","solution":"Use structured evidence classes with explicit validation state.","alternatives":["Assurance-case evidence","Evidence graph","Verification registry"],"tradeoffs":"More metadata improves trustworthiness but increases dataset complexity.","constraints":["No fabricated execution results.","Evidence scope and limitations must be preserved.","Historical evidence must remain versioned."],"edge_cases":["Conflicting tests","Simulation-only evidence","Third-party guarantees"],"security":"Evidence artifacts require access control and provenance integrity.","performance":"Evidence indexing should support retrieval by scope and strength.","testing":"Audit representative records for correct evidence classification.","verification":"Verify no record claims stronger evidence than its provenance supports.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B48-005","D22-B50-004","D22-B57-005"],"prerequisites":["Evidence provenance","Verification taxonomy"],"relationships":["D22-B60-005","D22-B49-001"],"provenance":["Derived evidence-driven dataset principle"],"evidence":"derived_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B60-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Verification","concept":"Reliability knowledge lock audit","knowledge_type":"dataset_verification_rule","question":"What should be verified before the D22 reliability knowledge phase is considered ready for downstream autonomous retrieval?","answer":"The phase should pass schema validation, semantic deduplication, relationship integrity, scope coverage analysis, provenance review, evidence-status review, version-scope review, contradiction handling, and machine-retrieval consistency checks.","explanation":"Downstream autonomous reasoning depends on reliable knowledge boundaries. A dataset containing duplicated, unsupported, stale, or incorrectly classified reliability claims can propagate errors into design and recovery decisions.","principle":"Knowledge-fabric reliability requires the same evidence discipline expected from the systems it describes.","rule":"Do not lock D22 merely because a target record count has been reached; lock only after defined coverage and quality checks pass.","examples":[{"scenario":"Two records express the same rule with different wording.","result":"They are semantically deduplicated."},{"scenario":"A recovery claim has design evidence but no execution evidence.","result":"The record retains the weaker evidence classification."},{"scenario":"Two tests conflict under different workloads.","result":"Both remain with scope and contradiction metadata."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Adding records solely to increase dataset size.","reason":"Record count is not a quality or coverage metric."},{"pattern":"Marking every record as verified because it passed schema validation.","reason":"Schema correctness does not establish factual or empirical verification."}],"problem":"Reliability knowledge can become large but untrustworthy.","failure":"Autonomous coding/reliability reasoning retrieves unsupported or contradictory guidance as fact.","cause":"Count-driven generation and weak evidence governance.","solution":"Apply coverage-driven generation, semantic deduplication, evidence classification, contradiction preservation, provenance, versioning, and validation-state separation.","alternatives":["Assurance graph","Ontology coverage audit","Evidence registry"],"tradeoffs":"Strict quality controls may produce fewer records but increase information density and trustworthiness.","constraints":["English-only machine-readable data.","Stable record IDs.","No fabricated facts or execution results.","No unsupported benchmarks.","No duplicate concepts disguised by wording."],"edge_cases":["Version-specific behavior","Contradictory authoritative sources","Stale evidence","Cross-dataset overlap"],"security":"Dataset must not contain secrets or unnecessary sensitive infrastructure information.","performance":"Retrieval should prioritize scoped, evidence-rich records and relationships.","testing":"Run schema, duplicate, relationship, provenance, contradiction, and coverage tests.","verification":"Require explicit lock evidence showing that D22's declared reliability scope has no material unresolved knowledge gap within the phase boundary.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["D22-B48-004","D22-B50-004","D22-B60-004"],"prerequisites":["Dataset QA","Coverage analysis","Deduplication"],"relationships":["D22-B60-001","D22-B60-004"],"provenance":["User-defined quality-first dataset-generation requirements plus derived reliability-dataset assurance principle"],"evidence":"derived_dataset_engineering_principle","confidence":0.99,"validation":"SEMANTIC_REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}

{"record_id":"D22-B61-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Chaos Engineering","concept":"Controlled fault injection","knowledge_type":"reliability_principle","question":"How should fault injection be used to validate system resilience?","answer":"Inject controlled failures into selected components or dependency paths, observe whether defined reliability invariants remain true, and stop or contain the experiment when safety boundaries are exceeded.","explanation":"Fault injection validates actual failure behavior rather than assuming that recovery mechanisms work because they exist in code. Experiments should have explicit hypotheses, blast-radius limits, observability requirements, abort conditions, and expected recovery behavior.","principle":"Resilience must be demonstrated under representative failure conditions, not inferred solely from design intent.","rule":"Never run a fault experiment without a bounded scope, measurable hypothesis, safety guard, and recovery criterion.","examples":[{"scenario":"Inject dependency timeout into a non-production test environment.","expected_behavior":"Requests fail within bounded deadlines and the service remains responsive."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Randomly terminate production components without a defined hypothesis.","why_invalid":"The experiment lacks controlled scope and measurable safety criteria."}],"problem":"Recovery mechanisms may exist but remain unverified.","failure":"Unexpected cascading failure during injected faults.","cause":"Missing fault boundaries, observability, or recovery controls.","solution":"Use controlled experiments with explicit hypotheses, abort thresholds, and post-experiment verification.","alternatives":["Failure-mode simulation","Deterministic integration tests","Staged resilience drills"],"tradeoffs":["Higher confidence requires realistic experiments but increases operational risk.","Small blast radius reduces risk but may miss systemic failures."],"constraints":["Experiments must respect authorization and safety boundaries.","Critical production actions require explicit governance."],"edge_cases":["The injected fault interacts with an unrelated active incident.","Observability itself fails during the experiment.","Recovery completes but leaves corrupted state."],"security":["Restrict experiment permissions.","Prevent fault injection from crossing tenant or environment boundaries."],"performance":["Measure latency and saturation during the experiment.","Avoid experiments that themselves create uncontrolled load."],"testing":["Define baseline, fault condition, expected invariant, and recovery condition.","Repeat experiments under different dependency conditions."],"verification":["Verify service invariants before, during, and after injection.","Confirm state convergence after recovery."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Observability","Fault isolation","Recovery mechanisms"],"prerequisites":["Failure model","Defined reliability invariants"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B62-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_practice","source":"Google SRE; Chaos Engineering literature","basis":"Controlled failure experiments are used to validate resilience assumptions."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Controlled resilience validation"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B61-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Chaos Engineering","concept":"Experiment hypothesis design","knowledge_type":"method","question":"What makes a chaos experiment useful for reliability engineering?","answer":"A useful experiment states a falsifiable reliability hypothesis, identifies the failure stimulus, defines expected system invariants, specifies measurable signals, limits blast radius, and defines recovery and abort criteria.","explanation":"Without a hypothesis, fault injection becomes random disruption rather than engineering evidence. The hypothesis should describe what the system is expected to preserve despite the injected failure.","principle":"Every resilience experiment should test a specific reliability claim.","rule":"Do not classify an experiment as successful merely because the process completed; verify the predicted system behavior.","examples":[{"scenario":"Hypothesis: loss of one stateless application instance does not violate the availability objective.","expected_behavior":"Traffic is redistributed and service remains within defined latency and availability bounds."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"The system should handle failures.","why_invalid":"The statement is too vague to falsify or measure."}],"problem":"Experiments generate disruption without actionable evidence.","failure":"False confidence after an unmeasured experiment.","cause":"No explicit hypothesis or acceptance criteria.","solution":"Define measurable preconditions, failure stimulus, invariants, metrics, and recovery criteria.","alternatives":["Scenario-based resilience testing","Model-based simulation"],"tradeoffs":["More precise hypotheses require more instrumentation and preparation."],"constraints":["Metrics must distinguish experiment effects from unrelated traffic."],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous faults obscure causal interpretation.","The baseline is already unhealthy."],"security":["Experiment authorization must be independently auditable."],"performance":["Capture resource saturation and tail latency, not only averages."],"testing":["Run baseline before injection and compare against fault-state measurements."],"verification":["Store experiment configuration and observed evidence."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Observability","Fault injection"],"prerequisites":["Reliability objectives"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B61-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_practice","source":"Chaos Engineering methodology","basis":"Experiments are hypothesis-driven and evaluated against expected steady-state behavior."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Chaos experiment methodology"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B61-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Chaos Engineering","concept":"Blast-radius control","knowledge_type":"safety_control","question":"How should the blast radius of a reliability experiment be controlled?","answer":"Limit affected instances, tenants, regions, traffic percentages, dependency scopes, duration, and permissions, and provide automatic termination when predefined safety thresholds are exceeded.","explanation":"A resilience experiment is itself a failure source. Bounding its impact prevents validation activity from becoming an uncontrolled outage.","principle":"Failure injection must be more controlled than the failure it is intended to simulate.","rule":"Prefer the smallest scope capable of testing the hypothesis and expand only after evidence supports expansion.","examples":[{"scenario":"Terminate one instance in a five-instance test deployment.","expected_behavior":"Only the selected instance is affected and the remaining instances absorb traffic."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Inject the same fault across every production replica immediately.","why_invalid":"The experiment removes the redundancy being tested and creates unnecessary outage risk."}],"problem":"Testing introduces excessive collateral impact.","failure":"Experiment-induced service outage.","cause":"Unbounded fault scope or missing abort controls.","solution":"Use staged blast-radius expansion and automated safety thresholds.","alternatives":["Synthetic environments","Shadow experiments","Canary fault injection"],"tradeoffs":["Smaller experiments provide safer evidence but may fail to expose systemic interactions."],"constraints":["Tenant isolation","Change governance","Production safety requirements"],"edge_cases":["Autoscaling immediately replaces failed instances.","The experiment affects shared infrastructure."],"security":["Use least-privilege experiment credentials.","Prevent arbitrary targeting of protected systems."],"performance":["Monitor induced load and resource consumption."],"testing":["Validate abort controls before increasing blast radius."],"verification":["Confirm only intended resources were affected."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Experiment controller","Monitoring"],"prerequisites":["Asset inventory","Dependency topology"],"relationships":[{"relation":"constrains","record_id":"D22-B61-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_practice","source":"Chaos Engineering practice","basis":"Experiments should be scoped and controlled to minimize unintended impact."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Experiment safety"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B61-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Chaos Engineering","concept":"Steady-state measurement","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"Why is a healthy baseline required before fault injection?","answer":"A baseline establishes the normal operating range against which degradation and recovery can be distinguished from pre-existing instability.","explanation":"Without a trustworthy baseline, an experiment cannot reliably attribute observed changes to the injected failure. Baseline measurements should cover the same reliability indicators used during the experiment.","principle":"Fault impact must be compared against a known operating state.","rule":"Do not declare experiment results valid when the pre-experiment system is already outside its accepted reliability envelope.","examples":[{"scenario":"Measure latency, error rate, saturation, and queue depth before dependency failure injection.","expected_behavior":"Post-fault measurements can be compared against the baseline."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Start the experiment during an unrelated severe outage.","why_invalid":"Causal attribution becomes unreliable."}],"problem":"Fault effects cannot be separated from existing instability.","failure":"Incorrect resilience conclusion.","cause":"Missing or invalid baseline.","solution":"Establish a stable baseline and record its evidence before injection.","alternatives":["Controlled replay","Dedicated test environment"],"tradeoffs":["Waiting for a stable baseline delays experiments but improves evidence quality."],"constraints":["Baseline must represent the intended workload."],"edge_cases":["Traffic is naturally highly variable.","Seasonal workloads alter normal ranges."],"security":["Baseline data must not expose sensitive tenant information."],"performance":["Use tail metrics and saturation indicators."],"testing":["Repeat baseline collection to establish variability."],"verification":["Mark baseline validity explicitly in experiment records."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Metrics","Tracing","Logging"],"prerequisites":["Defined reliability indicators"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B61-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_practice","source":"SRE reliability testing practice","basis":"Steady-state behavior is required to evaluate resilience experiments."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Experiment baseline"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B61-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Chaos Engineering","concept":"Recovery evidence","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"What evidence is required to claim successful recovery after a resilience experiment?","answer":"Evidence should show that the failure condition ended, service behavior returned within defined objectives, critical state converged correctly, queued or in-flight work was handled according to policy, and no delayed failure remained.","explanation":"Immediate metric recovery is insufficient when corrupted state, duplicate work, leaked resources, or latent queue failures remain.","principle":"Recovery means restoration of correct behavior and state, not merely restoration of process availability.","rule":"Do not mark an experiment recovered until functional and state-level recovery checks pass.","examples":[{"scenario":"A dependency becomes unavailable and later recovers.","expected_behavior":"Traffic resumes without duplicated side effects and application state remains consistent."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"CPU returned to normal, therefore recovery succeeded.","why_invalid":"Resource recovery does not prove functional or state correctness."}],"problem":"Systems appear healthy while hidden recovery defects remain.","failure":"Delayed post-recovery outage or data inconsistency.","cause":"Recovery verification limited to infrastructure metrics.","solution":"Verify functional, state, queue, resource, and dependency recovery.","alternatives":["Recovery checklist","Automated post-fault invariant checker"],"tradeoffs":["Deeper verification costs more instrumentation and execution time."],"constraints":["Recovery checks must be idempotent and safe."],"edge_cases":["Messages remain delayed after dependency recovery.","A partially completed transaction requires compensation."],"security":["Recovery must not bypass authorization checks."],"performance":["Verify resource cleanup and connection recovery."],"testing":["Include delayed-failure checks after apparent recovery."],"verification":["Require explicit recovery evidence before closure."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["State verification","Observability"],"prerequisites":["Defined recovery invariants"],"relationships":[{"relation":"verifies","record_id":"D22-B61-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_practice","source":"Reliability and recovery testing practice","basis":"Recovery validation must include functional correctness and system state."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Recovery validation"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B62-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Graceful Shutdown","concept":"Termination protocol","knowledge_type":"lifecycle_principle","question":"What must a reliable service do during shutdown?","answer":"Stop accepting new work, allow or explicitly terminate in-flight work according to policy, release resources, preserve required state, and signal readiness changes before termination where the platform requires propagation time.","explanation":"Abrupt termination can produce dropped requests, partial writes, leaked resources, or inconsistent state. Graceful shutdown converts termination into a controlled lifecycle transition.","principle":"Shutdown is a reliability state transition, not merely process termination.","rule":"Define explicit behavior for new work, in-flight work, background tasks, connections, and durable state during shutdown.","examples":[{"scenario":"Service receives a termination signal.","expected_behavior":"Readiness is withdrawn, new requests stop, active requests receive bounded completion time, and resources are released."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Immediately kill the process whenever shutdown begins.","why_invalid":"In-flight work and resource cleanup receive no controlled handling."}],"problem":"Service termination causes avoidable request and state loss.","failure":"Partial operations or dropped work.","cause":"No shutdown lifecycle policy.","solution":"Implement bounded graceful termination with explicit work-handling rules.","alternatives":["Immediate termination for disposable stateless workers","Checkpoint-and-restart"],"tradeoffs":["Graceful shutdown increases termination latency.","Long grace periods can delay recovery from unhealthy processes."],"constraints":["Shutdown time must remain bounded.","Stateful operations require stronger recovery handling."],"edge_cases":["Shutdown occurs during an external transaction.","Multiple termination signals arrive.","Dependency becomes unavailable during shutdown."],"security":["Shutdown must not bypass durable authorization or audit requirements."],"performance":["Bound graceful-drain duration."],"testing":["Test termination during idle, active, blocked, and state-changing operations."],"verification":["Verify no prohibited new work is accepted after shutdown begins."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Process lifecycle","Request management"],"prerequisites":["Defined termination policy"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B62-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B63-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_practice","source":"Reliable service lifecycle design","basis":"Controlled shutdown reduces dropped work and resource leakage."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Graceful termination"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B62-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Graceful Shutdown","concept":"In-flight work draining","knowledge_type":"implementation_rule","question":"How should in-flight work be handled during controlled shutdown?","answer":"Track active work, prevent new work from entering the draining instance, allow safe operations to complete within a deadline, and explicitly cancel or recover operations that exceed the deadline.","explanation":"Unbounded draining can prevent termination while immediate cancellation can lose work. A bounded policy must distinguish work that can safely complete from work requiring recovery.","principle":"Graceful draining must be bounded and state-aware.","rule":"Every in-flight operation must have a defined completion, cancellation, timeout, or recovery path.","examples":[{"scenario":"An HTTP request is processing during shutdown.","expected_behavior":"The request either completes within the drain deadline or is terminated in a way that permits safe retry/recovery."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Wait indefinitely for every active operation.","why_invalid":"A single stuck operation can block termination indefinitely."}],"problem":"Shutdown hangs or causes inconsistent work handling.","failure":"Zombie processes or lost operations.","cause":"Unbounded drain or undefined cancellation semantics.","solution":"Use operation deadlines and explicit recovery semantics.","alternatives":["Immediate cancellation with durable queue recovery"],"tradeoffs":["Longer drain improves completion probability but delays replacement.","Shorter drain improves restart speed but increases recovery workload."],"constraints":["Cancellation must be safe for the operation type."],"edge_cases":["Non-idempotent external side effects are partially completed.","Work depends on a dependency that is already down."],"security":["Do not leave privileged operations running beyond authorization lifetime."],"performance":["Track drain time and active-work count."],"testing":["Inject shutdown during every major operation stage."],"verification":["Confirm all work is either completed, durably recorded, or explicitly recoverable."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Deadline management","Work tracking"],"prerequisites":["Operation lifecycle model"],"relationships":[{"relation":"implements","record_id":"D22-B62-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B62-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_practice","source":"Reliable distributed service lifecycle design","basis":"In-flight work requires bounded completion and recovery semantics."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Shutdown draining"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B62-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Restart Reliability","concept":"Startup readiness versus process liveness","knowledge_type":"reliability_principle","question":"Why should process liveness and service readiness be treated separately?","answer":"A process may be alive while unable to safely serve traffic because initialization, dependency synchronization, state recovery, or internal startup work is incomplete.","explanation":"Conflating process existence with service readiness can route traffic to instances that cannot correctly handle requests.","principle":"Being alive is not equivalent to being ready.","rule":"Expose readiness based on service capability and liveness based on process health when the runtime supports separate concepts.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service starts while rebuilding required local state.","expected_behavior":"It remains alive but does not receive normal traffic until required state is ready."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declare readiness immediately after process creation.","why_invalid":"Initialization may still be incomplete."}],"problem":"Traffic reaches partially initialized instances.","failure":"Startup-induced errors or inconsistent behavior.","cause":"Readiness derived only from process existence.","solution":"Separate liveness and readiness criteria.","alternatives":["Startup state machine","Explicit registration after initialization"],"tradeoffs":["More state increases lifecycle complexity but improves routing correctness."],"constraints":["Readiness checks must not create excessive dependency load."],"edge_cases":["Dependency is temporarily unavailable during startup.","Recovery requires rebuilding large state."],"security":["Do not expose sensitive initialization diagnostics through public health interfaces."],"performance":["Keep readiness evaluation bounded."],"testing":["Test traffic arrival at every startup stage."],"verification":["Verify traffic is withheld until all required readiness invariants pass."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Health monitoring","Service discovery"],"prerequisites":["Lifecycle state model"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B62-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B62-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Microsoft Azure Architecture Center; Kubernetes health-check concepts","basis":"Readiness and liveness represent different operational conditions."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"official_documentation","claim_scope":"Service lifecycle health signaling"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B62-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Restart Reliability","concept":"Recovery-aware startup","knowledge_type":"recovery_principle","question":"How should startup behave after an unclean termination?","answer":"Startup should detect recoverable incomplete state, replay or repair only according to defined recovery rules, validate resulting state, and remain unavailable until recovery invariants are satisfied.","explanation":"Restarting the process does not automatically restore correctness. Durable logs, checkpoints, transactional state, and recovery markers may need reconciliation before normal traffic resumes.","principle":"Restart is a recovery operation when termination may have interrupted state transitions.","rule":"Never assume clean shutdown semantics after an abnormal termination.","examples":[{"scenario":"Process terminates during durable state update.","expected_behavior":"Startup detects incomplete state and performs the defined recovery procedure before accepting traffic."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Ignore recovery markers because the process restarted successfully.","why_invalid":"Process availability does not prove state correctness."}],"problem":"Abnormal restart exposes partially applied state.","failure":"Corrupt or inconsistent runtime state.","cause":"No recovery-aware initialization.","solution":"Implement startup recovery and post-recovery verification.","alternatives":["Write-ahead logging","Atomic state replacement","Checkpoint restoration"],"tradeoffs":["Recovery-aware startup increases startup time.","Skipping recovery reduces startup latency but risks state corruption."],"constraints":["Recovery must be deterministic or explicitly convergent."],"edge_cases":["Recovery itself fails.","Multiple incomplete operations exist."],"security":["Validate recovered state before enabling privileged operations."],"performance":["Bound recovery work or expose recovery progress."],"testing":["Terminate the system at multiple state-transition points and restart it."],"verification":["Compare recovered state against invariants and durable evidence."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Durable state","Recovery mechanism"],"prerequisites":["State transition model"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B62-003"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_practice","source":"Reliable stateful-service recovery practice","basis":"Unclean termination can require state recovery before service readiness."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Restart recovery"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B62-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Shutdown Reliability","concept":"Exactly-once shutdown side effects","knowledge_type":"correctness_constraint","question":"How can shutdown avoid duplicate external side effects?","answer":"Use durable operation state, idempotency controls, transactional boundaries where available, and recovery procedures that distinguish completed effects from unfinished effects.","explanation":"A process can terminate after an external side effect succeeds but before local completion state is recorded. Blindly retrying after restart can duplicate the side effect.","principle":"Shutdown correctness depends on recovering the relationship between local state and external effects.","rule":"External side effects must have explicit duplicate-prevention or compensation semantics.","examples":[{"scenario":"Payment request succeeds remotely but local process terminates before recording completion.","expected_behavior":"Recovery reconciles the external operation before issuing another side effect."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Always retry every unfinished operation after restart.","why_invalid":"The external effect may already have succeeded."}],"problem":"Crash windows create ambiguous operation outcomes.","failure":"Duplicate external side effect.","cause":"Unresolved local-versus-external commit boundary.","solution":"Use idempotency keys, reconciliation, transactional coordination where supported, or compensation.","alternatives":["Transactional outbox","Saga-style compensation","Provider-side idempotency"],"tradeoffs":["Stronger guarantees require coordination and durable state.","Compensation may not perfectly reverse irreversible effects."],"constraints":["External system capabilities limit achievable guarantees."],"edge_cases":["External system is unavailable during reconciliation.","Provider loses historical operation visibility."],"security":["Protect idempotency identifiers and operation records."],"performance":["Reconciliation should be bounded and asynchronous where appropriate."],"testing":["Inject termination at every side-effect boundary."],"verification":["Prove recovery distinguishes completed from incomplete operations."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Durable operation state","External API semantics"],"prerequisites":["Side-effect model"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B62-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B64-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"distributed_systems_practice","source":"Transactional messaging and distributed transaction literature","basis":"Crash windows require explicit handling of ambiguous external effects."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_distributed_systems_practice","claim_scope":"Crash-safe side effects"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B63-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Leader Coordination","concept":"Leader election safety","knowledge_type":"distributed_system_principle","question":"What reliability property must leader election preserve?","answer":"At most one authority should be able to perform operations requiring exclusive leadership within the same logical scope at a given time, subject to the coordination system's defined guarantees.","explanation":"Leader election is not merely selecting a preferred instance. It establishes authority for operations that may be unsafe when performed concurrently by multiple actors.","principle":"Leadership must be tied to an enforceable authority mechanism, not merely a local variable.","rule":"A node must not perform exclusive leader actions unless its leadership authority is currently valid.","examples":[{"scenario":"Two service instances attempt to become leader.","expected_behavior":"The coordination mechanism grants exclusive leadership according to its consistency guarantees."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Each process sets local is_leader=true when it starts.","why_invalid":"No distributed exclusivity exists."}],"problem":"Multiple instances perform mutually exclusive work.","failure":"Duplicate processing or conflicting state transitions.","cause":"Non-authoritative leader state.","solution":"Use a coordination mechanism with explicit ownership and validity semantics.","alternatives":["Partitioned work ownership","Consensus-backed coordination"],"tradeoffs":["Strong coordination adds latency and operational dependencies."],"constraints":["Leadership scope and lease semantics must be explicit."],"edge_cases":["Network partition separates leader from coordinator.","Old leader resumes after losing authority."],"security":["Leader credentials must be scoped to leadership authority."],"performance":["Avoid excessive election churn."],"testing":["Inject coordinator failures and network partitions."],"verification":["Prove stale leaders cannot continue protected actions."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Coordination service"],"prerequisites":["Distributed authority model"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B63-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B63-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"distributed_systems_practice","source":"Consensus and distributed coordination literature","basis":"Exclusive authority requires distributed coordination with defined safety guarantees."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_distributed_systems_practice","claim_scope":"Leader election"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B63-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Leader Coordination","concept":"Lease expiration safety","knowledge_type":"distributed_system_rule","question":"Why must a leader stop protected work when its lease expires?","answer":"A lease represents time-bounded authority. After expiry, the process cannot safely assume that another actor has not acquired the same authority.","explanation":"Local clocks, network delays, pauses, and coordinator decisions can make continued use of expired authority unsafe.","principle":"Authority lifetime must constrain action lifetime.","rule":"Protected leader operations must verify current authority rather than assuming a previously acquired lease remains valid indefinitely.","examples":[{"scenario":"A leader loses connectivity long enough for its lease to expire.","expected_behavior":"It stops protected operations and cannot continue acting as leader."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Continue leader work because the process still believes it owns the lease.","why_invalid":"Local belief is not proof of current authority."}],"problem":"Stale leaders continue after authority transfer.","failure":"Concurrent ownership and conflicting writes.","cause":"Lease validity not enforced at action boundaries.","solution":"Tie protected operations to current authority and safe fencing semantics.","alternatives":["Fencing tokens","Consensus sequence numbers"],"tradeoffs":["Additional authority checks add complexity and sometimes latency."],"constraints":["Clock assumptions must be explicitly bounded.","Coordination semantics must be understood."],"edge_cases":["Long runtime pause exceeds lease duration.","Network partition isolates the leader."],"security":["Expired credentials must not authorize privileged operations."],"performance":["Avoid unnecessarily frequent coordination calls while preserving safety."],"testing":["Simulate pauses, partitions, and delayed packets."],"verification":["Demonstrate stale authority cannot mutate protected state."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Lease mechanism","Authority validation"],"prerequisites":["Leader election model"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B63-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B63-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"distributed_systems_practice","source":"Lease and fencing-token literature","basis":"Time-bounded authority requires protection against stale owners."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_distributed_systems_practice","claim_scope":"Lease safety"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B63-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Leader Coordination","concept":"Fencing tokens","knowledge_type":"safety_mechanism","question":"How do fencing tokens prevent stale leaders from modifying shared state?","answer":"The authority service issues monotonically increasing ownership tokens and the protected resource rejects operations carrying an older token than the latest accepted authority.","explanation":"Stopping an old process is not always possible during partitions or long pauses. The protected resource itself can enforce ownership ordering.","principle":"The resource that needs exclusive ownership should enforce the authority boundary when possible.","rule":"Do not rely solely on a client remembering that its lease expired; enforce stale-owner rejection at the resource boundary when safety requires it.","examples":[{"scenario":"Leader A obtains token 10, loses authority, and leader B obtains token 11.","expected_behavior":"The protected resource rejects later operations from A carrying token 10."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Only the leader process checks whether it believes its token is current.","why_invalid":"A stale process can continue despite local state."}],"problem":"Stale processes remain capable of writing shared state.","failure":"Old leader corrupts or overwrites newer state.","cause":"Authority is not enforced at the resource boundary.","solution":"Use fencing tokens or equivalent monotonic authority validation.","alternatives":["Consensus-enforced writes","Resource-side epoch validation"],"tradeoffs":["Requires resource support and persistent authority metadata."],"constraints":["Token ordering must be durable and unambiguous."],"edge_cases":["Resource loses token state.","Token comparison wraps or uses inconsistent representation."],"security":["Tokens must not be forgeable or transferable outside authorized coordination."],"performance":["Resource-side token validation should be inexpensive."],"testing":["Delay stale leaders and verify resource rejection."],"verification":["Show old tokens cannot modify current ownership state."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Coordination authority","Protected resource"],"prerequisites":["Lease or epoch model"],"relationships":[{"relation":"implements","record_id":"D22-B63-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B63-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"distributed_systems_practice","source":"Distributed locking and fencing-token literature","basis":"Fencing prevents stale owners from acting after authority changes."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_distributed_systems_practice","claim_scope":"Stale-owner prevention"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B63-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Split Brain","concept":"Partition-induced dual authority","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"What is split brain in a distributed service?","answer":"Split brain occurs when multiple partitions or nodes independently believe they possess authority that should be exclusive, potentially producing conflicting actions or state.","explanation":"Network partitions can prevent nodes from observing one another. Without a sufficiently strong authority mechanism, both sides may continue as if they were the sole active owner.","principle":"Loss of communication must not automatically imply continued authority.","rule":"Design exclusive operations so that communication loss cannot create uncontrolled dual ownership.","examples":[{"scenario":"A cluster is divided by a network partition.","expected_behavior":"Only the partition satisfying the coordination system's authority rules may perform exclusive work."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Both partitions continue writing because each still has a local leader.","why_invalid":"The system has lost exclusive authority."}],"problem":"Partitioned components perform conflicting exclusive operations.","failure":"State divergence or duplicate side effects.","cause":"Authority derived from local state without partition safety.","solution":"Use quorum/consensus or resource-side fencing where appropriate.","alternatives":["Single-writer architecture","Partitioned ownership"],"tradeoffs":["Strong partition safety may reduce availability during coordination failures."],"constraints":["Consistency and availability requirements must be explicit."],"edge_cases":["Coordinator itself becomes partitioned.","Network heals after divergent writes."],"security":["Prevent partition-local authority escalation."],"performance":["Coordination adds network overhead."],"testing":["Inject network partitions and delayed recovery."],"verification":["Check that exclusive invariants remain true during partitions."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Distributed coordination"],"prerequisites":["Exclusive ownership model"],"relationships":[{"relation":"motivates","record_id":"D22-B63-001"},{"relation":"motivates","record_id":"D22-B63-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"distributed_systems_practice","source":"Distributed systems consistency and partition literature","basis":"Network partitions can create conflicting authority without coordination safeguards."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_distributed_systems_practice","claim_scope":"Split-brain failure mode"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B63-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Leader Coordination","concept":"Election churn","knowledge_type":"reliability_failure_mode","question":"How can excessive leader election churn reduce reliability?","answer":"Frequent leadership changes interrupt work, increase coordination traffic, invalidate caches or leases, and repeatedly trigger initialization and recovery paths.","explanation":"A leader election mechanism can be correct while the surrounding system becomes unreliable if transient instability causes repeated elections.","principle":"Correct coordination must also be operationally stable.","rule":"Detect and control election churn rather than treating every election as independent and harmless.","examples":[{"scenario":"Intermittent network latency repeatedly causes leader lease expiration.","expected_behavior":"The system detects churn and avoids continuously restarting expensive leader work."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Restart all leader-dependent work on every transient election without rate control.","why_invalid":"Election instability can amplify into service instability."}],"problem":"Coordination instability becomes an application-level reliability failure.","failure":"Repeated interruptions and reduced useful work.","cause":"Unbounded election sensitivity or expensive leader initialization.","solution":"Tune lease/election behavior, reduce initialization cost, and monitor election frequency.","alternatives":["Stable ownership partitions","Longer bounded leases"],"tradeoffs":["Longer leases can delay failover.","Shorter leases improve failure detection but can increase churn."],"constraints":["Timing must respect failure-detection requirements."],"edge_cases":["Temporary network jitter","Coordinator overload"],"security":["Election controls must not permit unauthorized leadership."],"performance":["Measure election frequency and leader initialization cost."],"testing":["Inject latency and packet loss."],"verification":["Define acceptable election-rate thresholds."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Leader election","Observability"],"prerequisites":["Leadership lifecycle"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B63-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"distributed_systems_practice","source":"Distributed coordination reliability practice","basis":"Unstable coordination can create secondary service instability."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Election stability"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B64-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Transactional Recovery","concept":"Transactional outbox reliability","knowledge_type":"distributed_consistency_pattern","question":"How does a transactional outbox reduce the database-to-message publication failure window?","answer":"Persist the business state change and an outgoing message record within the same local transaction, then publish the recorded message asynchronously with retry and duplicate-safe consumption.","explanation":"Without an outbox, a process can commit database state and fail before publishing the corresponding message, or publish first and fail before committing state. The outbox couples the durable intent to the transaction.","principle":"Persist the intent to publish atomically with the state change when atomic cross-system commit is unavailable.","rule":"Treat outbox publication as asynchronous delivery requiring idempotent or deduplicating consumers.","examples":[{"scenario":"Order state and order-created event are committed.","expected_behavior":"Both the state and durable outbox entry commit together; a publisher can later deliver the event."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Commit database state and assume a subsequent network publish cannot fail.","why_invalid":"The publication step is a separate failure domain."}],"problem":"Database state and external event stream diverge.","failure":"Missing or duplicate events.","cause":"Non-atomic cross-system operations.","solution":"Use transactional outbox plus reliable publication and duplicate-safe consumption.","alternatives":["Distributed transaction","Change-data-capture"],"tradeoffs":["Outbox storage and publisher management add operational complexity."],"constraints":["Consumer semantics still determine duplicate handling."],"edge_cases":["Outbox publisher crashes after successful publish.","Outbox record remains permanently blocked."],"security":["Protect event contents and publishing credentials."],"performance":["Batch publication carefully without violating ordering requirements."],"testing":["Crash publisher at pre-send and post-send boundaries."],"verification":["Compare committed business events with published records."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Transactional database","Message transport"],"prerequisites":["Event model","Idempotent consumer strategy"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B64-002"},{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B62-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"distributed_systems_pattern","source":"Transactional Outbox pattern literature","basis":"The pattern persists message intent in the same transaction as business state."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_distributed_systems_pattern","claim_scope":"Database-event consistency"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B64-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Transactional Recovery","concept":"Idempotent event consumption","knowledge_type":"reliability_pattern","question":"Why should event consumers tolerate duplicate delivery?","answer":"Distributed delivery commonly leaves an ambiguity window where a consumer may successfully apply an effect but fail before recording that the message was processed. Duplicate-safe consumption prevents replay from producing an additional incorrect effect.","explanation":"At-least-once delivery improves durability but requires consumers to handle duplicates explicitly.","principle":"Reliable delivery and duplicate-free side effects are separate properties.","rule":"Do not equate successful message delivery with exactly-once business effect.","examples":[{"scenario":"The same event arrives twice.","expected_behavior":"The consumer recognizes the duplicate and does not apply the protected side effect twice."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assume the transport guarantees exactly-once business semantics.","why_invalid":"Transport-level delivery guarantees do not automatically guarantee application-level effect semantics."}],"problem":"Message replay causes duplicate business effects.","failure":"Duplicate charge, record creation, or state transition.","cause":"Non-idempotent consumer behavior.","solution":"Use idempotency keys, deduplication records, natural idempotence, or transactional effect recording.","alternatives":["Exactly-once transactional stream processing where supported"],"tradeoffs":["Deduplication requires storage and retention policies."],"constraints":["Idempotency identity must be stable for the intended operation window."],"edge_cases":["Duplicate arrives after deduplication record expires.","Same payload represents two legitimate operations with different identities."],"security":["Protect deduplication keys from tampering."],"performance":["Deduplication lookup should be efficient."],"testing":["Replay identical messages and test concurrent duplicate delivery."],"verification":["Verify one logical event produces one protected business effect."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Message identity","Durable consumer state"],"prerequisites":["Business operation identity"],"relationships":[{"relation":"complements","record_id":"D22-B64-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B64-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"distributed_systems_pattern","source":"Reliable messaging and idempotent consumer patterns","basis":"At-least-once delivery requires duplicate-safe processing."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_distributed_systems_pattern","claim_scope":"Duplicate delivery"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B64-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Transactional Recovery","concept":"Poison message isolation","knowledge_type":"failure_handling_pattern","question":"How should a system handle a message that repeatedly fails processing?","answer":"Bound processing attempts, preserve the failed message and diagnostic context, isolate it from healthy work, and route it to a controlled recovery or dead-letter workflow.","explanation":"Repeatedly retrying an irreparably invalid message can block queues, consume resources, and create retry storms.","principle":"A permanently failing item must not indefinitely consume capacity required for healthy work.","rule":"Define an explicit terminal or quarantine state for messages that exceed recoverable failure criteria.","examples":[{"scenario":"A message violates an immutable schema requirement.","expected_behavior":"It is isolated after defined validation rather than retried indefinitely."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Retry malformed input forever.","why_invalid":"No amount of retry changes a deterministic invalid payload."}],"problem":"One pathological item repeatedly consumes processing capacity.","failure":"Queue starvation or retry storm.","cause":"No distinction between transient and permanent failure.","solution":"Classify failures and quarantine poison messages with durable evidence.","alternatives":["Dead-letter queue","Manual repair queue","Schema rejection"],"tradeoffs":["Quarantine reduces repeated damage but requires operational remediation."],"constraints":["Never silently discard business-critical messages."],"edge_cases":["A supposedly permanent failure becomes recoverable after a schema migration.","The dead-letter store itself becomes unavailable."],"security":["Treat quarantined payloads as potentially untrusted."],"performance":["Bound repeated processing attempts."],"testing":["Inject malformed, oversized, and dependency-dependent messages."],"verification":["Verify quarantine preserves message identity and failure evidence."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Queue","Failure classifier"],"prerequisites":["Message lifecycle"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B64-004"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B65-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reliability_pattern","source":"Reliable messaging and queue processing practice","basis":"Poison messages require isolation to prevent repeated consumption of processing capacity."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Message failure isolation"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B64-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Transactional Recovery","concept":"Compensating action","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"When should compensating actions be used in distributed workflows?","answer":"Use compensation when a multi-step business operation cannot be atomically committed and a later failure leaves an earlier effect that must be logically reversed or reconciled.","explanation":"Compensation is not equivalent to database rollback. External effects may be irreversible or only approximately reversible, so the workflow must define semantic compensation.","principle":"Distributed recovery often restores business invariants rather than reverting physical history.","rule":"Define compensation per business operation and verify that compensation itself is durable and retry-safe.","examples":[{"scenario":"Inventory reservation succeeds but shipment creation permanently fails.","expected_behavior":"A defined compensating operation releases the reservation if business rules permit."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assume every external operation has a perfect rollback.","why_invalid":"External systems may not support rollback."}],"problem":"Partial distributed workflow leaves business state inconsistent.","failure":"Resources remain reserved or actions remain partially applied.","cause":"No compensation or reconciliation policy.","solution":"Define explicit compensating transitions and durable workflow state.","alternatives":["Distributed transaction","Manual reconciliation"],"tradeoffs":["Compensation can be complex and may itself fail."],"constraints":["Compensation must respect business semantics."],"edge_cases":["The original operation succeeded after the workflow believed it failed.","Compensation is irreversible."],"security":["Compensation must preserve authorization boundaries."],"performance":["Compensation may increase total workflow latency."],"testing":["Inject failures after each workflow step."],"verification":["Check final business invariants rather than merely process completion."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Workflow state","External systems"],"prerequisites":["Business invariants"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B64-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B64-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"distributed_systems_pattern","source":"Saga and compensating transaction patterns","basis":"Distributed workflows can use compensating actions when atomic rollback is unavailable."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_distributed_systems_pattern","claim_scope":"Distributed workflow recovery"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B64-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Transactional Recovery","concept":"Recovery state machine","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"Why should distributed workflows model recovery as explicit states?","answer":"Explicit states distinguish pending, committed, failed, compensating, compensated, ambiguous, and terminal conditions so recovery can continue deterministically after process or dependency failure.","explanation":"Implicit workflow state is difficult to reconstruct after crashes. A durable state machine gives recovery logic an authoritative record of what has happened and what remains possible.","principle":"Recoverable workflows require durable state transitions, not only procedural code.","rule":"Every non-terminal workflow state must define valid forward, retry, recovery, and terminal transitions.","examples":[{"scenario":"Workflow crashes after an external operation but before local confirmation.","expected_behavior":"Recovery enters an ambiguous/reconciliation state rather than blindly repeating the operation."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Restart the workflow from step one after every crash.","why_invalid":"Previously completed external effects may be repeated."}],"problem":"Workflow restart cannot distinguish completed from incomplete steps.","failure":"Duplicate or skipped effects.","cause":"No durable workflow state machine.","solution":"Persist explicit workflow state and recovery transitions.","alternatives":["Event-sourced workflow","Durable workflow engine"],"tradeoffs":["State machines increase design effort but make recovery explicit."],"constraints":["State transitions must be version-compatible."],"edge_cases":["Workflow schema changes while old workflows remain active.","A recovery action fails repeatedly."],"security":["Workflow state must not allow unauthorized transition injection."],"performance":["Persist only necessary state while preserving recovery evidence."],"testing":["Crash at every transition boundary."],"verification":["Check that every state has deterministic recovery semantics."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Durable state","Workflow engine"],"prerequisites":["Workflow invariant model"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B64-004"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"distributed_systems_pattern","source":"Saga/workflow reliability practice","basis":"Explicit durable workflow state supports recovery after partial execution."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_distributed_systems_practice","claim_scope":"Workflow recovery"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B65-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Under Configuration Change","concept":"Configuration rollback safety","knowledge_type":"change_reliability_principle","question":"Why should configuration changes have rollback semantics?","answer":"A configuration can alter behavior without changing application binaries, so reliability controls must treat significant configuration changes as reversible or recoverable changes with versioned state and validation.","explanation":"A bad timeout, concurrency limit, routing rule, or feature flag can create a reliability failure even when application code is unchanged.","principle":"Operational configuration is executable behavior and requires change control.","rule":"Version significant configuration, validate it before activation, and preserve a known-good rollback state.","examples":[{"scenario":"A concurrency limit is changed and latency increases sharply.","expected_behavior":"The configuration can be reverted to the previous validated version."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Edit production configuration directly without recording the previous value.","why_invalid":"Rollback and causal analysis become difficult."}],"problem":"Configuration changes create outages without recoverable history.","failure":"Extended incident duration or repeated misconfiguration.","cause":"Unversioned or unvalidated configuration.","solution":"Use versioned configuration with validation and rollback.","alternatives":["Immutable configuration deployment","Configuration service with revisions"],"tradeoffs":["Versioning adds operational discipline and storage."],"constraints":["Secrets must not be exposed through configuration history."],"edge_cases":["Rollback configuration is incompatible with newer application code.","Two configuration changes interact."],"security":["Separate secret storage from general configuration history."],"performance":["Validate configuration before broad activation."],"testing":["Test configuration boundaries and invalid combinations."],"verification":["Record configuration version associated with runtime state."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Configuration management","Deployment controls"],"prerequisites":["Configuration schema"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B65-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reliability_practice","source":"SRE change-management practice","basis":"Configuration changes can materially affect reliability and require controlled rollback."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Configuration reliability"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B65-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Progressive Delivery","concept":"Canary reliability validation","knowledge_type":"change_validation","question":"What reliability evidence should be collected during a canary deployment?","answer":"Compare the candidate against a suitable baseline using relevant error, latency, saturation, correctness, and dependency-health indicators before expanding traffic.","explanation":"A canary reduces exposure but only provides useful safety if its behavior is actively compared against expected reliability signals.","principle":"Progressive exposure is a measurement strategy, not merely a traffic-routing strategy.","rule":"Promotion must depend on predefined reliability criteria rather than elapsed time alone.","examples":[{"scenario":"Five percent of traffic is routed to a new version.","expected_behavior":"The candidate is promoted only when defined reliability indicators remain within accepted bounds."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Promote after ten minutes regardless of observed errors.","why_invalid":"Time alone does not establish reliability."}],"problem":"Defective changes expand before evidence is available.","failure":"Broad outage caused by a bad release.","cause":"Promotion based on time or superficial health checks.","solution":"Use measurable promotion and rollback gates.","alternatives":["Blue-green deployment","Shadow traffic"],"tradeoffs":["Strict gates may slow releases.","Weak gates increase incident risk."],"constraints":["Traffic must be representative enough for the tested behavior."],"edge_cases":["Rare paths are absent from canary traffic.","Candidate has different dependency behavior."],"security":["Canary access must preserve production authorization boundaries."],"performance":["Compare tail latency and resource saturation."],"testing":["Exercise critical paths during canary."],"verification":["Record promotion decision and supporting metrics."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Traffic routing","Observability"],"prerequisites":["Reliability objectives"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B65-003"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reliability_practice","source":"Progressive delivery and SRE release engineering","basis":"Gradual exposure combined with reliability metrics limits change risk."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Canary validation"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B65-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Rollback","concept":"Rollback decision boundary","knowledge_type":"change_control_rule","question":"When should an automated deployment rollback be triggered?","answer":"Rollback should be triggered when predefined reliability or correctness thresholds are breached and the previous version is known to be safer and operationally recoverable.","explanation":"Automatic rollback is useful only when the rollback target is itself valid and the observed failure signal is sufficiently reliable to avoid oscillation.","principle":"Rollback is a controlled state transition based on evidence, not a reflex to any error.","rule":"Define rollback thresholds, minimum observation windows, cooldowns, and rollback-target validity before enabling automation.","examples":[{"scenario":"Candidate error rate exceeds the release threshold while baseline remains healthy.","expected_behavior":"Traffic is reduced or reverted according to the predefined policy."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Rollback on every single transient error.","why_invalid":"Noise can cause unnecessary deployment oscillation."}],"problem":"Bad releases remain active or automation oscillates.","failure":"Extended outage or rollback loop.","cause":"Poorly defined rollback conditions.","solution":"Use thresholded, evidence-based rollback policies with stabilization windows.","alternatives":["Manual approval","Progressive traffic reduction"],"tradeoffs":["Aggressive rollback reduces exposure but can react to noise."],"constraints":["Previous version must remain deployable and compatible with current data/state."],"edge_cases":["Database schema is forward-only.","Candidate and rollback versions use incompatible state formats."],"security":["Rollback artifacts must retain security fixes when possible."],"performance":["Rollback should not create additional overload."],"testing":["Test rollback under realistic traffic and state conditions."],"verification":["Verify rollback restores reliability rather than merely process availability."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Deployment system","Metrics"],"prerequisites":["Version compatibility"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B65-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B65-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reliability_practice","source":"Progressive delivery and deployment reliability practice","basis":"Rollback requires explicit safety conditions and a viable previous state."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Automated rollback"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B65-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Schema Evolution","concept":"Backward-compatible state transition","knowledge_type":"compatibility_rule","question":"Why does data-schema compatibility affect rollback reliability?","answer":"A newer application may write state that an older application cannot interpret, making application rollback unsafe even when the older binary itself is healthy.","explanation":"Deployment rollback must consider the persisted data contract, not just executable artifacts.","principle":"Rollback safety is constrained by state compatibility across versions.","rule":"Design schema changes so that deployment rollback remains possible or define an explicit forward-recovery strategy.","examples":[{"scenario":"New version adds an optional field while old version ignores unknown fields.","expected_behavior":"The old version can safely continue operating."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Deploy an irreversible schema change and assume binary rollback remains available.","why_invalid":"The old binary may not understand the new persisted state."}],"problem":"Application rollback fails because persisted state is incompatible.","failure":"Rollback-induced outage or data corruption.","cause":"Coupled application and schema changes.","solution":"Use compatible schema evolution or explicitly migrate recovery strategy.","alternatives":["Expand-contract migration","Dual-read/dual-write transition"],"tradeoffs":["Compatibility layers add temporary complexity."],"constraints":["Data contracts must remain valid across active versions."],"edge_cases":["Long-running transactions span versions.","Old and new consumers process the same records."],"security":["Schema migration must preserve access-control semantics."],"performance":["Compatibility layers may add conversion overhead."],"testing":["Run old and new versions against transitional schemas."],"verification":["Prove rollback target can safely read current state."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Data store","Application versions"],"prerequisites":["Schema contract"],"relationships":[{"relation":"constrains","record_id":"D22-B65-003"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B65-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"software_evolution_practice","source":"Backward-compatible schema evolution patterns","basis":"Persisted state compatibility constrains safe deployment rollback."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Schema rollback compatibility"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B65-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Change Reliability","concept":"Deployment-induced failure attribution","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How can reliability systems distinguish deployment failures from unrelated incidents?","answer":"Correlate reliability changes with deployment identity, configuration versions, traffic cohorts, affected instances, dependency behavior, and temporal boundaries while comparing against unaffected control populations when possible.","explanation":"Temporal proximity alone does not prove causation. Deployment metadata and differential behavior improve attribution.","principle":"Reliability diagnosis should preserve change context and comparative evidence.","rule":"Every significant deployment should emit immutable version and rollout metadata that can be joined with reliability telemetry.","examples":[{"scenario":"Latency rises only on instances running the new version.","expected_behavior":"Version-correlated evidence increases confidence that the deployment contributed to the regression."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assume every incident occurring after a deployment was caused by that deployment.","why_invalid":"Temporal correlation is insufficient evidence."}],"problem":"Incident diagnosis lacks precise change context.","failure":"Incorrect rollback or prolonged investigation.","cause":"Missing deployment-to-telemetry correlation.","solution":"Attach version, configuration, rollout, and cohort metadata to operational evidence.","alternatives":["Manual incident timeline","Distributed deployment tracing"],"tradeoffs":["Metadata increases telemetry volume and cardinality."],"constraints":["Avoid high-cardinality identifiers that overwhelm observability systems."],"edge_cases":["Multiple deployments overlap.","A delayed dependency failure appears after deployment."],"security":["Deployment metadata must not expose secrets."],"performance":["Use bounded-cardinality labels."],"testing":["Validate metadata propagation during deployment."],"verification":["Confirm telemetry can reconstruct the rollout timeline."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Deployment metadata","Observability"],"prerequisites":["Version identity"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B65-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"SRE_observability_practice","source":"SRE incident analysis and release engineering","basis":"Change correlation improves reliability diagnosis while requiring causal caution."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Deployment attribution"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B66-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Data Durability","concept":"Backup versus restore validation","knowledge_type":"recovery_principle","question":"Why is a backup not sufficient evidence of recoverability?","answer":"A backup demonstrates that data was stored somewhere, while recoverability requires evidence that the backup can be restored within required time, produces usable state, and satisfies integrity requirements.","explanation":"Backups can be incomplete, corrupted, inaccessible, incompatible, or operationally too slow to restore.","principle":"Recoverability must be demonstrated through restoration, not inferred from backup existence.","rule":"Regularly validate restore procedures and record measurable recovery evidence.","examples":[{"scenario":"A database backup completes successfully.","expected_behavior":"A controlled restore test later confirms the backup can reconstruct a valid database."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Mark recovery-ready because backup jobs report success.","why_invalid":"Backup completion does not prove restoration."}],"problem":"Organizations discover unusable backups during disaster recovery.","failure":"Extended data loss or recovery delay.","cause":"No restore validation.","solution":"Perform automated and periodic restore tests with integrity verification.","alternatives":["Replica-based recovery","Snapshot plus restore testing"],"tradeoffs":["Restore testing consumes compute and storage.","Less frequent testing reduces cost but increases uncertainty."],"constraints":["Recovered data must be handled securely."],"edge_cases":["Backup is valid but encryption keys are unavailable.","Backup format is incompatible with current software."],"security":["Protect backups and restore environments as sensitive data."],"performance":["Measure actual restore duration."],"testing":["Restore representative datasets and verify application usability."],"verification":["Record restore success, duration, integrity, and version compatibility."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Backup system","Restore tooling"],"prerequisites":["Recovery objectives"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B66-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reliability_practice","source":"Disaster recovery and backup engineering practice","basis":"Recoverability requires tested restoration."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Backup recoverability"},"confidence":0.99,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B66-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Data Durability","concept":"Backup integrity verification","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"What should be verified in a recovered backup?","answer":"Verify structural integrity, required records, relationships, checksums or equivalent integrity evidence, application readability, and consistency with the defined recovery point.","explanation":"A technically restorable backup can still contain incomplete or logically inconsistent application state.","principle":"Recovery validation must test semantic correctness, not only file readability.","rule":"Validate both storage integrity and application-level invariants after restoration.","examples":[{"scenario":"Restore a database backup to an isolated environment.","expected_behavior":"Integrity checks and representative application queries succeed."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Verify only that the backup archive can be decompressed.","why_invalid":"Decompression does not establish application correctness."}],"problem":"Corruption or logical incompleteness survives backup restoration.","failure":"Recovered application contains invalid state.","cause":"Insufficient restore validation.","solution":"Combine physical integrity checks with semantic application checks.","alternatives":["Checksum validation","Consistency scans"],"tradeoffs":["Deep validation takes longer and consumes resources."],"constraints":["Tests should not alter source backups."],"edge_cases":["Large datasets make full validation expensive.","Application invariants changed between backup creation and restore."],"security":["Restore environments must have equivalent protection."],"performance":["Use representative plus targeted deep checks when full validation is impractical."],"testing":["Test known corruption and incomplete-backup scenarios."],"verification":["Store restore evidence and validation results."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Backup","Application validation"],"prerequisites":["Data integrity invariants"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B66-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B66-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"data_recovery_practice","source":"Backup integrity and disaster recovery engineering","basis":"Restored data must be validated at structural and application levels."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Restore integrity"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B66-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Data Durability","concept":"Recovery point measurement","knowledge_type":"recovery_metric","question":"How should actual recovery point be measured?","answer":"Measure the newest durable state that can be reconstructed after the failure and compare it with the defined recovery point objective or equivalent data-loss requirement.","explanation":"Nominal backup frequency does not prove the actual amount of recoverable data at failure time.","principle":"Recovery objectives must be measured against actual recoverable state.","rule":"Record the effective recovery point produced by the recovery mechanism, not merely its scheduled interval.","examples":[{"scenario":"Backups run every hour but replication is delayed unpredictably.","expected_behavior":"Recovery evidence reflects the actual latest recoverable state rather than assuming one-hour loss."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Set RPO equal to the backup schedule without measuring successful recoverability.","why_invalid":"Scheduled execution does not guarantee usable recovery state."}],"problem":"Declared recovery objectives differ from actual data-loss exposure.","failure":"Unexpected data loss during recovery.","cause":"RPO treated as configuration rather than observed property.","solution":"Measure recoverable state and compare it with the objective.","alternatives":["Continuous replication","Write-ahead log archiving"],"tradeoffs":["Lower data-loss exposure usually requires additional infrastructure or write-path overhead."],"constraints":["RPO depends on durability semantics of every critical data source."],"edge_cases":["Multiple data stores have different recovery points.","Clock differences complicate event ordering."],"security":["Recovery metadata may reveal operational timing."],"performance":["Measure replication lag and backup completion."],"testing":["Perform controlled point-in-time recovery tests."],"verification":["Record observed recovery point and evidence source."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Durability mechanism","Recovery measurement"],"prerequisites":["Defined RPO"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B66-004"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"disaster_recovery_practice","source":"RPO and backup/recovery engineering","basis":"Effective recovery point should be established through actual recovery capability."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"RPO measurement"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B66-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Data Durability","concept":"Recovery time measurement","knowledge_type":"recovery_metric","question":"Why should actual recovery time be measured rather than assumed?","answer":"Recovery time includes detection, decision, resource acquisition, restoration, validation, routing, and stabilization, so the total duration can be substantially different from the raw restore operation.","explanation":"A database may restore quickly while application dependencies, configuration, caches, or verification add significant time before useful service resumes.","principle":"Recovery time is an end-to-end property of the service, not a single restoration command.","rule":"Measure recovery from failure recognition to verified service restoration using the same definition across exercises.","examples":[{"scenario":"Database restoration takes ten minutes but application verification takes another fifteen.","expected_behavior":"Observed recovery time includes the full service restoration process."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Report database restore duration as total application recovery time.","why_invalid":"It excludes other recovery stages."}],"problem":"Recovery commitments are based on incomplete timing.","failure":"Recovery exceeds operational objectives.","cause":"Narrow recovery-time measurement.","solution":"Measure end-to-end recovery milestones.","alternatives":["Automated disaster recovery","Warm standby"],"tradeoffs":["Comprehensive measurement requires more instrumentation."],"constraints":["Start and end timestamps must be defined consistently."],"edge_cases":["Service becomes partially available before full recovery.","Manual approval delays recovery."],"security":["Recovery operators require controlled access."],"performance":["Measure each recovery stage separately to identify bottlenecks."],"testing":["Run timed recovery drills."],"verification":["Compare measured recovery time with objective."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Recovery workflow","Observability"],"prerequisites":["Defined recovery-time objective"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B66-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B66-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"disaster_recovery_practice","source":"RTO and recovery engineering practice","basis":"End-to-end recovery duration includes restoration and service verification."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"RTO measurement"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B66-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Data Durability","concept":"Backup dependency closure","knowledge_type":"recovery_analysis","question":"Why must recovery planning include backup dependencies such as keys and configuration?","answer":"A data backup may be unusable without encryption keys, schema definitions, credentials, infrastructure configuration, software versions, or dependency metadata required to interpret and restore it.","explanation":"Recovery depends on a chain of artifacts. Losing any critical link can make an otherwise intact backup operationally useless.","principle":"A recoverable system requires recovery of the complete dependency closure, not just primary data.","rule":"Inventory and test every artifact required to reconstruct usable service state.","examples":[{"scenario":"Encrypted backup exists but the required key material is unavailable.","expected_behavior":"Recovery planning identifies key availability as a prerequisite rather than declaring the backup sufficient."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Store only the database backup and document recovery as complete.","why_invalid":"Required dependencies may be missing."}],"problem":"Recovery fails despite intact primary backup.","failure":"Unable to decrypt, interpret, or operate restored data.","cause":"Incomplete recovery dependency inventory.","solution":"Model and test the full recovery dependency graph.","alternatives":["Managed key escrow","Infrastructure-as-code reconstruction"],"tradeoffs":["Maintaining dependency artifacts increases governance requirements."],"constraints":["Recovery copies must remain protected."],"edge_cases":["Old software versions are required to read legacy backups.","Credentials expire during long-term retention."],"security":["Protect recovery keys separately but ensure controlled availability."],"performance":["Automate dependency verification."],"testing":["Perform full-stack recovery exercises."],"verification":["Check every required recovery artifact before declaring recoverability."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Backup","Keys","Configuration","Runtime artifacts"],"prerequisites":["Recovery dependency map"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B66-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"disaster_recovery_practice","source":"Business continuity and recovery engineering","basis":"Successful recovery depends on all required technical artifacts."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Recovery dependency closure"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B67-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Multi-Region Recovery","concept":"Failover readiness","knowledge_type":"disaster_recovery_principle","question":"What makes a secondary region genuinely failover-ready?","answer":"It must have the required application artifacts, data state, configuration, identity dependencies, networking, observability, capacity, and operational procedures needed to provide the defined service after failover.","explanation":"Having infrastructure provisioned does not guarantee that a secondary location can serve real workload.","principle":"Failover readiness is an end-to-end capability, not merely resource availability.","rule":"Validate the complete failover path periodically rather than assuming standby resources are usable.","examples":[{"scenario":"Primary region becomes unavailable.","expected_behavior":"Secondary environment can receive traffic and access required state within the defined recovery objectives."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declare the secondary ready because virtual machines are running.","why_invalid":"Application, data, routing, and operational dependencies may still be missing."}],"problem":"Disaster recovery fails when standby resources are needed.","failure":"Extended regional outage.","cause":"Untested or incomplete failover dependency chain.","solution":"Exercise end-to-end failover and verify service behavior.","alternatives":["Active-active architecture","Warm standby"],"tradeoffs":["Higher redundancy improves recovery but increases cost and operational complexity."],"constraints":["Data residency and regulatory requirements may limit replication."],"edge_cases":["Secondary region uses different capacity or dependency versions.","Failover occurs during an ongoing deployment."],"security":["Replicated data and credentials require equivalent protection."],"performance":["Validate capacity under disaster workload."],"testing":["Perform controlled regional failover exercises."],"verification":["Record actual recovery time, data point, and service correctness."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Multi-region infrastructure","Data replication","Traffic management"],"prerequisites":["RTO/RPO definitions"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B67-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reliability_documentation","source":"Microsoft Azure Architecture Center; cloud disaster recovery practices","basis":"Regional resilience requires tested failover capability and complete dependencies."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"official_documentation","claim_scope":"Regional failover"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B67-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Multi-Region Recovery","concept":"Failback safety","knowledge_type":"recovery_principle","question":"Why can failback be more dangerous than failover?","answer":"During failover, the secondary environment may become the active writer and accumulate new state. Returning to the original environment requires synchronization and authority transfer without losing or overwriting newer state.","explanation":"Failback is a separate distributed state transition and cannot be treated as simply reversing routing.","principle":"Every ownership transition must preserve the authoritative state lineage.","rule":"Do not fail back until state synchronization, write authority, and consistency conditions are explicitly verified.","examples":[{"scenario":"Secondary region served writes for several hours.","expected_behavior":"Primary is synchronized with the authoritative state before write ownership returns."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Switch traffic back immediately after primary infrastructure recovers.","why_invalid":"The recovered primary may contain stale state."}],"problem":"Failback overwrites newer state or creates divergence.","failure":"Data loss or conflicting writes.","cause":"Failback treated as routing-only operation.","solution":"Use controlled state synchronization and explicit authority transfer.","alternatives":["Keep secondary primary until planned migration","Active-active reconciliation"],"tradeoffs":["Longer failover duration simplifies some failback paths.","State synchronization adds recovery time."],"constraints":["Write authority must remain unambiguous."],"edge_cases":["Both regions accepted writes during partition.","Replication is incomplete."],"security":["Failback must preserve access controls and credentials."],"performance":["Measure replication lag before authority transfer."],"testing":["Test failback separately from failover."],"verification":["Verify state equivalence or documented reconciliation before switchback."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Replication","Traffic control","Authority model"],"prerequisites":["Failover architecture"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B67-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B67-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"disaster_recovery_practice","source":"Multi-region recovery engineering","basis":"Failback requires controlled synchronization and authority transfer."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Failback reliability"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B67-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Multi-Region Recovery","concept":"Regional dependency independence","knowledge_type":"architecture_principle","question":"Why should regional redundancy consider shared dependencies?","answer":"Two regions do not provide independent failure domains if both depend on the same globally shared component whose failure can disable both simultaneously.","explanation":"Nominal geographic separation does not equal fault isolation. Shared identity, control-plane, DNS, storage, or networking dependencies can become common-mode failures.","principle":"Redundancy is only as independent as its shared failure dependencies.","rule":"Identify and classify dependencies that remain common across supposedly independent recovery domains.","examples":[{"scenario":"Two application regions rely on one globally shared authentication dependency.","expected_behavior":"The authentication service is treated as a common failure dependency in the reliability model."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assume two regions are independent because their application servers are geographically separated.","why_invalid":"Shared dependencies can still create correlated failure."}],"problem":"Redundant environments fail together.","failure":"Common-mode regional outage.","cause":"Hidden shared dependencies.","solution":"Map cross-region dependencies and add alternatives or degraded modes where justified.","alternatives":["Independent control planes","Cached emergency authorization"],"tradeoffs":["Removing shared dependencies can increase complexity."],"constraints":["Some global services cannot be duplicated economically or technically."],"edge_cases":["Global provider outage","Cross-region DNS failure"],"security":["Fallback mechanisms must not weaken authorization."],"performance":["Independent dependencies may increase latency."],"testing":["Test common dependency failures explicitly."],"verification":["Maintain a cross-region dependency graph."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Dependency inventory","Failure-domain model"],"prerequisites":["Regional architecture map"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B67-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B67-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reliability_architecture_practice","source":"Cloud reliability and failure-domain design","basis":"Shared dependencies can create correlated failures across redundant domains."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Common-mode regional failures"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B67-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Disaster Recovery","concept":"Degraded regional operation","knowledge_type":"resilience_strategy","question":"When is regional degraded operation preferable to complete service failure?","answer":"When critical functions can remain safe and useful without nonessential dependencies, a controlled degraded mode can preserve core service while sacrificing lower-priority capabilities.","explanation":"Disaster recovery does not always require full feature equivalence. Priority-based degradation can reduce recovery dependency count and preserve essential operations.","principle":"During severe failures, preserving critical function can be more reliable than attempting full functionality.","rule":"Define explicit feature priorities and safe degraded behavior before disaster conditions occur.","examples":[{"scenario":"Recommendation service is unavailable while account access remains healthy.","expected_behavior":"Core account operations continue while recommendations are disabled."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Continue all features using unsafe emergency defaults.","why_invalid":"Unavailable dependencies may cause incorrect or unsafe behavior."}],"problem":"Noncritical features prevent critical functions from operating.","failure":"Complete outage despite partial capability.","cause":"No priority-aware degradation strategy.","solution":"Define critical and noncritical capabilities with safe degraded modes.","alternatives":["Full failover","Read-only mode"],"tradeoffs":["Users lose features during degradation.","Simpler degraded modes may preserve more critical availability."],"constraints":["Degraded behavior must remain correct and secure."],"edge_cases":["A supposedly noncritical dependency becomes required for authorization.","Degraded mode persists longer than expected."],"security":["Never disable critical security controls merely to preserve availability."],"performance":["Prioritize scarce capacity for critical operations."],"testing":["Exercise degraded modes under dependency failures."],"verification":["Confirm critical invariants remain true in degraded state."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Feature priority model","Dependency classification"],"prerequisites":["Business criticality mapping"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B67-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B68-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reliability_architecture_practice","source":"Graceful degradation and mission-critical system design","basis":"Priority-based degradation can preserve essential service during dependency failure."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Degraded regional operation"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B67-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Disaster Recovery","concept":"Recovery exercise realism","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"Why should disaster recovery exercises include realistic operational dependencies?","answer":"A recovery exercise should include routing, identity, configuration, data, observability, staffing, approval, and operational decision dependencies that would exist during a real incident.","explanation":"Technical failover may succeed while the organization remains unable to restore useful service because operational dependencies were excluded.","principle":"Disaster recovery is a socio-technical system property.","rule":"Validate the actual recovery workflow rather than testing only infrastructure primitives.","examples":[{"scenario":"Regional recovery requires an approval workflow and credential rotation.","expected_behavior":"The exercise validates those steps rather than assuming operators can bypass them."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declare disaster recovery tested after starting replacement servers only.","why_invalid":"The service recovery chain remains untested."}],"problem":"Recovery succeeds technically but fails operationally.","failure":"Long outage despite available infrastructure.","cause":"Incomplete exercise scope.","solution":"Include human, technical, security, and governance dependencies in exercises.","alternatives":["Automated recovery drills","Tabletop plus technical simulation"],"tradeoffs":["Realistic exercises require more coordination."],"constraints":["Exercises must remain safe and authorized."],"edge_cases":["Key staff are unavailable.","Recovery occurs during another incident."],"security":["Emergency procedures must preserve least privilege."],"performance":["Measure operator and system recovery stages."],"testing":["Repeat exercises under different failure assumptions."],"verification":["Record every failed recovery dependency and remediation."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["DR plan","Operations","Security","Infrastructure"],"prerequisites":["Recovery dependency map"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B66-005"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"business_continuity_practice","source":"Disaster recovery exercise methodology","basis":"Recovery exercises must test the operational recovery process, not only infrastructure."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Recovery exercise completeness"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B68-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload Resilience","concept":"Admission control","knowledge_type":"resilience_control","question":"How does admission control protect a system during overload?","answer":"Admission control limits the amount or type of work allowed into constrained resources so that accepted work can continue within defined reliability boundaries.","explanation":"When demand exceeds sustainable capacity, accepting all requests can cause queue growth, resource exhaustion, and cascading failure.","principle":"A reliable overloaded system must control what work it accepts.","rule":"Define explicit capacity limits and reject, defer, or prioritize excess work rather than allowing unbounded accumulation.","examples":[{"scenario":"Worker capacity is saturated.","expected_behavior":"New low-priority work is rejected or deferred while critical work retains capacity."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Allow unlimited requests into an already saturated queue.","why_invalid":"Backlog can grow without bound and increase recovery difficulty."}],"problem":"Demand exceeds sustainable capacity.","failure":"Resource exhaustion and broad service degradation.","cause":"No admission control.","solution":"Bound concurrency, queue size, or request rate and prioritize work.","alternatives":["Load shedding","Rate limiting","Priority queues"],"tradeoffs":["Rejected work reduces immediate functionality but protects system stability."],"constraints":["Critical work must have reserved capacity where required."],"edge_cases":["Traffic suddenly exceeds limits by a large factor.","Priority classification is incorrect."],"security":["Admission controls can mitigate resource-exhaustion attacks."],"performance":["Measure accepted versus rejected work and saturation."],"testing":["Load test beyond capacity boundaries."],"verification":["Verify bounded resource usage under overload."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Capacity model","Traffic control"],"prerequisites":["Resource limits"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B68-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B68-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE; Microsoft reliability design guidance","basis":"Load shedding, throttling, and bounded capacity help prevent overload cascades."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"official_documentation","claim_scope":"Overload control"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B68-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload Resilience","concept":"Concurrency limiting","knowledge_type":"resource_control","question":"Why should concurrency be bounded even when CPU capacity remains available?","answer":"Concurrency consumes multiple constrained resources such as memory, connections, locks, file descriptors, queues, and downstream capacity, so CPU alone is not a sufficient admission metric.","explanation":"Unbounded concurrent operations can exhaust a non-CPU resource and trigger cascading failures before overall CPU saturation becomes visible.","principle":"Concurrency must be bounded by the narrowest relevant resource constraint.","rule":"Set explicit concurrency limits based on measured resource and dependency capacity.","examples":[{"scenario":"Database connections are limited while application CPU remains low.","expected_behavior":"Application concurrency is capped before the connection pool becomes an outage trigger."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Increase worker concurrency because CPU is below 50 percent.","why_invalid":"Another resource may already be saturated."}],"problem":"A secondary resource becomes the bottleneck.","failure":"Connection, memory, lock, or queue exhaustion.","cause":"Concurrency tuned against a single metric.","solution":"Model all material resource constraints and bound concurrency accordingly.","alternatives":["Adaptive concurrency control","Per-dependency pools"],"tradeoffs":["Lower concurrency can reduce throughput but improve tail reliability."],"constraints":["Limits should reflect workload characteristics."],"edge_cases":["Different request classes consume radically different resources.","Downstream capacity changes dynamically."],"security":["Concurrency limits can reduce denial-of-service impact."],"performance":["Track queue wait and resource saturation."],"testing":["Stress individual resource bottlenecks."],"verification":["Demonstrate bounded resource usage at maximum concurrency."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Resource monitoring","Workload model"],"prerequisites":["Capacity measurements"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B68-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B68-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reliability_practice","source":"SRE overload control and resource management","basis":"Multiple resource constraints can limit reliable concurrency."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Concurrency control"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B68-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload Resilience","concept":"Priority-aware load shedding","knowledge_type":"resilience_strategy","question":"How should load shedding preserve critical service during overload?","answer":"Classify work by business or system criticality and reject, defer, or degrade lower-priority work before sacrificing protected critical work.","explanation":"Uniform rejection can unnecessarily disable essential functions while allowing nonessential workloads to consume scarce capacity.","principle":"When capacity is insufficient, scarce resources should be allocated according to explicit priority policy.","rule":"Make load-shedding priority deterministic, observable, and resistant to untrusted priority claims.","examples":[{"scenario":"System capacity drops sharply.","expected_behavior":"Analytics jobs are deferred while transactional operations retain protected capacity."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Drop requests randomly until load decreases.","why_invalid":"Critical operations may be discarded without policy."}],"problem":"Overload causes critical and noncritical work to compete equally.","failure":"Loss of essential service.","cause":"No priority model.","solution":"Use explicit work classes and protected capacity.","alternatives":["Separate resource pools","Static quotas"],"tradeoffs":["Priority systems add policy complexity.","Incorrect priority assignment can starve legitimate work."],"constraints":["Priority cannot bypass security or correctness controls."],"edge_cases":["All traffic claims highest priority.","Critical workload itself exceeds capacity."],"security":["Do not trust client-supplied priority without authorization."],"performance":["Monitor starvation and utilization per class."],"testing":["Simulate overload with mixed priorities."],"verification":["Verify critical work remains within objectives while lower-priority work is shed."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Admission control","Priority policy"],"prerequisites":["Business criticality model"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B68-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B68-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE overload and load-shedding guidance","basis":"Load shedding and prioritization help prevent cascading overload."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"official_documentation","claim_scope":"Priority-aware overload control"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B68-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload Resilience","concept":"Brownout mode","knowledge_type":"graceful_degradation","question":"What is a brownout reliability strategy?","answer":"A brownout temporarily disables or simplifies nonessential functionality so that critical service can continue under resource or dependency pressure.","explanation":"Brownout converts uncontrolled failure into intentional reduced functionality with predefined behavior.","principle":"Controlled reduction of capability can preserve core reliability.","rule":"Define exactly which features may be disabled and ensure their absence does not violate critical correctness or security requirements.","examples":[{"scenario":"Recommendation dependency becomes unavailable.","expected_behavior":"Recommendations are omitted while core transactions continue."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Disable authentication checks during overload.","why_invalid":"Security controls are not nonessential functionality."}],"problem":"Optional features consume resources required for critical service.","failure":"Whole-service outage.","cause":"No degraded operating mode.","solution":"Implement explicit brownout policies for noncritical functionality.","alternatives":["Feature flags","Read-only mode","Priority-based shedding"],"tradeoffs":["Users experience reduced functionality.","Explicit degradation can substantially improve stability."],"constraints":["Degraded state must remain correct and secure."],"edge_cases":["Optional feature unexpectedly becomes required for another operation.","Brownout persists for long duration."],"security":["Security controls must remain active."],"performance":["Monitor resource savings from disabled features."],"testing":["Inject dependency and capacity failures."],"verification":["Verify critical invariants during brownout."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Feature controls","Criticality model"],"prerequisites":["Feature dependency graph"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B67-004"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B68-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reliability_practice","source":"Graceful degradation and overload engineering","basis":"Selective functionality reduction can preserve critical service under stress."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Brownout behavior"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B68-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload Resilience","concept":"Overload recovery hysteresis","knowledge_type":"stability_control","question":"Why should overload recovery avoid immediately restoring full traffic?","answer":"Instantly restoring rejected or deferred work when capacity briefly recovers can recreate overload, producing oscillation between failure and recovery.","explanation":"Recovery must account for backlog, dependency health, resource recovery, and remaining capacity rather than using a single instantaneous threshold.","principle":"Recovery from overload should be gradual and stable.","rule":"Use hysteresis, staged admission, or equivalent stabilization controls when reintroducing load after overload.","examples":[{"scenario":"Queue depth begins decreasing after load shedding.","expected_behavior":"Admission increases gradually while monitoring latency and saturation."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Restore 100 percent traffic immediately when CPU drops below the overload threshold.","why_invalid":"Backlog and downstream pressure may remain."}],"problem":"System repeatedly enters and exits overload.","failure":"Recovery oscillation and unstable service.","cause":"No recovery hysteresis.","solution":"Gradually restore work based on multiple health signals.","alternatives":["Adaptive concurrency","Ramp-based traffic restoration"],"tradeoffs":["Slower recovery can prolong reduced capacity but avoids renewed collapse."],"constraints":["Critical traffic may require priority restoration."],"edge_cases":["Backlog is enormous while new traffic is low.","Dependency recovers more slowly than application."],"security":["Recovery controls must not create priority bypasses."],"performance":["Track backlog drain rate and saturation."],"testing":["Create overload and measure recovery oscillation."],"verification":["Show recovery remains stable after admission increases."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Admission control","Metrics"],"prerequisites":["Overload thresholds"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B68-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE overload guidance","basis":"Controlled load restoration helps prevent retry and overload amplification."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"official_documentation","claim_scope":"Overload recovery stability"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B69-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Governance","concept":"Reliability error budget policy","knowledge_type":"governance_rule","question":"How can an error budget influence reliability decisions?","answer":"Define an allowed amount of service unreliability over a measurement period and use its consumption to inform release, change, and reliability-investment decisions.","explanation":"An error budget provides a quantitative mechanism for balancing feature delivery and reliability work without treating reliability as an unlimited requirement.","principle":"Reliability decisions should be connected to measurable service objectives.","rule":"Use the same objective definition consistently and document actions triggered by significant budget consumption.","examples":[{"scenario":"Reliability budget is substantially consumed by recent incidents.","expected_behavior":"High-risk changes may require additional review or reliability remediation according to policy."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declare a budget but ignore it when making release decisions.","why_invalid":"The metric has no operational consequence."}],"problem":"Reliability objectives exist without decision linkage.","failure":"Repeated reliability degradation with no corrective feedback.","cause":"No governance mechanism connecting reliability measurements to changes.","solution":"Define objective, budget, measurement window, and policy actions.","alternatives":["Reliability scorecards","Risk-based change review"],"tradeoffs":["Strict policies can slow feature delivery."],"constraints":["Objectives must reflect actual user-facing service behavior."],"edge_cases":["Low traffic makes error rates statistically unstable.","One severe incident consumes the budget unexpectedly."],"security":["Security incidents should not be hidden by reliability metrics."],"performance":["Measure user-visible indicators rather than only infrastructure metrics."],"testing":["Test budget calculation against known incident data."],"verification":["Audit decisions against documented policy."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["SLO measurement","Incident data"],"prerequisites":["Service-level objectives"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B69-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Workbook and SRE principles","basis":"Error budgets connect reliability objectives with release and operational decisions."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"official_documentation","claim_scope":"Error-budget governance"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B69-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Governance","concept":"Reliability debt","knowledge_type":"risk_model","question":"What is reliability debt?","answer":"Reliability debt is accumulated known work, architectural limitation, operational weakness, or unverified assumption that increases future failure risk or recovery cost.","explanation":"Reliability debt differs from a single defect because it represents persistent conditions that make future incidents more likely, more severe, or harder to recover from.","principle":"Known reliability risk should be tracked as an explicit engineering liability.","rule":"Record reliability debt with evidence, affected systems, risk, mitigation, and review status rather than leaving it as informal knowledge.","examples":[{"scenario":"A critical service has no tested restore procedure.","expected_behavior":"The missing recovery evidence is tracked as reliability debt with remediation ownership."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Track only currently failing bugs and ignore known recovery gaps.","why_invalid":"Unresolved reliability risk remains invisible."}],"problem":"Known resilience weaknesses accumulate without ownership.","failure":"Repeated or increasingly expensive incidents.","cause":"No persistent reliability-risk inventory.","solution":"Maintain evidence-backed reliability debt records and remediation priorities.","alternatives":["Architecture risk register","Operational readiness backlog"],"tradeoffs":["Tracking debt increases governance workload but improves visibility."],"constraints":["Risk records should be evidence-based."],"edge_cases":["Risk cannot be fully eliminated due to external dependency.","Multiple systems share the same debt."],"security":["Security-related reliability debt may require separate urgency and controls."],"performance":["Prioritize debt using measurable impact and likelihood evidence."],"testing":["Link debt items to missing or failing tests."],"verification":["Close debt only after remediation evidence exists."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Risk register","Incident records"],"prerequisites":["Reliability assessment model"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B69-003"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_practice","source":"SRE and reliability risk-management practice","basis":"Known operational risks should remain visible and actionable."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Reliability risk management"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B69-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Incident Learning","concept":"Actionable post-incident learning","knowledge_type":"learning_method","question":"What makes a post-incident reliability action actionable?","answer":"An actionable item identifies a specific failure mechanism or missing control, assigns ownership, defines a measurable completion condition, and links the action to evidence from the incident.","explanation":"Generic actions such as 'improve monitoring' are difficult to verify and may not change the underlying failure condition.","principle":"Incident learning should produce testable changes to system behavior or engineering process.","rule":"Every reliability action should have an owner, evidence basis, explicit acceptance condition, and closure verification.","examples":[{"scenario":"Incident revealed that recovery was not tested.","expected_behavior":"Action specifies an automated restore test and measurable pass criterion."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Add more monitoring.","why_invalid":"No defined scope or verification criterion."}],"problem":"Post-incident work produces vague commitments.","failure":"The same failure mode returns.","cause":"Non-testable remediation actions.","solution":"Convert findings into evidence-backed, verifiable controls."],"alternatives":["Blameless postmortem actions","Reliability improvement backlog"],"tradeoffs":["Specific actions take longer to define but are easier to verify."],"constraints":["Actions must address system conditions rather than assign unsupported blame."],"edge_cases":["Root cause remains uncertain.","Multiple contributing factors exist."],"security":["Preserve sensitive incident evidence under appropriate access controls."],"performance":["Prioritize high-impact systemic improvements."],"testing":["Attach a test or verification method to each action."],"verification":["Close action only after evidence satisfies acceptance criteria."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Incident records","Testing"],"prerequisites":["Incident analysis"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B69-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"SRE_practice","source":"Google SRE incident management practice","basis":"Post-incident learning should produce concrete, trackable reliability improvements."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"official_documentation","claim_scope":"Incident learning"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B69-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Governance","concept":"Reliability evidence provenance","knowledge_type":"governance_principle","question":"Why should reliability claims retain evidence provenance?","answer":"A reliability claim should identify the test, measurement, environment, version, time, workload, and verification method that produced the evidence so later users can determine whether the claim still applies.","explanation":"Reliability properties can change after deployments, topology changes, traffic changes, or dependency changes.","principle":"Reliability evidence is contextual and must retain enough provenance to be interpreted correctly.","rule":"Never treat an old reliability result as universally valid without checking its applicability to the current system state.","examples":[{"scenario":"A restore test passed six months ago on an older schema.","expected_behavior":"Current recovery readiness is not assumed until compatibility is revalidated."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Use the historical test result as proof that today's system is recoverable.","why_invalid":"The system and dependencies may have changed."}],"problem":"Historical evidence is misapplied to current state.","failure":"False confidence.","cause":"Missing provenance and version context.","solution":"Store versioned evidence with environment and applicability metadata.","alternatives":["Continuous verification","Evidence expiry policies"],"tradeoffs":["Rich provenance increases storage and governance effort."],"constraints":["Evidence records must be tamper-resistant where required."],"edge_cases":["System version cannot be reconstructed.","Dependency version changed independently."],"security":["Protect evidence integrity and sensitive operational details."],"performance":["Index provenance for efficient retrieval."],"testing":["Test evidence retrieval and applicability checks."],"verification":["Require current evidence for high-impact reliability claims."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence store","Version metadata"],"prerequisites":["Evidence schema"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B69-005"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_evidence_practice","source":"Reliability verification and change-management practice","basis":"Operational claims require contextual and versioned evidence."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Reliability evidence provenance"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B69-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Governance","concept":"Reliability decision traceability","knowledge_type":"governance_pattern","question":"What should a reliability-sensitive automated decision record contain?","answer":"It should contain the triggering evidence, system state, policy or threshold used, decision, affected scope, authorization context, execution result, and subsequent verification outcome.","explanation":"Automated reliability actions can themselves affect availability, so their decisions need enough evidence for audit and reconstruction.","principle":"High-impact reliability automation should be explainable through an immutable decision trail.","rule":"Do not execute consequential automated recovery without retaining the evidence and policy basis for the decision.","examples":[{"scenario":"System automatically sheds low-priority load.","expected_behavior":"The action records the overload evidence, policy threshold, scope, and observed result."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Record only 'load shedding activated'.","why_invalid":"The decision cannot be reconstructed or audited."}],"problem":"Automated recovery actions cannot be explained after an incident.","failure":"Unsafe automation or inability to diagnose secondary effects.","cause":"Insufficient decision provenance.","solution":"Persist structured decision and outcome evidence.","alternatives":["Human approval for high-impact actions","Policy engine with audit trail"],"tradeoffs":["Detailed records increase storage and implementation complexity."],"constraints":["Sensitive evidence requires controlled access."],"edge_cases":["Automation fails midway.","Multiple policies trigger simultaneously."],"security":["Audit records should resist unauthorized modification."],"performance":["Use structured append-oriented logging."],"testing":["Verify every automated action emits a complete decision record."],"verification":["Reconstruct decisions from stored evidence."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Policy engine","Audit store","Observability"],"prerequisites":["Action model"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B69-004"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"governed_automation_practice","source":"Reliability automation and auditability practice","basis":"Consequential automated actions benefit from structured decision provenance."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Reliability automation auditability"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B70-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Verification","concept":"Recovery invariant verification","knowledge_type":"formal_verification_principle","question":"What is a recovery invariant?","answer":"A recovery invariant is a property that must remain true before, during, or after failure and recovery regardless of the specific execution path taken.","explanation":"Examples include no duplicate protected side effect, no unauthorized state transition, conservation of durable records, and bounded ownership count.","principle":"Recovery correctness should be expressed as properties that survive alternative failure paths.","rule":"Define critical invariants independently of implementation details and verify them across failure scenarios.","examples":[{"scenario":"A worker crashes after receiving a job.","expected_behavior":"The invariant 'one logical job produces at most one protected side effect' remains true."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Verify only that the worker process restarted.","why_invalid":"Process restart does not establish business correctness."}],"problem":"Recovery tests verify implementation steps rather than system properties.","failure":"Hidden correctness violations after recovery.","cause":"No explicit invariants.","solution":"Define and test state, safety, and business invariants across failure paths.","alternatives":["Property-based testing","Model checking"],"tradeoffs":["Invariant testing requires careful domain modeling."],"constraints":["Invariants must be observable or mechanically checkable."],"edge_cases":["Invariant depends on external system state.","Some invariants are temporarily relaxed during recovery."],"security":["Authorization invariants must remain active during recovery."],"performance":["Automated invariant checks should not materially disrupt service."],"testing":["Inject faults at transition boundaries and check invariants."],"verification":["Persist invariant results as evidence."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["State model","Fault injection"],"prerequisites":["Correctness properties"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"formal_methods_practice","source":"Distributed systems verification and reliability engineering","basis":"Invariant-based verification evaluates correctness across execution paths."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_verification_practice","claim_scope":"Recovery invariants"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B70-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Verification","concept":"Stateful fault-injection matrix","knowledge_type":"test_strategy","question":"Why should fault injection cover state-transition boundaries rather than only component outages?","answer":"Many severe reliability defects occur when failure interrupts a transition between states, such as before commit, after external effect, during acknowledgement, or during recovery.","explanation":"Component-level failure tests can miss ambiguous intermediate states that create duplicate, lost, or inconsistent work.","principle":"Failure testing should target both component failures and transition boundaries.","rule":"Construct fault scenarios around meaningful state transitions and ambiguous commit windows.","examples":[{"scenario":"Terminate a worker immediately after external side effect but before local acknowledgement.","expected_behavior":"Recovery reconciles the ambiguous outcome safely."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Only test complete process termination while idle.","why_invalid":"It misses state-dependent failure windows."}],"problem":"Recovery defects remain hidden in transition windows.","failure":"Duplicate, lost, or inconsistent state.","cause":"Insufficient state-aware fault coverage.","solution":"Generate fault scenarios from the system state machine.","alternatives":["Model-based testing","Property-based fault injection"],"tradeoffs":["Stateful testing has higher setup and modeling cost."],"constraints":["Fault scenarios must remain deterministic enough for diagnosis."],"edge_cases":["Concurrent transitions produce combinatorial state space."],"security":["Include authorization transitions in failure testing."],"performance":["Prioritize high-risk transition boundaries."],"testing":["Inject faults before, during, and after each critical transition."],"verification":["Map each invariant to tested failure windows."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["State machine","Fault injector"],"prerequisites":["Transition inventory"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B70-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"verification_practice","source":"State-machine testing and distributed systems reliability","basis":"Transition-boundary faults expose recovery correctness defects."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_verification_practice","claim_scope":"Stateful fault testing"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B70-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Verification","concept":"Fault combination testing","knowledge_type":"test_strategy","question":"Why should reliability testing sometimes combine independent faults?","answer":"Real incidents can involve interacting failures, such as dependency outage combined with elevated load or deployment change combined with resource exhaustion. Combined tests reveal nonlinear failure behavior that isolated tests may miss.","explanation":"Testing every possible combination is impractical, so combinations should be selected based on dependency relationships and plausible common failure interactions.","principle":"Some reliability failures emerge only from interacting conditions.","rule":"Test high-risk fault combinations identified by architecture and incident evidence without attempting indiscriminate combinatorial explosion.","examples":[{"scenario":"Inject dependency latency while increasing traffic toward peak capacity.","expected_behavior":"The system remains within defined safety boundaries or enters controlled degradation."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Test every possible pair or triple regardless of relevance.","why_invalid":"Unbounded combinations create low-value test volume."}],"problem":"Single-fault tests pass while multi-fault incidents still occur.","failure":"Unexpected cascading failure.","cause":"Independent-fault assumption.","solution":"Select evidence-based combinations from dependency and failure models.","alternatives":["Pairwise fault testing","Risk-based scenario generation"],"tradeoffs":["Combined testing increases complexity and diagnosis difficulty."],"constraints":["Blast radius must remain bounded."],"edge_cases":["One fault masks another.","Recovery from first fault changes system capacity."],"security":["Do not combine faults in ways that create uncontrolled access exposure."],"performance":["Prioritize combinations affecting shared bottlenecks."],"testing":["Maintain a fault-combination matrix."],"verification":["Record causal interpretation and invariant results."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Failure model","Dependency graph"],"prerequisites":["Single-fault evidence"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B61-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reliability_testing_practice","source":"Chaos and fault-injection engineering","basis":"Interacting failure conditions can expose nonlinear system behavior."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Multi-fault testing"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B70-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Verification","concept":"Reliability regression testing","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"How should reliability regressions be detected after code or infrastructure changes?","answer":"Maintain repeatable reliability scenarios and compare current results with previously validated behavior using versioned workloads, fault conditions, invariants, and acceptance thresholds.","explanation":"Functional tests can remain green while failure handling, latency under degradation, recovery time, or resource behavior regresses.","principle":"Reliability behavior is part of the system contract and should be regression-tested.","rule":"Re-run representative failure and recovery scenarios after material changes to reliability-sensitive components.","examples":[{"scenario":"A worker-pool implementation changes.","expected_behavior":"Concurrency-limit, queue-overload, shutdown, and recovery scenarios remain within validated bounds."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Run only unit tests because application functionality is unchanged.","why_invalid":"Reliability behavior may have changed independently."}],"problem":"Reliability regressions escape ordinary functional testing.","failure":"Previously handled failure becomes an outage.","cause":"No reliability regression suite.","solution":"Version and rerun representative resilience scenarios.","alternatives":["Continuous chaos testing","Pre-release resilience certification"],"tradeoffs":["Reliability tests increase CI/CD duration and infrastructure cost."],"constraints":["Tests must be deterministic enough to identify regressions."],"edge_cases":["Infrastructure version changes alter behavior without application changes.","Historical baseline is no longer applicable."],"security":["Test environments must not expose production secrets."],"performance":["Use targeted high-value scenarios rather than exhaustive repetition."],"testing":["Compare metrics and invariant outcomes across versions."],"verification":["Require evidence for changed reliability characteristics."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Test harness","Metrics","Version control"],"prerequisites":["Baseline reliability scenarios"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B69-004"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"software_testing_practice","source":"Reliability regression testing and SRE release engineering","basis":"Reliability properties can regress independently of functional behavior."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Reliability regression"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B70-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability Verification","concept":"Evidence-based reliability closure","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"When should a reliability failure or recovery gap be considered closed?","answer":"Closure requires verified remediation, successful relevant testing, updated operational evidence, and confirmation that the original failure mechanism or risk condition is no longer present within the defined scope.","explanation":"Implementing a code change is not equivalent to proving that the reliability problem has been solved.","principle":"Reliability issues close on evidence, not implementation status.","rule":"Do not mark a reliability defect or recovery gap resolved until its acceptance criteria have been independently verified.","examples":[{"scenario":"Restore procedure previously failed because encryption keys were unavailable.","expected_behavior":"The remediation is closed only after a complete restore test demonstrates usable recovery with key access."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Close the incident action because the code change was merged.","why_invalid":"No evidence demonstrates that the failure condition is eliminated."}],"problem":"Reliability work is marked complete without proof.","failure":"Known weakness returns during a future incident.","cause":"Implementation-based rather than evidence-based closure.","solution":"Define acceptance criteria and require test evidence before closure.","alternatives":["Automated compliance gates","Reliability certification"],"tradeoffs":["Evidence requirements increase completion effort but reduce false closure."],"constraints":["Evidence must correspond to the actual affected scope and version."],"edge_cases":["The original failure mechanism cannot be reproduced.","A workaround reduces impact without removing root risk."],"security":["Closure must include security-related recovery checks when relevant."],"performance":["Measure whether remediation introduces new resource pressure."],"testing":["Repeat the original failing scenario and relevant regression scenarios."],"verification":["Attach immutable evidence to the closure record."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Incident records","Testing","Evidence store"],"prerequisites":["Defined acceptance criteria"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B69-003"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B70-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reliability_verification_practice","source":"SRE incident remediation and engineering verification","basis":"Reliability remediation should be closed using measurable evidence."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_practice","claim_scope":"Reliability issue closure"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B71-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability boundaries","concept":"Explicit failure domains","knowledge_type":"architecture_principle","question":"How should reliability boundaries be defined in a distributed system?","answer":"A reliability boundary should isolate failures so that a fault inside one domain does not automatically consume the resources, correctness guarantees, or recovery mechanisms of another domain.","explanation":"A boundary is useful only when dependencies crossing it are constrained and observable. Merely placing components in separate processes or hosts does not create meaningful isolation if they share a database, queue, control plane, network bottleneck, or recovery dependency.","principle":"A failure domain is only as independent as its strongest shared dependency.","rule":"Identify shared resources and recovery dependencies before declaring components fault-isolated.","examples":[{"scenario":"Two service partitions use independent compute pools but share one saturated database connection pool.","expected_behavior":"The architecture must treat the database dependency as a shared failure domain and protect each partition from database-induced resource exhaustion."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declare two services isolated because they run on different machines.","why_invalid":"They may still share critical dependencies and therefore fail together."}],"problem":"Failures propagate across nominal architectural boundaries.","failure":"A local fault becomes a system-wide outage.","cause":"Hidden shared dependencies or unbounded cross-boundary resource consumption.","solution":"Map dependencies, assign explicit failure domains, enforce resource isolation, and test boundary behavior.","alternatives":["Cell-based architecture","Bulkhead isolation","Independent deployment domains"],"tradeoffs":["More isolation improves blast-radius control but increases operational complexity and duplicated infrastructure."],"constraints":["Shared dependencies may prevent complete independence.","Isolation must cover recovery paths as well as steady-state traffic."],"edge_cases":["Shared identity systems","Shared DNS","Shared control planes","Shared storage","Emergency recovery dependencies"],"security":["Compromise of a shared dependency can bypass otherwise strong isolation."],"performance":["Isolation can reduce resource sharing efficiency.","Per-domain capacity must be sized independently."],"testing":["Inject faults into each domain and verify that unrelated domains remain within defined service objectives."],"verification":["Dependency graph reviewed","Shared-resource inventory completed","Cross-domain failure test passed"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Dependency mapping","Fault isolation","Resource governance"],"prerequisites":["Distributed systems fundamentals"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B71-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Use fault isolation to protect your workload","basis":"Defines fault isolation as limiting the impact of a component or system failure to a defined boundary."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"General reliability architecture"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B71-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability boundaries","concept":"Cell architecture","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"When is cell-based architecture useful for reliability?","answer":"Cell-based architecture is useful when workloads can be partitioned into independently operating units whose failures can be contained within a bounded subset of traffic or customers.","explanation":"Cells reduce correlated impact by limiting the amount of workload exposed to a single failure. The design must prevent shared infrastructure or centralized control paths from defeating the intended isolation.","principle":"Reduce the maximum blast radius rather than assuming every component will remain healthy.","rule":"A cell architecture requires independent capacity, dependency paths, and recovery behavior sufficient to prevent one cell failure from becoming global.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service partitions tenants across cells with separate compute and queues.","expected_behavior":"Failure in one cell should primarily affect tenants assigned to that cell while other cells continue serving."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Multiple cells all depend synchronously on one global processing queue.","why_invalid":"The shared queue can become a common failure point and invalidate cell isolation."}],"problem":"A large distributed workload has excessive correlated-failure scope.","failure":"One incident affects a disproportionate fraction of users.","cause":"Insufficient partitioning or centralized dependencies.","solution":"Partition traffic and resources into independently recoverable cells and minimize shared dependencies.","alternatives":["Availability-zone isolation","Tenant sharding","Regional partitioning"],"tradeoffs":["Improves blast-radius control but complicates routing, balancing, deployment, and capacity management."],"constraints":["Partitioning must be deterministic or safely recoverable.","Cross-cell operations need explicit semantics."],"edge_cases":["Hot cells","Uneven tenant distribution","Cell migration during failure","Global configuration changes"],"security":["Cell isolation can reduce lateral impact but must not become a bypass around tenant authorization."],"performance":["Cross-cell operations may add latency.","Cell-local caching can improve locality."],"testing":["Fail one complete cell and verify unaffected cells continue operating.","Test cell evacuation and rebalancing."],"verification":["Cell independence review","Blast-radius measurement","Failure-containment test"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Failure domains","Traffic partitioning","Capacity isolation"],"prerequisites":["Distributed architecture"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B71-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar","basis":"Supports fault isolation and architectures that limit failure scope."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Distributed workload architecture"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B71-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability boundaries","concept":"Shared dependency amplification","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"Why can a small shared dependency become a system-wide reliability risk?","answer":"A shared dependency can amplify a local fault when many otherwise independent workloads compete for the same limited resource or all require the dependency before making progress.","explanation":"A dependency can turn parallel failure domains into a single correlated failure domain. Amplification becomes especially severe when retries, queueing, connection acquisition, or recovery activity increases load on the dependency.","principle":"Dependency fan-in determines how widely a failure can propagate.","rule":"Evaluate both dependency criticality and the number of consumers sharing its failure capacity.","examples":[{"scenario":"Hundreds of workers reconnect simultaneously after a dependency restart.","expected_behavior":"Connection attempts must be bounded and coordinated so recovery does not overload the dependency."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Measure only direct request traffic and ignore recovery traffic.","why_invalid":"Recovery traffic can exceed normal traffic and trigger a second failure."}],"problem":"A shared dependency becomes overloaded during an incident.","failure":"Recovery activity causes cascading failure.","cause":"High fan-in combined with unbounded recovery behavior.","solution":"Bound concurrency, introduce backoff, partition capacity, and model recovery load separately.","alternatives":["Dependency sharding","Admission control","Centralized connection management"],"tradeoffs":["Additional controls reduce peak recovery load but may increase recovery time."],"constraints":["Dependency capacity must be measured or conservatively bounded.","Recovery behavior must be deterministic enough to analyze."],"edge_cases":["Mass reconnect","Cache expiration storms","Certificate rotation","Regional failover"],"security":["Attackers may intentionally target high-fan-in dependencies to maximize blast radius."],"performance":["Connection pools and queues can become hidden bottlenecks."],"testing":["Simulate dependency restoration with realistic concurrent consumers."],"verification":["Recovery-load budget defined","Fan-in dependencies identified","Mass-reconnect test completed"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Dependency graph","Overload control","Recovery orchestration"],"prerequisites":["Distributed systems and capacity fundamentals"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B71-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Addressing Cascading Failures","basis":"Describes overload and failure amplification mechanisms in distributed systems."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Distributed failure propagation"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B71-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability boundaries","concept":"Recovery-domain independence","knowledge_type":"recovery_principle","question":"Why must recovery paths be included when defining failure domains?","answer":"A component is not truly isolated if its recovery requires a failed shared service, shared operator path, or shared resource that can prevent restoration.","explanation":"Designs often isolate serving resources but accidentally centralize recovery. During a major failure, the recovery dependency can become the actual system-wide bottleneck.","principle":"Recovery independence is part of failure independence.","rule":"For every critical failure domain, identify and test the resources required to restore it while the primary dependency is unavailable.","examples":[{"scenario":"A region can serve independently but requires a global control service to restart workloads.","expected_behavior":"The architecture must provide a recovery path that remains usable during global control-plane failure."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Claim regional independence while recovery requires a single global controller.","why_invalid":"The controller is a shared recovery dependency."}],"problem":"Recovery cannot proceed during a broad outage.","failure":"A theoretically isolated domain remains unavailable.","cause":"Recovery dependency was omitted from the failure model.","solution":"Design local recovery capability, cached configuration, or independent emergency procedures.","alternatives":["Static stability","Local recovery agents","Pre-provisioned recovery resources"],"tradeoffs":["Independent recovery increases complexity and may require additional capacity."],"constraints":["Emergency mechanisms must be secured and auditable.","Recovery configuration must remain sufficiently current."],"edge_cases":["Global control-plane outage","Expired credentials","Configuration drift","Network partition"],"security":["Emergency recovery paths require strict authorization and auditing."],"performance":["Cached recovery state can reduce restoration latency."],"testing":["Disable the primary recovery dependency and execute the recovery procedure."],"verification":["Recovery dependency graph","Independent recovery drill","Audit trail validation"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Fault domains","Disaster recovery","Control-plane reliability"],"prerequisites":["Failure-mode analysis"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B71-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Data plane and control plane recovery guidance","basis":"Recommends relying on the data plane rather than the control plane during recovery."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Recovery architecture"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B71-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability boundaries","concept":"Failure-domain validation","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"How can an engineer verify that an intended failure boundary actually contains failures?","answer":"Inject representative failures into the boundary and measure whether traffic, resources, state, and recovery activity outside the boundary remain within defined limits.","explanation":"Architectural diagrams alone cannot prove isolation. Validation must include shared dependencies, recovery behavior, resource exhaustion, and operational controls.","principle":"Isolation is an empirical property that should be tested, not merely declared.","rule":"Every critical failure boundary requires at least one controlled failure test covering both steady-state impact and recovery impact.","examples":[{"scenario":"Terminate all workers in one partition.","expected_behavior":"Other partitions remain available and do not consume abnormal recovery resources because of the failed partition."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Review architecture documentation and mark isolation verified without a failure test.","why_invalid":"Documentation cannot establish runtime containment."}],"problem":"Declared isolation does not match runtime behavior.","failure":"Failure crosses the intended boundary.","cause":"Undetected shared dependency or recovery coupling.","solution":"Use controlled fault injection and compare observed impact with the declared boundary.","alternatives":["Game days","Chaos experiments","Formal dependency analysis"],"tradeoffs":["Testing increases operational risk unless blast radius is carefully controlled."],"constraints":["Experiments require abort conditions and monitoring.","Production tests must be authorized."],"edge_cases":["Rare control-plane paths","Background jobs","Emergency automation","Telemetry dependencies"],"security":["Fault injection must not create unauthorized access or destructive effects."],"performance":["Measure secondary load created by the injected fault."],"testing":["Controlled failure injection","Recovery validation","Blast-radius measurement"],"verification":["Expected-versus-observed impact comparison","Recovery evidence retained"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Fault injection","Observability","Failure domains"],"prerequisites":["Defined reliability boundaries"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B71-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Test reliability","basis":"Recommends resiliency testing and game days to validate failure behavior."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Reliability verification"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B72-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure semantics","concept":"Partial success semantics","knowledge_type":"distributed_system_rule","question":"How should systems represent partial success in multi-step operations?","answer":"The system should explicitly represent which steps completed, which failed, and whether remaining work is retryable, compensatable, or requires human intervention.","explanation":"A binary success or failure result can hide completed side effects and cause unsafe retries or incorrect recovery.","principle":"Failure state must preserve enough information to determine safe next actions.","rule":"Never infer complete rollback from a generic failure response when earlier side effects may have committed.","examples":[{"scenario":"A workflow completes payment but fails before notification delivery.","expected_behavior":"Persist the payment completion and separately recover notification delivery."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Retry the entire workflow because the final response was an error.","why_invalid":"The retry may duplicate an already committed side effect."}],"problem":"A multi-step workflow reports only a final status.","failure":"Recovery duplicates or loses side effects.","cause":"Loss of intermediate execution state.","solution":"Persist step-level outcomes and define explicit recovery semantics.","alternatives":["Transactional orchestration","Saga state machine","Idempotent workflow replay"],"tradeoffs":["More durable state increases storage and implementation complexity."],"constraints":["Execution state must survive process restart.","State transitions require integrity guarantees."],"edge_cases":["Unknown outcome after timeout","Process crash after external commit","Duplicate completion messages"],"security":["Execution records may contain sensitive business information and require access control."],"performance":["Durable workflow state introduces persistence overhead."],"testing":["Crash at every workflow boundary and verify recovery state."],"verification":["Step outcome persisted","Replay produces correct final state","Unknown outcomes handled explicitly"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Durable state","Idempotency","Workflow orchestration"],"prerequisites":["Distributed transaction fundamentals"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B72-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Data Integrity and Reliable Product Launches","basis":"Supports explicit handling of correctness and failure state in distributed workflows."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Distributed workflow correctness"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B72-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure semantics","concept":"Unknown outcome handling","knowledge_type":"recovery_pattern","question":"What should a system do when it cannot determine whether an external operation succeeded?","answer":"It should enter an explicit unknown-outcome state and use a safe reconciliation mechanism rather than blindly repeating the operation.","explanation":"Timeouts and connection failures can occur after a remote system has committed an operation. The local caller therefore cannot safely interpret communication failure as operation failure.","principle":"Transport failure does not imply business-operation failure.","rule":"Unknown outcomes must be reconciled using authoritative state, idempotency, status queries, or durable transaction records before deciding whether to repeat the operation.","examples":[{"scenario":"A request times out after the remote service accepted it.","expected_behavior":"Query or reconcile the operation before issuing another non-idempotent command."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treat every timeout as a guaranteed failed operation.","why_invalid":"The remote operation may already have committed."}],"problem":"The caller cannot determine remote commit status.","failure":"Duplicate or contradictory side effects occur.","cause":"Ambiguous network failure after remote execution.","solution":"Implement explicit reconciliation and idempotent operation identifiers.","alternatives":["Transactional messaging","Authoritative status lookup","Exactly-once business semantics where achievable"],"tradeoffs":["Reconciliation adds latency and state management."],"constraints":["Remote service must expose sufficient status information or durable identifiers."],"edge_cases":["Remote service unavailable","Status query also times out","Operation partially committed"],"security":["Operation identifiers must not be guessable or authorize unintended access."],"performance":["Reconciliation queries can increase dependency load during incidents."],"testing":["Inject response loss after remote commit and validate reconciliation."],"verification":["Unknown state represented","No unsafe blind retry","Final business state reconciled"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Idempotency","Durable operation identity","Reconciliation"],"prerequisites":["Distributed failure semantics"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B72-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Data Integrity: What You Read Is What You Wrote","basis":"Emphasizes correctness of state and recovery in distributed systems."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Distributed operation semantics"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B72-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure semantics","concept":"Semantic idempotency","knowledge_type":"correctness_pattern","question":"What is semantic idempotency and why is it important for reliability?","answer":"Semantic idempotency means repeated execution of the same logical operation produces the same intended business result rather than merely avoiding duplicate transport requests.","explanation":"A technical request identifier alone is insufficient if retries can produce different business outcomes. The operation must define identity, state transitions, and duplicate behavior at the business level.","principle":"Reliable retries require stable business semantics, not only stable network behavior.","rule":"Define duplicate-operation behavior explicitly for every externally visible operation that may be retried.","examples":[{"scenario":"A create-order request is retried after an uncertain response.","expected_behavior":"The same operation identity returns or references the original order rather than creating another order."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Generate a new request ID on every retry of the same business operation.","why_invalid":"The server may interpret each retry as a new operation."}],"problem":"Retries create duplicate business effects.","failure":"Duplicate orders, charges, jobs, or records.","cause":"No stable business operation identity.","solution":"Use idempotency keys or equivalent domain identifiers with durable duplicate detection.","alternatives":["Natural business keys","Transactional deduplication","Command sequence numbers"],"tradeoffs":["Deduplication state requires retention and lifecycle management."],"constraints":["Operation identity must remain stable for the required retry window."],"edge_cases":["Expired idempotency records","Conflicting payloads using the same key","Concurrent duplicate requests"],"security":["Idempotency identifiers must not allow unauthorized access to another operation's result."],"performance":["Deduplication lookups add persistence overhead."],"testing":["Concurrent duplicate requests","Delayed retries","Payload mismatch tests"],"verification":["Repeated logical operation yields one defined business outcome","Conflicting reuse is rejected"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Durable state","Retry policy"],"prerequisites":["API reliability fundamentals"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B72-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_practice","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar","basis":"Reliability guidance includes retry and distributed-system failure handling patterns."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Retryable business operations"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B72-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure semantics","concept":"Monotonic recovery state","knowledge_type":"state_machine_principle","question":"Why should recovery state transitions often be monotonic?","answer":"Recovery state should move through explicitly ordered states without silently reverting to a less certain state, except through a defined compensating transition.","explanation":"Monotonic state progression reduces ambiguity during retries, restarts, and concurrent recovery workers. It makes replay and auditing easier because previously established facts are not casually discarded.","principle":"Recovery should preserve established facts and make reversals explicit.","rule":"Do not overwrite a confirmed state with an earlier or less informative state without a validated transition.","examples":[{"scenario":"A job is confirmed completed, then a delayed worker reports failure.","expected_behavior":"The system preserves the authoritative completion state and records the conflicting observation separately."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Last writer always wins for recovery state.","why_invalid":"A stale observation can overwrite newer authoritative state."}],"problem":"Concurrent recovery actors produce conflicting observations.","failure":"System state oscillates or regresses.","cause":"Unordered state updates.","solution":"Use versioned state transitions, monotonic facts, or explicit conflict resolution.","alternatives":["Compare-and-swap","Event sourcing","Versioned state machines"],"tradeoffs":["Versioning increases state-management complexity."],"constraints":["State ordering must have a reliable basis.","Conflicting observations require explicit resolution."],"edge_cases":["Clock skew","Delayed messages","Duplicate events","Restored stale replicas"],"security":["State transitions should be authorized and tamper-evident where critical."],"performance":["Conflict detection adds metadata and comparison work."],"testing":["Out-of-order event tests","Concurrent recovery tests","Stale replica tests"],"verification":["No illegal state regression","Conflicts retained and resolved deterministically"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["State versioning","Event ordering","Recovery orchestration"],"prerequisites":["State-machine fundamentals"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B72-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"distributed_systems_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Managing Critical State: Distributed Consensus for Reliability","basis":"Addresses reliable state management under distributed failures."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Distributed state recovery"},"confidence":0.93,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B72-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure semantics","concept":"Failure classification before recovery","knowledge_type":"decision_rule","question":"Why should recovery logic classify failures before selecting a recovery action?","answer":"Different failure classes require different actions, and applying a generic recovery mechanism can amplify the original failure or corrupt state.","explanation":"Transient communication failures, permanent invalid input, dependency overload, data corruption, and authorization failures have different safe responses.","principle":"Recovery action must be conditioned on failure semantics.","rule":"Classify failure cause and confidence before choosing retry, failover, compensation, quarantine, or manual intervention.","examples":[{"scenario":"A dependency returns a validation error.","expected_behavior":"Reject or correct the request rather than repeatedly retrying it."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Retry every non-success response using the same policy.","why_invalid":"Permanent failures can become amplified load."}],"problem":"Recovery mechanisms treat unrelated failures identically.","failure":"Retry storms, repeated invalid operations, or prolonged outages.","cause":"Missing failure taxonomy.","solution":"Define actionable failure classes and map each class to bounded recovery behavior.","alternatives":["Typed errors","Policy-driven recovery","State-machine recovery"],"tradeoffs":["Detailed classification requires additional engineering and telemetry."],"constraints":["Classification must remain robust when diagnostic information is incomplete."],"edge_cases":["Unknown errors","Mixed causes","Dependency-specific error translation"],"security":["Security failures should not be converted into retries that bypass authorization controls."],"performance":["Failure classification should be lightweight on hot paths."],"testing":["Inject each failure class and verify the selected recovery action."],"verification":["Failure taxonomy documented","Action mapping deterministic","Unknown class safely handled"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Error taxonomy","Recovery policy"],"prerequisites":["Reliability failure modeling"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B72-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Failure management","basis":"Separates detection, failover, automated healing, and recovery practices according to failure conditions."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Failure recovery design"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B73-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload resilience","concept":"Admission control","knowledge_type":"resilience_pattern","question":"What role does admission control play in reliable systems?","answer":"Admission control limits new work when available capacity or reliability budgets are insufficient, preventing accepted work from overwhelming the system.","explanation":"A system that accepts unlimited work can turn overload into resource exhaustion and cascading failure. Admission control converts uncontrolled demand into bounded behavior.","principle":"Protect system capacity before accepting additional work.","rule":"Define explicit admission limits for critical resources and reject or defer work when those limits are reached.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service reaches its safe concurrency limit.","expected_behavior":"Reject or defer excess requests rather than allowing unlimited queue growth."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Accept every request and let workers process them eventually.","why_invalid":"Unbounded backlog can exhaust memory, increase latency, and make recovery harder."}],"problem":"Demand exceeds safe processing capacity.","failure":"Resource exhaustion and cascading latency.","cause":"Unlimited admission.","solution":"Implement bounded concurrency, queue limits, priority rules, and explicit overload responses.","alternatives":["Load shedding","Rate limiting","Priority queues"],"tradeoffs":["Rejected work may reduce immediate availability but preserve system stability."],"constraints":["Admission limits require capacity-aware configuration.","Clients need explicit overload semantics."],"edge_cases":["Bursty traffic","Priority traffic","Recovery after dependency restoration"],"security":["Admission controls can reduce resource-exhaustion attack impact."],"performance":["Bounded concurrency improves predictability under overload."],"testing":["Progressively increase load until admission thresholds activate."],"verification":["System remains within defined resource limits under overload","Rejected work is observable"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Capacity management","Overload detection","Queue management"],"prerequisites":["Resource saturation concepts"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B73-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Handling Overload","basis":"Describes overload protection and controlled rejection as reliability mechanisms."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Overload resilience"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B73-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload resilience","concept":"Priority-aware load shedding","knowledge_type":"resilience_strategy","question":"How should load shedding preserve critical functionality during overload?","answer":"Load shedding should preferentially reject lower-value or non-critical work while preserving capacity for explicitly protected critical operations.","explanation":"Uniform rejection can unnecessarily disable essential functionality. Priority-aware shedding requires stable workload classification and bounded capacity reserved for important operations.","principle":"When capacity is scarce, preserve the most important work deliberately.","rule":"Define workload priority before overload occurs and enforce priority-specific admission or shedding behavior.","examples":[{"scenario":"A platform is overloaded by analytics jobs while transaction processing remains critical.","expected_behavior":"Analytics work is deferred or rejected before critical transaction capacity is exhausted."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Randomly reject requests without considering business criticality.","why_invalid":"Critical and non-critical work receive the same protection."}],"problem":"Overload threatens all workload classes equally.","failure":"Critical operations become unavailable.","cause":"No priority-aware capacity policy.","solution":"Partition capacity and define priority-specific shedding rules.","alternatives":["Dedicated capacity pools","Weighted queues","Separate service tiers"],"tradeoffs":["Priority systems increase policy complexity and can starve lower classes if poorly designed."],"constraints":["Priority must be explicit and resistant to abuse.","Reserved capacity requires accurate workload classification."],"edge_cases":["All workloads marked critical","Priority inversion","Changing business priorities during incidents"],"security":["Users must not be able to self-assign elevated priority without authorization."],"performance":["Priority queues can improve critical-path latency during overload."],"testing":["Saturate the system with low-priority work and verify critical traffic remains protected."],"verification":["Priority policy enforced","Critical capacity reservation observed","Starvation limits validated"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Admission control","Workload classification","Capacity partitioning"],"prerequisites":["Overload management"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B73-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Cascading Failures: Load Shedding and Graceful Degradation","basis":"Describes load shedding and preserving useful work under overload."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Overload behavior"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B73-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload resilience","concept":"Recovery traffic budgeting","knowledge_type":"capacity_rule","question":"Why should recovery traffic receive an explicit capacity budget?","answer":"Recovery actions such as retries, synchronization, cache rebuilding, and replica catch-up can create substantial additional load while the system is already impaired.","explanation":"Without a recovery budget, attempts to restore the system can compete with production traffic and prolong the incident.","principle":"Recovery consumes capacity and must be modeled as workload.","rule":"Reserve or bound capacity for recovery activity and prevent recovery concurrency from exceeding safe limits.","examples":[{"scenario":"A database replica returns after an outage and immediately attempts unrestricted synchronization.","expected_behavior":"Catch-up work is rate-limited so production traffic remains within safe capacity."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Allow all failed clients to reconnect simultaneously after recovery.","why_invalid":"The reconnect surge can overload the restored dependency."}],"problem":"Recovery itself creates overload.","failure":"Recovered components fail again or remain unstable.","cause":"Unbounded recovery work.","solution":"Throttle synchronization, reconnects, rebuilds, and background repair.","alternatives":["Staged recovery","Priority recovery","Warm standby capacity"],"tradeoffs":["Slower recovery can be safer than uncontrolled rapid recovery."],"constraints":["Recovery limits must be compatible with RTO requirements."],"edge_cases":["Large backlog","Multiple simultaneous dependencies recovering","Partial capacity restoration"],"security":["Recovery endpoints should remain protected against unauthorized mass requests."],"performance":["Recovery throughput must be balanced against serving capacity."],"testing":["Measure system behavior during simultaneous restoration and backlog replay."],"verification":["Recovery workload bounded","Serving capacity protected","RTO remains within defined objective where applicable"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Recovery orchestration","Capacity management","Queue control"],"prerequisites":["Overload and recovery fundamentals"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B73-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Cascading Failures","basis":"Highlights recovery-related overload and the need to control load during failure conditions."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Recovery under overload"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B73-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload resilience","concept":"Graceful degradation correctness","knowledge_type":"degradation_pattern","question":"What makes graceful degradation reliable rather than merely reducing functionality?","answer":"A degraded mode is reliable when it has explicitly defined behavior, preserves critical invariants, is bounded in resource use, and can be safely exited.","explanation":"Degraded behavior can introduce hidden correctness failures if it is rarely exercised or implemented as an emergency shortcut.","principle":"Reduced functionality must remain correct within its declared guarantees.","rule":"Define invariants and resource limits for every degraded mode and test transitions into and out of that mode.","examples":[{"scenario":"A recommendation system cannot reach its model service.","expected_behavior":"The product returns a documented fallback result without blocking critical transaction processing."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Return arbitrary cached or default data without defining freshness or correctness guarantees.","why_invalid":"The fallback may violate business invariants."}],"problem":"Normal functionality becomes unsafe under dependency loss.","failure":"Degraded mode introduces incorrect or misleading behavior.","cause":"Fallback behavior was not designed as a first-class mode.","solution":"Specify degraded-mode contracts, invariants, limits, observability, and recovery transitions.","alternatives":["Fail closed","Fail open for non-critical features","Feature suppression"],"tradeoffs":["Degraded service preserves availability but may reduce functionality or freshness."],"constraints":["Critical correctness properties cannot be sacrificed without explicit authorization."],"edge_cases":["Repeated mode transitions","Stale fallback data","Nested degraded dependencies"],"security":["Fallback paths must preserve authorization and data isolation."],"performance":["Fallback logic must be cheaper than the failed primary path when used for overload protection."],"testing":["Regularly exercise degraded mode and transition back to normal mode."],"verification":["Degraded invariants verified","Resource use bounded","Exit transition tested"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Load shedding","Fallback design","State management"],"prerequisites":["Failure-mode analysis"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B73-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Cascading Failures: Load Shedding and Graceful Degradation","basis":"Discusses degraded service behavior and risks of rarely exercised failure paths."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Graceful degradation"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B73-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Overload resilience","concept":"Overload feedback-loop prevention","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"How can overload controls themselves create instability?","answer":"Overload controls can create oscillations when their activation thresholds, recovery behavior, or feedback signals cause synchronized enable-disable cycles or shift excessive load to another component.","explanation":"A control mechanism changes system behavior and therefore becomes part of the feedback loop. Poorly designed thresholds can produce repeated shedding, recovery surges, or dependency oscillation.","principle":"Every resilience mechanism must be analyzed as part of the system dynamics it changes.","rule":"Test overload controls for hysteresis, synchronization, oscillation, and secondary bottlenecks.","examples":[{"scenario":"All instances simultaneously leave degraded mode after a short recovery.","expected_behavior":"Recovery should be staggered or controlled to prevent a second overload spike."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Use identical instantaneous thresholds on every instance without considering synchronized transitions.","why_invalid":"Instances may enter and exit modes simultaneously."}],"problem":"Resilience controls amplify rather than suppress instability.","failure":"Repeated overload and recovery cycles.","cause":"Synchronized feedback or threshold oscillation.","solution":"Use hysteresis, randomized staggering where appropriate, bounded recovery, and independent signals.","alternatives":["Adaptive control","Token-bucket admission","Staged recovery"],"tradeoffs":["More sophisticated controls require careful validation."],"constraints":["Control parameters must be observable and adjustable safely."],"edge_cases":["Rapid demand oscillation","Clock synchronization","Correlated autoscaling"],"security":["Attackers may exploit threshold behavior to trigger repeated state transitions."],"performance":["Control calculations should not materially increase hot-path latency."],"testing":["Oscillating-load tests","Recovery-surge tests","Threshold boundary tests"],"verification":["No sustained control oscillation","Secondary bottlenecks identified","Abort conditions defined"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Feedback control","Overload management","Observability"],"prerequisites":["Control-system fundamentals"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B73-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Cascading Failures","basis":"Discusses complex interactions between overload protection and system behavior."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Reliability control loops"},"confidence":0.92,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B74-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Maintenance reliability","concept":"Maintenance as a controlled failure mode","knowledge_type":"operational_principle","question":"Why should planned maintenance be modeled as a reliability event?","answer":"Maintenance changes capacity, topology, dependencies, and sometimes availability, so it should be evaluated as a controlled transition rather than assumed to be risk-free.","explanation":"A maintenance operation can expose correlated dependencies or remove redundancy. Treating it as a failure-relevant event enables capacity and blast-radius analysis.","principle":"Planned unavailability can create the same failure conditions as accidental faults.","rule":"Model maintenance actions as temporary changes to failure domains and available capacity.","examples":[{"scenario":"One redundant node is removed for maintenance.","expected_behavior":"The remaining capacity is verified to tolerate expected demand and additional faults."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assume maintenance is safe because the component is intentionally offline.","why_invalid":"Redundancy and capacity may be reduced during maintenance."}],"problem":"Planned changes unexpectedly reduce resilience.","failure":"A second fault causes outage during maintenance.","cause":"Maintenance state excluded from failure analysis.","solution":"Define maintenance modes, capacity margins, sequencing, and abort criteria.","alternatives":["Rolling maintenance","Blue-green replacement","Immutable replacement"],"tradeoffs":["Slower maintenance can reduce simultaneous exposure."],"constraints":["Maintenance windows may have business constraints.","Redundancy must be sufficient throughout the operation."],"edge_cases":["Multiple maintenance tasks","Unexpected demand spike","Failed restoration"],"security":["Maintenance access requires controlled authorization and audit."],"performance":["Reduced capacity may increase latency."],"testing":["Simulate maintenance state under realistic demand and fault injection."],"verification":["Capacity remains safe","Redundancy assumptions remain valid","Rollback path tested"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Change management","Fault domains","Capacity planning"],"prerequisites":["Reliability architecture"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B74-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Change management","basis":"Treats changes to workload and environment as reliability-relevant events."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Planned operational changes"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B74-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Maintenance reliability","concept":"Maintenance sequencing","knowledge_type":"change_strategy","question":"How should maintenance sequencing reduce correlated failure risk?","answer":"Maintenance should be sequenced so that independent redundancy is preserved and multiple components sharing a failure domain are not unnecessarily degraded at the same time.","explanation":"The order and concurrency of changes determine whether redundancy remains available. Sequential or fault-domain-aware changes can limit the probability that a second failure becomes service-impacting.","principle":"Change concurrency should respect failure-domain independence.","rule":"Do not perform simultaneous maintenance across components whose failure would remove the same resilience boundary unless explicitly validated.","examples":[{"scenario":"Three independent zones provide service capacity.","expected_behavior":"Maintenance removes capacity from one zone at a time while verifying remaining capacity."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Restart all redundant nodes simultaneously to reduce maintenance duration.","why_invalid":"Redundancy disappears during the operation."}],"problem":"Maintenance removes multiple layers of redundancy at once.","failure":"A routine change creates an outage.","cause":"Unsafe change concurrency.","solution":"Sequence changes by fault domain and verify health before proceeding.","alternatives":["Canary maintenance","Automated rolling replacement","Staged deployment"],"tradeoffs":["Sequential changes take longer but reduce correlated risk."],"constraints":["Maintenance must tolerate partial completion.","Automation must stop safely on health degradation."],"edge_cases":["Uneven capacity","Hidden shared dependencies","Simultaneous emergency changes"],"security":["Automation credentials must be scoped to the intended maintenance domain."],"performance":["Reduced capacity can temporarily increase load."],"testing":["Test stop-and-resume behavior after each maintenance stage."],"verification":["Fault-domain sequencing enforced","Health gates verified","Abort behavior tested"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Fault isolation","Change automation","Health verification"],"prerequisites":["Failure-domain mapping"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B74-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework — Implement change","basis":"Recommends fault-isolated deployment strategies and controlled changes."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Maintenance and deployment sequencing"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B74-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Maintenance reliability","concept":"Maintenance abort criteria","knowledge_type":"operational_guardrail","question":"What makes an automated maintenance operation safely abortable?","answer":"It requires measurable health gates, explicit stop conditions, bounded progress, and a safe state from which the operation can pause without increasing impact.","explanation":"Automation can execute changes faster than humans can react. Abort criteria prevent a healthy change from becoming a prolonged outage when observed behavior diverges from expectations.","principle":"Every automated change needs a safe stopping point.","rule":"Define abort conditions before execution and ensure the system can stop without leaving an unrecoverable intermediate state.","examples":[{"scenario":"Latency exceeds the deployment threshold during a rolling change.","expected_behavior":"The automation stops further changes and preserves the current healthy capacity."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Continue deployment because the operation was already approved.","why_invalid":"Approval does not override newly observed reliability evidence."}],"problem":"Automation continues despite worsening system health.","failure":"A localized issue becomes widespread.","cause":"No runtime abort guard.","solution":"Implement health gates, stop conditions, and safe checkpoints.","alternatives":["Manual approval gates","Progressive delivery","Transaction-like change orchestration"],"tradeoffs":["More gates can slow routine changes."],"constraints":["Health signals must be sufficiently reliable.","Abort logic itself must remain available during incidents."],"edge_cases":["Telemetry outage","False-positive health signal","Partial deployment"],"security":["Only authorized actors or automation should alter abort policies."],"performance":["Health checks must not become a new bottleneck."],"testing":["Force health threshold violations and verify immediate safe halt."],"verification":["Abort condition observed","No unsafe continuation","Intermediate state recoverable"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Observability","Change automation","Health gates"],"prerequisites":["Deployment reliability"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B74-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework — Implement change","basis":"Emphasizes automated, controlled changes and testing around deployment."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Automated change reliability"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B74-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Maintenance reliability","concept":"Operational readiness evidence","knowledge_type":"readiness_control","question":"What evidence should exist before a critical reliability-sensitive change enters production?","answer":"Evidence should cover functional behavior, resilience behavior, observability, rollback or recovery capability, operational procedures, and known limitations relevant to the change.","explanation":"A deployment is operationally incomplete if the system can be changed but operators cannot detect or recover from resulting failure.","principle":"Production readiness includes recovery readiness, not only feature correctness.","rule":"Require evidence for failure behavior and operational recovery before approving high-impact changes.","examples":[{"scenario":"A database schema change affects a critical workflow.","expected_behavior":"Evidence includes compatibility testing, rollback or forward-recovery strategy, monitoring, and recovery procedure."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Approve solely because unit tests pass.","why_invalid":"Unit tests do not establish operational recovery readiness."}],"problem":"Changes pass functional validation but fail operationally.","failure":"Production incident with no effective recovery path.","cause":"Incomplete readiness evidence.","solution":"Use an operational readiness checklist and explicit evidence gates.","alternatives":["Release readiness review","Automated deployment gates","Risk-based approval"],"tradeoffs":["Additional evidence collection increases release effort."],"constraints":["Evidence requirements should scale with change risk."],"edge_cases":["Emergency patches","Third-party changes","Unknown dependencies"],"security":["Readiness evidence should include security-relevant failure behavior for critical systems."],"performance":["Load and resilience tests may increase release lead time."],"testing":["Functional, resilience, recovery, observability tests"],"verification":["Evidence checklist complete","Known risks documented","Recovery owner identified"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Change management","Testing","Observability"],"prerequisites":["Operational readiness concepts"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B74-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework — Implement change","basis":"Describes operational readiness reviews involving testing, monitoring, and auditability."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Production change readiness"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B74-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Maintenance reliability","concept":"Configuration drift during maintenance","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why is configuration drift a reliability risk during repeated maintenance?","answer":"Drift causes nominally identical components or environments to behave differently, making failures less predictable and recovery procedures less trustworthy.","explanation":"Manual changes and incomplete synchronization can create hidden differences between production and recovery environments.","principle":"Recoverability depends on maintaining known configuration state.","rule":"Detect and reconcile configuration drift before and after reliability-sensitive changes.","examples":[{"scenario":"A standby environment contains an older configuration than the primary.","expected_behavior":"Drift is detected before failover and either corrected or explicitly accounted for."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assume standby configuration is identical because it was initially cloned.","why_invalid":"Subsequent changes may have diverged it."}],"problem":"Recovery environment differs from the tested configuration.","failure":"Failover behaves differently than expected.","cause":"Undetected configuration drift.","solution":"Use declarative configuration, drift detection, reconciliation, and periodic recovery validation.","alternatives":["Immutable infrastructure","Configuration management","Environment reconstruction"],"tradeoffs":["Continuous reconciliation adds tooling and operational complexity."],"constraints":["Some environment-specific values legitimately differ.","Drift detection must distinguish intended differences."],"edge_cases":["Emergency manual changes","Secret rotation","Version skew"],"security":["Configuration drift can introduce security-control inconsistencies."],"performance":["Frequent reconciliation consumes control-plane resources."],"testing":["Compare primary and recovery configurations and execute failover after drift injection."],"verification":["Drift inventory complete","Expected differences documented","Recovery behavior validated"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Configuration management","Disaster recovery","Change management"],"prerequisites":["Environment management"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B74-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework — Failure management","basis":"Includes management of configuration drift at disaster recovery sites or regions."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Recovery configuration reliability"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B75-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Incident reliability","concept":"Incident command separation","knowledge_type":"operational_pattern","question":"Why should incident coordination be separated from unrestricted technical execution?","answer":"Separating coordination from execution reduces conflicting actions, preserves situational awareness, and allows technical responders to focus on diagnosis and mitigation.","explanation":"During complex incidents, many concurrent changes can create additional uncertainty. A coordination role can maintain objectives, priorities, communication, and change discipline.","principle":"Incident response benefits from explicit control of concurrent actions.","rule":"For sufficiently complex incidents, assign clear coordination and technical responsibilities with explicit authority boundaries.","examples":[{"scenario":"Multiple teams investigate a production outage.","expected_behavior":"One coordination function maintains incident state while technical teams execute bounded investigations and mitigations."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Allow every responder to independently modify production during a major incident.","why_invalid":"Concurrent uncontrolled changes can obscure causality and amplify impact."}],"problem":"Incident response becomes chaotic and difficult to reason about.","failure":"Conflicting actions prolong recovery.","cause":"Undefined command and change ownership.","solution":"Define incident roles, communication channels, action ownership, and escalation paths.","alternatives":["Single incident commander","Rotating incident lead","Automated orchestration with human oversight"],"tradeoffs":["Formal coordination introduces process overhead but improves complex incident control."],"constraints":["Roles must be understood before incidents.","Authority must remain clear under pressure."],"edge_cases":["Single-person incidents","Cross-organization incidents","Incident commander unavailable"],"security":["Emergency authority must remain auditable and bounded."],"performance":["Coordination should not block urgent low-risk mitigation."],"testing":["Conduct game days with explicit role assignment and escalation."],"verification":["Roles documented","Decision log maintained","Conflicting actions detected"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Incident management","Change control","Communication"],"prerequisites":["Operational incident response"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B75-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Managing Incidents and Emergency Response","basis":"Covers structured incident response and defined operational roles."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Incident management"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B75-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Incident reliability","concept":"Decision logging during incidents","knowledge_type":"operational_evidence","question":"Why should critical incident decisions be recorded during response?","answer":"Decision records preserve context about observations, assumptions, actions, and reversals so responders can coordinate and later determine which interventions affected system behavior.","explanation":"Without decision context, teams may repeat failed actions, contradict each other, or lose evidence needed for post-incident learning.","principle":"Incident state should be reconstructable from durable evidence.","rule":"Record material decisions, observed evidence, action ownership, and important changes during major incidents.","examples":[{"scenario":"A team disables a feature to reduce dependency load.","expected_behavior":"The action, reason, observed evidence, owner, and restoration condition are recorded."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Rely entirely on memory after the incident.","why_invalid":"Memory is incomplete and loses temporal context."}],"problem":"Incident reasoning cannot be reconstructed.","failure":"Repeated mistakes and weak post-incident analysis.","cause":"Insufficient operational evidence.","solution":"Maintain a structured incident timeline and decision log.","alternatives":["Automated audit logs","Incident state documents","Event-sourced operations"],"tradeoffs":["Logging adds operational effort and may generate sensitive information."],"constraints":["Logs must preserve confidentiality and access controls."],"edge_cases":["Fast-moving incidents","Conflicting observations","Missing telemetry"],"security":["Incident records may contain sensitive operational details and require restricted access."],"performance":["Logging should not depend on the failing production path."],"testing":["Game-day verification of decision capture under pressure."],"verification":["Timeline complete","Actions attributable","Evidence preserved"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Incident management","Auditability","Observability"],"prerequisites":["Incident response"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B75-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Managing Incidents and Postmortem Culture","basis":"Supports structured incident management and learning from operational events."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Incident evidence"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B75-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Incident reliability","concept":"Mitigation before diagnosis","knowledge_type":"incident_strategy","question":"When can mitigation appropriately precede complete root-cause diagnosis?","answer":"Mitigation can precede complete diagnosis when the action is low-risk, reversible, evidence-supported, and likely to reduce current harm without destroying critical evidence.","explanation":"Waiting for perfect understanding can prolong user impact, while uncontrolled mitigation can make the system harder to diagnose. The correct approach balances immediate harm reduction with evidence preservation.","principle":"Incident response optimizes for safe stabilization before complete explanation when appropriate.","rule":"Prefer reversible, bounded mitigation when ongoing harm is material and diagnostic certainty is incomplete.","examples":[{"scenario":"A non-critical feature is causing extreme dependency load.","expected_behavior":"Disable the feature using a reversible control while investigation continues."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Perform broad irreversible changes before understanding their impact.","why_invalid":"The action can worsen the incident and destroy causal evidence."}],"problem":"Diagnosis takes longer than acceptable user impact.","failure":"Outage persists unnecessarily.","cause":"No safe mitigation path.","solution":"Predefine reversible emergency controls and use evidence-driven mitigation."],"alternatives":["Failover","Feature suppression","Traffic reduction"],"tradeoffs":["Early mitigation may reduce evidence or mask the original trigger."],"constraints":["Mitigation must be authorized and reversible where possible."],"edge_cases":["Security incidents","Data corruption","Unknown side effects"],"security":["Mitigation must not disable essential security controls without explicit authorization."],"performance":["Mitigation should reduce load or resource pressure rapidly."],"testing":["Exercise emergency controls during game days."],"verification":["Mitigation effect measurable","Rollback available","Evidence retained"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Incident response","Emergency controls","Observability"],"prerequisites":["Operational incident management"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B75-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Emergency Response","basis":"Structured incident response prioritizes restoring service while maintaining disciplined investigation."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Incident mitigation"},"confidence":0.93,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B75-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Incident reliability","concept":"Evidence-preserving mitigation","knowledge_type":"incident_rule","question":"How can incident responders mitigate failures without destroying diagnostic evidence?","answer":"Prefer reversible configuration changes, preserve logs and state snapshots, record exact action times, and avoid destructive cleanup until required evidence has been captured.","explanation":"Recovery actions can change system state and eliminate clues about failure propagation. Evidence preservation allows later causal analysis without delaying necessary stabilization.","principle":"Recovery and investigation must be coordinated rather than treated as independent activities.","rule":"Capture critical evidence before destructive remediation whenever doing so does not materially increase harm.","examples":[{"scenario":"A process is consuming abnormal memory but service availability remains stable.","expected_behavior":"Capture diagnostic evidence before restarting it if the evidence can be collected safely."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Immediately delete logs and restart all components without preserving state.","why_invalid":"The action destroys evidence needed to determine cause."}],"problem":"Recovery actions erase evidence.","failure":"Root cause remains unknown and recurrence risk stays high.","cause":"No evidence-preservation procedure.","solution":"Define evidence capture priorities and integrate them into incident runbooks.","alternatives":["Snapshot before restart","Out-of-band diagnostics","Immutable telemetry"],"tradeoffs":["Evidence capture consumes time and resources."],"constraints":["User impact may require immediate destructive recovery."],"edge_cases":["Rapidly worsening incidents","Data corruption","Privacy-sensitive telemetry"],"security":["Evidence must be protected against unauthorized access and tampering."],"performance":["Diagnostic collection can increase system load."],"testing":["Test evidence capture under resource pressure."],"verification":["Required evidence preserved","Recovery still within acceptable impact limits"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Incident response","Observability","Forensics"],"prerequisites":["Failure investigation"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B75-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Troubleshooting and Managing Incidents","basis":"Supports structured troubleshooting and disciplined incident response."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Operational incident investigation"},"confidence":0.93,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B75-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Incident reliability","concept":"Incident closure criteria","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"What conditions should be satisfied before declaring a reliability incident closed?","answer":"Closure should require restored service behavior, stabilized dependencies, verified removal or containment of active failure conditions, and completion of required follow-up evidence capture.","explanation":"A temporary recovery signal is not sufficient if the system remains unstable or recovery work is incomplete.","principle":"Incident closure is a verified state transition, not merely disappearance of alerts.","rule":"Define explicit closure criteria based on service behavior, system stability, and remaining risk.","examples":[{"scenario":"Traffic returns to normal after failover.","expected_behavior":"Confirm dependency health, backlog recovery, data correctness, and stability before closure."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Close the incident immediately when the primary alert clears.","why_invalid":"The underlying failure or recovery backlog may remain."}],"problem":"Incidents are closed before the system is stable.","failure":"Premature closure followed by recurrence.","cause":"Alert-driven rather than state-driven closure.","solution":"Use multi-signal closure criteria and post-recovery observation windows.","alternatives":["Automated stability gates","Manual closure review"],"tradeoffs":["Longer observation delays formal closure but reduces premature closure risk."],"constraints":["Closure criteria must be measurable.","Critical incidents require accountable ownership."],"edge_cases":["Intermittent failures","Partial recovery","Long-running background repair"],"security":["Security-related incidents may require separate containment closure criteria."],"performance":["Backlog drain and repair can temporarily affect performance."],"testing":["Simulate false recovery and verify closure is blocked."],"verification":["Service healthy","Dependencies stable","Backlog acceptable","Required evidence captured"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Incident management","Observability","Recovery validation"],"prerequisites":["Incident lifecycle"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B75-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Managing Incidents and Tracking Outages","basis":"Supports structured incident lifecycle and outage tracking."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Incident closure"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B76-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Recovery orchestration","concept":"Recovery dependency ordering","knowledge_type":"recovery_algorithm","question":"How should recovery ordering be determined for dependent components?","answer":"Recover foundational dependencies before consumers when consumers cannot operate safely without them, while avoiding recovery actions that overload dependencies still stabilizing.","explanation":"Recovery is a dependency graph problem. Restoring services in arbitrary order can produce repeated failures or excessive retry traffic.","principle":"Recovery order should follow dependency readiness and capacity constraints.","rule":"Construct a recovery dependency graph and define safe readiness conditions for each recovery stage.","examples":[{"scenario":"Application workers depend on a database and message broker.","expected_behavior":"Database and broker reach verified readiness before workers resume normal processing."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Start all services simultaneously because automation is faster.","why_invalid":"Consumers may generate load against unavailable or unstable dependencies."}],"problem":"Recovery order causes repeated failures.","failure":"Restored services immediately fail again.","cause":"Missing dependency-aware recovery sequencing.","solution":"Use staged recovery with readiness gates and bounded concurrency.","alternatives":["Orchestrated state machine","Dependency-aware scheduler"],"tradeoffs":["Sequential recovery can increase total restoration time."],"constraints":["Dependency graph must remain current.","Some components may be safely restored in parallel."],"edge_cases":["Circular dependencies","Optional dependencies","Degraded dependencies"],"security":["Recovery orchestration requires privileged access and strict authorization."],"performance":["Parallel recovery should be limited by dependency capacity."],"testing":["Simulate staged restoration with dependency failures."],"verification":["Dependency order validated","Readiness gates enforced","Recovery load bounded"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Dependency graph","Recovery state machine","Health checks"],"prerequisites":["Distributed recovery"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B76-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Failure management","basis":"Supports automated recovery and designing workloads to withstand component failures."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Recovery orchestration"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B76-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Recovery orchestration","concept":"Readiness versus liveness during recovery","knowledge_type":"health_semantics","question":"Why should recovery orchestration distinguish readiness from basic process liveness?","answer":"A process can be running while its dependencies, state, or data are not ready to serve production traffic safely.","explanation":"Starting a process is not equivalent to restoring service capability. Recovery requires readiness criteria appropriate to the business operation.","principle":"Process existence is weaker evidence than functional readiness.","rule":"Resume production traffic only after the component satisfies dependency, state, and correctness readiness conditions.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service process starts but has not loaded required state.","expected_behavior":"It remains unavailable for production traffic until state initialization completes and is verified."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Route traffic as soon as the process accepts a network connection.","why_invalid":"The service may still be unable to perform correct operations."}],"problem":"Recovery automation marks components healthy too early.","failure":"Traffic reaches partially initialized components.","cause":"Liveness used as readiness signal.","solution":"Define functional readiness checks that reflect actual serving capability.","alternatives":["Synthetic transactions","Dependency-aware health checks","Warmup states"],"tradeoffs":["Deeper readiness checks add startup time and implementation complexity."],"constraints":["Readiness checks must avoid creating significant dependency load."],"edge_cases":["Partial state restoration","Read-only readiness","Degraded dependency"],"security":["Readiness endpoints must not expose sensitive diagnostic information."],"performance":["Synthetic checks consume resources and must be bounded."],"testing":["Start components with incomplete state and verify traffic remains blocked."],"verification":["Readiness criteria documented","Traffic gating verified","False-ready scenario tested"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Health checking","Recovery orchestration","State restoration"],"prerequisites":["Service lifecycle fundamentals"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B76-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Design workloads to withstand component failures","basis":"Emphasizes monitoring components and failover to healthy resources."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Recovery health semantics"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B76-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Recovery orchestration","concept":"Staged recovery","knowledge_type":"recovery_strategy","question":"Why is staged recovery safer than unrestricted simultaneous recovery?","answer":"Staged recovery limits concurrency, provides observation points, and allows the system to stop when a recovery stage produces unexpected load or behavior.","explanation":"Simultaneous recovery can create synchronization storms, dependency overload, and insufficient observability of intermediate states.","principle":"Recovery should progress through controlled states rather than one unbounded transition.","rule":"Use bounded recovery batches and verify each stage before expanding recovery scope.","examples":[{"scenario":"Thousands of workers are unavailable after an outage.","expected_behavior":"Restore a controlled subset, verify dependency load and correctness, then progressively expand."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Restart all workers at once to minimize restoration time.","why_invalid":"The recovery surge may overload dependencies."}],"problem":"Large-scale recovery creates secondary failure.","failure":"Restored resources repeatedly crash or overload dependencies.","cause":"Unbounded recovery concurrency.","solution":"Use staged recovery with capacity-aware gates.","alternatives":["Canary recovery","Cell-by-cell restoration","Priority-based recovery"],"tradeoffs":["May increase RTO compared with unrestricted parallel recovery."],"constraints":["Stage size must align with available capacity."],"edge_cases":["Very small surviving capacity","Hot partitions","Uneven workload distribution"],"security":["Recovery scope must be authorized and isolated."],"performance":["Recovery throughput should be measured against safe capacity."],"testing":["Large-scale restoration experiments"],"verification":["Stage gates pass","Dependency load remains safe","Expansion can halt cleanly"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Recovery capacity","Health verification","Admission control"],"prerequisites":["Recovery orchestration"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B76-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework — Failure management","basis":"Supports automated healing and controlled recovery practices."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Large-scale recovery"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B76-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Recovery orchestration","concept":"Recovery convergence","knowledge_type":"verification_concept","question":"What does convergence mean in a recovery process?","answer":"Recovery converges when repeated recovery actions move the system toward a stable intended state rather than repeatedly creating new failures or oscillating between states.","explanation":"A recovery algorithm can be active and making changes without actually approaching stability. Convergence requires measurable progress and bounded residual work.","principle":"Recovery should have a directionally stable path toward a defined target state.","rule":"Define target state, progress indicators, and failure thresholds that determine whether recovery is converging or should stop.","examples":[{"scenario":"A backlog decreases steadily while recovered workers remain healthy.","expected_behavior":"The system demonstrates measurable convergence toward normal operation."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Consider recovery successful because automated actions continue running.","why_invalid":"Activity does not prove progress toward stability."}],"problem":"Recovery actions continue without meaningful progress.","failure":"Endless recovery loop or oscillation.","cause":"No convergence criterion.","solution":"Track state transitions, backlog, health, and recovery progress against explicit convergence conditions.","alternatives":["Recovery state machine","Control-loop monitoring","Manual intervention threshold"],"tradeoffs":["Detailed convergence tracking increases observability requirements."],"constraints":["Progress metrics must reflect actual service recovery."],"edge_cases":["Noisy metrics","Intermittent dependencies","Partial recovery"],"security":["Recovery loops must have bounded authority to prevent runaway automated actions."],"performance":["Progress tracking should be lightweight."],"testing":["Inject recovery stalls and verify escalation."],"verification":["Convergence metric defined","Stall detected","Escalation path verified"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["State machine","Recovery metrics","Escalation"],"prerequisites":["Recovery lifecycle"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B76-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reliability_engineering","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar","basis":"Supports automated recovery while emphasizing validation of recovery effectiveness."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"medium_high","claim_scope":"General recovery control"},"confidence":0.91,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B76-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Recovery orchestration","concept":"Recovery escalation threshold","knowledge_type":"control_rule","question":"When should automated recovery stop and escalate to human intervention?","answer":"Automation should escalate when recovery exceeds bounded attempts, violates safety conditions, loses required evidence, encounters an unknown state, or fails to demonstrate convergence.","explanation":"Unbounded automation can amplify damage when assumptions are wrong. Escalation is a reliability control, not a failure of automation.","principle":"Automation must have explicit authority limits and failure boundaries.","rule":"Define maximum retries, time limits, state uncertainty conditions, and safety violations that force escalation.","examples":[{"scenario":"A repair loop repeatedly fails to restore a dependency.","expected_behavior":"The automation stops after the configured boundary and creates an actionable escalation."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Retry indefinitely until recovery succeeds.","why_invalid":"Persistent failure can consume resources and obscure the original fault."}],"problem":"Automated recovery persists after its assumptions become invalid.","failure":"Runaway repair activity or prolonged outage.","cause":"No escalation boundary.","solution":"Implement bounded recovery policies and explicit human escalation states.","alternatives":["Operator approval gates","Recovery circuit breaker","Manual takeover"],"tradeoffs":["Escalation may increase restoration time but limits uncontrolled automation."],"constraints":["Escalation channels must remain available during outages."],"edge_cases":["Communication failure","Unknown state","Conflicting telemetry"],"security":["Automated authority should be least-privilege and revocable."],"performance":["Bounded retries prevent resource exhaustion."],"testing":["Force repeated repair failure and verify automation halts."],"verification":["Escalation threshold tested","Authority revoked or halted","Operator receives actionable state"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Recovery orchestration","Governance","Incident management"],"prerequisites":["Automated recovery"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B76-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Automatically recover from failure","basis":"Supports automated recovery while requiring monitored and controlled behavior."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Automated recovery governance"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B77-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Data integrity under failure","concept":"Write acknowledgement semantics","knowledge_type":"data_reliability_rule","question":"Why must write acknowledgement semantics be explicit in reliable systems?","answer":"A write acknowledgement must define what durability, replication, validation, and visibility guarantees have actually been achieved before the system reports success.","explanation":"A successful transport response does not necessarily mean data is durably stored or visible to all required consumers.","principle":"Success responses must correspond to defined data guarantees.","rule":"Document exactly what a successful write acknowledgement guarantees and what remains asynchronous.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service acknowledges a write before replication completes.","expected_behavior":"The contract explicitly states that replication is asynchronous and defines the recovery behavior if the primary fails before replication."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treat any successful network response as proof of durable persistence.","why_invalid":"The storage layer may not have committed the data durably."}],"problem":"Clients misunderstand persistence guarantees.","failure":"Data loss or inconsistent recovery expectations.","cause":"Ambiguous acknowledgement semantics.","solution":"Define durability, visibility, and replication guarantees in the API or storage contract.","alternatives":["Synchronous replication","Durable queue","Write-ahead logging"],"tradeoffs":["Stronger acknowledgement guarantees can increase latency and resource cost."],"constraints":["Guarantees must match actual implementation."],"edge_cases":["Primary failure after acknowledgement","Replication lag","Partial commit"],"security":["Sensitive data must not be acknowledged as protected unless required security controls are actually applied."],"performance":["Stronger durability can increase write latency."],"testing":["Crash immediately after acknowledgement and validate documented guarantees."],"verification":["Acknowledgement semantics documented","Crash test matches contract"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Storage durability","Replication","API contracts"],"prerequisites":["Data consistency fundamentals"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B77-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Data Integrity: What You Read Is What You Wrote","basis":"Addresses correctness and integrity of persisted data."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Reliable data writes"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B77-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Data integrity under failure","concept":"Corruption detection versus availability","knowledge_type":"reliability_tradeoff","question":"How should a system respond when data integrity cannot be established?","answer":"The system should prefer an explicit safe state over silently serving data whose correctness cannot be established, with behavior determined by the criticality of the affected data.","explanation":"Serving corrupt data can cause durable business damage that is worse than temporary unavailability. Reliability therefore includes correctness, not only uptime.","principle":"Availability does not justify violating critical data invariants.","rule":"Define integrity checks and safe behavior for data whose validity is uncertain.","examples":[{"scenario":"A checksum or consistency validation fails for a critical record.","expected_behavior":"Quarantine or recover the record rather than silently returning potentially corrupt data."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Ignore integrity failures to preserve availability.","why_invalid":"Corrupted data may propagate incorrect state."}],"problem":"Data may be incorrect but the system remains technically available.","failure":"Corrupt information becomes authoritative.","cause":"Availability prioritized without integrity policy.","solution":"Use integrity validation, quarantine, repair, and explicit degraded behavior.","alternatives":["Replica comparison","Immutable backups","Reconstruction from authoritative sources"],"tradeoffs":["Integrity checks can increase latency and may temporarily reduce availability."],"constraints":["Criticality determines acceptable behavior.","Recovery sources must themselves be trustworthy."],"edge_cases":["Corruption in all replicas","Checksum metadata corruption","Partial record corruption"],"security":["Integrity mechanisms should be tamper-resistant."],"performance":["Frequent validation has computational cost."],"testing":["Inject corrupted records and verify containment and recovery."],"verification":["Corruption detected","Affected data isolated","Recovery source validated"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Integrity validation","Backup/recovery","Data provenance"],"prerequisites":["Data integrity concepts"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B77-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Data Integrity: What You Read Is What You Wrote","basis":"Emphasizes data correctness as a reliability concern."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Data integrity reliability"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B77-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Data integrity under failure","concept":"Backup recovery verification","knowledge_type":"recovery_verification","question":"Why is backup existence insufficient evidence of recoverability?","answer":"A backup can exist while being incomplete, corrupted, inaccessible, incompatible, or unusable by the current recovery procedure.","explanation":"Recoverability must be demonstrated through restoration and integrity validation rather than inferred from successful backup creation alone.","principle":"A backup is a recovery input, not proof of recovery capability.","rule":"Periodically restore representative backups and verify data integrity and operational usability.","examples":[{"scenario":"A database backup completes successfully every night.","expected_behavior":"Scheduled recovery tests restore samples or full environments and validate expected application behavior."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Mark recovery verified because backup jobs report success.","why_invalid":"Backup creation does not prove restoration works."}],"problem":"Recovery assumptions are based on backup status alone.","failure":"Backup cannot be restored during an outage.","cause":"No restoration testing.","solution":"Automate backup integrity checks and periodic recovery exercises.","alternatives":["Continuous replication","Point-in-time recovery tests","Reconstruction from source systems"],"tradeoffs":["Recovery tests consume storage and compute resources."],"constraints":["Testing must avoid corrupting production data.","Sensitive data requires appropriate controls."],"edge_cases":["Version mismatch","Missing dependencies","Partial backup corruption"],"security":["Restored data must maintain access controls and encryption."],"performance":["Large restore tests can be resource-intensive."],"testing":["Automated restore","Integrity validation","Application-level verification"],"verification":["Restore succeeds","Data integrity verified","Application reads expected state"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Backup","Recovery environment","Data validation"],"prerequisites":["Disaster recovery"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B77-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Backup and recovery","basis":"Explicitly recommends periodic recovery of data to verify backup integrity and processes."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Backup recoverability"},"confidence":0.99,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B77-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Data integrity under failure","concept":"Recovery consistency validation","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"How should recovered data be validated beyond structural restoration?","answer":"Validation should compare recovered state against defined correctness invariants, expected business relationships, and authoritative reference information where available.","explanation":"A database can restore without errors while containing logically inconsistent state caused by corruption, incomplete transactions, or incompatible snapshots.","principle":"Successful restoration is necessary but not sufficient for logical correctness.","rule":"Define application-level recovery invariants and validate them after restoration.","examples":[{"scenario":"An order database is restored from backup.","expected_behavior":"Validate referential relationships, transaction state, and business invariants before declaring recovery complete."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declare success because the database process starts and tables exist.","why_invalid":"Logical corruption can remain undetected."}],"problem":"Structural restore hides logical inconsistency.","failure":"Recovered system serves invalid business state.","cause":"No application-level recovery validation.","solution":"Implement post-restore consistency checks and reconciliation.","alternatives":["Checksums","Invariant queries","Source-system reconciliation"],"tradeoffs":["Deep validation increases recovery time."],"constraints":["Validation rules must be maintained with application changes."],"edge_cases":["Large datasets","Eventually consistent replicas","Historical schema versions"],"security":["Validation tools require controlled access to recovered data."],"performance":["Full reconciliation may require substantial compute."],"testing":["Restore intentionally inconsistent fixtures and verify detection."],"verification":["Business invariants pass","Known corruption detected","Recovery state approved"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Data integrity","Recovery testing","Business invariants"],"prerequisites":["Database and application correctness"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B77-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Test reliability and backup recovery","basis":"Supports validating recovery procedures rather than assuming recovery works."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Application-level recovery validation"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B77-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Data integrity under failure","concept":"Temporal consistency after recovery","knowledge_type":"data_recovery_rule","question":"Why must recovery procedures account for temporal relationships between datasets?","answer":"Restored datasets may represent different points in time, creating inconsistencies even when each dataset is individually valid.","explanation":"Independent backups, replicas, and logs can have different recovery points. Application correctness may require coordinated temporal state.","principle":"Correct data at different times is not necessarily a correct combined state.","rule":"Define recovery-point relationships for datasets that participate in the same business transaction or invariant.","examples":[{"scenario":"Orders are restored to one point while payment records are restored to an earlier point.","expected_behavior":"The recovery procedure reconciles or rejects inconsistent combinations before normal processing resumes."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Restore each dataset independently to its newest available backup.","why_invalid":"The resulting combined state may be temporally inconsistent."}],"problem":"Recovery produces cross-system temporal inconsistency.","failure":"Business records disagree after restoration.","cause":"Uncoordinated recovery points.","solution":"Use coordinated snapshots, event replay, reconciliation, or explicit recovery-point semantics.","alternatives":["Distributed snapshots","Event logs","Business reconciliation"],"tradeoffs":["Coordinated recovery can increase operational complexity and recovery time."],"constraints":["Some systems cannot provide atomic cross-system snapshots."],"edge_cases":["Clock skew","Delayed events","Partially replicated data"],"security":["Recovery reconciliation must preserve authorization and audit integrity."],"performance":["Replay and reconciliation can be expensive."],"testing":["Recover datasets at mismatched points and verify inconsistency detection."],"verification":["Temporal dependencies documented","Cross-system consistency validated"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Recovery points","Event ordering","Data reconciliation"],"prerequisites":["Distributed data consistency"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B77-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Data Processing Pipelines and Data Integrity","basis":"Addresses correctness across distributed data-processing and storage systems."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Cross-system recovery consistency"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B78-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency contract reliability","concept":"Dependency contract testing","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"How can dependency contracts reduce reliability failures?","answer":"Contract tests verify that producers and consumers agree on required behavior, data formats, error semantics, and compatibility assumptions before deployment.","explanation":"Many reliability incidents arise not from component failure but from incompatible changes between independently deployed components.","principle":"A dependency contract is a reliability boundary as well as an integration boundary.","rule":"Test critical producer-consumer contracts independently from full end-to-end tests.","examples":[{"scenario":"A provider changes an API field from optional to required.","expected_behavior":"Contract validation detects incompatibility before affected consumers deploy."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assume compatibility because both services pass their individual unit tests.","why_invalid":"Unit tests do not establish cross-component compatibility."}],"problem":"Independent component changes break consumers.","failure":"Runtime errors or incorrect interpretation of data.","cause":"Unverified dependency contract.","solution":"Define executable contracts and compatibility tests.","alternatives":["Schema compatibility checks","Consumer-driven contracts","Compatibility suites"],"tradeoffs":["Contracts require maintenance as interfaces evolve."],"constraints":["Contracts must represent behavior that matters to consumers."],"edge_cases":["Optional fields","Unknown enum values","Version negotiation"],"security":["Contract tests should include authorization and sensitive-data behavior where relevant."],"performance":["Contract suites should run quickly enough to gate changes."],"testing":["Backward and forward compatibility tests"],"verification":["Contract passes against supported versions","Breaking changes detected"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["API/schema contracts","Compatibility testing"],"prerequisites":["Interface design"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B78-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_practice","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework — Change management","basis":"Supports controlled change and validation before deployment."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Dependency compatibility"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B78-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency contract reliability","concept":"Compatibility window","knowledge_type":"versioning_strategy","question":"Why should distributed interfaces maintain an explicit compatibility window?","answer":"A compatibility window allows independently deployed components to evolve without requiring simultaneous deployment or immediate global coordination.","explanation":"During rolling deployments, rollback, delayed clients, and partial outages, multiple versions can coexist. Reliability requires predictable behavior across the supported version combinations.","principle":"Distributed deployment creates temporal version overlap.","rule":"Define which producer and consumer versions can safely interoperate and test those combinations.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service rolls out version 2 while some consumers still run version 1.","expected_behavior":"Version 2 remains compatible with the declared consumer version range."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Support only the newest producer and newest consumer combination.","why_invalid":"Rolling deployments and rollback create intermediate version combinations."}],"problem":"Mixed versions interact unpredictably.","failure":"Deployment or rollback causes runtime incompatibility.","cause":"No explicit compatibility policy.","solution":"Define compatibility windows and automate compatibility verification.","alternatives":["Versioned APIs","Feature negotiation","Expand-and-contract migrations"],"tradeoffs":["Supporting multiple versions increases code and testing complexity."],"constraints":["Compatibility windows must have an expiration strategy."],"edge_cases":["Long-lived clients","Rollback after partial migration","Schema evolution"],"security":["Old versions may contain weaker security behavior and require explicit support policy."],"performance":["Compatibility layers can add processing overhead."],"testing":["Matrix testing across supported versions"],"verification":["Supported combinations documented","Unsupported combinations rejected safely"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["API versioning","Schema evolution","Deployment strategy"],"prerequisites":["Distributed deployment"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B78-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework — Implement change","basis":"Promotes controlled and automated change management."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Version compatibility"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B78-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency contract reliability","concept":"Dependency timeout ownership","knowledge_type":"distributed_system_rule","question":"Why should timeout ownership be explicit across dependency chains?","answer":"Each layer should know whether it owns the remaining deadline and avoid allowing nested operations to continue after the caller's useful deadline has expired.","explanation":"Uncoordinated timeouts can cause wasted work, queue buildup, and responses arriving after the caller no longer needs them.","principle":"A request deadline is a resource budget that must propagate through the dependency chain.","rule":"Propagate remaining deadlines and stop downstream work when the operation can no longer meet its useful completion window.","examples":[{"scenario":"An API has 500 ms remaining and calls two downstream services.","expected_behavior":"Downstream work receives a bounded remaining deadline rather than starting an unconstrained timeout."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Give every downstream service a fresh full timeout.","why_invalid":"Nested calls can continue far beyond the original deadline."}],"problem":"Expired requests continue consuming resources.","failure":"Timeout amplification and resource exhaustion.","cause":"Independent timeout budgets.","solution":"Propagate deadlines and enforce cancellation where safe.","alternatives":["Absolute deadlines","Request cancellation","Budget-based scheduling"],"tradeoffs":["Deadline propagation requires consistent interface support."],"constraints":["Cancellation semantics must be safe.","Some operations may be non-cancellable once committed."],"edge_cases":["Clock skew","Queue delay","Partial completion"],"security":["Deadline metadata must not bypass authorization or resource controls."],"performance":["Early cancellation reduces wasted work."],"testing":["Inject slow downstream dependencies and verify deadline propagation."],"verification":["Remaining budget propagated","Expired work stopped or safely completed","No hidden full-timeout layers"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Timeouts","Request context","Cancellation"],"prerequisites":["Distributed request lifecycle"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B78-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_practice","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Distributed system interactions","basis":"Addresses timeout handling and resilient distributed interactions."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Distributed timeout management"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B78-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency contract reliability","concept":"Dependency failure contract","knowledge_type":"interface_reliability","question":"What should a dependency contract specify about failure behavior?","answer":"It should specify timeout semantics, retryability, error categories, partial-result behavior, rate limits, consistency guarantees, and expected recovery behavior where relevant.","explanation":"Consumers need to know which failures are temporary, permanent, ambiguous, or unsafe to retry. Without this information, clients invent incompatible recovery behavior.","principle":"Failure semantics are part of an interface contract.","rule":"Document machine-actionable failure categories and their safe consumer responses.","examples":[{"scenario":"A dependency can return overload, validation, and authorization failures.","expected_behavior":"The contract distinguishes them so consumers can reject, retry, or escalate appropriately."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Return one generic error code for every failure.","why_invalid":"Consumers cannot select safe recovery behavior."}],"problem":"Consumers apply incorrect recovery policies.","failure":"Retry storms or incorrect fallback behavior.","cause":"Missing failure semantics.","solution":"Define structured error contracts with retryability and state semantics.","alternatives":["Typed exceptions","Problem-details schemas","Status-code taxonomy"],"tradeoffs":["More detailed contracts require maintenance and versioning."],"constraints":["Error information must not reveal sensitive internals."],"edge_cases":["Unknown errors","Partial success","Dependency overload"],"security":["Authorization failures must remain distinguishable without leaking sensitive information."],"performance":["Structured errors add small serialization overhead."],"testing":["Contract tests for each documented failure class"],"verification":["Every failure class has defined consumer behavior","Unknown errors fail safely"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["API contracts","Failure classification","Retry policy"],"prerequisites":["Interface reliability"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B78-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar","basis":"Reliability patterns include controlled retries, timeouts, throttling, and failure management."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Dependency failure contracts"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B78-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency contract reliability","concept":"Dependency substitution contract","knowledge_type":"degradation_strategy","question":"When can a dependency be safely substituted during failure?","answer":"Substitution is safe only when the alternative provides the minimum required correctness, security, freshness, and semantic guarantees for the operation being performed.","explanation":"A fallback that merely returns data or a response can hide correctness violations if its semantics differ from the primary dependency.","principle":"Fallback compatibility is semantic, not merely syntactic.","rule":"Define explicit substitution guarantees before enabling automatic fallback.","examples":[{"scenario":"A recommendation dependency fails and a cached recommendation source is used.","expected_behavior":"The system applies an explicit freshness and authorization policy before serving cached results."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Use any available backup source because it returns the same data shape.","why_invalid":"The backup may have different freshness or correctness guarantees."}],"problem":"Fallback dependency changes operation semantics.","failure":"Users receive incorrect or unsafe results.","cause":"Fallback selected based only on interface compatibility.","solution":"Define semantic fallback contracts and validate them before activation.","alternatives":["Graceful degradation","Fail closed","Precomputed safe defaults"],"tradeoffs":["Strict fallback requirements reduce available alternatives."],"constraints":["Critical operations may have no safe substitute."],"edge_cases":["Stale data","Different authorization models","Partial source outage"],"security":["Fallback must preserve access control and data classification."],"performance":["Fallback may have different latency characteristics."],"testing":["Dependency failure with fallback validation","Stale-data tests"],"verification":["Semantic equivalence or documented degradation verified","Security controls preserved"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Dependency contracts","Graceful degradation","Data consistency"],"prerequisites":["Distributed dependency design"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B78-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"engineering_practice","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar","basis":"Supports graceful degradation and failure-aware workload architecture."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Dependency fallback"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B79-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability of automation","concept":"Automation idempotency","knowledge_type":"automation_reliability","question":"Why must reliability automation itself be idempotent where possible?","answer":"Automation may be retried after timeouts, crashes, or ambiguous execution results, so repeated execution should converge on the intended state rather than create duplicate or conflicting changes.","explanation":"The automation controller can fail independently of the target system. Durable desired-state reconciliation reduces dependence on knowing whether the previous attempt completed.","principle":"Recovery automation must survive its own failures.","rule":"Design repeatable automation around desired state, idempotent actions, and observable completion state.","examples":[{"scenario":"A deployment controller times out after creating a resource.","expected_behavior":"A repeated reconciliation discovers the existing resource and converges it rather than creating a duplicate."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assume controller timeout means the action never happened.","why_invalid":"The target may already have applied the change."}],"problem":"Automation crashes during execution.","failure":"Retries create duplicate or conflicting state.","cause":"Non-idempotent automation.","solution":"Use desired-state reconciliation and stable resource identity.","alternatives":["Transactional automation","Operation journals","Compare-and-apply"],"tradeoffs":["Desired-state systems require durable state and reconciliation logic."],"constraints":["Some external actions are inherently non-idempotent and require special handling."],"edge_cases":["Partial execution","Concurrent controllers","Lost acknowledgement"],"security":["Automation identity must be authorized for reconciliation actions."],"performance":["Frequent reconciliation consumes control-plane resources."],"testing":["Kill automation at every execution boundary and rerun reconciliation."],"verification":["Repeated reconciliation converges","No duplicate side effects","Unknown outcomes recover safely"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Desired state","Reconciliation","Stable identity"],"prerequisites":["Automation systems"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B79-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Manage change through automation","basis":"Supports controlled automated changes and reliable operational processes."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Reliability automation"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B79-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability of automation","concept":"Automation concurrency control","knowledge_type":"control_mechanism","question":"Why should recovery or deployment automation control concurrent actions?","answer":"Unbounded automation concurrency can exhaust dependencies, produce conflicting changes, and create recovery storms.","explanation":"Automation often operates faster than human responders and can multiply load across a large fleet. Concurrency limits convert this behavior into bounded work.","principle":"Automation requires the same capacity protection as production traffic.","rule":"Bound concurrent automation actions by target scope, dependency capacity, and failure-domain constraints.","examples":[{"scenario":"An automated repair process detects 10,000 failed instances.","expected_behavior":"Repairs are processed in controlled batches rather than launched simultaneously."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Start a repair task for every detected failure immediately.","why_invalid":"The repair system can overload shared dependencies."}],"problem":"Automation creates its own incident amplification.","failure":"Repair workload overwhelms the system.","cause":"Unbounded concurrency.","solution":"Use queues, concurrency limits, stages, and dependency-aware scheduling.","alternatives":["Token buckets","Worker pools","Cell-local controllers"],"tradeoffs":["Lower concurrency may increase recovery duration."],"constraints":["Limits must reflect recovery objectives and available capacity."],"edge_cases":["Large-scale correlated failure","Controller restart","Priority recovery"],"security":["Automation concurrency should not bypass authorization boundaries."],"performance":["Bounded concurrency improves resource predictability."],"testing":["Mass-failure simulation and recovery-load measurement"],"verification":["Concurrency limits enforced","Recovery load remains bounded","Controller restart safe"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Recovery orchestration","Capacity management","Admission control"],"prerequisites":["Automated recovery"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B79-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"research_and_practice","source":"Google SRE Book — Cascading Failures","basis":"Demonstrates how uncontrolled load can amplify failures and recommends bounded workload behavior."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Automation under failure"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B79-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability of automation","concept":"Automation observability independence","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why should critical recovery automation avoid depending exclusively on the system it is repairing for its own observability?","answer":"If automation relies on the failed system for all telemetry, state, or control signals, the automation may become blind exactly when it is needed most.","explanation":"A recovery mechanism must retain enough independent evidence to determine whether actions succeeded and whether it is safe to continue.","principle":"Recovery control must survive partial observability failure.","rule":"Provide independent or differently sourced signals for critical recovery decisions.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service's internal metrics fail with the service.","expected_behavior":"External health signals or infrastructure-level telemetry continue to guide recovery."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Use only the failing application's own health endpoint to decide whether to restart it.","why_invalid":"The endpoint may be unavailable or misleading during the failure."}],"problem":"Recovery automation loses its control signal.","failure":"Automation loops, stops, or takes unsafe actions.","cause":"Single-source observability dependency.","solution":"Use layered health signals and independent control-plane evidence where justified.","alternatives":["External monitors","Synthetic checks","Infrastructure telemetry"],"tradeoffs":["Independent monitoring adds cost and operational complexity."],"constraints":["Independent signals must have clearly defined semantics."],"edge_cases":["Telemetry partition","False positives","Monitoring dependency outage"],"security":["Monitoring systems require protection because they influence recovery actions."],"performance":["Synthetic monitoring consumes some resources."],"testing":["Disable primary telemetry and verify safe automation behavior."],"verification":["Independent signal available","Automation fails safe when evidence is absent"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Observability","Recovery automation","Control plane"],"prerequisites":["Monitoring architecture"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B79-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Monitor workload resources and failure management","basis":"Supports monitoring all components and automated healing."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Recovery observability"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B79-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability of automation","concept":"Fail-safe automation state","knowledge_type":"safety_pattern","question":"What should automation do when it loses reliable evidence about the target state?","answer":"It should transition to a bounded safe state, stop potentially destructive actions, and request reconciliation or human intervention rather than guessing.","explanation":"Automation without trustworthy state can perform actions based on false assumptions and amplify an outage.","principle":"Uncertainty should reduce automation authority, not increase it.","rule":"When critical state becomes unknown, stop irreversible actions until state can be reconciled.","examples":[{"scenario":"A controller loses connectivity while a destructive migration is in progress.","expected_behavior":"The controller does not blindly restart the migration; it first reconciles actual target state."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Repeat the migration because the controller cannot confirm completion.","why_invalid":"The original operation may have already committed."}],"problem":"Automation loses authoritative state.","failure":"Duplicate or destructive actions occur.","cause":"Implicit assumption that unknown equals absent.","solution":"Represent unknown state explicitly and require reconciliation before continuation.","alternatives":["Read-after-write verification","Operation journals","Manual approval"],"tradeoffs":["Safe stopping can increase recovery time."],"constraints":["Reconciliation path must remain available.","Critical actions may require stronger confirmation."],"edge_cases":["Network partition","Partial controller failure","Conflicting state sources"],"security":["Unknown state must not grant elevated authority."],"performance":["Reconciliation may add latency before continuing."],"testing":["Force state loss and verify fail-safe behavior."],"verification":["Unknown state represented","Unsafe action blocked","Recovery path available"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["State reconciliation","Automation governance","Auditability"],"prerequisites":["Distributed state management"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B79-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"reliability_engineering","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar","basis":"Supports controlled automated recovery and failure-aware operation."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Safe automation"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B79-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability of automation","concept":"Automation blast-radius limits","knowledge_type":"governance_pattern","question":"How should automated reliability actions be constrained to prevent large-scale mistakes?","answer":"Automation should operate within explicit scope, concurrency, authorization, time, and failure-domain limits, with escalation when those limits are reached.","explanation":"Automation can execute harmful actions much faster than humans. Blast-radius controls prevent a single incorrect decision from becoming a system-wide incident.","principle":"Automation authority should be proportional to validated confidence and bounded by blast-radius controls.","rule":"Every high-impact automated action requires explicit scope and maximum-impact limits.","examples":[{"scenario":"A repair controller identifies a configuration defect across many nodes.","expected_behavior":"It repairs a bounded subset, validates results, and expands only when safety conditions remain satisfied."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Apply the repair globally immediately after one successful test.","why_invalid":"One sample does not validate fleet-wide safety."}],"problem":"Incorrect automation scales an error rapidly.","failure":"Large-scale configuration or availability incident.","cause":"Unbounded automation authority.","solution":"Use canary scope, progressive expansion, authorization, and automatic halt thresholds.","alternatives":["Human approval gates","Cell-based automation","Progressive rollout"],"tradeoffs":["Progressive automation is slower than unrestricted execution."],"constraints":["Limits must be enforceable by the control system itself."],"edge_cases":["Emergency overrides","Correlated failure","Controller compromise"],"security":["Least privilege and independent authorization are critical for high-impact automation."],"performance":["Progressive execution increases total completion time."],"testing":["Simulate incorrect automation and verify maximum impact remains bounded."],"verification":["Scope limit enforced","Expansion gated","Automatic halt validated"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Automation governance","Fault isolation","Change management"],"prerequisites":["Controlled automation"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B79-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework — Implement change","basis":"Supports controlled, automated, progressive changes."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Automated change blast radius"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B80-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability assurance","concept":"Reliability argument","knowledge_type":"assurance_method","question":"What is a reliability assurance argument?","answer":"A reliability assurance argument links explicit reliability claims to assumptions, evidence, verification results, and known limitations so that confidence in system behavior can be evaluated systematically.","explanation":"A collection of tests is not automatically a reliability argument. Evidence must be connected to the specific claims being made and their operating assumptions.","principle":"Reliability confidence should be traceable from claim to evidence.","rule":"For critical reliability claims, record the claim, assumptions, evidence, verification method, residual uncertainty, and validity scope.","examples":[{"scenario":"The architecture claims that a dependency failure will not interrupt critical transactions.","expected_behavior":"Evidence includes dependency isolation design, controlled failure tests, and limits under which the claim remains valid."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"State that the system is resilient because many tests passed.","why_invalid":"The tests may not cover the actual reliability claim or its assumptions."}],"problem":"Reliability claims lack traceable evidence.","failure":"False confidence in resilience.","cause":"Evidence collected without claim-to-test mapping.","solution":"Build structured reliability assurance cases.","alternatives":["Reliability review","Safety case","Verification matrix"],"tradeoffs":["Assurance documentation adds engineering effort."],"constraints":["Claims must remain scoped to tested conditions.","Evidence quality varies."],"edge_cases":["Conflicting evidence","Changing architecture","Rare failure modes"],"security":["Critical security-reliability claims require trustworthy evidence sources."],"performance":["Assurance analysis can be expensive for complex systems."],"testing":["Map each critical claim to verification evidence."],"verification":["Claim-evidence traceability","Assumptions reviewed","Residual risks documented"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Verification","Evidence management","Risk analysis"],"prerequisites":["Reliability engineering fundamentals"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B80-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"systems_engineering_practice","source":"NIST Systems Security Engineering guidance and reliability assurance practices","basis":"Supports explicit claims, assumptions, evidence, and risk reasoning in systems engineering."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"medium_high","claim_scope":"Reliability assurance methodology"},"confidence":0.9,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B80-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability assurance","concept":"Assumption tracking","knowledge_type":"verification_principle","question":"Why must reliability assumptions be tracked explicitly?","answer":"A reliability design can remain valid only while its assumptions about capacity, dependencies, traffic, configuration, and failure independence remain valid.","explanation":"Assumptions often become obsolete as systems evolve. Untracked assumptions create hidden reliability debt and can invalidate previous verification evidence.","principle":"An untracked assumption is an unmonitored reliability dependency.","rule":"Record critical assumptions and define observable indicators or review triggers for assumptions that can change.","examples":[{"scenario":"A recovery design assumes a dependency remains available in a separate fault domain.","expected_behavior":"The dependency topology is monitored and the assumption is revisited after architectural changes."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Keep assumptions only in the original architecture document.","why_invalid":"The assumption may outlive the document's review cycle."}],"problem":"Reliability evidence becomes stale without obvious warning.","failure":"Previously valid recovery behavior no longer works.","cause":"Changed assumptions.","solution":"Maintain assumption registers linked to architecture and verification evidence.","alternatives":["Architecture decision records","Risk registers","Automated policy checks"],"tradeoffs":["Ongoing maintenance is required."],"constraints":["Only material assumptions need explicit tracking."],"edge_cases":["Third-party dependency changes","Traffic growth","Organizational changes"],"security":["Security assumptions can affect both availability and recovery."],"performance":["Assumption monitoring should avoid excessive overhead."],"testing":["Invalidate assumptions deliberately and verify detection."],"verification":["Assumptions linked to evidence","Review triggers defined","Stale assumptions detected"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Architecture records","Verification evidence","Change management"],"prerequisites":["Reliability assurance"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B80-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"systems_engineering_practice","source":"NIST Systems Security Engineering guidance","basis":"Supports explicit assumptions, constraints, and lifecycle assurance."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"medium_high","claim_scope":"Reliability assurance lifecycle"},"confidence":0.91,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B80-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability assurance","concept":"Residual reliability risk","knowledge_type":"risk_representation","question":"How should residual reliability risk be represented after mitigation?","answer":"Residual risk should identify the remaining failure condition, likelihood or uncertainty where supportable, impact, existing controls, evidence limitations, and accepted operating boundaries.","explanation":"Mitigation rarely eliminates every failure mode. Explicit residual risk prevents reliability reviews from treating mitigated risk as nonexistent.","principle":"Risk reduced is not the same as risk eliminated.","rule":"Record important residual failure modes after controls and distinguish measured evidence from assumptions.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service has redundant dependencies but still has a shared regional control plane.","expected_behavior":"The shared dependency remains documented as residual correlated-failure risk."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Mark a failure mode closed because redundancy was added.","why_invalid":"The new design may still contain correlated failure paths."}],"problem":"Risk records imply complete elimination when only mitigation occurred.","failure":"Known residual failure is ignored.","cause":"Binary risk closure model.","solution":"Use residual-risk states with evidence and operating boundaries.","alternatives":["Risk register","Reliability debt register","Assurance case"],"tradeoffs":["More explicit risk tracking increases governance effort."],"constraints":["Do not assign numerical probabilities without defensible evidence."],"edge_cases":["Unknown probability","Conflicting evidence","Changing threat or workload"],"security":["Security-induced availability risks should remain represented separately where appropriate."],"performance":["Risk analysis has little runtime performance impact but requires maintenance."],"testing":["Review residual risks after major architecture changes and incidents."],"verification":["Residual risks have owners","Evidence scope documented","Acceptance boundaries explicit"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Risk management","Verification","Failure analysis"],"prerequisites":["Reliability risk assessment"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B80-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"systems_engineering_practice","source":"NIST risk and systems engineering guidance","basis":"Supports explicit risk treatment and documentation of remaining uncertainty."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"medium_high","claim_scope":"Reliability risk management"},"confidence":0.91,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B80-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability assurance","concept":"Verification coverage versus confidence","knowledge_type":"evidence_reasoning","question":"Why does high test coverage not automatically prove high reliability?","answer":"Test coverage measures exercised code or scenarios according to a defined metric, while reliability confidence depends on whether important failure modes, interactions, assumptions, and recovery behaviors were actually validated.","explanation":"A system can have high structural coverage while leaving critical distributed failure paths untested.","principle":"Coverage is evidence about testing scope, not a universal measure of reliability.","rule":"Evaluate reliability evidence by failure-mode coverage and claim relevance, not by coverage percentage alone.","examples":[{"scenario":"Most application branches are covered but no dependency outage has been tested.","expected_behavior":"The reliability claim remains unverified for dependency-failure behavior."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declare reliability proven because code coverage exceeds a threshold.","why_invalid":"Structural coverage does not establish resilience to operational failures."}],"problem":"Testing metrics are mistaken for reliability proof.","failure":"Critical failure modes remain untested.","cause":"Evidence metric interpreted outside its scope.","solution":"Map tests to reliability claims and failure modes.","alternatives":["Fault-injection matrix","Requirements traceability","Scenario coverage"],"tradeoffs":["Failure-mode testing is more expensive than simple structural coverage."],"constraints":["Not every theoretical failure can be tested directly."],"edge_cases":["Rare failures","Combinatorial interactions","Production-only dependencies"],"security":["Security-related failure modes may require controlled test environments."],"performance":["Extensive resilience testing consumes resources."],"testing":["Claim-to-test traceability","Fault injection","Recovery drills"],"verification":["Critical failure modes covered","Unverified claims explicitly marked"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Testing","Failure modeling","Evidence management"],"prerequisites":["Software verification"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B80-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Test reliability","basis":"Separates functional, performance, resilience, chaos, game-day, and recovery testing."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Reliability verification"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B80-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability assurance","concept":"Reliability closure criteria","knowledge_type":"lifecycle_rule","question":"When can a reliability engineering phase or remediation item be considered closed?","answer":"Closure requires that the defined failure condition has an implemented control, relevant verification evidence exists, residual risk is documented, and the evidence remains valid for the current system version and operating assumptions.","explanation":"Implementation alone does not prove reliability. Closure must connect the original failure or requirement to a tested control and current evidence.","principle":"Reliability work closes on verified risk treatment, not code completion.","rule":"Do not mark a reliability item closed until implementation, verification, evidence scope, residual risk, and ownership are recorded.","examples":[{"scenario":"A cascading-failure vulnerability is mitigated with admission control.","expected_behavior":"Closure requires overload testing, observed containment, configuration verification, and documentation of remaining limits."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Close the item when the code change is merged.","why_invalid":"The reliability behavior has not yet been verified."}],"problem":"Reliability remediation becomes permanently incomplete or falsely marked done.","failure":"Known reliability gap remains hidden.","cause":"Implementation-based closure.","solution":"Use evidence-based closure criteria linked to the original failure mode.","alternatives":["Reliability acceptance gate","Operational readiness review","Risk-based closure workflow"],"tradeoffs":["Evidence-based closure takes longer but improves traceability."],"constraints":["Evidence must correspond to the deployed version.","Residual risk may require explicit acceptance."],"edge_cases":["Emergency fixes","Partial remediation","Architecture changes during remediation"],"security":["Security-sensitive reliability controls require appropriate verification and authorization."],"performance":["Verification may require production-like environments."],"testing":["Regression tests","Failure injection","Recovery validation"],"verification":["Control implemented","Failure scenario tested","Evidence current","Residual risk recorded"],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Reliability requirements","Verification","Change management","Risk management"],"prerequisites":["Reliability lifecycle"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B80-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_guidance","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Test reliability and change management","basis":"Emphasizes tested recovery, controlled change, and operational readiness."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"high","claim_scope":"Reliability lifecycle closure"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B81-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Common-cause failures","concept":"Common-cause failure independence","knowledge_type":"architectural_principle","question":"Why can nominal redundancy fail to improve reliability when redundant components share a common failure cause?","answer":"Redundancy improves reliability only when failure modes are sufficiently independent. Shared power, network paths, software dependencies, configuration, credentials, operators, or deployment mechanisms can cause nominally separate replicas to fail together.","explanation":"Two or more components may appear independent at the service topology level while remaining coupled through infrastructure or operational dependencies. A common-cause failure can therefore defeat replication and create correlated outages.","principle":"Reliability gains from redundancy depend on independence of relevant failure modes, not merely on the number of replicas.","rule":"For every critical redundancy mechanism, identify shared failure domains and common dependencies before treating replicas as independent protection.","examples":[{"scenario":"Two service replicas run in separate processes but depend on the same unavailable credential provider.","expected_behavior":"The architecture recognizes the credential provider as a shared failure dependency and defines an alternative or containment strategy."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declare a service highly redundant because it has three replicas.","why_invalid":"Replica count alone does not establish failure independence."}],"problem":"Redundant components fail simultaneously during a shared dependency outage.","failure":"Expected redundancy provides little or no protection.","cause":"Common dependency or shared failure mechanism.","solution":"Map common-cause dependencies and diversify or isolate critical shared resources where justified.","alternatives":["Accept the common dependency with explicit residual risk.","Introduce a dependency-independent degraded mode.","Use a separate recovery path."],"tradeoffs":["More independence can increase operational complexity.","Duplicating infrastructure increases cost.","Diversification may increase configuration variance."],"constraints":["Not every shared dependency can be eliminated.","Independence must be evaluated for the actual failure model."],"edge_cases":["A rare common-cause failure may dominate many independent component failures.","Operational tooling itself may be a common dependency.","Geographic separation does not guarantee software or credential independence."],"security":["Shared credentials or control-plane access can create correlated security failures.","Recovery paths must not bypass authorization controls."],"performance":["Independent infrastructure may add latency or synchronization overhead.","Diversified paths may have different performance characteristics."],"testing":["Inject failures into shared dependencies and verify whether replicas remain independently useful.","Test infrastructure and operational dependency assumptions explicitly."],"verification":["Maintain a dependency-to-failure-domain map.","Verify that claimed redundancy has at least one meaningful failure scenario in which replicas remain available independently."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["failure-domain modeling","dependency analysis"],"prerequisites":["fault isolation","redundancy concepts"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B81-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Fault Isolation","basis":"Guidance on isolating failures and limiting correlated impact."},{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE — Cascading Failures","basis":"Guidance on correlated and cascading failure behavior."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"General reliability architecture"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B81-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Common-cause failures","concept":"Failure-domain diversity","knowledge_type":"design_strategy","question":"How should a reliability design use diverse failure domains rather than simply increasing replica count?","answer":"Place critical redundant components across failure domains that do not share the dominant failure mechanisms, while preserving controlled communication and consistent recovery procedures.","explanation":"Diversity can reduce correlated failure probability when failures originate from shared infrastructure, software versions, configuration, location, or operational processes. Excessive diversity can itself increase complexity and configuration risk.","principle":"Redundancy should be diversified against the failure modes that matter most.","rule":"Choose failure-domain diversity from an explicit failure model rather than assuming that physical separation automatically creates independence.","examples":[{"scenario":"A critical service has replicas separated by independent compute and network domains but uses the same deployment artifact.","expected_behavior":"The architecture treats artifact defects as a remaining common-cause risk."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Use different hosts and conclude all common-cause failures are eliminated.","why_invalid":"Software, configuration, control-plane, and operational dependencies may remain shared."}],"problem":"Replicas fail together despite physical separation.","failure":"Availability objective is violated during a correlated fault.","cause":"Insufficient diversity against the actual failure mechanism.","solution":"Identify dominant common causes and introduce targeted diversity or an independent recovery path.","alternatives":["Use a smaller but genuinely independent recovery environment.","Reduce shared dependencies rather than duplicating all infrastructure."],"tradeoffs":["Diversity increases testing and operational burden.","Different implementations can introduce interoperability risk.","Independent environments can increase cost."],"constraints":["Diversity must remain supportable and observable.","Critical interfaces still require compatibility."],"edge_cases":["Different software versions can reduce correlated defects but introduce version-skew failures.","Independent regions may still share a provider control plane."],"security":["Diverse recovery paths must maintain equivalent authorization and audit requirements.","Avoid weakening security controls merely to create independence."],"performance":["Cross-domain traffic can add latency.","Independent environments may have different capacity characteristics."],"testing":["Perform failure-domain-specific fault injection.","Test both isolated and simultaneous domain failures."],"verification":["Document which failure mechanisms each diversity choice is intended to mitigate.","Verify that the intended protection exists in deployment topology."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["failure-domain analysis","dependency mapping"],"prerequisites":["common-cause failure analysis"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B81-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Fault Isolation","basis":"Failure isolation and limiting correlated failure impact."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Distributed and service architectures"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B81-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Common-cause failures","concept":"Configuration-induced correlated failure","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"How can configuration changes create simultaneous failures across otherwise independent instances?","answer":"A shared configuration source, synchronized rollout, malformed configuration, or incorrect policy can cause multiple instances to enter the same invalid state simultaneously.","explanation":"Configuration is executable operational state. Replication does not protect against a configuration defect when the same configuration is distributed to all replicas.","principle":"Configuration distribution is part of the reliability failure surface.","rule":"Treat configuration changes as potentially common-cause events and validate, stage, observe, and roll back them accordingly.","examples":[{"scenario":"A routing policy update is propagated to all service instances.","expected_behavior":"The rollout is staged or otherwise protected so an invalid policy cannot simultaneously disable the entire service."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Apply an unvalidated configuration globally because the service has multiple replicas.","why_invalid":"All replicas can receive the same defective state."}],"problem":"A configuration change causes simultaneous component failures.","failure":"Replica redundancy is ineffective.","cause":"Shared configuration defect or synchronized propagation.","solution":"Validate configuration, stage distribution, observe impact, and retain rollback capability.","alternatives":["Canary configuration.","Versioned configuration with atomic rollback.","Independent configuration validation service."],"tradeoffs":["Staging slows propagation.","Maintaining multiple configuration versions increases complexity."],"constraints":["Configuration must remain compatible with deployed software.","Rollback must account for state already produced under the bad configuration."],"edge_cases":["A rollback can fail if the configuration system itself is degraded.","Partial propagation can create version skew."],"security":["Configuration validation must prevent unauthorized policy changes.","Secrets must not be exposed through configuration diagnostics."],"performance":["Frequent validation can increase control-plane load.","Configuration convergence may take longer with staged rollout."],"testing":["Test malformed, incompatible, partially propagated, and rollback configurations.","Verify no invalid configuration can bypass validation."],"verification":["Record configuration version and propagation state for each instance.","Verify recovery returns instances to a known valid configuration."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["configuration management","change management"],"prerequisites":["common-cause failure analysis"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B81-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Change Management","basis":"Controlled changes, validation, and rollback reduce reliability risk."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Configuration and operational changes"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B81-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Common-cause failures","concept":"Operator-induced correlated failure","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why can human operational actions become a common-cause reliability risk?","answer":"A single operator action can affect many components when the action has broad scope, weak safeguards, or insufficient preview and validation.","explanation":"Automation and administrative interfaces can amplify a single mistake across an entire fleet. Reliability controls therefore need bounded scope, authorization, validation, and reversibility for high-impact operations.","principle":"Operational authority should be constrained according to potential blast radius.","rule":"High-blast-radius operations require explicit scope, validation, authorization, and recoverability controls.","examples":[{"scenario":"An administrator issues a fleet-wide configuration command.","expected_behavior":"The system presents the scope, validates the operation, requires appropriate authorization, and preserves rollback evidence."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Allow unrestricted fleet-wide destructive operations because the administrator is trusted.","why_invalid":"Trust does not eliminate human error or operational misinterpretation."}],"problem":"A single operational action creates widespread service failure.","failure":"Large-scale correlated outage.","cause":"Unbounded operational blast radius.","solution":"Introduce scoped permissions, previews, staged execution, approval controls, and rollback mechanisms.","alternatives":["Automatic canary execution.","Two-person authorization for critical operations.","Time-limited administrative privileges."],"tradeoffs":["Additional controls can slow urgent remediation.","Fine-grained authorization increases policy complexity."],"constraints":["Emergency procedures still require auditable safeguards.","Controls must not prevent legitimate recovery."],"edge_cases":["A global rollback may itself be required during a severe incident.","Automation can amplify both correct and incorrect actions."],"security":["Use least privilege and auditable authorization.","Separate emergency access from routine administrative access."],"performance":["Validation and staged execution may delay fleet-wide convergence."],"testing":["Exercise high-impact operational controls in a safe environment.","Verify scope restrictions and rollback behavior."],"verification":["Capture intended scope and actual affected scope.","Require evidence that the operation completed within authorized boundaries."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["authorization","change management","audit logging"],"prerequisites":["blast-radius analysis"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B81-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Change Management","basis":"Controlled changes and safe implementation practices."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Operational reliability and change control"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B81-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Common-cause failures","concept":"Common-cause failure review","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"What should a common-cause failure review verify before accepting a redundancy claim?","answer":"It should identify shared infrastructure, software, configuration, control-plane, data, credential, deployment, and operator dependencies and determine whether each can defeat the intended redundancy.","explanation":"A redundancy claim is meaningful only relative to explicit failure assumptions. Reviewing common dependencies exposes hidden coupling that topology diagrams can miss.","principle":"Reliability claims must state their independence assumptions.","rule":"Do not mark a redundancy control verified until shared failure mechanisms have been analyzed against the target failure model.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service claims protection through three replicas.","expected_behavior":"The review identifies which failures one replica can survive and which common failures can still affect all three."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Mark redundancy verified from replica count alone.","why_invalid":"Replica count does not prove independence."}],"problem":"Reliability architecture overstates the protection provided by redundancy.","failure":"Unexpected correlated outage.","cause":"Unexamined shared failure mechanism.","solution":"Perform structured common-cause analysis and document residual dependencies.","alternatives":["Failure-mode workshop.","Dependency graph analysis.","Controlled correlated-failure testing."],"tradeoffs":["Analysis requires engineering effort.","Some common dependencies may remain by design."],"constraints":["The analysis must match actual production topology.","Unknown dependencies should remain explicit uncertainty rather than being assumed absent."],"edge_cases":["Hidden dependencies may only appear during failover.","Third-party services may have undocumented shared infrastructure."],"security":["Include identity and security control dependencies in the common-cause review."],"performance":["Independence improvements may change latency and capacity."],"testing":["Test representative common-cause failures where safe.","Use fault injection to validate critical assumptions."],"verification":["Maintain an auditable list of assumptions, evidence, and residual risks."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["failure modeling","dependency mapping","fault injection"],"prerequisites":["redundancy analysis"],"relationships":[{"relation":"verifies","record_id":"D22-B81-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Fault Isolation","basis":"Fault isolation and dependency analysis guidance."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability architecture review"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B82-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability growth","concept":"Reliability growth through failure learning","knowledge_type":"engineering_process","question":"How should repeated production failures improve reliability rather than merely trigger isolated fixes?","answer":"Each significant failure should update the failure model, corrective controls, tests, monitoring, operational procedures, and residual-risk assessment when evidence shows that existing assumptions were incomplete.","explanation":"A local fix can remove one symptom without reducing the broader failure class. Reliability growth requires converting incident evidence into reusable engineering controls and verification.","principle":"Reliability improves when failure evidence changes the system or its validated operating assumptions.","rule":"For recurring or high-impact failures, identify the underlying failure class and add preventive or detective controls plus regression evidence.","examples":[{"scenario":"A timeout failure repeatedly appears in different services.","expected_behavior":"The organization identifies the shared failure mechanism and adds a reusable control or verification rule instead of patching each occurrence independently."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Close each incident after restoring service without updating tests or failure models.","why_invalid":"The same failure class remains capable of recurring."}],"problem":"Failures recur despite previous incident fixes.","failure":"Reliability improvement stagnates.","cause":"Lessons remain local and are not converted into durable controls.","solution":"Classify failure mechanisms, update engineering controls, and add regression verification.","alternatives":["Reliability review board.","Failure-pattern knowledge base.","Automated regression generation from incidents."],"tradeoffs":["Systematic remediation requires more analysis time.","Generalizing a failure incorrectly can create unnecessary controls."],"constraints":["Only evidence-supported generalizations should become mandatory rules.","Incident data may be incomplete."],"edge_cases":["A recurrence may have a different root cause despite similar symptoms.","A control can become obsolete after architecture changes."],"security":["Do not expose sensitive incident details in generalized knowledge artifacts.","Security incidents should preserve appropriate access controls."],"performance":["Additional controls can introduce runtime overhead.","Regression tests can increase CI duration."],"testing":["Create regression tests for confirmed failure mechanisms.","Test whether the proposed control blocks or detects the failure."],"verification":["Link remediation to the observed failure and record verification evidence.","Reassess the control after relevant architecture changes."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["incident analysis","testing","change management"],"prerequisites":["failure classification"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B82-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Postmortem Culture","basis":"Learning from failures and using postmortems to improve reliability."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Operational reliability improvement"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B82-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability growth","concept":"Failure-class regression prevention","knowledge_type":"testing_strategy","question":"How can a reliability team prevent a known failure class from silently returning after later changes?","answer":"Represent the failure mechanism as a durable regression invariant, test scenario, monitoring condition, or architectural rule that remains applicable after implementation changes.","explanation":"A one-time test may disappear or become irrelevant as the system evolves. Failure-class regression prevention preserves the underlying reliability property rather than only the original implementation detail.","principle":"Regression controls should preserve failure-prevention intent, not merely reproduce historical implementation details.","rule":"For important failure classes, encode the expected invariant in a durable verification mechanism.","examples":[{"scenario":"A previous release caused work duplication after retry.","expected_behavior":"A regression test verifies the idempotency invariant across future retry-related changes."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Keep only the original bug test without checking whether the underlying invariant still matters.","why_invalid":"The implementation can change while the failure mechanism remains possible in a different form."}],"problem":"A previously fixed failure returns through a different code path.","failure":"Regression escapes existing tests.","cause":"Tests were tied to implementation rather than failure invariant.","solution":"Convert the historical failure into an invariant-based regression control.","alternatives":["Property-based testing.","Model-based testing.","Production invariant monitoring."],"tradeoffs":["Invariant tests may require more abstraction.","Broad properties can be difficult to define precisely."],"constraints":["The invariant must be observable or testable.","False positives must remain manageable."],"edge_cases":["Some failures depend on timing or external systems.","A historical symptom may no longer represent the same failure class."],"security":["Security-sensitive failure invariants must avoid exposing protected data."],"performance":["Continuous invariant monitoring may add overhead."],"testing":["Run the invariant against normal, degraded, and recovery scenarios."],"verification":["Trace the regression control to the historical failure and current architecture."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["failure taxonomy","automated testing"],"prerequisites":["reliability growth"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B82-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Testing for Reliability","basis":"Reliability testing and preventing recurrence of failure modes."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability regression testing"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B82-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability growth","concept":"Reliability trend interpretation","knowledge_type":"analytical_method","question":"Why should reliability trends be interpreted using failure exposure and system changes rather than raw incident counts alone?","answer":"Incident counts do not account for workload, system scale, dependency changes, detection changes, or exposure time. A meaningful trend relates failures to an appropriate exposure measure and changes in the operating environment.","explanation":"A growing system may experience more absolute failures while becoming more reliable per unit of exposure, while improved detection can increase observed incidents without increasing actual failure frequency.","principle":"Reliability trends require normalized evidence and contextual interpretation.","rule":"Do not infer reliability improvement or degradation from raw failure counts without examining exposure and measurement changes.","examples":[{"scenario":"Traffic doubles and incident count rises modestly.","expected_behavior":"Reliability analysis considers failure rate per relevant exposure unit and whether detection coverage changed."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Conclude reliability degraded because incident count increased after traffic doubled.","why_invalid":"The raw count does not establish the failure rate."}],"problem":"Reliability reporting gives misleading trends.","failure":"Engineering decisions are based on incorrect conclusions.","cause":"Unnormalized or changed measurement conditions.","solution":"Track appropriate exposure metrics and annotate architecture, workload, and detection changes.","alternatives":["Failure rate per request.","Failure rate per transaction or operating hour.","SLO-based measurement."],"tradeoffs":["Choosing an exposure denominator can be difficult.","Normalized metrics may hide rare high-impact failures."],"constraints":["The denominator must correspond to the failure mechanism.","Rare catastrophic failures require separate treatment."],"edge_cases":["A metric can improve while business impact worsens.","Detection changes can alter apparent failure frequency."],"security":["Security incidents may require separate severity-based treatment rather than normalization alone."],"performance":["High-cardinality metrics can increase telemetry overhead."],"testing":["Validate metric calculations using known synthetic scenarios.","Check that denominator changes are versioned."],"verification":["Document metric definitions, units, and population.","Audit historical trend breaks after instrumentation changes."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["observability","SLO measurement"],"prerequisites":["failure measurement"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B82-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Service Level Objectives","basis":"Reliability measurement should be tied to meaningful service objectives."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability measurement"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B82-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability growth","concept":"Reliability improvement evidence chain","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"What evidence chain is needed to demonstrate that a reliability improvement actually addresses a failure?","answer":"The chain should connect the observed failure to a failure mechanism, corrective change, expected invariant, verification method, observed verification result, and residual limitations.","explanation":"A deployment alone does not prove reliability improvement. Traceability makes it possible to distinguish implemented changes from verified risk reduction.","principle":"Reliability claims should be traceable from evidence to control to verification.","rule":"Do not mark a reliability remediation complete solely because the code or configuration change was deployed.","examples":[{"scenario":"A recovery defect is corrected.","expected_behavior":"The record links the defect, corrective change, recovery invariant, test evidence, and remaining assumptions."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Mark the remediation verified because production has not reported another failure yet.","why_invalid":"Absence of a subsequent observation is not sufficient verification."}],"problem":"Reliability remediation status overstates evidence.","failure":"Unverified controls are treated as proven.","cause":"Implementation evidence is confused with effectiveness evidence.","solution":"Separate implementation, test, production observation, and residual-risk evidence.","alternatives":["Evidence matrix.","Assurance case.","Change-to-test traceability."],"tradeoffs":["Traceability requires metadata and process discipline.","Some evidence can only establish limited confidence."],"constraints":["Evidence must match the claim scope.","No execution result should be asserted without actual execution."],"edge_cases":["Rare failures may require controlled fault injection.","A test can prove a local property but not system-wide reliability."],"security":["Evidence artifacts should follow access-control requirements."],"performance":["Extensive verification can increase delivery time."],"testing":["Use targeted tests tied to the failure mechanism.","Include negative and recovery cases."],"verification":["Require explicit status for implementation and verification evidence."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["change management","testing","incident analysis"],"prerequisites":["reliability growth"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B82-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Failure Management","basis":"Failure management and recovery verification practices."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability remediation assurance"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B82-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability growth","concept":"Reliability debt retirement","knowledge_type":"governance_principle","question":"How should accumulated reliability debt be retired without creating new reliability risk?","answer":"Reliability debt should be prioritized by failure impact and exposure, converted into bounded engineering work, verified with explicit evidence, and tracked until the underlying risk is reduced or consciously accepted.","explanation":"Unbounded reliability debt can become a hidden source of outages. Treating it as a measurable engineering backlog makes risk reduction visible and reviewable.","principle":"Reliability debt is an explicit risk inventory, not merely unfinished engineering work.","rule":"Every material reliability debt item should have an owner, failure mechanism, risk rationale, remediation strategy, and verification criterion.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service lacks an independent recovery path.","expected_behavior":"The gap is recorded as a reliability risk with explicit remediation and verification criteria."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Track only technical tasks without recording the reliability risk they reduce.","why_invalid":"Completion cannot demonstrate that the intended risk was reduced."}],"problem":"Reliability gaps remain invisible after normal feature work resumes.","failure":"Known risk persists without governance visibility.","cause":"Reliability debt is not tied to explicit risk and verification.","solution":"Maintain a reliability debt register linked to failure modes and evidence.","alternatives":["Risk-based engineering backlog.","Reliability review gates.","Error-budget-driven prioritization."],"tradeoffs":["Reliability work competes with feature delivery.","Risk prioritization can be uncertain when evidence is incomplete."],"constraints":["Debt should not be treated as a license to ignore urgent safety or security issues.","Acceptance must be explicit."],"edge_cases":["Some debt becomes irrelevant after architecture replacement.","A partial remediation may change rather than eliminate the risk."],"security":["Security-related reliability debt may require separate governance requirements."],"performance":["Reliability improvements can consume capacity needed for optimization work."],"testing":["Each remediation needs a corresponding verification strategy."],"verification":["Close debt only after evidence satisfies its predefined acceptance criteria."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["risk management","verification","change management"],"prerequisites":["failure classification"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B82-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Managing Risk and Error Budgets","basis":"Balancing reliability risk with engineering change and delivery."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability governance"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B83-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Hidden dependencies","concept":"Implicit dependency discovery","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"Why are implicit dependencies particularly dangerous to reliability?","answer":"An implicit dependency is required for correct operation but is not represented clearly in architecture, ownership, configuration, or monitoring. Such dependencies can fail without triggering the expected isolation or recovery mechanisms.","explanation":"Reliability analysis depends on knowing what a component actually requires. Hidden dependencies often become visible only during degraded operation or failover.","principle":"Unknown dependencies are reliability risks because failure behavior cannot be planned or verified reliably.","rule":"Critical dependencies should be explicitly identified, owned, monitored, and included in failure analysis.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service requires a metadata endpoint during startup but the dependency is absent from the service contract.","expected_behavior":"The dependency is documented and startup/recovery behavior is defined for its failure."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assume a dependency is non-critical because normal request processing rarely accesses it.","why_invalid":"It may still be critical during startup, recovery, or exceptional paths."}],"problem":"Recovery fails because an undocumented dependency is unavailable.","failure":"Recovery path stalls despite primary service health.","cause":"Implicit dependency not included in recovery design.","solution":"Discover and document runtime, startup, recovery, security, and operational dependencies.","alternatives":["Dynamic dependency tracing.","Architecture-as-code dependency registry.","Failure-path dependency reviews."],"tradeoffs":["Complete dependency mapping can be difficult in dynamic systems.","Tracing introduces instrumentation overhead."],"constraints":["Dependency inventories can become stale.","Third-party dependencies may be partially opaque."],"edge_cases":["Lazy-loaded dependencies.","Dependencies used only during failover or maintenance."],"security":["Authentication and authorization providers are dependencies and must be included.","Secrets management dependencies can become recovery blockers."],"performance":["Dependency tracing can add telemetry overhead."],"testing":["Exercise startup, degraded, and recovery paths with dependencies unavailable.","Compare observed dependency graph with declared graph."],"verification":["Require declared-versus-observed dependency reconciliation for critical services."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["dependency mapping","observability"],"prerequisites":["failure-path analysis"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B83-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Fault Isolation","basis":"Understanding dependencies and isolating failure impact."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Dependency reliability"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B83-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Hidden dependencies","concept":"Recovery-only dependency","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"What is a recovery-only dependency and why must it be analyzed separately from normal runtime dependencies?","answer":"A recovery-only dependency is needed primarily when restoring or reconfiguring a system. It can become a single point of recovery failure even if it is irrelevant to normal operation.","explanation":"Recovery paths often use administrative APIs, metadata services, backup systems, identity providers, or orchestration components that are not part of the normal request path.","principle":"Recovery architecture must be analyzed as its own dependency graph.","rule":"For each critical recovery procedure, enumerate dependencies required specifically by the recovery process and test their failure behavior.","examples":[{"scenario":"A database restore requires an external identity service that may be unavailable during the incident.","expected_behavior":"The recovery plan identifies this dependency and provides a validated alternative or prerequisite."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declare disaster recovery validated because normal application traffic does not use the recovery dependency.","why_invalid":"The dependency can still block restoration."}],"problem":"Recovery procedure cannot execute during the failure it was designed to address.","failure":"Recovery objective is missed.","cause":"Recovery-only dependency was not included in the resilience model.","solution":"Create and validate a recovery dependency graph.","alternatives":["Independent recovery credentials.","Offline recovery tooling.","Separate recovery control plane."],"tradeoffs":["Independent recovery paths increase maintenance burden.","Offline tooling can become stale."],"constraints":["Emergency recovery mechanisms must remain secured.","Recovery alternatives must be tested periodically."],"edge_cases":["The recovery dependency may be partially available.","Recovery may require dependencies that are themselves being restored."],"security":["Emergency recovery access requires strong authorization and auditability.","Offline recovery artifacts require protection against tampering."],"performance":["Independent recovery tooling may have lower capacity."],"testing":["Run recovery drills with selected dependencies intentionally unavailable."],"verification":["Record which recovery dependencies were available during each drill and which assumptions remain untested."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["disaster recovery","dependency mapping"],"prerequisites":["recovery orchestration"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B83-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Disaster Recovery","basis":"Recovery planning and dependency-aware restoration."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Recovery reliability"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B83-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Hidden dependencies","concept":"Dependency observability gap","knowledge_type":"observability_principle","question":"How can observability reveal dependencies that architecture documentation misses?","answer":"Distributed traces, dependency metrics, error correlations, runtime configuration, and service-to-service telemetry can reveal actual communication and resource dependencies that are absent from static documentation.","explanation":"Declared architecture describes intended relationships while runtime telemetry can expose actual relationships. Comparing both can identify undocumented dependencies and architectural drift.","principle":"Reliability engineering benefits from reconciling declared topology with observed behavior.","rule":"Use runtime evidence to detect important dependency relationships that are missing or inconsistent with declared architecture.","examples":[{"scenario":"Telemetry shows a service calling an undocumented metadata API during errors.","expected_behavior":"The dependency is investigated and either documented or removed."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treat runtime traces as proof that every dependency is reliable.","why_invalid":"Observing a dependency does not establish its resilience."}],"problem":"Architecture documentation diverges from actual runtime behavior.","failure":"Reliability analysis omits important dependency paths.","cause":"Static documentation was not reconciled with runtime evidence.","solution":"Compare declared and observed dependency graphs and investigate material differences.","alternatives":["Automated topology discovery.","Periodic architecture reconciliation.","Service mesh telemetry."],"tradeoffs":["Dynamic discovery can produce noisy or transient edges.","Telemetry storage can be expensive."],"constraints":["Observed communication may include diagnostic or incidental traffic.","Sensitive metadata must be protected."],"edge_cases":["Dependencies may be hidden behind proxies or gateways.","Encrypted traffic can limit observability."],"security":["Dependency telemetry must avoid leaking credentials or sensitive payloads.","Access to topology data should be controlled."],"performance":["Tracing introduces some runtime overhead."],"testing":["Verify that test scenarios expose expected dependency edges.","Test missing telemetry behavior so absence of evidence is not interpreted as absence of dependency."],"verification":["Maintain confidence and timestamp for discovered dependency relationships."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["distributed tracing","dependency inventory"],"prerequisites":["observability fundamentals"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B83-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Fault Isolation","basis":"Dependency identification and failure isolation."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Runtime dependency analysis"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B83-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Hidden dependencies","concept":"Dependency criticality classification","knowledge_type":"analytical_method","question":"How should dependencies be classified for reliability planning?","answer":"Classify dependencies according to whether they are required for normal operation, degraded operation, startup, recovery, administration, or security-critical functions, and assess the consequences of their unavailability.","explanation":"Binary critical/non-critical labels are often insufficient because the same dependency can be irrelevant to normal traffic but essential during recovery.","principle":"Dependency criticality is path- and state-dependent.","rule":"Classify dependencies against the system states and workflows in which their availability is required.","examples":[{"scenario":"A telemetry backend is not required for request processing but is required for automated remediation.","expected_behavior":"It is classified as remediation-critical even if it is not request-path-critical."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Mark telemetry as non-critical solely because users can still make requests when telemetry is unavailable.","why_invalid":"Automation and diagnosis may depend on it."}],"problem":"Dependency impact is underestimated because classification uses only the normal request path.","failure":"Recovery or operations fail unexpectedly.","cause":"State-specific dependency criticality was not modeled.","solution":"Classify dependencies across lifecycle states and critical workflows.","alternatives":["Dependency criticality matrix.","State-machine dependency analysis.","Business-impact mapping."],"tradeoffs":["More detailed classification requires maintenance.","Some dependencies change criticality dynamically."],"constraints":["Criticality must reflect actual system behavior.","Business impact should be included for important workflows."],"edge_cases":["A dependency can become critical only during a specific incident.","An optional dependency may become mandatory due to fallback exhaustion."],"security":["Identity, authorization, key-management, and audit dependencies need explicit classification."],"performance":["Additional classification metadata has minimal runtime cost but operational overhead."],"testing":["Test each declared critical dependency in the states where it is required."],"verification":["Review criticality classifications after architecture or workflow changes."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["dependency mapping","state modeling"],"prerequisites":["failure-path analysis"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B83-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Failure Management","basis":"Failure analysis across system operation and recovery."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Dependency criticality"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B83-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Hidden dependencies","concept":"Dependency inventory freshness","knowledge_type":"governance_principle","question":"Why must reliability-critical dependency inventories be kept current?","answer":"A stale dependency inventory can cause failure analysis, recovery plans, and redundancy claims to rely on relationships that no longer match the deployed system.","explanation":"Architectures evolve continuously. New services, providers, APIs, identity mechanisms, and operational tools can create new failure paths.","principle":"Dependency knowledge has a validity period and must evolve with the architecture.","rule":"Material dependency changes should trigger dependency inventory updates and appropriate reliability review.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service adopts a new external API for recovery operations.","expected_behavior":"The dependency is added to the recovery dependency model before the change is considered fully reliable."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Review the dependency graph only during initial architecture design.","why_invalid":"Runtime architecture changes after initial design."}],"problem":"Reliability analysis uses obsolete dependency information.","failure":"New failure paths remain untested or unmanaged.","cause":"Dependency inventory drift.","solution":"Integrate dependency updates into architecture and change-management workflows.","alternatives":["Automated topology discovery.","Configuration-derived dependency inventory.","Periodic dependency audits."],"tradeoffs":["Automated discovery can contain false positives.","Manual review increases process cost."],"constraints":["The authoritative dependency model should have clear ownership.","Transient dependencies should not automatically become permanent architecture facts."],"edge_cases":["Temporary migration dependencies.","Dependencies introduced only during feature flags."],"security":["Dependency inventories can reveal sensitive infrastructure relationships and require access control."],"performance":["Continuous discovery may increase telemetry or control-plane load."],"testing":["Detect mismatches between declared and observed dependency graphs."],"verification":["Record inventory version and last validation evidence."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["dependency discovery","change management"],"prerequisites":["dependency criticality"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B83-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Operational Excellence and Reliability Practices","basis":"Keeping operational architecture and reliability practices aligned with system changes."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Dependency governance"},"confidence":0.91,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B84-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability and security interaction","concept":"Security control failure as reliability event","knowledge_type":"cross_domain_principle","question":"When should failure of a security control be treated as a reliability concern?","answer":"When loss or degradation of the control can prevent required business operation, create unsafe fallback behavior, or cause widespread service unavailability, it should be modeled as part of the reliability failure surface.","explanation":"Security and availability can interact through identity, authorization, key management, certificate validation, policy engines, and protective controls. Reliability does not justify disabling security, but the system should define safe behavior when security dependencies fail.","principle":"Security dependencies belong in reliability analysis when their failure affects service availability or safe operation.","rule":"Define fail-safe or fail-closed behavior explicitly for security-critical dependencies rather than relying on accidental fallback behavior.","examples":[{"scenario":"An authorization service becomes unavailable.","expected_behavior":"The application follows a predefined access policy rather than silently granting unrestricted access."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Disable authorization checks whenever the authorization provider is unavailable.","why_invalid":"This converts an availability problem into an uncontrolled security exposure."}],"problem":"Security dependency outage produces undefined or unsafe behavior.","failure":"Service becomes unavailable or security boundaries are bypassed.","cause":"No explicit failure policy for the security dependency.","solution":"Define and verify failure behavior that preserves required security properties while maintaining justified degraded functionality.","alternatives":["Short-lived cached authorization decisions.","Local policy evaluation.","Restricted emergency mode."],"tradeoffs":["Caching can create authorization staleness.","Local fallback increases policy-management complexity."],"constraints":["Security requirements must remain authoritative.","Emergency modes need explicit expiration and auditability."],"edge_cases":["Revocation may occur while cached authorization remains valid.","Key rotation can coincide with identity-provider failure."],"security":["Never treat reliability fallback as authorization bypass.","Protect cached credentials and policy data."],"performance":["Local validation may reduce dependency latency.","Cryptographic fallback operations can consume additional CPU."],"testing":["Test security dependency failure, stale authorization, key rotation, and recovery.","Verify no fail-open behavior occurs unintentionally."],"verification":["Record the security invariant that must remain true during degraded operation."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["identity","authorization","key management"],"prerequisites":["failure-mode analysis","security architecture"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B84-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Volume 1 — Systems Security Engineering","basis":"Security properties and system engineering considerations across the lifecycle."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"standards_guidance","claim_scope":"Security-reliability interaction"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B84-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability and security interaction","concept":"Fail-open versus fail-closed selection","knowledge_type":"decision_rule","question":"How should an architecture choose between fail-open and fail-closed behavior during dependency failure?","answer":"The behavior should be selected from the protected asset, failure impact, security requirements, and business continuity requirements. There is no universal choice independent of context.","explanation":"Fail-closed behavior can preserve protection but reduce availability. Fail-open behavior can preserve availability but may violate security or safety requirements. The decision must be explicit and bounded.","principle":"Fallback behavior is a risk decision and must be specified rather than accidental.","rule":"Define the invariant that must survive dependency failure and select the fallback behavior that preserves that invariant.","examples":[{"scenario":"A policy engine protects highly sensitive operations.","expected_behavior":"Unavailable policy evaluation prevents unauthorized high-impact actions while allowing explicitly safe low-risk functions where appropriate."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Always fail open to maximize availability.","why_invalid":"Availability can be achieved by violating security invariants."}],"problem":"Failure behavior conflicts with security or business requirements.","failure":"Either service availability or protection is unintentionally sacrificed.","cause":"Fallback semantics were not explicitly designed.","solution":"Define state-specific fallback policies and verify them under dependency failure.","alternatives":["Risk-tiered fallback.","Cached bounded authorization.","Read-only degraded mode."],"tradeoffs":["Strict failure closure can reduce availability.","Permissive fallback increases exposure."],"constraints":["High-impact actions may require stronger protection.","Fallback state must be observable and auditable."],"edge_cases":["Different operations may require different fallback policies.","Cached permissions can become stale."],"security":["Fail-open must never be used without explicit authorization and bounded scope.","Audit fallback activation."],"performance":["Fallback policy evaluation can add local computation."],"testing":["Test each fallback branch under dependency loss and recovery.","Verify transitions back to normal enforcement."],"verification":["Review fallback policy against explicit security and availability requirements."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["security policy","failure handling"],"prerequisites":["security invariants"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B84-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standards_guidance","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Volume 1 — Systems Security Engineering","basis":"Engineering security requirements and system behavior."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"standards_guidance","claim_scope":"Fallback decision design"},"confidence":0.93,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B84-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability and security interaction","concept":"Certificate and key dependency resilience","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why can certificate or key-management failures become service reliability failures?","answer":"Services that require cryptographic validation or key retrieval may reject legitimate traffic, fail startup, or become unable to communicate when certificates expire or key services are unavailable.","explanation":"Cryptographic infrastructure is often a hard dependency. Reliability design must account for lifecycle, renewal, clock behavior, revocation, key availability, and recovery without weakening security.","principle":"Cryptographic lifecycle dependencies require explicit reliability design.","rule":"Model certificate, key, trust-store, and cryptographic service dependencies in startup, runtime, and recovery paths.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service certificate expires while its renewal provider is unavailable.","expected_behavior":"The architecture detects impending expiration and has a validated, secure renewal or recovery process."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Ignore certificate expiration because the service has multiple replicas.","why_invalid":"All replicas may share the same certificate or trust dependency."}],"problem":"Cryptographic dependency failure causes widespread communication or startup failure.","failure":"Secure communication becomes unavailable.","cause":"Unmanaged key or certificate lifecycle dependency.","solution":"Monitor lifecycle state, provide secure renewal paths, and test recovery from key-management failures.","alternatives":["Pre-provisioned bounded certificate overlap.","Independent trust infrastructure.","Automated renewal with validation."],"tradeoffs":["Longer certificate validity can increase exposure.","Independent key infrastructure increases operational complexity."],"constraints":["Security policy may limit fallback options.","Private keys require strict protection."],"edge_cases":["Clock skew can make valid certificates appear invalid.","Revocation infrastructure may fail while local trust remains valid."],"security":["Never bypass certificate validation solely to restore availability.","Protect private keys during recovery."],"performance":["Cryptographic validation and rotation can consume CPU during mass recovery."],"testing":["Test expiration, renewal failure, clock skew, key-service outage, and recovery."],"verification":["Verify certificate and key lifecycle assumptions with time-based tests."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["PKI","key management","time synchronization"],"prerequisites":["security-reliability interaction"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B84-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standards_guidance","source":"NIST SP 800-57 — Key Management","basis":"Key-management lifecycle and protection considerations."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"standards_guidance","claim_scope":"Cryptographic dependency reliability"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B84-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability and security interaction","concept":"Security recovery path equivalence","knowledge_type":"verification_principle","question":"Why should emergency recovery paths be evaluated for equivalent security properties rather than treated as exceptions?","answer":"A recovery path can become the highest-risk path because it operates during degraded conditions and often has elevated privileges. It must preserve required security controls while enabling recovery.","explanation":"Operational pressure can encourage bypasses that create persistent vulnerabilities. Recovery mechanisms should therefore have explicit authorization, audit, scope, and expiration controls.","principle":"Recovery availability does not justify unbounded security privilege.","rule":"Emergency recovery mechanisms must be separately modeled, authorized, tested, audited, and bounded.","examples":[{"scenario":"An emergency administrator account restores a critical service.","expected_behavior":"The account has restricted scope, explicit authorization, audit logging, and controlled expiration."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Create an unrestricted emergency account and keep it permanently enabled.","why_invalid":"It creates a persistent high-impact security dependency."}],"problem":"Recovery controls introduce a new attack or misuse path.","failure":"Recovery succeeds but creates unacceptable security exposure.","cause":"Emergency mechanism bypasses normal security governance.","solution":"Design bounded, auditable, least-privilege recovery mechanisms.","alternatives":["Break-glass access with expiration.","Two-person authorization.","Offline recovery credentials with controlled custody."],"tradeoffs":["Stronger emergency controls can slow recovery.","Offline credentials can be operationally difficult."],"constraints":["Recovery must remain possible under the intended incident conditions.","Audit infrastructure may itself be degraded."],"edge_cases":["Identity-provider outage during emergency access.","Recovery requires access to a compromised control plane."],"security":["Use least privilege, strong authentication, explicit scope, and post-use review."],"performance":["Additional authorization steps can add recovery latency."],"testing":["Exercise emergency recovery under realistic identity and infrastructure failures."],"verification":["Verify emergency access controls before relying on them in production."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["authorization","audit","incident response"],"prerequisites":["recovery design"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B84-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standards_guidance","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Volume 1 — Systems Security Engineering","basis":"Security engineering across system lifecycle and operational states."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"standards_guidance","claim_scope":"Emergency recovery security"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B84-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability and security interaction","concept":"Security-reliability tradeoff documentation","knowledge_type":"governance_principle","question":"How should reliability decisions that affect security be documented?","answer":"Document the affected security invariant, availability objective, failure condition, chosen behavior, alternatives considered, residual risk, authorization, and verification evidence.","explanation":"Explicit documentation prevents emergency assumptions from becoming permanent undocumented architecture. It also allows future engineers to reassess the decision when requirements change.","principle":"Cross-domain tradeoffs should be explicit, reviewable, and reversible where practical.","rule":"Do not encode security-reliability tradeoffs only in implementation behavior; preserve the decision rationale and verification criteria.","examples":[{"scenario":"A degraded mode permits read-only access when a security dependency is unavailable.","expected_behavior":"The mode documents which operations remain allowed and which security guarantees remain enforced."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Rely on developers remembering why a fallback was implemented.","why_invalid":"The rationale can be lost during staff or architecture changes."}],"problem":"A fallback behavior becomes misunderstood or incorrectly modified.","failure":"Future changes violate the intended security-reliability balance.","cause":"Missing decision context.","solution":"Store decision records with explicit invariants and verification evidence.","alternatives":["Architecture decision records.","Security exception records.","Reliability assurance cases."],"tradeoffs":["Documentation requires maintenance.","Overly broad records can become difficult to consume."],"constraints":["Sensitive details require appropriate access controls."],"edge_cases":["Emergency decisions may initially have incomplete evidence.","Requirements can change after deployment."],"security":["Do not expose security-sensitive implementation details broadly."],"performance":["Documentation has negligible runtime impact but governance cost."],"testing":["Link the documented invariant to automated or procedural verification."],"verification":["Review the decision when dependencies, threats, or availability objectives change."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["architecture decisions","security governance"],"prerequisites":["cross-domain risk analysis"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B84-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standards_guidance","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Volume 1 — Systems Security Engineering","basis":"Traceability and explicit engineering decisions."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"standards_guidance","claim_scope":"Security-reliability governance"},"confidence":0.93,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B85-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability modeling","concept":"Reliability allocation","knowledge_type":"analytical_method","question":"How can a system-level reliability objective be allocated to components without assuming every component contributes equally?","answer":"Allocate reliability budgets according to architectural dependency, criticality, exposure, failure contribution, and available controls rather than dividing the system objective equally among components.","explanation":"A component on a critical serial path can have a much larger effect on system reliability than a component with independent redundancy or limited business impact.","principle":"Reliability budgets should reflect system structure and failure contribution.","rule":"Use dependency and failure models to allocate reliability objectives instead of arbitrary equal budgets.","examples":[{"scenario":"One authentication dependency sits on the critical path while several analytics components are optional.","expected_behavior":"The authentication dependency receives stricter reliability requirements because its failure has broader impact."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assign the same availability target to every component regardless of topology.","why_invalid":"Component criticality and dependency structure differ."}],"problem":"Reliability investment is distributed without regard to system-level impact.","failure":"Critical weak points remain under-protected.","cause":"Uniform rather than topology-aware allocation.","solution":"Allocate reliability objectives from system-level failure paths and business impact.","alternatives":["Fault-tree allocation.","SLO hierarchy.","Risk-weighted budgeting."],"tradeoffs":["Detailed allocation requires accurate dependency models.","Over-optimizing one component can shift failure elsewhere."],"constraints":["Objectives must remain achievable and measurable.","Business criticality must be defined."],"edge_cases":["Parallel redundancy changes component contribution.","Dynamic routing can change criticality over time."],"security":["Security-critical dependencies may require stronger objectives even if traffic volume is low."],"performance":["Higher reliability targets may require extra capacity or redundancy."],"testing":["Validate allocation assumptions against failure models.","Test critical component failures separately."],"verification":["Trace each component objective to a system-level reliability requirement."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["failure modeling","SLO design"],"prerequisites":["system reliability objectives"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B85-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Service Level Objectives","basis":"Hierarchical service objectives and reliability budgeting."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability objective allocation"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B85-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability modeling","concept":"Serial dependency reliability","knowledge_type":"mathematical_principle","question":"Why does a system with multiple required serial components become sensitive to the reliability of each dependency?","answer":"When all components are required for successful operation, failure of any required component can cause system failure. The system reliability is therefore constrained by the combined behavior of the required components and their dependencies.","explanation":"For independent components over a defined interval, a simple serial model multiplies component reliabilities. Real systems often violate independence, so the model is useful only when its assumptions are explicit.","principle":"Required serial dependencies compound system reliability risk.","rule":"Use serial reliability models only with clearly stated assumptions about independence, interval, and failure definition.","examples":[{"scenario":"A request requires service A and service B to both succeed.","expected_behavior":"Reliability analysis recognizes both as required dependencies and evaluates their combined effect."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Multiply availability values without checking whether outages are correlated or measured over compatible definitions.","why_invalid":"The calculation can be invalid under correlated failures or incompatible metrics."}],"problem":"System reliability is estimated incorrectly from component metrics.","failure":"Reliability target is overstated or understated.","cause":"Unstated model assumptions.","solution":"Define failure event, observation interval, dependency structure, and correlation assumptions before calculating."],"alternatives":["Fault-tree analysis.","Empirical end-to-end measurement.","SLO-based service measurement."],"tradeoffs":["Analytical models simplify reality.","Empirical measurement can be expensive or incomplete."],"constraints":["Reliability definitions must be compatible.","Independence cannot be assumed without justification."],"edge_cases":["Shared dependencies invalidate simple multiplication.","Retries can alter observed success rates without changing underlying dependency failure."],"security":["Security controls may be serial dependencies for high-risk operations."],"performance":["Retries can improve observed success while increasing latency and load."],"testing":["Compare model predictions with controlled failure scenarios where possible."],"verification":["Record assumptions with every reliability calculation."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["probability models","dependency graphs"],"prerequisites":["basic reliability mathematics"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B85-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"primary_research","source":"Classical reliability engineering theory","basis":"Series-system reliability under explicit independence assumptions."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_reliability_theory","claim_scope":"Independent series reliability model"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B85-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability modeling","concept":"Parallel redundancy reliability","knowledge_type":"mathematical_principle","question":"Under what assumptions can parallel redundancy reduce the probability of system failure?","answer":"If at least one redundant component can independently provide the required function and failures are sufficiently independent, parallel redundancy can reduce system failure probability.","explanation":"The benefit disappears when replicas share a dominant common-cause failure or cannot independently provide the required service.","principle":"Parallel redundancy provides protection only when surviving components remain functional and sufficiently independent.","rule":"State independence and functional equivalence assumptions before using parallel redundancy calculations.","examples":[{"scenario":"Two independent replicas can each serve requests without the other.","expected_behavior":"A single-replica failure does not prevent service operation."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Count two replicas as redundant when both require the same unavailable single control dependency.","why_invalid":"The common dependency can defeat both replicas."}],"problem":"Redundancy calculations overstate protection.","failure":"System fails during a shared fault despite apparent replication.","cause":"Independence or functional assumptions are invalid.","solution":"Model common dependencies and verify that surviving replicas can independently perform the required function.","alternatives":["Diverse recovery path.","N+1 capacity planning.","Active-passive failover."],"tradeoffs":["More replicas increase cost and operational complexity.","Active-active systems can create consistency and coordination risks."],"constraints":["Capacity must remain sufficient after one or more failures.","Replica health must be independently observable."],"edge_cases":["All replicas may overload after one fails.","Failover itself may introduce a new failure mode."],"security":["Replication must preserve authorization and data-protection properties."],"performance":["Redundant synchronization can increase network and storage load."],"testing":["Remove individual replicas and verify service continuity.","Test simultaneous shared dependency failure."],"verification":["Verify both independence and sufficient surviving capacity."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["redundancy","failure-domain modeling"],"prerequisites":["probability basics"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B85-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"primary_research","source":"Classical reliability engineering theory","basis":"Parallel-system reliability under explicit independence assumptions."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_reliability_theory","claim_scope":"Parallel redundancy model"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B85-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability modeling","concept":"Model uncertainty","knowledge_type":"analytical_principle","question":"Why should reliability models represent uncertainty rather than present a single calculated value as fact?","answer":"Reliability models depend on assumptions, incomplete measurements, dependency structure, workload conditions, and failure definitions. A calculated value can therefore be precise mathematically while uncertain as a representation of the real system.","explanation":"Separating mathematical calculation from model validity prevents false confidence. Sensitivity analysis and explicit assumptions reveal which uncertainties materially affect the conclusion.","principle":"Model output is conditional on model assumptions and evidence quality.","rule":"Record assumptions, uncertainty sources, evidence quality, and sensitivity when using analytical reliability models.","examples":[{"scenario":"A reliability estimate assumes independent failures but common-cause evidence is incomplete.","expected_behavior":"The result is labeled conditional and the independence assumption is recorded as uncertainty."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Present a calculated availability percentage as measured production reliability when no production measurement exists.","why_invalid":"The calculation is not an empirical observation."}],"problem":"Analytical results are mistaken for verified production behavior.","failure":"Reliability decisions rely on false precision.","cause":"Model assumptions and evidence quality are hidden.","solution":"Separate model output, assumptions, empirical evidence, and confidence.","alternatives":["Sensitivity analysis.","Interval estimates.","Empirical SLO measurement."],"tradeoffs":["Explicit uncertainty can make decisions less simple.","Collecting empirical evidence requires operational instrumentation."],"constraints":["Uncertainty must not be fabricated or assigned arbitrary statistical meaning."],"edge_cases":["Sparse failures make empirical estimates noisy.","Architecture changes can invalidate historical measurements."],"security":["Security-related failures may have asymmetric impact not captured by average rates."],"performance":["Detailed models can increase analysis complexity."],"testing":["Validate model assumptions against observed behavior where possible."],"verification":["Require explicit evidence level and assumptions for each reliability claim."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["reliability modeling","measurement"],"prerequisites":["probability concepts"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B85-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"primary_research","source":"Classical reliability engineering and statistical modeling principles","basis":"Reliability estimates are conditional on assumptions and evidence."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_principle","claim_scope":"Reliability model interpretation"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B85-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability modeling","concept":"Reliability model validation","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"How should a reliability model be validated against a real system?","answer":"Validate model assumptions and outputs against representative empirical observations, controlled failure experiments, architecture evidence, and known operating conditions, while identifying model limitations.","explanation":"Model validation is not the same as proving future reliability. It establishes whether the model adequately represents the measured behavior within a defined scope.","principle":"A reliability model is credible only within the conditions for which its assumptions and predictions have been checked.","rule":"Compare model assumptions with observed system behavior and document discrepancies rather than silently adjusting results.","examples":[{"scenario":"A model predicts the impact of a dependency failure.","expected_behavior":"A controlled test or historical evidence is used to determine whether observed behavior matches the model assumptions."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treat model agreement with one historical incident as universal validation.","why_invalid":"One observation cannot establish validity across all operating conditions."}],"problem":"A model is used outside the conditions where it was validated.","failure":"Predictions become misleading.","cause":"Validation scope was not defined.","solution":"Define validation conditions, compare predictions with evidence, and record limitations.","alternatives":["Simulation plus fault injection.","Historical incident replay.","Digital reliability model."],"tradeoffs":["Validation experiments consume engineering resources.","Controlled experiments may not reproduce every production condition."],"constraints":["No unexecuted experiment should be represented as evidence.","Model inputs must be traceable."],"edge_cases":["Architecture changes invalidate prior validation.","Rare common-cause events may lack empirical samples."],"security":["Validation environments must not expose production secrets or sensitive data."],"performance":["Simulation and fault injection can consume significant resources."],"testing":["Validate model under normal and degraded conditions.","Test sensitivity to major assumptions."],"verification":["Store model version, input assumptions, validation scope, and discrepancies."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["reliability modeling","fault injection","observability"],"prerequisites":["model uncertainty"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B85-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"primary_research","source":"Classical reliability engineering model validation principles","basis":"Model credibility depends on assumptions, evidence, and validation scope."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"established_engineering_principle","claim_scope":"Reliability model validation"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B86-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure detection quality","concept":"Detection latency versus failure duration","knowledge_type":"observability_principle","question":"Why does detection latency matter independently from recovery latency?","answer":"A system can recover quickly after detection yet still experience substantial impact if failure detection is delayed. Total disruption depends on both time to detect and time to recover.","explanation":"Detection and recovery are separate control loops. Optimizing only remediation speed can leave significant undetected failure intervals.","principle":"Reliability response time includes detection as well as diagnosis and recovery.","rule":"Measure and analyze detection latency separately from diagnosis, mitigation, and recovery latency.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service fails silently for several minutes before an alert triggers.","expected_behavior":"The reliability analysis records the detection delay separately from subsequent remediation time."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Report only the time engineers needed to fix the service.","why_invalid":"It excludes the period before the failure was detected."}],"problem":"Reliability metrics hide delayed detection.","failure":"Customer impact grows before remediation begins.","cause":"Detection latency is not measured.","solution":"Track timestamps for failure onset where observable, detection, diagnosis, mitigation, and recovery.","alternatives":["Synthetic monitoring.","Invariant-based alerts.","Client-side failure detection."],"tradeoffs":["Earlier detection can increase false positives.","More telemetry increases operational cost."],"constraints":["Failure onset may not be precisely observable.","Metrics must distinguish detection from alert delivery."],"edge_cases":["Intermittent failures complicate onset definition.","Detection can occur before customer-visible impact."],"security":["Security detection may have different evidence and confidentiality requirements."],"performance":["High-frequency health checks can add load."],"testing":["Inject failures and measure each response stage.","Test silent and intermittent failure scenarios."],"verification":["Validate timestamp semantics and clock synchronization."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["observability","incident response"],"prerequisites":["failure lifecycle modeling"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B86-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Monitoring Distributed Systems","basis":"Monitoring and alerting practices for reliable services."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Failure detection and response"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B86-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure detection quality","concept":"Detection signal quality","knowledge_type":"observability_principle","question":"What makes a reliability detection signal useful for automated or human response?","answer":"A useful signal has a defined failure meaning, appropriate sensitivity, bounded noise, sufficient context, stable semantics, and a known response path.","explanation":"A signal that triggers frequently without actionable failures creates alert fatigue, while a signal that misses important failures delays recovery.","principle":"Detection quality depends on actionable information, not alert volume.","rule":"Every reliability-critical alert should have a defined condition, expected action, owner, and validation method.","examples":[{"scenario":"An alert fires when a service violates a defined reliability condition.","expected_behavior":"The alert includes enough context to identify scope and directs responders toward a documented action."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Create an alert for every low-level metric threshold without an associated response.","why_invalid":"It creates noise without improving reliability response."}],"problem":"Alerting fails to distinguish actionable failures from normal variation.","failure":"Delayed response or alert fatigue.","cause":"Poor signal semantics and missing response mapping.","solution":"Define alert semantics, thresholds, context, ownership, and response procedures.","alternatives":["SLO-based alerts.","Multi-signal correlation.","Symptom-based monitoring."],"tradeoffs":["More sophisticated alerts can be harder to understand.","Lower alert volume may reduce sensitivity if designed poorly."],"constraints":["Alert semantics must remain stable enough for automation.","Thresholds should reflect actual failure objectives."],"edge_cases":["A single alert may represent multiple underlying causes.","A metric can be normal while users experience failure."],"security":["Alert payloads must not leak sensitive information."],"performance":["High-cardinality telemetry can increase monitoring cost."],"testing":["Generate both true failure and normal-variation scenarios.","Measure false-positive and false-negative behavior."],"verification":["Review alerts against historical incidents and controlled tests."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["monitoring","incident response"],"prerequisites":["detection latency"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B86-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Monitoring Distributed Systems","basis":"Actionable monitoring and alerting design."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability alert quality"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B86-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure detection quality","concept":"Detection blind spots","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"How should reliability teams identify monitoring blind spots?","answer":"Compare known failure modes and customer-visible failure conditions against the signals capable of detecting each condition, then identify modes with no timely or reliable detection path.","explanation":"Monitoring coverage is not equivalent to metric quantity. A system can expose many metrics while still failing to detect important customer-impacting conditions.","principle":"Observability coverage should be evaluated against failure modes, not telemetry volume.","rule":"Maintain a failure-mode-to-detection mapping for reliability-critical failure classes.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service can return incorrect results while latency and availability remain normal.","expected_behavior":"The reliability model includes correctness signals capable of detecting the failure."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Claim complete monitoring because CPU, memory, and latency metrics exist.","why_invalid":"Infrastructure metrics do not guarantee detection of semantic failures."}],"problem":"Important failure modes remain undetected.","failure":"Customer impact persists without alerting.","cause":"Detection coverage was not mapped to failure modes.","solution":"Map failure modes to detection signals and test coverage gaps.","alternatives":["Synthetic transactions.","Invariant monitoring.","End-to-end correctness checks."],"tradeoffs":["Semantic monitoring can be more expensive.","Additional checks may increase system load."],"constraints":["Some correctness properties are difficult to observe directly.","Detection should not create new critical dependencies."],"edge_cases":["A monitoring system can fail while the application remains healthy.","A detector may detect symptoms but not distinguish causes."],"security":["Detection data may contain sensitive information.","Security monitoring should preserve integrity and access controls."],"performance":["Deep correctness checks may increase latency or backend load."],"testing":["Inject representative failure modes and verify detection coverage."],"verification":["Record detection confidence and detection latency for each critical failure mode."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["failure taxonomy","observability"],"prerequisites":["detection signal quality"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B86-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Monitoring Distributed Systems","basis":"Monitoring should focus on meaningful service behavior."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Detection coverage"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B86-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure detection quality","concept":"Detection independence","knowledge_type":"architectural_principle","question":"Why should critical failure detection avoid depending entirely on the component being monitored?","answer":"A component failure can disable both the service and its self-monitoring path. Independent or differently scoped detection provides a chance to observe failures that local instrumentation cannot report.","explanation":"A detector sharing the same process, host, network path, or control plane can fail along with the target. Independence is therefore part of detection resilience.","principle":"Critical detection should remain useful when the monitored component fails.","rule":"Evaluate whether each critical detector shares the failure domains it is intended to detect.","examples":[{"scenario":"An application process fails completely.","expected_behavior":"An external health signal or client-side observation can still detect the failure."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Use only an in-process watchdog to detect total process failure.","why_invalid":"The watchdog disappears with the process."}],"problem":"Monitoring disappears during the exact failure it should detect.","failure":"Detection gap during critical outage.","cause":"Detector shares the monitored failure domain.","solution":"Use appropriately independent external, client, or infrastructure signals.","alternatives":["External synthetic monitoring.","Load-balancer health checks.","Independent watchdog."],"tradeoffs":["Independent monitoring adds infrastructure.","External signals may have less internal diagnostic detail."],"constraints":["Independence must be real for the target failure mode.","Monitoring must not become a new single point of failure."],"edge_cases":["A network partition can isolate both target and detector.","An external monitor can report stale results."],"security":["External monitors require controlled access and secure telemetry channels."],"performance":["Synthetic probes consume request capacity."],"testing":["Terminate the monitored component and verify independent detection.","Test detector failure separately."],"verification":["Document the failure domain of each critical detection mechanism."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["observability","failure domains"],"prerequisites":["detection blind spots"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B86-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Monitoring Distributed Systems","basis":"Monitoring architecture and independent service signals."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Detection resilience"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B86-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Failure detection quality","concept":"Detection failure as a modeled failure mode","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"Why should the monitoring system itself be included in reliability failure analysis?","answer":"Monitoring can become unavailable, delayed, corrupted, or misleading. If reliability decisions depend on it, its own failure modes can materially affect detection and recovery.","explanation":"A monitoring system is part of the operational control loop. Its failure can produce false confidence or unnecessary remediation.","principle":"Observability infrastructure is itself a reliability dependency when operational decisions depend on it.","rule":"Model monitoring availability, correctness, freshness, and integrity for critical automated or human decisions.","examples":[{"scenario":"An alert pipeline stops ingesting metrics while the application remains degraded.","expected_behavior":"The monitoring system reports its own health and avoids representing missing data as healthy state."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treat missing monitoring data as evidence that the service is healthy.","why_invalid":"Absence of telemetry is not evidence of absence of failure."}],"problem":"Monitoring outage creates false confidence.","failure":"Operational response is delayed or misdirected.","cause":"Monitoring failure is not modeled.","solution":"Monitor telemetry pipeline health and distinguish healthy observations from missing observations.","alternatives":["Independent heartbeat monitoring.","Multiple telemetry paths.","Client-side measurement."],"tradeoffs":["Multiple monitoring paths increase cost.","More signals can complicate interpretation."],"constraints":["Missing data semantics must be explicit.","Monitoring must not overload the production system."],"edge_cases":["Partial telemetry loss.","Clock or timestamp corruption.","Stale cached metrics."],"security":["Telemetry integrity must be protected from tampering.","Monitoring credentials require least privilege."],"performance":["Additional health monitoring consumes resources."],"testing":["Disable telemetry components and verify the system reports observability degradation.","Inject stale and malformed telemetry."],"verification":["Track monitoring freshness and ingestion completeness as explicit signals."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["observability","monitoring pipeline"],"prerequisites":["detection independence"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B86-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Monitoring Distributed Systems","basis":"Monitoring systems require reliable signal collection and interpretation."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Monitoring-system reliability"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B87-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability testing economics","concept":"Risk-based reliability testing","knowledge_type":"test_strategy","question":"How should reliability testing effort be prioritized when exhaustive failure testing is impossible?","answer":"Prioritize tests by failure impact, likelihood or exposure, uncertainty, dependency centrality, detectability, and effectiveness of existing controls.","explanation":"A finite test budget should target scenarios that can materially change reliability confidence. High-impact unknowns often deserve more attention than numerous low-impact cases.","principle":"Reliability test selection should maximize risk reduction per unit of testing effort.","rule":"Use explicit risk factors to prioritize reliability scenarios rather than selecting tests only by convenience or frequency.","examples":[{"scenario":"A rare but catastrophic recovery failure is poorly understood.","expected_behavior":"It receives targeted verification despite low historical frequency."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Test only failures that happen frequently in normal development.","why_invalid":"Rare high-impact failures can dominate risk."}],"problem":"Testing budget is consumed by low-value scenarios.","failure":"Critical unknown failure modes remain unverified.","cause":"No risk-based prioritization.","solution":"Rank scenarios by impact, uncertainty, exposure, and control weakness, then test the highest-value unknowns.","alternatives":["Fault-tree prioritization.","FMEA-based test selection.","Risk-weighted chaos experiments."],"tradeoffs":["Risk models can contain subjective inputs.","Rare-event testing may be difficult to reproduce."],"constraints":["Prioritization must not justify ignoring mandatory safety or security tests."],"edge_cases":["A low-probability failure may have unacceptable consequences.","A frequent low-impact failure may still affect user trust."],"security":["Include security-induced outages and control failures in reliability test prioritization."],"performance":["Large fault-injection campaigns can consume significant resources."],"testing":["Record why each scenario was selected and what uncertainty it addresses."],"verification":["Review whether testing reduced a documented reliability uncertainty."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["risk analysis","fault injection","testing"],"prerequisites":["failure classification"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B87-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Failure Management","basis":"Testing failure and recovery behavior as part of reliability."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability test prioritization"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B87-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability testing economics","concept":"Failure injection representativeness","knowledge_type":"test_quality","question":"Why must fault-injection scenarios represent realistic failure mechanisms rather than arbitrary disruptions?","answer":"A fault injection provides useful evidence only when the injected condition corresponds to a meaningful failure mode and the system's response is relevant to the production architecture.","explanation":"Random disruption can test general robustness but may not validate the specific assumptions behind a reliability control. Scenario realism determines the interpretation of results.","principle":"Reliability experiments should be tied to explicit failure hypotheses.","rule":"Every material fault-injection scenario should identify the failure mode, expected invariant, affected scope, and evidence required.","examples":[{"scenario":"Test loss of a dependency identified as a critical failure mode.","expected_behavior":"The experiment verifies the documented degraded behavior and recovery invariant."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Inject arbitrary failures and conclude the architecture is resilient because no visible error occurred.","why_invalid":"The experiment may not represent a meaningful production failure."}],"problem":"Fault-injection results provide weak or irrelevant evidence.","failure":"Reliability confidence is overstated.","cause":"Experiments are not linked to failure hypotheses.","solution":"Design experiments from explicit failure models and expected behavior.","alternatives":["Model-based testing.","Historical incident replay.","Scenario libraries."],"tradeoffs":["Realistic experiments require more preparation.","Highly controlled tests may omit unexpected interactions."],"constraints":["Production experiments require strict blast-radius controls.","Sensitive systems may require isolated environments."],"edge_cases":["A realistic failure may have multiple simultaneous effects.","The same injection can have different effects under different load."],"security":["Fault injection must not create uncontrolled security exposure.","Experiment authorization must be explicit."],"performance":["Experiments can temporarily consume capacity."],"testing":["Vary workload and timing where they materially affect the hypothesis."],"verification":["Store scenario definition, expected invariant, observed outcome, and limitations."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["failure modeling","fault injection"],"prerequisites":["risk-based testing"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B87-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Failure Management","basis":"Testing failure recovery and resilience mechanisms."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Fault-injection quality"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B87-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability testing economics","concept":"Test stopping criteria","knowledge_type":"test_governance","question":"What should determine when a reliability test campaign can stop?","answer":"Testing can stop when predefined coverage, evidence, risk-reduction, and acceptance criteria are satisfied for the intended reliability claims, with remaining uncertainty explicitly documented.","explanation":"Stopping because a fixed number of tests has been executed does not establish that important failure modes were covered. Completion should depend on evidence and remaining risk.","principle":"Reliability testing is complete when the required evidence is achieved, not when an arbitrary test count is reached.","rule":"Define test objectives and closure criteria before the campaign and record residual gaps when closing it.","examples":[{"scenario":"A recovery campaign targets all critical recovery paths and their known failure dependencies.","expected_behavior":"Closure occurs after required scenarios and evidence criteria are satisfied, not after a preset number of runs."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Stop after 1,000 tests even though critical recovery paths remain untested.","why_invalid":"Count does not substitute for coverage."}],"problem":"Testing appears complete while critical gaps remain.","failure":"Reliability claims exceed available evidence.","cause":"Count-based stopping criteria.","solution":"Use coverage and evidence-based closure criteria.","alternatives":["Risk-based exit criteria.","Requirement traceability matrix.","Assurance-case completion."],"tradeoffs":["Coverage criteria can be harder to define than test counts.","Some rare conditions remain uncertain even after extensive testing."],"constraints":["Uncertainty must remain explicit.","Mandatory tests cannot be skipped because other evidence is strong."],"edge_cases":["A test environment may prevent reproducing a rare production condition.","Architecture changes can invalidate completed evidence."],"security":["Security-critical reliability claims require their own acceptance criteria."],"performance":["Broad testing can increase infrastructure cost."],"testing":["Track scenario coverage, invariants, failures, and unresolved hypotheses."],"verification":["Review closure against the original reliability claim scope."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["test coverage","reliability requirements"],"prerequisites":["risk-based reliability testing"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B87-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Failure Management","basis":"Testing and verifying recovery mechanisms."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability test completion"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B87-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability testing economics","concept":"Evidence freshness","knowledge_type":"verification_principle","question":"Why can previously successful reliability tests lose evidentiary value over time?","answer":"Architecture, workload, dependencies, versions, configuration, operational procedures, and failure assumptions can change. Old test results may therefore no longer represent the current system.","explanation":"Reliability evidence has scope and temporal validity. A test result should not automatically be treated as current evidence after material changes.","principle":"Reliability evidence is contextual and can expire when its assumptions change.","rule":"Revalidate important reliability claims after changes that affect the tested behavior or assumptions.","examples":[{"scenario":"A recovery workflow is redesigned after a previously successful drill.","expected_behavior":"The old result is not treated as proof of the redesigned workflow."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Reuse a two-year-old recovery test as current evidence without checking architecture changes.","why_invalid":"The tested system may no longer match the current system."}],"problem":"Outdated evidence supports current reliability claims.","failure":"False confidence in recovery or resilience.","cause":"Evidence freshness not tracked.","solution":"Record test version, architecture scope, dependencies, and revalidation triggers.","alternatives":["Continuous validation.","Change-triggered testing.","Evidence expiration policy."],"tradeoffs":["Frequent revalidation consumes resources.","Too-frequent testing can create operational burden."],"constraints":["Freshness criteria must reflect actual risk and change velocity."],"edge_cases":["A stable component may retain evidence longer than rapidly changing components.","A small configuration change can invalidate a critical assumption."],"security":["Security changes can invalidate reliability evidence even without functional code changes."],"performance":["Revalidation can require staging or production-like capacity."],"testing":["Tag test results with system and dependency versions.","Re-run tests after defined material changes."],"verification":["Reject stale evidence automatically when its validity conditions are no longer met."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["change management","test evidence"],"prerequisites":["evidence chain"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B87-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Change Management","basis":"Reliability practices should be updated with system changes."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability evidence lifecycle"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B87-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability testing economics","concept":"Reliability test portfolio","knowledge_type":"governance_strategy","question":"Why should reliability validation use a portfolio of different test types rather than one dominant test method?","answer":"Different test methods expose different classes of uncertainty. Unit and integration tests can verify local properties, fault injection can examine failure response, recovery drills can validate operational procedures, and production telemetry can validate real-world behavior.","explanation":"No single test type covers implementation defects, distributed interactions, operational failures, and environmental variability simultaneously.","principle":"Reliability confidence should be built from complementary evidence types.","rule":"Select a test portfolio that covers prevention, detection, degradation, recovery, and operational execution.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service has strong unit tests but no recovery drill.","expected_behavior":"The reliability assessment identifies the unverified operational recovery dimension."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declare disaster recovery reliable because unit tests pass.","why_invalid":"Unit tests do not validate operational recovery."}],"problem":"Evidence is concentrated in one testing layer.","failure":"Important failure dimensions remain unverified.","cause":"Test portfolio imbalance.","solution":"Combine appropriate static, functional, failure-injection, recovery, and operational evidence.","alternatives":["Layered verification strategy.","Assurance-case evidence map.","Risk-driven validation portfolio."],"tradeoffs":["Multiple test types increase cost.","Different evidence types require different environments and expertise."],"constraints":["Each evidence type must have a defined claim scope.","Testing should not imply broader verification than actually performed."],"edge_cases":["Some tests may be mutually disruptive.","Production experiments require stricter controls."],"security":["Security validation should be integrated where security controls affect reliability."],"performance":["Large validation portfolios increase CI and environment usage."],"testing":["Map each reliability requirement to one or more suitable evidence types."],"verification":["Ensure every high-impact reliability claim has at least one appropriate verification method."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["testing","fault injection","recovery validation"],"prerequisites":["test stopping criteria"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B87-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Testing for Reliability","basis":"Multiple testing approaches contribute to reliable systems."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability validation strategy"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B88-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability and workload evolution","concept":"Workload-shape reliability","knowledge_type":"capacity_principle","question":"Why can a system remain reliable at the same average workload while becoming unreliable as workload shape changes?","answer":"Reliability can depend on burstiness, concurrency, request mix, object sizes, dependency fan-out, and timing rather than average volume alone.","explanation":"Average traffic can remain constant while peak concurrency or expensive operation proportions increase enough to exhaust a bottleneck.","principle":"Reliability capacity must account for workload characteristics, not only aggregate volume.","rule":"Model reliability-sensitive workload dimensions that materially affect resource consumption or dependency behavior.","examples":[{"scenario":"Average requests per minute stays constant while burst size doubles.","expected_behavior":"Capacity and overload analysis considers increased instantaneous concurrency."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assume capacity is unchanged because daily request volume is unchanged.","why_invalid":"Burst shape can change resource exhaustion and queue behavior."}],"problem":"Reliability degrades under changed workload shape despite stable averages.","failure":"Latency, errors, or resource exhaustion increase.","cause":"Capacity model ignores workload dimensions.","solution":"Model burstiness, concurrency, request mix, and expensive operations.","alternatives":["Load-shape profiles.","Peak-concurrency modeling.","Adaptive admission control."],"tradeoffs":["Detailed workload models require more telemetry.","Overly conservative capacity can increase cost."],"constraints":["Workload data must represent relevant operating periods.","Privacy constraints may limit payload-level analysis."],"edge_cases":["Rare synchronized events can create extreme bursts.","Autoscaling may react too slowly to sharp workload changes."],"security":["Abuse can intentionally alter workload shape.","Rate controls should account for adversarial bursts."],"performance":["More detailed workload modeling can improve capacity efficiency."],"testing":["Replay representative burst and request-mix profiles.","Test transitions between workload regimes."],"verification":["Compare model assumptions with observed workload distributions."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["capacity planning","observability"],"prerequisites":["overload control"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B88-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Workbook — Handling Operational Overload","basis":"Overload behavior and protection against resource exhaustion."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Workload-dependent reliability"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B88-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability and workload evolution","concept":"Capacity margin under uncertainty","knowledge_type":"capacity_principle","question":"Why is operating exactly at measured peak capacity unreliable even when measurements show sufficient average capacity?","answer":"Measurements contain uncertainty and workloads vary. Operating without margin leaves little room for bursts, degraded dependencies, measurement error, or capacity loss during failures.","explanation":"Reliability requires capacity for expected workload plus defined degradation scenarios. Margin should be tied to workload variability and failure assumptions rather than arbitrary percentages.","principle":"Capacity planning must include uncertainty and degraded-state requirements.","rule":"Define capacity requirements for normal operation and relevant failure states before selecting operating limits.","examples":[{"scenario":"One instance is expected to fail while traffic remains constant.","expected_behavior":"Remaining instances have enough validated capacity to handle the required degraded workload."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Use average observed CPU utilization as proof that no capacity margin is required.","why_invalid":"Average utilization does not capture peaks or failure states."}],"problem":"Normal capacity is insufficient after component loss or workload burst.","failure":"Failure triggers overload and secondary failures.","cause":"No degraded-state capacity requirement.","solution":"Model normal and degraded capacity envelopes with explicit workload assumptions.","alternatives":["N+1 capacity planning.","Autoscaling.","Admission control."],"tradeoffs":["Extra capacity costs money.","Too much headroom can reduce resource efficiency."],"constraints":["Capacity claims must use representative workload conditions.","Autoscaling cannot be assumed instantaneous."],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous failures.","Capacity loss due to dependency throttling rather than local CPU."],"security":["Security controls can increase resource consumption during attacks.","Protect capacity from uncontrolled tenants."],"performance":["More capacity improves headroom but may not fix latency caused by dependency bottlenecks."],"testing":["Test degraded capacity under representative peak workload.","Measure recovery and scaling response time."],"verification":["Record the capacity model and assumptions used to select limits."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["capacity planning","failure modeling"],"prerequisites":["workload-shape reliability"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B88-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Workbook — Handling Operational Overload","basis":"Capacity and overload protection guidance."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Capacity resilience"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B88-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability and workload evolution","concept":"Load-dependent failure modes","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"What is a load-dependent failure mode?","answer":"It is a failure whose probability or impact changes materially with workload, concurrency, queue depth, request complexity, or resource utilization.","explanation":"Some systems behave correctly at low load but fail when queues grow, locks contend, caches churn, garbage collection increases, or dependencies throttle requests.","principle":"Failure models should include workload-dependent behavior where supported by evidence.","rule":"Identify reliability controls whose behavior changes across workload regimes and test those regimes explicitly.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service remains healthy at low concurrency but experiences lock contention at high concurrency.","expected_behavior":"Concurrency-dependent reliability behavior is represented in the failure model and tests."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Test only at low load because functional correctness is unchanged.","why_invalid":"Load can change timing, resource availability, and failure probability."}],"problem":"Reliability testing misses failures that emerge only under stress.","failure":"Production incidents occur at high load despite passing functional tests.","cause":"Workload-dependent failure mode omitted.","solution":"Include load regimes in reliability modeling and validation.","alternatives":["Stress testing.","Soak testing.","Concurrency fault injection."],"tradeoffs":["High-load tests require resources.","Stress testing can reveal behavior that is difficult to reproduce."],"constraints":["Test environments must be representative enough for conclusions.","Production safety controls are required for live experiments."],"edge_cases":["Failure threshold can shift after configuration changes.","Long-duration load can reveal leaks absent in short tests."],"security":["Adversarial workload can intentionally trigger load-dependent failures."],"performance":["Load testing directly consumes significant resources."],"testing":["Test threshold, sustained, burst, and recovery load conditions where relevant."],"verification":["Capture workload parameters with every reliability result."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["load testing","capacity analysis"],"prerequisites":["workload-shape reliability"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B88-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Workbook — Handling Operational Overload","basis":"Load-induced failure and overload behavior."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Load-dependent failure"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B88-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability and workload evolution","concept":"Workload transition reliability","knowledge_type":"failure_model","question":"Why can transitions between workload regimes be less reliable than steady-state operation?","answer":"Scaling, cache warming, connection establishment, queue movement, and control-loop response can create temporary resource pressure during transitions even when both steady states are healthy.","explanation":"A system may be stable at low and high steady loads but unstable while moving between them because adaptation mechanisms have finite speed and capacity.","principle":"Reliability validation should include meaningful workload transitions, not only steady states.","rule":"Test ramp-up, ramp-down, failover-induced load shifts, and recovery transitions when adaptation behavior is part of reliability.","examples":[{"scenario":"One replica fails and traffic rapidly shifts to remaining replicas.","expected_behavior":"The transition remains within defined overload and recovery limits."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Validate autoscaling only by checking low-load and high-load steady states.","why_invalid":"Scaling transitions can create temporary overload."}],"problem":"Transition overload causes secondary failures.","failure":"System collapses during recovery or scaling.","cause":"Control-loop response slower than workload change.","solution":"Model transition dynamics and test rate-of-change scenarios.","alternatives":["Pre-scaling.","Load shedding.","Ramp-rate limits."],"tradeoffs":["Pre-scaling increases cost.","Ramp limits may temporarily reject demand."],"constraints":["Transition limits must preserve critical workload priorities."],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous transitions.","Recovery can create sudden demand on restored components."],"security":["Attackers may deliberately create rapid workload transitions."],"performance":["Pre-warming improves transition performance at additional resource cost."],"testing":["Replay rapid traffic shifts and dependency recovery.","Measure queue growth and control-loop response."],"verification":["Define acceptable transition envelopes and verify them."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["autoscaling","overload control"],"prerequisites":["load-dependent failure"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B88-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Workbook — Handling Operational Overload","basis":"Overload and system response under changing demand."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Workload transition reliability"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B88-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability and workload evolution","concept":"Workload-aware reliability envelope","knowledge_type":"verification_principle","question":"What is a workload-aware reliability envelope?","answer":"It is an explicitly defined range of workload conditions under which a reliability claim is expected to hold, including relevant volume, concurrency, request mix, burstiness, and degraded-state assumptions.","explanation":"A reliability claim without workload scope can be misleading because behavior may change outside the tested operating regime.","principle":"Reliability claims require an explicit operating envelope.","rule":"State the workload conditions and degraded scenarios covered by reliability evidence.","examples":[{"scenario":"A recovery test validates service continuity under a specified peak request rate and one dependency failure.","expected_behavior":"The claim is limited to that workload and failure scope unless additional evidence exists."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Generalize a low-load recovery test to all production traffic.","why_invalid":"The evidence does not cover the broader workload envelope."}],"problem":"Reliability evidence is generalized beyond tested conditions.","failure":"Unexpected failures occur outside the evidence scope.","cause":"Workload assumptions are implicit.","solution":"Attach workload scope to requirements, tests, and reliability claims.","alternatives":["Load profiles.","SLO scope definitions.","Capacity envelopes."],"tradeoffs":["Narrow claims are less convenient but more defensible.","Broader validation costs more."],"constraints":["The envelope must reflect realistic production conditions.","Changes in workload distribution may require revalidation."],"edge_cases":["Rare workloads may exceed normal envelope.","Multi-tenant workloads can vary independently."],"security":["Adversarial traffic should be considered where relevant."],"performance":["Expanding the envelope increases testing and capacity requirements."],"testing":["Test boundaries and selected points inside the envelope.","Test transition behavior at important boundaries."],"verification":["Record workload parameters with evidence artifacts."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["capacity modeling","load testing","SLOs"],"prerequisites":["workload transition reliability"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B88-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Workbook — Handling Operational Overload","basis":"Reliability under operational load and overload conditions."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Workload-scoped reliability claims"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B89-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability state reconstruction","concept":"State reconstruction correctness","knowledge_type":"recovery_principle","question":"What makes state reconstruction after failure reliable?","answer":"Reliable state reconstruction restores the required invariants from authoritative evidence while preventing duplication, omission, corruption, or application of stale state.","explanation":"Recovery is not simply loading the newest available snapshot. The process must establish which state is authoritative and verify that reconstructed state satisfies system invariants.","principle":"Recovery correctness is defined by restored invariants, not merely successful data loading.","rule":"Define authoritative state sources and post-reconstruction invariants before relying on automated state recovery.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service reconstructs in-memory state from durable records after restart.","expected_behavior":"The reconstructed state is validated against integrity and consistency invariants before serving dependent operations."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assume state is correct because the restore process completed without an error.","why_invalid":"A technically successful restore can still produce semantically invalid state."}],"problem":"Recovered state is internally inconsistent or stale.","failure":"Service resumes with incorrect behavior.","cause":"Recovery validates transfer but not semantic invariants.","solution":"Define authoritative sources, reconstruction rules, integrity checks, and post-recovery validation.","alternatives":["Event replay.","Snapshot plus journal.","Reconciliation against an authoritative store."],"tradeoffs":["Strong validation increases recovery time.","Multiple authoritative sources increase complexity."],"constraints":["Recovery must not accept corrupted or unauthorized state.","State validity criteria must be deterministic enough to verify."],"edge_cases":["Partial writes before failure.","Conflicting state sources.","Schema evolution during replay."],"security":["Recovered state must preserve authorization and confidentiality properties.","Integrity checks must be authenticated where required."],"performance":["Large state reconstruction can increase startup time.","Incremental recovery can reduce downtime but complicate correctness."],"testing":["Corrupt snapshots and truncate journals in controlled tests.","Verify reconstruction rejects invalid state."],"verification":["Run explicit invariant checks before declaring recovery complete."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["durable state","integrity verification"],"prerequisites":["recovery semantics"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B89-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Data Integrity and Reliability","basis":"Data integrity and correctness considerations during reliable operation and recovery."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"State recovery correctness"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B89-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability state reconstruction","concept":"Recovery invariant hierarchy","knowledge_type":"verification_principle","question":"Why should recovery validation use multiple levels of invariants?","answer":"Low-level integrity checks can confirm that bytes or records are intact while higher-level invariants confirm that relationships, business state, authorization, and system semantics remain valid.","explanation":"A recovery artifact can pass checksum validation while containing logically inconsistent state. Layered invariants provide stronger evidence of recovery correctness.","principle":"Recovery validation should progress from physical integrity to structural consistency to semantic correctness.","rule":"Define recovery invariants at the levels necessary to establish the intended recovery claim.","examples":[{"scenario":"A database snapshot passes checksum validation but references missing records.","expected_behavior":"Structural and semantic validation rejects or quarantines the inconsistent state."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treat checksum success as proof of application correctness.","why_invalid":"Integrity does not establish semantic validity."}],"problem":"Recovered data is intact but semantically invalid.","failure":"Application resumes with incorrect state.","cause":"Validation stops at low-level integrity.","solution":"Use layered integrity, consistency, and semantic validation.","alternatives":["Reconciliation.","Invariant queries.","End-to-end recovery checks."],"tradeoffs":["Higher-level checks take additional time and compute.","Complex semantic invariants can be difficult to maintain."],"constraints":["Validation must be safe before serving recovered state.","Business rules may change over time."],"edge_cases":["Historical data may legitimately violate current rules.","Partial recovery can temporarily violate invariants."],"security":["Authorization relationships must be included where relevant.","Integrity checks must resist tampering."],"performance":["Deep validation can delay service restoration."],"testing":["Inject structurally valid but semantically invalid recovery artifacts.","Verify each validation layer behaves correctly."],"verification":["Record which invariant layers were verified and which remain assumptions."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["data integrity","schema validation"],"prerequisites":["state reconstruction correctness"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B89-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Data Integrity and Reliability","basis":"Data correctness and integrity are distinct reliability concerns."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Layered recovery validation"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B89-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability state reconstruction","concept":"Recovery state fencing","knowledge_type":"consistency_principle","question":"Why must stale instances be prevented from writing after a recovery or failover?","answer":"A stale instance can reintroduce outdated state, duplicate effects, or conflicting writes after another instance becomes authoritative.","explanation":"Recovery changes authority relationships. Without fencing or equivalent coordination, an old process can continue operating against shared state and undermine the recovered system.","principle":"Recovery must control which participants are authorized to mutate shared state.","rule":"Establish and verify an authority boundary that prevents stale participants from producing conflicting writes.","examples":[{"scenario":"A failed leader restarts after a replacement leader has taken control.","expected_behavior":"The old leader cannot continue authoritative writes."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assume a restarted old process is harmless because it was previously healthy.","why_invalid":"It may possess stale authority or state."}],"problem":"Stale components write after failover.","failure":"State divergence or corruption.","cause":"Missing fencing or authority transition.","solution":"Use leases, epochs, fencing tokens, or another validated authority mechanism appropriate to the system.","alternatives":["Explicit leader epochs.","External fencing.","Generation numbers."],"tradeoffs":["Coordination adds complexity.","Incorrect fencing can block legitimate recovery."],"constraints":["Authority semantics must remain valid across partitions and restarts.","Fencing mechanism itself must be reliable."],"edge_cases":["Delayed network messages.","Process pause or long garbage-collection intervals.","Clock uncertainty when leases are used."],"security":["Authority tokens must be protected against forgery or replay."],"performance":["Coordination can add latency to writes."],"testing":["Pause old participants and recover replacements, then verify stale writes are rejected.","Test delayed messages."],"verification":["Verify stale participants cannot mutate authoritative state after fencing."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["distributed coordination","state authority"],"prerequisites":["state reconstruction"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B89-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Distributed Systems and Critical State","basis":"Distributed state and correctness under failure."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Failover state authority"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B89-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability state reconstruction","concept":"Partial recovery convergence","knowledge_type":"recovery_principle","question":"How should a distributed system handle recovery when different components restore at different times?","answer":"The system should define transitional states and convergence rules so partially recovered components cannot create invalid or conflicting outcomes before required dependencies and state are ready.","explanation":"Distributed recovery is often incremental. Treating partial recovery as normal operation can cause secondary failures or inconsistent state.","principle":"Partial recovery is a distinct system state that requires explicit semantics.","rule":"Define readiness conditions and convergence behavior for each recovery stage before allowing dependent operations.","examples":[{"scenario":"Storage recovers before the application cache and index.","expected_behavior":"The application remains in a controlled recovery state until required consistency conditions are satisfied."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Start all components as soon as their individual processes are healthy.","why_invalid":"Process health does not imply system readiness."}],"problem":"Components become operational at incompatible recovery stages.","failure":"Requests observe incomplete or inconsistent state.","cause":"No partial-recovery semantics.","solution":"Model staged recovery states, readiness conditions, and convergence checks.","alternatives":["Dependency-aware recovery DAG.","Read-only recovery mode.","Warm-up state."],"tradeoffs":["Staged recovery increases time to full service.","Strict readiness can reduce availability temporarily."],"constraints":["Readiness criteria must be observable.","Recovery state transitions must be auditable."],"edge_cases":["A dependency recovers and fails again during convergence.","Multiple recovery waves occur simultaneously."],"security":["Authorization state must converge before privileged operations are enabled."],"performance":["Warm-up can consume substantial resources."],"testing":["Interrupt recovery at each stage and verify safe behavior.","Test repeated recovery and partial dependency failure."],"verification":["Record state transitions and evidence for readiness gates."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["recovery orchestration","state modeling"],"prerequisites":["state reconstruction correctness"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B89-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Recovery","basis":"Recovery sequencing and readiness considerations."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Distributed recovery"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B89-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability state reconstruction","concept":"Recovery correctness versus recovery completion","knowledge_type":"recovery_principle","question":"Why are recovery completion and recovery correctness separate conditions?","answer":"A recovery process can finish all procedural steps while leaving incorrect, incomplete, stale, or inconsistent state. Completion means the workflow ended; correctness means the required invariants hold.","explanation":"Operational procedures often report success when commands complete. Reliability requires evidence that the resulting system state is acceptable.","principle":"Procedural completion is not equivalent to system correctness.","rule":"Define recovery acceptance using system-state invariants rather than workflow completion alone.","examples":[{"scenario":"A database restore command succeeds but application reconciliation is incomplete.","expected_behavior":"Recovery remains incomplete until application-level correctness checks pass."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Declare recovery successful because all restore commands returned success.","why_invalid":"Command success does not establish recovered system correctness."}],"problem":"Recovery status overstates actual service correctness.","failure":"Users receive incorrect or incomplete behavior after recovery.","cause":"Workflow completion used as acceptance criterion.","solution":"Separate procedural status from readiness and correctness verification.","alternatives":["Post-recovery reconciliation.","Synthetic transactions.","Business invariant checks."],"tradeoffs":["Correctness checks can extend recovery time.","Some semantic checks may require domain-specific logic."],"constraints":["Acceptance criteria must be defined before incidents.","No verification result should be claimed without evidence."],"edge_cases":["Some invariants may become temporarily relaxed during controlled recovery.","Large datasets may require asynchronous verification."],"security":["Security state must be included in recovery acceptance where relevant."],"performance":["Deep validation can consume resources during recovery."],"testing":["Test cases where procedural steps succeed but semantic state is intentionally invalid."],"verification":["Require explicit correctness evidence before marking recovery complete."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["recovery verification","state invariants"],"prerequisites":["partial recovery convergence"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B89-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE Book — Data Integrity and Reliability","basis":"Correctness and integrity must be distinguished from procedural operation."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Recovery acceptance"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B90-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability architecture assurance","concept":"Reliability claim traceability","knowledge_type":"assurance_principle","question":"What should a traceability chain contain for an important reliability claim?","answer":"It should connect the reliability requirement to the failure model, architectural control, implementation or configuration, verification method, evidence, assumptions, and residual risk.","explanation":"Traceability prevents reliability claims from becoming unsupported statements. It also enables impact analysis when requirements or architecture change.","principle":"Every material reliability claim should have an evidence-backed chain from requirement to verification.","rule":"A reliability claim is not considered fully supported until its required controls and verification evidence are traceable.","examples":[{"scenario":"The system claims recovery within a defined objective.","expected_behavior":"The claim links to the recovery design, dependency assumptions, test scenario, measured evidence, and limitations."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Store the recovery objective without linking it to implementation or verification.","why_invalid":"The requirement alone does not establish feasibility or achievement."}],"problem":"Reliability requirements cannot be mapped to evidence.","failure":"Unsupported reliability claims persist into operations.","cause":"Missing traceability.","solution":"Maintain requirement-to-control-to-evidence relationships.","alternatives":["Reliability assurance case.","Verification matrix.","Architecture traceability graph."],"tradeoffs":["Traceability requires metadata and maintenance.","Overly granular traceability can become expensive."],"constraints":["Links must refer to stable identifiers.","Evidence status must distinguish executed from planned verification."],"edge_cases":["One control may support multiple claims.","A single claim may require multiple evidence types."],"security":["Access-controlled evidence must still preserve traceability metadata."],"performance":["Traceability has minimal runtime impact but process overhead."],"testing":["Audit a sample of claims from requirement through evidence.","Check broken or stale links after changes."],"verification":["Reject closed claims with missing mandatory traceability links."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["requirements","verification","knowledge graph"],"prerequisites":["reliability evidence chain"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B90-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standards_guidance","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Volume 1 — Systems Security Engineering","basis":"Lifecycle traceability and evidence-oriented systems engineering."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"standards_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability assurance traceability"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B90-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability architecture assurance","concept":"Reliability assumption register","knowledge_type":"assurance_principle","question":"Why should reliability assumptions be stored explicitly instead of being embedded implicitly in designs and tests?","answer":"Explicit assumptions identify the conditions under which reliability claims are expected to hold and allow engineers to detect when architecture, workload, dependencies, or operations invalidate them.","explanation":"Many reliability failures occur because an assumption changed without the corresponding design or test being updated.","principle":"An untracked reliability assumption is a potential hidden dependency or stale evidence source.","rule":"Record material assumptions and define conditions that trigger their revalidation.","examples":[{"scenario":"A recovery design assumes a dependency remains available during restoration.","expected_behavior":"The assumption is recorded and recovery is tested when that dependency is unavailable."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assume the network remains available during recovery without documenting or testing the assumption.","why_invalid":"The recovery claim depends on an unverified condition."}],"problem":"Reliability evidence becomes invalid after assumption drift.","failure":"Recovery or resilience behavior differs from expectations.","cause":"Implicit assumptions were not tracked.","solution":"Maintain an assumption register with scope, evidence, owner, and revalidation triggers.","alternatives":["Architecture decision records.","Reliability contracts.","Assumption-aware assurance cases."],"tradeoffs":["Assumption management creates governance overhead.","Some assumptions are difficult to measure continuously."],"constraints":["Only material assumptions should be tracked to avoid noise.","Assumptions should have stable identifiers."],"edge_cases":["Assumptions can be probabilistic or conditional.","An assumption can become invalid only during rare failure states."],"security":["Security assumptions such as trusted identity services must be included when reliability depends on them."],"performance":["Assumption monitoring may require additional telemetry."],"testing":["Create tests that challenge high-impact assumptions.","Revalidate after material architecture changes."],"verification":["Each critical assumption should have supporting evidence or explicit residual uncertainty."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["reliability claims","change management"],"prerequisites":["traceability"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B90-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standards_guidance","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Volume 1 — Systems Security Engineering","basis":"Explicit assumptions, requirements, and lifecycle traceability."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"standards_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability assumption management"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B90-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability architecture assurance","concept":"Reliability readiness gate","knowledge_type":"governance_control","question":"What should a reliability readiness gate evaluate before a high-impact system change is released?","answer":"It should evaluate reliability requirements, failure modes, dependency changes, recovery behavior, observability, capacity, testing evidence, rollback capability, assumptions, and unresolved residual risk relevant to the change.","explanation":"A release can be functionally correct while introducing new failure modes or invalidating recovery assumptions. A reliability gate evaluates the change in system context.","principle":"Release readiness should consider failure behavior and recovery evidence, not only functional correctness.","rule":"Require explicit reliability acceptance criteria for changes that materially affect failure behavior or critical dependencies.","examples":[{"scenario":"A change introduces a new critical external dependency.","expected_behavior":"Release review evaluates dependency failure, fallback behavior, capacity, monitoring, and recovery evidence."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Approve release because functional tests pass while the new dependency has no failure test.","why_invalid":"Functional success does not establish resilience to dependency failure."}],"problem":"Changes introduce reliability regressions despite passing normal tests.","failure":"Production reliability decreases after release.","cause":"Reliability-specific release evidence absent.","solution":"Add change-impact-based reliability readiness gates.","alternatives":["Progressive delivery.","Automated reliability policy checks.","Risk-based approval."],"tradeoffs":["Gates can slow deployment.","Overly broad gates create process fatigue."],"constraints":["Gate requirements should scale with change risk.","Emergency changes require explicit exception and post-change validation."],"edge_cases":["A small code change can alter timing or resource use significantly.","A dependency-only configuration change can alter failure behavior."],"security":["Security control changes can create reliability dependencies and require review."],"performance":["Reliability checks may increase release pipeline time."],"testing":["Automate applicable failure and rollback tests before approval."],"verification":["Record gate criteria, evidence, approver, and residual risks."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["change management","testing","risk assessment"],"prerequisites":["reliability traceability"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B90-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar — Change Management","basis":"Safe change implementation and validation."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability release governance"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B90-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability architecture assurance","concept":"Residual reliability risk acceptance","knowledge_type":"risk_governance","question":"When is it appropriate to accept residual reliability risk?","answer":"Residual risk can be accepted when the remaining risk is explicitly understood, within the organization's defined tolerance, supported by available evidence, assigned to an accountable authority, and not contrary to mandatory requirements.","explanation":"No system can eliminate every failure. Explicit risk acceptance is preferable to silently treating unverified behavior as safe.","principle":"Uneliminated reliability risk should be visible and consciously governed.","rule":"Never convert unknown reliability gaps into assumed safety; record uncertainty and obtain appropriate risk acceptance when remediation is deferred.","examples":[{"scenario":"A rare failure mode cannot currently be eliminated but has a tested containment strategy.","expected_behavior":"The remaining risk and evidence limitations are recorded and formally accepted by the appropriate authority."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Treat a failure mode as acceptable because it has never occurred in production.","why_invalid":"Absence of observed failure does not establish absence of risk."}],"problem":"Unresolved reliability gaps disappear from engineering visibility.","failure":"Known uncertainty becomes hidden operational risk.","cause":"Informal or implicit risk acceptance.","solution":"Record residual risk, evidence, assumptions, owner, and acceptance authority.","alternatives":["Further testing.","Additional containment.","Architecture redesign."],"tradeoffs":["Risk acceptance can accelerate delivery but preserves exposure.","Overly conservative acceptance criteria can delay low-risk work."],"constraints":["Mandatory requirements cannot be waived informally.","Acceptance must be time-bounded where appropriate."],"edge_cases":["Evidence may improve after deployment.","A new dependency can change previously accepted risk."],"security":["Security-related residual risk may require specialized authorization.","Do not use reliability acceptance to bypass mandatory security controls."],"performance":["Mitigating residual risk may require additional resources."],"testing":["Test containment controls even when primary failure remains unresolved."],"verification":["Review accepted risks periodically and after material changes."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["risk governance","evidence management"],"prerequisites":["assumption register"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B90-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"standards_guidance","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Volume 1 — Systems Security Engineering","basis":"Risk management and explicit treatment of unresolved engineering concerns."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"standards_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability residual-risk governance"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B90-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability architecture assurance","concept":"Reliability closure audit","knowledge_type":"dataset_and_system_validation","question":"What should a final reliability closure audit verify before a reliability phase is considered complete?","answer":"The audit should verify scope coverage, unique stable records, requirement traceability, failure-model coverage, recovery evidence, testing status, assumptions, residual risks, provenance, contradictions, and unresolved gaps.","explanation":"Completion should mean that the reliability knowledge and engineering evidence are internally coherent and that remaining uncertainty is explicit. A record count alone cannot establish closure.","principle":"Reliability phase closure is an evidence and coverage decision, not a record-count decision.","rule":"Lock a reliability dataset or engineering phase only after schema, semantic, coverage, provenance, deduplication, and gap audits pass within the defined scope.","examples":[{"scenario":"A dataset contains reliability concepts across failure, recovery, testing, observability, and assurance.","expected_behavior":"Closure checks confirm each required scope area is represented and no unresolved critical gap is hidden."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Lock the dataset because the planned number of records has been reached.","why_invalid":"Record count does not prove scope completeness or quality."}],"problem":"A reliability phase is declared complete with hidden gaps or duplicates.","failure":"Downstream systems learn incomplete or contradictory reliability knowledge.","cause":"Count-based rather than evidence-based closure.","solution":"Use a structured closure audit with explicit acceptance criteria.","alternatives":["Automated dataset validator.","Coverage matrix.","Assurance-case closure review."],"tradeoffs":["Closure audits require additional time.","Strict closure can expose unresolved gaps that require more work."],"constraints":["Unverified execution claims must remain explicitly unverified.","Historical or technology-specific evidence must retain scope."],"edge_cases":["A scope item may be intentionally excluded and must be documented.","New evidence can require reopening a locked phase."],"security":["Dataset provenance and sensitive operational evidence require appropriate handling."],"performance":["Large-scale validation may require indexing and deduplication tooling."],"testing":["Run schema, duplicate, relationship, retrieval, contradiction, and coverage tests."],"verification":["Require deterministic validation reports before lock status.","Record validator version and dataset hash when available."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["dataset validation","deduplication","provenance","coverage analysis"],"prerequisites":["reliability traceability","residual risk governance"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B90-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B90-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Framework Reliability Pillar","basis":"Reliability assessment, operational readiness, failure management, and continuous improvement."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"documented_engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability phase and knowledge-fabric closure"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D22-B91-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Software aging","concept":"Reliability degradation through accumulated system age","knowledge_type":"architectural_principle","question":"How should a system account for reliability degradation caused by long-running operation and accumulated state?","answer":"Reliability engineering must consider degradation mechanisms that emerge only after prolonged operation, such as resource leakage, unbounded state growth, fragmentation, stale metadata, accumulated queues, certificate or credential expiration, and operational drift.","explanation":"A system can pass short-duration tests while becoming less reliable over long periods. Reliability validation therefore needs temporal scenarios that exercise state accumulation, renewal boundaries, cleanup behavior, and long-lived dependencies.","principle":"Reliability is a lifecycle property, not only a startup property.","rule":"Identify every state, resource, credential, cache, queue, and metadata structure whose behavior can change as operating time increases.","examples":[{"scenario":"A long-running service accumulates expired internal records.","expected_behavior":"Retention and cleanup mechanisms bound state growth without deleting required data."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Assume a service is reliable because a one-hour test completed successfully.","why_invalid":"The test does not establish behavior under long-duration state accumulation."}],"problem":"Reliability degrades after prolonged operation.","failure":"Progressive resource exhaustion or stale-state behavior.","cause":"Unbounded state, leaks, expiration, or cleanup failure.","solution":"Define bounded-state invariants, lifecycle cleanup, renewal behavior, and long-duration validation.","alternatives":["Periodic restart","State compaction","Externalized state management"],"tradeoffs":["Restarts may reduce accumulated state but can introduce recovery risk.","Aggressive cleanup can remove data required for recovery."],"constraints":["Retention requirements must be respected.","Cleanup must be safe under concurrent activity."],"edge_cases":["Cleanup itself fails.","State grows faster than cleanup capacity.","A restart occurs during compaction."],"security":["Expired credentials and certificates must not remain implicitly trusted."],"performance":["Cleanup and compaction consume resources and must be capacity-bounded."],"testing":["Run extended-duration tests with controlled state growth.","Inject cleanup failures and recovery interruptions."],"verification":["Verify state-growth invariants before and after prolonged operation."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["State management","Resource lifecycle management"],"prerequisites":["Understanding of system state and resource ownership"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B91-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B91-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Vol. 1 Rev. 1 — Engineering Trustworthy Secure Systems","basis":"Lifecycle-oriented engineering and validation of trustworthy systems."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"authoritative_guidance","claim_scope":"General reliability engineering"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B91-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Software aging","concept":"Bounded state growth","knowledge_type":"failure_prevention","question":"Why must reliability-critical state have explicit growth bounds?","answer":"State without a defined lifecycle can grow until storage, memory, indexes, queues, or processing capacity become constrained. Reliability requires explicit ownership, retention, compaction, archival, or deletion policies for state that is not permanently required.","explanation":"The relevant invariant is not merely that state exists, but that its growth remains compatible with available resources and operational objectives.","principle":"Every persistent or accumulating state structure requires a lifecycle policy.","rule":"Do not introduce unbounded accumulation into a reliability-critical path without an explicit capacity and lifecycle strategy.","examples":[{"scenario":"An event journal grows continuously.","expected_behavior":"Retention, archival, or compaction keeps operational storage within defined limits while preserving required recovery evidence."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Keep every event indefinitely in primary operational storage.","why_invalid":"It creates an unbounded dependency on storage capacity."}],"problem":"Unbounded data growth.","failure":"Storage exhaustion or degraded query and recovery performance.","cause":"Missing retention and capacity controls.","solution":"Define maximum operational footprint and transition older state to appropriate lifecycle tiers.","alternatives":["Partitioning","Archival storage","Compaction"],"tradeoffs":["Archival may increase retrieval latency.","Compaction can increase CPU and I/O requirements."],"constraints":["Regulatory or forensic retention may prohibit deletion."],"edge_cases":["Retention metadata becomes corrupted.","Archive destination is unavailable.","Compaction runs during peak load."],"security":["Retention must preserve access controls and confidentiality."],"performance":["Lifecycle operations should be rate-limited."],"testing":["Test near-capacity behavior and cleanup failure."],"verification":["Demonstrate bounded operational state under representative growth."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Storage","Capacity management"],"prerequisites":["Resource capacity concepts"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B91-001"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B96-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Reliability Pillar — Foundations","basis":"Reliability foundations require adequate resources and capacity planning."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"authoritative_guidance","claim_scope":"Operational reliability"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B91-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Lifecycle expiration","concept":"Expiration boundary reliability","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"How should systems handle expiration events that can suddenly invalidate previously working dependencies?","answer":"Expiration events such as certificates, tokens, leases, keys, licenses, cached metadata, and service registrations must have explicit detection, renewal, fallback, and failure-handling behavior.","explanation":"Expiration differs from random failure because it is often predictable. Reliability engineering can therefore detect approaching boundaries and avoid avoidable outages through proactive renewal and validation.","principle":"Predictable expiration should be treated as an engineered lifecycle event rather than an unexpected fault.","rule":"Every reliability-critical expiring artifact must have an observable owner, renewal mechanism, validity check, and failure path.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service certificate approaches expiration.","expected_behavior":"Renewal occurs before expiration and the resulting artifact is validated before activation."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Renew only after requests begin failing.","why_invalid":"Reactive renewal can convert a predictable event into an outage."}],"problem":"Critical artifact expires.","failure":"Authentication, communication, or service startup failure.","cause":"Missing or failed renewal.","solution":"Monitor validity windows and implement proactive renewal with safe activation.","alternatives":["Manual renewal","Redundant credentials"],"tradeoffs":["Early renewal increases operational complexity.","Redundant credentials require secure lifecycle management."],"constraints":["Renewal must preserve compatibility with consumers."],"edge_cases":["Renewal succeeds but activation fails.","Clock skew changes perceived expiration.","Both old and new credentials become invalid."],"security":["Credential lifecycle must prevent unauthorized substitution."],"performance":["Expiration checks should not add excessive request overhead."],"testing":["Simulate near-expiration and failed renewal."],"verification":["Verify continued service operation across the expiration boundary."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Identity or credential lifecycle"],"prerequisites":["Understanding of time and lifecycle boundaries"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B34-001"},{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B91-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Vol. 1 Rev. 1","basis":"Lifecycle engineering, validation, and trustworthy system requirements."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"authoritative_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability-critical lifecycle events"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B91-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Operational drift","concept":"Reliability degradation caused by configuration drift","knowledge_type":"failure_prevention","question":"Why can configuration drift become a reliability failure even when application code has not changed?","answer":"Operational environments can diverge from validated configurations through manual changes, inconsistent defaults, stale policies, or undocumented dependencies. Reliability requires detecting and controlling such divergence.","explanation":"The system tested during validation may no longer match the system operating in production. Drift therefore creates a gap between verified assumptions and actual behavior.","principle":"Reliability evidence applies only to the configuration and environment it actually represents.","rule":"Detect material divergence from approved reliability-relevant configuration and either reconcile it or explicitly revalidate the changed state.","examples":[{"scenario":"A production timeout is manually changed from its validated value.","expected_behavior":"The change is detected, assessed, and either reverted or subjected to appropriate validation."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Ignore configuration differences because application binaries are unchanged.","why_invalid":"Reliability behavior can depend directly on configuration."}],"problem":"Validated and deployed environments diverge.","failure":"Unexpected behavior under load or failure.","cause":"Undetected configuration drift.","solution":"Version, monitor, reconcile, and validate reliability-relevant configuration.","alternatives":["Immutable infrastructure","Configuration enforcement"],"tradeoffs":["Strict enforcement can reduce operational flexibility.","Continuous reconciliation adds control-plane complexity."],"constraints":["Emergency changes may temporarily bypass normal workflows."],"edge_cases":["Drift detector itself is unavailable.","A legitimate emergency configuration is incorrectly reverted."],"security":["Configuration drift can weaken security controls."],"performance":["Drift detection must avoid excessive polling overhead."],"testing":["Inject controlled configuration divergence."],"verification":["Compare deployed configuration against the approved baseline."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Configuration management","Change management"],"prerequisites":["Versioned configuration concepts"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B91-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Reliability Pillar — Change Management","basis":"Reliable systems require controlled and validated changes."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"authoritative_guidance","claim_scope":"Production reliability"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B91-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Long-run validation","concept":"Reliability evidence over time","knowledge_type":"verification_principle","question":"What evidence is needed to support a claim that a reliability mechanism remains effective over long operation?","answer":"Evidence should cover temporal state growth, repeated recovery cycles, expiration boundaries, resource trends, configuration stability, and the behavior of maintenance mechanisms.","explanation":"Short tests demonstrate point behavior; long-run reliability claims require evidence that important invariants remain true across time and repeated lifecycle transitions.","principle":"Reliability claims must match the duration and conditions of the behavior being claimed.","rule":"Do not generalize short-duration test evidence into an unqualified long-duration reliability claim.","examples":[{"scenario":"A service is expected to operate continuously for months.","expected_behavior":"Validation includes long-duration state, resource, renewal, and recovery behavior."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"A 30-minute test proves indefinite reliability.","why_invalid":"The evidence scope is narrower than the claim."}],"problem":"Evidence does not match reliability claim scope.","failure":"Undetected long-term degradation.","cause":"Insufficient temporal validation.","solution":"Align validation duration and scenarios with the operational claim.","alternatives":["Accelerated aging tests","Synthetic lifecycle simulation"],"tradeoffs":["Long tests cost time and infrastructure.","Acceleration may not reproduce every real mechanism."],"constraints":["Some failures depend on rare environmental events."],"edge_cases":["Aging interacts with deployment changes.","Long tests themselves require recovery."],"security":["Long-lived credentials and keys need lifecycle validation."],"performance":["Track resource trends rather than only point measurements."],"testing":["Combine soak, stress, recovery, and lifecycle tests."],"verification":["Require explicit evidence for each long-duration assumption."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Observability","Testing","Capacity management"],"prerequisites":["Reliability evidence concepts"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B99-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Vol. 1 Rev. 1","basis":"Verification and validation should support trustworthy system claims across the system lifecycle."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"authoritative_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability assurance"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B92-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability observability","concept":"Observability completeness","knowledge_type":"observability_principle","question":"What does complete reliability observability require?","answer":"Reliability observability requires enough telemetry to detect relevant failures, distinguish degraded states, understand affected dependencies, evaluate recovery progress, and verify restoration.","explanation":"A single availability metric cannot explain whether the system is failing, why it is failing, what remains affected, or whether recovery actually succeeded.","principle":"Telemetry must support reliability decisions, not merely produce dashboards.","rule":"For each critical reliability state, define observable indicators for detection, diagnosis, recovery, and verification.","examples":[{"scenario":"A dependency becomes partially unavailable.","expected_behavior":"Telemetry distinguishes dependency failure, local impact, fallback activation, and recovery state."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Use only aggregate request success rate for all reliability diagnosis.","why_invalid":"Aggregate success can hide localized or dependency-specific failures."}],"problem":"Insufficient reliability evidence.","failure":"Slow or incorrect diagnosis and recovery.","cause":"Telemetry gaps.","solution":"Map reliability states and transitions to observable signals.","alternatives":["Distributed tracing","Structured event logs","State metrics"],"tradeoffs":["More telemetry increases storage and processing cost.","Excess telemetry can reduce signal clarity."],"constraints":["Telemetry must remain available during degraded operation."],"edge_cases":["The telemetry pipeline fails with the workload.","Metrics become stale during network partition."],"security":["Telemetry must avoid leaking secrets or sensitive payloads."],"performance":["Instrumentation overhead must be bounded."],"testing":["Disable telemetry paths and verify degraded observability behavior."],"verification":["Trace every critical failure path from detection through recovery verification."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Monitoring","Logging","Tracing"],"prerequisites":["Failure lifecycle concepts"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B92-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B100-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Reliability Pillar — Design your workload to withstand component failures","basis":"Monitoring all workload components supports failure detection and recovery."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"authoritative_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability observability"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B92-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability observability","concept":"Telemetry failure independence","knowledge_type":"architectural_principle","question":"Why should reliability-critical telemetry avoid sharing the same failure path as the workload it observes?","answer":"If telemetry depends on the same overloaded or failed resources as the workload, the system can become blind precisely when observability is most needed. Critical telemetry should therefore have sufficient isolation or degradation behavior.","explanation":"Observability is part of the recovery mechanism. Its failure mode must be considered independently rather than assumed to remain available during incidents.","principle":"The mechanism used to detect failure must not fail for the same reason as the component being observed.","rule":"Identify common failure dependencies between workload and telemetry and provide bounded fallback where required.","examples":[{"scenario":"Application storage becomes saturated.","expected_behavior":"Critical health signals remain available through an independent or lower-resource path."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Write diagnostic events to the same exhausted storage path and assume they will remain available.","why_invalid":"The telemetry path shares the failure."}],"problem":"Observability disappears during incidents.","failure":"Loss of detection and recovery evidence.","cause":"Shared resource or dependency failure.","solution":"Isolate critical telemetry resources and define degraded telemetry behavior.","alternatives":["Local emergency buffers","Out-of-band telemetry"],"tradeoffs":["Isolation increases architectural complexity.","Buffers can lose data if capacity is exceeded."],"constraints":["Telemetry must remain privacy and security compliant."],"edge_cases":["Telemetry destination is unavailable for extended periods.","Local buffers fill during a prolonged outage."],"security":["Out-of-band channels require authentication and protection."],"performance":["Telemetry must have bounded resource consumption."],"testing":["Inject telemetry-destination and resource failures."],"verification":["Demonstrate that critical detection survives representative workload failures."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Observability infrastructure","Resource isolation"],"prerequisites":["Failure-domain concepts"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B92-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Reliability Pillar — Failure Management","basis":"Failure detection and automated recovery depend on monitoring and fault-aware design."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"authoritative_guidance","claim_scope":"Reliability observability architecture"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B92-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Telemetry quality","concept":"Telemetry semantic correctness","knowledge_type":"verification_principle","question":"Why is syntactically valid telemetry insufficient for reliability engineering?","answer":"Telemetry can be present yet semantically misleading because labels may be wrong, timestamps may be stale, units may differ, aggregation may hide failures, or events may be emitted before an operation is actually complete.","explanation":"Reliability decisions depend on the meaning of telemetry, not merely its presence. Incorrect semantics can cause false recovery conclusions or hide ongoing faults.","principle":"Telemetry is evidence and must have verified semantics.","rule":"Define and test the semantic meaning, units, timing, cardinality, and lifecycle point represented by critical signals.","examples":[{"scenario":"A recovery metric is emitted when remediation starts.","expected_behavior":"The metric clearly represents recovery initiation, while a separate signal represents verified recovery."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Call remediation started equivalent to service restored.","why_invalid":"Initiation and verified outcome are different states."}],"problem":"Operators misinterpret telemetry.","failure":"Incorrect recovery decisions.","cause":"Ambiguous or semantically incorrect signals.","solution":"Document signal contracts and test lifecycle semantics.","alternatives":["Typed event schemas","State transition telemetry"],"tradeoffs":["Stronger schemas require more maintenance.","More lifecycle signals increase telemetry volume."],"constraints":["Backward compatibility may constrain schema evolution."],"edge_cases":["Events arrive out of order.","Clocks differ across components."],"security":["Telemetry schemas must prevent injection or untrusted metadata abuse."],"performance":["Validation should be efficient for high-volume signals."],"testing":["Test timestamps, state transitions, units, and missing events."],"verification":["Compare telemetry against authoritative system state during controlled scenarios."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Telemetry schema","State model"],"prerequisites":["Observability fundamentals"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B92-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Vol. 1 Rev. 1","basis":"Verification and validation require trustworthy evidence about system behavior."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Telemetry reliability"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B92-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Telemetry quality","concept":"Detection coverage versus observability volume","knowledge_type":"tradeoff","question":"Why does adding more telemetry not automatically improve reliability?","answer":"Additional telemetry can increase cost, cardinality, noise, processing load, and operator confusion. Reliability observability should optimize for decision-relevant coverage rather than maximum data volume.","explanation":"Useful observability identifies the signals required to detect and reason about important reliability states. Excessive low-value telemetry can obscure meaningful changes.","principle":"Signal quality and decision relevance matter more than raw telemetry volume.","rule":"Every critical signal should have an identified reliability decision or verification purpose.","examples":[{"scenario":"Thousands of debug events are generated during overload.","expected_behavior":"High-value health and recovery signals remain distinguishable from diagnostic noise."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Collect every possible event without retention or prioritization.","why_invalid":"Volume without semantic prioritization can degrade observability itself."}],"problem":"Telemetry overload.","failure":"Important reliability signals become difficult to detect.","cause":"Unbounded or low-value telemetry.","solution":"Prioritize, sample, aggregate, and retain evidence according to reliability importance.","alternatives":["Adaptive sampling","Tiered telemetry","Event prioritization"],"tradeoffs":["Sampling can remove detail needed for rare-event diagnosis.","Aggregation can hide localized failures."],"constraints":["Critical evidence must not be sampled away."],"edge_cases":["Incident causes telemetry volume to spike.","High-cardinality labels explode storage usage."],"security":["Telemetry access must be controlled."],"performance":["Instrumentation and transport overhead must be bounded."],"testing":["Generate telemetry overload and verify critical-signal preservation."],"verification":["Confirm required detection and recovery signals remain observable under stress."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Monitoring","Logging","Tracing"],"prerequisites":["Observability architecture"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B92-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Reliability Pillar","basis":"Reliability monitoring should support failure detection, analysis, and recovery."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Observability design"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B92-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Reliability observability","concept":"Recovery verification telemetry","knowledge_type":"verification_principle","question":"What telemetry is required to distinguish attempted recovery from successful recovery?","answer":"Recovery telemetry should represent at least initiation, action outcome, resulting system state, residual impact, and restoration verification where applicable.","explanation":"A remediation command completing successfully does not prove the system returned to its required state. Recovery evidence must observe the post-action invariant.","principle":"Action completion and outcome verification are distinct reliability states.","rule":"Do not mark a failure recovered solely because the recovery procedure returned success.","examples":[{"scenario":"A failed component is restarted.","expected_behavior":"Recovery is considered complete only after health and functional invariants are verified."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Restart command returned exit code zero, therefore service is recovered.","why_invalid":"The process can start successfully while the service remains functionally degraded."}],"problem":"False recovery confirmation.","failure":"Incident is prematurely closed.","cause":"Recovery action treated as recovery proof.","solution":"Define post-recovery health and functional acceptance criteria.","alternatives":["Synthetic transactions","Invariant checks","Dependency verification"],"tradeoffs":["Functional verification adds load.","Synthetic checks may themselves have dependencies."],"constraints":["Verification must not worsen the incident."],"edge_cases":["Partial recovery","Recovery oscillation","Healthy infrastructure with corrupted application state"],"security":["Verification probes must use controlled credentials and permissions."],"performance":["Verification traffic must be bounded."],"testing":["Inject successful-looking remediation with failed postconditions."],"verification":["Require independent evidence of restored service behavior."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Monitoring","Health checks","Functional validation"],"prerequisites":["Recovery lifecycle concepts"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B99-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Reliability Pillar — Design your workload to withstand component failures","basis":"Failure recovery should include monitoring, failover, healing, and availability validation."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"authoritative_guidance","claim_scope":"Recovery verification"},"confidence":0.98,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B93-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Security-reliability interaction","concept":"Security control failure as a reliability event","knowledge_type":"cross_domain_principle","question":"Why should security-control failures be modeled as reliability events?","answer":"Authentication, authorization, encryption, certificate validation, policy enforcement, and security telemetry can become dependencies whose failure affects availability and recovery. Reliability architecture must define safe behavior when those controls or their dependencies fail.","explanation":"Security and reliability are coupled system properties. A security dependency can fail closed, fail open, degrade, or block recovery; each behavior has different risk and reliability consequences.","principle":"Security mechanisms are part of the system dependency graph and must have explicit failure behavior.","rule":"For each security-critical dependency, define its failure mode, safe state, recovery path, and operational impact.","examples":[{"scenario":"Authorization service becomes unavailable.","expected_behavior":"The system follows a predefined security-preserving degraded mode rather than an undefined behavior."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Disable authorization whenever it becomes inconvenient during an outage.","why_invalid":"This creates an uncontrolled security failure mode."}],"problem":"Security dependency failure disrupts service.","failure":"Availability loss or unsafe access behavior.","cause":"Unmodeled dependency failure.","solution":"Design explicit security-preserving degraded modes and recovery paths.","alternatives":["Local authorization cache","Redundant authorization service"],"tradeoffs":["Caching can introduce stale authorization decisions.","Redundancy increases complexity."],"constraints":["Security requirements may prohibit fail-open behavior."],"edge_cases":["Emergency access during identity-provider outage.","Stale policy cache during incident."],"security":["Security must remain effective in degraded modes."],"performance":["Local policy evaluation may reduce dependency latency."],"testing":["Test security dependency outages and recovery."],"verification":["Verify both availability and authorization invariants."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Identity","Authorization","Security policy"],"prerequisites":["Failure modeling","Security architecture"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B93-002"},{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B99-003"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Vol. 1 Rev. 1","basis":"Trustworthiness integrates security, resilience, verification, and lifecycle engineering."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"authoritative_guidance","claim_scope":"Security-reliability interaction"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B93-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Security-reliability interaction","concept":"Fail-safe versus fail-open reliability decisions","knowledge_type":"decision_rule","question":"How should fail-safe and fail-open behavior be selected when a security dependency fails?","answer":"The choice must be based on explicit system requirements, threat consequences, business criticality, and acceptable degraded states rather than generic preference. The selected behavior must be deterministic and tested.","explanation":"Fail-open may preserve availability while increasing unauthorized access risk; fail-safe behavior may protect assets while reducing availability. Reliability engineering must make the tradeoff explicit.","principle":"Security degradation policy is a system requirement, not an implicit implementation detail.","rule":"Document and verify the required state for every security dependency failure.","examples":[{"scenario":"A sensitive administrative operation loses authorization connectivity.","expected_behavior":"The operation is denied or safely deferred according to the defined security requirement."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Choose fail-open globally to maximize uptime.","why_invalid":"The resulting security risk may violate system requirements."}],"problem":"Undefined degraded security state.","failure":"Unsafe access or unnecessary outage.","cause":"Missing failure policy.","solution":"Specify per-operation degraded behavior based on protection requirements.","alternatives":["Cached decisions","Break-glass workflows"],"tradeoffs":["Caching introduces staleness.","Break-glass access increases operational complexity and audit requirements."],"constraints":["High-impact actions may require stronger controls."],"edge_cases":["Dependency unavailable during disaster recovery.","Policy changes occur while cache is stale."],"security":["Security policy remains authoritative over availability preference."],"performance":["Local decisions may improve latency."],"testing":["Exercise each degraded security mode."],"verification":["Verify authorization outcomes against explicit requirements."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Authorization","Risk requirements"],"prerequisites":["Security policy modeling"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B93-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Vol. 1 Rev. 1","basis":"Trustworthiness requires engineering security requirements together with other critical requirements."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Security failure behavior"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B93-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Security-reliability interaction","concept":"Recovery authorization reliability","knowledge_type":"governance_principle","question":"Why must recovery automation itself be subject to authorization controls?","answer":"Recovery actions can modify production state and therefore must have defined authority, scope, auditability, and safety constraints. Reliability does not justify unrestricted automated access.","explanation":"An automated recovery system that can alter infrastructure or data becomes a high-impact control path. Its own failure or compromise can create a secondary incident.","principle":"Recovery authority must be bounded as carefully as normal operational authority.","rule":"Define least-privilege recovery permissions and constrain automated actions to explicitly authorized resources and conditions.","examples":[{"scenario":"An automated recovery restarts a failed service.","expected_behavior":"The automation can affect only approved services within the defined recovery scope."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Grant recovery automation unrestricted administrative access.","why_invalid":"A fault or compromise could expand recovery failure into broad system impact."}],"problem":"Recovery mechanism becomes a high-impact dependency.","failure":"Unauthorized or excessive remediation.","cause":"Overbroad recovery authority.","solution":"Use scoped permissions, policy checks, audit trails, and action limits.","alternatives":["Human approval","Pre-authorized runbooks"],"tradeoffs":["Human approval can slow recovery.","Strict scopes can limit automation flexibility."],"constraints":["Emergency recovery must remain operationally usable."],"edge_cases":["Recovery requires access to a previously unknown dependency.","Identity service itself is unavailable."],"security":["Strong authentication and authorization are mandatory."],"performance":["Authorization checks must not create unacceptable recovery delay."],"testing":["Attempt out-of-scope recovery actions."],"verification":["Audit actual recovery actions against policy."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Authorization","Automation","Audit"],"prerequisites":["Recovery orchestration"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B98-004"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Vol. 1 Rev. 1","basis":"Trustworthy systems require explicit security requirements, authorization, validation, and verification."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"authoritative_guidance","claim_scope":"Recovery governance"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B93-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Security-reliability interaction","concept":"Integrity protection during recovery","knowledge_type":"data_integrity_principle","question":"Why must recovery preserve data integrity rather than only restore service availability?","answer":"A service can become reachable while its state is corrupted, inconsistent, incomplete, or unauthorized. Recovery acceptance must therefore include integrity checks appropriate to the data and operation.","explanation":"Availability without integrity can produce silently incorrect outcomes and may be worse than controlled unavailability.","principle":"Recovery is successful only when required safety, security, integrity, and functional invariants are restored.","rule":"Define integrity acceptance criteria for every recovery workflow that modifies or reconstructs state.","examples":[{"scenario":"A database is restored after corruption.","expected_behavior":"Restored data is validated against integrity and consistency requirements before normal traffic resumes."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Database accepts connections, therefore recovery is complete.","why_invalid":"Connectivity does not establish data correctness."}],"problem":"Availability restored with invalid state.","failure":"Silent corruption or incorrect application behavior.","cause":"Insufficient recovery verification.","solution":"Add integrity and consistency validation to recovery acceptance.","alternatives":["Checksums","Reconciliation","Independent validation"],"tradeoffs":["Validation adds recovery time.","Deep validation may require additional resources."],"constraints":["Validation must be compatible with recovery RTO."],"edge_cases":["Some data cannot be independently reconstructed.","Corruption affects the validation source."],"security":["Integrity evidence must be protected from tampering."],"performance":["Use incremental or sampled checks where full validation is impractical, while preserving required assurance."],"testing":["Inject corruption before recovery."],"verification":["Compare recovered state against defined integrity invariants."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Data integrity","Recovery"],"prerequisites":["Recovery acceptance criteria"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B99-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Vol. 2 Rev. 1 — Developing Cyber-Resilient Systems","basis":"Cyber-resilient systems must withstand, recover from, and adapt to adverse conditions while preserving trustworthiness."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"authoritative_guidance","claim_scope":"Recovery integrity"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B93-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Security-reliability interaction","concept":"Adversarial reliability","knowledge_type":"cross_domain_principle","question":"How should reliability engineering account for intentional disruption rather than only accidental failure?","answer":"Reliability models should include deliberate exhaustion, dependency manipulation, invalid input, control-plane disruption, credential compromise, and recovery-path abuse where relevant to the threat model.","explanation":"A system can be technically resilient to random failures while remaining vulnerable to an adversary who intentionally triggers expensive or unsafe recovery behavior.","principle":"Reliability mechanisms must be evaluated against both accidental and adversarial stress.","rule":"Threat-model reliability-critical controls and test whether defensive mechanisms remain bounded under intentional abuse.","examples":[{"scenario":"Repeated invalid requests trigger expensive remediation.","expected_behavior":"Rate limits and bounded recovery prevent attacker-controlled amplification."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Automatically perform unlimited recovery attempts after every failure.","why_invalid":"An adversary could deliberately induce repeated failures."}],"problem":"Reliability control becomes an attack amplifier.","failure":"Resource exhaustion or recovery-loop instability.","cause":"Unbounded automated response.","solution":"Bound retries, remediation frequency, resource use, and authority.","alternatives":["Circuit breaking","Rate limiting","Human approval"],"tradeoffs":["Stronger limits can delay legitimate recovery."],"constraints":["Security controls must preserve essential service behavior."],"edge_cases":["Attack coincides with genuine infrastructure failure.","Telemetry itself is manipulated."],"security":["Protect control signals and recovery authority."],"performance":["Defensive limits must be computationally cheap."],"testing":["Combine fault injection with adversarial workload."],"verification":["Verify recovery remains bounded under deliberate repeated failure."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Threat modeling","Automation","Resource controls"],"prerequisites":["Reliability failure modeling"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B98-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"NIST SP 800-160 Vol. 2 Rev. 1","basis":"Cyber-resiliency addresses adverse conditions, stresses, attacks, and compromises."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"authoritative_guidance","claim_scope":"Adversarial reliability"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B94-001","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Multi-tenant reliability","concept":"Tenant failure containment","knowledge_type":"fault_isolation_principle","question":"How should multi-tenant systems prevent one tenant from destabilizing others?","answer":"Tenant-specific resource consumption, queues, concurrency, storage, and recovery actions should be bounded or isolated according to the service's reliability requirements.","explanation":"Shared infrastructure creates a common failure domain. Without tenant-aware controls, one tenant's workload can consume resources required by others.","principle":"Logical tenancy boundaries should have enforceable resource and failure boundaries.","rule":"Define and enforce per-tenant or per-class limits for resources whose exhaustion can affect other tenants.","examples":[{"scenario":"One tenant generates a large workload spike.","expected_behavior":"The spike is contained without exhausting shared capacity needed by other tenants."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Allow every tenant unrestricted access to shared worker capacity.","why_invalid":"A single tenant can create cross-tenant resource exhaustion."}],"problem":"Noisy-neighbor failure.","failure":"Cross-tenant degradation or outage.","cause":"Unbounded shared resource consumption.","solution":"Use quotas, scheduling, isolation, and tenant-aware backpressure.","alternatives":["Dedicated capacity","Priority classes"],"tradeoffs":["Isolation can reduce utilization efficiency.","Quotas can reject legitimate bursts."],"constraints":["Fairness and contractual service objectives must be respected."],"edge_cases":["Large tenant migration","Tenant-specific recovery storm","Shared dependency outage"],"security":["Tenant isolation must also prevent unauthorized data access."],"performance":["Scheduling overhead must be bounded."],"testing":["Generate asymmetric tenant workloads."],"verification":["Measure impact of one tenant's overload on unaffected tenants."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Resource management","Multi-tenancy"],"prerequisites":["Capacity and fault isolation"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B94-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Reliability Pillar — Fault Isolation","basis":"Fault isolation limits the impact of component or system failures to defined boundaries."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"authoritative_guidance","claim_scope":"Multi-tenant reliability"},"confidence":0.97,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B94-002","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Multi-tenant reliability","concept":"Tenant-aware recovery","knowledge_type":"recovery_principle","question":"Why should recovery actions consider tenant boundaries?","answer":"A global recovery action can unnecessarily disrupt healthy tenants or create simultaneous load spikes. Recovery should target the smallest sufficient failure domain and preserve unaffected tenants where possible.","explanation":"Recovery scope is itself a reliability control. Broad remediation can amplify impact even when the original fault was localized.","principle":"Recovery scope should match failure scope unless broader intervention is required and justified.","rule":"Prefer tenant-scoped recovery when evidence supports tenant-local failure.","examples":[{"scenario":"One tenant's data pipeline is corrupted.","expected_behavior":"Recover the affected tenant without unnecessarily restarting unrelated tenants."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Restart the entire shared platform for every tenant-local fault.","why_invalid":"The recovery action expands the failure domain."}],"problem":"Recovery amplifies impact.","failure":"Healthy tenants are disrupted.","cause":"Overbroad recovery scope.","solution":"Use tenant-aware diagnosis and scoped remediation.","alternatives":["Shard-level recovery","Resource-pool isolation"],"tradeoffs":["Scoped recovery requires stronger isolation and targeting."],"constraints":["Shared dependencies may require broader recovery."],"edge_cases":["Tenant fault affects shared infrastructure.","Tenant identity metadata is unavailable."],"security":["Recovery targeting must enforce tenant authorization."],"performance":["Scoped recovery reduces unnecessary workload."],"testing":["Inject tenant-local failures and verify isolation."],"verification":["Confirm unaffected tenants maintain required service behavior."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Tenant isolation","Recovery orchestration"],"prerequisites":["Multi-tenant architecture"],"relationships":[{"relation":"related_to","record_id":"D22-B94-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Reliability Pillar — Fault Isolation","basis":"Isolation reduces the scope of failure impact."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Multi-tenant recovery"},"confidence":0.96,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B94-003","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Multi-tenant reliability","concept":"Tenant fairness under overload","knowledge_type":"scheduling_principle","question":"How should reliability engineering distinguish fairness from equal resource allocation?","answer":"Fairness should be defined according to service objectives, tenant contracts, priority, and failure containment rather than assuming every tenant must receive identical resources at every moment.","explanation":"Equal allocation can be inappropriate when workloads have different criticality or contractual requirements. Reliability requires explicit allocation policy.","principle":"Resource fairness is a policy decision constrained by reliability requirements.","rule":"Define tenant scheduling and overload behavior explicitly rather than relying on incidental queue ordering.","examples":[{"scenario":"Critical and best-effort tenants share workers.","expected_behavior":"The scheduler preserves the defined service objectives during overload."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Use first-come-first-served as an implicit reliability policy.","why_invalid":"Queue order may create priority inversion and starvation."}],"problem":"Implicit scheduling policy.","failure":"Critical workloads fail while lower-value work consumes resources.","cause":"Undefined priority and fairness model.","solution":"Specify priority, fairness, quotas, and starvation controls.","alternatives":["Weighted scheduling","Reserved capacity"],"tradeoffs":["Reserved capacity can reduce aggregate utilization.","Priority scheduling can delay lower-priority workloads."],"constraints":["Business and contractual requirements must be represented."],"edge_cases":["All priority classes become overloaded.","Priority metadata is missing."],"security":["Tenant priority must not be user-modifiable without authorization."],"performance":["Scheduling must remain efficient at high concurrency."],"testing":["Test starvation, priority inversion, and overload."],"verification":["Measure behavior against explicit fairness and service objectives."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Scheduling","Capacity management"],"prerequisites":["Overload control"],"relationships":[{"relation":"extends","record_id":"D22-B94-001"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Google SRE — Handling Operational Overload","basis":"Operational overload requires deliberate load and resource management."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"authoritative_guidance","claim_scope":"Overload and tenant scheduling"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B94-004","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Multi-tenant reliability","concept":"Tenant-specific failure evidence","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_principle","question":"Why should reliability telemetry preserve tenant or workload scope where isolation requires it?","answer":"Aggregated metrics can hide localized tenant failures and make blast radius difficult to determine. Scope-aware telemetry allows operators and automation to distinguish local degradation from platform-wide failure.","explanation":"Reliability diagnosis depends on knowing which failure domain is affected. Tenant identity should be represented in telemetry only to the level needed for reliability and governance.","principle":"Failure evidence should preserve the scope necessary for correct diagnosis.","rule":"Tag critical reliability signals with bounded, authorized scope identifiers when tenant isolation is part of the reliability model.","examples":[{"scenario":"Only one tenant experiences elevated errors.","expected_behavior":"Telemetry identifies tenant-local impact without exposing unrelated tenant data."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Only report global error rate.","why_invalid":"Localized failure can disappear inside the aggregate."}],"problem":"Insufficient failure localization.","failure":"Overbroad or ineffective remediation.","cause":"Loss of tenant scope in telemetry.","solution":"Use privacy-aware tenant-scoped metrics and traces.","alternatives":["Aggregated tenant classes","Per-tenant dashboards"],"tradeoffs":["Higher cardinality increases telemetry cost.","Tenant labels can create privacy concerns."],"constraints":["Tenant identifiers must be protected."],"edge_cases":["Anonymous workloads","Tenant migration","Cross-tenant shared requests"],"security":["Avoid exposing tenant identity across authorization boundaries."],"performance":["Bound metric cardinality."],"testing":["Inject tenant-local failures and validate detection."],"verification":["Confirm failure scope can be identified without cross-tenant disclosure."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Observability","Tenant isolation"],"prerequisites":["Multi-tenant failure model"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B94-005"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Reliability Pillar","basis":"Monitoring supports failure detection and analysis across workload components."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Tenant-scoped observability"},"confidence":0.94,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D22-B94-005","dataset_id":"D22","topic":"Reliability Engineering","subtopic":"Multi-tenant reliability","concept":"Cross-tenant recovery verification","knowledge_type":"verification_principle","question":"What must be verified after a tenant-scoped recovery?","answer":"Verification must establish that the target tenant recovered, unrelated tenants remained within acceptable service behavior, and the recovery did not create shared-resource instability.","explanation":"A localized recovery can still produce secondary effects through shared databases, caches, queues, workers, or control systems.","principle":"Scoped recovery must be verified both locally and at the shared-resource boundary.","rule":"Verify target recovery and non-target impact after tenant-scoped remediation.","examples":[{"scenario":"One tenant's worker pool is rebuilt.","expected_behavior":"Target functionality is restored and shared resource health remains within defined limits."}],"invalid_examples":[{"pattern":"Verify only the repaired tenant.","why_invalid":"Secondary impact on other tenants may remain undetected."}],"problem":"Recovery causes hidden secondary impact.","failure":"Cross-tenant degradation after apparent recovery.","cause":"Shared dependency effects.","solution":"Use target and non-target verification criteria.","alternatives":["Canary recovery","Dedicated recovery capacity"],"tradeoffs":["Additional verification increases recovery duration.","Dedicated capacity costs more."],"constraints":["Verification must not violate tenant isolation."],"edge_cases":["Shared cache invalidation","Shared connection pool exhaustion"],"security":["Cross-tenant verification must use authorized aggregate signals."],"performance":["Verification workload must be bounded."],"testing":["Inject scoped failures with shared dependencies."],"verification":["Check both affected and unaffected service objectives."],"version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Tenant recovery","Shared-resource monitoring"],"prerequisites":["Tenant isolation"],"relationships":[{"relation":"supports","record_id":"D22-B99-002"}],"provenance":[{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"AWS Well-Architected Reliability Pillar — Fault Isolation","basis":"Isolation and bounded impact are core reliability objectives."}],"evidence":{"evidence_level":"engineering_guidance","claim_scope":"Tenant recovery"},"confidence":0.95,"validation":{"schema_status":"REVIEWED","semantic_status":"REVIEWED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","dedup_status":"REVIEWED"},"created_at":"2026-09-19","updated_at":"2026-09-19"}
