{"record_id":"D24-B75-001","dataset_id":"D24","topic":"Refactoring","subtopic":"Documentation Refactoring","concept":"Documentation-as-contract preservation","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"Why must documentation be considered during refactoring?","answer":"Documentation can describe externally observable behavior, configuration, usage constraints, supported interfaces, operational procedures, and architectural assumptions. Refactoring can make documentation false even when code remains functionally valid.","explanation":"Documentation is often consumed by users, operators, developers, and automation. Moving or renaming code can invalidate examples, module paths, configuration names, CLI usage, API descriptions, or operational procedures.","principle":"Refactoring is incomplete when contract-relevant documentation becomes inaccurate.","rule":"Identify documentation coupled to changed interfaces and update it whenever the documented contract changes.","examples":["Updating a documented import path after a public module relocation.","Updating a configuration example after a key migration."],"invalid_examples":["Leaving obsolete API examples because the underlying implementation still works internally."],"problem":"Users and maintainers follow stale instructions after a refactoring.","failure":"Documentation-driven integrations or operational procedures fail.","cause":"Documentation was excluded from the refactoring impact surface.","solution":"Treat contract documentation as a refactoring dependency and verify examples against current interfaces.","alternatives":["Generated API documentation.","Executable documentation tests."],"tradeoffs":"Generated documentation reduces drift but may not cover conceptual or operational guidance.","constraints":["Documentation may exist outside the repository.","Examples can depend on environment-specific behavior."],"edge_cases":["Versioned documentation.","Archived migration guides.","Documentation describing deprecated behavior intentionally."],"security":"Review documentation for accidental exposure of internal secrets, sensitive endpoints, or unsafe operational assumptions.","performance":"Documentation generation should not unnecessarily slow routine development workflows.","testing":"Validate executable examples or documentation-linked tests where feasible.","verification":"Search for changed identifiers, paths, configuration keys, and commands across documentation.","version":"Documentation must correspond to the applicable software version.","dependencies":["Documentation sources","Public interfaces"],"prerequisites":["Impact analysis of changed contracts"],"relationships":[],"provenance":["General documentation and API maintenance principles"],"evidence":["Documentation is part of the interface through which users understand and operate software."],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D24-B75-002","dataset_id":"D24","topic":"Refactoring","subtopic":"Documentation Refactoring","concept":"Executable-example preservation","knowledge_type":"verification_pattern","question":"How can refactoring reduce documentation drift when examples are executable?","answer":"Examples can be validated through automated checks that compile, execute, parse, or otherwise validate the documented usage against the current implementation.","explanation":"Executable examples create a stronger connection between documentation and code. During refactoring, these checks can detect renamed APIs, changed signatures, invalid configuration, and obsolete invocation patterns.","principle":"Where practical, validate important documentation examples through the same interfaces users are expected to use.","rule":"Prioritize executable validation for documentation that serves as an integration or operational contract.","examples":["Compiling a documented API usage snippet.","Running a sanitized CLI example against a test environment."],"invalid_examples":["Testing only that the documentation file exists while its commands reference removed interfaces."],"problem":"Documentation remains syntactically plausible but operationally obsolete.","failure":"Users encounter errors following official examples.","cause":"Documentation and implementation evolved independently.","solution":"Automate validation for high-value examples.","alternatives":["Documentation linting.","API schema validation.","Example extraction into tests."],"tradeoffs":"Executable documentation adds maintenance and CI cost but reduces interface drift.","constraints":["Examples may require environment setup.","Some examples intentionally describe hypothetical or conceptual behavior."],"edge_cases":["Version-specific examples.","Examples requiring secrets or external services.","Examples that are destructive in production."],"security":"Use isolated and sanitized environments for executable examples; never embed production credentials.","performance":"Keep documentation checks bounded and separate expensive integration examples where appropriate.","testing":"Test representative executable examples across supported versions where required.","verification":"Record which examples are validated automatically and which remain informational.","version":"Version-specific examples must declare applicable versions.","dependencies":["Documentation tooling","Current interfaces"],"prerequisites":["Identifiable executable examples"],"relationships":["D24-B75-001"],"provenance":["General executable-documentation engineering"],"evidence":["Executable examples can expose interface drift earlier than manual documentation review."],"confidence":0.95,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D24-B75-003","dataset_id":"D24","topic":"Refactoring","subtopic":"Documentation Refactoring","concept":"Migration-guide boundary","knowledge_type":"design_pattern","question":"When should a refactoring include a migration guide rather than only updated documentation?","answer":"A migration guide is appropriate when existing users must change code, configuration, deployment procedures, data formats, or operational practices to adopt the refactored interface.","explanation":"Normal documentation describes the current state; migration guidance explains the transition from a previous state. Refactorings that preserve compatibility may require only documentation updates, while compatibility-breaking changes need explicit transition instructions.","principle":"Document transitions separately from steady-state usage when consumers must migrate.","rule":"Provide migration guidance when adoption requires consumer action or when old and new behavior coexist during a transition.","examples":["Explaining how to move from an old configuration key to its replacement.","Providing a sequence for migrating from an old API namespace to a new one."],"invalid_examples":["Publishing only the new API reference when existing consumers must change imports and configuration."],"problem":"Consumers know the destination but not the safe migration path.","failure":"Partial migrations, incompatible deployments, or inconsistent consumer states.","cause":"Transition behavior was documented as if it were merely current-state documentation.","solution":"Document prerequisites, sequence, compatibility window, validation, and rollback considerations.","alternatives":["Automated migration tool.","Compatibility layer with staged deprecation."],"tradeoffs":"Migration documentation adds maintenance cost but reduces adoption ambiguity.","constraints":["Consumer environments may be independently versioned."],"edge_cases":["Mixed-version deployments.","Rollback after partial migration.","Multiple independent consumers."],"security":"Include security-sensitive migration prerequisites and avoid publishing confidential deployment details.","performance":"Migration steps should identify any temporary performance implications when relevant.","testing":"Validate migration steps in representative supported configurations.","verification":"Ensure each migration step has an observable success criterion.","version":"Explicitly identify source and target versions.","dependencies":["Versioning policy","Consumer contract"],"prerequisites":["Known breaking or transitional change"],"relationships":["D24-B75-001"],"provenance":["General API and migration documentation practices"],"evidence":["Migration documentation addresses transition state rather than only final-state usage."],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

