{"record_id":"D24-B76-001","dataset_id":"D24","topic":"Refactoring","subtopic":"Dependency Injection Refactoring","concept":"Dependency injection boundary preservation","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"What should remain stable when refactoring dependency injection?","answer":"The dependency contract, ownership semantics, lifecycle expectations, and substitution boundaries should remain stable unless the refactoring intentionally changes them.","explanation":"Changing constructors, providers, factories, or service registration can alter object lifetime, dependency scope, initialization order, and test substitution behavior even when method signatures remain unchanged.","principle":"Dependency injection is both a construction mechanism and a lifecycle contract.","rule":"Model dependency ownership and lifetime before changing injection structure.","examples":["Replacing a provider implementation while preserving singleton or request-scoped lifetime semantics."],"invalid_examples":["Changing a dependency from request-scoped to global solely to simplify registration."],"problem":"A DI refactoring changes hidden lifecycle or ownership behavior.","failure":"State leakage, resource exhaustion, initialization failures, or cross-request contamination.","cause":"Injection configuration was treated as wiring only.","solution":"Preserve or explicitly redesign dependency lifecycle semantics.","alternatives":["Constructor injection.","Factory injection.","Provider-based injection."],"tradeoffs":"Explicit constructor dependencies improve visibility but may increase constructor size; provider patterns improve lifecycle control but can hide dependencies.","constraints":["Framework-specific scope semantics.","Circular dependency constraints."],"edge_cases":["Lazy providers.","Async resource initialization.","Per-tenant or per-request dependencies."],"security":"Preserve isolation boundaries and ensure security-sensitive dependencies are not accidentally shared across tenants or requests.","performance":"Avoid creating expensive dependencies at broader scopes than necessary.","testing":"Test dependency creation, scope, substitution, and cleanup behavior.","verification":"Verify lifecycle and ownership semantics independently from functional outputs.","version":"Framework-specific","dependencies":["DI framework or composition root"],"prerequisites":["Dependency graph and lifecycle knowledge"],"relationships":[],"provenance":["General dependency-injection architecture principles"],"evidence":["Dependency scope affects runtime behavior beyond type compatibility."],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D24-B76-002","dataset_id":"D24","topic":"Refactoring","subtopic":"Dependency Injection Refactoring","concept":"Composition-root consolidation","knowledge_type":"design_pattern","question":"Why can consolidating dependency construction at a composition root improve refactoring safety?","answer":"A composition root centralizes object construction and dependency wiring, making dependency changes easier to inspect and reducing hidden construction paths.","explanation":"Scattered construction logic makes impact analysis difficult because dependencies may be instantiated inside business components. Centralized composition allows refactoring to change implementations or wiring without modifying unrelated business logic.","principle":"Keep dependency construction close to application composition boundaries when architecture permits.","rule":"Prefer explicit composition over hidden construction when refactoring dependency relationships.","examples":["Constructing repositories and services in an application startup composition layer."],"invalid_examples":["Business classes silently constructing concrete infrastructure dependencies internally."],"problem":"A dependency implementation change requires widespread code modifications.","failure":"Hidden construction prevents substitution and complicates lifecycle reasoning.","cause":"Construction responsibilities were distributed across consumers.","solution":"Move construction toward a stable composition boundary while preserving behavior.","alternatives":["Factory modules.","Dependency containers.","Manual composition."],"tradeoffs":"Centralization improves visibility but can create a large composition module if not organized by subsystem.","constraints":["Legacy systems may contain unavoidable hidden construction.","Framework lifecycle may determine construction points."],"edge_cases":["Plugin discovery.","Runtime-selected implementations.","Dynamic tenant-specific dependencies."],"security":"Ensure centralized wiring does not accidentally bypass authorization or trust-boundary checks.","performance":"Avoid eager construction of expensive dependencies that were previously lazy.","testing":"Verify production composition and test composition independently.","verification":"Inspect the final dependency graph for hidden concrete construction paths.","version":"Framework-specific","dependencies":["Application composition layer"],"prerequisites":["Dependency graph inventory"],"relationships":["D24-B76-001"],"provenance":["General dependency-injection and composition-root pattern"],"evidence":["Centralized construction makes dependency relationships explicit."],"confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D24-B76-003","dataset_id":"D24","topic":"Refactoring","subtopic":"Dependency Injection Refactoring","concept":"Lifecycle-preserving provider replacement","knowledge_type":"edge_case","question":"What can go wrong when replacing one dependency provider with another?","answer":"The replacement can preserve the type-level interface while changing initialization timing, cleanup behavior, caching, thread safety, or scope, producing runtime differences that ordinary unit tests may miss.","explanation":"Providers often encode lifecycle behavior beyond object construction. A refactoring that swaps providers must compare those semantics rather than only method signatures.","principle":"Provider compatibility includes lifecycle and concurrency semantics, not only interface compatibility.","rule":"Compare initialization, reuse, disposal, synchronization, and failure behavior before replacing providers.","examples":["Replacing a factory while preserving per-request creation and cleanup semantics."],"invalid_examples":["Replacing a scoped resource provider with a process-wide cached object because both expose the same methods."],"problem":"A dependency replacement introduces lifecycle bugs despite interface compatibility.","failure":"Resource leaks, stale state, cross-request contamination, or concurrency errors.","cause":"Provider semantics were not included in compatibility analysis.","solution":"Define and verify lifecycle equivalence or explicitly redesign the lifecycle.","alternatives":["Adapter preserving old lifecycle.","Explicit scope wrapper."],"tradeoffs":"Lifecycle adapters preserve compatibility but can add complexity and overhead.","constraints":["Provider framework semantics","Resource ownership rules"],"edge_cases":["Database connections.","File handles.","Async clients.","Thread-local state."],"security":"Avoid broadening resource sharing across users or trust boundaries.","performance":"Resource reuse can improve performance but must not violate isolation or cleanup requirements.","testing":"Test creation, reuse, disposal, failure, and concurrent access where applicable.","verification":"Compare lifecycle traces or resource ownership behavior, not just functional outputs.","version":"Provider-framework-specific","dependencies":["Dependency provider","Resource lifecycle"],"prerequisites":["Ownership and scope model"],"relationships":["D24-B76-001"],"provenance":["General dependency lifecycle engineering"],"evidence":["Provider implementations can encode lifecycle behavior beyond interfaces."],"confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D24-B76-004","dataset_id":"D24","topic":"Refactoring","subtopic":"Dependency Injection Refactoring","concept":"Circular dependency elimination through boundary extraction","knowledge_type":"implementation_guidance","question":"How can refactoring remove a circular dependency in a dependency-injection graph?","answer":"Identify the shared responsibility causing the cycle, extract a narrower abstraction or coordination component, and orient dependencies around a stable ownership boundary rather than introducing arbitrary lazy resolution.","explanation":"Lazy resolution can hide a cycle without correcting architectural coupling. A structural refactoring should clarify which component owns the shared behavior and reduce bidirectional knowledge.","principle":"Resolve dependency cycles by improving responsibility boundaries rather than merely deferring construction.","rule":"Trace the semantic reason for the cycle before selecting extraction, inversion, eventing, or coordination strategies.","examples":["Extracting a policy component used by two services instead of making the services depend directly on each other."],"invalid_examples":["Adding a service locator solely to avoid a constructor cycle while retaining mutual conceptual dependency."],"problem":"Two components require each other directly or indirectly.","failure":"Construction cycles, hidden runtime dependencies, or tightly coupled changes.","cause":"Responsibilities cross boundaries in both directions.","solution":"Extract shared policy or introduce a stable coordinator/abstraction with clear ownership.","alternatives":["Dependency inversion.","Coordinator component.","Domain event where semantically appropriate."],"tradeoffs":"Boundary extraction can create a new abstraction that requires careful ownership; eventing introduces temporal behavior.","constraints":["Do not extract merely coincidental code.","Preserve transactional and ordering semantics where required."],"edge_cases":["Cycles involving initialization.","Cycles spanning plugins or dynamic registration."],"security":"Ensure boundary extraction does not bypass authorization checks formerly enforced by one component.","performance":"Avoid introducing unnecessary indirection on hot paths.","testing":"Test the new dependency graph and behavior across both former directions of interaction.","verification":"Confirm the semantic cycle is removed rather than merely hidden by a resolver.","version":"Architecture-specific","dependencies":["Dependency graph","Responsibility model"],"prerequisites":["Cycle identification"],"relationships":["D24-B76-001","D24-B76-002"],"provenance":["General dependency-graph and architecture refactoring principles"],"evidence":["Structural dependency cycles often reflect responsibility coupling."],"confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D24-B77-001","dataset_id":"D24","topic":"Refactoring","subtopic":"Resource-Ownership Refactoring","concept":"Ownership contract preservation","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"Why must resource ownership be explicit during refactoring?","answer":"Files, sockets, database connections, memory buffers, locks, subprocesses, and similar resources require clear ownership to determine who creates, uses, transfers, and releases them.","explanation":"Moving resource creation or consumption can silently change who is responsible for cleanup. This can cause leaks, premature closure, double release, or use-after-close behavior.","principle":"Every resource should have an unambiguous ownership and lifetime contract.","rule":"Before moving resource creation or cleanup, identify the owning scope and transfer points.","examples":["Moving file creation into a helper while keeping ownership with the caller through an explicit resource-return contract."],"invalid_examples":["Returning a resource from a helper that also closes it before the caller finishes using it."],"problem":"A refactoring changes resource lifetime unintentionally.","failure":"Leaks, premature cleanup, double cleanup, or invalid resource access.","cause":"Ownership was implicit in the old call structure.","solution":"Define ownership explicitly and preserve transfer semantics.","alternatives":["Scoped resource manager.","Context-managed ownership.","RAII-style wrapper where supported."],"tradeoffs":"Explicit ownership improves safety but may require broader interface changes.","constraints":["Language resource-management semantics.","External resource behavior."],"edge_cases":["Exceptions during partial initialization.","Ownership transfer after asynchronous handoff.","Shared read-only resources."],"security":"Incorrect cleanup can expose sensitive data in temporary resources or leave privileged resources active.","performance":"Premature cleanup can increase setup cost; leaks can exhaust resources.","testing":"Test normal, exceptional, partial-initialization, and repeated-use paths.","verification":"Trace creation, transfer, use, and release for each affected resource.","version":"Language/framework-specific","dependencies":["Resource lifecycle model"],"prerequisites":["Resource inventory"],"relationships":[],"provenance":["General resource ownership and lifetime principles"],"evidence":["Resource lifetime errors commonly arise when structural ownership boundaries change."],"confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

