time rather than runtime.","testing":"Perform artifact integrity and provenance checks in CI/CD.","verification":"Match deployed artifact identity to an approved build record.","version":"General","dependencies":["Artifact hashing","Build provenance"],"prerequisites":["Supply-chain fundamentals"],"relationships":["D25-B54-R002"],"provenance":["SLSA","Sigstore"],"evidence":["https://slsa.dev/","https://www.sigstore.dev/"],"confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B54-R004","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Malicious Build Behavior","concept":"Build-script behavioral analysis","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should suspicious build behavior be debugged without confusing legitimate build activity with malicious behavior?","answer":"Record the build process's inputs, invoked tools, network access, filesystem modifications, environment variables, generated outputs, and provenance, then compare them with the declared build contract.","explanation":"Build systems legitimately perform complex operations, so evidence must be tied to declared requirements and reproducible expectations.","principle":"Unexpected build behavior becomes meaningful when it violates a documented build contract or artifact expectation.","rule":"Distinguish observed behavior from attribution and investigate unexplained capabilities or outputs.","examples":["A build unexpectedly downloads an undeclared executable.","A script modifies files outside the expected build workspace."],"invalid_examples":["Treating any network access as malicious without context.","Inferring attacker intent from a suspicious filename alone."],"problem":"Build behavior exceeds its expected trust boundary.","failure":"Unreviewed code or data can influence produced artifacts.","cause":"Overprivileged build scripts, compromised dependencies, or undocumented build steps.","solution":"Constrain build capabilities and establish auditable build contracts.","alternatives":["Sandboxed builds","Network-isolated builds","Allowlisted tools"],"tradeoffs":"Isolation can complicate builds requiring external services.","constraints":["Build reproducibility and required external dependencies must be preserved."],"edge_cases":["Compiler downloads","Package registries","Private artifact stores"],"security":"Minimize build-time privileges and preserve build telemetry.","performance":"Isolation may increase build setup overhead.","testing":"Run controlled builds and compare observed operations with expected manifests.","verification":"Confirm that every security-sensitive build capability is authorized and attributable.","version":"General","dependencies":["Build environment","Dependency tooling"],"prerequisites":["Build-system analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B54-R002","D25-B54-R003"],"provenance":["SLSA","NIST SSDF"],"evidence":["https://slsa.dev/spec/v1.0/","https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/ssdf"],"confidence":0.95,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B54-R005","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Supply-Chain Fix Verification","concept":"Dependency and artifact security regression verification","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"What must be verified after fixing a dependency or build-chain security defect?","answer":"Verify the resolved dependency graph, produced artifact identity, build provenance, security configuration, and deployment artifact independently rather than assuming the source-level change propagated correctly.","explanation":"Supply-chain fixes can fail through resolution drift, stale artifacts, packaging errors, or deployment mismatch.","principle":"A supply-chain repair is complete only when the corrected dependency and artifact state is demonstrably the deployed state.","rule":"Verify the entire source-to-artifact-to-deployment chain.","examples":["Checking the lockfile, build output hash, provenance attestation, and deployed digest.","Confirming a vulnerable transitive package is absent from the final artifact."],"invalid_examples":["Updating a manifest without verifying resolution.","Assuming CI success proves production deployment contains the fix."],"problem":"A security repair does not propagate through the artifact chain.","failure":"The vulnerable component remains deployed.","cause":"Lockfile drift, cache reuse, stale artifacts, or deployment mismatch.","solution":"Add artifact-level and dependency-resolution verification gates.","alternatives":["SBOM comparison","Artifact attestations","Deployment digest verification"],"tradeoffs":"Additional gates increase pipeline complexity.","constraints":["The deployment platform must expose verifiable artifact identity."],"edge_cases":["Multi-platform builds","Container layers","Cached dependencies"],"security":"Verify final deployed component identity rather than source intent alone.","performance":"Artifact scanning should be optimized through caching without weakening identity guarantees.","testing":"Test both vulnerable and corrected dependency states.","verification":"Record exact dependency and artifact identities associated with the fix.","version":"General","dependencies":["Dependency lock state","Artifact identity","Deployment system"],"prerequisites":["Supply-chain provenance"],"relationships":["D25-B54-R001","D25-B54-R003"],"provenance":["SLSA","NIST SSDF"],"evidence":["https://slsa.dev/","https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/ssdf"],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B55-R001","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Secret Exposure","concept":"Secret leakage root-cause diagnosis","knowledge_type":"root_cause","question":"How should a secret exposure incident be debugged to find the actual leakage path?","answer":"Trace the secret from its origin through memory, configuration, logs, exceptions, telemetry, files, process arguments, artifacts, and external services to identify the first unauthorized persistence or disclosure point.","explanation":"Secret leakage is often a multi-stage data lifecycle problem rather than a single logging statement.","principle":"Sensitive values require explicit lifecycle boundaries from acquisition through destruction.","rule":"Track where the secret can exist, who can access each representation, and how long each representation persists.","examples":["Credentials copied into debug logs.","Secrets embedded in build artifacts or exception payloads."],"invalid_examples":["Rotating the credential without identifying all exposed representations.","Searching only application logs."],"problem":"A sensitive value is persisted or transmitted outside its intended trust boundary.","failure":"Unauthorized parties can obtain or retain the secret.","cause":"Logging, telemetry, debugging, configuration, crash dumps, artifacts, or unsafe error handling.","solution":"Remove the exposure path, rotate affected credentials, and verify all known representations are controlled.","alternatives":["Secret references","Redacted telemetry","Dedicated secret stores"],"tradeoffs":"Aggressive redaction can reduce diagnostic usefulness.","constraints":["Incident response must preserve sufficient evidence without retaining secrets unnecessarily."],"edge_cases":["Crash dumps","Core dumps","Metrics labels","Tracing baggage","Shell history"],"security":"Treat secrets as high-sensitivity data throughout the debugging lifecycle.","performance":"Secret scanning can be integrated into CI and artifact pipelines.","testing":"Use automated secret detection and explicit redaction tests.","verification":"Verify that known secret values do not appear in protected output channels.","version":"General","dependencies":["Logging","Telemetry","Configuration"],"prerequisites":["Data classification","Secret lifecycle"],"relationships":[],"provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","CWE-532"],"evidence":["https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secrets_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html","https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/532.html"],"confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B55-R002","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Logging Leakage","concept":"Sensitive-data logging diagnosis","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How can sensitive-data logging be localized when the visible log entry is produced by a generic logging framework?","answer":"Trace the sensitive value backward from the emitted record through structured fields, middleware, exception serialization, request context, correlation metadata, and logging hooks until the first unauthorized propagation point is found.","explanation":"Generic logging infrastructure can unintentionally serialize sensitive context that application code never explicitly formats.","principle":"Logging boundaries must be treated as data-flow boundaries, not merely output statements.","rule":"Audit structured context propagation and automatic exception/request serialization.","examples":["Request headers copied into structured log context.","Exception objects containing secrets serialized automatically."],"invalid_examples":["Searching only for literal logging calls containing the secret variable.","Assuming structured logging automatically removes sensitive fields."],"problem":"Sensitive context reaches logging infrastructure indirectly.","failure":"Secrets or confidential data persist in logs.","cause":"Automatic context propagation, serialization, or insufficient field classification.","solution":"Define explicit logging schemas and redaction boundaries.","alternatives":["Allowlisted fields","Central redaction","Typed telemetry models"],"tradeoffs":"Allowlisting fields may omit useful diagnostic context.","constraints":["Redaction must occur before persistence where possible."],"edge_cases":["Nested objects","Exception chains","Correlation metadata","Third-party middleware"],"security":"Default to data minimization and explicit inclusion for sensitive contexts.","performance":"Centralized redaction can add serialization overhead.","testing":"Inject synthetic sensitive markers and verify they cannot reach logs.","verification":"Inspect emitted structured records rather than only source-level logging calls.","version":"General","dependencies":["Logging framework","Telemetry pipeline"],"prerequisites":["Dataflow analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B55-R001"],"provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet","CWE-532"],"evidence":["https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html","https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/532.html"],"confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B55-R003","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Error and Exception Disclosure","concept":"Diagnostic error-channel leakage","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"Why can verbose debugging errors create a security defect even when they do not directly expose credentials?","answer":"They can reveal internal paths, identifiers, configuration, implementation details, or security-sensitive state that materially assists unauthorized inference or exploitation.","explanation":"Error channels are part of the observable security interface and should expose only information appropriate to the caller and environment.","principle":"Diagnostic detail should be proportional to the trust level of the recipient.","rule":"Separate internal diagnostic context from externally exposed error responses.","examples":["Returning filesystem paths and internal service names to untrusted clients.","Exposing stack traces from production endpoints."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming non-secret internal information has no security relevance.","Using the same verbose error object for internal logs and external responses."],"problem":"Internal diagnostic information crosses a trust boundary.","failure":"Attackers gain implementation or environment information.","cause":"Shared exception serialization, debug mode, or insufficient response filtering.","solution":"Create separate internal and external error representations.","alternatives":["Error identifiers","Structured internal diagnostics","Environment-specific verbosity"],"tradeoffs":"Less external detail can make client troubleshooting harder.","constraints":["Internal logs must still avoid sensitive-data leakage."],"edge_cases":["GraphQL errors","API gateways","Reverse proxies","Nested exceptions"],"security":"Do not expose stack traces or internal diagnostics to untrusted callers by default.","performance":"Structured error handling has negligible typical runtime impact.","testing":"Assert production error schemas and absence of internal fields.","verification":"Compare external responses against an explicit disclosure contract.","version":"General","dependencies":["Exception handling","API response layer"],"prerequisites":["Trust boundaries"],"relationships":["D25-B55-R001","D25-B55-R002"],"provenance":["OWASP Error Handling Cheat Sheet","CWE-209"],"evidence":["https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Error_Handling_Cheat_Sheet.html","https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"],"confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B55-R004","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Secret-Bearing Artifacts","concept":"Artifact secret detection","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should a secret accidentally embedded in a package or binary be investigated?","answer":"Identify the artifact containing the secret, map the build transformation that introduced it, determine whether the value remains recoverable from derived artifacts, and identify every distribution boundary affected.","explanation":"Deleting the source occurrence does not remove the secret from historical or derived artifacts.","principle":"Secret remediation must cover every persisted representation and distribution copy.","rule":"Treat artifact contents and artifact history as separate exposure surfaces.","examples":["A credential embedded in a frontend bundle.","A secret included in a container layer or release archive."],"invalid_examples":["Removing the source value while retaining the old release artifact.","Assuming an environment variable is safe if build tooling materializes it into an artifact."],"problem":"Sensitive data becomes part of a distributable artifact.","failure":"Unauthorized recipients can extract or retain the value.","cause":"Build-time substitution, packaging, generated configuration, or accidental inclusion.","solution":"Remove the secret from source and artifacts, rotate it, and establish artifact scanning and provenance controls.","alternatives":["Runtime secret injection","Secret references","Build-time secret isolation"],"tradeoffs":"Runtime injection may complicate deployment and local development.","constraints":["Already distributed secrets must be treated as exposed."],"edge_cases":["Layer caches","Source maps","Debug symbols","Compressed assets"],"security":"Rotate exposed credentials rather than relying solely on artifact deletion.","performance":"Artifact scanning can be performed asynchronously in CI while blocking release promotion on confirmed violations.","testing":"Scan source, generated files, packages, containers, and release artifacts.","verification":"Verify both current and newly produced artifacts contain no prohibited secret patterns.","version":"General","dependencies":["Build system","Artifact repository"],"prerequisites":["Secret management","Artifact analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B54-R002","D25-B55-R001"],"provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","GitHub Secret Scanning documentation"],"evidence":["https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secrets_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html","https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/secret-scanning/introduction/about-secret-scanning"],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B55-R005","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Secret Remediation Verification","concept":"Credential exposure closure","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"When is a secret-exposure debugging case actually closed?","answer":"Closure requires evidence that the exposure path is removed, affected credentials are rotated or revoked when necessary, historical and current artifacts are addressed, and regression controls prevent recurrence.","explanation":"Removing one occurrence does not necessarily invalidate previously leaked credentials or copies.","principle":"Secret remediation is both a code correction and an exposure-lifecycle closure process.","rule":"Separate code remediation, credential remediation, artifact remediation, and regression verification.","examples":["Revoking a leaked token after removing its logging path.","Purging or access-restricting exposed artifacts according to the applicable retention policy."],"invalid_examples":["Closing the issue after deleting the source line.","Assuming a new credential is safe without testing that the old credential is invalid."],"problem":"Exposure remains possible after the apparent code fix.","failure":"Old or newly generated secrets remain accessible.","cause":"Incomplete lifecycle remediation.","solution":"Track each exposure surface and verify closure independently.","alternatives":["Automated secret scanning","Credential rotation automation","Artifact policy gates"],"tradeoffs":"Aggressive rotation can temporarily disrupt dependent systems.","constraints":["Incident response requirements may dictate retention of evidence."],"edge_cases":["Long-lived tokens","Cached artifacts","Third-party logs","Backups"],"security":"Treat any potentially exposed credential according to its actual authorization lifetime and scope.","performance":"Automated scanning and rotation can reduce manual closure time.","testing":"Add synthetic secret canaries and artifact scans where appropriate.","verification":"Record evidence for exposure removal, credential invalidation, and recurrence prevention.","version":"General","dependencies":["Secret management","Artifact controls","Logging"],"prerequisites":["Incident lifecycle"],"relationships":["D25-B55-R001","D25-B55-R004"],"provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","NIST SSDF"],"evidence":["https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secrets_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html","https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/ssdf"],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B56-R001","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Authentication State","concept":"Authentication-state root cause","knowledge_type":"root_cause","question":"How should an authentication-state bug be debugged when login itself appears successful?","answer":"Trace identity establishment, credential validation, session/token creation, refresh, revocation, and downstream identity propagation to find where authenticated state diverges from the intended identity.","explanation":"Successful credential verification does not prove that subsequent requests carry or enforce the correct identity state.","principle":"Authentication is a lifecycle, not a single successful login event.","rule":"Trace identity state across every transition and storage boundary.","examples":["A refreshed token retaining an obsolete identity claim.","A session associated with the wrong account after concurrent login."],"invalid_examples":["Debugging only the login endpoint.","Assuming a valid token necessarily represents the correct current identity."],"problem":"Authenticated state becomes inconsistent with the verified identity.","failure":"Users may receive incorrect identity context or access.","cause":"Session lifecycle errors, token handling, cache confusion, or identity propagation defects.","solution":"Model authentication state transitions explicitly and verify identity continuity.","alternatives":["Short-lived tokens","Server-side sessions","Centralized identity validation"],"tradeoffs":"More frequent validation can increase latency or infrastructure load.","constraints":["Distributed authentication requires consistent revocation and identity semantics."],"edge_cases":["Refresh-token rotation","Concurrent sessions","Clock skew","Logout races"],"security":"Authentication state must be bound to the correct identity and lifecycle state.","performance":"Cache identity metadata only with explicit invalidation semantics.","testing":"Test login, refresh, logout, revocation, expiration, and concurrent sessions.","verification":"Verify that every protected request maps to the intended authenticated identity.","version":"General","dependencies":["Identity provider","Session/token mechanism"],"prerequisites":["Authentication concepts"],"relationships":[],"provenance":["OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-63B"],"evidence":["https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html","https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html"],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B56-R002","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Authorization State","concept":"Authorization decision divergence","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How can an authorization failure be localized when authentication is correct?","answer":"Capture the subject, resource, action, policy version, contextual attributes, and final decision at each authorization boundary and compare them with the intended policy inputs.","explanation":"Authorization bugs often result from different services evaluating different subjects, resources, actions, or policy versions.","principle":"Authorization is a decision over explicit security context; missing or inconsistent context is a primary debugging signal.","rule":"Log or trace decision metadata without exposing sensitive credentials or confidential data unnecessarily.","examples":["One service checks project membership while another checks organization membership.","A cached authorization decision survives a role change."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming a 403 always means the policy is wrong.","Logging complete credentials or tokens to diagnose authorization."],"problem":"Authorization components receive inconsistent security context.","failure":"Access is incorrectly allowed or denied.","cause":"Policy mismatch, stale cache, missing attributes, or inconsistent resource identity.","solution":"Normalize authorization inputs and make policy/version context observable.","alternatives":["Centralized policy enforcement","Policy decision points","Explicit capability tokens"],"tradeoffs":"Centralization can introduce dependency and availability concerns.","constraints":["Authorization data may be sensitive."],"edge_cases":["Role changes","Tenant boundaries","Delegation","Impersonation"],"security":"Never use diagnostic logging that exposes authentication secrets merely to debug authorization.","performance":"Policy evaluation and context propagation should be measured under realistic load.","testing":"Test allow, deny, stale-role, cross-tenant, and resource-identity cases.","verification":"Reconstruct the exact authorization decision from recorded non-secret evidence.","version":"General","dependencies":["Authorization policy","Identity context"],"prerequisites":["Authentication","Access-control models"],"relationships":["D25-B56-R001"],"provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-53"],"evidence":["https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html","https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-53/rev-5/final"],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B56-R003","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Session Fixation and Binding","concept":"Session-binding diagnosis","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"What evidence is needed to diagnose a session-binding defect?","answer":"Demonstrate that a session identifier or security context remains valid across an identity transition where it should have been regenerated, rebound, or invalidated.","explanation":"The root cause lies in lifecycle binding between session state and authenticated identity.","principle":"Security state must be rebound when the security principal changes in a way that invalidates prior state.","rule":"Trace session identifiers across authentication, privilege changes, logout, and reauthentication events.","examples":["A pre-authentication session surviving account authentication without appropriate regeneration.","A privileged session retaining an earlier security context."],"invalid_examples":["Treating any persistent session as session fixation.","Checking only cookie values without correlating identity transitions."],"problem":"Session state is not correctly bound to the authenticated security context.","failure":"An attacker or stale context can influence an authenticated session.","cause":"Missing regeneration, improper invalidation, or incorrect session lifecycle handling.","solution":"Regenerate or rebind session state at security-boundary transitions according to the session model.","alternatives":["Server-side sessions","Short-lived tokens","Explicit session rotation"],"tradeoffs":"Session rotation can complicate multi-device or distributed session handling.","constraints":["Session semantics differ between architectures."],"edge_cases":["SSO transitions","Privilege elevation","Concurrent sessions","Device binding"],"security":"Session identifiers should not remain trusted across incompatible identity transitions.","performance":"Session rotation should be evaluated for storage and replication impact.","testing":"Test pre-authentication, authentication, privilege changes, logout, and reauthentication.","verification":"Verify identity-to-session binding across each security transition.","version":"General","dependencies":["Session management"],"prerequisites":["Authentication lifecycle"],"relationships":["D25-B56-R001"],"provenance":["OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet"],"evidence":["https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html"],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B56-R004","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Multi-Tenant Isolation","concept":"Tenant-boundary diagnosis","knowledge_type":"root_cause","question":"How should a cross-tenant data exposure be localized?","answer":"Trace tenant identity from authentication through routing, authorization, query construction, caching, storage, background jobs, and response serialization to identify the first layer where tenant scope is lost or substituted.","explanation":"Tenant isolation can fail even when individual authorization checks appear correct if tenant context is dropped between layers.","principle":"Tenant identity is a security invariant that must survive every data-access path.","rule":"Require explicit tenant scoping at every storage and cache boundary where cross-tenant access is possible.","examples":["A cache key omits tenant identity.","A background task uses a global database session without tenant context."],"invalid_examples":["Checking tenant membership only at the API gateway.","Assuming database row ownership is enforced if one query path includes a tenant filter."],"problem":"Tenant context is lost or misapplied during data processing.","failure":"One tenant can observe or modify another tenant's resources.","cause":"Missing scope propagation, cache-key collisions, unscoped queries, or asynchronous context loss.","solution":"Make tenant scope explicit and enforce it at multiple independent boundaries.","alternatives":["Database row-level security","Tenant-scoped repositories","Per-tenant data stores"],"tradeoffs":"Defense in depth can increase implementation and query complexity.","constraints":["Shared infrastructure requires explicit isolation semantics."],"edge_cases":["Background jobs","Caches","Exports","Search indexes","Batch operations"],"security":"Tenant identity should be treated as security-critical context, not ordinary metadata.","performance":"Tenant filters and partitioning strategies should be benchmarked for scale.","testing":"Test cross-tenant reads/writes across every access path.","verification":"Prove or test that tenant scope cannot be omitted from security-sensitive operations.","version":"General","dependencies":["Identity context","Data-access layer","Caching"],"prerequisites":["Multi-tenancy","Authorization"],"relationships":["D25-B56-R002"],"provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","OWASP Multi-Tenant guidance"],"evidence":["https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html"],"confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B56-R005","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Access-Control Verification","concept":"Authorization regression verification","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"How should an authorization fix be verified against privilege escalation and cross-boundary regressions?","answer":"Test the complete authorization matrix across identities, resources, actions, tenants, lifecycle states, and policy versions, including both direct and indirect access paths.","explanation":"A patch to one endpoint can leave equivalent access through another route, background task, cache, export, or batch operation.","principle":"Authorization verification must cover the security policy surface, not only the reported endpoint.","rule":"Represent security decisions as explicit testable cases and include negative cases as first-class requirements.","examples":["Testing user, administrator, service-account, and cross-tenant combinations.","Testing direct API access and export/background-job paths."],"invalid_examples":["Testing only that the reported 403 becomes correct.","Checking one role without testing resource ownership."],"problem":"An authorization patch is locally correct but globally incomplete.","failure":"Equivalent unauthorized access remains available.","cause":"Narrow regression coverage or duplicated policy implementations.","solution":"Build an authorization matrix and test all relevant enforcement paths.","alternatives":["Central policy enforcement","Generated authorization tests","Policy-as-code"],"tradeoffs":"Large authorization matrices can become expensive to maintain.","constraints":["Policy changes require synchronized test updates."],"edge_cases":["Delegation","Impersonation","Expired roles","Tenant migration"],"security":"Negative authorization cases are mandatory evidence for access-control changes.","performance":"Use fast policy-level tests plus smaller end-to-end suites.","testing":"Include allow/deny matrices and boundary transitions.","verification":"Map each test to an explicit authorization rule and resource scope.","version":"General","dependencies":["Authorization policy","Test framework"],"prerequisites":["Access-control modeling"],"relationships":["D25-B56-R002","D25-B56-R004"],"provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-53"],"evidence":["https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html","https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-53/rev-5/final"],"confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B57-R001","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Cryptographic Misuse","concept":"Cryptographic API misuse diagnosis","knowledge_type":"root_cause","question":"How should a cryptographic failure be debugged when the implementation uses a standard cryptographic library?","answer":"Inspect algorithm selection, mode, nonce or IV handling, key lifecycle, randomness, authentication properties, encoding, verification order, and library API semantics rather than assuming library use is automatically secure.","explanation":"Cryptographic libraries provide primitives; security depends on correct composition and parameter handling.","principle":"A correct cryptographic primitive can be insecure when composed with incorrect protocol or lifecycle semantics.","rule":"Debug cryptographic failures against the security property the protocol actually requires.","examples":["Nonce reuse in a nonce-sensitive construction.","Accepting unauthenticated plaintext before integrity verification."],"invalid_examples":["Changing algorithms without identifying the violated security property.","Assuming encryption alone provides authenticity."],"problem":"Cryptographic primitives are composed in a way that fails the intended security property.","failure":"Confidentiality, integrity, authenticity, or key secrecy is weakened.","cause":"API misuse, nonce handling, incorrect modes, weak randomness, or protocol errors.","solution":"Use well-defined constructions and high-level APIs where appropriate, and verify protocol properties explicitly.","alternatives":["Authenticated encryption","Standard protocol libraries","Managed key services"],"tradeoffs":"High-level abstractions can constrain specialized protocol requirements.","constraints":["Cryptographic correctness depends on exact construction and threat model."],"edge_cases":["Nonce reuse","Key rotation","Encoding ambiguity","Downgrade paths"],"security":"Avoid custom cryptographic constructions unless independently justified and reviewed.","performance":"Cryptographic choices should be evaluated under the actual workload without weakening security properties.","testing":"Test key, nonce, authentication, and failure semantics rather than ciphertext equality alone.","verification":"Verify the intended cryptographic security property at the protocol boundary.","version":"General","dependencies":["Cryptographic library","Key-management system"],"prerequisites":["Cryptographic primitives","Threat modeling"],"relationships":[],"provenance":["OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet","NIST Cryptographic Standards"],"evidence":["https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html","https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-standards-and-guidelines"],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B57-R002","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Randomness","concept":"Randomness failure diagnosis","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"How can weak randomness be localized as a root cause?","answer":"Trace the origin of security-sensitive random values, identify the entropy source and initialization lifecycle, and determine whether the generated values satisfy the unpredictability requirement of the consuming protocol.","explanation":"Random-looking output is not evidence of cryptographic unpredictability.","principle":"Randomness must be evaluated against its security purpose, not visual appearance.","rule":"Use cryptographically appropriate randomness APIs for security-sensitive values and verify the lifecycle of the underlying generator.","examples":["Predictable session identifiers caused by non-cryptographic pseudo-random generation.","Repeated initialization producing correlated security tokens."],"invalid_examples":["Checking randomness by eyeballing output.","Using statistical uniformity alone as proof of cryptographic security."],"problem":"Security-sensitive values are predictable or insufficiently independent.","failure":"Tokens, keys, nonces, or reset values become guessable or correlated.","cause":"Weak generator, incorrect seeding, insufficient entropy, or misuse of deterministic PRNGs.","solution":"Use a platform-supported cryptographic randomness source appropriate to the security purpose.","alternatives":["Operating-system CSPRNG","Managed cryptographic APIs"],"tradeoffs":"Cryptographic randomness can have different performance characteristics than non-security PRNGs.","constraints":["Embedded and constrained systems require platform-specific entropy analysis."],"edge_cases":["Early boot","Forking processes","VM snapshots","Container cloning"],"security":"Never infer cryptographic unpredictability from statistical tests alone.","performance":"Use appropriate secure APIs rather than repeatedly constructing generators unnecessarily.","testing":"Test API selection, lifecycle, and failure handling; do not attempt to prove unpredictability through small samples.","verification":"Verify that security-sensitive values originate from an approved cryptographic randomness mechanism.","version":"General","dependencies":["OS randomness API","Cryptographic library"],"prerequisites":["Randomness concepts"],"relationships":["D25-B57-R001"],"provenance":["NIST SP 800-90 series","OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet"],"evidence":["https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-90a/rev-1/final","https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html"],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B57-R003","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Key Lifecycle","concept":"Key-management state diagnosis","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should a cryptographic key lifecycle defect be debugged?","answer":"Trace key generation, storage, access, use, rotation, revocation, backup, and destruction states and identify where the implementation diverges from the required key lifecycle policy.","explanation":"Many cryptographic failures are lifecycle failures rather than primitive failures.","principle":"Keys are security state with explicit lifecycle requirements.","rule":"Represent key state transitions and authorization for each transition explicitly.","examples":["A revoked key remaining accepted by a cache.","A rotated key being used by one service while another expects the old key."],"invalid_examples":["Rotating keys without verifying old-key invalidation semantics.","Searching source code for hard-coded keys only."],"problem":"Key state diverges between components or lifecycle stages.","failure":"Unauthorized or stale keys remain usable.","cause":"Caching, synchronization, rotation sequencing, or lifecycle omissions.","solution":"Define authoritative key state and verify propagation of lifecycle transitions.","alternatives":["Managed key services","Short-lived credentials","Versioned key identifiers"],"tradeoffs":"Frequent rotation can increase operational complexity.","constraints":["Distributed systems require coordinated key-state transitions."],"edge_cases":["Clock skew","Offline services","Rollback deployments","Disaster recovery"],"security":"Key revocation and rotation must be tested as state transitions, not merely configuration changes.","performance":"Key retrieval and caching require explicit invalidation semantics.","testing":"Test rotation, revocation, restart, rollback, and partial-failure scenarios.","verification":"Verify that every consumer honors the intended key lifecycle state.","version":"General","dependencies":["Key-management system","Service configuration"],"prerequisites":["Cryptography","Distributed state"],"relationships":["D25-B57-R001"],"provenance":["NIST Key Management Guidelines","OWASP Key Management guidance"],"evidence":["https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/key-management","https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Key_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html"],"confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B57-R004","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Cryptographic Configuration","concept":"Security-configuration root cause","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"How can a cryptographic configuration regression be distinguished from a code defect?","answer":"Compare effective runtime cryptographic configuration with the intended policy, including algorithms, protocol versions, certificate validation, trust stores, key identifiers, and fallback behavior.","explanation":"The source may remain unchanged while deployment configuration silently changes the effective cryptographic behavior.","principle":"Effective security configuration is part of executable behavior.","rule":"Debug the resolved runtime configuration rather than only configuration source files.","examples":["A deployment enables an obsolete protocol version.","A service uses a different trust store than expected."],"invalid_examples":["Reviewing only checked-in configuration.","Assuming default library settings are identical across versions."],"problem":"Runtime cryptographic behavior differs from intended policy.","failure":"Weak or unintended security settings are active.","cause":"Configuration drift, defaults, environment variables, library changes, or deployment overrides.","solution":"Make effective security configuration observable, versioned, and validated.","alternatives":["Configuration schemas","Policy-as-code","Startup validation"],"tradeoffs":"Strict startup validation can prevent availability when configuration is incomplete.","constraints":["Different environments may legitimately require different settings."],"edge_cases":["Environment variables","Container secrets","Library defaults","Certificate rotation"],"security":"Fail closed when required cryptographic policy cannot be established.","performance":"Startup validation is preferable to repeated runtime policy checks where possible.","testing":"Test configuration variants and invalid security settings.","verification":"Record and compare effective runtime security configuration against approved policy.","version":"General","dependencies":["TLS/crypto configuration","Deployment environment"],"prerequisites":["Configuration management"],"relationships":["D25-B57-R001","D25-B57-R003"],"provenance":["OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet","NIST cryptographic guidance"],"evidence":["https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html","https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-standards-and-guidelines"],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B57-R005","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Cryptographic Fix Verification","concept":"Security-property verification","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"How should a cryptographic repair be verified without relying on ciphertext samples alone?","answer":"Verify the intended security property, protocol state transitions, failure behavior, key lifecycle, and downgrade resistance under both normal and adversarial conditions.","explanation":"Matching ciphertext or successful encryption does not establish authenticity, confidentiality, key separation, or protocol correctness.","principle":"Cryptographic verification must target security properties and protocol invariants rather than output appearance.","rule":"Define the security property before selecting the verification method.","examples":["Verifying authentication failure on modified ciphertext.","Verifying old keys are rejected after a defined revocation transition."],"invalid_examples":["Comparing one ciphertext before and after a patch.","Using randomness statistics as proof of cryptographic correctness."],"problem":"A cryptographic patch passes functional tests but its security property remains unverified.","failure":"The implementation remains vulnerable despite appearing operational.","cause":"Testing focused on functionality rather than security semantics.","solution":"Combine protocol-level negative tests, property checks, configuration verification, and independent review.","alternatives":["Formal protocol analysis","Known-answer tests","Security regression suites"],"tradeoffs":"Security-property testing can require specialized expertise.","constraints":["Testing cannot establish arbitrary cryptographic security by itself."],"edge_cases":["Downgrade","Replay","Key compromise","Partial protocol failure"],"security":"Security properties must be explicitly documented and tested.","performance":"Separate expensive cryptographic assurance tests from normal functional suites.","testing":"Include tampering, replay, invalid-key, invalid-certificate, and downgrade cases as applicable.","verification":"Record evidence for each intended cryptographic property.","version":"General","dependencies":["Crypto library","Protocol specification"],"prerequisites":["Cryptographic security properties"],"relationships":["D25-B57-R001","D25-B57-R003","D25-B57-R004"],"provenance":["NIST Cryptographic Standards","OWASP TLS and Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheets"],"evidence":["https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-standards-and-guidelines","https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html"],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B58-R001","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Privilege Boundaries","concept":"Least-privilege failure diagnosis","knowledge_type":"root_cause","question":"How should excessive-privilege behavior be debugged when no direct authorization bypass is present?","answer":"Identify the effective privileges of the executing principal, compare them with the minimum privileges required for the operation, and trace how elevated capabilities became available.","explanation":"Excessive privilege increases the impact of otherwise ordinary defects and can itself violate security policy.","principle":"Effective authority should be no broader than the operation requires.","rule":"Debug effective capabilities, not merely declared roles or intended permissions.","examples":["A service account retaining administrative filesystem permissions after a feature no longer needs them.","A worker process inheriting unnecessary cloud permissions."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming a privilege is safe because the application rarely uses it.","Reviewing role names without examining effective permissions."],"problem":"A component operates with unnecessary authority.","failure":"A defect or compromise gains excessive impact.","cause":"Overprivileged identities, inherited permissions, or stale role assignments.","solution":"Reduce effective privileges and verify operation-specific capability requirements.","alternatives":["Capability-based access","Dedicated service identities","Fine-grained policies"],"tradeoffs":"Fine-grained privileges can increase operational complexity.","constraints":["Some platforms expose coarse-grained permission models."],"edge_cases":["Temporary elevation","Break-glass access","Inherited cloud roles"],"security":"Treat unnecessary authority as a root-cause amplifier.","performance":"Authorization checks may add overhead depending on architecture.","testing":"Test that operations fail when unnecessary privileges are removed.","verification":"Demonstrate that the component retains only the authority required by its defined contract.","version":"General","dependencies":["Identity system","Permission model"],"prerequisites":["Authorization","Privilege models"],"relationships":[],"provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","NIST Least Privilege guidance"],"evidence":["https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html","https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-53/rev-5/final"],"confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B58-R002","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Capability Leakage","concept":"Capability propagation diagnosis","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How can accidental capability propagation be identified?","answer":"Trace security-sensitive handles, tokens, file descriptors, object references, delegated credentials, and authority-bearing context through process, thread, task, and service boundaries.","explanation":"Authority can propagate implicitly through inherited handles or context even when no explicit permission check grants it.","principle":"Security authority must be tracked as dataflow with explicit ownership and transfer semantics.","rule":"Identify every operation that creates, copies, inherits, or delegates an authority-bearing capability.","examples":["A child process inheriting an administrative file descriptor.","A background task retaining a privileged context after the initiating request ends."],"invalid_examples":["Auditing only explicit role checks.","Assuming capability transfer cannot occur through process inheritance or context propagation."],"problem":"Authority crosses a boundary unintentionally.","failure":"A component can perform operations outside its intended trust domain.","cause":"Implicit inheritance, shared context, or unsafe delegation.","solution":"Make capability transfer explicit and constrain inheritance.","alternatives":["Capability tokens","Explicit delegation","Process isolation"],"tradeoffs":"Explicit capability passing can increase API complexity.","constraints":["Operating systems and runtimes have different inheritance semantics."],"edge_cases":["Fork/exec","Async task context","Thread-local state","IPC"],"security":"Authority-bearing state should be minimized and explicitly scoped.","performance":"Isolation and explicit delegation can add IPC or context-propagation cost.","testing":"Test capability absence as well as capability presence.","verification":"Demonstrate that unauthorized components cannot obtain or retain the capability.","version":"General","dependencies":["Process model","Identity/context propagation"],"prerequisites":["Least privilege","Dataflow analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B58-R001"],"provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","MITRE CWE-269"],"evidence":["https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html","https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-53/rev-5/final"],"confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B58-R003","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Security Boundary Failures","concept":"Trust-boundary transition diagnosis","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"What is the correct debugging target when a trust boundary is crossed incorrectly?","answer":"Identify the boundary, enumerate the assumptions that change across it, and determine which untrusted state crossed without the required validation, normalization, authorization, or isolation transition.","explanation":"A trust boundary is meaningful only when the receiving component does not blindly inherit the sender's security assumptions.","principle":"Trust must be established independently at each security boundary according to the receiving component's requirements.","rule":"Document explicit preconditions and postconditions for every security-sensitive boundary crossing.","examples":["A backend trusting an authorization decision encoded by an untrusted client.","A worker trusting metadata that was not authenticated by the producer."],"invalid_examples":["Calling a component trusted because it runs inside the same network.","Treating transport security as proof of application authorization."],"problem":"A receiving component inherits trust it did not independently establish.","failure":"Untrusted state receives trusted treatment.","cause":"Implicit trust, missing validation, or boundary confusion.","solution":"Define explicit boundary contracts and enforce required security properties at the receiving side.","alternatives":["Mutual authentication","Signed assertions","Central policy enforcement"],"tradeoffs":"Repeated verification increases complexity and processing cost.","constraints":["Security boundaries must match actual attack paths rather than network topology alone."],"edge_cases":["Internal APIs","Service meshes","Message queues","Shared databases"],"security":"Internal location is not itself proof of trust.","performance":"Use cryptographic or policy mechanisms proportionally to the boundary risk.","testing":"Test boundary inputs independently of trusted upstream behavior.","verification":"Show that receiving components reject invalid or unauthenticated security context.","version":"General","dependencies":["Authentication","Authorization","Data validation"],"prerequisites":["Trust-boundary modeling"],"relationships":["D25-B56-R002","D25-B58-R001"],"provenance":["OWASP Threat Modeling","NIST Zero Trust Architecture"],"evidence":["https://owasp.org/www-community/Threat_Modeling","https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-207/final"],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B58-R004","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Privilege Separation","concept":"Privilege-separation fault localization","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How can a privilege-separation failure be localized?","answer":"Map operations to the process, thread, service, or identity that performs them and identify where a privileged operation is reachable from a less-trusted context without an explicit mediated transition.","explanation":"Privilege separation reduces the consequences of defects by constraining which components can perform sensitive operations.","principle":"Sensitive operations should execute only within narrowly scoped privileged components.","rule":"Trace the call or message path from untrusted input to privileged effect.","examples":["A web process directly accessing a sensitive system resource instead of a constrained helper.","A parser running with permissions unnecessary for parsing."],"invalid_examples":["Calling separate modules privilege separation when they execute under the same authority.","Assuming code organization alone creates a security boundary."],"problem":"Untrusted code executes with privileges beyond its functional requirements.","failure":"A compromise or bug can directly reach sensitive resources.","cause":"Co-location, excessive service privileges, or missing mediation.","solution":"Move sensitive operations behind narrowly scoped privileged interfaces.","alternatives":["Sandboxing","Helper processes","Capability-based design"],"tradeoffs":"IPC and mediation introduce latency and operational complexity.","constraints":["Privilege boundaries must be enforceable by the runtime or operating system."],"edge_cases":["Plugin systems","Native extensions","Embedded interpreters"],"security":"Privilege separation is effective only when the boundary is enforced independently of application intent.","performance":"Measure IPC and context-switch overhead after separation.","testing":"Attempt sensitive operations from each unprivileged component and verify denial.","verification":"Demonstrate that sensitive effects require the intended privileged transition.","version":"General","dependencies":["OS permissions","Process architecture"],"prerequisites":["Least privilege","Process isolation"],"relationships":["D25-B58-R001","D25-B58-R003"],"provenance":["OWASP Threat Modeling","NIST SP 800-53"],"evidence":["https://owasp.org/www-community/Threat_Modeling","https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-53/rev-5/final"],"confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B58-R005","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Privilege Boundary Verification","concept":"Least-privilege regression verification","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"How should a least-privilege security fix be verified?","answer":"Remove the unnecessary authority and execute the complete supported operation set, then explicitly test that the removed capabilities remain unavailable while required operations continue to succeed.","explanation":"A privilege reduction is successful only if required behavior survives without restoring unnecessary authority.","principle":"Least privilege must be verified by capability necessity and capability absence.","rule":"Test both positive functionality and negative authorization cases after privilege reduction.","examples":["Running service tests with the reduced service account.","Attempting previously unnecessary filesystem or cloud operations and confirming denial."],"invalid_examples":["Checking only that the service starts.","Restoring privileges when one unrelated test fails without investigating the dependency."],"problem":"Privilege reduction may break hidden functional dependencies.","failure":"Teams restore excessive privileges to regain functionality.","cause":"Unidentified capability dependencies.","solution":"Map required capabilities, remove extras, and test explicit negative cases.","alternatives":["Capability inventories","Policy-as-code","Sandboxed integration tests"],"tradeoffs":"Discovering implicit dependencies can require significant test coverage.","constraints":["Emergency or break-glass privileges should remain separately controlled."],"edge_cases":["Optional features","Migration jobs","Disaster recovery","Administrative maintenance"],"security":"Never treat broad privilege restoration as the default fix for a capability failure.","performance":"Privilege checks should be measured only where they materially affect hot paths.","testing":"Combine functional and negative capability tests.","verification":"Record the minimal capability set required by the component.","version":"General","dependencies":["Identity/permission system","Integration tests"],"prerequisites":["Least privilege","Capability mapping"],"relationships":["D25-B58-R001","D25-B58-R004"],"provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet"],"evidence":["https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-53/rev-5/final","https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html"],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B59-R001","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Security Regression Engineering","concept":"Exploit-to-root-cause regression mapping","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"How should a security regression test be derived from a root-cause analysis?","answer":"Translate the violated security invariant into a deterministic test condition, reproduce the original failure when possible, and add adjacent cases that exercise the same root cause through alternate inputs or paths.","explanation":"A regression test should preserve knowledge of why the defect occurred rather than merely preserving one exploit string.","principle":"Security regression tests should encode the violated invariant and its relevant boundary conditions.","rule":"Associate each regression with a documented root cause and expected security property.","examples":["A tenant-isolation test generated from the missing tenant-scope invariant.","A parser differential test generated from the normalization discrepancy."],"invalid_examples":["Adding only the original proof-of-concept input.","Writing a test whose pass condition does not correspond to the security invariant."],"problem":"Regression tests capture symptoms but not the security root cause.","failure":"Equivalent variants bypass the patch.","cause":"Example-specific testing.","solution":"Derive tests from the causal invariant and expand them across equivalent paths.","alternatives":["Property-based security tests","Differential tests","Fuzz regression corpus"],"tradeoffs":"Broader tests require more maintenance.","constraints":["The invariant must be precise enough to test."],"edge_cases":["Multiple root causes","Environment-dependent exploits","Non-deterministic failures"],"security":"Keep security regression evidence reproducible and free of real secrets.","performance":"Separate expensive fuzz/security suites from rapid regression checks.","testing":"Include original, adjacent, negative, and legitimate cases.","verification":"Record the relationship between the test, root cause, and security property.","version":"General","dependencies":["Security test framework","Root-cause model"],"prerequisites":["Root-cause analysis","Security invariants"],"relationships":["D25-B51-R005"],"provenance":["OWASP Testing Guide","NIST SSDF"],"evidence":["https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/","https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/ssdf"],"confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B59-R002","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Patch Completeness","concept":"Security patch surface analysis","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How can an engineer determine whether a security patch covers the complete defect surface?","answer":"Map the root cause to all reachable implementations, interfaces, duplicated logic, alternate protocols, configuration paths, and generated artifacts that can reproduce the violated invariant.","explanation":"A vulnerability may have multiple manifestations even when only one path was reported.","principle":"Patch scope should follow the root cause and invariant, not the first observed symptom.","rule":"Search for semantic equivalents rather than only textual copies of the vulnerable code.","examples":["Multiple endpoints independently constructing unsafe queries.","Several parsers implementing the same insecure normalization rule."],"invalid_examples":["Patching one function because it was the first reported location.","Searching only for identical code strings."],"problem":"The repair addresses one manifestation while equivalent vulnerable paths remain.","failure":"The same vulnerability persists through alternate implementations.","cause":"Duplicated logic, inconsistent abstractions, or incomplete dependency analysis.","solution":"Perform semantic impact analysis and centralize shared security-sensitive behavior where appropriate.","alternatives":["Shared security libraries","Static analysis rules","Architecture-level controls"],"tradeoffs":"Centralization can increase coupling if poorly designed.","constraints":["Not all semantic equivalents can be safely consolidated."],"edge_cases":["Generated code","Plugins","Legacy APIs","Versioned endpoints"],"security":"Patch scope should be determined by attack surface and violated invariant.","performance":"Centralization can reduce repeated validation but may introduce shared-service latency.","testing":"Test every identified reachable variant.","verification":"Document why each affected path is fixed, excluded, or independently controlled.","version":"General","dependencies":["Code search","Call/dataflow analysis"],"prerequisites":["Security root-cause analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B59-R001"],"provenance":["OWASP Code Review Guide","OWASP ASVS"],"evidence":["https://owasp.org/www-project-code-review-guide/","https://owasp.org/www-project-application-security-verification-standard/"],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B59-R003","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Security Patch Overfitting","concept":"Security-test overfitting detection","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"What is security patch overfitting?","answer":"It occurs when a patch makes the known security test pass without restoring the broader security invariant, leaving semantically equivalent failure paths available.","explanation":"A test-specific patch can suppress an observed input while preserving the underlying vulnerability class.","principle":"Passing the reported exploit test is necessary but not sufficient evidence of a correct security repair.","rule":"Test behavioral equivalence classes and the underlying invariant after patching.","examples":["Rejecting one malicious string while retaining unsafe string construction.","Blocking one path while another endpoint reaches the same vulnerable sink."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring success after one proof-of-concept test passes.","Using a blacklist tailored to known exploit strings as complete remediation."],"problem":"The patch is optimized for observed examples instead of the root cause.","failure":"Variants bypass the repair.","cause":"Narrow regression testing and symptom-level patching.","solution":"Replace symptom suppression with structural remediation and invariant-based tests.","alternatives":["Parameterized APIs","Policy enforcement","Property-based tests"],"tradeoffs":"Structural fixes can require larger refactoring than input-specific filters.","constraints":["Compatibility may limit immediate architectural changes."],"edge_cases":["Encoding variants","Equivalent syntax","Alternate endpoints"],"security":"Evaluate security fixes against classes of behavior rather than individual payloads.","performance":"Structural fixes may improve performance by eliminating repeated filtering layers.","testing":"Use generated variants and alternate execution paths.","verification":"Demonstrate that the root cause no longer exists under relevant representations.","version":"General","dependencies":["Security regression tests"],"prerequisites":["Root-cause modeling"],"relationships":["D25-B59-R001","D25-B59-R002"],"provenance":["OWASP Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet","OWASP Testing Guide"],"evidence":["https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html","https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/"],"confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B59-R004","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Security Fix Differential Analysis","concept":"Before-after security behavior comparison","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"Why is differential behavior analysis useful when validating a security fix?","answer":"It reveals unintended changes by comparing the vulnerable and corrected versions over controlled inputs, security contexts, configurations, and execution paths.","explanation":"A patch can remove the reported vulnerability while accidentally changing unrelated behavior or introducing a new security weakness.","principle":"A security repair should explain both the intended behavioral change and the unintended behavioral differences.","rule":"Compare security-relevant behavior before and after the patch using controlled environments.","examples":["Comparing authorization outcomes across role/resource matrices.","Comparing parser acceptance and rejection sets."],"invalid_examples":["Comparing only application exit codes.","Using different dependency or configuration states for the two versions."],"problem":"Patch effects are incompletely understood.","failure":"Regression or residual vulnerability remains hidden.","cause":"Insufficient differential coverage.","solution":"Control environment and compare relevant observable behavior systematically.","alternatives":["Golden tests","Contract tests","Differential fuzzing"],"tradeoffs":"Broad differential testing can be computationally expensive.","constraints":["Old vulnerable versions may require isolated testing environments."],"edge_cases":["Nondeterminism","External services","Time-sensitive policies"],"security":"Do not expose vulnerable systems to uncontrolled environments merely for comparison.","performance":"Use minimized representative input sets before expanding differential coverage.","testing":"Compare positive, negative, boundary, and adversarial cases.","verification":"Record which behavioral differences are expected and which require investigation.","version":"General","dependencies":["Version control","Controlled test environment"],"prerequisites":["Regression testing","Environment control"],"relationships":["D25-B59-R001","D25-B59-R003"],"provenance":["NIST SSDF","OWASP Testing Guide"],"evidence":["https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/ssdf","https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/"],"confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B59-R005","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security-Oriented Debugging","subtopic":"Security Closure Criteria","concept":"Evidence-based security issue closure","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"What evidence should be required before declaring a security debugging case resolved?","answer":"Require a documented root cause, affected surface, corrective change, regression evidence, dependency/configuration state, residual-risk statement, and verification of deployment propagation.","explanation":"Code modification alone does not establish that the security issue is closed.","principle":"Security closure is an evidence package, not a source-code event.","rule":"Do not close a security defect when the root cause or deployment state remains unverified.","examples":["Root-cause trace linked to tests and deployed artifact identity.","Residual unsupported assumptions explicitly recorded."],"invalid_examples":["Closing because the issue no longer reproduces locally.","Closing after a patch review without deployment verification."],"problem":"Security status is inferred from incomplete evidence.","failure":"Residual vulnerability or deployment mismatch remains undiscovered.","cause":"Premature closure criteria.","solution":"Define explicit evidence requirements for security issue closure.","alternatives":["Security acceptance gates","Risk-based review","Formal verification for high-assurance components"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger closure evidence increases process cost.","constraints":["Evidence requirements should match vulnerability severity and system criticality."],"edge_cases":["Third-party components","Compensating controls","Unavailable historical artifacts"],"security":"Unknown verification status must not be represented as verified closure.","performance":"Closure evidence can be collected asynchronously except for release-blocking controls.","testing":"Require reproducible regression evidence appropriate to the root cause.","verification":"Record code, test, dependency, configuration, artifact, and deployment evidence.","version":"General","dependencies":["Issue tracking","CI/CD","Security testing"],"prerequisites":["Root-cause analysis","Verification discipline"],"relationships":["D25-B59-R001","D25-B59-R004"],"provenance":["NIST SSDF","OWASP ASVS"],"evidence":["https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/ssdf","https://owasp.org/www-project-application-security-verification-standard/"],"confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B60-R001","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security Debugging Integration","subtopic":"Cross-Layer Evidence","concept":"Integrated security root-cause synthesis","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"How should evidence from code, runtime, dependency, configuration, and security telemetry be integrated into one debugging diagnosis?","answer":"Represent observations as time- and provenance-bound evidence, connect them through explicit causal hypotheses, and distinguish observed facts from inferred relationships and unresolved alternatives.","explanation":"Security failures frequently cross multiple layers, making isolated debugging artifacts insufficient.","principle":"Cross-layer diagnosis should preserve provenance and uncertainty instead of collapsing evidence into a single unverified explanation.","rule":"Every causal link must identify its supporting evidence and confidence.","examples":["Connecting a dependency change to a parser behavior change and then to an authorization failure.","Linking artifact provenance to a production configuration discrepancy."],"invalid_examples":["Treating temporal correlation as proof of causation.","Combining logs from incompatible versions without recording provenance."],"problem":"Relevant evidence is fragmented across engineering layers.","failure":"Root-cause conclusions become speculative or incomplete.","cause":"Disconnected observability, version drift, and undocumented assumptions.","solution":"Build an evidence graph linking artifacts, versions, events, hypotheses, and verification results.","alternatives":["Causal graphs","Evidence ledgers","Structured incident records"],"tradeoffs":"Evidence graphs require disciplined metadata and storage.","constraints":["Correlation does not establish causation without additional evidence."],"edge_cases":["Clock skew","Partial telemetry","Missing artifacts","Concurrent changes"],"security":"Preserve sensitive evidence according to access-control and retention requirements.","performance":"Use indexed evidence relationships for large incident datasets.","testing":"Validate evidence-link consistency and provenance fields.","verification":"Require each root-cause claim to trace back to concrete evidence.","version":"General","dependencies":["Observability","Version metadata","Artifact provenance"],"prerequisites":["Root-cause analysis","Evidence modeling"],"relationships":["D25-B40-R001"],"provenance":["NIST SSDF","NIST SP 800-61"],"evidence":["https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/ssdf","https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-61/rev-2/final"],"confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B60-R002","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security Debugging Integration","subtopic":"Security Diagnostic Decisioning","concept":"Evidence-weighted security diagnosis","knowledge_type":"reasoning_method","question":"How should competing security root-cause hypotheses be evaluated?","answer":"Maintain multiple hypotheses, score them only according to explicitly defined evidence criteria, seek observations that discriminate between them, and preserve unresolved alternatives when evidence is insufficient.","explanation":"Premature commitment to a security explanation can cause incorrect remediation and missed vulnerabilities.","principle":"A plausible security hypothesis is not equivalent to an established root cause.","rule":"Separate observations, hypotheses, confidence, contradictions, and verification status.","examples":["Comparing dependency regression, configuration drift, and code change hypotheses for the same failure.","Seeking a reproduction that differs only in dependency version."],"invalid_examples":["Selecting the first plausible vulnerability explanation.","Treating model-generated explanations as evidence."],"problem":"Security diagnosis is uncertain and competing causes exist.","failure":"Engineering effort targets the wrong cause.","cause":"Incomplete evidence, correlated changes, or ambiguous symptoms.","solution":"Use discriminating experiments and maintain explicit hypothesis state.","alternatives":["Differential diagnosis","Bayesian evidence updates","Decision tables"],"tradeoffs":"Maintaining alternatives requires additional investigation effort.","constraints":["Confidence values must not be presented as measured probabilities unless empirically calibrated."],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous vulnerabilities","Confounded deployments","Missing historical evidence"],"security":"Do not let speculative explanations drive high-impact remediation without appropriate verification.","performance":"Prioritize discriminating evidence with high information value.","testing":"Design tests that distinguish competing hypotheses.","verification":"Close a hypothesis only when supporting evidence satisfies defined criteria.","version":"General","dependencies":["Evidence store","Testing infrastructure"],"prerequisites":["Hypothesis-driven debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B60-R001"],"provenance":["NIST SP 800-61","NIST SSDF"],"evidence":["https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-61/rev-2/final","https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/ssdf"],"confidence":0.95,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B60-R003","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security Debugging Integration","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Reuse","concept":"Reusable security debugging playbook","knowledge_type":"knowledge_representation","question":"What makes a security debugging case reusable for future incidents?","answer":"Store the symptom, affected boundary, evidence sequence, competing hypotheses, root cause, corrective pattern, verification strategy, version context, and known limitations as structured data.","explanation":"A reusable case must preserve reasoning and applicability conditions rather than merely storing a final answer.","principle":"Debugging knowledge should preserve causal context and verification evidence.","rule":"Separate reusable engineering patterns from incident-specific identifiers and secrets.","examples":["A playbook for parser differential diagnosis with required evidence fields.","A playbook for dependency-induced security regression."],"invalid_examples":["Saving only the final fix.","Copying incident logs containing production secrets into a knowledge base."],"problem":"Past debugging knowledge cannot be reliably retrieved or generalized.","failure":"Teams repeatedly rediscover the same root causes.","cause":"Unstructured postmortems and loss of provenance.","solution":"Represent debugging cases using stable structured schemas and explicit applicability conditions.","alternatives":["Case-based reasoning","Knowledge graphs","Structured incident databases"],"tradeoffs":"Rich schemas require disciplined maintenance and deduplication.","constraints":["Sensitive incident data requires controlled access and sanitization."],"edge_cases":["Conflicting cases","Version-specific behavior","Deprecated fixes"],"security":"Remove or protect secrets and sensitive operational details before knowledge reuse.","performance":"Index concepts, symptoms, failure modes, and relationships for retrieval.","testing":"Validate that stored cases preserve enough evidence to reproduce the reasoning path.","verification":"Audit each reusable case for provenance, version, and verification status.","version":"General","dependencies":["Knowledge storage","Retrieval system"],"prerequisites":["Structured debugging records"],"relationships":["D25-B60-R001","D25-B60-R002"],"provenance":["NIST SSDF","NIST SP 800-61"],"evidence":["https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/ssdf","https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-61/rev-2/final"],"confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B60-R004","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security Debugging Integration","subtopic":"AI-Assisted Security Debugging","concept":"AI evidence-boundary control","knowledge_type":"governance_pattern","question":"What controls should an AI debugging system apply when assisting with security root-cause analysis?","answer":"The system should distinguish observed artifacts from generated hypotheses, cite evidence for every material claim, expose uncertainty, prohibit fabricated execution results, and require deterministic verification before treating a suggested repair as established.","explanation":"AI assistance can accelerate hypothesis generation but cannot substitute for evidence or execution verification.","principle":"AI-generated reasoning is an input to debugging, not proof of system behavior.","rule":"No generated diagnosis or patch should be marked verified without independent evidence.","examples":["AI proposes a likely parser mismatch and requests differential testing.","AI suggests a patch but leaves execution_status as NOT_EXECUTED until tests actually run."],"invalid_examples":["Claiming a patch passes tests when no execution occurred.","Presenting an inferred root cause as an observed fact."],"problem":"AI-generated debugging output can blur evidence and inference.","failure":"False diagnoses, unsafe patches, or fabricated verification enter engineering workflows.","cause":"Unbounded generation and weak provenance controls.","solution":"Use evidence-linked reasoning records, tool-grounded actions, explicit status fields, and verification gates.","alternatives":["Human approval gates","Automated deterministic validators","Sandboxed repair agents"],"tradeoffs":"Strict verification reduces autonomous speed but increases reliability.","constraints":["AI confidence is not equivalent to empirical correctness."],"edge_cases":["Incomplete telemetry","Conflicting evidence","Unavailable test environments"],"security":"Sensitive source, logs, credentials, and exploit artifacts require access-controlled handling.","performance":"Use automated evidence retrieval and deterministic checks before expensive human review.","testing":"Test the AI system against intentionally incomplete, contradictory, and misleading debugging evidence.","verification":"Audit whether every claimed observation has provenance and every claimed execution has actual execution evidence.","version":"General","dependencies":["AI reasoning system","Evidence store","Verification tools"],"prerequisites":["Evidence-grounded debugging","Security governance"],"relationships":["D25-B60-R001","D25-B60-R002","D25-B60-R003"],"provenance":["NIST AI Risk Management Framework","NIST SSDF"],"evidence":["https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework","https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/ssdf"],"confidence":0.95,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B60-R005","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security Debugging Integration","subtopic":"Coverage Gate","concept":"Security-debugging dataset completion criteria","knowledge_type":"validation_rule","question":"What must be checked before the security-debugging portion of a knowledge dataset can be considered complete?","answer":"Completion requires coverage of major vulnerability-root-cause classes in scope, distinct implementation and failure patterns, remediation and verification methods, version/provenance metadata, contradictions and unknowns, deduplication, and relationship integrity; record count alone is not a completion criterion.","explanation":"A high-quality debugging dataset is complete when required engineering knowledge is covered without redundant padding and when claims remain traceable to evidence.","principle":"Coverage and evidence quality determine dataset completeness, not record volume.","rule":"Do not lock the dataset until semantic deduplication, schema validation, provenance review, contradiction review, and relationship checks are complete across the full dataset.","examples":["Checking that injection, deserialization, memory/concurrency, supply-chain, secret, identity, cryptography, privilege, and integrated diagnosis knowledge are represented without duplicate records.","Checking that NOT_EXECUTED records are not represented as executed evidence."],"invalid_examples":["Locking because a target record count was reached.","Treating repeated payload examples as new engineering knowledge."],"problem":"Dataset size is mistaken for knowledge completeness.","failure":"Important security-debugging gaps remain while redundant records inflate the dataset.","cause":"Count-driven generation and insufficient validation.","solution":"Use a coverage matrix plus semantic deduplication and provenance audits as the final gate.","alternatives":["Ontology-based coverage checks","Graph integrity validation","Human expert review"],"tradeoffs":"Comprehensive validation requires more effort than simple record counting.","constraints":["Full-dataset validation must include earlier batches and cross-dataset relationships."],"edge_cases":["Contradictory standards","Version-specific behavior","Deprecated techniques","Unverified claims"],"security":"Sensitive examples must be sanitized and provenance must never expose credentials or private operational data.","performance":"Validation should be automated where deterministic checks are possible.","testing":"Validate schema, IDs, references, provenance, status fields, and semantic duplicates.","verification":"Lock only after all required coverage and quality gates pass.","version":"D25 dataset lifecycle","dependencies":["D25 B1-B100","Related knowledge datasets"],"prerequisites":["Stable schema","Coverage map","Deduplication rules"],"relationships":["D25-B60-R001","D25-B60-R002","D25-B60-R003","D25-B60-R004"],"provenance":["Dataset quality specification","NIST SSDF"],"evidence":["https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/ssdf"],"confidence":0.99,"validation":"Record-level schema and semantic review completed for B51-B60; full D25 cross-batch validation remains pending.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B61-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Temporal Causality","concept":"Temporal ordering as debugging evidence","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"Why must temporal ordering be preserved when diagnosing concurrent or distributed failures?","answer":"A failure may depend on the relative order of state transitions rather than on any single event. Debugging should therefore preserve timestamps, causal identifiers, event sequence, and relevant concurrency relationships before inferring causation.","explanation":"Observed temporal order constrains possible explanations but does not by itself prove causality. Clock skew, buffering, asynchronous delivery, and instrumentation delay can distort apparent order.","principle":"Preserve event ordering evidence before constructing a causal explanation.","rule":"Do not infer causality solely from wall-clock ordering across independent execution contexts.","examples":["Correlating a timeout with the request that initiated the downstream operation.","Reconstructing the order of lock acquisition and release."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming the latest log line caused the failure merely because it appears immediately before the exception."],"problem":"Concurrent failures can appear nondeterministic when relevant ordering information is lost.","failure":"Incorrect root-cause attribution.","cause":"Incomplete temporal and causal evidence.","solution":"Capture monotonic timestamps where applicable, trace/span identifiers, sequence numbers, and synchronization events.","alternatives":["Deterministic replay","Execution tracing","Event-sourced reconstruction"],"tradeoffs":"More instrumentation improves reconstruction but adds storage and runtime overhead.","constraints":["Clock synchronization may be imperfect.","Some events are observed asynchronously."],"edge_cases":["Clock rollback","Buffered logs","Cross-host clock skew","Concurrent events with equivalent timestamps"],"security":"Telemetry must avoid exposing sensitive payloads while retaining identifiers required for correlation.","performance":"Prefer compact identifiers and sampled detail where full event capture is too expensive.","testing":"Create concurrency tests that vary event ordering and verify that diagnosis remains evidence-based.","verification":"Confirm that each causal claim maps to recorded events or explicitly remains a hypothesis.","version":"General","dependencies":["Concurrent execution","Observability"],"prerequisites":["Event tracing","Concurrency fundamentals"],"relationships":["D25-B38"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Distributed tracing research demonstrates the value of reconstructing execution relationships across large distributed systems.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B61-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Causal Reconstruction","concept":"Causal graph reconstruction","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How can a debugger represent causal relationships between events?","answer":"Represent relevant events as nodes and evidence-supported dependency relationships as directed edges. Distinguish observed edges from inferred edges and preserve identifiers that allow every edge to be traced back to telemetry, code, synchronization, or data dependencies.","explanation":"A causal graph prevents a flat event list from hiding important dependencies. It must not turn temporal adjacency into unsupported causation.","principle":"Represent causality explicitly and distinguish evidence from inference.","rule":"Every inferred causal edge should carry supporting evidence and an uncertainty state.","examples":["Request A causes database transaction B through a traced RPC.","Configuration change C affects process restart D through deployment metadata."],"invalid_examples":["Creating an edge solely because two events occurred close together."],"problem":"Complex failures involve many interacting events.","failure":"Debugger produces an attractive but unsupported causal narrative.","cause":"Loss of dependency semantics.","solution":"Build evidence-linked causal graphs with observed, inferred, and unknown relationship types.","alternatives":["Trace trees","Dependency graphs","Event DAGs"],"tradeoffs":"Graphs improve reasoning but require normalization and can become large.","constraints":["Incomplete telemetry","Missing dependencies","Asynchronous systems"],"edge_cases":["Cycles caused by feedback systems","Retries creating repeated nodes","Merged traces"],"security":"Restrict graph access according to telemetry sensitivity and tenant boundaries.","performance":"Compress repetitive event patterns and retain references to raw evidence.","testing":"Inject known causal chains and verify graph reconstruction.","verification":"Check that each graph edge is supported by an explicit artifact or labeled inference.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B61-R01"],"prerequisites":["Graph modeling","Distributed tracing"],"relationships":["D25-B40"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Distributed tracing systems model relationships among operations to support diagnosis and performance analysis.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B61-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Logical Time","concept":"Logical sequence identifiers","knowledge_type":"technique","question":"When are logical sequence identifiers preferable to wall-clock timestamps?","answer":"Logical sequence identifiers are useful when deterministic ordering within a stream matters more than globally synchronized time. They can preserve producer order, message sequence, state-transition order, or replay position.","explanation":"Wall-clock timestamps describe physical time but can be affected by clock skew and synchronization errors. Logical ordering captures relationships defined by the system itself.","principle":"Use the ordering mechanism that matches the causal property being investigated.","rule":"Do not substitute logical sequence numbers for timestamps when elapsed-time analysis is required.","examples":["Message sequence numbers in a queue.","Monotonic event counters in a state machine."],"invalid_examples":["Using sequence numbers to estimate real-world latency without timing data."],"problem":"Distributed clocks may disagree.","failure":"False temporal relationships.","cause":"Reliance on unsynchronized wall-clock timestamps.","solution":"Combine monotonic or logical sequence identifiers with timestamps where both ordering and duration matter.","alternatives":["Lamport-style logical clocks","Vector clocks","Monotonic local clocks"],"tradeoffs":"Logical clocks improve ordering but do not directly measure physical latency.","constraints":["Identifiers must survive retries and aggregation where ordering is required."],"edge_cases":["Duplicate sequence numbers","Partitioned producers","Merged streams"],"security":"Sequence identifiers should not reveal sensitive business volume if exposed externally.","performance":"Compact integer counters are inexpensive to record.","testing":"Generate reordered and duplicated events and verify ordering logic.","verification":"Compare reconstructed order against known event-generation order.","version":"General","dependencies":["Event systems"],"prerequisites":["Concurrency and distributed-systems basics"],"relationships":["D25-B61-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Context Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/context/"}],"evidence":"Context propagation provides a mechanism for associating execution-scoped information across API boundaries.","confidence":0.93,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B61-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Concurrency Diagnosis","concept":"Happens-before evidence","knowledge_type":"method","question":"What evidence should be used when reasoning about happens-before relationships?","answer":"Use synchronization operations, message send/receive relationships, lock operations, task dependencies, memory-ordering guarantees, and explicit sequence relationships. Wall-clock proximity alone is insufficient.","explanation":"Concurrency bugs often arise because two operations have an unexpected ordering relationship or lack one entirely.","principle":"Establish ordering from program semantics rather than visual log proximity.","rule":"Treat unsynchronized concurrent accesses as potentially unordered until the program's synchronization semantics establish otherwise.","examples":["A lock release preceding a later lock acquisition.","A message send preceding the corresponding receive."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming thread A finished first because its log was printed first."],"problem":"Concurrent operations may be reordered or observed differently.","failure":"Incorrect race diagnosis.","cause":"Ignoring synchronization semantics.","solution":"Map synchronization and communication operations into an ordering model.","alternatives":["Thread sanitizer evidence","Scheduler traces","Formal concurrency analysis"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed scheduling evidence can be expensive to collect.","constraints":["Language memory model","Runtime scheduler","Instrumentation fidelity"],"edge_cases":["Weak memory ordering","Spurious wakeups","Lock-free algorithms"],"security":"Avoid logging secret memory values merely to establish synchronization evidence.","performance":"Capture synchronization metadata selectively in production.","testing":"Run repeated schedule variations and compare invariant violations.","verification":"Confirm diagnosis against the language/runtime concurrency model.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B61-R01"],"prerequisites":["Concurrency","Memory models"],"relationships":["D25-B15"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Tracing API","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/trace/api/"}],"evidence":"Tracing APIs provide structured execution relationships but do not replace language-level concurrency semantics.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B61-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Temporal Evidence","concept":"Temporal evidence confidence","knowledge_type":"rule","question":"How should confidence be assigned to temporal debugging conclusions?","answer":"Confidence should reflect the quality of ordering evidence, synchronization semantics, instrumentation completeness, and alternative explanations. A timestamp alone should normally support correlation, not causal certainty.","explanation":"Strong temporal conclusions require independent evidence such as trace propagation, sequence identifiers, synchronization records, or deterministic replay.","principle":"Separate temporal correlation from causal proof.","rule":"Label unsupported causal claims as hypotheses.","examples":["High confidence when deterministic replay reproduces the same ordering and failure.","Low confidence when only application logs are available."],"invalid_examples":["Calling a timestamp correlation a proven root cause."],"problem":"Debugging systems may overstate certainty.","failure":"Premature closure.","cause":"Conflating correlation with causation.","solution":"Use explicit evidence levels and competing hypotheses.","alternatives":["Bayesian evidence models","Rule-based confidence","Human review"],"tradeoffs":"Explicit uncertainty improves correctness but may reduce apparent decisiveness.","constraints":["Evidence availability","Instrumentation coverage"],"edge_cases":["Partial traces","Clock skew","Sampling"],"security":"Do not expose internal confidence metadata if it leaks sensitive diagnostic information.","performance":"Confidence computation should use existing evidence rather than expensive repeated instrumentation.","testing":"Evaluate known causal and non-causal event pairs.","verification":"Require supporting evidence before promoting a hypothesis to root cause.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B61-R01","D25-B61-R02"],"prerequisites":["Causal reasoning"],"relationships":["D25-B41"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Distributed tracing is designed to help understand relationships across complex systems, but interpretation still requires causal discipline.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B62-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Distributed Causality","concept":"Trace-context propagation","knowledge_type":"mechanism","question":"Why is trace-context propagation important for debugging distributed failures?","answer":"Trace context allows related operations across service boundaries to retain shared identifiers, enabling logs, spans, and downstream operations to be correlated as one execution path.","explanation":"Without propagation, each service may produce locally valid telemetry that cannot be reliably connected to the originating operation.","principle":"Carry stable execution identity across relevant boundaries.","rule":"Preserve valid trace context across supported process and protocol boundaries without inventing new unrelated roots.","examples":["HTTP request propagating traceparent to a downstream service.","RPC client continuing an existing trace."],"invalid_examples":["Generating a new unrelated trace for every internal call."],"problem":"Distributed operations become disconnected telemetry fragments.","failure":"Incomplete failure path.","cause":"Broken context propagation.","solution":"Implement standards-compatible propagation and validate extracted context.","alternatives":["Custom correlation IDs","Message metadata"],"tradeoffs":"Propagation improves correlation but introduces metadata overhead and privacy considerations.","constraints":["Protocol support","Trust boundaries","Sampling policy"],"edge_cases":["Malformed context","Untrusted external headers","Asynchronous queues"],"security":"Validate incoming propagation data and prevent attacker-controlled identifiers from becoming authorization decisions.","performance":"Keep context compact and avoid excessive baggage.","testing":"Test propagation across every supported boundary and failure mode.","verification":"Confirm downstream spans retain the expected trace relationship.","version":"OpenTelemetry Specification 1.x","dependencies":["Distributed tracing"],"prerequisites":["HTTP/RPC/messaging concepts"],"relationships":["D25-B38"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Context","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/context/"},{"source":"OpenTelemetry Trace API","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/trace/api/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry defines context propagation and SpanContext identifiers for distributed execution correlation.","confidence":0.99,"validation":"Source-grounded semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B62-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Trace Integrity","concept":"Broken trace detection","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_rule","question":"How can a debugger detect broken distributed traces?","answer":"Detect missing parent relationships, impossible span ancestry, unexpected trace-root creation, invalid identifiers, abrupt context changes, and gaps at known communication boundaries.","explanation":"A trace can appear complete while silently losing context at one boundary. Trace integrity checks should therefore be treated as diagnostic evidence.","principle":"Validate the diagnostic instrument before trusting its reconstruction.","rule":"Do not interpret a partial trace as a complete causal path without checking context continuity.","examples":["A downstream service unexpectedly creates a new trace root.","A message consumer receives no valid parent context."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming missing spans prove that the operation never occurred."],"problem":"Telemetry can be incomplete or malformed.","failure":"False causal conclusions.","cause":"Unvalidated trace continuity.","solution":"Run structural trace-integrity checks before diagnosis.","alternatives":["Log correlation","Explicit request IDs"],"tradeoffs":"Integrity checks add processing but reduce misleading diagnosis.","constraints":["Sampling","Instrumentation coverage"],"edge_cases":["Intentional trace breaks","Security gateways","Batch processing"],"security":"Treat externally supplied trace context as untrusted metadata.","performance":"Validate structure using lightweight identifier checks.","testing":"Inject missing and malformed propagation at each boundary.","verification":"Compare trace structure against known service topology.","version":"OpenTelemetry Specification 1.x","dependencies":["D25-B62-R01"],"prerequisites":["Trace context"],"relationships":["D25-B61-R02"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Trace API","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/trace/api/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry defines trace and span identifiers and propagation semantics that can be structurally validated.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B62-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Telemetry Semantics","concept":"Semantic normalization across services","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"Why do common telemetry semantic conventions improve debugging?","answer":"Common attribute names and meanings allow diagnostic systems to correlate information across languages, libraries, and services without maintaining separate interpretation rules for every implementation.","explanation":"Normalization does not make telemetry complete; it makes available telemetry more consistently interpretable.","principle":"Standardize meaning before building cross-system diagnostic logic.","rule":"Prefer established semantic conventions over arbitrary equivalent field names when interoperability is required.","examples":["Using standardized service and exception attributes.","Consistent database operation metadata across services."],"invalid_examples":["Mapping identical concepts to unrelated field names across teams without a compatibility layer."],"problem":"Polyglot systems emit incompatible telemetry schemas.","failure":"Cross-service queries miss relevant evidence.","cause":"Semantic fragmentation.","solution":"Adopt and version semantic conventions with explicit migration rules.","alternatives":["Internal telemetry schema","Translation layer"],"tradeoffs":"Standards reduce integration cost but may require migration from legacy telemetry.","constraints":["Existing instrumentation","Convention maturity"],"edge_cases":["Experimental attributes","Deprecated conventions","Vendor-specific fields"],"security":"Standardization must not force sensitive fields into telemetry.","performance":"Normalized attributes can improve indexing and query efficiency but increase payload size.","testing":"Validate telemetry producers against schema contracts.","verification":"Check that equivalent operations produce semantically equivalent fields.","version":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","dependencies":["Telemetry schema"],"prerequisites":["Observability fundamentals"],"relationships":["D25-B38","D25-B49"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry documents semantic conventions as common names and meanings for telemetry data.","confidence":0.99,"validation":"Source-grounded semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B62-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Sampling","concept":"Sampling-aware diagnosis","knowledge_type":"constraint","question":"Why must debugging systems account for telemetry sampling?","answer":"Sampling means the absence of an event or trace does not necessarily mean the event did not occur. Diagnostic reasoning must distinguish missing evidence from negative evidence.","explanation":"Sampling can improve scalability but creates incomplete observations. A diagnosis engine must know the sampling policy before interpreting absence.","principle":"Treat sampled telemetry as an observation subset, not a complete execution history.","rule":"Never infer non-occurrence solely from absence in sampled telemetry.","examples":["A sampled trace omits a downstream operation.","A high-volume event is retained at a configured sampling rate."],"invalid_examples":["Concluding that a service never called a dependency because no sampled span exists."],"problem":"Large systems cannot always retain every event.","failure":"False negatives in diagnosis.","cause":"Ignoring sampling semantics.","solution":"Record sampling metadata and distinguish observed absence from known non-occurrence.","alternatives":["Tail sampling","Targeted capture","Full trace during reproduction"],"tradeoffs":"Higher capture increases diagnostic fidelity and storage cost.","constraints":["Telemetry budget","Privacy","System load"],"edge_cases":["Adaptive sampling","Error-biased sampling","Burst traffic"],"security":"Sampling policy should not systematically exclude security-relevant evidence without an explicit risk decision.","performance":"Use targeted high-detail capture for suspected paths.","testing":"Validate diagnostic behavior under different sampling rates.","verification":"Ensure the diagnosis engine receives sampling metadata.","version":"General","dependencies":["Distributed tracing"],"prerequisites":["Observability"],"relationships":["D25-B62-R03"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Dapper discusses sampling as an important design choice for large-scale tracing.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Source-grounded semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B62-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Cross-Layer Correlation","concept":"Trace-log-metric correlation","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should traces, logs, and metrics be combined during diagnosis?","answer":"Use shared resource identity, trace context, timestamps, operation names, deployment metadata, and service identity to align signals. Each signal should retain its original evidentiary status rather than being treated as interchangeable proof.","explanation":"Metrics provide aggregate behavior, logs provide discrete events, and traces provide execution relationships. Their combination can narrow hypotheses more effectively than any one signal.","principle":"Correlate complementary evidence without collapsing their distinct semantics.","rule":"Do not convert an aggregate metric anomaly into a specific root cause without supporting event-level evidence.","examples":["Latency metric spike correlated with traces showing a downstream timeout.","Error logs correlated to affected deployment version."],"invalid_examples":["Using a CPU spike alone to conclude that a particular function caused a failure."],"problem":"Individual observability signals are incomplete.","failure":"Overconfident diagnosis.","cause":"Signal semantics are conflated.","solution":"Build cross-signal evidence graphs with explicit provenance.","alternatives":["Trace-only diagnosis","Log-centric diagnosis"],"tradeoffs":"Cross-signal correlation increases complexity but improves diagnostic context.","constraints":["Clock alignment","Identifier availability","Sampling"],"edge_cases":["Missing traces","Aggregated metrics","Duplicate logs"],"security":"Apply consistent access controls across correlated telemetry.","performance":"Index correlation keys and avoid expensive unrestricted joins.","testing":"Generate known incidents and verify cross-signal linkage.","verification":"Require evidence from appropriate signal types for each claim.","version":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","dependencies":["D25-B62-R03"],"prerequisites":["Observability"],"relationships":["D25-B61-R02","D25-B38"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/semantic-conventions/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry defines semantic conventions across traces, metrics, logs, profiles, and resources.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B63-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Deterministic Replay","concept":"Execution recording and replay","knowledge_type":"technique","question":"What is the value of deterministic execution replay in debugging?","answer":"Replay allows investigators to revisit an execution history and inspect earlier program states without relying on the original live failure conditions. It is particularly useful when a failure is intermittent or difficult to reproduce.","explanation":"Replay fidelity depends on what execution state and nondeterministic inputs were recorded. Replay should not automatically be assumed identical to the original execution.","principle":"Use replay to convert difficult temporal failures into inspectable historical executions.","rule":"Verify replay fidelity before treating replay behavior as proof of original execution behavior.","examples":["Revisiting an exception after the original process has terminated.","Inspecting earlier memory accesses in a recorded execution."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming a replayed execution is identical when external inputs were not captured."],"problem":"Live failures may be transient.","failure":"Root cause disappears before inspection.","cause":"Nondeterministic execution and limited observability.","solution":"Use record/replay tooling where supported and document captured nondeterminism.","alternatives":["Snapshot debugging","Enhanced tracing","Reproduction harness"],"tradeoffs":"Recording can impose storage and runtime overhead.","constraints":["Platform support","External I/O","Nondeterminism"],"edge_cases":["Network responses","Randomness","Time sources","Hardware interaction"],"security":"Recorded traces may contain sensitive memory or input data and require strict handling.","performance":"Use targeted recording where full-process capture is too expensive.","testing":"Compare replay state against independently captured checkpoints.","verification":"Confirm key state transitions and failure signatures match the original.","version":"WinDbg Time Travel Debugging","dependencies":["Temporal debugging"],"prerequisites":["Debugger fundamentals"],"relationships":["D25-B22","D25-B61"],"provenance":[{"source":"Microsoft WinDbg Time Travel Debugging","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timetravel-preview"}],"evidence":"Microsoft documents Time Travel Debugging as recording execution traces that can be navigated through events such as calls, memory access, and exceptions.","confidence":0.99,"validation":"Source-grounded semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B63-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Replay Fidelity","concept":"Nondeterminism capture","knowledge_type":"constraint","question":"Which nondeterministic inputs can invalidate debugging replay?","answer":"External responses, scheduling, random values, time, filesystem state, environment variables, process identifiers, hardware behavior, and concurrent external actors can influence replay.","explanation":"If relevant nondeterministic inputs are not captured or controlled, replay may diverge even when the program binary is unchanged.","principle":"Capture or control every nondeterministic dependency relevant to the failure.","rule":"Treat unexplained replay divergence as evidence of an uncontrolled input, environment difference, or instrumentation limitation.","examples":["Recorded random seed reproducing a parser failure.","Controlled clock reproducing a timeout path."],"invalid_examples":["Changing the application binary and assuming replay divergence is caused by the code change alone."],"problem":"Replay follows a different path from the original.","failure":"False debugging conclusions.","cause":"Uncaptured nondeterminism.","solution":"Inventory external and runtime nondeterministic inputs and control or record them.","alternatives":["Deterministic test harness","Dependency virtualization"],"tradeoffs":"More control improves reproducibility but reduces realism.","constraints":["External systems may not be replayable."],"edge_cases":["Kernel scheduling","Distributed retries","Hardware interrupts"],"security":"Recorded secrets and external responses require protection.","performance":"Selective capture reduces overhead.","testing":"Intentionally vary nondeterministic inputs and verify expected divergence detection.","verification":"Compare replay fingerprints at defined checkpoints.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B63-R01"],"prerequisites":["Replay systems"],"relationships":["D25-B63-R03"],"provenance":[{"source":"Microsoft WinDbg Time Travel Debugging","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timetravel-preview"}],"evidence":"Time-travel debugging relies on recorded execution information; replay fidelity remains dependent on what the recording captures.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B63-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Replay Analysis","concept":"Checkpoint-based replay comparison","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How can checkpoints improve replay diagnosis?","answer":"Define stable program-state checkpoints and compare state fingerprints or selected variables across original, replayed, and corrected executions. The earliest meaningful divergence is often more useful than the final failure symptom.","explanation":"Checkpoint comparison narrows the search interval and supports differential diagnosis without requiring complete state equivalence.","principle":"Locate the earliest consequential divergence rather than focusing only on the terminal failure.","rule":"Choose checkpoint state that reflects causal invariants, not arbitrary memory snapshots.","examples":["Comparing transaction state after each pipeline stage.","Comparing parser state after each input segment."],"invalid_examples":["Comparing raw process memory whose addresses are inherently unstable."],"problem":"Replay traces may contain too much state for manual inspection.","failure":"Investigation becomes impractical.","cause":"No structured checkpoints.","solution":"Define domain-relevant state fingerprints and compare them incrementally.","alternatives":["Full memory diff","Event sequence comparison"],"tradeoffs":"Selective checkpoints reduce cost but may miss the relevant divergence.","constraints":["State serialization","Address instability"],"edge_cases":["Pointer-heavy state","Randomized data structures","Concurrent state"],"security":"Do not fingerprint secrets in ways that permit reconstruction.","performance":"Use compact deterministic hashes for large stable state regions.","testing":"Inject known divergence and verify the first checkpoint identifies it.","verification":"Trace the checkpoint difference back to executable operations.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B63-R01"],"prerequisites":["State modeling","Replay"],"relationships":["D25-B05","D25-B65"],"provenance":[{"source":"Microsoft WinDbg Timelines","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timeline-preview"}],"evidence":"WinDbg timelines support navigating important execution events and their relative positions during recorded execution.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B63-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Temporal Navigation","concept":"Timeline-guided fault localization","knowledge_type":"technique","question":"How can execution timelines narrow a debugging search?","answer":"Prioritize exceptions, memory accesses, breakpoints, function transitions, and other high-value events around the failure, then navigate backward to the earliest state transition inconsistent with the expected invariant.","explanation":"Timeline navigation transforms a long execution into an ordered set of diagnostic landmarks.","principle":"Search temporal landmarks before examining every instruction or event.","rule":"Use timeline events as navigation aids, not automatic proof of causation.","examples":["Walking backward from an exception to the first invalid memory access.","Finding the function transition preceding a state invariant violation."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring the immediately preceding timeline event the root cause without state analysis."],"problem":"Long executions contain huge event volumes.","failure":"Investigator misses the causal transition.","cause":"Unstructured temporal search.","solution":"Use event classes and checkpoints to prioritize navigation.","alternatives":["Trace filtering","Binary search over execution"],"tradeoffs":"Filtering increases efficiency but can hide unusual events if overly aggressive.","constraints":["Trace detail","Debugger capabilities"],"edge_cases":["Repeated exceptions","High-frequency memory events"],"security":"Access to memory timelines should follow diagnostic authorization.","performance":"Index high-value events for rapid navigation.","testing":"Use known failures with controlled landmark events.","verification":"Confirm selected landmark leads to the actual invariant violation.","version":"WinDbg Timelines","dependencies":["D25-B63-R01"],"prerequisites":["Time-travel debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B63-R03"],"provenance":[{"source":"Microsoft WinDbg Timelines","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timeline-preview"}],"evidence":"Microsoft documents timeline views for exceptions, memory access, breakpoints, function calls, and returns.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Source-grounded semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B63-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Replay Safety","concept":"Replay artifact integrity","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"What should be verified before trusting a recorded debugging artifact?","answer":"Verify artifact completeness, binary and symbol compatibility, recording integrity, relevant environment metadata, trace boundaries, and whether required external inputs were captured or controlled.","explanation":"A replay artifact can be internally valid while still being insufficient to reproduce the original failure.","principle":"Validate the diagnostic artifact before validating the diagnosis.","rule":"Never classify replay divergence as application behavior until artifact and environment integrity have been checked.","examples":["Matching executable build identity with recorded symbols.","Confirming trace was not truncated before the failure."],"invalid_examples":["Replaying a trace with different binaries and attributing every difference to runtime nondeterminism."],"problem":"Replay artifacts may be incomplete.","failure":"Misleading diagnosis.","cause":"Artifact provenance gaps.","solution":"Attach build, environment, capture, and integrity metadata to replay artifacts.","alternatives":["Reproduction package","Containerized reproduction"],"tradeoffs":"Metadata improves reliability but increases artifact management complexity.","constraints":["Tool-specific metadata availability"],"edge_cases":["Expired symbols","Partial traces","Mixed binary versions"],"security":"Replay artifacts may contain credentials or secrets; encrypt and restrict access.","performance":"Integrity checks should be incremental for large artifacts.","testing":"Corrupt or alter artifacts and verify detection.","verification":"Require successful artifact-integrity validation before replay conclusions are promoted.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B63-R01"],"prerequisites":["Debug artifact management"],"relationships":["D25-B49"],"provenance":[{"source":"Microsoft WinDbg Time Travel Debugging","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timetravel-preview"}],"evidence":"Recorded execution is a diagnostic artifact whose usefulness depends on the recorded execution information and associated debugger context.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B64-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Differential Execution","concept":"Execution differential debugging","knowledge_type":"method","question":"What is differential execution debugging?","answer":"It compares corresponding executions of two program versions, configurations, platforms, or inputs to identify the earliest meaningful behavioral divergence.","explanation":"Differential execution is most effective when executions are normalized around equivalent inputs and comparable observation points.","principle":"Compare behavior under controlled equivalence rather than comparing arbitrary runs.","rule":"Define what must remain equivalent before interpreting a difference as a regression.","examples":["Comparing old and new parser state for the same input.","Comparing two dependency versions under identical requests."],"invalid_examples":["Comparing runs with different input data and attributing every difference to the code change."],"problem":"Traditional tests may reveal failure without locating divergence.","failure":"Large search space for regression cause.","cause":"No execution-level comparison.","solution":"Align runs at checkpoints and compare observable state and outputs.","alternatives":["Git bisect","Golden-output testing"],"tradeoffs":"Differential analysis provides strong localization but requires normalization.","constraints":["Equivalent environments","Stable observation points"],"edge_cases":["Intentional behavior changes","Nondeterministic output"],"security":"Do not compare or expose sensitive state unnecessarily.","performance":"Compare selected state features instead of full process state.","testing":"Use known semantic-preserving and semantic-changing modifications.","verification":"Confirm differences correspond to intended or unintended behavioral changes.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B63"],"prerequisites":["Testing","Version comparison"],"relationships":["D25-B27","D25-B43"],"provenance":[{"source":"Microsoft WinDbg Timelines","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timeline-preview"}],"evidence":"Execution timelines provide structured event histories that can support comparison of execution behavior.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B64-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Differential State","concept":"Earliest semantic divergence","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_rule","question":"Why is the earliest semantic divergence more valuable than the final output difference?","answer":"The final difference may be an indirect consequence of an earlier state transition. Finding the first divergence that violates a relevant invariant reduces the number of downstream effects that must be considered.","explanation":"Not every low-level difference is meaningful. Debuggers should prioritize differences that cross a semantic boundary or invalidate an invariant.","principle":"Localize the first consequential divergence, not merely the first byte-level difference.","rule":"Classify differences by semantic relevance before declaring a regression point.","examples":["First incorrect account state rather than later incorrect report output.","First parser token mismatch rather than final parse failure."],"invalid_examples":["Treating different memory addresses caused by allocator layout as application divergence."],"problem":"Modern systems produce many harmless execution differences.","failure":"Noise overwhelms diagnosis.","cause":"Byte-level comparison without semantic filtering.","solution":"Define stable semantic checkpoints and invariants.","alternatives":["Output differential testing","Trace comparison"],"tradeoffs":"Semantic filtering requires domain knowledge.","constraints":["Observable state"],"edge_cases":["Equivalent alternative algorithms","Randomized scheduling"],"security":"Semantic state may contain sensitive information.","performance":"Use compact normalized representations.","testing":"Validate that harmless implementation differences are ignored.","verification":"Trace the semantic divergence to an executable change or environmental difference.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B64-R01"],"prerequisites":["Invariants","State modeling"],"relationships":["D25-B05"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Distributed tracing emphasizes understanding system behavior through structured execution information rather than isolated final symptoms.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B64-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Configuration Differential","concept":"Configuration-differential diagnosis","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should configuration differences be analyzed between a working and failing execution?","answer":"Normalize configuration into explicit key-value representations, classify differences by scope and expected effect, and test whether candidate differences can reproduce the observed failure.","explanation":"Configuration differences can include environment variables, feature flags, runtime settings, dependency configuration, resource limits, and deployment metadata.","principle":"Treat configuration as executable input to the system.","rule":"Do not attribute a failure to a configuration difference without testing its causal relevance.","examples":["Feature flag enabled only in failing deployment.","Connection pool size differs between environments."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming every environment difference is causal."],"problem":"Environment-specific bugs resist source-only analysis.","failure":"Code is modified to compensate for unrelated configuration differences.","cause":"Uncontrolled environment variance.","solution":"Generate normalized configuration diffs and test candidates systematically.","alternatives":["Environment snapshot comparison","Container image comparison"],"tradeoffs":"Full configuration capture can expose secrets.","constraints":["Secret values","Dynamic configuration"],"edge_cases":["Generated configuration","Runtime overrides","Feature flag targeting"],"security":"Redact secrets while preserving presence, type, and relevant metadata.","performance":"Hash normalized configuration sections for efficient comparison.","testing":"Reproduce candidate configurations independently.","verification":"Require configuration-to-failure evidence before root-cause assignment.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B43"],"prerequisites":["Configuration management"],"relationships":["D25-B33"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/"}],"evidence":"Resource and service metadata are part of standardized observability semantics and can support environment correlation.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B64-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Binary Differential","concept":"Build-artifact identity comparison","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"Why must binary identity be established before comparing executions?","answer":"Two source trees with similar source-level appearance can produce different binaries because of compiler versions, flags, dependencies, generated code, linkers, or build configuration. Artifact identity establishes what was actually executed.","explanation":"Source revision alone is insufficient when generated or externally resolved build inputs can differ.","principle":"Debug the artifact that executed, not merely the source that was expected to execute.","rule":"Record immutable build identity for every execution used in differential diagnosis.","examples":["Comparing executable hashes or build IDs.","Recording compiler and dependency versions."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming two binaries are identical because they came from the same commit."],"problem":"Build reproducibility may be incomplete.","failure":"False attribution to source changes.","cause":"Unverified artifact identity.","solution":"Capture artifact fingerprints and build metadata.","alternatives":["Reproducible builds","Container image digests"],"tradeoffs":"Metadata capture increases build and deployment bookkeeping.","constraints":["Generated artifacts","Third-party dependencies"],"edge_cases":["Timestamped binaries","Embedded build paths","Post-build patching"],"security":"Protect signing keys and do not expose sensitive build metadata unnecessarily.","performance":"Hashing artifacts is generally cheaper than repeated source-level investigation.","testing":"Verify that artifact fingerprints change when relevant build inputs change.","verification":"Require exact artifact identity for comparative diagnosis.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B64-R01"],"prerequisites":["Build systems"],"relationships":["D25-B28"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/"}],"evidence":"Standardized resource and service metadata demonstrate the value of identifying the executing software context.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B64-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Differential Hypothesis Testing","concept":"Evidence-ranked differential hypotheses","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should multiple explanations be evaluated in differential debugging?","answer":"Generate candidate causes from observed differences, rank them by explanatory coverage and evidence strength, and design discriminating tests that distinguish candidates rather than merely confirming the preferred hypothesis.","explanation":"Differential debugging can produce many plausible differences. Discriminating experiments prevent confirmation bias.","principle":"Prefer experiments that eliminate competing explanations.","rule":"Do not promote the most visible difference to root cause without a discriminating test.","examples":["Testing dependency version independently of configuration changes.","Repeating a run with only one feature flag changed."],"invalid_examples":["Changing five variables simultaneously and declaring success."],"problem":"Multiple differences coexist between executions.","failure":"Causal ambiguity remains.","cause":"Poor experimental isolation.","solution":"Control variables and design hypothesis-discriminating experiments.","alternatives":["Factorial experiments","Bisecting"],"tradeoffs":"Isolation increases experiment count but improves attribution.","constraints":["Reproduction cost","Production safety"],"edge_cases":["Interacting variables","Rare race conditions"],"security":"Run experiments within authorized environments and avoid unsafe production mutation.","performance":"Prioritize high-information experiments.","testing":"Measure whether each experiment can distinguish candidate hypotheses.","verification":"Require reproducible evidence for the selected cause.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B64-R01"],"prerequisites":["Scientific debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B41"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Large-scale tracing supports structured observation, while causal attribution still requires controlled diagnostic reasoning.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B65-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Fault Injection","concept":"Controlled fault injection","knowledge_type":"technique","question":"What is the purpose of fault injection in debugging?","answer":"Fault injection intentionally introduces controlled failures or degraded conditions so that system behavior, detection logic, recovery paths, and diagnostic evidence can be examined under known conditions.","explanation":"Fault injection converts assumptions about failure handling into observable experiments.","principle":"Test failure behavior under controlled and bounded conditions.","rule":"Every fault experiment should define scope, expected effect, stop conditions, and rollback or recovery procedures.","examples":["Injecting network latency in a test environment.","Temporarily failing a dependency during a resilience experiment."],"invalid_examples":["Injecting uncontrolled production failures without authorization or stop conditions."],"problem":"Failure paths are often less exercised than success paths.","failure":"Unknown behavior during real incidents.","cause":"Insufficient controlled failure testing.","solution":"Use bounded fault-injection experiments with explicit safety controls.","alternatives":["Mock failures","Dependency simulators","Chaos experiments"],"tradeoffs":"Improves resilience knowledge but can itself cause disruption.","constraints":["Authorization","Blast radius","Recovery capability"],"edge_cases":["Cascading failures","State corruption","Irreversible external actions"],"security":"Require strict authorization and target validation.","performance":"Measure experiment overhead separately from system behavior.","testing":"Verify stop conditions and recovery before increasing experiment scope.","verification":"Compare observed behavior with predefined steady-state and recovery expectations.","version":"AWS Fault Injection Service","dependencies":["Failure diagnosis","Observability"],"prerequisites":["Controlled test environments"],"relationships":["D25-B67"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"AWS documents fault injection experiments, target controls, actions, stop conditions, and visibility into experiment execution.","confidence":0.99,"validation":"Source-grounded semantic review completed; no experiment executed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B65-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Fault Isolation","concept":"Single-fault experiments","knowledge_type":"method","question":"Why should fault injection normally isolate one principal failure mode at a time?","answer":"Isolating a fault reduces ambiguity about which mechanism produced the observed behavior. Multiple simultaneous injected faults can create interactions that obscure the original diagnostic question.","explanation":"Complex experiments may be justified later, but foundational diagnosis should first establish individual failure behavior.","principle":"Increase experimental complexity only after individual failure mechanisms are understood.","rule":"Start with the smallest fault capable of testing the hypothesis.","examples":["Injecting timeout into one dependency before combining it with resource pressure.","Testing packet loss independently from CPU exhaustion."],"invalid_examples":["Simultaneously injecting latency, packet loss, memory pressure, and process termination during a first experiment."],"problem":"Failure interactions obscure causal interpretation.","failure":"Experiment produces unexplained cascading behavior.","cause":"Excessive simultaneous perturbations.","solution":"Use staged fault experiments with controlled escalation.","alternatives":["Factorial experiments","Scenario-based chaos testing"],"tradeoffs":"Isolation requires more experiments but yields clearer evidence.","constraints":["Experiment budget","System complexity"],"edge_cases":["Faults that only appear in combination"],"security":"Keep every experiment within approved blast radius.","performance":"Measure injected overhead independently.","testing":"Validate that each injected fault produces its intended condition.","verification":"Confirm observed effects match the experiment specification.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B65-R01"],"prerequisites":["Experimental design"],"relationships":["D25-B65-R03"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"Fault-injection systems define actions and controlled experiment scenarios to test workload behavior.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B65-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Fault Injection Safety","concept":"Experiment guardrails","knowledge_type":"safety_rule","question":"What guardrails are required for automated fault injection?","answer":"Guardrails should include explicit target scope, authorization, maximum blast radius, duration limits, stop conditions, health checks, rollback capability, and immutable experiment records.","explanation":"Automation can execute destructive actions quickly; safety must therefore be enforced before the action rather than relying only on operator reaction.","principle":"Make unsafe fault experiments structurally difficult to execute.","rule":"Fail closed when target identity, authorization, or stop-condition configuration is missing.","examples":["Allowing experiments only against tagged test resources.","Stopping automatically when error rate exceeds a defined threshold."],"invalid_examples":["Selecting targets from an unrestricted production resource list."],"problem":"Automated experiments can amplify mistakes.","failure":"Unintended service disruption.","cause":"Insufficient authorization and blast-radius controls.","solution":"Implement preflight validation and runtime stop conditions.","alternatives":["Manual approval gates","Dedicated test environments"],"tradeoffs":"Guardrails may prevent some useful experiments but reduce operational risk.","constraints":["Infrastructure capabilities","Authorization model"],"edge_cases":["Tag drift","Resource replacement","Partial rollback"],"security":"Treat fault injection as a privileged capability.","performance":"Preflight checks should be inexpensive and deterministic.","testing":"Test guardrails using simulated targets before live experiments.","verification":"Audit every action and verify stop conditions were active.","version":"AWS Fault Injection Service","dependencies":["D25-B65-R01"],"prerequisites":["Authorization","Reliability engineering"],"relationships":["D25-B42"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/documentation-overview/fis//"}],"evidence":"AWS describes target controls, safety controls, stop conditions, experiment visibility, and affected-resource reporting.","confidence":0.99,"validation":"Source-grounded semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B65-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Fault Injection Diagnosis","concept":"Expected-versus-observed failure signatures","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"How should fault-injection results be interpreted?","answer":"Define expected signals before the experiment, then compare observed telemetry, state transitions, alerts, recovery behavior, and user-visible effects against those expectations.","explanation":"Predefined expectations reduce post-hoc interpretation and make the experiment falsifiable.","principle":"Specify the expected diagnostic signature before injecting the fault.","rule":"Do not redefine success criteria after observing the result.","examples":["Expected timeout metric plus fallback activation.","Expected dependency error with preserved application state."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring an experiment successful because the system eventually recovered without predefined criteria."],"problem":"Failure experiments can produce ambiguous outcomes.","failure":"No clear conclusion about resilience or diagnosis.","cause":"Undefined expected behavior.","solution":"Create measurable preconditions, expected observations, and pass/fail criteria.","alternatives":["Runbook validation","Regression tests"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed criteria require preparation but improve repeatability.","constraints":["Telemetry availability"],"edge_cases":["Partial recovery","Delayed alerts","Sampling"],"security":"Avoid collecting unnecessary sensitive data during experiments.","performance":"Use existing telemetry where possible.","testing":"Automate comparison against expected signatures.","verification":"Record observed and expected states separately.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B65-R01"],"prerequisites":["Observability","Test design"],"relationships":["D25-B48"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"Fault-injection documentation emphasizes experiment conditions, metrics, actions, and stop-condition outcomes.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B65-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Fault Injection Learning","concept":"Fault experiment knowledge capture","knowledge_type":"knowledge_model","question":"What should be preserved from a completed fault-injection debugging experiment?","answer":"Preserve the target scope, injected condition, exact action, environment identity, expected behavior, observed evidence, recovery result, anomalies, limitations, and resulting engineering changes.","explanation":"Without structured experiment records, lessons are difficult to retrieve and compare across incidents.","principle":"Treat experiments as reusable evidence, not disposable operations.","rule":"Record both successful and unsuccessful experiments.","examples":["A timeout experiment reveals that fallback is never activated.","A dependency failure experiment confirms graceful degradation."],"invalid_examples":["Recording only the final conclusion without the observed evidence."],"problem":"Engineering teams repeat the same diagnostic experiments.","failure":"Knowledge loss.","cause":"Unstructured experiment history.","solution":"Store normalized experiment records with provenance and version context.","alternatives":["Incident reports","Test case repository"],"tradeoffs":"Structured storage requires maintenance but enables retrieval.","constraints":["Data retention","Privacy"],"edge_cases":["Conflicting experiment results","Changed architecture"],"security":"Redact secrets and restrict experiment artifacts.","performance":"Index fault type, target, version, and outcome.","testing":"Validate that stored records can reconstruct the experiment.","verification":"Require evidence references for every conclusion.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B49","D25-B65-R01"],"prerequisites":["Knowledge representation"],"relationships":["D25-B39"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/documentation-overview/fis//"}],"evidence":"AWS documents experiment visibility and records of executed actions, stop conditions, metrics, and affected resources.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B66-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Recovery Debugging","concept":"Recovery-path diagnosis","knowledge_type":"method","question":"Why must recovery behavior be debugged independently from failure detection?","answer":"A system can correctly detect a failure but still recover incorrectly, incompletely, or unsafely. Diagnosis should therefore inspect detection, state transition, recovery action, verification, and post-recovery convergence separately.","explanation":"Recovery can introduce secondary failures or leave stale state even when the initial failure is correctly identified.","principle":"Model recovery as an independent state transition sequence.","rule":"Do not equate successful failure detection with successful recovery.","examples":["Timeout detected but stale cache remains after fallback.","Database failover completes but clients retain invalid connections."],"invalid_examples":["Closing an incident immediately after an alert fires correctly."],"problem":"Recovery defects can remain hidden until real incidents.","failure":"Repeated or cascading failures.","cause":"Recovery path not tested or verified.","solution":"Trace recovery state transitions and validate post-recovery invariants.","alternatives":["Resilience tests","Fault injection"],"tradeoffs":"Recovery testing adds complexity but exposes operational failure modes.","constraints":["External dependencies","State consistency"],"edge_cases":["Partial recovery","Retry storms","Split-brain states"],"security":"Recovery automation must respect authorization and avoid unsafe escalation.","performance":"Measure recovery latency and resource consumption.","testing":"Inject failures and verify complete recovery invariants.","verification":"Require explicit post-recovery health and state checks.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B65"],"prerequisites":["State machines","Reliability"],"relationships":["D25-B48"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"Fault injection experiments are designed to observe workload behavior under controlled failures and recovery conditions.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B66-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"State Convergence","concept":"Post-recovery convergence debugging","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"What does convergence mean in recovery debugging?","answer":"Convergence means that, after a failure and recovery sequence, relevant system state eventually returns to an accepted consistent condition rather than merely appearing healthy at the interface.","explanation":"A service may respond successfully while background state remains stale, duplicated, or inconsistent.","principle":"Verify state convergence, not just service availability.","rule":"Define explicit post-recovery invariants for stateful systems.","examples":["Replicas converge to the same committed dataset.","Caches converge to values derived from authoritative state."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring recovery complete because HTTP health checks return success."],"problem":"Superficial health checks miss latent inconsistency.","failure":"Delayed secondary failures.","cause":"No state-level recovery verification.","solution":"Add convergence checks appropriate to system semantics.","alternatives":["Consistency probes","Reconciliation jobs"],"tradeoffs":"Deep verification consumes resources but reduces latent inconsistency.","constraints":["Eventual consistency","Large datasets"],"edge_cases":["Long-running reconciliation","Partial partitions"],"security":"Convergence checks must not expose protected data.","performance":"Use sampled or hashed state comparisons when exact comparison is expensive.","testing":"Inject recovery scenarios and measure convergence time.","verification":"Record convergence status independently of availability status.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B66-R01"],"prerequisites":["Distributed state"],"relationships":["D25-B35"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"Fault experiments can compare observed behavior and recovery against expected steady-state conditions.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B66-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Retry Recovery","concept":"Recovery-induced secondary failures","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"How can recovery mechanisms themselves create failures?","answer":"Retries, failover, replay, reconciliation, and fallback actions can increase load, duplicate effects, invalidate assumptions, or create inconsistent state. Debugging must therefore include recovery actions in the causal graph.","explanation":"The original fault may be necessary for the incident but not sufficient to explain its eventual severity.","principle":"Trace both the initiating failure and the recovery response.","rule":"Include automated recovery actions as potential causal contributors.","examples":["Retries amplify dependency load.","Replay duplicates a non-idempotent operation."],"invalid_examples":["Attributing all incident impact to the original network timeout."],"problem":"Recovery can amplify incidents.","failure":"Cascading degradation.","cause":"Unbounded or incorrect recovery behavior.","solution":"Analyze retry budgets, idempotency, backoff, and recovery state transitions.","alternatives":["Circuit breakers","Bulkheads"],"tradeoffs":"Recovery improves availability but can consume additional resources.","constraints":["Dependency capacity","Operation semantics"],"edge_cases":["Retry synchronization","Partial success","Unknown operation outcome"],"security":"Recovery automation must not bypass authorization.","performance":"Bound retry and recovery resource usage.","testing":"Inject dependency failures and observe recovery amplification.","verification":"Verify recovery actions against expected limits and state invariants.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B66-R01"],"prerequisites":["Retries","Distributed systems"],"relationships":["D25-B37","D25-B34"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"Fault injection provides a mechanism for observing system behavior under controlled failure conditions, including resulting workload effects.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B66-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Recovery Verification","concept":"Recovery completion criteria","knowledge_type":"rule","question":"What constitutes sufficient evidence that recovery is complete?","answer":"Recovery should be considered complete only when required service-level, data-level, dependency-level, and operational invariants are restored and monitored for an appropriate stabilization period.","explanation":"Immediate availability after a fault does not prove that background repair, replication, queues, caches, or dependent systems have stabilized.","principle":"Define recovery completion as verified state restoration.","rule":"Separate recovery initiation, recovery execution, and recovery verification states.","examples":["Database failover complete plus replication healthy.","Queue processing restored with backlog returning toward expected bounds."],"invalid_examples":["Marking recovery complete immediately after the primary process restarts."],"problem":"Incident status becomes misleading.","failure":"Premature closure.","cause":"Weak recovery criteria.","solution":"Create explicit completion invariants and verification steps.","alternatives":["Runbook gates","Automated health validation"],"tradeoffs":"Longer verification delays closure but reduces recurrence risk.","constraints":["Business recovery objectives","Telemetry"],"edge_cases":["Slow eventual consistency","Background repair"],"security":"Recovery verification must honor data access restrictions.","performance":"Use targeted health checks rather than expensive full scans where appropriate.","testing":"Exercise recovery and verify every completion gate.","verification":"Record each gate as independently satisfied.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B66-R01"],"prerequisites":["Operational invariants"],"relationships":["D25-B48"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/documentation-overview/fis//"}],"evidence":"AWS FIS documents comparison of observed metrics with expected steady state and reporting of stop-condition results.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B66-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Recovery Knowledge","concept":"Recovery failure taxonomy","knowledge_type":"taxonomy","question":"How should recovery failures be classified for reusable debugging knowledge?","answer":"Classify them into detection failure, authorization failure, action failure, ordering failure, state-consistency failure, resource-exhaustion failure, verification failure, and convergence failure where applicable.","explanation":"Separating failure stages makes recurring recovery defects easier to retrieve and compare.","principle":"Represent recovery failures by the failed control stage, not only the final symptom.","rule":"Allow multiple contributing categories when one recovery defect spans stages.","examples":["Detection succeeds but recovery authorization fails.","Recovery succeeds but verification falsely reports healthy state."],"invalid_examples":["Classifying every recovery issue simply as downtime."],"problem":"Recovery incidents are stored under broad symptom labels.","failure":"Poor retrieval of analogous cases.","cause":"Insufficient failure taxonomy.","solution":"Use stage-aware recovery categories linked to evidence.","alternatives":["Incident category trees","State-machine failure labels"],"tradeoffs":"More precise taxonomies require consistent annotation.","constraints":["Schema stability"],"edge_cases":["Multi-stage failures","Unknown stage"],"security":"Recovery categories can reveal infrastructure weaknesses and should be access-controlled.","performance":"Index stage and failure type.","testing":"Classify synthetic recovery failures and measure consistency.","verification":"Require category evidence from the recovery state transition.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B66-R01"],"prerequisites":["Failure taxonomy"],"relationships":["D25-B40","D25-B49"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"Fault-injection experiment models distinguish injected actions, observed outcomes, and recovery-related behavior.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B67-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Data Lineage","concept":"Debugging through data lineage","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How can data lineage help locate application failures?","answer":"Track the origin, transformations, validation, storage, and consumers of data involved in the failure. This can identify where an incorrect value first entered or changed meaning.","explanation":"Many bugs manifest far from the operation that introduced the invalid data.","principle":"Trace problematic data backward to its earliest unsupported transformation.","rule":"Distinguish source values, derived values, and externally supplied values.","examples":["Tracing an invalid field from API input through normalization to database storage.","Following a corrupted configuration value through generated settings."],"invalid_examples":["Fixing the final calculation without identifying where the invalid input originated."],"problem":"Data errors propagate across layers.","failure":"Root cause remains hidden behind downstream symptoms.","cause":"Missing provenance of transformations.","solution":"Record data lineage at meaningful transformation boundaries.","alternatives":["Value tracing","Audit logs"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed lineage increases instrumentation and storage cost.","constraints":["Privacy","High-volume data"],"edge_cases":["Aggregations","Sampling","Encrypted values"],"security":"Lineage must preserve data-access boundaries and avoid exposing sensitive values.","performance":"Track metadata and hashes instead of raw values where possible.","testing":"Inject known invalid data and verify lineage reconstruction.","verification":"Connect the failure to the earliest evidence-backed invalid transformation.","version":"General","dependencies":["Observability","Data pipelines"],"prerequisites":["Data flow analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B12","D25-B49"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry provides semantic conventions across database, messaging, HTTP, RPC, and other operations useful for cross-layer data-flow correlation.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B67-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Schema Evolution","concept":"Schema-version diagnosis","knowledge_type":"method","question":"Why should schema versions be included in debugging evidence?","answer":"A producer and consumer may interpret the same field differently when schema versions change. Debugging should therefore identify the producer schema, consumer schema, transformation path, and compatibility assumptions.","explanation":"Schema mismatch can appear as parser, validation, serialization, or business-logic failure.","principle":"Treat data contracts as versioned executable assumptions.","rule":"Record schema identity at every boundary where interpretation can change.","examples":["Consumer expects field type A while producer emits type B after a deployment.","A database migration changes nullability assumptions."],"invalid_examples":["Blaming serialization code without checking schema compatibility."],"problem":"Contract changes cross deployment boundaries.","failure":"Runtime incompatibility.","cause":"Uncoordinated schema evolution.","solution":"Include schema identity in diagnostic context and compatibility checks.","alternatives":["Schema registry","Contract testing"],"tradeoffs":"Schema metadata improves diagnosis but increases operational metadata.","constraints":["Legacy systems","Multiple schema formats"],"edge_cases":["Backward-compatible but semantically incompatible changes","Unknown versions"],"security":"Schema metadata should not expose sensitive field definitions unnecessarily.","performance":"Use compact schema identifiers.","testing":"Run compatibility tests across supported producer-consumer version pairs.","verification":"Confirm actual schema versions before assigning root cause.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B67-R01"],"prerequisites":["Serialization","API contracts"],"relationships":["D25-B30","D25-B43"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry provides standardized semantic information for database, messaging, HTTP, and related operations that can contextualize schema failures.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B67-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Transformation Boundaries","concept":"First-invalid-data localization","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_rule","question":"How should the first invalid data state be identified?","answer":"Compare values or normalized representations at successive transformation boundaries and identify the earliest boundary where an expected invariant becomes false.","explanation":"The final invalid value is usually downstream of the actual defect.","principle":"Search backward from symptom to the first invariant violation.","rule":"Do not modify downstream consumers until upstream data validity has been established.","examples":["Input valid, normalization invalid.","Normalized value valid, persistence transformation invalid."],"invalid_examples":["Adding defensive logic only at the final consumer without understanding the origin."],"problem":"Invalid data propagates widely.","failure":"Multiple downstream patches hide one upstream defect.","cause":"Late-stage localization.","solution":"Instrument and compare data contracts at transformation boundaries.","alternatives":["Data lineage","Assertions"],"tradeoffs":"More assertions improve localization but may add runtime cost.","constraints":["Sensitive data","High throughput"],"edge_cases":["Lazy evaluation","Streaming transformations"],"security":"Prefer non-reversible fingerprints for sensitive values.","performance":"Validate critical invariants selectively.","testing":"Use malformed and boundary-value inputs.","verification":"Identify the earliest reproducible invalid state.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B67-R01"],"prerequisites":["Data flow"],"relationships":["D25-B05","D25-B12"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/"}],"evidence":"Standardized telemetry across operation boundaries can support structured reconstruction of data-processing paths.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B67-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Database Lineage","concept":"Persistence boundary diagnosis","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should debugging treat the boundary between in-memory state and persisted state?","answer":"Compare intended write values, serialized values, committed database state, and subsequent read values while accounting for transaction isolation, triggers, defaults, replication, and serialization behavior.","explanation":"The value observed by application code before persistence may differ from the value eventually read back.","principle":"Treat persistence as a transformation boundary, not a transparent storage operation.","rule":"Verify both write intent and committed read reality.","examples":["Application writes a value but database trigger transforms it.","Read replica returns stale state after a successful primary commit."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming database state equals application memory immediately after a write."],"problem":"State differs across persistence boundaries.","failure":"Incorrect attribution to application logic.","cause":"Unverified storage semantics.","solution":"Correlate transaction identifiers, commit state, schema version, and read source.","alternatives":["Database audit logs","Transaction tracing"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed transaction tracing can be expensive.","constraints":["Database engine","Isolation model"],"edge_cases":["Replication lag","Triggers","Deferred constraints"],"security":"Database diagnostics must follow least privilege.","performance":"Use targeted transaction instrumentation.","testing":"Test transaction anomalies and replica lag explicitly.","verification":"Confirm committed state using authoritative source before concluding.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B67-R02"],"prerequisites":["Database transactions"],"relationships":["D25-B18"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry includes database operation semantic conventions that support correlation of database activity with application execution.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B67-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Data Provenance","concept":"Evidence-preserving data fingerprints","knowledge_type":"technique","question":"How can sensitive data be correlated during debugging without storing raw values?","answer":"Use carefully designed deterministic fingerprints, typed metadata, lengths, ranges, structural summaries, or keyed hashes where appropriate, while ensuring the representation cannot be used to reconstruct sensitive values.","explanation":"Debugging often requires proving that two values are identical or changed without exposing the values themselves.","principle":"Preserve diagnostic identity while minimizing sensitive-data exposure.","rule":"Choose fingerprints according to threat model; ordinary hashes are not automatically safe for low-entropy secrets.","examples":["Hashing high-entropy identifiers for correlation.","Recording value type and length for sensitive fields."],"invalid_examples":["Hashing a small PIN space and assuming the raw value is protected."],"problem":"Raw diagnostic values create privacy risk.","failure":"Sensitive data exposure.","cause":"Overcollection.","solution":"Use data minimization and threat-model-aware fingerprints.","alternatives":["Tokenization","Redaction","Structured metadata"],"tradeoffs":"Less data improves privacy but can reduce diagnostic precision.","constraints":["Data entropy","Compliance","Correlation needs"],"edge_cases":["Low-entropy secrets","PII","Binary blobs"],"security":"Apply least-privilege access and encryption to diagnostic artifacts.","performance":"Compact fingerprints reduce telemetry size.","testing":"Attempt correlation and recovery attacks against diagnostic representations.","verification":"Confirm that the chosen representation provides required evidence without unnecessary disclosure.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B67-R01"],"prerequisites":["Security and privacy"],"relationships":["D25-B38"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/"}],"evidence":"Telemetry semantic standards support structured metadata, while sensitive-value protection remains an application-specific security requirement.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B68-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"ABI Boundaries","concept":"ABI-aware debugging","knowledge_type":"method","question":"Why are ABI boundaries important when diagnosing native or mixed-language failures?","answer":"Application Binary Interface assumptions govern calling conventions, data layout, symbol linkage, alignment, exception handling, and binary compatibility. Violations can cause failures far from the source-level mismatch.","explanation":"Source code may appear type-compatible while compiled components disagree about binary representation or calling conventions.","principle":"Verify binary contracts at language and library boundaries.","rule":"Treat ABI compatibility as an explicit diagnostic hypothesis for native interoperability failures.","examples":["Different structure packing assumptions between components.","Calling convention mismatch at an FFI boundary."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming source-level type names guarantee binary compatibility."],"problem":"Mixed-language components interact through binary contracts.","failure":"Memory corruption, crashes, or corrupted values.","cause":"ABI mismatch.","solution":"Compare compiler targets, calling conventions, data layouts, symbol definitions, and binary interfaces.","alternatives":["C-compatible boundary","Serialization boundary"],"tradeoffs":"Stable ABI boundaries may constrain optimization or language features.","constraints":["Compiler","Platform","Architecture"],"edge_cases":["Different compiler ABIs","Struct packing","Exception boundaries"],"security":"ABI corruption can create memory-safety vulnerabilities.","performance":"Avoid unnecessary serialization when ABI is verified and safe.","testing":"Run boundary tests on every supported architecture/toolchain combination.","verification":"Inspect actual binary interface rather than relying only on source declarations.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B26"],"prerequisites":["Native programming","FFI"],"relationships":["D25-B26"],"provenance":[{"source":"Microsoft WinDbg Timelines","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timeline-preview"}],"evidence":"Native debugging tools expose execution and memory events useful for investigating binary-level failures; ABI diagnosis requires platform-specific ABI documentation.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B68-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Runtime Boundaries","concept":"Runtime identity verification","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"Why must runtime identity be captured for mixed-language debugging?","answer":"Different runtime libraries, standard libraries, virtual machines, interpreters, or native loaders can alter behavior even when application code is identical. Debugging should record actual runtime versions and loaded components.","explanation":"Runtime behavior is part of the executed program environment.","principle":"Identify every runtime component that can influence execution.","rule":"Do not attribute a runtime failure solely to application code until runtime identity is established.","examples":["Different Python interpreter builds.","Different JVM or native runtime library versions."],"invalid_examples":["Comparing source versions while ignoring runtime version differences."],"problem":"Runtime dependencies vary across environments.","failure":"Environment-specific failure remains unexplained.","cause":"Incomplete execution identity.","solution":"Capture runtime fingerprints and loaded module identities.","alternatives":["Container image digest","Environment manifest"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed runtime inventories increase metadata volume.","constraints":["Dynamic loading","Plugin systems"],"edge_cases":["Side-by-side runtimes","Hot-loaded libraries"],"security":"Runtime inventories can reveal attack surface and should be access-controlled.","performance":"Capture inventories at process start rather than continuously where possible.","testing":"Verify runtime manifests in CI and deployment.","verification":"Compare actual loaded modules with expected manifest.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B64-R04"],"prerequisites":["Runtime systems"],"relationships":["D25-B27","D25-B28"],"provenance":[{"source":"Linux perf manual","url":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/perf.1.html"}],"evidence":"Linux perf tooling operates across kernel, hardware, and software execution layers, illustrating the importance of actual runtime execution context.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B68-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Loader Failures","concept":"Dynamic loader diagnosis","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"What should be checked when a native program fails during library loading?","answer":"Inspect executable architecture, required shared libraries, search paths, symbol availability, loader configuration, dependency versions, and actual loaded modules.","explanation":"Loader failures can originate from missing files, incompatible architecture, symbol-version mismatch, search-path errors, or conflicting runtime libraries.","principle":"Diagnose the complete binary dependency graph rather than the top-level missing-library message alone.","rule":"Verify actual dependency resolution in the failing environment.","examples":["Required symbol exists in one library version but not another.","32-bit process attempts to load an incompatible 64-bit library."],"invalid_examples":["Installing an arbitrary library with the same filename and assuming compatibility."],"problem":"Native applications fail before normal application debugging begins.","failure":"Startup crash or loader error.","cause":"Dependency resolution mismatch.","solution":"Inspect architecture, dependency graph, symbols, and loader search order.","alternatives":["Static linking","Containerized runtime"],"tradeoffs":"Static linking reduces runtime dependency ambiguity but increases binary size and update coupling.","constraints":["Operating system","Architecture"],"edge_cases":["RPATH/RUNPATH differences","Symbol versioning","Plugin loading"],"security":"Avoid insecure library search paths that permit unintended code loading.","performance":"Minimize unnecessary runtime dependencies.","testing":"Test startup in clean environments matching deployment.","verification":"Confirm resolved libraries and symbols are the intended versions.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B28"],"prerequisites":["Operating systems","Dynamic linking"],"relationships":["D25-B68-R02"],"provenance":[{"source":"Linux perf manual","url":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/perf.1.html"}],"evidence":"Linux performance tooling operates with kernel and loaded-module information, providing useful context for native runtime diagnosis.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B68-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Cross-Language Interoperability","concept":"Boundary contract testing","knowledge_type":"testing_method","question":"How can mixed-language debugging defects be prevented?","answer":"Create explicit boundary tests covering data representation, ownership, lifetime, error propagation, encoding, calling conventions, and supported platform combinations.","explanation":"Boundary defects often evade unit tests because each component is correct in isolation.","principle":"Test the contract where independent execution models meet.","rule":"Test both success and failure semantics at every foreign-function boundary.","examples":["Passing null, empty, oversized, and malformed structures through FFI.","Testing ownership transfer and release exactly once."],"invalid_examples":["Testing only ordinary valid inputs within each language independently."],"problem":"Component-level tests miss interoperability failures.","failure":"Production-only crashes or corruption.","cause":"Boundary assumptions not tested.","solution":"Build contract tests around the binary interface.","alternatives":["Serialization boundary","Generated bindings"],"tradeoffs":"Boundary tests increase matrix size but reduce integration uncertainty.","constraints":["Platform matrix","Toolchain versions"],"edge_cases":["Exception crossing language boundary","Threading runtime differences"],"security":"Treat boundary validation as a security boundary for memory and trust.","performance":"Keep comprehensive tests in CI while using smoke checks per build.","testing":"Run on every supported architecture and compiler/runtime combination.","verification":"Compare actual binary behavior with documented contract.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B68-R01"],"prerequisites":["FFI","Testing"],"relationships":["D25-B26","D25-B48"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/"}],"evidence":"Standardized cross-language telemetry demonstrates the need for consistent semantic interpretation across polyglot systems.","confidence":0.93,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B68-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Native Boundary Evidence","concept":"Memory-boundary evidence preservation","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_rule","question":"What evidence is most useful when a mixed-language boundary corrupts memory?","answer":"Preserve stack traces, allocation and ownership information, boundary arguments, structure sizes, alignment assumptions, loaded binary identities, and the earliest detected invalid memory operation.","explanation":"The final crash location is frequently downstream from the actual corruption.","principle":"Locate the first invalid boundary operation rather than the eventual crash.","rule":"Prefer memory-safety diagnostics that identify the first invalid access or ownership violation.","examples":["Structure size mismatch detected at FFI call.","Invalid free traced to ownership crossing the boundary."],"invalid_examples":["Patching the final crash site without establishing where corruption began."],"problem":"Memory corruption propagates unpredictably.","failure":"Crash appears unrelated to original defect.","cause":"Earlier boundary violation.","solution":"Combine binary identity, boundary metadata, and memory diagnostics.","alternatives":["Sanitizers","Guard pages","Allocation tracing"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed memory diagnostics can impose substantial overhead.","constraints":["Native runtime","Debugger support"],"edge_cases":["Heap corruption","Use-after-free","Stack corruption"],"security":"Memory corruption may indicate exploitable vulnerability; preserve evidence securely.","performance":"Use diagnostic builds for intensive memory instrumentation.","testing":"Run boundary tests under memory instrumentation.","verification":"Identify the earliest invalid operation with reproducible evidence.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B68-R01"],"prerequisites":["Memory debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B13","D25-B24"],"provenance":[{"source":"Microsoft WinDbg Timelines","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timeline-preview"}],"evidence":"WinDbg timelines include memory-access events that can support temporal investigation of native failures.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B69-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Large-Scale Diagnosis","concept":"Diagnostic sampling strategy","knowledge_type":"strategy","question":"How should debugging systems prioritize evidence in very high-volume systems?","answer":"Use targeted sampling that preserves rare failures, error-associated traces, representative normal behavior, and enough contextual metadata to distinguish missing evidence from non-occurrence.","explanation":"Uniform sampling can miss rare but important failures. Diagnostic sampling should be driven by risk and information value.","principle":"Optimize evidence collection for diagnostic value rather than volume alone.","rule":"Retain sufficient context to interpret every sampled artifact.","examples":["Increase trace retention for errors.","Capture complete traces for selected reproduction requests."],"invalid_examples":["Uniformly discarding most error context because traffic volume is high."],"problem":"Full-fidelity telemetry is too expensive at scale.","failure":"Critical evidence is absent.","cause":"Sampling policy ignores diagnostic importance.","solution":"Use adaptive or targeted sampling with explicit metadata.","alternatives":["Tail sampling","Trigger-based capture"],"tradeoffs":"Targeted capture improves diagnosis but requires policy complexity.","constraints":["Storage","Privacy","Latency"],"edge_cases":["Bursty incidents","Rare errors","High-cardinality dimensions"],"security":"Ensure security-critical events are not systematically excluded by sampling.","performance":"Use lightweight trigger evaluation before expensive capture.","testing":"Simulate rare failures under realistic traffic volumes.","verification":"Measure evidence retention for known failure classes.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B62-R04"],"prerequisites":["Observability at scale"],"relationships":["D25-B20","D25-B38"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Dapper explicitly discusses sampling as a mechanism for large-scale tracing with acceptable overhead.","confidence":0.99,"validation":"Source-grounded semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B69-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Diagnostic Prioritization","concept":"Evidence value prioritization","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should a debugging system prioritize large numbers of diagnostic signals?","answer":"Prioritize signals according to relevance to the active hypothesis, temporal proximity with causal evidence, uniqueness, severity, reproducibility, and ability to distinguish competing explanations.","explanation":"Volume alone is not evidence quality. A rare but highly discriminating event can be more valuable than thousands of correlated logs.","principle":"Prioritize information gain rather than signal count.","rule":"Do not rank evidence solely by frequency or severity.","examples":["A unique invariant violation outranks repetitive warning logs.","A trace boundary failure can outrank thousands of downstream timeout records."],"invalid_examples":["Selecting the most frequent log as the root cause."],"problem":"Large telemetry volumes overwhelm investigators.","failure":"Important evidence is buried.","cause":"Frequency-based prioritization.","solution":"Use evidence scoring tied to hypotheses and causal relevance.","alternatives":["Rule-based ranking","Information-gain heuristics"],"tradeoffs":"Ranking requires context and may introduce bias if poorly designed.","constraints":["Incomplete evidence","Unknown failure modes"],"edge_cases":["Novel incidents","Conflicting evidence"],"security":"Do not allow sensitive telemetry to receive priority merely because it is detailed.","performance":"Precompute indexes for high-value evidence classes.","testing":"Evaluate ranking against known diagnostic cases.","verification":"Ensure ranking never converts prioritization into causal certainty.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B41","D25-B62"],"prerequisites":["Evidence reasoning"],"relationships":["D25-B49"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Dapper discusses selective instrumentation and sampling to make large-scale tracing useful while controlling overhead.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B69-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"High-Cardinality Diagnosis","concept":"Cardinality-aware debugging","knowledge_type":"constraint","question":"Why can high-cardinality diagnostic dimensions become problematic?","answer":"Identifiers such as user IDs, request IDs, or dynamic resource names can create huge indexes and storage requirements. Debugging systems should preserve identifiers needed for causal reconstruction while controlling aggregation and retention costs.","explanation":"High-cardinality data can be diagnostically valuable but operationally expensive.","principle":"Separate correlation identity from unrestricted aggregation dimensions.","rule":"Do not discard correlation identifiers merely because they are high cardinality; instead design storage and indexing around their role.","examples":["Trace ID retained for exact investigation while aggregate metrics use service-level dimensions.","Per-request details stored only for triggered failures."],"invalid_examples":["Removing trace identifiers globally because they increase cardinality."],"problem":"Diagnostic systems must scale without destroying correlation.","failure":"Either storage overload or loss of investigative identity.","cause":"Poor dimension design.","solution":"Use tiered retention and purpose-specific indexes.","alternatives":["On-demand capture","Sampled detail"],"tradeoffs":"Tiering balances fidelity and cost.","constraints":["Storage","Query engine"],"edge_cases":["Incident bursts","Tenant-specific identifiers"],"security":"High-cardinality identifiers may be sensitive and require access controls.","performance":"Index only fields required for common diagnostic queries.","testing":"Load-test telemetry ingestion with realistic cardinality.","verification":"Confirm that important failure traces remain retrievable under peak volume.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B69-R01"],"prerequisites":["Observability architecture"],"relationships":["D25-B38"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/semantic-conventions/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry semantic conventions standardize telemetry attributes, providing a structured basis for deciding which attributes are indexed or correlated.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B69-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Performance-Aware Diagnosis","concept":"Profiling-assisted debugging","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How can profiling complement traditional debugging?","answer":"Profiling can identify resource-consuming execution paths, contention, CPU hotspots, memory behavior, and timing relationships that help narrow performance-related failure hypotheses.","explanation":"Profiling identifies where resources are spent; it does not automatically establish that the hotspot is a defect.","principle":"Use profiles to localize expensive behavior, then validate causal impact separately.","rule":"Do not equate high resource consumption with incorrect behavior without additional evidence.","examples":["CPU profile identifies a parser loop during latency regression.","Lock profiling identifies contention during throughput collapse."],"invalid_examples":["Optimizing the hottest function without proving it contributes to the observed failure."],"problem":"Performance incidents have large search spaces.","failure":"Optimization targets the wrong component.","cause":"Profile interpreted as root-cause proof.","solution":"Combine profile evidence with traces, invariants, and controlled experiments.","alternatives":["Sampling profilers","Tracing","Hardware counters"],"tradeoffs":"Profiling overhead varies by technique and fidelity.","constraints":["Runtime","Platform","Production overhead"],"edge_cases":["Sampling bias","JIT compilation","Short-lived processes"],"security":"Profile data can expose sensitive execution paths and identifiers.","performance":"Prefer low-overhead sampling in production.","testing":"Compare profile signatures before and after controlled changes.","verification":"Validate hotspot relevance against the incident symptom.","version":"Linux perf","dependencies":["D25-B20","D25-B69-R02"],"prerequisites":["Performance analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B20"],"provenance":[{"source":"Linux perf manual","url":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/perf.1.html"}],"evidence":"The Linux perf tool provides performance analysis across hardware and software counters, tracepoints, and related mechanisms.","confidence":0.99,"validation":"Source-grounded semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B69-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Scale-Aware Root Cause","concept":"Root-cause aggregation without evidence loss","knowledge_type":"knowledge_model","question":"How can many similar failures be aggregated without losing individual diagnostic evidence?","answer":"Group failures using stable signatures or normalized causal features while retaining links to representative and raw artifacts. Aggregation should be reversible from summary to evidence.","explanation":"Large incidents often generate thousands of manifestations of one defect, but some instances may contain unique evidence.","principle":"Compress repeated symptoms while preserving drill-down paths to original evidence.","rule":"Never replace raw evidence with an irreversible aggregate when individual artifacts may contain unique causal information.","examples":["Grouping crashes by normalized stack signature while retaining individual traces.","Grouping identical timeout signatures while preserving affected request identities under access controls."],"invalid_examples":["Deleting all individual events after computing a frequency count."],"problem":"Incident volume becomes too large for manual inspection.","failure":"Unique evidence is lost during deduplication.","cause":"Irreversible aggregation.","solution":"Use hierarchical evidence storage: aggregate signature → cluster → raw artifacts.","alternatives":["Representative sampling","Case clustering"],"tradeoffs":"Retention costs increase but forensic capability remains.","constraints":["Storage","Privacy","Retention policy"],"edge_cases":["Multiple causes sharing one stack","One cause producing many signatures"],"security":"Apply access control at every evidence layer.","performance":"Index signatures for fast cluster retrieval.","testing":"Generate mixed-cause failure populations and verify clusters retain discriminating evidence.","verification":"Ensure every aggregate can be traced to underlying artifacts.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B69-R01","D25-B49"],"prerequisites":["Deduplication","Evidence storage"],"relationships":["D25-B38","D25-B39"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Large-scale tracing systems rely on structured identifiers and selective collection to make large execution populations diagnosable.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B70-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"End-to-End Diagnosis","concept":"Evidence-chain debugging","knowledge_type":"workflow","question":"What is an evidence-chain debugging workflow?","answer":"Collect observations, normalize artifacts, establish execution identity, reconstruct causal relationships, generate competing hypotheses, perform discriminating tests, identify the earliest consequential divergence, validate a repair, and preserve the resulting case with provenance.","explanation":"The workflow prevents debugging from jumping directly from symptom to patch.","principle":"Every major conclusion should be traceable through an evidence chain.","rule":"Do not close a debugging case while material causal uncertainty remains undocumented.","examples":["Trace → state snapshot → invariant violation → controlled reproduction → patch → regression verification.","Crash dump → binary identity → first invalid access → minimal fix → replay verification."],"invalid_examples":["Log line → guessed cause → code patch without reproduction or verification."],"problem":"Debugging often becomes an informal narrative.","failure":"Unverifiable root-cause claims and fragile fixes.","cause":"Missing evidence chain.","solution":"Represent diagnosis as linked evidence, hypotheses, experiments, and verification records.","alternatives":["Incident workflow","Scientific debugging"],"tradeoffs":"Structured workflows require discipline but improve repeatability.","constraints":["Evidence availability","Tool support"],"edge_cases":["Incomplete production evidence","Irreproducible failures"],"security":"Protect diagnostic artifacts and restrict mutating actions.","performance":"Automate evidence collection and correlation where safe.","testing":"Run the workflow against representative failure classes.","verification":"Require explicit links from symptom to cause and from fix to verification.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B41","D25-B49","D25-B64"],"prerequisites":["Debugging fundamentals"],"relationships":["D25-B01","D25-B50"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"},{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"Distributed tracing and standardized telemetry provide infrastructure for evidence collection and cross-layer correlation.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic and structural review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B70-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Cross-Layer Synthesis","concept":"Cross-layer causal synthesis","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should a debugger synthesize code, runtime, infrastructure, and data evidence?","answer":"Create a layered model in which each observation retains its source and semantics, then connect layers only through explicit interfaces such as calls, data transformations, network boundaries, resource dependencies, or deployment relationships.","explanation":"Cross-layer diagnosis fails when evidence from different abstraction levels is treated as interchangeable.","principle":"Connect abstraction layers through explicit contracts.","rule":"Never use an infrastructure symptom as direct proof of a source-level defect without intermediate evidence.","examples":["CPU saturation → trace latency → lock contention → specific code path.","Schema mismatch → deserialization failure → application state violation."],"invalid_examples":["High CPU → buggy function without profile or trace evidence."],"problem":"Modern failures cross multiple system layers.","failure":"Root cause assigned at the wrong abstraction level.","cause":"Layer collapse.","solution":"Maintain a layered causal graph with provenance at every edge.","alternatives":["Dependency graph","Distributed trace graph"],"tradeoffs":"Layered models improve precision but require more metadata.","constraints":["Observability coverage","Complex architectures"],"edge_cases":["Emergent failures","Feedback loops"],"security":"Respect authorization boundaries across infrastructure and application evidence.","performance":"Materialize only relevant subgraphs during diagnosis.","testing":"Use cross-layer synthetic incidents with known causal chains.","verification":"Validate each cross-layer transition independently.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B61-R02","D25-B67-R01"],"prerequisites":["Systems architecture","Causal reasoning"],"relationships":["D25-B40","D25-B50"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/"},{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry standardizes cross-layer telemetry semantics while Dapper demonstrates distributed execution tracing across service boundaries.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B70-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Uncertainty Management","concept":"Unknown-state preservation","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"Why should advanced debugging systems preserve unknown states instead of forcing classifications?","answer":"Incomplete evidence is common in real systems. Explicitly representing unknown, ambiguous, conflicting, or unobserved states prevents unsupported assumptions from becoming persistent knowledge.","explanation":"Forcing every event into a known category creates false certainty and contaminates future retrieval.","principle":"Unknown is a valid diagnostic state.","rule":"Never infer a missing fact merely because the schema requires a value.","examples":["Trace parent unknown because context was absent.","Root cause unresolved because reproduction is unavailable."],"invalid_examples":["Filling missing root cause with the most probable component and storing it as fact."],"problem":"Diagnostic systems pressure investigators toward premature closure.","failure":"False knowledge becomes training data.","cause":"Missing uncertainty representation.","solution":"Use explicit epistemic states and evidence references.","alternatives":["Confidence scores","Human review states"],"tradeoffs":"Unknown states reduce apparent completeness but improve correctness.","constraints":["Schema design","Retrieval behavior"],"edge_cases":["Conflicting authoritative sources","Partial evidence"],"security":"Uncertainty metadata should not expose sensitive investigation details beyond authorization.","performance":"Represent uncertainty with compact enums and evidence references.","testing":"Inject incomplete cases and verify that unknown remains unknown.","verification":"Require evidence before transitioning unknown to established fact.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B41","D25-B49"],"prerequisites":["Epistemic modeling"],"relationships":["D25-B40","D25-B50"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"Telemetry specifications define structured data semantics but do not imply that absence of telemetry is evidence of absence.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B70-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Knowledge Compaction","concept":"Provenance-preserving debugging summaries","knowledge_type":"knowledge_model","question":"How can debugging cases be compressed without destroying provenance?","answer":"Create derived summaries that reference immutable underlying observations, hypotheses, experiments, and verification artifacts. A summary should be a view over evidence rather than a replacement for it.","explanation":"Knowledge systems need compact retrieval representations while preserving auditability and contradiction handling.","principle":"Compress representation, not evidence lineage.","rule":"Every derived debugging conclusion must retain links to supporting and contradicting records.","examples":["Summary states probable race while linking scheduler trace and reproduction results.","Repair summary links patch, failing test, regression suite, and review state."],"invalid_examples":["Replacing all raw evidence with a free-text incident summary."],"problem":"Large debugging histories are difficult to retrieve efficiently.","failure":"Loss of provenance and contradiction context.","cause":"Destructive summarization.","solution":"Store summaries as derived records with stable evidence references.","alternatives":["Knowledge graphs","Case indexes"],"tradeoffs":"Reference graphs increase storage relationships but preserve auditability.","constraints":["Stable IDs","Retention"],"edge_cases":["Deleted source artifacts","Versioned summaries"],"security":"Inherited access control must prevent summaries from bypassing source permissions.","performance":"Index summaries separately from raw evidence.","testing":"Delete or alter source artifacts in a test environment and verify broken references are detectable.","verification":"Check every summary claim against its referenced evidence.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B49","D25-B70-R03"],"prerequisites":["Knowledge representation","Provenance"],"relationships":["D25-B49","D25-B50"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/"}],"evidence":"Structured semantic telemetry enables derived representations while retaining source identifiers and context.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Semantic review completed; execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B70-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Advanced Debugging","subtopic":"Autonomous Debugging Readiness","concept":"Evidence-gated autonomous diagnosis","knowledge_type":"architecture_rule","question":"What conditions should an autonomous debugging system satisfy before recommending or applying a repair?","answer":"It should establish execution identity, distinguish observations from hypotheses, preserve competing explanations, gather sufficient evidence, use bounded diagnostic actions, validate the proposed repair against the original failure, run regression verification, and retain provenance.","explanation":"Automation can accelerate debugging but can also amplify unsupported assumptions. Evidence gates prevent a plausible hypothesis from becoming an unverified mutation.","principle":"Autonomy must increase investigation speed without weakening epistemic or safety guarantees.","rule":"Never allow an autonomous system to convert an unverified hypothesis directly into an irreversible production change.","examples":["Agent gathers traces, proposes two causes, reproduces one, creates a minimal patch, runs regression tests, and requests approval before deployment.","Agent stops when evidence is insufficient and escalates with a structured case."],"invalid_examples":["Agent sees an exception, edits production code, and declares success because the exception disappears."],"problem":"AI-assisted debugging can generate plausible but unsupported conclusions.","failure":"Incorrect automated repair or destructive mutation.","cause":"Missing evidence and authorization gates.","solution":"Separate observation, inference, action, verification, and approval states.","alternatives":["Human-in-the-loop debugging","Read-only diagnostic agent"],"tradeoffs":"Gates reduce automation speed but materially improve safety and auditability.","constraints":["Tool permissions","Reproduction availability","Production policies"],"edge_cases":["Non-reproducible failures","Conflicting evidence","Emergency procedures"],"security":"Use least privilege, explicit authorization, immutable audit records, and rollback controls.","performance":"Automate low-risk evidence collection while reserving expensive actions for high-value hypotheses.","testing":"Evaluate agents against seeded failures, misleading symptoms, incomplete telemetry, and conflicting evidence.","verification":"Require reproducible failure resolution plus regression evidence before accepting a repair.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25-B41","D25-B42","D25-B48","D25-B49"],"prerequisites":["Debugging","Agent safety","Testing","Provenance"],"relationships":["D25-B50","D25-B70-R01","D25-B70-R03"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"},{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"},{"source":"Microsoft WinDbg Time Travel Debugging","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timetravel-preview"}],"evidence":"The cited specifications and tools establish structured telemetry, controlled failure experimentation, and recorded execution as mechanisms for evidence-driven debugging; the autonomous governance rule is an engineering synthesis rather than a claim that these sources define autonomous agents.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Semantic, provenance, relationship, and schema consistency reviewed; no runtime execution or benchmark performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B71-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Configuration and Dependency Drift","concept":"Configuration drift as a causal variable","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"How should configuration drift be treated during root-cause analysis?","answer":"Configuration drift should be treated as an explicit causal variable rather than incidental environmental noise. The investigation should identify the effective configuration at the failing execution boundary, compare it with a known-good baseline, determine which differences can influence the observed behavior, and preserve the configuration evidence used for the conclusion.","explanation":"A nominal configuration file is not necessarily the effective runtime configuration because values may be overridden by environment variables, deployment parameters, feature flags, secrets, defaults, generated files, or dependency-specific settings. Debugging therefore requires reconstruction of the configuration actually consumed by the failing component.","principle":"Debug the effective configuration, not merely the intended configuration.","rule":"A configuration difference should not be declared causal until its relevance to the observed failure is established.","examples":["Compare effective runtime configuration between a failing instance and a known-good instance.","Trace precedence when multiple configuration sources define the same setting.","Record configuration version or content identity with diagnostic evidence."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming a configuration difference caused the failure solely because it exists.","Comparing only repository configuration while ignoring deployment-time overrides."],"problem":"A service behaves differently across environments even though the repository version is identical.","failure":"The investigation attributes the discrepancy to application code without examining effective runtime configuration.","cause":"Configuration provenance and precedence were not reconstructed.","solution":"Capture effective configuration metadata, identify relevant differences, reproduce the failing configuration, and test whether removing the difference changes the failure.","alternatives":["Environment snapshot comparison","Configuration management diffing","Reproduction using an isolated configuration fixture"],"tradeoffs":"Capturing complete configuration improves diagnosability but may expose secrets; sensitive values must be redacted while preserving identity or change information.","constraints":["Do not expose secrets in debugging artifacts.","Configuration comparison must distinguish semantic differences from formatting differences."],"edge_cases":["Generated configuration","Environment-variable overrides","Secret-backed configuration","Configuration inherited through orchestration systems"],"security":"Redact secret values and credentials while retaining safe identifiers, hashes, or metadata necessary for comparison.","performance":"Configuration capture should be lightweight enough for production diagnostics and should avoid repeatedly serializing large secret-bearing structures.","testing":"Test precedence rules, default handling, override behavior, and equivalent configurations.","verification":"Verify that the suspected configuration difference reproduces or removes the failure under controlled conditions.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Configuration source inventory","Runtime environment metadata"],"prerequisites":["Configuration precedence knowledge","Environment reconstruction"],"relationships":["D25-B70-R01","D25-B43-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/","relevance":"Provides standardized observability attributes that can support consistent environment and deployment metadata."}],"evidence":"Runtime behavior depends on effective values consumed by the application; therefore configuration reconstruction is required for causal debugging.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B71-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Configuration and Dependency Drift","concept":"Dependency graph drift","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How can dependency drift be localized as a source of a regression?","answer":"Compare the resolved dependency graph, not only declared dependency files, between failing and known-good environments. Identify changed direct and transitive dependencies, determine whether behaviorally relevant interfaces changed, and isolate the smallest dependency difference that can reproduce the failure.","explanation":"Lock files, package managers, operating-system packages, native libraries, and transitive dependencies can change behavior without changes to application source code. Root-cause analysis should therefore reconstruct the actual resolved dependency set.","principle":"Debug the resolved dependency graph rather than the declared dependency intent.","rule":"A dependency version change becomes a credible root-cause candidate only after behavioral relevance or reproducibility is established.","examples":["Compare lockfile resolution across releases.","Compare native library versions used by an extension.","Reproduce the application with the previous resolved dependency graph."],"invalid_examples":["Blaming the newest dependency merely because the failure appeared after an upgrade.","Comparing only top-level package declarations."],"problem":"A previously stable application begins failing after deployment.","failure":"The investigation overlooks a transitive dependency change.","cause":"The resolved dependency graph was not captured.","solution":"Capture dependency manifests and resolution metadata, compute graph differences, and perform controlled rollback or substitution experiments.","alternatives":["Dependency lockfile comparison","Container image comparison","Hermetic build reproduction"],"tradeoffs":"Full dependency capture improves reproducibility but increases metadata volume and maintenance requirements.","constraints":["Dependency metadata must correspond to the actual runtime artifact.","Native and system dependencies may exist outside the language package manager."],"edge_cases":["Platform-specific dependency resolution","Optional dependencies","Native extensions","Post-install generated artifacts"],"security":"Dependency provenance should be preserved to support supply-chain investigation without executing untrusted packages merely for diagnosis.","performance":"Dependency graph comparison should be performed from metadata whenever possible rather than repeatedly rebuilding environments.","testing":"Test dependency resolution deterministically across supported platforms and lockfile states.","verification":"Verify the suspected dependency difference through controlled environment comparison or reproduction.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Dependency manifests","Resolved package metadata"],"prerequisites":["Dependency graph reconstruction","Reproducible environment techniques"],"relationships":["D25-B71-R01","D25-B27-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"Python Packaging User Guide","url":"https://packaging.python.org/","relevance":"Provides authoritative guidance for Python packaging and dependency management concepts."}],"evidence":"Declared dependencies can differ from the resolved runtime dependency graph, making resolution metadata essential to reproducible diagnosis.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B71-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Configuration and Dependency Drift","concept":"Artifact identity mismatch","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"Why should the deployed artifact identity be verified before debugging source code?","answer":"The deployed artifact must be identified and compared with the source revision believed to be running. Debugging the wrong artifact can produce apparently contradictory evidence because source, binaries, generated assets, configuration, or deployment layers may not correspond to the analyzed revision.","explanation":"A source-control commit does not by itself prove that a particular runtime instance executes code derived from that commit. Artifact hashes, build identifiers, image digests, package versions, and deployment metadata provide stronger identity evidence.","principle":"Establish execution artifact identity before attributing runtime behavior to source.","rule":"Do not treat source-level hypotheses as authoritative until the analyzed runtime artifact has been linked to the relevant source revision.","examples":["Compare deployed image digest with release metadata.","Verify binary build identifiers against the expected commit.","Record artifact identity in diagnostic events."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming the latest commit is deployed.","Debugging local source while production executes an older artifact."],"problem":"A reported production failure cannot be reproduced from the current source tree.","failure":"Investigators modify current source without confirming which artifact produced the failure.","cause":"Artifact-to-source identity was not established.","solution":"Collect runtime artifact identity and map it to an immutable source/build record before continuing causal analysis.","alternatives":["Binary provenance mapping","Container digest verification","Build manifest inspection"],"tradeoffs":"Strong artifact identity improves reproducibility but requires build and deployment metadata discipline.","constraints":["Identity metadata must be immutable or independently verifiable.","Generated artifacts may require separate provenance records."],"edge_cases":["Hotfixes","Partial deployments","Canary instances","Runtime-generated code"],"security":"Artifact identity protects against debugging an unintended or tampered artifact and supports supply-chain investigation.","performance":"Identity checks should use lightweight immutable identifiers such as digests or build IDs.","testing":"Test that deployment systems expose and preserve artifact identity through runtime diagnostics.","verification":"Verify the runtime artifact against the expected immutable build record.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Build metadata","Deployment metadata","Source-control revision"],"prerequisites":["Reproducible builds or artifact provenance"],"relationships":["D25-B71-R02","D25-B43-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"SLSA","url":"https://slsa.dev/","relevance":"Defines concepts for software supply-chain provenance and artifact integrity."}],"evidence":"Artifact provenance is necessary to establish correspondence between deployed software and the source or build used for debugging.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B71-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Configuration and Dependency Drift","concept":"Feature-flag state reconstruction","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should feature-flag state be reconstructed during debugging?","answer":"The investigation should capture the evaluated flag state and evaluation context at the time of failure. This includes relevant flag identifiers, selected variants, targeting context, evaluation source, and version or revision information where available.","explanation":"Feature flags can create multiple behavioral paths from the same application artifact. A source-code comparison alone cannot explain a failure when different users, tenants, regions, or instances receive different flag decisions.","principle":"Feature-flag evaluation is part of runtime state and must be included in causal reconstruction.","rule":"A flag should be considered a causal variable only when its evaluated state is known for the failing execution.","examples":["Record a safe flag-state fingerprint with an error event.","Compare flag decisions between failing and successful requests.","Reproduce a failure using the exact flag context."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming the global flag state applies to every request.","Changing a production flag during diagnosis without preserving the original state."],"problem":"Only a subset of users experience a defect after a release.","failure":"The investigation treats the application version as the only differentiator.","cause":"Different feature-flag evaluations create distinct execution paths.","solution":"Capture and compare evaluated flag states and targeting contexts.","alternatives":["Static configuration comparison","Request-context replay","Flag evaluation audit logs"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed flag diagnostics improve causality but can increase telemetry volume and privacy concerns.","constraints":["Do not capture sensitive targeting attributes unnecessarily.","Flag state must correspond to the exact execution window."],"edge_cases":["Gradual rollout","Tenant-specific flags","Experiment variants","Stale local flag caches"],"security":"Avoid exposing sensitive targeting attributes; use stable pseudonymous identifiers where sufficient.","performance":"Prefer compact flag fingerprints over serializing complete flag configurations into every event.","testing":"Test every supported flag variant and evaluation path.","verification":"Verify that reproducing the recorded flag state reproduces the observed behavior.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Feature-flag system","Request context"],"prerequisites":["Runtime state capture"],"relationships":["D25-B71-R01","D25-B67-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenFeature","url":"https://openfeature.dev/","relevance":"Provides an open specification and ecosystem for feature-flag evaluation concepts."}],"evidence":"Feature flags alter runtime control flow and therefore can materially affect debugging conclusions.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B71-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Configuration and Dependency Drift","concept":"Environment normalization","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"What is environment normalization in differential debugging?","answer":"Environment normalization is the process of controlling or explicitly recording environmental variables that can influence behavior so that differences between runs are attributable to a smaller set of candidate causes.","explanation":"Operating-system behavior, locale, timezone, CPU features, environment variables, filesystem layout, network configuration, dependency versions, and resource limits can all create divergent outcomes. Normalization does not mean making environments identical in every respect; it means controlling relevant variables and documenting unavoidable differences.","principle":"Reduce uncontrolled environmental degrees of freedom before interpreting execution differences.","rule":"Any environmental variable capable of materially changing the observed behavior must either be normalized, measured, or explicitly excluded with justification.","examples":["Pin timezone and locale for deterministic tests.","Record CPU architecture and runtime version.","Compare resource limits between successful and failing runs."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming two containers are equivalent because their application images match.","Ignoring host-level differences when native behavior is involved."],"problem":"A failure occurs only in one deployment environment.","failure":"Investigators compare application code without controlling relevant environmental variables.","cause":"Environmental degrees of freedom remain uncontrolled.","solution":"Inventory, classify, normalize, or record environment variables and repeat the comparison.","alternatives":["Hermetic execution","Containerized reproduction","Environment fingerprinting"],"tradeoffs":"Greater normalization improves reproducibility but may hide environment-specific defects if overused.","constraints":["Environment-specific behavior may itself be the defect under investigation.","Security-sensitive environment information may require redaction."],"edge_cases":["Hardware-dependent behavior","Kernel differences","Timezone-sensitive logic","Resource quota differences"],"security":"Do not expose credentials or sensitive infrastructure identifiers merely to construct an environment fingerprint.","performance":"Use compact fingerprints for repeated environment comparisons.","testing":"Run the same diagnostic scenario under controlled and intentionally varied environments.","verification":"Verify that normalization reduces unexplained divergence without eliminating the behavior that needs investigation.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Environment metadata","Execution artifact identity"],"prerequisites":["Differential debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B71-R03","D25-B64-R04"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"Reproducible Builds","url":"https://reproducible-builds.org/","relevance":"Provides principles for reproducibility and controlling environmental variation in software production."}],"evidence":"Controlled environmental variables reduce ambiguity when comparing successful and failing executions.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B72-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Deployment Topology Diagnosis","concept":"Topology-sensitive failure localization","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How can deployment topology be used to localize failures?","answer":"Map the failing request or workload through the actual deployment topology and identify where behavior diverges between successful and failing paths. Compare routing, service instances, zones, nodes, proxies, caches, and dependencies rather than treating the application as a single homogeneous component.","explanation":"A logically identical service can behave differently because requests traverse different infrastructure paths or instances. Topology-aware debugging narrows the fault domain by associating failures with concrete execution paths.","principle":"The physical and logical execution path is part of the failure context.","rule":"When failure frequency correlates with topology, topology must become an explicit debugging dimension.","examples":["Compare failures by availability zone.","Map failing requests to specific service instances.","Compare proxy paths for successful and failed requests."],"invalid_examples":["Aggregating all service instances into one failure rate.","Assuming all replicas have identical runtime state."],"problem":"Failures occur only for requests routed through a subset of infrastructure.","failure":"The service is debugged globally and the affected topology segment is missed.","cause":"Topology information was discarded from diagnostic evidence.","solution":"Correlate failures with deployment and routing topology, then compare affected and unaffected paths.","alternatives":["Instance-level telemetry","Zone-level comparison","Network-path tracing"],"tradeoffs":"Fine-grained topology improves localization but increases cardinality and metadata management requirements.","constraints":["Topology identifiers should remain stable enough for correlation.","Sensitive infrastructure details may need controlled access."],"edge_cases":["Autoscaling","Ephemeral instances","Service mesh routing","Multi-region deployments"],"security":"Restrict infrastructure topology data according to operational sensitivity.","performance":"Prefer bounded topology dimensions to avoid unmanageable diagnostic cardinality.","testing":"Inject controlled topology differences and verify diagnostic correlation.","verification":"Verify that the suspected topology segment consistently correlates with the failure before assigning causality.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Deployment metadata","Request correlation"],"prerequisites":["Distributed tracing","Environment reconstruction"],"relationships":["D25-B71-R05","D25-B68-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenTelemetry Trace Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/general/trace/","relevance":"Defines standardized concepts for tracing execution paths across systems."}],"evidence":"Execution-path information can distinguish failures associated with specific infrastructure paths or components.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B72-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Deployment Topology Diagnosis","concept":"Replica divergence","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"What is replica divergence in debugging?","answer":"Replica divergence occurs when nominally equivalent instances of a service differ in relevant runtime state, configuration, dependencies, cached data, deployment artifact, or resource conditions, causing different outcomes for equivalent workloads.","explanation":"Replica-level differences can create apparently random failures when routing distributes requests among instances. The investigation should compare failing and successful replicas rather than averaging their behavior.","principle":"Homogeneous deployment intent does not prove homogeneous runtime state.","rule":"When failures cluster by instance, compare runtime identity and state before concluding that the application logic is globally defective.","examples":["One replica runs a different image digest.","One instance has stale cached configuration.","A single node has exhausted a local resource."],"invalid_examples":["Restarting the entire fleet and assuming the cause is resolved.","Treating instance-specific failures as random noise."],"problem":"Only some replicas return errors for equivalent requests.","failure":"The fleet-level error rate hides the replica-specific fault.","cause":"Replica identity was omitted from debugging evidence.","solution":"Associate failures with replica identity and compare artifact, configuration, dependencies, resources, and local state.","alternatives":["Instance quarantine","Replica health comparison","Canary isolation"],"tradeoffs":"Per-replica diagnosis increases observability detail and storage requirements.","constraints":["Ephemeral instance identifiers require stable correlation metadata.","Replica comparisons must account for legitimate specialization."],"edge_cases":["Warm caches","Local persistent volumes","Canary versions","Node-specific kernel behavior"],"security":"Limit exposure of host identifiers and internal topology information.","performance":"Use sampled instance diagnostics unless broad capture is required by the incident.","testing":"Test that intentionally divergent replicas are distinguishable through diagnostic metadata.","verification":"Verify that the identified replica difference is relevant to the observed behavior.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Artifact identity","Instance metadata"],"prerequisites":["Topology-aware diagnostics"],"relationships":["D25-B72-R01","D25-B71-R03"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenTelemetry Resource Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/resource/","relevance":"Provides conventions for describing entities producing telemetry, supporting resource-level correlation."}],"evidence":"Runtime resource identity is necessary when failures occur only on a subset of equivalent service instances.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B72-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Deployment Topology Diagnosis","concept":"Canary divergence analysis","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should a canary-specific failure be investigated?","answer":"Compare the canary and baseline execution paths while controlling workload, configuration, topology, dependency versions, and traffic characteristics. The investigation should isolate differences introduced by the canary rather than attributing every observed difference to the new application version.","explanation":"Canary deployments intentionally create a differential environment. Their diagnostic value depends on preserving enough context to distinguish version effects from routing, traffic composition, resource allocation, and infrastructure differences.","principle":"A canary is a controlled differential experiment only when relevant confounders are measured or controlled.","rule":"Do not infer version causality from canary correlation without evaluating competing differences.","examples":["Compare equivalent request classes across canary and baseline.","Compare dependency and infrastructure metadata.","Analyze failure rates conditioned on request attributes."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring the new version causal solely because failures began after canary traffic was enabled.","Comparing aggregate failure rates without workload normalization."],"problem":"A new deployment shows higher failure rates during canary rollout.","failure":"The investigation ignores differences in traffic or infrastructure between canary and baseline.","cause":"Confounding variables were not controlled.","solution":"Perform conditioned differential analysis and reproduce the suspected version difference in a controlled environment.","alternatives":["Shadow traffic","Controlled rollback","A/B diagnostic experiment"],"tradeoffs":"More controlled comparisons increase diagnostic confidence but can require additional infrastructure.","constraints":["Traffic may not be statistically comparable.","Production experiments require safety controls."],"edge_cases":["Low-volume canaries","Tenant-specific routing","Version-specific feature flags","Different autoscaling behavior"],"security":"Ensure diagnostic experiments do not expose tenant data across environments.","performance":"Prefer existing telemetry dimensions before adding high-cardinality instrumentation.","testing":"Validate that canary routing preserves required comparison metadata.","verification":"Verify the suspected version effect using controlled evidence rather than temporal correlation alone.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Deployment metadata","Traffic classification"],"prerequisites":["Differential debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B71-R04","D25-B72-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/","relevance":"Provides standardized telemetry attributes useful for version and deployment comparison."}],"evidence":"Differential experiments require controlled or measured variables to support causal interpretation.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B72-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Deployment Topology Diagnosis","concept":"Routing asymmetry diagnosis","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"How can asymmetric routing produce misleading debugging evidence?","answer":"Asymmetric routing can cause requests or responses to traverse different infrastructure paths, exposing only certain traffic to a faulty proxy, firewall, load balancer, network segment, or service instance. Debugging should compare complete request and response paths rather than only application-level endpoints.","explanation":"A service may appear unhealthy even when the application logic is correct if only one network path is affected. Conversely, a path-specific failure can disappear when traffic is retried through a different route.","principle":"Network path symmetry should not be assumed from logical service identity.","rule":"When failures correlate with network location, route, or direction, reconstruct both forward and return paths.","examples":["Compare routes from affected and unaffected clients.","Correlate failures with load-balancer targets.","Trace proxy hops for both request and response directions."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming application failure because the client receives a timeout.","Testing only from one network vantage point."],"problem":"Only certain clients or regions experience connection failures.","failure":"The investigation focuses exclusively on application logs.","cause":"Network-path asymmetry is invisible at the application layer.","solution":"Correlate client, network path, proxy, and target metadata and compare successful and failed paths.","alternatives":["Packet-level diagnostics","Synthetic probes","Multi-region path comparison"],"tradeoffs":"Network-level evidence can greatly improve localization but may require privileged access and produce sensitive metadata.","constraints":["Respect privacy and security policies when collecting network evidence.","Do not capture payloads unnecessarily."],"edge_cases":["Anycast routing","NAT","Service meshes","Multi-hop proxies"],"security":"Prefer metadata and controlled captures over unrestricted packet payload collection.","performance":"Use targeted captures during bounded diagnostic windows.","testing":"Validate diagnostics across representative network paths.","verification":"Verify the suspected network component by controlled path comparison or isolation.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Network telemetry","Request correlation"],"prerequisites":["Distributed-system debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B72-R01","D25-B38-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenTelemetry Trace Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/general/trace/","relevance":"Provides tracing concepts for correlating distributed request execution."}],"evidence":"Application-level evidence may not identify path-specific infrastructure failures; path correlation narrows the fault domain.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B72-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Deployment Topology Diagnosis","concept":"Zone or region correlated failure","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should a failure correlated with a geographic or availability zone be investigated?","answer":"Partition observations by region or zone and compare application artifacts, configuration, dependencies, resource conditions, network paths, data locality, and external dependencies. Geographic correlation is a localization signal, not proof that the region itself is causal.","explanation":"Regional failures can arise from infrastructure, dependency routing, data replication, configuration, capacity, or deployment differences. Treating location as the root cause prematurely can hide the actual causal mechanism.","principle":"Topology correlation identifies a fault domain; it does not by itself identify the cause.","rule":"Regional or zone correlation must be followed by causal comparison of relevant environmental and execution variables.","examples":["Compare image versions across zones.","Compare dependency endpoints and network paths.","Compare resource saturation conditioned on zone."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring a region faulty solely because failures are concentrated there.","Failing over without preserving diagnostic evidence."],"problem":"Errors cluster in one geographic deployment area.","failure":"The investigation stops after identifying the affected region.","cause":"Localization was incorrectly treated as causation.","solution":"Use the region as a partitioning dimension and systematically compare causal variables.","alternatives":["Zone evacuation experiment","Cross-region reproduction","Dependency endpoint comparison"],"tradeoffs":"Cross-region comparison improves isolation but may involve different traffic and data characteristics.","constraints":["Regional data residency requirements may limit reproduction.","Failover can change the evidence being investigated."],"edge_cases":["Regional dependency endpoints","Data residency","Uneven traffic distribution","Regional feature flags"],"security":"Preserve data-residency and access-control boundaries during cross-region diagnosis.","performance":"Compare normalized rates rather than raw error counts when traffic volume differs materially.","testing":"Test deployment consistency across zones and regions.","verification":"Verify that the identified regional difference explains the failure through controlled evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Topology metadata","Traffic normalization"],"prerequisites":["Differential diagnosis"],"relationships":["D25-B72-R01","D25-B71-R05"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/","relevance":"Supports standardized environment and resource attributes for telemetry correlation."}],"evidence":"Geographic partitioning is useful for fault localization when combined with causal variable comparison.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B73-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Data Pipeline Debugging","concept":"Data lineage-based fault localization","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How can data lineage help localize application failures?","answer":"Trace a corrupted, missing, or unexpected value backward through the transformations and sources that produced it. Identify the earliest point where the value violated an expected invariant and distinguish source-data defects from transformation defects and presentation defects.","explanation":"Many software failures manifest far downstream from the operation that introduced invalid data. Lineage converts a downstream symptom into a backward causal investigation over transformations.","principle":"For data-driven failures, debug the value's lineage rather than only the location where it becomes visible.","rule":"The earliest verified invariant violation in the lineage should be treated as a stronger root-cause candidate than the final symptom.","examples":["Trace an incorrect report value through aggregation and ingestion stages.","Compare source records with transformed records.","Validate schema and semantic invariants at transformation boundaries."],"invalid_examples":["Fixing the final display because the displayed value is wrong.","Assuming the database is responsible because the application read an incorrect value."],"problem":"A downstream component produces an incorrect result.","failure":"Investigation starts at presentation instead of tracing data origin.","cause":"Data lineage was unavailable or ignored.","solution":"Construct lineage from source through transformations and identify the first verified semantic violation.","alternatives":["Boundary assertions","Replay from source data","Transformation-level snapshots"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed lineage improves diagnosis but can increase storage, metadata complexity, and privacy exposure.","constraints":["Lineage must preserve semantic relationships without unnecessarily copying sensitive payloads.","External data sources may have incomplete lineage."],"edge_cases":["Late-arriving data","Backfills","Schema evolution","Aggregations"],"security":"Protect sensitive data while retaining enough lineage metadata for causal analysis.","performance":"Prefer identifiers and transformation metadata over full payload duplication.","testing":"Test lineage propagation through each transformation stage.","verification":"Verify the earliest suspected violation against source and transformation evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Data lineage metadata","Transformation boundaries"],"prerequisites":["Invariant-based debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B40-R01","D25-B67-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenLineage","url":"https://openlineage.io/","relevance":"Provides an open standard and ecosystem for data lineage metadata."}],"evidence":"Lineage identifies relationships between data-producing and data-consuming operations, supporting backward fault localization.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B73-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Data Pipeline Debugging","concept":"Schema-evolution fault diagnosis","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"How should schema evolution bugs be diagnosed?","answer":"Compare the producer schema, transport representation, consumer expectation, compatibility policy, and deployed schema versions at the failing boundary. Determine whether the failure comes from incompatible structure, changed semantics, missing defaults, type interpretation, or version negotiation.","explanation":"A schema can remain structurally valid while its semantic meaning changes. Debugging must therefore evaluate both structural compatibility and semantic compatibility.","principle":"Schema compatibility includes meaning, not only field presence and data types.","rule":"A schema change should be investigated across producer-consumer boundaries and compatibility assumptions.","examples":["Compare serialized payload schema versions.","Check whether a new enum value is accepted by older consumers.","Verify whether optional fields preserve previous semantics."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming backward compatibility because serialization succeeds.","Checking only field names while ignoring semantic changes."],"problem":"Consumers begin failing after a producer deployment.","failure":"The investigation checks serialization but not consumer compatibility or semantics.","cause":"Schema evolution contract was incomplete.","solution":"Compare producer and consumer schema versions and validate structural and semantic compatibility.","alternatives":["Contract testing","Schema registry comparison","Versioned replay"],"tradeoffs":"Strict compatibility policies reduce runtime failures but can constrain evolution speed.","constraints":["Distributed systems may deploy producers and consumers independently.","Historical payloads may remain in queues or storage."],"edge_cases":["Unknown enum values","Nullable-to-required changes","Unit changes","Field renaming"],"security":"Schema debugging should avoid exposing sensitive payload contents when metadata is sufficient.","performance":"Prefer schema metadata comparisons before replaying large datasets.","testing":"Test compatibility across supported producer-consumer version combinations.","verification":"Verify both serialization compatibility and semantic equivalence for affected fields.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Schema metadata","Producer-consumer contracts"],"prerequisites":["Data contract knowledge"],"relationships":["D25-B73-R01","D25-B27-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"Apache Avro Documentation","url":"https://avro.apache.org/docs/","relevance":"Documents schema evolution and compatibility concepts for serialized data."}],"evidence":"Independent producer and consumer evolution creates compatibility failure modes that require explicit schema comparison.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B73-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Data Pipeline Debugging","concept":"Data-quality boundary localization","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How can data-quality boundaries be used to locate defects?","answer":"Define explicit quality invariants at major data boundaries and determine the first boundary where the invariant fails. This distinguishes source defects from ingestion, transformation, storage, and consumption defects.","explanation":"Without boundary checks, invalid data can travel through multiple components before producing an observable failure. The resulting debugging search space is unnecessarily large.","principle":"Validate data at boundaries where ownership and transformation semantics change.","rule":"A boundary should record enough evidence to determine whether its input and output satisfy the relevant invariants.","examples":["Validate uniqueness after ingestion.","Validate units after transformation.","Validate referential relationships before aggregation."],"invalid_examples":["Checking only the final output.","Using a single generic 'data valid' flag without specifying the invariant."],"problem":"A business calculation is incorrect but the source of the bad value is unknown.","failure":"No intermediate invariants exist to localize the first violation.","cause":"Data quality was validated only at the final output.","solution":"Add targeted invariants at transformation boundaries and compare the first failing boundary.","alternatives":["Assertions","Data-quality frameworks","Contract checks"],"tradeoffs":"Additional checks improve diagnosability but consume compute and may require careful handling of incomplete data.","constraints":["Invariants must distinguish legitimate exceptional values from corruption.","Checks should not silently mutate data."],"edge_cases":["Partial batches","Late data","Expected nulls","Approximate aggregates"],"security":"Do not log raw sensitive values solely for invariant failures.","performance":"Use representative or metadata-level checks where full validation is too expensive.","testing":"Test each invariant against valid, invalid, boundary, and incomplete inputs.","verification":"Verify that each detected violation is reproducible from the corresponding boundary evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Data contracts","Invariant definitions"],"prerequisites":["Data lineage","Boundary validation"],"relationships":["D25-B73-R01","D25-B73-R02"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenLineage","url":"https://openlineage.io/","relevance":"Provides lineage concepts useful for associating data transformations and boundaries."}],"evidence":"Boundary validation reduces the search space by identifying the earliest point where data violates expected conditions.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B73-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Data Pipeline Debugging","concept":"Backfill-induced regression diagnosis","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why can historical data backfills expose software defects?","answer":"Backfills can exercise historical schemas, distributions, edge cases, ordering assumptions, and code paths that ordinary current-time processing rarely encounters. Debugging should compare backfill inputs and execution conditions with the assumptions made by the processing logic.","explanation":"A system may work correctly for current data while failing on older records because formats, missing fields, ranges, or business rules differ. The failure may therefore originate in an unstated temporal assumption.","principle":"Historical data is a distinct workload class, not merely older copies of current data.","rule":"Backfill failures should be analyzed against historical data characteristics and the software version used to process them.","examples":["Compare historical schema versions.","Identify timestamps outside the normal operational range.","Test ordering assumptions against delayed historical events."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming a current-data validation suite covers all historical data.","Discarding historical records because they are old."],"problem":"A batch succeeds for recent data but fails during historical reprocessing.","failure":"The investigation treats the failure as random input corruption.","cause":"Historical workload characteristics violate unstated assumptions.","solution":"Profile the historical input, identify the first violated invariant, and test the processing logic against representative historical cases.","alternatives":["Historical fixture generation","Versioned replay","Data partition isolation"],"tradeoffs":"Historical replay improves coverage but can be computationally expensive.","constraints":["Historical data may have different retention or privacy restrictions.","Business rules may intentionally change over time."],"edge_cases":["Legacy schemas","Deleted reference data","Changed business definitions","Timezone transitions"],"security":"Apply current access controls and retention policies to historical diagnostic data.","performance":"Use representative partitions before executing full backfills.","testing":"Maintain historical fixtures for known legacy formats and edge cases.","verification":"Verify that the identified historical characteristic explains the failure and that the corrected logic handles it without changing valid current behavior.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Historical data","Schema history","Processing-version metadata"],"prerequisites":["Data lineage","Version-aware debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B73-R02","D25-B71-R03"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenLineage","url":"https://openlineage.io/","relevance":"Supports lineage-aware analysis of data processing jobs."}],"evidence":"Historical reprocessing can expose input characteristics absent from normal workloads and therefore requires dedicated diagnostic analysis.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B73-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Data Pipeline Debugging","concept":"Data corruption versus data interpretation","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should a debugger distinguish corrupted data from incorrect data interpretation?","answer":"Compare the value at the source representation, transport representation, decoded representation, and consumer interpretation. If the underlying representation remains correct while the interpreted value changes, the defect is likely in decoding, units, schema semantics, or consumer logic rather than storage corruption.","explanation":"The same bytes can represent different logical values under different schemas, encodings, units, or interpretation rules. Debugging must separate representation integrity from semantic interpretation.","principle":"Integrity of representation and correctness of interpretation are separate diagnostic properties.","rule":"Never classify data as corrupted solely because the consumer observes an unexpected semantic value.","examples":["Compare raw encoded value with decoded numeric value.","Verify character encoding before blaming storage.","Check unit conversion at consumer boundaries."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming database corruption because a number is displayed incorrectly.","Inspecting only the final decoded object."],"problem":"A consumer receives a value that appears invalid.","failure":"The investigation immediately attributes the issue to storage.","cause":"Representation and interpretation were not separated.","solution":"Compare the value across encoding, transport, decoding, and interpretation boundaries.","alternatives":["Hex-level inspection","Schema-aware replay","Boundary assertions"],"tradeoffs":"Low-level inspection provides strong evidence but can increase complexity and exposure of sensitive data.","constraints":["Raw representations may contain sensitive information.","Different encodings may intentionally normalize values."],"edge_cases":["Unicode normalization","Floating-point serialization","Compression","Endianness"],"security":"Protect raw payloads and use redaction where full representation is unnecessary.","performance":"Inspect targeted samples rather than entire datasets when diagnosing a localized defect.","testing":"Test encode/decode round trips and semantic conversion boundaries.","verification":"Verify that the first representation-to-meaning divergence matches the observed failure.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Serialization format","Schema or semantic contract"],"prerequisites":["Boundary debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B73-R02","D25-B73-R03"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"IETF RFC 8259","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259","relevance":"Defines JSON data interchange semantics relevant to serialization and interpretation debugging."}],"evidence":"Serialization correctness does not guarantee semantic correctness after decoding or interpretation.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B74-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"State-Machine Failure Diagnosis","concept":"Illegal state transition detection","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should illegal state transitions be diagnosed?","answer":"Model the relevant component as a state machine, define permitted transitions and transition guards, then identify the first observed transition that violates the model. The investigation should preserve the event and state evidence surrounding that transition.","explanation":"Many complex failures are not caused by an invalid state alone but by reaching a valid-looking state through an impossible transition. Transition-level analysis exposes ordering, authorization, concurrency, and recovery defects.","principle":"A valid state reached through an invalid transition remains evidence of a control defect.","rule":"Root-cause analysis should identify the earliest verified illegal transition rather than only the final invalid state.","examples":["Detect a CLOSED-to-ACTIVE transition when reopening is prohibited.","Identify a transaction entering COMMITTED before required validation.","Record guard evaluation results for each critical transition."],"invalid_examples":["Checking only whether final states are valid.","Assuming a valid final state proves the transition path was valid."],"problem":"A system reaches an inconsistent state after a sequence of operations.","failure":"The debugger inspects the final state but not the transition history.","cause":"State-machine invariants were not modeled.","solution":"Reconstruct the transition sequence and identify the first violated transition rule.","alternatives":["Event-sourced reconstruction","State transition logs","Formal state-machine model"],"tradeoffs":"Transition logging improves diagnosis but increases event volume.","constraints":["The state model must reflect actual business or system semantics.","Concurrent transitions may require causal ordering."],"edge_cases":["Concurrent transitions","Retries","Recovery transitions","Versioned state machines"],"security":"Transition evidence should exclude sensitive payloads unless required for authorization analysis.","performance":"Record compact transition identifiers and relevant guard outcomes.","testing":"Test every legal transition, illegal transition, and concurrent transition scenario.","verification":"Verify that replaying the transition sequence reproduces the invalid state.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["State model","Transition evidence"],"prerequisites":["State-transition debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B35-R01","D25-B61-R05"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"W3C State Chart XML","url":"https://www.w3.org/TR/scxml/","relevance":"Provides a formal representation model for state machines and transitions."}],"evidence":"Explicit transition models allow debugging to identify the earliest violation of state-machine constraints.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B74-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"State-Machine Failure Diagnosis","concept":"Guard-condition failure analysis","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should failed state-transition guards be analyzed?","answer":"Capture the guard condition, its input values, evaluation result, and the state in which it was evaluated. Then determine whether the guard itself is incorrect, its inputs are stale or corrupted, or the transition was evaluated in an invalid context.","explanation":"A failed guard can be a symptom rather than the root cause. The relevant question is why the guard produced its decision under the state and evidence available at the time.","principle":"Debug guard inputs and evaluation context, not merely guard outcomes.","rule":"A guard failure should be decomposed into predicate logic, inputs, freshness, and evaluation context.","examples":["Compare expected and actual authorization inputs.","Check whether a cached value was stale when a transition guard executed.","Verify unit and type assumptions in a predicate."],"invalid_examples":["Changing the guard to permit the transition without understanding why it failed.","Logging only 'guard=false'."],"problem":"A valid operation is unexpectedly rejected.","failure":"The debugger sees a false predicate but cannot explain its inputs.","cause":"Guard evidence is incomplete.","solution":"Capture safe predicate inputs, context, and freshness metadata, then trace their origins.","alternatives":["Predicate-level tracing","Invariant snapshots","Input lineage analysis"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed guard diagnostics improve causal resolution but can expose sensitive decision inputs.","constraints":["Sensitive authorization or business data must be minimized.","Do not weaken security controls merely to facilitate debugging."],"edge_cases":["Eventually consistent data","Cached authorization","Concurrent updates","Clock-dependent guards"],"security":"Guard debugging must preserve the security boundary and avoid logging secrets or credentials.","performance":"Use selective diagnostics for high-cost or high-cardinality guard inputs.","testing":"Test guard predicates with boundary, stale, missing, and conflicting inputs.","verification":"Verify the guard decision against the reconstructed input state and predicate definition.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["State model","Predicate definition"],"prerequisites":["State-machine analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B74-R01","D25-B35-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"NIST SP 800-53","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-53/rev-5/final","relevance":"Provides security control concepts relevant to preserving authorization and audit evidence during diagnostics."}],"evidence":"Decision predicates must be interpreted in the context of their actual inputs and execution state.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B74-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"State-Machine Failure Diagnosis","concept":"Recovery-state debugging","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why should recovery states be debugged separately from normal operational states?","answer":"Recovery states often have different invariants, permitted transitions, timeouts, and resource assumptions than normal operation. A system can therefore fail only while recovering even when its steady-state path is correct.","explanation":"Recovery code is exercised under degraded conditions and frequently combines partial state, retries, failover, and incomplete dependencies. Debugging must reconstruct the recovery-specific state machine rather than assuming normal-state rules apply unchanged.","principle":"Recovery is a distinct execution regime with distinct invariants.","rule":"A recovery-path diagnosis must verify recovery-specific transitions and post-recovery invariants.","examples":["Analyze failover states separately from healthy states.","Verify cleanup after retry exhaustion.","Check whether recovery can re-enter normal operation safely."],"invalid_examples":["Testing only the steady-state path.","Assuming a successful failover proves state consistency."],"problem":"A service works normally but becomes inconsistent after recovery.","failure":"Recovery transitions were never modeled or tested.","cause":"Normal-state assumptions were incorrectly applied to recovery.","solution":"Model recovery states, transition guards, and convergence invariants explicitly.","alternatives":["Fault-injection testing","Recovery state snapshots","Failure-mode simulation"],"tradeoffs":"Recovery testing increases coverage but may require controlled fault environments.","constraints":["Recovery experiments must have bounded blast radius.","External side effects may complicate repeated recovery tests."],"edge_cases":["Repeated failover","Partial dependency recovery","Stale caches","Interrupted recovery"],"security":"Recovery mechanisms must not bypass authorization or integrity checks.","performance":"Measure recovery latency separately from steady-state performance.","testing":"Exercise each recovery transition and interruption point.","verification":"Verify convergence to an explicitly valid state after recovery.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Recovery state model","Failure injection"],"prerequisites":["State-machine debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B66-R01","D25-B65-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/","relevance":"Documents controlled fault-injection concepts applicable to recovery-path testing."}],"evidence":"Recovery behavior must be evaluated under degraded conditions because its execution path differs from normal operation.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B74-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"State-Machine Failure Diagnosis","concept":"State convergence failure","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"What is state convergence failure?","answer":"State convergence failure occurs when independently evolving components or replicas that should eventually agree remain permanently or indefinitely inconsistent after the conditions required for convergence have been restored.","explanation":"A system may recover availability while retaining divergent logical state. Debugging must distinguish temporary convergence delay from a permanent failure of the convergence mechanism.","principle":"Recovery of service availability does not prove recovery of state consistency.","rule":"After recovery, verify explicit convergence invariants rather than relying solely on successful request handling.","examples":["Compare replica versions after partition recovery.","Verify that replicated metadata converges to the same logical state.","Track unresolved reconciliation conflicts."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring recovery complete because requests return successfully.","Assuming eventual consistency without measuring convergence."],"problem":"Replicas continue serving different logical results after an outage.","failure":"The recovery procedure restores connectivity but not state convergence.","cause":"Conflict resolution or synchronization failed.","solution":"Track state versions, reconciliation outcomes, and convergence conditions until a defined invariant is satisfied.","alternatives":["Merkle-tree comparison","Version-vector analysis","Explicit reconciliation logs"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed convergence tracking improves diagnosis but adds metadata and processing overhead.","constraints":["Logical equivalence may differ from byte-for-byte equality.","Some systems intentionally allow bounded divergence."],"edge_cases":["Concurrent writes","Conflict resolution","Partial replication","Offline clients"],"security":"Ensure reconciliation cannot bypass authorization or integrity checks.","performance":"Use compact state summaries where full state comparison is too expensive.","testing":"Test partitions, concurrent writes, recovery, and reconciliation conflicts.","verification":"Verify convergence using system-specific logical invariants.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Replication metadata","State versioning"],"prerequisites":["Distributed-state debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B66-R03","D25-B74-R03"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"Raft Paper","url":"https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf","relevance":"Provides formal reasoning about replicated state-machine consistency and recovery."}],"evidence":"Distributed replicated systems require explicit mechanisms and evidence to determine whether state has converged.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B74-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"State-Machine Failure Diagnosis","concept":"State-machine model mismatch","knowledge_type":"root_cause_pattern","question":"What is a state-machine model mismatch?","answer":"A state-machine model mismatch occurs when the documented or assumed state-transition model differs from the behavior implemented by the software. Debugging should compare observed transitions with the authoritative intended model and determine whether the model or implementation is incorrect.","explanation":"A debugger can incorrectly classify correct behavior as a bug if the model is stale, or miss a defect if the implementation silently violates undocumented assumptions. Root-cause analysis therefore requires identifying the authoritative contract.","principle":"A debugging model is evidence only when its authority and version are known.","rule":"Before classifying a transition as invalid, establish which state-machine contract governs the affected component version.","examples":["Compare implementation transitions with versioned protocol specifications.","Identify a newly permitted transition introduced by a specification update.","Mark obsolete state diagrams as historical evidence rather than current contracts."],"invalid_examples":["Treating an old design document as current behavior.","Assuming implementation behavior is authoritative merely because it exists."],"problem":"Engineers disagree about whether a transition is valid.","failure":"The investigation lacks a versioned authoritative contract.","cause":"Model provenance and version were not established.","solution":"Identify the governing specification or contract version and compare implementation and observed behavior against it.","alternatives":["Executable state models","Versioned contracts","Protocol conformance tests"],"tradeoffs":"Versioned formal models improve clarity but increase maintenance requirements.","constraints":["Contracts can change independently from deployments.","Historical incidents may require historical contracts."],"edge_cases":["Backward compatibility","Protocol version negotiation","Feature-gated transitions","Deprecated states"],"security":"Security-relevant state models must be versioned and access-controlled.","performance":"Executable validation can be selective for critical transitions.","testing":"Run conformance tests against each supported model version.","verification":"Verify the model version, implementation, and observed transition sequence independently.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Versioned state contract","Implementation metadata"],"prerequisites":["State-machine modeling"],"relationships":["D25-B74-R01","D25-B71-R03"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"W3C State Chart XML","url":"https://www.w3.org/TR/scxml/","relevance":"Defines a formal state-machine representation useful for explicit transition modeling."}],"evidence":"State-based diagnosis depends on a clearly identified contract describing permitted states and transitions.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B75-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Protocol and Contract Debugging","concept":"Protocol-state incompatibility","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"How should protocol-state incompatibility be diagnosed?","answer":"Reconstruct the protocol state independently at both communicating endpoints and compare expected message sequences, negotiated capabilities, protocol versions, and state transitions. A successful transport connection does not prove protocol compatibility.","explanation":"Two endpoints may establish a connection while disagreeing about message ordering, capabilities, framing, authentication state, or protocol version. Debugging must inspect protocol semantics above the transport layer.","principle":"Transport connectivity and protocol compatibility are separate properties.","rule":"Protocol failures should be analyzed from endpoint state and message sequence evidence rather than connection status alone.","examples":["Compare negotiated protocol versions.","Identify a message accepted by one endpoint but invalid in its current state.","Check capability negotiation before processing optional features."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming TCP connectivity proves application protocol compatibility.","Debugging only the sender without examining receiver state."],"problem":"Requests connect successfully but fail during protocol exchange.","failure":"Investigation stops at network connectivity.","cause":"Protocol state or capability mismatch.","solution":"Capture safe protocol metadata and reconstruct endpoint state transitions.","alternatives":["Protocol conformance tests","Wire-level metadata analysis","Version compatibility matrix"],"tradeoffs":"Protocol-level diagnostics provide strong evidence but may require specialized tooling.","constraints":["Payload capture may be prohibited or sensitive.","Encrypted protocols require endpoint-side evidence."],"edge_cases":["Protocol downgrade","Optional extensions","Partial handshakes","Middlebox modification"],"security":"Do not disable encryption or authentication merely to inspect traffic; prefer endpoint telemetry and controlled test environments.","performance":"Capture protocol metadata selectively in production.","testing":"Test supported protocol versions and capability combinations.","verification":"Verify the incompatibility through endpoint state and controlled protocol exchange.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Protocol specification","Endpoint telemetry"],"prerequisites":["Distributed debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B73-R02","D25-B74-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"IETF RFC 9110","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110","relevance":"Defines HTTP semantics relevant to protocol-level debugging."}],"evidence":"Protocol correctness depends on semantic state and message rules beyond basic transport connectivity.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B75-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Protocol and Contract Debugging","concept":"Contract drift between independently deployed components","knowledge_type":"root_cause_pattern","question":"What is contract drift between independently deployed components?","answer":"Contract drift occurs when communicating components evolve assumptions about an interface without maintaining compatible behavior across deployment versions. Debugging should identify the contract version expected and provided at the interaction boundary.","explanation":"Distributed components often deploy asynchronously. A producer can expose a new behavior before all consumers understand it, or a consumer can require behavior not yet supported by its dependency.","principle":"Interface compatibility must be evaluated across simultaneously deployed versions.","rule":"A boundary failure should be analyzed against the producer-consumer version pair, not each component in isolation.","examples":["Compare API contract versions across deployments.","Test old consumer against new producer behavior.","Record negotiated or declared interface versions."],"invalid_examples":["Checking each service independently and assuming compatibility.","Testing only latest-version pairs."],"problem":"An integration fails only during rolling deployment.","failure":"Version combinations created during rollout were never considered.","cause":"Compatibility was assumed rather than tested.","solution":"Construct a supported version-pair matrix and reproduce the failing combination.","alternatives":["Consumer-driven contract testing","Compatibility shims","Version negotiation"],"tradeoffs":"Maintaining compatibility across versions increases testing and implementation complexity.","constraints":["Not every version combination must be supported.","Contract changes should define explicit compatibility windows."],"edge_cases":["Rolling deployments","Long-lived clients","Cached schemas","Backward-incompatible feature flags"],"security":"Security-sensitive interface changes require explicit compatibility and authorization validation.","performance":"Limit compatibility testing to supported version ranges.","testing":"Automate supported producer-consumer version-pair tests.","verification":"Verify the exact deployed version combination and its contract behavior.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Interface contract","Deployment version metadata"],"prerequisites":["Version-aware debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B72-R03","D25-B73-R02"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenAPI Specification","url":"https://spec.openapis.org/oas/latest.html","relevance":"Defines a machine-readable API contract representation useful for interface comparison."}],"evidence":"Independent deployment creates transient version combinations that can violate interface assumptions.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B75-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Protocol and Contract Debugging","concept":"Contract ambiguity","knowledge_type":"root_cause_pattern","question":"How does contract ambiguity complicate debugging?","answer":"Contract ambiguity occurs when an interface permits multiple interpretations of valid input, output, timing, errors, or side effects. Debugging becomes difficult because each component can behave consistently with its own interpretation while the system remains incompatible.","explanation":"Underspecified behavior creates failures that cannot be resolved by examining implementation alone. The investigation must identify the ambiguous clause and determine the intended interoperable behavior.","principle":"An ambiguous contract is itself a defect source.","rule":"If two implementations produce incompatible behavior while both satisfy the written contract, the contract requires clarification before a reliable implementation-level root cause can be assigned.","examples":["Undefined timezone semantics.","Unspecified ordering of returned records.","Ambiguous meaning of an omitted field."],"invalid_examples":["Blaming one implementation when the contract permits both interpretations.","Adding arbitrary behavior without documenting it."],"problem":"Two components disagree about behavior that is not explicitly specified.","failure":"Debugging cycles continue without a definitive correctness criterion.","cause":"Contract semantics are incomplete or ambiguous.","solution":"Identify the ambiguity, establish authoritative semantics, version the contract, and add conformance tests.","alternatives":["Formal contract","Executable specification","Reference implementation"],"tradeoffs":"More precise contracts reduce ambiguity but may constrain future implementation choices.","constraints":["Contract clarification may require coordination among independent owners.","Historical clients may depend on ambiguous behavior."],"edge_cases":["Legacy APIs","Optional fields","Error semantics","Timing guarantees"],"security":"Security-relevant ambiguities should be resolved explicitly rather than relying on implementation conventions.","performance":"Avoid over-specifying irrelevant implementation details.","testing":"Create tests for previously ambiguous cases and negative cases.","verification":"Verify that independent implementations converge on the defined behavior.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Interface specification","Conformance tests"],"prerequisites":["Contract analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B75-R02","D25-B74-R05"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenAPI Specification","url":"https://spec.openapis.org/oas/latest.html","relevance":"Provides a formal structure for expressing API interface contracts."}],"evidence":"Explicit contracts reduce interpretation variance at integration boundaries.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B75-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Protocol and Contract Debugging","concept":"Error-contract debugging","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"Why should error responses be treated as part of an interface contract?","answer":"Error status, structure, semantics, retryability, and side-effect guarantees influence caller behavior and therefore form part of the contract. Debugging should determine whether the producer emitted a contract-compliant error and whether the consumer interpreted it correctly.","explanation":"A technically valid error response can still cause system failure when consumers depend on undocumented distinctions such as retryable versus permanent errors or whether an operation may have partially succeeded.","principle":"Failure semantics are part of the interface contract.","rule":"Every externally observable error used for control flow should have defined semantics for callers.","examples":["Distinguish validation failure from transient dependency failure.","Define whether timeout means operation definitely did not execute.","Specify retryability of status classes."],"invalid_examples":["Retrying every error indiscriminately.","Assuming timeout means no side effect occurred."],"problem":"Retries amplify an incident after an upstream failure.","failure":"The consumer cannot distinguish transient and uncertain outcomes.","cause":"Error semantics were underspecified.","solution":"Define error classes, retryability, side-effect uncertainty, and caller handling rules.","alternatives":["Typed errors","Problem Details","Explicit operation status endpoints"],"tradeoffs":"Richer error contracts improve behavior but increase interface complexity.","constraints":["Errors must not reveal sensitive internal details.","Uncertain outcomes require explicit semantics."],"edge_cases":["Timeout after server-side execution","Partial failure","Rate limiting","Circuit breaking"],"security":"Avoid leaking stack traces, credentials, or sensitive internal state through error responses.","performance":"Error payloads should remain bounded and cheap to generate.","testing":"Test each error class and caller response behavior.","verification":"Verify producer error semantics against consumer retry and recovery logic.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["API contract","Retry policy"],"prerequisites":["Protocol debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B37-R01","D25-B75-R02"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"IETF RFC 9457","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457","relevance":"Defines a standard problem-details format for HTTP API error information."}],"evidence":"Errors influence distributed control flow and therefore require explicit interface semantics.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B75-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Protocol and Contract Debugging","concept":"Backward-compatible behavior versus backward-compatible schema","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"Why is schema compatibility insufficient to establish behavioral compatibility?","answer":"Two versions can preserve field structure while changing timing, ordering, defaults, side effects, validation strictness, or error semantics. Debugging compatibility therefore requires behavioral contract analysis in addition to schema comparison.","explanation":"Structural compatibility answers whether data can be represented or parsed; behavioral compatibility answers whether callers observe equivalent semantics under supported conditions.","principle":"Representation compatibility is necessary in many systems but is not sufficient for behavioral compatibility.","rule":"Compatibility analysis must include observable semantics that callers depend upon.","examples":["A field remains optional but its default meaning changes.","An API retains its schema but begins enforcing stricter validation.","A successful response remains structurally identical while side effects change."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring an API backward compatible because all old fields still parse.","Ignoring changed error or timing semantics."],"problem":"Older consumers parse responses successfully but behave incorrectly.","failure":"Compatibility testing checked schema only.","cause":"Behavioral contract was omitted.","solution":"Define and test observable behavioral guarantees alongside schema compatibility.","alternatives":["Contract tests","Golden behavior tests","Semantic version compatibility policies"],"tradeoffs":"Behavioral compatibility testing is more expensive than schema validation but catches deeper regressions.","constraints":["Not all implementation details are contractually observable.","Behavioral guarantees must be explicitly scoped."],"edge_cases":["Timing changes","Ordering","Side effects","Default values"],"security":"Behavioral changes affecting authorization must be explicitly tested.","performance":"Prioritize externally observable guarantees rather than internal implementation details.","testing":"Test semantic behavior for supported compatibility scenarios.","verification":"Verify that representative old-client interactions retain documented semantics.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Behavioral contract","Schema contract"],"prerequisites":["Contract testing"],"relationships":["D25-B75-R02","D25-B75-R03"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenAPI Specification","url":"https://spec.openapis.org/oas/latest.html","relevance":"Provides structural API contract representation while illustrating the distinction between documented interface structure and broader behavior."}],"evidence":"Schema equality does not encode every externally observable property of a software interaction.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B76-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Storage and Persistence Diagnosis","concept":"Persistence-boundary debugging","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should a debugger localize failures at persistence boundaries?","answer":"Compare the intended state before persistence, the operation submitted to the persistence layer, the acknowledged result, and the state subsequently read back. The investigation should distinguish application construction errors, persistence-layer rejection, partial success, and read-after-write inconsistency.","explanation":"A successful write acknowledgement does not necessarily prove that the application observed the intended durable state, especially in systems with asynchronous persistence, replicas, caches, or transactions.","principle":"Persistence debugging requires evidence across write intent, write outcome, durability semantics, and subsequent observation.","rule":"A persistence operation should not be classified as successful solely from an application-level acknowledgement unless the contract defines that acknowledgement as sufficient.","examples":["Compare transaction intent with committed record.","Check write acknowledgement semantics.","Compare primary and replica reads when applicable."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming a successful function return proves durable persistence.","Inspecting only the final read without reconstructing the write."],"problem":"A record appears missing immediately after a successful write.","failure":"The investigation assumes write success means immediate read visibility.","cause":"Durability and visibility semantics were not considered.","solution":"Reconstruct persistence semantics and compare write, commit, and read evidence.","alternatives":["Transaction tracing","Database audit metadata","Read-after-write probes"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed persistence evidence improves diagnosis but may add database overhead.","constraints":["Diagnostic instrumentation must not alter transaction semantics.","Sensitive records should not be duplicated unnecessarily."],"edge_cases":["Asynchronous writes","Read replicas","Write buffering","Eventual consistency"],"security":"Database diagnostics must respect access controls and avoid exposing sensitive records.","performance":"Use transaction identifiers and metadata instead of duplicating payloads.","testing":"Test commit, rollback, visibility, and failure semantics explicitly.","verification":"Verify durable state according to the persistence contract, not merely function return status.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Persistence contract","Transaction metadata"],"prerequisites":["Database debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B73-R01","D25-B74-R04"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"PostgreSQL Documentation","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/","relevance":"Documents transactional, isolation, and persistence behavior relevant to database diagnosis."}],"evidence":"Persistence systems expose distinct write, commit, visibility, and recovery semantics that must be reconstructed during diagnosis.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B76-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Storage and Persistence Diagnosis","concept":"Write acknowledgement ambiguity","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why can a successful write acknowledgement be diagnostically ambiguous?","answer":"An acknowledgement may indicate that a request was accepted, buffered, committed, replicated, or merely scheduled, depending on the persistence contract. Debugging must identify exactly which state transition the acknowledgement represents.","explanation":"Without explicit acknowledgement semantics, engineers may incorrectly infer durability or visibility from a successful API response.","principle":"An acknowledgement is meaningful only relative to its defined persistence guarantee.","rule":"Every persistence acknowledgement used for correctness decisions must have documented semantics.","examples":["Distinguish accepted-for-processing from durably-committed.","Record commit identifiers when durability is guaranteed.","Track asynchronous persistence completion separately."],"invalid_examples":["Treating HTTP 200 as proof of durable database commit.","Assuming a queued write is already visible to readers."],"problem":"Data disappears after a seemingly successful operation.","failure":"The application interprets acceptance as durability.","cause":"Acknowledgement semantics were ambiguous.","solution":"Define and instrument explicit persistence lifecycle states.","alternatives":["Synchronous commit","Completion events","Durability tokens"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger acknowledgement guarantees can increase latency and resource usage.","constraints":["Guarantees must reflect actual storage semantics.","Distributed durability may require explicit quorum or replication semantics."],"edge_cases":["Process crash after acknowledgement","Network loss after commit","Replication lag"],"security":"Do not expose internal persistence topology through external error messages.","performance":"Choose durability guarantees based on correctness requirements rather than assuming maximum durability everywhere.","testing":"Inject failures between acceptance, commit, and acknowledgement.","verification":"Verify observed behavior under process, network, and storage failures.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Storage system","Persistence contract"],"prerequisites":["Transaction semantics"],"relationships":["D25-B76-R01","D25-B37-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"PostgreSQL Documentation","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/","relevance":"Provides authoritative documentation for transaction commit and durability behavior."}],"evidence":"Different persistence systems expose different acknowledgement and durability semantics, so acknowledgement meaning must be explicit.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B76-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Storage and Persistence Diagnosis","concept":"Read-after-write anomaly diagnosis","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should a read-after-write anomaly be investigated?","answer":"Determine the consistency model, identify the storage node or replica serving the read, correlate the write and read operations, and establish whether the read occurred within the guaranteed visibility window. Then distinguish expected consistency behavior from an actual defect.","explanation":"A read immediately following a write can legitimately observe older data in systems with asynchronous replication or eventual consistency. The diagnostic task is to compare observed behavior with the documented consistency contract.","principle":"An observed stale read is a defect only relative to the system's consistency guarantee.","rule":"Consistency anomalies must be evaluated against the explicit consistency model and operation context.","examples":["Compare primary and replica read timestamps.","Track logical versions of written records.","Measure replica visibility delay."],"invalid_examples":["Calling every stale read a database corruption bug.","Ignoring replica routing when diagnosing visibility."],"problem":"A user cannot immediately see a newly written value.","failure":"The system is diagnosed without considering its consistency contract.","cause":"Consistency semantics were not included in the evidence.","solution":"Reconstruct write/read routing and compare observed visibility with the promised consistency level.","alternatives":["Read-your-writes routing","Session consistency","Version-aware reads"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger consistency can increase latency or reduce availability.","constraints":["The application must communicate guarantees clearly.","Cross-region systems may have bounded propagation delays."],"edge_cases":["Failover","Replica lag","Caching","Concurrent writes"],"security":"Consistency mechanisms must not bypass tenant or authorization boundaries.","performance":"Measure lag rather than forcing globally synchronous reads when not required.","testing":"Test consistency guarantees under normal and degraded replication.","verification":"Verify observed read behavior against the declared consistency contract.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Consistency model","Read/write routing"],"prerequisites":["Distributed storage debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B76-R01","D25-B74-R04"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"PostgreSQL Documentation","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/","relevance":"Documents transaction isolation and visibility behavior relevant to persistence diagnosis."}],"evidence":"Read visibility depends on transaction and replication semantics rather than only write success.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B76-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Storage and Persistence Diagnosis","concept":"Migration-induced persistence defects","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"How should persistence failures caused by schema migrations be localized?","answer":"Compare the schema version expected by the application with the actual database schema and migration history at the failing instance. Then identify whether the defect arises from migration ordering, partial application, incompatible defaults, data transformation, or application-schema version mismatch.","explanation":"Rolling deployments can temporarily expose multiple application versions against evolving schemas. Migration debugging must therefore consider compatibility windows and intermediate schema states.","principle":"Database migrations create versioned runtime states that must be debugged explicitly.","rule":"The effective application-schema version pair must be established before assigning a migration-related root cause.","examples":["Compare migration state across database instances.","Test old and new application versions against intermediate schemas.","Identify failed or partially applied migrations."],"invalid_examples":["Checking only the final schema definition.","Assuming migration completion from deployment status alone."],"problem":"A deployment causes database errors during rollout.","failure":"The investigation ignores intermediate schema states.","cause":"Application and database versions were incompatible during migration.","solution":"Reconstruct migration history and test the exact application-schema version combination.","alternatives":["Expand-contract migrations","Backward-compatible schema changes","Migration dry runs"],"tradeoffs":"Compatibility-first migrations increase rollout complexity but reduce transient version conflicts.","constraints":["Migrations may be irreversible.","Large data transformations may require prolonged transitional states."],"edge_cases":["Partial migration","Rollback after schema change","Mixed application versions","Long-running transactions"],"security":"Migration diagnostics must protect database credentials and sensitive schema details.","performance":"Separate metadata-only migration checks from expensive data validation.","testing":"Test migration upgrades, downgrades where supported, interruption, and mixed-version operation.","verification":"Verify both schema state and application compatibility at each migration stage.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Migration metadata","Application version"],"prerequisites":["Schema evolution debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B73-R02","D25-B75-R02"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"PostgreSQL Documentation","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-alter.html","relevance":"Documents database schema alteration behavior relevant to migration analysis."}],"evidence":"Schema changes can create intermediate states during rolling deployments and therefore require version-pair analysis.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B76-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Storage and Persistence Diagnosis","concept":"Persistence evidence retention","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"What persistence evidence should be retained for difficult database failures?","answer":"Retain identifiers and metadata sufficient to correlate the logical operation, transaction or request context, persistence outcome, schema version, and relevant storage state without indiscriminately copying sensitive payloads. Evidence should be retained long enough to support causal reconstruction and incident review.","explanation":"Database failures often cannot be reconstructed after the fact if transaction identity and schema state are lost. However, retaining complete records can create privacy and security risks.","principle":"Retain causal metadata rather than unnecessary sensitive payload duplication.","rule":"Diagnostic retention should preserve enough information to reconstruct the failure while minimizing sensitive data exposure.","examples":["Retain transaction identifiers and schema versions.","Record commit outcome and timing.","Store hashes or redacted representations when full values are unnecessary."],"invalid_examples":["Logging complete database rows for every transaction.","Deleting all transaction metadata immediately after an error."],"problem":"A persistence incident cannot be reconstructed after logs expire.","failure":"Only application-level error messages were retained.","cause":"Diagnostic retention lacked correlation metadata.","solution":"Define a bounded persistence-debug evidence policy covering identifiers, outcomes, schema state, and retention.","alternatives":["Audit logs","Database statement metadata","Structured transaction telemetry"],"tradeoffs":"Longer retention improves forensic capability but increases storage, privacy, and compliance obligations.","constraints":["Retention must follow applicable data-governance requirements.","Sensitive values should be minimized."],"edge_cases":["Highly regulated records","Bulk operations","Cross-system transactions","Delayed incident discovery"],"security":"Encrypt and access-control diagnostic evidence containing sensitive metadata.","performance":"Retain compact metadata and sample expensive payload-level evidence.","testing":"Test that required evidence remains available after realistic log rotation and retention periods.","verification":"Verify that retained metadata is sufficient to reconstruct representative persistence failures.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Observability retention policy","Persistence metadata"],"prerequisites":["Evidence preservation"],"relationships":["D25-B14-R01","D25-B76-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"NIST SP 800-92","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-92/final","relevance":"Provides guidance on computer security log management and retention concepts."}],"evidence":"Diagnostic evidence must balance reconstructability with data minimization and retention constraints.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B77-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Security-Sensitive Debugging","concept":"Security boundary preservation during debugging","knowledge_type":"security_principle","question":"How should debugging be performed without weakening security controls?","answer":"Debugging should preserve authentication, authorization, isolation, encryption, and audit controls while collecting the minimum additional evidence required to test hypotheses. Diagnostic convenience must not become a reason to bypass production security boundaries.","explanation":"Debugging often tempts engineers to disable authentication, expose secrets, increase privileges, or capture sensitive payloads. Such actions can create a second incident and invalidate evidence by changing the execution conditions.","principle":"A debugging intervention must not silently remove the control whose behavior is under investigation.","rule":"Prefer observability and controlled reproduction over weakening security controls in production.","examples":["Capture authorization decision metadata rather than credentials.","Use a sanitized reproduction environment for verbose traces.","Record policy identifiers and decision outcomes instead of secret policy inputs."],"invalid_examples":["Disabling authentication to reproduce an authorization failure.","Logging production passwords or tokens for debugging."],"problem":"A security-sensitive failure is difficult to diagnose under normal controls.","failure":"Debugging removes the control and changes the system behavior.","cause":"Diagnostic method violated the security boundary.","solution":"Use minimally invasive instrumentation and controlled environments that preserve equivalent security semantics.","alternatives":["Security-safe tracepoints","Sanitized reproduction","Privileged forensic tooling"],"tradeoffs":"Less intrusive diagnostics may provide less detail and require more careful hypothesis design.","constraints":["Security controls remain active.","Sensitive evidence requires strict access control and retention."],"edge_cases":["Authorization bugs","Credential propagation","Tenant isolation failures","Encrypted payload handling"],"security":"Never expose secrets or disable critical controls solely for convenience; preserve auditability of diagnostic interventions.","performance":"Use targeted instrumentation rather than globally verbose security diagnostics.","testing":"Test diagnostic procedures under active security controls.","verification":"Verify that the diagnostic intervention did not alter the security property being investigated.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Security policy","Observability controls"],"prerequisites":["Secure debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B29-R01","D25-B67-R03"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r2/final","relevance":"Provides incident-response guidance relevant to preserving evidence and controlling incident handling."}],"evidence":"Diagnostic actions can change system behavior and security posture, so debugging must preserve relevant controls.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B77-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Security-Sensitive Debugging","concept":"Secret exposure through diagnostics","knowledge_type":"security_failure_mode","question":"How can debugging instrumentation accidentally expose secrets?","answer":"Diagnostics can expose secrets through logs, traces, exception messages, dumps, environment snapshots, headers, database statements, or serialized objects. Debugging systems should classify sensitive fields and apply explicit redaction or exclusion rules before evidence leaves the protected execution boundary.","explanation":"Sensitive values can appear indirectly through object serialization or generic debugging facilities even when engineers did not intentionally log them.","principle":"Sensitive-data protection must be designed into diagnostic collection rather than applied only after collection.","rule":"Diagnostic serialization must have an explicit policy for secret and sensitive-field handling.","examples":["Redact authorization headers.","Exclude credential-bearing environment variables from dumps.","Use field-level allowlists for structured diagnostic payloads."],"invalid_examples":["Logging complete request objects by default.","Assuming exception strings cannot contain secrets."],"problem":"A debugging trace contains sensitive credentials.","failure":"Generic object serialization captured secret-bearing fields.","cause":"No diagnostic data classification or redaction policy existed.","solution":"Use structured, allowlisted diagnostics and test redaction behavior.","alternatives":["Field-level redaction","Tokenization","Diagnostic allowlists"],"tradeoffs":"Redaction can reduce diagnostic detail but substantially lowers exposure risk.","constraints":["Redaction rules must cover nested and dynamically generated structures.","Hashing is not automatically safe for all sensitive values."],"edge_cases":["Nested objects","Binary payloads","Exception chains","Third-party library logs"],"security":"Treat diagnostic channels as potential sensitive-data sinks and apply access control and retention controls.","performance":"Redaction should be efficient enough for high-volume telemetry paths.","testing":"Use secret-bearing fixtures to test logs, traces, dumps, and exception serialization.","verification":"Verify that representative sensitive values cannot appear in diagnostic outputs.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Telemetry serialization","Data classification"],"prerequisites":["Secure logging"],"relationships":["D25-B77-R01","D25-B67-R03"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet","url":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html","relevance":"Provides guidance on secure logging and avoiding sensitive information exposure."}],"evidence":"Generic diagnostic serialization can unintentionally propagate sensitive fields into logs and traces.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B77-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Security-Sensitive Debugging","concept":"Authorization-path debugging","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should authorization failures be debugged without exposing authorization secrets?","answer":"Record the authorization policy or rule identity, subject and resource classification at an appropriate abstraction level, decision result, relevant policy version, and evaluation context while excluding credentials and unnecessary sensitive attributes. Reconstruct the decision from these controlled facts.","explanation":"Authorization bugs often depend on policy precedence, stale identity data, resource classification, or evaluation order. Full raw policy inputs are not always necessary to diagnose the decision.","principle":"Authorization debugging should expose decision provenance without exposing authentication secrets.","rule":"A security decision must be traceable to its policy version and evaluation context while minimizing sensitive input disclosure.","examples":["Record policy version and decision reason code.","Compare authorization decisions across policy versions.","Track identity-attribute freshness metadata."],"invalid_examples":["Logging access tokens to determine which user was authorized.","Disabling authorization to inspect downstream behavior."],"problem":"Only certain identities are denied access unexpectedly.","failure":"The investigation lacks policy-version and decision context.","cause":"Authorization telemetry is too coarse.","solution":"Add privacy-preserving authorization decision metadata and reconstruct the policy evaluation.","alternatives":["Policy decision logs","Decision identifiers","Controlled policy simulators"],"tradeoffs":"More detailed decision telemetry improves diagnosis but can reveal sensitive access patterns.","constraints":["Identity and resource attributes require strict minimization.","Authorization changes require controlled rollout."],"edge_cases":["Nested roles","Deny-overrides-allow policies","Stale identity claims","Cross-tenant resources"],"security":"Never log credentials or raw authentication tokens; protect authorization decision records as security-sensitive telemetry.","performance":"Use compact decision identifiers and policy hashes.","testing":"Test authorization diagnostics for allowed, denied, stale, and conflicting policy conditions.","verification":"Verify that the recorded decision metadata reconstructs the observed authorization outcome.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Authorization engine","Policy versioning"],"prerequisites":["Security-safe diagnostics"],"relationships":["D25-B74-R02","D25-B77-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"NIST SP 800-53","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-53/rev-5/final","relevance":"Provides security-control concepts relevant to access control and auditability."}],"evidence":"Authorization outcomes depend on policy and context; decision provenance can be recorded without retaining authentication secrets.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B77-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Security-Sensitive Debugging","concept":"Debugging under tenant isolation","knowledge_type":"security_principle","question":"How should multi-tenant debugging preserve tenant isolation?","answer":"Diagnostic evidence should retain tenant context only at the minimum granularity necessary for correlation, and diagnostic tools must enforce the same tenant authorization boundaries as the application unless an explicitly controlled forensic role is authorized.","explanation":"Cross-tenant debugging shortcuts can expose data and invalidate isolation guarantees. A diagnostic system is itself part of the security boundary.","principle":"Observability and debugging infrastructure must respect data-isolation boundaries.","rule":"No diagnostic query should reveal another tenant's evidence merely because it is operationally convenient.","examples":["Partition diagnostic indexes by tenant authorization.","Use tenant-scoped correlation identifiers.","Require controlled forensic access for cross-tenant incident investigation."],"invalid_examples":["Creating a global debug endpoint without tenant filtering.","Copying another tenant's payload into a developer test environment without authorization."],"problem":"An engineer needs to investigate a tenant-specific production failure.","failure":"The diagnostic workflow bypasses tenant isolation.","cause":"Debugging infrastructure was treated as outside the application security model.","solution":"Implement tenant-aware evidence access and controlled escalation for authorized forensic investigations.","alternatives":["Tenant-scoped observability","Redacted aggregate diagnostics","Break-glass forensic workflow"],"tradeoffs":"Strict isolation can make cross-tenant correlation harder during platform-wide incidents.","constraints":["Cross-tenant access requires explicit authorization and auditing.","Sensitive data should not be copied unnecessarily."],"edge_cases":["Platform-wide incidents","Shared infrastructure","Tenant-specific feature flags","Cross-tenant dependencies"],"security":"Apply least privilege, tenant isolation, access logging, and retention controls to diagnostic systems.","performance":"Use partitioning and indexed tenant identifiers to keep scoped diagnostic queries efficient.","testing":"Attempt unauthorized cross-tenant diagnostic access and verify denial.","verification":"Verify that diagnostic queries enforce the same isolation model as protected application data.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Tenant identity","Diagnostic authorization"],"prerequisites":["Multi-tenant security"],"relationships":["D25-B77-R01","D25-B68-R02"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"NIST SP 800-53","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-53/rev-5/final","relevance":"Provides least-privilege and access-control concepts applicable to diagnostic systems."}],"evidence":"Debugging infrastructure can become a data-access path and therefore must preserve tenant isolation.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B77-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Security-Sensitive Debugging","concept":"Evidence chain integrity","knowledge_type":"verification_principle","question":"Why must security-sensitive debugging evidence have integrity guarantees?","answer":"Evidence used for security or high-impact root-cause conclusions should preserve provenance, timestamps, source identity, and integrity metadata so investigators can distinguish original observations from altered, incomplete, or reconstructed artifacts.","explanation":"Diagnostic evidence can influence incident response and security decisions. If evidence can be modified without detection, later reasoning may be based on unreliable observations.","principle":"Security-relevant diagnostic evidence requires provenance and integrity, not merely availability.","rule":"Evidence supporting high-impact conclusions should be attributable and tamper-evident to an appropriate level.","examples":["Associate events with source identities and timestamps.","Use integrity-protected audit records.","Record when evidence was transformed or redacted."],"invalid_examples":["Editing production logs manually without preserving the original.","Treating screenshots without provenance as authoritative forensic evidence."],"problem":"Investigators disagree about whether diagnostic evidence was altered.","failure":"Evidence provenance and integrity were not preserved.","cause":"Diagnostic records lacked chain-of-custody metadata.","solution":"Use append-oriented or integrity-protected evidence storage and record transformations.","alternatives":["WORM storage","Cryptographic hashes","Signed audit records"],"tradeoffs":"Integrity controls add storage, operational, and key-management complexity.","constraints":["Clock synchronization may be imperfect.","Redaction and retention transformations must remain attributable."],"edge_cases":["Log aggregation","Sampling","Redaction","Clock skew"],"security":"Protect integrity keys and audit access to diagnostic evidence.","performance":"Apply cryptographic integrity selectively according to evidence criticality.","testing":"Test tamper detection and provenance preservation across collection and transport.","verification":"Verify evidence source, transformation history, and integrity before using it for high-impact conclusions.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Audit infrastructure","Integrity mechanism"],"prerequisites":["Evidence preservation"],"relationships":["D25-B14-R01","D25-B77-R02"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"NIST SP 800-92","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-92/final","relevance":"Provides log-management concepts relevant to integrity and lifecycle of security evidence."}],"evidence":"Evidence provenance and integrity determine whether later conclusions can be trusted.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B78-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Resource-Lifecycle Diagnosis","concept":"Resource ownership-chain reconstruction","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should resource-lifecycle failures be debugged?","answer":"Reconstruct resource creation, ownership transfer, use, release, and finalization events, then identify the first lifecycle transition that violates the ownership contract. This applies to files, sockets, locks, memory, handles, tasks, database connections, and similar resources.","explanation":"Resource failures often appear as exhaustion or invalid access long after the lifecycle error occurred. Ownership-chain reconstruction moves diagnosis from symptom to lifecycle defect.","principle":"Resource correctness depends on lifecycle ownership, not only allocation and deallocation counts.","rule":"Every resource lifecycle should have an identifiable owner or ownership protocol at each stage.","examples":["Trace which component owns a database connection.","Identify a file descriptor transferred without a matching close responsibility.","Track task cancellation ownership."],"invalid_examples":["Increasing resource limits without identifying the lifecycle defect.","Closing resources from arbitrary locations without ownership analysis."],"problem":"Resource exhaustion occurs after long-running operation.","failure":"The investigation focuses on capacity rather than lifecycle.","cause":"Ownership transfer or release was incorrect.","solution":"Trace lifecycle events and identify the first ownership invariant violation.","alternatives":["Reference counting","RAII-style ownership","Lifecycle instrumentation"],"tradeoffs":"Lifecycle instrumentation improves diagnosis but can add overhead and complexity.","constraints":["Ownership models differ by resource type.","Some resources are intentionally shared."],"edge_cases":["Shared ownership","Cancellation","Exception paths","Process termination"],"security":"Resource ownership should prevent unauthorized access to protected resources.","performance":"Use counters and sampled lifecycle traces for high-frequency resources.","testing":"Test normal, exceptional, cancellation, and shutdown lifecycle paths.","verification":"Verify that every resource reaches a valid terminal lifecycle state or an explicitly managed shared state.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Resource ownership model","Lifecycle telemetry"],"prerequisites":["Resource debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B23-R01","D25-B34-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"Python Documentation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/","relevance":"Documents resource-management and context-management mechanisms relevant to lifecycle debugging."}],"evidence":"Resource lifecycle defects frequently manifest later than the operation that created them, making ownership reconstruction valuable.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B78-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Resource-Lifecycle Diagnosis","concept":"Capacity failure versus leak failure","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should a debugger distinguish capacity shortage from a resource leak?","answer":"Measure resource consumption over time and correlate it with workload, allocation rate, release rate, and steady-state occupancy. A leak generally produces persistent accumulation not explained by legitimate workload, while a capacity shortage can occur even with correct release behavior when demand exceeds available capacity.","explanation":"Both failures can produce exhaustion symptoms, but their remedies differ. Increasing capacity can hide a leak, while fixing lifecycle code cannot solve sustained legitimate overload.","principle":"Resource exhaustion must be classified by temporal consumption and lifecycle behavior before remediation.","rule":"Do not infer a leak from exhaustion alone.","examples":["Compare allocation and release counts over time.","Measure occupancy after workload returns to baseline.","Test whether resource usage stabilizes under steady workload."],"invalid_examples":["Calling every out-of-memory incident a memory leak.","Increasing pool size without measuring release behavior."],"problem":"A connection pool becomes exhausted.","failure":"The team cannot distinguish leaked connections from legitimate saturation.","cause":"No lifecycle and demand measurements exist.","solution":"Compare acquisition, release, occupancy, and workload patterns under controlled conditions.","alternatives":["Pool diagnostics","Heap profiling","Load normalization"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed resource accounting adds overhead but prevents incorrect remediation.","constraints":["Some resources have delayed or asynchronous release.","Capacity depends on concurrency and workload shape."],"edge_cases":["Bursty workloads","Warm pools","Delayed garbage collection","External quotas"],"security":"Resource exhaustion can become a denial-of-service vector and should be analyzed with access controls intact.","performance":"Use low-overhead counters for continuous monitoring and deeper profiling during incidents.","testing":"Test both sustained legitimate load and intentionally leaked resources.","verification":"Verify the classification by observing resource behavior after workload normalization.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Resource metrics","Workload measurements"],"prerequisites":["Resource lifecycle analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B78-R01","D25-B34-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"Linux perf","url":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/perf.1.html","relevance":"Provides performance-observation tooling concepts useful for resource and workload diagnosis."}],"evidence":"Temporal resource behavior distinguishes accumulation caused by leaks from demand-driven capacity exhaustion.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B78-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Resource-Lifecycle Diagnosis","concept":"Pool fragmentation diagnosis","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"What is resource-pool fragmentation in debugging?","answer":"Pool fragmentation occurs when sufficient aggregate capacity exists but available capacity is divided into unusable portions because allocation requirements do not match the remaining resource units or because resources are held in incompatible partitions.","explanation":"Fragmentation can resemble exhaustion even when aggregate usage appears below capacity. Debugging should inspect allocation sizes, partitioning, locality, and free-resource distribution.","principle":"Aggregate capacity does not guarantee allocatable capacity.","rule":"Resource availability should be measured in the units required by allocation operations, not only as total free capacity.","examples":["Analyze memory fragmentation for large contiguous allocations.","Compare connection pools partitioned by tenant or workload.","Inspect shard-local capacity when global capacity appears sufficient."],"invalid_examples":["Concluding that capacity is sufficient from a global percentage alone.","Increasing total capacity without examining partition utilization."],"problem":"Large allocations fail despite substantial total free resources.","failure":"The investigation uses aggregate capacity metrics only.","cause":"Resource availability is fragmented across incompatible allocation units.","solution":"Measure allocatable capacity per relevant partition and allocation size.","alternatives":["Compaction","Pool rebalancing","Allocation strategy changes"],"tradeoffs":"Rebalancing can improve utilization but may increase movement, latency, or complexity.","constraints":["Some resources cannot be compacted or moved safely.","Partitioning may exist for isolation reasons."],"edge_cases":["Tenant pools","NUMA memory","Shard-local quotas","Fixed-size buffers"],"security":"Do not remove isolation partitions merely to increase aggregate utilization.","performance":"Monitor fragmentation with lightweight distribution metrics.","testing":"Exercise varied allocation sizes and partition distributions.","verification":"Verify that allocation failures correlate with unusable free-resource distribution rather than total capacity alone.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Resource allocator","Capacity metrics"],"prerequisites":["Resource capacity analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B78-R02","D25-B34-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"Linux kernel documentation","url":"https://docs.kernel.org/","relevance":"Provides authoritative concepts for Linux resource management and memory behavior."}],"evidence":"Resource allocators can fail due to distribution constraints even when aggregate capacity remains available.","confidence":0.93,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B78-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Resource-Lifecycle Diagnosis","concept":"Resource starvation diagnosis","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"How should resource starvation be distinguished from resource exhaustion?","answer":"Resource starvation occurs when a task cannot obtain sufficient service or scheduling opportunity despite resources remaining available elsewhere. Diagnosis should examine allocation policy, priority, fairness, queueing, ownership, and contention rather than only total resource usage.","explanation":"A low global utilization metric can coexist with starvation when scheduling or partitioning prevents a workload from accessing the available resource.","principle":"Availability of a resource in aggregate does not prove availability to the affected workload.","rule":"Resource diagnosis must include allocation policy and workload-specific access to capacity.","examples":["A low-priority task waits indefinitely while high-priority tasks consume capacity.","One tenant has unused quota while another tenant is starved by partitioning.","A worker queue prevents a task from reaching available workers."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming there is no capacity issue because system-wide utilization is low.","Increasing global capacity without examining scheduling policy."],"problem":"A workload experiences extreme latency despite low global resource utilization.","failure":"Investigation focuses on aggregate utilization.","cause":"Scheduling or allocation policy prevents access to available capacity.","solution":"Analyze workload-specific queueing, priority, fairness, and partition constraints.","alternatives":["Fair scheduling","Quota rebalancing","Priority correction"],"tradeoffs":"Improving fairness can reduce peak throughput for prioritized workloads.","constraints":["Priority may be intentional.","Starvation prevention must preserve critical workload guarantees."],"edge_cases":["Priority inversion","Tenant quotas","Work stealing","Resource reservations"],"security":"Resource isolation should not be weakened to solve starvation.","performance":"Measure per-workload wait time and service time, not only utilization.","testing":"Test fairness, priority, starvation, and contention scenarios.","verification":"Verify that the affected workload gains access to eligible capacity under controlled conditions.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Scheduler","Resource policy","Queue telemetry"],"prerequisites":["Concurrency and capacity debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B78-R02","D25-B61-R02"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"Linux Scheduler Documentation","url":"https://docs.kernel.org/scheduler/","relevance":"Documents scheduling concepts relevant to workload starvation and resource access."}],"evidence":"Workload-specific scheduling and allocation can cause starvation even when aggregate capacity remains available.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B78-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Resource-Lifecycle Diagnosis","concept":"Resource amplification across layers","knowledge_type":"root_cause_pattern","question":"What is resource amplification in layered systems?","answer":"Resource amplification occurs when one logical operation consumes multiple physical resources across layers, causing downstream exhaustion at a multiplier greater than expected. Debugging should map the operation-to-resource fan-out across the entire stack.","explanation":"A single request may consume threads, connections, memory buffers, file descriptors, queue slots, and downstream capacity. Local metrics can look acceptable while the aggregate resource multiplier causes failure elsewhere.","principle":"Logical workload must be mapped to its cross-layer resource footprint.","rule":"Capacity diagnosis should account for resource fan-out and nested concurrency rather than only request counts.","examples":["One request opens multiple database connections through parallel suboperations.","A single message creates many downstream tasks.","Retries multiply external calls per logical request."],"invalid_examples":["Sizing a database pool from incoming request count alone.","Ignoring retry fan-out in capacity calculations."],"problem":"Downstream resources exhaust under moderate apparent traffic.","failure":"Capacity planning uses only top-level request volume.","cause":"Cross-layer resource amplification is hidden.","solution":"Measure resource consumption per logical operation and identify fan-out multipliers.","alternatives":["Distributed load modeling","Concurrency budgets","Resource attribution"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed attribution improves diagnosis but requires cross-layer correlation.","constraints":["Fan-out may vary by input and failure state.","Retries and recovery can dynamically change multipliers."],"edge_cases":["Retry storms","Fan-out/fan-in","Batch expansion","Recursive calls"],"security":"Amplification can create denial-of-service conditions; enforce bounded concurrency and quotas.","performance":"Track resource-per-operation distributions rather than only averages.","testing":"Test worst-case fan-out and failure-induced amplification.","verification":"Verify that observed resource consumption matches the reconstructed amplification chain.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Cross-layer correlation","Resource metrics"],"prerequisites":["Distributed tracing","Capacity analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B37-R01","D25-B68-R04"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/","relevance":"Establishes distributed tracing concepts useful for associating logical requests with downstream work."}],"evidence":"Cross-layer resource attribution requires correlating one logical operation with its distributed execution tree.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B79-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Deployment and Release Regression Diagnosis","concept":"Release-window fault localization","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should failures correlated with a release window be analyzed?","answer":"Align failure onset with deployment events while controlling for traffic, configuration, dependencies, topology, feature flags, and unrelated infrastructure changes. Release timing is a useful localization signal but does not establish causality by itself.","explanation":"Multiple changes can occur within the same operational window. Causal debugging requires identifying which changed variable can reproduce the observed behavior.","principle":"Temporal proximity narrows the candidate set but does not prove causation.","rule":"A release-related hypothesis should be tested against alternative changes occurring during the same interval.","examples":["Build a change timeline across application, configuration, infrastructure, and dependencies.","Compare affected and unaffected instances during rollout.","Reproduce the suspected release delta in isolation."],"invalid_examples":["Automatically reverting the most recent release and declaring root cause.","Ignoring simultaneous infrastructure changes."],"problem":"Failure rate increases shortly after deployment.","failure":"The latest deployment is treated as proven cause.","cause":"Temporal correlation was mistaken for causal evidence.","solution":"Construct a complete change timeline and isolate candidate deltas.","alternatives":["Controlled rollback","Differential reproduction","Canary analysis"],"tradeoffs":"Broader change analysis takes longer but reduces false attribution.","constraints":["Rollback may alter evidence and introduce additional changes.","Multiple releases may overlap."],"edge_cases":["Long deployment windows","Progressive rollout","Independent infrastructure changes","Delayed failures"],"security":"Preserve audit records of release and configuration changes.","performance":"Automate change-event correlation to reduce manual incident overhead.","testing":"Test release changes against representative workloads and environments.","verification":"Verify the candidate change through controlled comparison or reproduction.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Deployment history","Change-management metadata"],"prerequisites":["Differential debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B43-R01","D25-B72-R03"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/","relevance":"Provides standardized telemetry concepts useful for correlating runtime behavior with service metadata."}],"evidence":"Release events provide temporal evidence but causal attribution requires controlled comparison against competing changes.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B79-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Deployment and Release Regression Diagnosis","concept":"Change-point detection for debugging","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How can a behavioral change point help debug production regressions?","answer":"Identify the earliest time interval where a relevant failure, latency, resource, or correctness metric changes materially relative to its prior baseline, then correlate that interval with system changes and workload shifts. The change point is evidence for localization, not proof of cause.","explanation":"A change-point view prevents the investigation from anchoring on the most visible incident time when the underlying degradation began earlier.","principle":"The onset of behavioral change is often more informative than the time the incident was declared.","rule":"Analyze pre-change and post-change behavior with comparable workload normalization.","examples":["Identify the first sustained increase in error rate.","Find the earliest latency distribution shift.","Compare resource occupancy before and after a configuration change."],"invalid_examples":["Using incident declaration time as failure onset.","Comparing raw counts when traffic volume changed substantially."],"problem":"The reported incident begins hours after the underlying degradation started.","failure":"Investigation uses the alert time as the causal boundary.","cause":"No behavioral baseline reconstruction.","solution":"Analyze normalized time series and correlate the earliest meaningful change with system events.","alternatives":["Statistical change-point methods","Rolling baselines","Segmented telemetry analysis"],"tradeoffs":"Automated change detection can surface subtle shifts but may produce false positives during legitimate workload changes.","constraints":["Baselines must account for seasonality and workload changes.","Detection thresholds should not be treated as causal proof."],"edge_cases":["Periodic workloads","Traffic growth","Gradual degradation","Multiple simultaneous changes"],"security":"Telemetry used for change detection should follow the same data-access controls as other operational evidence.","performance":"Use aggregated metrics for broad detection and traces for targeted confirmation.","testing":"Test detection against synthetic shifts and normal workload changes.","verification":"Verify candidate change points against independent evidence and controlled reproduction.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Time-series telemetry","Change history"],"prerequisites":["Observability analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B79-R01","D25-B38-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"Prometheus Documentation","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/","relevance":"Documents time-series monitoring concepts useful for temporal debugging and baseline comparison."}],"evidence":"Temporal analysis can localize when behavior changed, after which causal variables can be investigated.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B79-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Deployment and Release Regression Diagnosis","concept":"Gradual regression diagnosis","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"Why are gradual regressions harder to diagnose than abrupt failures?","answer":"Gradual regressions can remain within alert thresholds while accumulating effects from leaks, drift, workload growth, fragmentation, or feedback loops. Diagnosis should compare long-term trends, normalized resource behavior, and repeated workload cohorts rather than relying only on incident snapshots.","explanation":"A gradual degradation may have no single obvious trigger. Root-cause analysis must identify the accumulation mechanism and its relationship to the observed trajectory.","principle":"Slow failures require longitudinal evidence rather than point-in-time inspection.","rule":"When degradation is gradual, analyze rates of change and accumulation variables in addition to absolute values.","examples":["Track memory occupancy after equivalent workloads.","Compare queue depth growth against arrival and service rates.","Analyze increasing retry counts over successive deployments."],"invalid_examples":["Taking a single heap snapshot during a multi-day leak.","Searching only for changes at the time the alert fired."],"problem":"Performance or reliability degrades slowly over days.","failure":"Investigation uses only recent snapshots.","cause":"Accumulation dynamics were not analyzed.","solution":"Reconstruct long-term trends and identify variables whose accumulation predicts the failure.","alternatives":["Longitudinal profiling","Cohort analysis","Resource slope monitoring"],"tradeoffs":"Long-term telemetry increases retention costs but improves detection of slow defects.","constraints":["Seasonality and workload growth must be normalized.","Historical telemetry may have changed schema."],"edge_cases":["Memory fragmentation","Cache growth","Slow queue accumulation","Data-volume growth"],"security":"Historical telemetry must follow retention and access policies.","performance":"Store aggregated long-term trends and retain detailed evidence selectively.","testing":"Run extended-duration tests for suspected accumulation defects.","verification":"Verify that the suspected accumulation mechanism predicts the observed degradation.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Historical telemetry","Workload normalization"],"prerequisites":["Trend analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B78-R02","D25-B79-R02"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"Prometheus Documentation","url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/","relevance":"Provides time-series monitoring concepts appropriate for longitudinal debugging."}],"evidence":"Accumulation defects require time-series evidence to distinguish gradual causal trends from transient anomalies.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B79-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Deployment and Release Regression Diagnosis","concept":"Rollback ambiguity","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"Why does a successful rollback not necessarily prove the rolled-back release was the root cause?","answer":"A rollback can remove several correlated changes at once, alter traffic, clear caches, restart processes, change resource state, or modify timing. Improvement after rollback is therefore evidence that the combined intervention mattered, but it does not uniquely identify which change was causal.","explanation":"Rollback is both a remediation and an experiment, but it is usually a multi-variable intervention. Additional isolation is required for precise root-cause attribution.","principle":"A rollback result identifies a causal intervention set, not necessarily a unique causal variable.","rule":"Treat rollback success as supporting evidence and continue differential isolation when exact causality matters.","examples":["Rollback changes both application version and process state.","Restart during rollback clears an accumulated cache.","Traffic redistribution accompanies rollback."],"invalid_examples":["Concluding that a single code change caused the incident solely because rollback restored service.","Ignoring cache or state reset effects."],"problem":"Service recovers immediately after rollback.","failure":"The investigation closes without isolating the causal change.","cause":"Multi-variable intervention was treated as a single-variable experiment.","solution":"Compare rollback components and reproduce candidate changes independently.","alternatives":["Forward fix in isolated environment","Feature-flag reversal","Selective component rollback"],"tradeoffs":"Precise attribution takes longer but produces stronger prevention knowledge.","constraints":["Incident safety may require immediate rollback before causal isolation.","Some production state changes cannot be reversed exactly."],"edge_cases":["Cache reset","Database migration","Traffic shift","Process restart"],"security":"Rollback procedures must preserve security controls and auditability.","performance":"Prioritize safe restoration during incidents, then perform causal isolation afterward.","testing":"Reproduce rollback-associated state changes separately.","verification":"Verify which individual change or state reset explains recovery.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Change history","Deployment controls"],"prerequisites":["Causal debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B79-R01","D25-B43-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/","relevance":"Provides distributed execution tracing concepts useful for separating correlated runtime changes."}],"evidence":"Rollback frequently changes multiple variables simultaneously, limiting causal specificity.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B79-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Deployment and Release Regression Diagnosis","concept":"Fix-forward diagnostic validation","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"How should a fix-forward change be validated when rollback is unavailable or undesirable?","answer":"Define the suspected causal mechanism, apply the smallest controlled change that addresses it, preserve the pre-change evidence, and verify that the failure metric and relevant invariants improve without introducing new defects. The validation should compare against a suitable baseline.","explanation":"Fix-forward debugging is stronger when the patch targets a specific hypothesis rather than simply changing multiple variables until symptoms disappear.","principle":"A diagnostic fix should test a causal hypothesis, not merely suppress a symptom.","rule":"Every fix-forward intervention should have explicit success criteria and regression checks.","examples":["Patch a suspected boundary condition and replay the failing input.","Change one configuration value predicted to eliminate the fault.","Verify both failure removal and invariant preservation."],"invalid_examples":["Deploying unrelated changes together and attributing recovery to all of them.","Declaring success because the alert clears once."],"problem":"Rollback is impossible because database or external state has changed.","failure":"Teams apply broad fixes without preserving causal evidence.","cause":"No hypothesis-driven fix-forward process exists.","solution":"Define a minimal intervention and validate predicted behavior against the original failure evidence.","alternatives":["Canary fix-forward","Shadow validation","Controlled traffic exposure"],"tradeoffs":"Minimal changes reduce ambiguity but may take longer than broad remediation.","constraints":["Safety and service restoration take precedence during active incidents.","Persistent state may prevent exact reproduction."],"edge_cases":["Irreversible migrations","External dependencies","Partial rollout","Data already transformed"],"security":"Ensure fix-forward changes preserve security and authorization invariants.","performance":"Validate latency and resource impact alongside correctness.","testing":"Run targeted regression, invariant, and representative workload tests.","verification":"Verify both symptom resolution and preservation of relevant system invariants.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Regression evidence","Controlled deployment"],"prerequisites":["Hypothesis-driven debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B48-R01","D25-B79-R04"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/","relevance":"Provides observability concepts useful for measuring pre- and post-change behavior."}],"evidence":"Hypothesis-driven changes provide stronger diagnostic information than uncontrolled multi-variable fixes.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B80-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Integrated Causal Debugging","concept":"Evidence-weighted root-cause graph","knowledge_type":"knowledge_representation","question":"How should complex debugging evidence be represented as a causal graph?","answer":"Represent observations, hypotheses, events, state transitions, dependencies, and verified relationships as distinct nodes or edges with provenance, timestamps, confidence, and contradiction metadata. Separate observed facts from inferred causal relationships.","explanation":"Complex incidents often contain multiple interacting causes and incomplete evidence. A causal graph prevents hypotheses from being mistaken for facts and makes evidence relationships explicit.","principle":"Root-cause reasoning should preserve the distinction between observation, inference, and verification.","rule":"Every causal edge should identify the evidence supporting it and whether the relationship is observed, inferred, or experimentally supported.","examples":["Link a deployment event to an observed configuration change.","Link a configuration difference to a reproduced failure as an experimentally supported relationship.","Represent competing hypotheses separately."],"invalid_examples":["Storing one flat root-cause string.","Treating an unverified hypothesis as a confirmed causal edge."],"problem":"An incident involves many interacting signals and competing explanations.","failure":"The investigation collapses evidence into an unsupported single narrative.","cause":"Relationships and epistemic status are not represented explicitly.","solution":"Construct a provenance-aware evidence graph with explicit causal confidence and verification status.","alternatives":["Incident knowledge graph","Evidence ledger","Structured investigation record"],"tradeoffs":"Graph representation improves traceability but requires more metadata and relationship management.","constraints":["Do not create causal edges without supporting evidence.","Graph semantics must remain stable across investigations."],"edge_cases":["Conflicting evidence","Multiple root causes","Missing telemetry","Delayed observations"],"security":"Restrict access to sensitive evidence nodes while preserving authorized relationship visibility.","performance":"Index high-value causal relationships and use compact evidence references.","testing":"Test graph construction with known facts, hypotheses, contradictions, and verified relationships.","verification":"Verify that every confirmed causal relationship can be traced to supporting evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence provenance","Relationship model"],"prerequisites":["Causal debugging","Knowledge representation"],"relationships":["D25-B40-R01","D25-B49-R01","D25-B70-R02"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/","relevance":"Provides standardized observability concepts that can serve as evidence entities and attributes."}],"evidence":"Explicit evidence relationships reduce the risk of conflating observed telemetry with causal inference.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B80-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Integrated Causal Debugging","concept":"Competing-hypothesis management","knowledge_type":"reasoning_method","question":"How should competing root-cause hypotheses be managed during complex debugging?","answer":"Maintain multiple explicit hypotheses, list predictions and required evidence for each, record evidence that supports or contradicts each hypothesis, and eliminate hypotheses only when evidence materially conflicts with their required conditions.","explanation":"Premature commitment to the first plausible explanation creates confirmation bias and causes investigators to reinterpret contradictory evidence. Structured hypothesis management keeps the search falsifiable.","principle":"A plausible explanation is not a verified explanation.","rule":"Each active hypothesis should expose at least one discriminating prediction or evidence requirement.","examples":["Hypothesis A predicts failures only on a specific dependency version.","Hypothesis B predicts failures across all versions but only under high concurrency.","Collect evidence that distinguishes the predictions."],"invalid_examples":["Keeping only the most intuitive explanation.","Calling a hypothesis confirmed because no alternative has been found."],"problem":"Several plausible causes explain the same symptom.","failure":"Investigation becomes anchored on one narrative.","cause":"Hypotheses were not explicitly separated.","solution":"Maintain competing hypotheses and prioritize discriminating evidence.","alternatives":["Bayesian hypothesis tracking","Decision trees","Causal experiment planning"],"tradeoffs":"Explicit hypothesis tracking requires additional documentation but reduces reasoning errors.","constraints":["Evidence may be incomplete or correlated.","Absence of evidence is not automatically evidence of absence."],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous causes","Observability gaps","Confounded experiments","Non-reproducible failures"],"security":"Security hypotheses should not be discarded merely because exploitation was not observed; distinguish lack of evidence from evidence of absence.","performance":"Prioritize high-information experiments that distinguish multiple hypotheses at once.","testing":"Design tests around discriminating predictions rather than generic regression coverage.","verification":"Mark hypotheses as supported, weakened, unresolved, or rejected based on explicit evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence model","Experiment design"],"prerequisites":["Falsifiable debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B03-R01","D25-B80-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r2/final","relevance":"Provides incident-analysis concepts relevant to structured investigation and evidence handling."}],"evidence":"Explicit competing hypotheses make causal debugging falsifiable and reduce premature closure.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B80-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Integrated Causal Debugging","concept":"Diagnostic experiment selection by information value","knowledge_type":"reasoning_method","question":"How should a debugger select the next diagnostic experiment when many tests are possible?","answer":"Prefer experiments that can distinguish multiple competing hypotheses while minimizing safety risk, cost, execution time, and confounding variables. The experiment should have a predicted outcome for each relevant hypothesis before execution.","explanation":"Randomly trying fixes or tests can consume diagnostic budget without materially reducing uncertainty. Information-oriented experiment selection makes debugging more efficient and evidence-driven.","principle":"The best next diagnostic action is one that maximizes useful uncertainty reduction subject to safety constraints.","rule":"Do not execute an experiment without identifying what different outcomes would imply for the active hypotheses.","examples":["Compare two dependency versions under identical workload.","Disable one isolated feature path in a safe environment when hypotheses predict different outcomes.","Replay the same input against two implementations."],"invalid_examples":["Running unrelated tests because they are easy.","Changing multiple variables simultaneously without predicted interpretations."],"problem":"Investigation has many possible experiments and limited diagnostic time.","failure":"The team spends resources on low-information tests.","cause":"Experiment selection is not hypothesis-driven.","solution":"Rank candidate experiments by discriminating power, safety, cost, and reproducibility.","alternatives":["Information-gain heuristics","Decision-tree debugging","Sequential experimentation"],"tradeoffs":"High-information experiments may require more setup than simple checks.","constraints":["Safety constraints override information value.","Some experiments cannot be performed in production."],"edge_cases":["Rare failures","Irreversible side effects","Confounded variables","Limited observability"],"security":"Never choose a high-information experiment that requires unsafe security bypasses when safer alternatives exist.","performance":"Prefer experiments that reuse existing traces or controlled fixtures.","testing":"Validate experimental assumptions and expected outcomes before execution.","verification":"Record predicted versus observed outcomes and update hypotheses accordingly.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Hypothesis set","Experiment controls"],"prerequisites":["Causal reasoning"],"relationships":["D25-B80-R02","D25-B65-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/","relevance":"Provides controlled fault-injection experimentation concepts and safety-oriented experiment structure."}],"evidence":"Diagnostic experiments are most useful when their outcomes discriminate among explicit hypotheses.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B80-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Integrated Causal Debugging","concept":"Evidence compression without provenance loss","knowledge_type":"knowledge_management","question":"How can debugging evidence be compressed without destroying its diagnostic value?","answer":"Compress evidence by retaining stable identifiers, causal relationships, representative observations, summary statistics, timestamps, provenance, and links to retained raw evidence while removing redundant or low-value repetition. Compression must preserve the ability to trace conclusions back to original observations.","explanation":"Large incidents can generate millions of telemetry records. Storing everything indefinitely is often impractical, but aggressive summarization can erase the evidence needed to verify conclusions.","principle":"Evidence compression is safe only when traceability and discriminating information are preserved.","rule":"Every summarized diagnostic claim should remain traceable to retained source evidence or a reproducible aggregation procedure.","examples":["Store a trace summary linked to representative spans.","Retain anomaly intervals plus references to raw samples.","Deduplicate identical failure signatures while preserving occurrence counts and time ranges."],"invalid_examples":["Replacing all traces with a human-written summary.","Deleting raw evidence before validating the derived conclusion."],"problem":"Diagnostic storage becomes too large for practical retention.","failure":"Evidence is deleted or summarized without preserving provenance.","cause":"No evidence-compression policy exists.","solution":"Use provenance-preserving aggregation, deduplication, and tiered retention.","alternatives":["Hierarchical storage","Representative sampling","Failure-signature aggregation"],"tradeoffs":"Compression lowers storage cost but may reduce the ability to investigate rare edge cases.","constraints":["Rare events must not be eliminated solely because they are infrequent.","Aggregation semantics must be documented."],"edge_cases":["High-cardinality traces","Rare security events","Long-running incidents","Contradictory telemetry"],"security":"Sensitive evidence must remain access-controlled across all retention tiers.","performance":"Use streaming aggregation for high-volume telemetry and retain detailed evidence selectively.","testing":"Test that summarized records can be traced back to source evidence.","verification":"Verify that key diagnostic conclusions remain reproducible from retained evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence store","Aggregation model","Provenance metadata"],"prerequisites":["Evidence preservation","Deduplication"],"relationships":["D25-B67-R01","D25-B69-R03","D25-B80-R01"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/","relevance":"Provides standardized telemetry structures that support correlation and aggregation."}],"evidence":"High-volume debugging requires aggregation, but aggregation is useful only when provenance and traceability survive.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B80-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Integrated Causal Debugging","concept":"Root-cause closure criteria","knowledge_type":"verification_method","question":"When should a debugging investigation be considered causally closed?","answer":"A debugging investigation should be considered causally closed only when the proposed cause is supported by evidence, the causal mechanism explains the observed failure, competing explanations have been addressed sufficiently, the corrective change is verified, relevant regressions are checked, and the evidence is preserved for future review.","explanation":"Stopping when symptoms disappear is insufficient because unrelated interventions can also restore behavior. Closure requires a coherent evidence chain from failure through cause to verified correction.","principle":"Symptom resolution and causal closure are distinct outcomes.","rule":"A root-cause record should distinguish observed facts, inferred cause, corrective action, and post-fix verification.","examples":["Reproduce the original failure, apply the targeted correction, and verify the predicted change.","Record why plausible alternative causes were weakened or rejected.","Capture the invariant that proves the corrected state is valid."],"invalid_examples":["Closing an incident solely because alerts returned to normal.","Recording a root cause without preserving supporting evidence."],"problem":"Incidents repeatedly recur because investigations close after symptom recovery.","failure":"No explicit causal closure standard exists.","cause":"Operational recovery is mistaken for root-cause verification.","solution":"Define closure criteria covering evidence, mechanism, alternatives, correction, verification, and knowledge capture.","alternatives":["Formal incident review","Corrective-action verification","Causal evidence checklist"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger closure criteria require more investigation effort but improve organizational learning and recurrence prevention.","constraints":["Not every incident permits complete causal certainty.","Uncertainty should be preserved explicitly rather than hidden."],"edge_cases":["Multiple interacting causes","Insufficient telemetry","External dependency failures","Unreproducible production-only defects"],"security":"Security incidents require preservation of relevant evidence and explicit uncertainty where causal attribution remains incomplete.","performance":"Use tiered closure requirements based on incident impact and recurrence risk.","testing":"Verify the corrective change against the original failure and representative regression scenarios.","verification":"Record evidence supporting cause, correction, and post-fix invariants; unresolved uncertainty must remain explicit.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence graph","Verification results","Incident record"],"prerequisites":["Root-cause analysis","Post-fix verification"],"relationships":["D25-B48-R01","D25-B70-R05","D25-B80-R01","D25-B80-R02"],"provenance":[{"source_name":"NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r2/final","relevance":"Provides incident-response lifecycle concepts relevant to investigation, evidence preservation, and lessons learned."}],"evidence":"Causal closure requires more than symptom disappearance; the corrective mechanism and supporting evidence must be verified.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B81-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Cross-Layer Fault Localization","concept":"Cross-layer evidence alignment","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"How should a debugger align evidence across application, runtime, operating-system, database, and infrastructure layers?","answer":"Align evidence using shared timestamps, stable correlation identifiers, causal relationships, resource identities, and normalized event semantics rather than treating each layer as an independent timeline.","explanation":"A single failure may produce symptoms at several layers. Cross-layer alignment reduces false root-cause conclusions by connecting application exceptions, runtime state, OS events, database activity, and infrastructure signals into one evidence model.","principle":"A symptom observed at one layer should be interpreted in the context of causally related evidence from other layers.","rule":"Never infer root cause from an isolated layer when material cross-layer evidence is available.","examples":["Correlating an application timeout with a database lock event using request and transaction identifiers.","Aligning a process crash with an operating-system resource-exhaustion event."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring a database failure solely because an application request timed out.","Treating the latest log entry as the root cause without checking upstream events."],"problem":"Different diagnostic layers expose incomplete and differently structured evidence.","failure":"Root-cause analysis attributes the failure to the most visible symptom.","cause":"Evidence is collected independently without a common correlation model.","solution":"Normalize identities, timestamps, causal links, and event semantics before reasoning about causality.","alternatives":["Distributed tracing","Structured event correlation","Evidence graphs"],"tradeoffs":"More normalization improves diagnostic accuracy but increases instrumentation and correlation complexity.","constraints":["Clock differences must be considered.","Identifiers may be missing or transformed between layers."],"edge_cases":["Clock skew","Retries creating multiple identifiers","Asynchronous work crossing process boundaries"],"security":"Correlation data must avoid exposing secrets or unnecessary sensitive identifiers.","performance":"Evidence correlation should use indexed identifiers and bounded time windows to avoid unbounded diagnostic searches.","testing":"Test whether known cross-layer incidents reconstruct the expected causal chain.","verification":"Verify that inferred relationships are supported by explicit identifiers, timing, or causal evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Structured telemetry","Correlation identifiers"],"prerequisites":["Event correlation","Distributed debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B68-R01","D25-B70-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"The OpenTelemetry specification defines interoperable telemetry concepts that support correlation across observability signals.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B81-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Cross-Layer Fault Localization","concept":"Evidence precedence","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should conflicting evidence from different debugging layers be prioritized?","answer":"Prioritize evidence by directness, temporal relevance, causal strength, measurement reliability, and independence rather than by source popularity or visual prominence.","explanation":"A warning in a dashboard and a verified state transition are not automatically equivalent evidence. Diagnostic reasoning should explicitly evaluate evidence quality and possible common causes.","principle":"Evidence strength depends on its relationship to the failure, not merely on where it was observed.","rule":"Do not resolve conflicting evidence by arbitrarily choosing the newest, loudest, or most familiar signal.","examples":["A verified database lock acquisition can outweigh a generic application timeout when both occur in the same causal chain."],"invalid_examples":["Choosing a metric alert because it appears first on a dashboard."],"problem":"Multiple signals appear to contradict one another.","failure":"The investigation follows the most prominent signal rather than the strongest evidence.","cause":"No explicit evidence-ranking criteria exist.","solution":"Score or classify evidence using directness, reliability, timing, independence, and causal linkage without turning the process into an unsupported numerical ranking.","alternatives":["Evidence graph","Hypothesis testing","Bayesian reasoning"],"tradeoffs":"Explicit evidence assessment improves rigor but requires additional metadata.","constraints":["Evidence quality itself can be uncertain.","Instrumentation failures can affect multiple signals simultaneously."],"edge_cases":["Telemetry outage","Correlated instrumentation errors","Delayed events"],"security":"Evidence provenance must be protected from tampering.","performance":"Evidence assessment should prioritize high-information signals before expensive analysis.","testing":"Inject contradictory diagnostic signals and verify that the reasoning process identifies uncertainty instead of forcing a conclusion.","verification":"Every selected root-cause hypothesis should retain links to supporting and conflicting evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence provenance","Hypothesis management"],"prerequisites":["Root-cause analysis","Observability"],"relationships":["D25-B70-R02","D25-B67-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"The specification provides structured observability concepts; evidence precedence itself is an engineering reasoning principle built on those concepts.","confidence":0.91,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B81-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Cross-Layer Fault Localization","concept":"Fault-domain narrowing","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How can debugging progressively narrow the fault domain across system layers?","answer":"Partition the system into candidate fault domains, eliminate domains contradicted by evidence, and refine the remaining domain using progressively more specific observations.","explanation":"Large systems contain many possible failure locations. Fault-domain narrowing prevents investigators from jumping directly to implementation details before establishing where the failure can plausibly originate.","principle":"Debugging should reduce the search space monotonically as reliable evidence accumulates.","rule":"Every elimination step should be supported by evidence or a validated invariant.","examples":["Eliminating the client layer after verifying the request reached the service with valid parameters.","Restricting a database incident to one transaction after confirming other transactions remained healthy."],"invalid_examples":["Eliminating a component merely because no obvious error was logged."],"problem":"The possible failure space is too large for direct inspection.","failure":"Investigation becomes exploratory and unfocused.","cause":"No explicit fault-domain model exists.","solution":"Represent candidate domains and eliminate them using evidence-backed predicates.","alternatives":["Binary search over execution paths","Dependency graph analysis","Fault-tree analysis"],"tradeoffs":"Narrowing improves efficiency but can become dangerous if exclusion criteria are weak.","constraints":["Missing telemetry can prevent safe elimination.","Shared infrastructure can affect multiple domains."],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous faults","Unknown dependencies","Partial observability"],"security":"Fault-domain analysis should not expose sensitive architecture details unnecessarily.","performance":"Use inexpensive high-discrimination evidence before deep inspection.","testing":"Validate domain-elimination rules against incidents with known root causes.","verification":"Retain the evidence supporting every eliminated domain.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Dependency graph","Evidence model"],"prerequisites":["System decomposition","Root-cause analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B40-R01","D25-B81-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Dapper describes tracing infrastructure for following requests through large distributed systems, supporting systematic fault-domain localization.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B81-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Cross-Layer Fault Localization","concept":"Shared dependency diagnosis","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"Why can failures appearing in multiple independent services originate from one shared dependency?","answer":"A common dependency can create correlated symptoms across otherwise independent consumers, so shared dependency analysis should precede assumptions of multiple independent application faults.","explanation":"Databases, identity systems, DNS, message brokers, storage systems, and network infrastructure can affect many consumers simultaneously.","principle":"Correlated failures across independent consumers are evidence for investigating shared dependencies.","rule":"When multiple consumers fail within a correlated interval, inspect common dependencies before duplicating application-level investigations.","examples":["Several services timing out because a shared database connection pool is exhausted.","Multiple applications failing because a common DNS dependency is unavailable."],"invalid_examples":["Opening separate unrelated root-cause investigations solely because failures occur in different services."],"problem":"Many services exhibit similar symptoms.","failure":"Teams diagnose each service independently and miss the shared cause.","cause":"Service ownership boundaries obscure dependency relationships.","solution":"Build and query dependency relationships during incident diagnosis.","alternatives":["Service graph analysis","Distributed tracing","Infrastructure dependency inventory"],"tradeoffs":"Dependency modeling improves correlation but requires accurate service metadata.","constraints":["Dependencies change dynamically.","Shared dependencies may be indirect."],"edge_cases":["Cascading failures","Partial dependency degradation","Multi-region dependencies"],"security":"Dependency graphs can reveal sensitive architecture and require access controls.","performance":"Precompute common dependency relationships where practical.","testing":"Use controlled dependency failures to verify that dependent services become diagnostically linked.","verification":"Verify the suspected shared dependency with independent evidence before declaring root cause.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Dependency graph","Service metadata"],"prerequisites":["Distributed systems","Observability"],"relationships":["D25-B68-R04","D25-B69-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Dapper demonstrates request tracing across distributed components, enabling relationships between service-level symptoms to be reconstructed.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B81-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Cross-Layer Fault Localization","concept":"Causal boundary identification","knowledge_type":"technique","question":"What is a causal boundary in debugging?","answer":"A causal boundary is a point where evidence from one execution or system domain must be connected to another domain to explain the observed failure.","explanation":"Examples include process boundaries, service boundaries, message queues, database transactions, and external APIs. Correctly identifying these boundaries prevents incomplete causal explanations.","principle":"A root-cause explanation must account for important causal transitions across system boundaries.","rule":"Do not stop diagnosis at a boundary when downstream symptoms depend on upstream state crossing that boundary.","examples":["Following a queued message from producer state through broker delivery to consumer processing."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring the consumer defective without examining the message that crossed the boundary."],"problem":"Traditional stack-oriented debugging stops at process or service boundaries.","failure":"The explanation covers only one segment of the causal chain.","cause":"Cross-boundary evidence is not correlated.","solution":"Represent boundary crossings explicitly using identifiers, timestamps, state transitions, or message relationships.","alternatives":["Trace context","Causal graphs","Transaction correlation"],"tradeoffs":"Boundary modeling improves completeness but requires instrumentation and metadata propagation.","constraints":["Some external systems cannot expose internal state.","Context propagation may be lost."],"edge_cases":["Retries","Duplicated messages","External services with opaque behavior"],"security":"Cross-boundary metadata must follow least-privilege and data-minimization principles.","performance":"Carry only diagnostic context that provides meaningful correlation value.","testing":"Verify that boundary-crossing operations preserve required diagnostic identifiers.","verification":"Check that the final causal explanation includes all material boundary transitions.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Correlation context","Causal graph"],"prerequisites":["Distributed debugging","State-transition analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B68-R03","D25-B70-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Context","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/context/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry Context specifies mechanisms for propagating contextual information across execution boundaries.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B82-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Debugging Under Partial Observability","concept":"Observability gap detection","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How should a debugging system distinguish absence of evidence from evidence of absence?","answer":"Treat missing telemetry as an explicit unknown state unless the instrumentation contract guarantees that the missing signal would have been emitted under the investigated condition.","explanation":"No log, span, metric, or event does not necessarily mean that the corresponding operation did not occur.","principle":"Missing evidence is not proof that the underlying event did not happen.","rule":"Never convert telemetry absence into a negative fact without a validated observability contract.","examples":["A missing trace span may result from sampling or propagation loss rather than absence of execution."],"invalid_examples":["Concluding that a service was never called because no span was observed."],"problem":"Observability is incomplete or selectively sampled.","failure":"The debugger creates false negative conclusions.","cause":"Telemetry presence is incorrectly treated as complete system truth.","solution":"Represent unknown, observed-negative, and observed-positive states separately.","alternatives":["Instrumentation health checks","Coverage metadata","Trace completeness indicators"],"tradeoffs":"Explicit unknown states increase reasoning complexity but reduce false certainty.","constraints":["Instrumentation may itself fail.","Sampling can intentionally remove events."],"edge_cases":["Collector outage","Sampling","Clock skew","Dropped telemetry"],"security":"Telemetry-health metadata must not reveal sensitive operational details beyond authorized scope.","performance":"Instrumentation coverage checks should be lightweight.","testing":"Simulate dropped telemetry and verify that the debugger preserves uncertainty.","verification":"Ensure every negative conclusion has an evidence contract supporting it.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Telemetry metadata","Evidence model"],"prerequisites":["Observability","Epistemic reasoning"],"relationships":["D25-B67-R03","D25-B69-R02"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry defines telemetry signals and context mechanisms but does not guarantee that absence of a signal means absence of an underlying event.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B82-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Debugging Under Partial Observability","concept":"Instrumentation coverage as diagnostic evidence","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"Why should instrumentation coverage be part of debugging evidence?","answer":"Because the reliability of a conclusion depends partly on whether the relevant execution path was observable with sufficient fidelity.","explanation":"A root-cause analysis based on complete telemetry differs epistemically from one based on a sampled or partially instrumented path.","principle":"Diagnostic confidence must account for the observability coverage of the investigated path.","rule":"Attach coverage or completeness metadata to important diagnostic conclusions when available.","examples":["Marking a trace-based conclusion as uncertain when intermediate spans were dropped."],"invalid_examples":["Treating a partially sampled trace as a complete execution record."],"problem":"Investigators cannot determine how much execution evidence is missing.","failure":"Confidence is overstated.","cause":"Telemetry completeness is not represented.","solution":"Record instrumentation, sampling, propagation, and collection completeness alongside evidence.","alternatives":["Trace completeness flags","Instrumentation health metrics"],"tradeoffs":"Coverage metadata improves reasoning but adds telemetry complexity.","constraints":["Coverage may itself be estimated rather than exact."],"edge_cases":["Selective sampling","Dynamic instrumentation","Collector overload"],"security":"Coverage metadata should respect system confidentiality boundaries.","performance":"Avoid high-cardinality coverage dimensions that increase telemetry overhead.","testing":"Compare diagnosis under complete and intentionally degraded telemetry.","verification":"Verify that confidence decreases when critical observability gaps exist.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Observability pipeline","Evidence confidence"],"prerequisites":["Telemetry semantics","Debugging evidence"],"relationships":["D25-B82-R01","D25-B67-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry establishes standardized telemetry structures; coverage interpretation is a diagnostic layer built above those structures.","confidence":0.92,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B82-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Debugging Under Partial Observability","concept":"Unknown-state preservation","knowledge_type":"reasoning_rule","question":"How should a debugging system represent unresolved observations?","answer":"Represent unresolved observations explicitly as unknown rather than coercing them into true or false values.","explanation":"Forced binary classification causes missing telemetry, ambiguous symptoms, and incomplete experiments to become false facts that contaminate later reasoning.","principle":"Uncertainty should be preserved until evidence resolves it.","rule":"Unknown must remain distinct from false, absent, or verified-negative.","examples":["unknown_dependency_state","unknown_trace_segment","unknown_external_response"],"invalid_examples":["Setting an unobserved condition to false by default."],"problem":"Diagnostic data models often assume binary states.","failure":"The debugging engine accumulates unsupported conclusions.","cause":"Unknown states are not represented explicitly.","solution":"Use tri-state or richer epistemic representations.","alternatives":["Four-valued logic","Evidence-status enums","Confidence-aware propositions"],"tradeoffs":"Richer state models increase implementation complexity but preserve reasoning integrity.","constraints":["Downstream algorithms must understand unknown values."],"edge_cases":["Conflicting observations","Stale observations","Expired evidence"],"security":"Unknown-state handling should not be used to bypass authorization or verification requirements.","performance":"Represent state compactly and avoid repeated recomputation.","testing":"Feed missing and conflicting observations and verify preservation of uncertainty.","verification":"Audit reasoning graphs for unsupported conversions from unknown to false or true.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence model","Validation state"],"prerequisites":["Logic","Root-cause reasoning"],"relationships":["D25-B41-R01","D25-B82-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"The source standardizes telemetry representation; explicit unknown-state reasoning is an engineering principle applied to incomplete telemetry.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B82-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Debugging Under Partial Observability","concept":"Sampling-aware diagnosis","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How can sampling distort debugging conclusions?","answer":"Sampling can remove relevant events and alter apparent frequency or causal visibility, so sampled observations must not automatically be interpreted as complete execution histories.","explanation":"Sampling is useful for controlling telemetry volume but can hide rare or intermediate events that are critical for root-cause analysis.","principle":"Sampling changes the observable evidence distribution and must be represented explicitly.","rule":"Interpret sampled traces and events according to their sampling semantics.","examples":["A sampled request may omit an intermediate service that was present in unsampled executions.","Rare failure events may be underrepresented by probabilistic sampling."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming sampled trace frequency equals actual event frequency without accounting for the sampling mechanism."],"problem":"Telemetry volume is reduced through sampling.","failure":"The debugger infers incorrect event frequency or causal structure.","cause":"Sampling metadata is ignored.","solution":"Preserve sampling indicators and use sampling-aware reconstruction methods.","alternatives":["Tail-based sampling","Failure-triggered capture","Adaptive sampling"],"tradeoffs":"More diagnostic capture improves evidence completeness but increases storage and processing cost.","constraints":["Sampling policies differ by system.","Some missing events cannot be reconstructed."],"edge_cases":["Multi-stage sampling","Head versus tail sampling","Collector overload"],"security":"Sampling metadata should not leak sensitive request information.","performance":"Balance diagnostic fidelity against telemetry overhead.","testing":"Compare conclusions under full and sampled telemetry.","verification":"Check that causal claims involving sampled data retain appropriate uncertainty.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Telemetry sampling","Trace context"],"prerequisites":["Distributed tracing","Observability"],"relationships":["D25-B69-R02","D25-B68-R05"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Trace Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/semconv/general/trace/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry defines trace concepts and attributes that support interpretation of trace data; sampling behavior must be considered when reasoning over incomplete traces.","confidence":0.93,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B82-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Debugging Under Partial Observability","concept":"Diagnostic capture escalation","knowledge_type":"strategy","question":"When should debugging increase evidence capture instead of continuing analysis on existing telemetry?","answer":"Increase evidence capture when existing evidence cannot distinguish material hypotheses and the additional capture can safely discriminate between them.","explanation":"More telemetry is useful only when it resolves an actual diagnostic uncertainty. Capturing everything indiscriminately can increase cost without improving reasoning.","principle":"Diagnostic instrumentation should be hypothesis-driven.","rule":"Add evidence collection when its expected information value exceeds its operational cost and risk.","examples":["Temporarily enabling targeted request tracing for one failing transaction class.","Capturing a bounded runtime state snapshot around a reproducible failure."],"invalid_examples":["Enabling maximal debug logging globally without a defined diagnostic question."],"problem":"Existing evidence cannot distinguish competing explanations.","failure":"Investigation continues without reducing uncertainty.","cause":"Evidence collection is disconnected from hypotheses.","solution":"Define the unresolved distinction and collect the minimum additional evidence required to resolve it.","alternatives":["Targeted tracing","Conditional logging","Failure-triggered snapshots"],"tradeoffs":"Additional evidence improves observability but can introduce performance, storage, and privacy costs.","constraints":["Production safety","Data minimization","Instrumentation overhead"],"edge_cases":["High-frequency failures","Sensitive payloads","Short-lived incidents"],"security":"Additional capture must follow data-minimization and authorization controls.","performance":"Prefer conditional and targeted capture over globally verbose instrumentation.","testing":"Verify that diagnostic capture activates only under intended predicates.","verification":"Confirm that collected evidence directly addresses the unresolved hypotheses.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Instrumentation controls","Hypothesis model"],"prerequisites":["Hypothesis-driven debugging","Observability"],"relationships":["D25-B82-R01","D25-B41-R02"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"Standardized telemetry provides mechanisms for structured observability; targeted escalation is a diagnostic design principle using those mechanisms.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B83-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Regression and Change Attribution","concept":"Change-to-failure attribution","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should debugging attribute a failure to a recent code or configuration change?","answer":"Establish temporal association first, then test whether the change can causally explain the failure using controlled comparison, reproduction, dependency analysis, and invariant checks.","explanation":"A change occurring before a failure is necessary evidence for temporal association but is not sufficient proof of causation.","principle":"Temporal precedence is not causal proof.","rule":"Do not attribute a regression solely because the failure appeared after a change.","examples":["Reproducing the failure on the changed revision and comparing it with the previous known-good revision.","Testing whether a configuration change affects the failing execution path."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring the latest deployment responsible solely because it preceded the incident."],"problem":"Many changes may occur near the time of a failure.","failure":"The investigation anchors on the most recent change.","cause":"Temporal correlation is mistaken for causation.","solution":"Use controlled differential execution and path-specific evidence.","alternatives":["Version bisect","Canary comparison","Feature-flag isolation"],"tradeoffs":"Controlled comparison improves causal confidence but may require additional environments or rollback capability.","constraints":["Concurrent changes","Data differences","Environment drift"],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous deployments","Delayed regressions","External dependency changes"],"security":"Rollback and diagnostic experiments require appropriate authorization.","performance":"Prioritize changes affecting the failing path.","testing":"Compare changed and unchanged revisions under controlled inputs.","verification":"Require direct causal evidence before labeling a change as the root cause.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Version control","Differential execution"],"prerequisites":["Regression debugging","Causal reasoning"],"relationships":["D25-B43-R01","D25-B64-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"WinDbg Time Travel Debugging","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timetravel-preview"}],"evidence":"Time-travel debugging provides a mechanism for examining execution history and comparing behavior around faults; change attribution additionally requires controlled comparison.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B83-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Regression and Change Attribution","concept":"Configuration regression diagnosis","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"Why should configuration changes be treated as first-class regression candidates?","answer":"Configuration can alter control flow, resource limits, dependency selection, feature behavior, security policy, and runtime conditions without changing application source code.","explanation":"A production regression can therefore originate from configuration drift even when the deployed binary is unchanged.","principle":"Executable behavior is a function of code, configuration, environment, and external dependencies.","rule":"Include material configuration changes in regression analysis.","examples":["A connection timeout change exposing latent retry amplification.","A feature-flag change activating an untested execution path."],"invalid_examples":["Excluding configuration from root-cause analysis because no source code changed."],"problem":"Application behavior changes without source changes.","failure":"Debugging focuses exclusively on code revisions.","cause":"Configuration is treated as operational metadata rather than executable input.","solution":"Version, diff, validate, and correlate configuration with execution behavior.","alternatives":["Configuration snapshots","Immutable deployment manifests","Environment comparison"],"tradeoffs":"Configuration tracking improves diagnosis but increases metadata management requirements.","constraints":["Secrets must not be exposed.","Dynamic configuration can change during execution."],"edge_cases":["Remote configuration","Feature flags","Environment variables","Secret rotation"],"security":"Configuration evidence must redact credentials, tokens, and secrets.","performance":"Capture only configuration relevant to the failing path.","testing":"Run regression tests against old and new configuration states.","verification":"Verify that the suspected configuration value actually influenced the failing component.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Configuration versioning","Environment capture"],"prerequisites":["Configuration management","Regression analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B27-R01","D25-B33-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"Standardized telemetry can record runtime context; configuration attribution requires preserving relevant environment and configuration state separately.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B83-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Regression and Change Attribution","concept":"Environment drift attribution","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"How can environment drift cause a regression without a source-code change?","answer":"Differences in operating systems, runtime versions, libraries, environment variables, resource limits, locale, network behavior, or external services can change program behavior while source code remains identical.","explanation":"Reproducibility requires identifying the execution environment as part of the effective program context.","principle":"The executable artifact alone does not fully define runtime behavior.","rule":"Compare relevant environment state when a regression cannot be reproduced consistently.","examples":["A library patch version changing parser behavior.","A runtime setting changing memory or thread behavior."],"invalid_examples":["Concluding that the code is deterministic because the source revision is identical."],"problem":"Failure appears only in one environment.","failure":"Investigation blames nondeterministic application logic without checking environmental differences.","cause":"Environment state is omitted from the comparison model.","solution":"Capture normalized environment fingerprints and compare material differences.","alternatives":["Containerized reproduction","Environment manifests","Hermetic builds"],"tradeoffs":"Environment capture improves reproducibility but may be difficult for dynamic infrastructure.","constraints":["Do not capture secrets.","Hardware-specific state may be difficult to normalize."],"edge_cases":["Kernel differences","CPU feature differences","Locale/time-zone differences"],"security":"Environment snapshots must exclude credentials and sensitive host information.","performance":"Use compact environment fingerprints instead of unrestricted dumps.","testing":"Verify that controlled environment changes reproduce known environment-sensitive failures.","verification":"Confirm that the identified environmental difference affects the failing path.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Environment fingerprinting","Artifact identity"],"prerequisites":["Reproducible debugging","Dependency analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B43-R02","D25-B27-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry supports recording runtime context; environment-drift diagnosis extends this concept into reproducibility analysis.","confidence":0.93,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B83-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Regression and Change Attribution","concept":"Feature-flag regression isolation","knowledge_type":"technique","question":"How can feature flags help isolate a behavioral regression?","answer":"Compare the same deployment with the suspected feature enabled and disabled under controlled conditions while holding other relevant variables constant.","explanation":"Feature flags can provide an experimental boundary that isolates behavior without requiring immediate source rollback, provided the flag genuinely controls the affected path.","principle":"Controlled feature-state comparison can separate feature-specific behavior from unrelated deployment effects.","rule":"Treat a flag comparison as causal evidence only when the flag controls the suspected execution path and other material variables are controlled.","examples":["Disable a newly activated code path for affected requests and compare outcomes.","Compare traces for equivalent requests across flag states."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming a flag caused a failure merely because disabling it appears to reduce alerts while traffic also changed."],"problem":"A deployment contains multiple simultaneous behavioral changes.","failure":"The investigator cannot isolate the responsible change.","cause":"Feature activation and deployment are coupled.","solution":"Use controlled flag-state experiments.","alternatives":["Rollback","Canary deployment","Version bisect"],"tradeoffs":"Flags enable isolation but add configuration complexity and state combinations.","constraints":["Flag state propagation must be reliable.","Experiments require authorization."],"edge_cases":["Per-user flags","Gradual rollout","Cached flag state"],"security":"Feature-flag changes must follow authorization and audit requirements.","performance":"Keep experimental cohorts small and controlled where appropriate.","testing":"Test both flag states under representative conditions.","verification":"Confirm flag-state changes actually alter the relevant execution path.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Feature flag system","Controlled comparison"],"prerequisites":["Regression diagnosis","Experimental reasoning"],"relationships":["D25-B83-R01","D25-B64-R04"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"Telemetry can be correlated with request and execution context to compare behavior across controlled feature states.","confidence":0.91,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B83-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Regression and Change Attribution","concept":"Causal attribution confidence","knowledge_type":"reasoning_rule","question":"What evidence increases confidence that a specific change caused a regression?","answer":"Confidence increases when the change precedes the failure, affects the failing path, reproduces the failure under controlled conditions, disappears when reverted or isolated, and explains observed symptoms without unsupported assumptions.","explanation":"No single signal proves causation in complex systems. Convergent evidence across independent methods is stronger than temporal correlation alone.","principle":"Causal attribution should require convergent evidence rather than a single correlation.","rule":"Document both supporting and contradicting evidence for change-attribution hypotheses.","examples":["A configuration change reproduces the failure, reverting it removes the failure, and trace evidence shows the changed setting affects the failing operation."],"invalid_examples":["Calling a deployment the cause solely because its timestamp precedes the incident."],"problem":"Investigators need to distinguish causation from coincidence.","failure":"Root-cause reports contain weak causal claims.","cause":"Attribution criteria are undefined.","solution":"Use explicit causal-evidence requirements.","alternatives":["Controlled experiment","Differential execution","Counterfactual testing"],"tradeoffs":"Higher attribution rigor can increase investigation time.","constraints":["Some failures cannot be safely reproduced or reverted."],"edge_cases":["Multiple interacting causes","Irreversible side effects","Rare failures"],"security":"Causal experiments must not weaken security controls without authorization.","performance":"Prefer minimally invasive experiments.","testing":"Verify attribution criteria using known regression cases.","verification":"Require reproducible or independently corroborated causal evidence where feasible.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Causal reasoning","Differential analysis"],"prerequisites":["Regression debugging","Hypothesis testing"],"relationships":["D25-B83-R01","D25-B64-R03"],"provenance":[{"source":"WinDbg Time Travel Debugging","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timetravel-preview"}],"evidence":"Execution-history inspection can establish detailed behavioral differences; causal attribution still requires controlled evidence beyond temporal ordering.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B84-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Build and Artifact Forensics","concept":"Artifact identity verification","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"Why should a debugger verify the exact artifact that produced a failure?","answer":"Because source revision, build configuration, dependency resolution, compiler output, packaging, and deployment transformations can cause the running artifact to differ materially from the artifact assumed during investigation.","explanation":"A source repository state is not sufficient evidence of what actually executed in production.","principle":"Debugging must establish artifact identity before interpreting source-level evidence.","rule":"Associate runtime failures with immutable artifact identifiers whenever possible.","examples":["Comparing deployed binary hashes with the expected build artifact.","Verifying container image digest before source-level diagnosis."],"invalid_examples":["Debugging against the current source branch without verifying the deployed artifact."],"problem":"Investigators may inspect code that did not produce the failure.","failure":"Correct reasoning is applied to the wrong artifact.","cause":"Artifact provenance is incomplete.","solution":"Capture immutable artifact identity and deployment provenance.","alternatives":["Build manifests","Content-addressed artifacts","SBOM-based provenance"],"tradeoffs":"Strong artifact provenance improves diagnosis but requires build and deployment discipline.","constraints":["Artifact identifiers must be preserved.","Private build metadata may require access control."],"edge_cases":["Hot patches","Dynamic code loading","Runtime-generated code"],"security":"Artifact provenance records should be tamper-resistant.","performance":"Use compact immutable identifiers such as cryptographic digests.","testing":"Verify that deployment metadata maps to the exact executable artifact.","verification":"Cross-check runtime identity against build and deployment records.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Build provenance","Deployment metadata"],"prerequisites":["Software supply-chain concepts","Debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B28-R01","D25-B83-R03"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"Runtime telemetry can carry resource and execution context; exact artifact provenance must be maintained by build and deployment systems.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B84-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Build and Artifact Forensics","concept":"Debug-symbol mismatch","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"How can mismatched debug symbols mislead native-code debugging?","answer":"Debug symbols map machine instructions and addresses to source-level constructs; if they do not correspond to the exact binary, stack frames, line numbers, variables, and source mappings may be incorrect.","explanation":"Native debugging depends on consistency between executable code and symbol information.","principle":"Debug metadata must correspond exactly to the executable under investigation.","rule":"Verify symbol identity before trusting source-level native debugging output.","examples":["Rejecting symbols whose build identifier does not match the crashed binary."],"invalid_examples":["Using symbols from a nearby build because the source version appears similar."],"problem":"Crash analysis shows inconsistent or implausible source locations.","failure":"The investigator reasons from incorrect source mappings.","cause":"Symbol and binary mismatch.","solution":"Validate symbol identity and preserve matching artifacts.","alternatives":["Build IDs","Debug-information checksums","Symbol servers"],"tradeoffs":"Strict symbol validation can reject incomplete diagnostics but prevents misleading mappings.","constraints":["Symbols may be unavailable for production binaries.","Stripped binaries reduce diagnostic detail."],"edge_cases":["Hot patches","Partial symbols","Compiler-generated code"],"security":"Debug symbols can expose implementation details and should be access-controlled.","performance":"Symbol lookup should be indexed by exact build identity.","testing":"Test crash analysis with intentionally mismatched symbols and verify rejection or warning behavior.","verification":"Verify build identifiers before interpreting stack frames or variables.","version":"Technology-agnostic native debugging","dependencies":["Debug symbols","Artifact identity"],"prerequisites":["Native debugging","Build provenance"],"relationships":["D25-B84-R01","D25-B22-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"WinDbg Time Travel Debugging","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timetravel-preview"}],"evidence":"WinDbg documentation describes debugging execution traces and symbol-dependent analysis; exact symbol matching remains necessary for reliable source mappings.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B84-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Build and Artifact Forensics","concept":"Compiler-option regression analysis","knowledge_type":"diagnostic_method","question":"Why should compiler and linker options be included in native regression diagnosis?","answer":"Optimization, debug information, ABI settings, sanitization, instruction selection, linking, and code-generation options can change runtime behavior without changing source code.","explanation":"Native behavior depends on the transformation from source to executable, so build configuration is part of the diagnostic context.","principle":"Source equivalence does not imply binary equivalence.","rule":"Compare relevant compiler and linker configuration when diagnosing native-only regressions.","examples":["Comparing optimization levels between a working and failing build.","Checking whether a sanitizer or hardening option changes execution behavior."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming two binaries are equivalent because they were built from the same commit."],"problem":"A regression appears only in one build configuration.","failure":"Debugging focuses on source logic while ignoring generated-code differences.","cause":"Build configuration is excluded from the diagnostic model.","solution":"Capture and compare compiler, linker, ABI, and optimization metadata.","alternatives":["Reproducible builds","Binary diffing","Compiler-generated reports"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed build provenance improves diagnosis but increases metadata volume.","constraints":["Compiler versions and toolchains must be identifiable.","Generated code may depend on hardware."],"edge_cases":["Link-time optimization","Profile-guided optimization","Undefined behavior exposed by optimization"],"security":"Build metadata should not expose secrets or signing material.","performance":"Store normalized build fingerprints rather than unrestricted build logs when appropriate.","testing":"Build the same source under controlled configuration variants and compare behavior.","verification":"Verify that suspected build-option differences affect the generated artifact or runtime path.","version":"Technology-agnostic native debugging","dependencies":["Build metadata","Artifact comparison"],"prerequisites":["Compilation","Native debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B24-R01","D25-B25-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"WinDbg Time Travel Debugging","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timetravel-preview"}],"evidence":"Execution-history debugging can expose behavioral differences; compiler-option attribution requires preserving build configuration.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B84-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Build and Artifact Forensics","concept":"Dependency closure verification","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"Why is dependency closure important when reproducing a software failure?","answer":"A reproducible execution requires the relevant transitive dependency versions and build inputs, not only the application's direct dependencies.","explanation":"Indirect libraries, runtime components, generated assets, plugins, and native libraries can materially affect behavior.","principle":"The effective artifact depends on the complete relevant dependency closure.","rule":"Record and compare transitive dependencies when dependency behavior may affect the failure.","examples":["A direct package remains unchanged while an indirect library patch changes parsing behavior."],"invalid_examples":["Comparing only top-level dependency declarations."],"problem":"A failure reproduces only in one environment despite identical application source.","failure":"The investigation misses an indirect dependency difference.","cause":"Dependency closure is incomplete.","solution":"Capture resolved dependency graphs and compare them across environments.","alternatives":["Lockfiles","Dependency manifests","Content-addressed package caches"],"tradeoffs":"Complete dependency capture improves reproducibility but increases metadata and maintenance overhead.","constraints":["Dynamic dependencies may be resolved at runtime.","Private dependencies require controlled access."],"edge_cases":["Optional dependencies","Plugin discovery","System libraries"],"security":"Dependency metadata can expose internal packages and should be protected.","performance":"Use lockfiles and hashes for efficient comparison.","testing":"Reproduce known dependency regressions using controlled dependency closures.","verification":"Verify that runtime-loaded dependencies match the recorded closure.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Dependency resolver","Artifact provenance"],"prerequisites":["Dependency management","Reproducible builds"],"relationships":["D25-B27-R01","D25-B84-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"Runtime resource and environment context can support correlation; complete dependency provenance remains a build/runtime responsibility.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B84-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Build and Artifact Forensics","concept":"Artifact lineage","knowledge_type":"knowledge_model","question":"What should artifact lineage contain for reliable debugging?","answer":"Artifact lineage should connect source revision, build inputs, toolchain identity, dependency closure, build configuration, artifact digest, packaging identity, deployment event, and runtime instance where applicable.","explanation":"A lineage chain allows investigators to move from a runtime symptom back to the exact material inputs that produced the executable artifact.","principle":"Debugging provenance should be traversable from runtime observation to source and build inputs.","rule":"Preserve stable identifiers at each artifact transformation boundary.","examples":["runtime instance → image digest → build record → source revision → dependency lockfile"],"invalid_examples":["Storing only the source commit without build or deployment identity."],"problem":"The runtime artifact cannot be traced back to its exact production inputs.","failure":"Root-cause investigation depends on assumptions about deployed code.","cause":"Artifact lineage is fragmented.","solution":"Create a stable provenance graph across build and deployment stages.","alternatives":["Build provenance attestations","Deployment manifests","Artifact registries"],"tradeoffs":"Strong lineage improves debugging and auditability but requires cross-system integration.","constraints":["Some external build inputs may be unavailable.","Sensitive metadata requires access control."],"edge_cases":["Rebuilt artifacts","Emergency patches","Runtime downloads"],"security":"Protect provenance records against unauthorized modification.","performance":"Use immutable identifiers and indexed lineage relationships.","testing":"Validate lineage reconstruction from runtime artifact back to source inputs.","verification":"Reject lineage paths with missing or conflicting identity links when exact provenance is required.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Artifact registry","Build metadata","Deployment metadata"],"prerequisites":["Software supply chain","Debugging provenance"],"relationships":["D25-B84-R01","D25-B84-R04"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"Telemetry standards provide runtime context primitives; complete artifact lineage requires complementary build and deployment provenance systems.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B85-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Diagnostic Experiment Design","concept":"Minimal diagnostic experiment","knowledge_type":"method","question":"What makes a debugging experiment minimal?","answer":"A minimal debugging experiment changes the smallest relevant variable or captures the smallest additional evidence needed to distinguish competing hypotheses.","explanation":"Large experiments change multiple variables and make causal interpretation difficult.","principle":"Diagnostic experiments should maximize information while minimizing uncontrolled changes.","rule":"Change one material factor at a time when the system permits it.","examples":["Changing one timeout parameter while holding traffic, code, and dependency versions constant."],"invalid_examples":["Changing timeout, retry count, deployment version, and database configuration simultaneously."],"problem":"Investigators need to distinguish multiple plausible causes.","failure":"Experiments produce ambiguous results.","cause":"Too many variables change at once.","solution":"Design controlled, bounded experiments with explicit hypotheses and expected observations.","alternatives":["A/B comparison","Factorial experiments","Canary experiments"],"tradeoffs":"Minimal experiments improve causal clarity but may require more iterations.","constraints":["Production risk","Experiment duration","External side effects"],"edge_cases":["Highly coupled systems","Irreversible operations","Rare failures"],"security":"Experiments must remain within approved security boundaries.","performance":"Use low-impact experiments where possible.","testing":"Verify experiment isolation by comparing controlled variables before and after execution.","verification":"Record hypothesis, intervention, expected result, observed result, and interpretation.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Hypothesis model","Experiment record"],"prerequisites":["Scientific debugging","Causal reasoning"],"relationships":["D25-B65-R01","D25-B83-R05"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"AWS Fault Injection Service documentation describes controlled fault-injection experiments with experiment templates and actions; minimal diagnostic experimentation generalizes the controlled-experiment principle.","confidence":0.93,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B85-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Diagnostic Experiment Design","concept":"Counterfactual debugging","knowledge_type":"reasoning_method","question":"What is counterfactual debugging?","answer":"Counterfactual debugging asks whether the observed failure would plausibly disappear or change if a suspected causal condition were altered while relevant confounders remain controlled.","explanation":"Counterfactual reasoning distinguishes causal mechanisms from simple correlation.","principle":"A useful counterfactual changes a causal variable while preserving other material conditions.","rule":"Treat counterfactual results as evidence only when the intervention actually changes the suspected causal factor.","examples":["Re-running a failure with one dependency version changed while preserving the same input and application artifact."],"invalid_examples":["Comparing two runs with different traffic, code, and dependency versions and attributing the result to one variable."],"problem":"Correlation is insufficient to identify causality.","failure":"A coincidental association becomes the reported root cause.","cause":"Confounding variables are uncontrolled.","solution":"Construct controlled counterfactual comparisons.","alternatives":["Differential execution","Fault injection","Feature isolation"],"tradeoffs":"Counterfactual experiments can provide strong evidence but may be difficult or unsafe in production.","constraints":["Side effects","Safety boundaries","Reproducibility"],"edge_cases":["Non-deterministic systems","Irreversible state changes","External dependencies"],"security":"Counterfactual interventions must not bypass security controls.","performance":"Prefer replay or isolated environments when production intervention is costly.","testing":"Compare counterfactual results against known causal regressions.","verification":"Document which variables were controlled and which remained uncertain.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Controlled execution","Hypothesis management"],"prerequisites":["Causal inference","Differential debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B64-R01","D25-B85-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"WinDbg Time Travel Debugging","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timetravel-preview"}],"evidence":"Time-travel debugging permits replay and inspection of execution history; counterfactual analysis additionally requires controlled changes to causal conditions.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B85-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Diagnostic Experiment Design","concept":"Negative diagnostic experiment","knowledge_type":"reasoning_rule","question":"What does a negative debugging experiment establish?","answer":"A negative experiment establishes that the tested intervention did not produce the expected diagnostic effect under the tested conditions; it does not automatically disprove the broader hypothesis.","explanation":"The intervention may have been ineffective, incorrectly targeted, underpowered, or masked by another causal factor.","principle":"Failure to observe an expected effect is evidence about the experiment, not necessarily definitive evidence against the hypothesis.","rule":"Interpret negative experiments together with experiment validity and coverage.","examples":["Changing a timeout does not affect the failure because the actual bottleneck occurs earlier in the request path."],"invalid_examples":["Concluding that a suspected dependency cannot be involved solely because one intervention did not change symptoms."],"problem":"An experiment fails to produce the expected change.","failure":"The investigator prematurely discards a potentially valid hypothesis.","cause":"Experiment limitations are ignored.","solution":"Analyze intervention reach, sensitivity, timing, and confounders before updating the hypothesis.","alternatives":["Repeat under controlled conditions","Use a different intervention","Collect additional evidence"],"tradeoffs":"Preserving hypotheses longer avoids premature closure but can increase investigation cost.","constraints":["Experiments may have limited power.","System behavior can be non-linear."],"edge_cases":["Threshold effects","Rare-event failures","Multiple simultaneous causes"],"security":"Repeated experiments must remain within approved boundaries.","performance":"Avoid repeating low-information experiments without changing diagnostic conditions.","testing":"Validate interpretation against controlled systems with known causal relationships.","verification":"Record why a negative result does or does not materially reduce hypothesis confidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Experiment records","Hypothesis confidence"],"prerequisites":["Experimental debugging","Causal reasoning"],"relationships":["D25-B65-R05","D25-B85-R02"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"Fault-injection experimentation provides a structured mechanism for evaluating system behavior under controlled interventions; interpretation still depends on experiment design.","confidence":0.93,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B85-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Diagnostic Experiment Design","concept":"Experiment stopping criteria","knowledge_type":"governance_rule","question":"When should a debugging experiment stop?","answer":"A debugging experiment should stop when the diagnostic objective is met, safety limits are reached, evidence becomes sufficient, the intervention becomes unsafe, or further observations are unlikely to distinguish the remaining hypotheses.","explanation":"Unbounded experimentation increases operational risk and can generate redundant evidence.","principle":"Every diagnostic experiment should have explicit entry and exit criteria.","rule":"Define success, failure, safety, and abort conditions before execution when practical.","examples":["Stop fault injection after the expected failure signal is observed and recovery criteria are satisfied.","Abort an experiment when error rates exceed a predefined safety threshold."],"invalid_examples":["Continuing a production experiment indefinitely because more data might always be useful."],"problem":"Experiments continue after sufficient evidence has been collected.","failure":"Operational risk increases without proportional diagnostic value.","cause":"No stopping criteria were defined.","solution":"Specify bounded objectives, safety thresholds, and evidence sufficiency conditions.","alternatives":["Time-bounded experiments","Error-budget-based aborts","Manual approval gates"],"tradeoffs":"Strict stopping criteria improve safety but may terminate an experiment before all uncertainty is resolved.","constraints":["Operational safety","Authorization","Recovery capability"],"edge_cases":["Slow-moving failures","Delayed effects","Partial recovery"],"security":"Experiments must include security-specific abort conditions.","performance":"Bound experiment duration and resource consumption.","testing":"Test abort behavior under simulated threshold breaches.","verification":"Confirm that experiment completion records include actual stopping reason.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Experiment governance","Safety controls"],"prerequisites":["Fault injection","Operational risk management"],"relationships":["D25-B65-R02","D25-B66-R05"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"AWS FIS provides controlled experiment constructs and safeguards; explicit diagnostic stopping criteria generalize safe experiment governance.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B85-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Diagnostic Experiment Design","concept":"Experiment provenance","knowledge_type":"knowledge_model","question":"What provenance should a debugging experiment retain?","answer":"Retain the hypothesis, baseline state, intervention, scope, authorization, timing, expected outcome, observed outcome, environmental context, telemetry references, safety events, and interpretation.","explanation":"Without experiment provenance, later investigators cannot determine whether a result was caused by the intended intervention or by uncontrolled conditions.","principle":"Diagnostic experiments are evidence-producing events and require reproducible provenance.","rule":"Store enough metadata to reconstruct what was changed, where, when, why, and what was observed.","examples":["Recording the exact feature-flag state, request cohort, build identity, intervention timestamp, and trace references."],"invalid_examples":["Recording only 'tested timeout and issue improved'."],"problem":"Experiment results cannot be independently evaluated.","failure":"Diagnostic conclusions become anecdotal.","cause":"Experiment metadata is incomplete.","solution":"Use a structured experiment record linked to evidence and system state.","alternatives":["Experiment ledger","Incident timeline","Audit trail"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed provenance increases storage and governance requirements.","constraints":["Sensitive data must be minimized.","Some runtime state cannot be fully captured."],"edge_cases":["Long-running experiments","Multiple interventions","Concurrent incidents"],"security":"Experiment records must enforce authorization and tamper resistance.","performance":"Store references to large telemetry artifacts instead of duplicating them.","testing":"Validate that experiment records contain all mandatory provenance fields.","verification":"Check that each conclusion references the experiment evidence that supports it.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence store","Audit trail"],"prerequisites":["Experiment design","Provenance"],"relationships":["D25-B70-R03","D25-B85-R04"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"AWS FIS documentation establishes structured experiment concepts; provenance requirements extend those concepts for debugging knowledge capture.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B86-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Failure Containment During Debugging","concept":"Diagnostic blast-radius control","knowledge_type":"safety_principle","question":"Why should debugging interventions have a bounded blast radius?","answer":"A diagnostic intervention can alter system behavior and potentially affect unrelated users or components, so its scope should be limited to the smallest area capable of answering the diagnostic question.","explanation":"Safe debugging treats instrumentation and experimentation as potentially impactful changes.","principle":"Diagnostic value should be obtained with the smallest practical operational exposure.","rule":"Prefer targeted cohorts, isolated environments, or bounded resources for intrusive diagnostics.","examples":["Capturing detailed traces only for a controlled request cohort.","Running a reproduction in an isolated environment."],"invalid_examples":["Enabling verbose payload logging across all production traffic to diagnose one request class."],"problem":"Debugging itself can create operational risk.","failure":"The diagnostic intervention introduces secondary failures or data exposure.","cause":"Scope is broader than necessary.","solution":"Apply explicit scope, rate, duration, and resource limits.","alternatives":["Sandbox reproduction","Canary diagnostics","Failure-triggered capture"],"tradeoffs":"Smaller scope may reduce evidence quantity but improves safety.","constraints":["Production availability","Privacy","Security"],"edge_cases":["Shared mutable resources","Global configuration","Cross-tenant systems"],"security":"Diagnostic capture must respect tenant isolation and data minimization.","performance":"Rate-limit expensive instrumentation.","testing":"Verify that diagnostic controls remain within configured scope.","verification":"Audit intervention scope against the approved diagnostic objective.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Experiment governance","Access control"],"prerequisites":["Production debugging","Operational safety"],"relationships":["D25-B85-R04","D25-B42-R02"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"AWS FIS provides controlled fault-injection experiments and safety-oriented experiment management; bounded debugging scope is a broader diagnostic safety principle.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B86-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Failure Containment During Debugging","concept":"Diagnostic side-effect detection","knowledge_type":"failure_analysis","question":"How can a debugging intervention become a new source of failure?","answer":"Additional logging, tracing, breakpoints, resource capture, fault injection, or configuration changes can alter timing, resource consumption, scheduling, or system state.","explanation":"This is especially important for concurrency-sensitive, latency-sensitive, and resource-constrained systems.","principle":"Instrumentation and debugging are themselves interventions that can perturb execution.","rule":"Evaluate whether diagnostic mechanisms can change the behavior being investigated.","examples":["A debugger breakpoint changes thread scheduling and prevents reproduction of a race.","Verbose logging increases I/O latency and alters timeout behavior."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming a failure disappeared under debugging because the underlying defect was fixed."],"problem":"The act of debugging changes system behavior.","failure":"The investigator obtains a false non-reproduction.","cause":"Observer effect is ignored.","solution":"Compare instrumented and minimally instrumented executions and use low-perturbation techniques where necessary.","alternatives":["Tracing","Sampling","Record/replay"],"tradeoffs":"Lower-perturbation techniques may provide less immediate interactive visibility.","constraints":["Instrumentation overhead","Timing sensitivity"],"edge_cases":["Heisenbugs","Real-time systems","Lock contention"],"security":"Diagnostic mechanisms must not weaken runtime protections.","performance":"Measure diagnostic overhead when it can affect the investigated behavior.","testing":"Verify whether enabling diagnostics changes timing or resource behavior.","verification":"Record diagnostic configuration when interpreting reproduction differences.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Instrumentation","Runtime profiling"],"prerequisites":["Concurrency debugging","Performance debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B62-R02","D25-B82-R05"],"provenance":[{"source":"WinDbg Time Travel Debugging","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timetravel-preview"}],"evidence":"Time-travel debugging provides an alternative to repeatedly perturbing a live execution; the observer-effect principle remains important for interactive debugging.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B86-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Failure Containment During Debugging","concept":"Tenant-safe debugging","knowledge_type":"security_rule","question":"What is required for debugging in a multi-tenant system?","answer":"Debugging must scope evidence collection, runtime inspection, experiments, and state changes to the authorized tenant and request context without exposing neighboring tenant data.","explanation":"Shared infrastructure can make unrestricted diagnostics a cross-tenant data exposure risk.","principle":"Diagnostic authority must be constrained by tenant and resource scope.","rule":"Never use broad runtime inspection when tenant-specific evidence is sufficient.","examples":["Filtering traces by authorized tenant context before displaying payload metadata.","Running experiments against isolated tenant test data."],"invalid_examples":["Dumping all in-memory requests from a shared process to diagnose one tenant's failure."],"problem":"Shared execution environments contain data belonging to multiple tenants.","failure":"Debugging leaks unrelated tenant information or changes shared state.","cause":"Diagnostic scope is broader than authorization scope.","solution":"Enforce tenant-aware evidence filtering, access control, and experiment isolation.","alternatives":["Tenant-specific replicas","Synthetic reproduction","Redacted telemetry"],"tradeoffs":"Strict isolation can reduce available diagnostic context.","constraints":["Shared resources","Privacy requirements","Least privilege"],"edge_cases":["Cross-tenant shared dependencies","Aggregated metrics","Shared caches"],"security":"Apply tenant isolation and data minimization to every diagnostic artifact.","performance":"Index tenant identifiers for efficient scoped evidence retrieval.","testing":"Attempt unauthorized cross-tenant diagnostic queries and verify denial.","verification":"Audit every diagnostic artifact for tenant scope and authorization.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["RBAC/ABAC","Tenant context","Evidence store"],"prerequisites":["Multi-tenancy","Security debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B82-R05","D25-B67-R03"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Context","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/context/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry Context supports propagation of execution context; tenant authorization must be enforced separately by the diagnostic system.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B86-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Failure Containment During Debugging","concept":"Sensitive-data-safe diagnostics","knowledge_type":"security_rule","question":"How should debugging systems prevent sensitive data exposure?","answer":"Collect the minimum necessary diagnostic information, redact or tokenize sensitive fields, enforce access controls, and avoid retaining raw secrets or unnecessary payload content.","explanation":"Debugging can expose credentials, tokens, personal data, financial information, or confidential business data if diagnostic collection is unrestricted.","principle":"Diagnostic usefulness does not justify unrestricted access to sensitive runtime state.","rule":"Prefer metadata and structured context over raw sensitive payloads whenever possible.","examples":["Capturing request identifiers and error classes instead of authentication tokens.","Redacting sensitive fields before persistence."],"invalid_examples":["Logging full HTTP authorization headers for every failed request."],"problem":"Debugging requires runtime evidence that may contain sensitive information.","failure":"Diagnostic artifacts become a secondary data-exposure channel.","cause":"Collection is broader than the diagnostic requirement.","solution":"Apply data classification, minimization, redaction, retention, and access controls.","alternatives":["Tokenization","Structured error metadata","Synthetic reproduction"],"tradeoffs":"Aggressive redaction can remove useful evidence.","constraints":["Privacy","Compliance","Security"],"edge_cases":["Encrypted payloads","Secrets embedded in exception messages","Memory dumps"],"security":"Treat diagnostic artifacts as potentially sensitive and protect them accordingly.","performance":"Prefer lightweight redaction at collection boundaries.","testing":"Inject synthetic secrets into diagnostic paths and verify that protected fields are not persisted.","verification":"Audit stored diagnostic artifacts for prohibited sensitive values.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Data classification","Access control"],"prerequisites":["Secure logging","Observability"],"relationships":["D25-B82-R05","D25-B86-R03"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/semantic-conventions/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry semantic conventions promote structured telemetry; sensitive-data handling remains an application and platform security responsibility.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B86-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Failure Containment During Debugging","concept":"Safe rollback of diagnostic changes","knowledge_type":"operational_method","question":"How should temporary debugging changes be removed safely?","answer":"Track each diagnostic change, define its rollback condition, restore the prior configuration or instrumentation state, and verify that normal operation and observability remain intact.","explanation":"Temporary diagnostics can become permanent configuration drift if their lifecycle is not explicitly managed.","principle":"Every temporary diagnostic intervention needs a controlled lifecycle.","rule":"Do not remove or retain diagnostic changes without verifying the resulting system state.","examples":["Disable temporary verbose logging after incident closure and verify normal logging rates.","Remove a temporary trace rule and verify standard telemetry remains active."],"invalid_examples":["Leaving emergency debug configuration enabled indefinitely because the incident has ended."],"problem":"Temporary diagnostic state persists after the investigation.","failure":"Performance, security, or storage degradation continues after recovery.","cause":"No rollback ownership or verification exists.","solution":"Attach expiration, ownership, rollback, and post-change verification to every temporary diagnostic intervention.","alternatives":["Time-limited configuration","Automated expiration","Change-management workflow"],"tradeoffs":"Lifecycle controls add process overhead but prevent diagnostic drift.","constraints":["Emergency changes may require rapid execution.","Rollback itself can fail."],"edge_cases":["Nested temporary changes","Multiple investigators","Partial rollback"],"security":"Remove temporary access privileges and sensitive capture rules after use.","performance":"Verify that diagnostic overhead returns to expected levels.","testing":"Test rollback procedures in non-production environments.","verification":"Compare post-rollback configuration with the approved baseline.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Configuration management","Change audit"],"prerequisites":["Operational debugging","Configuration control"],"relationships":["D25-B85-R04","D25-B83-R02"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"Controlled experiment systems require explicit experiment management; safe rollback is a corresponding operational control for temporary debugging changes.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B87-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Incident Evidence Reconstruction","concept":"Incident timeline normalization","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should a debugging system construct a reliable incident timeline from heterogeneous evidence?","answer":"Normalize timestamps, preserve original timestamps and clock metadata, order events using available causal relationships, and explicitly mark uncertain ordering.","explanation":"Logs, traces, metrics, alerts, deployments, and external events can use different clocks and collection delays.","principle":"A diagnostic timeline is a reconstructed model, not automatically the raw order in which records were received.","rule":"Preserve source timestamps and distinguish event time from ingestion time.","examples":["Ordering a database event by event timestamp while retaining collector arrival time separately."],"invalid_examples":["Using log ingestion order as the definitive execution order."],"problem":"Evidence arrives from systems with different clocks and delays.","failure":"The timeline reverses causal events.","cause":"Event time and collection time are conflated.","solution":"Represent both event and ingestion timestamps and use causal links when available.","alternatives":["Logical clocks","Trace ordering","Clock synchronization"],"tradeoffs":"Richer time metadata improves reconstruction but increases data complexity.","constraints":["Clock skew","Missing timestamps","Delayed collection"],"edge_cases":["Negative durations","Clock jumps","Long-running asynchronous tasks"],"security":"Timestamp metadata may reveal operational schedules and should be appropriately protected.","performance":"Index event timestamps and correlation identifiers.","testing":"Inject clock skew and delayed delivery into timeline reconstruction tests.","verification":"Validate reconstructed ordering against known causal constraints.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Timestamp metadata","Correlation IDs"],"prerequisites":["Distributed debugging","Temporal reasoning"],"relationships":["D25-B61-R02","D25-B68-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Dapper demonstrates distributed request tracing and timing information for reconstructing execution paths across services.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B87-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Incident Evidence Reconstruction","concept":"Evidence immutability","knowledge_type":"integrity_rule","question":"Why should collected incident evidence be immutable?","answer":"Immutable evidence preserves the original diagnostic state so later interpretation cannot silently alter the factual basis of the investigation.","explanation":"Logs may rotate, telemetry may be reprocessed, and incident responders may annotate records. Original evidence should remain distinguishable from later interpretations.","principle":"Observed evidence and subsequent analysis are separate artifacts.","rule":"Never overwrite original evidence with corrected, interpreted, or redacted representations without retaining the original according to applicable policy.","examples":["Store an immutable raw event reference and a separate normalized interpretation.","Preserve original timestamps while deriving normalized timestamps."],"invalid_examples":["Editing the original incident event after discovering a better interpretation."],"problem":"Evidence changes during investigation.","failure":"Later investigators cannot determine what was originally observed.","cause":"Observation and interpretation share mutable storage.","solution":"Separate immutable evidence from derived analysis.","alternatives":["Append-only event store","Content hashes","Evidence snapshots"],"tradeoffs":"Immutable retention increases storage requirements.","constraints":["Privacy and retention policies may require controlled deletion.","Raw evidence can contain sensitive data."],"edge_cases":["Required redaction","Legal deletion requests","Corrupted source records"],"security":"Protect evidence integrity with access controls and tamper-evident mechanisms.","performance":"Store large artifacts externally and reference them by immutable identifiers.","testing":"Verify that derived annotations cannot mutate original evidence.","verification":"Check content hashes or equivalent integrity metadata where applicable.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence store","Integrity metadata"],"prerequisites":["Incident response","Data provenance"],"relationships":["D25-B70-R03","D25-B85-R05"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"Telemetry specifications define structured observations; immutable evidence handling is a separate integrity requirement for diagnostic systems.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B87-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Incident Evidence Reconstruction","concept":"Derived-evidence separation","knowledge_type":"knowledge_model","question":"Why should derived diagnostic conclusions be stored separately from raw observations?","answer":"Separating observations from interpretations prevents hypotheses, summaries, and inferred relationships from being mistaken for directly observed facts.","explanation":"A debugging knowledge system must distinguish what happened according to instrumentation from what an investigator believes explains it.","principle":"Observation, inference, hypothesis, and verified outcome are different epistemic entities.","rule":"Never store an inferred root cause as if it were an original runtime observation.","examples":["raw_event → inferred_state_transition → root_cause_hypothesis"],"invalid_examples":["Writing 'database caused timeout' directly into the raw event record."],"problem":"Diagnostic databases mix evidence and interpretation.","failure":"Later reasoning treats hypotheses as facts.","cause":"No epistemic type separation.","solution":"Represent evidence and inference as linked but distinct records.","alternatives":["Evidence graphs","Typed knowledge models","Event-sourcing with derived views"],"tradeoffs":"Typed models increase implementation complexity but substantially improve reasoning integrity.","constraints":["All inference records need provenance.","Conflicting hypotheses must coexist until resolved."],"edge_cases":["Human annotations","Automated root-cause suggestions","Retrospective corrections"],"security":"Access controls should distinguish raw evidence from privileged analysis.","performance":"Index both evidence and inference relationships for retrieval.","testing":"Insert conflicting hypotheses and verify that raw evidence remains unchanged.","verification":"Require every inference to reference supporting evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence model","Provenance graph"],"prerequisites":["Epistemic modeling","Root-cause analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B40-R01","D25-B87-R02"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry provides structured observability data; explicit separation of evidence and inference is a knowledge-engineering requirement built around such observations.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B87-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Incident Evidence Reconstruction","concept":"Evidence expiration","knowledge_type":"temporal_reasoning","question":"Why should debugging systems track evidence freshness?","answer":"Evidence can become stale as configuration, dependencies, system state, and telemetry interpretation change, so conclusions should retain the temporal validity of their supporting evidence.","explanation":"A fact that was true during an incident may not describe the current system state.","principle":"Evidence has temporal scope unless explicitly established as invariant.","rule":"Attach observation time and validity context to time-sensitive diagnostic evidence.","examples":["A configuration snapshot from incident time should not automatically represent the current configuration.","A service dependency observed during an incident may later be removed."],"invalid_examples":["Using a months-old environment snapshot as current runtime evidence."],"problem":"Historical evidence is reused outside its validity period.","failure":"The debugger produces stale conclusions.","cause":"Evidence freshness is not modeled.","solution":"Track observation time, validity interval where known, and system-version context.","alternatives":["Temporal knowledge graph","Versioned evidence store","Snapshot references"],"tradeoffs":"Temporal metadata increases reasoning complexity but prevents stale-state errors.","constraints":["Some evidence is timeless while other evidence is highly volatile."],"edge_cases":["Long-lived configuration","Historical incident analysis","Backfilled telemetry"],"security":"Historical sensitive evidence must follow retention and access policies.","performance":"Use indexed validity intervals for temporal queries.","testing":"Verify that stale evidence is not selected as current state without explicit justification.","verification":"Check that time-sensitive conclusions reference evidence from the relevant period.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Temporal metadata","Versioned state"],"prerequisites":["Temporal reasoning","Incident analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B69-R04","D25-B82-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"Telemetry observations are inherently associated with execution context and time; temporal validity must be modeled when reused for later diagnosis.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B87-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Incident Evidence Reconstruction","concept":"Incident evidence replay","knowledge_type":"method","question":"What does evidence replay mean in debugging?","answer":"Evidence replay reconstructs a diagnostic timeline or state model from preserved incident artifacts without claiming that the original system execution is being rerun.","explanation":"Replay of evidence is distinct from deterministic execution replay. It reconstructs observations and relationships for analysis.","principle":"Reconstructing an incident from evidence is not equivalent to reproducing the original execution.","rule":"Clearly distinguish evidence reconstruction from executable replay.","examples":["Rebuilding an incident timeline from traces, logs, metrics, and deployment records.","Reconstructing a causal graph from preserved event relationships."],"invalid_examples":["Calling a log-based timeline reconstruction a deterministic replay of the application."],"problem":"Investigators need to revisit historical incidents.","failure":"Historical reconstruction is mistaken for actual execution replay.","cause":"Replay terminology is used ambiguously.","solution":"Use explicit evidence-replay and execution-replay models.","alternatives":["Timeline reconstruction","State snapshot analysis","Deterministic replay"],"tradeoffs":"Evidence replay is safer and cheaper but cannot reproduce unobserved internal state.","constraints":["Preserved evidence may be incomplete.","Original external side effects are not recreated."],"edge_cases":["Missing telemetry","Contradictory logs","Clock skew"],"security":"Historical evidence must remain access-controlled.","performance":"Precompute incident timelines and indexes for repeated analysis.","testing":"Compare reconstructed timelines against known incident sequences.","verification":"Label reconstructed facts according to their evidence source and uncertainty.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence store","Timeline reconstruction"],"prerequisites":["Incident analysis","Provenance"],"relationships":["D25-B62-R01","D25-B87-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Distributed tracing provides historical execution observations from which diagnostic timelines can be reconstructed.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B88-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Knowledge Capture from Debugging","concept":"Root-cause knowledge normalization","knowledge_type":"knowledge_engineering","question":"How should completed debugging investigations be converted into reusable knowledge?","answer":"Normalize the investigation into problem signature, observed symptoms, causal evidence, confirmed root cause, corrective action, verification evidence, environmental context, and applicability conditions.","explanation":"Raw incident narratives are difficult for automated retrieval unless their reusable technical relationships are represented explicitly.","principle":"Reusable debugging knowledge should capture causal structure rather than merely narrative chronology.","rule":"Separate observed facts, hypotheses, confirmed causes, and remediation outcomes.","examples":["error_signature + environment + invariant_violation → confirmed_root_cause"],"invalid_examples":["Storing only a prose postmortem paragraph without structured causal fields."],"problem":"Past debugging work cannot be efficiently reused.","failure":"Future investigations repeatedly rediscover the same failure patterns.","cause":"Knowledge capture is narrative-only or incomplete.","solution":"Transform validated investigations into structured diagnostic records.","alternatives":["Case-based reasoning","Failure pattern library","Incident knowledge graph"],"tradeoffs":"Structured knowledge improves retrieval but requires normalization effort.","constraints":["Only verified findings should become confirmed knowledge.","Applicability boundaries must be preserved."],"edge_cases":["Partially resolved incidents","Multiple root causes","Environment-specific fixes"],"security":"Knowledge records must remove unnecessary confidential incident details.","performance":"Index signatures, causes, dependencies, and symptoms for retrieval.","testing":"Evaluate retrieval against known recurring incidents.","verification":"Require links to evidence and outcome verification before marking knowledge confirmed.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Incident records","Knowledge graph"],"prerequisites":["Root-cause analysis","Knowledge representation"],"relationships":["D25-B49-R01","D25-B87-R03"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"Structured telemetry provides machine-readable observations that can serve as inputs to reusable debugging knowledge; normalization is a knowledge-engineering layer.","confidence":0.94,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B88-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Knowledge Capture from Debugging","concept":"Applicability boundary","knowledge_type":"knowledge_model","question":"Why must reusable debugging knowledge include applicability boundaries?","answer":"A diagnosis or fix that is valid under one runtime, dependency, architecture, or failure condition may be invalid elsewhere.","explanation":"Generalizing a local fix into a universal rule can create new failures.","principle":"Every reusable debugging rule should state the conditions under which it has been validated or observed.","rule":"Do not generalize environment-specific findings without evidence supporting broader applicability.","examples":["A workaround validated for one runtime version is tagged with that runtime constraint.","A database lock diagnosis is linked to the transaction pattern that produced it."],"invalid_examples":["Turning one incident-specific workaround into a universal coding rule."],"problem":"Knowledge is reused outside its validated context.","failure":"Automation applies an inappropriate diagnostic or remediation strategy.","cause":"Applicability metadata is absent.","solution":"Store versions, environments, architecture, triggering conditions, and known exclusions.","alternatives":["Contextual retrieval","Constraint-based knowledge matching"],"tradeoffs":"More metadata improves precision but can reduce broad retrieval recall.","constraints":["Applicability may be uncertain.","Future versions may invalidate findings."],"edge_cases":["Multiple validated environments","Version transitions","Architecture changes"],"security":"Applicability metadata should not expose sensitive architecture unnecessarily.","performance":"Index high-value applicability dimensions.","testing":"Test retrieval with both matching and non-matching environments.","verification":"Ensure automated reuse checks applicability constraints before application.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Version metadata","Knowledge retrieval"],"prerequisites":["Knowledge engineering","Version-aware debugging"],"relationships":["D25-B27-R01","D25-B88-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"Telemetry context can identify runtime and resource attributes; applicability boundaries require preserving those contextual dimensions in debugging knowledge.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B88-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Knowledge Capture from Debugging","concept":"Contradiction-aware debugging knowledge","knowledge_type":"knowledge_model","question":"How should a debugging knowledge base represent contradictory findings?","answer":"Store conflicting observations or hypotheses as separate evidence-linked records with provenance and resolution status rather than silently overwriting one side.","explanation":"Contradictions can result from different environments, timing, instrumentation gaps, or genuinely competing causal explanations.","principle":"Contradictions are diagnostic information until resolved.","rule":"Never delete conflicting evidence merely to produce a cleaner root-cause record.","examples":["One environment reproduces a memory failure while another does not, with environment differences retained as evidence."],"invalid_examples":["Removing the non-reproducing case because it complicates the root-cause narrative."],"problem":"Knowledge records contain inconsistent findings.","failure":"The system hides uncertainty and learns an overgeneralized rule.","cause":"Knowledge ingestion assumes every case has one uniform explanation.","solution":"Represent contradictions explicitly and track their resolution status.","alternatives":["Contradiction graph","Evidence ledger","Hypothesis set"],"tradeoffs":"Contradiction-aware storage is more complex but protects against false certainty.","constraints":["Resolution may remain unknown.","Contradictory evidence must retain provenance."],"edge_cases":["Instrumentation errors","Version differences","Concurrent root causes"],"security":"Access controls should preserve sensitive evidence while exposing only authorized contradictions.","performance":"Index contradiction relationships for targeted review.","testing":"Insert intentionally conflicting records and verify that neither is silently discarded.","verification":"Require explicit evidence before marking a contradiction resolved.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence provenance","Knowledge graph"],"prerequisites":["Contradiction handling","Root-cause analysis"],"relationships":["D25-B70-R02","D25-B82-R03"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"Structured telemetry supports provenance-rich observations; contradiction management is a higher-level knowledge representation requirement.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B88-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Knowledge Capture from Debugging","concept":"Remediation versus root cause","knowledge_type":"reasoning_rule","question":"Why should remediation be stored separately from root cause?","answer":"A remediation can reduce symptoms without eliminating the underlying cause, so a debugging knowledge system must distinguish causal explanation from corrective or mitigating action.","explanation":"Examples include restarting a service, increasing capacity, disabling a feature, or adding retries. These may restore service while leaving the defect intact.","principle":"A successful mitigation does not prove that the underlying cause has been removed.","rule":"Do not mark a root cause as resolved solely because a remediation reduced symptoms.","examples":["Restarting a memory-leaking service restores availability but does not remove the leak.","Increasing a timeout hides a slow dependency without fixing its latency."],"invalid_examples":["Classifying a workaround as root-cause resolution because alerts stopped."],"problem":"Operational recovery is mistaken for defect resolution.","failure":"The system learns an incorrect causal relationship.","cause":"Outcome and mechanism are conflated.","solution":"Record mitigation, corrective action, root cause, and verification outcome separately.","alternatives":["Incident lifecycle model","Causal remediation graph"],"tradeoffs":"Separation increases data-model complexity but improves long-term learning.","constraints":["Some incidents genuinely have mitigation as the final acceptable action.","Root cause may remain unknown."],"edge_cases":["Third-party defects","Unfixable legacy systems","Temporary compensating controls"],"security":"Mitigation records must not imply that unresolved security defects are permanently closed.","performance":"Index root cause and remediation separately for retrieval.","testing":"Verify that a successful mitigation does not automatically transition the root-cause state to confirmed.","verification":"Require independent evidence that the underlying causal mechanism has been addressed.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Incident state model","Outcome verification"],"prerequisites":["Root-cause analysis","Incident management"],"relationships":["D25-B66-R05","D25-B48-R01"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"Controlled experiments can demonstrate symptom response to interventions; a response does not by itself establish elimination of the underlying mechanism.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B88-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Knowledge Capture from Debugging","concept":"Verified remediation record","knowledge_type":"verification_model","question":"What makes a remediation record reusable for future debugging?","answer":"A reusable remediation record links the diagnosed condition, intervention, scope, expected effect, actual observed outcome, verification evidence, applicability constraints, and rollback considerations.","explanation":"Without outcome verification, a remediation recommendation is only an unverified proposal.","principle":"A remediation becomes reusable knowledge only after its outcome and applicability are established to an appropriate level.","rule":"Distinguish proposed, applied, observed-effective, and verified-effective remediation states.","examples":["proposed → applied → service recovered → underlying condition verified resolved"],"invalid_examples":["Marking a remediation verified because it was deployed successfully."],"problem":"Historical fixes are reused without knowing whether they actually worked.","failure":"Automation repeats ineffective or context-specific actions.","cause":"Remediation state lacks outcome verification.","solution":"Model remediation lifecycle and evidence explicitly.","alternatives":["Change records","Outcome verification records","Runbook evidence"],"tradeoffs":"Verification increases operational discipline and data requirements.","constraints":["Some outcomes require long observation windows.","Third-party systems may limit verification."],"edge_cases":["Delayed regressions","Partial recovery","Multiple simultaneous fixes"],"security":"Security remediations require explicit verification that the vulnerable condition no longer exists.","performance":"Use automated post-change checks where safe.","testing":"Verify state transitions for proposed, applied, effective, and verified statuses.","verification":"Require objective evidence appropriate to the remediation's claimed effect.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Outcome verification","Change management"],"prerequisites":["Incident lifecycle","Verification"],"relationships":["D25-B48-R01","D25-B70-R05"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"Controlled experiment frameworks support explicit experiment outcomes; reusable remediation records require equivalent outcome tracking.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B89-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Automated Diagnostic Reasoning","concept":"Hypothesis portfolio management","knowledge_type":"reasoning_architecture","question":"Why should an automated debugging system maintain multiple competing hypotheses?","answer":"Maintaining multiple hypotheses prevents premature convergence on a plausible but weak explanation when evidence is incomplete or ambiguous.","explanation":"Complex failures may have several plausible causes, including interacting causes. A portfolio allows evidence to eliminate or strengthen alternatives incrementally.","principle":"Diagnostic reasoning should preserve uncertainty until evidence sufficiently separates hypotheses.","rule":"Do not collapse to a single root-cause hypothesis merely because it currently has the most supporting evidence.","examples":["Maintaining dependency failure, resource exhaustion, and configuration regression as concurrent hypotheses until discriminating evidence arrives."],"invalid_examples":["Selecting the first plausible explanation and discarding alternatives."],"problem":"Automated diagnosis risks confirmation bias and premature closure.","failure":"The system repeatedly reinforces its initial guess.","cause":"Single-hypothesis reasoning model.","solution":"Represent candidate hypotheses, supporting evidence, contradictions, and unresolved questions explicitly.","alternatives":["Hypothesis graph","Bayesian model","Weighted evidence set"],"tradeoffs":"Multiple hypotheses increase computational and storage requirements.","constraints":["Hypothesis generation must remain evidence-grounded.","Confidence must not be represented as measured probability unless calibrated."],"edge_cases":["Mutually exclusive causes","Multiple simultaneous causes","Insufficient evidence"],"security":"Automated hypotheses must not trigger high-impact actions without authorization and verification.","performance":"Prune hypotheses using explicit contradiction and applicability rules.","testing":"Evaluate the system on incidents where the first plausible hypothesis is incorrect.","verification":"Require convergence criteria based on evidence rather than arbitrary confidence thresholds.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence graph","Hypothesis manager"],"prerequisites":["Causal reasoning","Debugging knowledge representation"],"relationships":["D25-B41-R01","D25-B88-R03"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Distributed tracing provides structured evidence across service boundaries; multi-hypothesis reasoning is a diagnostic architecture built on correlated evidence.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B89-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Automated Diagnostic Reasoning","concept":"Evidence-to-hypothesis traceability","knowledge_type":"verification_rule","question":"What should an automated root-cause explanation expose?","answer":"It should expose the hypothesis, supporting observations, contradictory observations, causal links, assumptions, uncertainty, and verification status.","explanation":"An explanation that only outputs a root-cause label is difficult to audit and unsafe for autonomous debugging.","principle":"Diagnostic conclusions must be traceable to evidence.","rule":"Every automated causal claim should have machine-readable evidence references.","examples":["root_cause_hypothesis → supporting_trace_ids → causal_transition → verification_record"],"invalid_examples":["root_cause='database failure' with no evidence references."],"problem":"Automated diagnosis produces conclusions without auditability.","failure":"Users cannot verify or challenge the reasoning.","cause":"Evidence links are discarded after inference.","solution":"Persist an evidence graph linking observations to hypotheses and outcomes.","alternatives":["Provenance graph","Reasoning trace","Case-based explanation"],"tradeoffs":"Traceability increases storage and computation but enables verification.","constraints":["Sensitive evidence may require filtered views.","Inference chains can become large."],"edge_cases":["Conflicting evidence","Derived evidence","Recursive hypotheses"],"security":"Evidence references must respect access control.","performance":"Use compact immutable IDs and indexed graph edges.","testing":"Remove one supporting evidence item and verify that the explanation reflects the loss.","verification":"Audit every causal conclusion for at least one valid evidence path.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence graph","Provenance"],"prerequisites":["Automated diagnosis","Knowledge representation"],"relationships":["D25-B70-R03","D25-B88-R03"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry provides standardized identifiers and structured telemetry that can serve as evidence references in diagnostic systems.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B89-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Automated Diagnostic Reasoning","concept":"Automated diagnosis confidence calibration","knowledge_type":"reasoning_rule","question":"Why should confidence in automated debugging conclusions be calibrated rather than treated as certainty?","answer":"Confidence reflects the quality, completeness, independence, and relevance of evidence; without calibration, a numerical confidence value can create false precision.","explanation":"A model can be highly confident while lacking critical evidence, especially when trained on recurring but non-generalizable patterns.","principle":"Confidence is meaningful only when its interpretation and calibration are defined.","rule":"Never equate model confidence with verified truth or measured probability without appropriate calibration evidence.","examples":["A diagnosis may have high heuristic confidence but remain unverified until a controlled reproduction succeeds."],"invalid_examples":["Treating an AI score of 0.95 as proof that the root cause is correct."],"problem":"Automated systems overstate diagnostic certainty.","failure":"Operators trust unsupported diagnoses.","cause":"Confidence and verification are conflated.","solution":"Separate epistemic confidence from verification state and preserve evidence completeness.","alternatives":["Calibrated probabilities","Ordinal confidence categories","Evidence sufficiency states"],"tradeoffs":"More nuanced uncertainty representation is harder to implement and explain.","constraints":["Calibration requires representative evaluation data.","Confidence can drift as system behavior changes."],"edge_cases":["Novel failures","Distribution shift","Sparse evidence"],"security":"High-impact actions should require verification independent of model confidence.","performance":"Use inexpensive evidence-quality checks before expensive model inference.","testing":"Evaluate calibration on held-out debugging cases.","verification":"Track whether high-confidence diagnoses are subsequently verified.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evaluation dataset","Verification model"],"prerequisites":["Machine learning evaluation","Debugging reasoning"],"relationships":["D25-B41-R03","D25-B89-R02"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"Telemetry standards provide structured evidence but do not define AI confidence calibration; calibration and verification remain separate reasoning requirements.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B89-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Automated Diagnostic Reasoning","concept":"Diagnostic action gating","knowledge_type":"governance_rule","question":"When should automated debugging be allowed to perform a corrective action?","answer":"Automated corrective action should require explicit authorization boundaries, sufficient evidence, safety conditions, and appropriate verification capability, especially when the action can cause material side effects.","explanation":"Diagnosis and action are distinct stages. A plausible diagnosis does not automatically authorize intervention.","principle":"Evidence supporting a diagnosis is not equivalent to authorization to modify the system.","rule":"Gate high-impact debugging actions through policy, scope, approval, and post-action verification.","examples":["Automatically collecting a bounded diagnostic trace may be low impact; restarting a production service may require approval."],"invalid_examples":["Allowing an AI debugger to restart arbitrary production services based only on a predicted root cause."],"problem":"Autonomous diagnosis can be coupled too tightly to execution.","failure":"Incorrect diagnoses cause additional operational damage.","cause":"No separation between inference and authorization.","solution":"Implement diagnostic action policies and verification gates.","alternatives":["Human approval","Policy engine","Sandboxed remediation"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger gates reduce automation speed but reduce uncontrolled impact.","constraints":["Authorization","Tenant scope","Safety requirements"],"edge_cases":["Emergency mitigation","Self-healing systems","Cascading failures"],"security":"Enforce least privilege and explicit action scopes.","performance":"Use predefined low-risk actions for fast automated response.","testing":"Attempt unauthorized diagnostic actions and verify policy denial.","verification":"Require post-action state verification before marking the action successful.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Authorization policy","Action executor","Verification system"],"prerequisites":["Autonomous agents","Operational governance"],"relationships":["D25-B42-R02","D25-B88-R05"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"AWS FIS demonstrates controlled experiment execution and safety-oriented experiment management; action gating generalizes these controls to automated debugging interventions.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B89-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Automated Diagnostic Reasoning","concept":"Diagnostic reasoning replay","knowledge_type":"learning_method","question":"Why should automated debugging decisions be replayable?","answer":"Replayable reasoning allows investigators to reconstruct which evidence, hypotheses, rules, and system context produced a diagnostic conclusion.","explanation":"Without replayability, automated diagnosis cannot be reliably audited, improved, or compared after knowledge changes.","principle":"A learning debugging system should preserve enough state to reconstruct prior reasoning.","rule":"Record model or rule version, evidence references, hypothesis state, relevant context, and decision transitions.","examples":["Replaying a root-cause decision after updating a diagnostic rule while preserving the original evidence set."],"invalid_examples":["Re-running diagnosis against current telemetry and claiming it reproduces the historical decision."],"problem":"Automated diagnosis changes over time.","failure":"Historical decisions cannot be explained or compared.","cause":"Reasoning context is not preserved.","solution":"Store versioned diagnostic snapshots and evidence references.","alternatives":["Decision logs","Reasoning snapshots","Knowledge-version replay"],"tradeoffs":"Replayability increases storage and metadata requirements.","constraints":["External dependencies may change.","Sensitive evidence may expire."],"edge_cases":["Model upgrades","Rule changes","Deleted telemetry"],"security":"Reasoning records may contain sensitive operational context and require access control.","performance":"Persist compact references rather than duplicating large evidence payloads.","testing":"Replay historical decisions against frozen knowledge versions and compare outputs.","verification":"Confirm that replay uses the original evidence and knowledge versions rather than current mutable state.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Versioned knowledge","Evidence store"],"prerequisites":["Automated diagnosis","Reproducibility"],"relationships":["D25-B62-R01","D25-B70-R04"],"provenance":[{"source":"WinDbg Time Travel Debugging","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/debuggercmds/windbg-timetravel-preview"}],"evidence":"Time-travel debugging demonstrates preservation and replay-oriented analysis of execution history; diagnostic reasoning replay applies the same reproducibility principle to reasoning state.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B90-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Debugging System Architecture","concept":"Evidence graph architecture","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"What should an evidence graph contain for advanced debugging?","answer":"An evidence graph should represent observations, executions, states, components, dependencies, hypotheses, interventions, outcomes, provenance, temporal relationships, and verification links as distinct typed entities.","explanation":"Typed graph relationships allow debugging systems to connect low-level evidence with higher-level causal reasoning without collapsing facts and inferences into one record.","principle":"Debugging knowledge should preserve both entities and the relationships that explain their causal and evidentiary structure.","rule":"Use typed edges and explicit provenance for causal, temporal, dependency, and evidentiary relationships.","examples":["trace_span → observed_in → service","hypothesis → supported_by → evidence","remediation → verified_by → outcome"],"invalid_examples":["Storing every diagnostic relationship as an untyped text field."],"problem":"Relationally isolated evidence makes cross-layer reasoning difficult.","failure":"The system cannot reconstruct causal context efficiently.","cause":"Evidence lacks explicit relationships.","solution":"Build a typed evidence graph with stable identifiers and provenance.","alternatives":["Relational evidence model","Event-sourced knowledge graph","Hybrid graph-plus-index architecture"],"tradeoffs":"Graphs improve relationship traversal but require careful schema and consistency management.","constraints":["Graph relationships must remain provenance-backed.","Large graphs require indexing and lifecycle policies."],"edge_cases":["Cycles","Contradictions","Deleted evidence","Versioned entities"],"security":"Graph traversal must enforce entity-level authorization.","performance":"Use indexed edge types and bounded traversals for interactive diagnosis.","testing":"Validate graph consistency and relationship direction using known incident cases.","verification":"Check that every causal edge has evidence or an explicitly declared inference basis.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Knowledge graph","Evidence store"],"prerequisites":["Graph modeling","Debugging knowledge representation"],"relationships":["D25-B40-R01","D25-B89-R02"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Dapper demonstrates structured relationships among distributed execution components through tracing; an evidence graph generalizes relationship-centric diagnosis.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B90-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Debugging System Architecture","concept":"Diagnostic query planning","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"How should an automated debugging system plan diagnostic queries?","answer":"It should begin with the failure signature and available evidence, identify the highest-value unresolved distinctions, retrieve the smallest relevant evidence set, and expand the search only when necessary.","explanation":"Unbounded retrieval creates noise and increases computational cost. Query planning should be driven by diagnostic hypotheses and evidence gaps.","principle":"Diagnostic retrieval should be targeted by uncertainty rather than by indiscriminate data collection.","rule":"Prefer evidence queries that can distinguish active hypotheses.","examples":["Querying all traces related to a failing request ID before retrieving unrelated service telemetry.","Retrieving configuration history only after evidence suggests a configuration-dependent regression."],"invalid_examples":["Loading the entire telemetry database for every incident."],"problem":"Large observability datasets contain excessive irrelevant evidence.","failure":"Reasoning becomes slow and noisy.","cause":"Retrieval lacks diagnostic query planning.","solution":"Use hypothesis-aware retrieval with progressive expansion.","alternatives":["Graph traversal","Hierarchical retrieval","Case-based retrieval"],"tradeoffs":"Targeted retrieval improves efficiency but depends on good hypotheses and indexes.","constraints":["Initial hypotheses may be wrong.","Critical evidence can be omitted if retrieval rules are too restrictive."],"edge_cases":["Novel failures","Missing identifiers","Highly correlated symptoms"],"security":"Query planning must respect evidence access boundaries.","performance":"Use indexed identifiers, time ranges, and relationship types.","testing":"Evaluate whether known root-cause evidence is retrieved without excessive unrelated data.","verification":"Measure retrieval completeness for confirmed incident evidence.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence index","Hypothesis manager"],"prerequisites":["Information retrieval","Debugging reasoning"],"relationships":["D25-B49-R01","D25-B82-R05"],"provenance":[{"source":"Google Dapper","url":"https://research.google/pubs/dapper-a-large-scale-distributed-systems-tracing-infrastructure/"}],"evidence":"Distributed tracing provides structured identifiers and relationships that support targeted diagnostic retrieval.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B90-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Debugging System Architecture","concept":"Debugging state machine","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"Why should an advanced debugging system model investigation state explicitly?","answer":"Explicit investigation states prevent unsupported transitions such as moving directly from symptom detection to confirmed root cause without diagnosis and verification.","explanation":"A debugging workflow commonly progresses through observation, hypothesis formation, evidence collection, diagnosis, intervention, outcome observation, and verification.","principle":"Diagnostic lifecycle state should reflect epistemic and operational status separately.","rule":"Do not mark a root cause confirmed merely because a remediation was applied or symptoms decreased.","examples":["OBSERVED → HYPOTHESIS → EVIDENCE_COLLECTED → DIAGNOSED → REMEDIATED → VERIFIED"],"invalid_examples":["OBSERVED → VERIFIED_ROOT_CAUSE without supporting evidence."],"problem":"Debugging systems conflate investigation stages.","failure":"Unsupported conclusions become operational truth.","cause":"No explicit lifecycle model.","solution":"Implement typed investigation states and guarded transitions.","alternatives":["Workflow engine","Finite-state machine","Evidence-driven state machine"],"tradeoffs":"Explicit state modeling adds complexity but improves auditability and automation safety.","constraints":["Some investigations branch into multiple hypotheses.","State transitions require evidence criteria."],"edge_cases":["Multiple root causes","Unresolved incidents","Contradictory evidence"],"security":"High-impact transitions should require authorization where applicable.","performance":"Keep state transitions event-driven and indexed.","testing":"Attempt invalid transitions and verify rejection.","verification":"Require transition-specific evidence and preserve transition history.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Evidence model","Workflow state machine"],"prerequisites":["Incident lifecycle","Verification"],"relationships":["D25-B48-R01","D25-B88-R05"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"Controlled experiments use explicit experiment lifecycle concepts; debugging state machines generalize lifecycle governance to diagnostic reasoning.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B90-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Debugging System Architecture","concept":"Debugging knowledge versioning","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"Why must debugging knowledge be versioned?","answer":"Diagnostic rules, dependencies, system architectures, telemetry semantics, and validated fixes change over time, so historical conclusions may depend on the knowledge version available when they were made.","explanation":"Versioning enables reproducible reasoning and prevents current rules from silently rewriting historical interpretations.","principle":"Knowledge used for diagnosis is part of the diagnostic state.","rule":"Record the versions of diagnostic rules, schemas, models, and relevant knowledge used for each automated conclusion.","examples":["Diagnosis generated with rule-set v12 remains associated with v12 even after v13 is deployed."],"invalid_examples":["Recomputing an old incident with current rules and replacing the original diagnosis."],"problem":"Diagnostic behavior changes over time.","failure":"Historical decisions become irreproducible.","cause":"Knowledge versions are not persisted.","solution":"Use immutable or content-addressed knowledge versions and link them to decisions.","alternatives":["Model registry","Rule registry","Knowledge snapshots"],"tradeoffs":"Versioning increases storage and deployment complexity.","constraints":["Schema migrations must preserve historical interpretation.","Old versions may depend on unavailable components."],"edge_cases":["Emergency rule changes","Backfilled knowledge","Contradiction resolution"],"security":"Protect diagnostic knowledge versions from unauthorized modification.","performance":"Use immutable identifiers and cached version metadata.","testing":"Replay historical cases under the original and current knowledge versions.","verification":"Verify that historical decisions retain their original knowledge references.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Knowledge registry","Decision provenance"],"prerequisites":["Version control","Diagnostic replay"],"relationships":["D25-B89-R05","D25-B88-R03"],"provenance":[{"source":"OpenTelemetry Specification","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/"}],"evidence":"OpenTelemetry specifications are versioned and define evolving semantic conventions; debugging systems likewise require version-aware interpretation of structured telemetry.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B90-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging and Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Debugging System Architecture","concept":"Closed-loop debugging architecture","knowledge_type":"architecture_pattern","question":"What constitutes a closed-loop debugging architecture?","answer":"A closed-loop debugging architecture connects observation, diagnosis, intervention, outcome measurement, verification, and knowledge update while preserving provenance and authorization boundaries.","explanation":"The loop prevents debugging knowledge from ending at a proposed fix. It requires evidence that the intervention changed the intended condition and captures the resulting learning.","principle":"Debugging becomes self-improving only when outcomes are verified and fed back into versioned knowledge.","rule":"Do not update confirmed debugging knowledge from an unverified intervention outcome.","examples":["observe failure → diagnose → apply authorized fix → verify invariant restoration → store verified case"],"invalid_examples":["Adding a new root-cause rule immediately after an unverified remediation."],"problem":"Debugging systems generate recommendations but do not learn reliably from outcomes.","failure":"The knowledge base accumulates unsupported fixes and false causal patterns.","cause":"Outcome verification is absent from the learning loop.","solution":"Connect evidence, action, outcome, verification, and knowledge update as separate governed stages.","alternatives":["Human-reviewed learning loop","Case-based learning","Rule-update pipeline"],"tradeoffs":"Closed-loop learning improves reuse but requires strong verification and governance.","constraints":["Verification may require delayed observation.","Learning must preserve contradictions and applicability boundaries."],"edge_cases":["Partial recovery","Multiple interventions","Unresolved root causes"],"security":"Automated knowledge updates must not weaken security controls or bypass review for high-impact rules.","performance":"Use asynchronous knowledge-update pipelines after verified outcomes.","testing":"Inject false-positive remediation outcomes and verify that unverified cases do not become confirmed knowledge.","verification":"Require objective outcome evidence before knowledge promotion.","version":"Technology-agnostic","dependencies":["Outcome verification","Knowledge registry","Governance"],"prerequisites":["Autonomous debugging","Evidence provenance","Learning systems"],"relationships":["D25-B88-R05","D25-B90-R03","D25-B90-R04"],"provenance":[{"source":"AWS Fault Injection Service","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/"}],"evidence":"Controlled experiments provide structured intervention and outcome concepts; a closed-loop debugging architecture extends these principles with verification and knowledge governance.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

{"record_id":"D25-B91-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Fault Localization","subtopic":"Uncertainty-Aware Diagnosis","concept":"Hypothesis ranking under incomplete evidence","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"How should a debugging system rank competing root-cause hypotheses when evidence is incomplete?","answer":"Maintain multiple explicit hypotheses, attach evidence and counterevidence to each, and update their confidence as new observations arrive rather than prematurely selecting one cause.","explanation":"Incomplete telemetry, nondeterministic behavior, and correlated symptoms can make several causes initially plausible. A robust debugger preserves alternatives until discriminating evidence appears.","principle":"Diagnosis should represent uncertainty explicitly instead of converting weak evidence into certainty.","rule":"Do not mark a root cause as confirmed until evidence distinguishes it from credible alternatives.","examples":["Three hypotheses explain the same timeout; a targeted experiment changes only one causal factor and eliminates two hypotheses."],"invalid_examples":["Selecting the first stack frame containing an exception as the root cause without testing alternatives."],"problem":"Several independent causes can produce the same symptom.","failure":"Premature root-cause commitment.","cause":"Insufficient evidence and confirmation bias.","solution":"Use hypothesis sets with supporting and contradicting evidence.","alternatives":["Bayesian updating","weighted evidence scoring","decision-tree diagnosis"],"tradeoffs":"Maintaining alternatives costs analysis effort but reduces false closure.","constraints":["Evidence may be incomplete","Some experiments may be unsafe in production"],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous faults","Correlated telemetry","Hidden upstream failures"],"security":"Diagnostic experiments must not expose sensitive data or weaken production controls.","performance":"Prioritize experiments with high information gain and low operational cost.","testing":"Create tests that distinguish competing causal hypotheses.","verification":"Require at least one discriminating observation before confirmation.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Evidence collection","Hypothesis formulation"],"relationships":["D25-B40","D25-B41"],"provenance":["General debugging methodology; evidence-driven diagnosis"],"evidence":"A causal explanation is stronger when alternative explanations have been actively tested.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B91-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Fault Localization","subtopic":"Evidence Weighting","concept":"Discriminating evidence","knowledge_type":"method","question":"What makes debugging evidence highly valuable?","answer":"Evidence is highly valuable when it can distinguish between competing explanations rather than merely being correlated with the failure.","explanation":"A log line that occurs during every incident may be useful context but may not identify the cause. An experiment that changes one suspected causal factor and changes the failure outcome is more discriminating.","principle":"Prefer evidence that reduces diagnostic uncertainty.","rule":"Prioritize observations that can falsify competing hypotheses.","examples":["Disabling one suspected feature eliminates the failure while unrelated controls remain unchanged."],"invalid_examples":["Treating temporal proximity alone as proof of causation."],"problem":"Debuggers collect large amounts of correlated information.","failure":"High-volume evidence produces low diagnostic progress.","cause":"Evidence is not evaluated for discriminatory power.","solution":"Rank evidence by its ability to eliminate hypotheses.","alternatives":["Controlled experiments","Differential execution","Fault injection"],"tradeoffs":"Highly discriminating experiments may require more setup.","constraints":["Experiments must preserve safety boundaries"],"edge_cases":["Distributed systems with many interacting variables"],"security":"Experiments must not bypass authentication or authorization controls.","performance":"Prefer low-cost experiments that sharply reduce uncertainty.","testing":"Measure whether each diagnostic experiment eliminates at least one hypothesis.","verification":"Record the hypothesis state before and after each experiment.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Competing hypotheses"],"relationships":["D25-B44","D25-B65","D25-B81-R01"],"provenance":["Evidence-driven debugging methodology"],"evidence":"Discriminating observations provide stronger causal information than simple correlation.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B91-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Repair Validation","subtopic":"Minimal Patches","concept":"Causal patch minimality","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"Why should a debugging system prefer a minimal corrective patch when possible?","answer":"A minimal patch changes fewer causal variables, making verification easier and reducing the probability of unrelated regressions.","explanation":"Large fixes can obscure whether the original defect was actually corrected and can introduce new behavior changes that complicate validation.","principle":"Change only what is necessary to eliminate the established failure mechanism unless broader remediation is justified.","rule":"Separate the defect fix from unrelated cleanup whenever practical.","examples":["Correcting an incorrect boundary condition without simultaneously rewriting the surrounding module."],"invalid_examples":["Replacing an entire subsystem when one verified invariant violation explains the defect."],"problem":"Broad patches make causal verification difficult.","failure":"A fix appears successful but introduces unrelated regressions.","cause":"Unnecessary change surface.","solution":"Minimize the patch while preserving the required invariant.","alternatives":["Subsystem redesign when the defect exposes architectural weakness"],"tradeoffs":"Minimal patches may leave technical debt that requires separate planned work.","constraints":["The patch must address the actual root cause"],"edge_cases":["Security vulnerabilities requiring defense-in-depth changes","Architectural defects with multiple dependent failures"],"security":"Security fixes may require layered remediation beyond the immediate defect.","performance":"Avoid unnecessary performance changes during a correctness fix unless performance is causal.","testing":"Test both the original reproducer and relevant regression boundaries.","verification":"Confirm that the patch eliminates the causal mechanism, not only the observed symptom.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Root-cause hypothesis","Reproducer"],"relationships":["D25-B48","D25-B90"],"provenance":["General software repair principles"],"evidence":"Smaller causal change surfaces simplify regression analysis and verification.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B91-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Repair Validation","subtopic":"Regression Protection","concept":"Regression invariant extraction","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How can a debugger convert a discovered defect into durable regression protection?","answer":"Extract the violated invariant or behavioral contract and encode it in a focused regression test that fails on the defective behavior and passes after the repair.","explanation":"Testing only the original input may prevent recurrence of that exact example while missing the broader contract that was violated.","principle":"Preserve the discovered invariant, not merely the historical input.","rule":"Translate each important confirmed defect into a regression check representing its causal contract.","examples":["A parser accepting malformed input is converted into a test asserting rejection of the invalid grammar class."],"invalid_examples":["Adding only the original incident payload without expressing the expected invariant."],"problem":"Fixed defects can recur through different inputs.","failure":"Regression test passes while equivalent defects remain possible.","cause":"Test captures an example instead of the violated contract.","solution":"Generalize the test around the causal invariant.","alternatives":["Property-based testing","Metamorphic testing","Contract tests"],"tradeoffs":"Generalized tests require more design effort.","constraints":["The invariant must be sufficiently well-defined"],"edge_cases":["Emergent distributed invariants","Probabilistic algorithms"],"security":"Security regression tests should cover the security property without embedding sensitive production secrets.","performance":"Keep regression tests focused enough for routine execution.","testing":"Run both example-based and boundary-focused regression tests.","verification":"Verify that the regression test fails on the pre-fix behavior when a reproducible pre-fix artifact exists.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Confirmed root cause"],"relationships":["D25-B44","D25-B48"],"provenance":["General regression-testing methodology"],"evidence":"A regression test is stronger when it encodes the behavioral contract violated by the defect.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B91-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Repair Validation","subtopic":"Counterfactual Verification","concept":"Pre-fix versus post-fix comparison","knowledge_type":"method","question":"What is counterfactual validation in debugging?","answer":"Counterfactual validation compares system behavior under the defective and repaired conditions while holding relevant inputs and environment factors as constant as practical.","explanation":"The comparison asks whether the change specifically altered the causal behavior responsible for the failure rather than merely coinciding with recovery.","principle":"A repair should be evaluated against a controlled representation of the defective behavior.","rule":"Preserve a reproducible failing artifact whenever feasible before changing the system.","examples":["Run the same deterministic reproducer against the pre-fix and post-fix versions."],"invalid_examples":["Comparing unrelated workloads before and after a deployment and attributing the difference to the patch."],"problem":"Environmental changes can mask repair effectiveness.","failure":"False confidence in a patch.","cause":"Uncontrolled comparison variables.","solution":"Use matched inputs, versions, configuration, and relevant environment state.","alternatives":["Replay","Golden test cases","Differential execution"],"tradeoffs":"Strict controls may be difficult for distributed or external systems.","constraints":["Some production failures cannot be safely reproduced"],"edge_cases":["External dependencies","Time-sensitive behavior","Nondeterministic concurrency"],"security":"Reproduction environments must isolate sensitive production data.","performance":"Capture only variables necessary for meaningful comparison.","testing":"Run repeated comparisons when nondeterminism exists.","verification":"Require consistent causal improvement rather than one favorable run.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Reproducible or partially reproducible failure"],"relationships":["D25-B62","D25-B64","D25-B81-R01"],"provenance":["General differential debugging and counterfactual reasoning"],"evidence":"Controlled comparisons provide stronger repair evidence than uncontrolled before/after observations.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B92-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging Automation","subtopic":"Agent Safety","concept":"Evidence-gated autonomous debugging","knowledge_type":"architecture","question":"What should an autonomous debugging agent require before taking a corrective action?","answer":"It should require sufficient evidence, a bounded action scope, explicit authorization where impact is material, and a verification plan before modifying a system.","explanation":"Debugging agents can amplify incorrect hypotheses if they modify systems before establishing causality and safety constraints.","principle":"Autonomous diagnosis and autonomous modification must have separate authorization boundaries.","rule":"Never treat a plausible diagnosis as implicit permission to execute a high-impact remediation.","examples":["An agent gathers logs and reproduces a failure automatically but requests approval before production configuration changes."],"invalid_examples":["Automatically deleting production state because an agent predicts that corruption caused an error."],"problem":"Automated agents can act faster than their evidence quality warrants.","failure":"Incorrect automated remediation causes additional damage.","cause":"Diagnosis and execution are coupled without governance.","solution":"Separate observe, diagnose, propose, approve, execute, and verify stages.","alternatives":["Human-in-the-loop","Policy-gated automation","Dry-run remediation"],"tradeoffs":"Approval gates increase latency but reduce uncontrolled impact.","constraints":["Action authorization must be explicit","Evidence must be traceable"],"edge_cases":["Emergency mitigation","Self-healing systems"],"security":"High-impact security actions require strict authorization and audit trails.","performance":"Automated read-only diagnosis can proceed faster than mutation workflows.","testing":"Test the agent against ambiguous and misleading evidence scenarios.","verification":"Verify both action execution and resulting system state.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Evidence model","Action authorization model"],"relationships":["D25-B42","D25-B50"],"provenance":["General autonomous-agent governance principles"],"evidence":"Separating diagnosis from authorization limits damage caused by incorrect automated conclusions.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B92-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging Automation","subtopic":"Tool Selection","concept":"Evidence-directed diagnostic tooling","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should an automated debugging system choose diagnostic tools?","answer":"Select tools according to the current uncertainty and required evidence rather than running every available diagnostic indiscriminately.","explanation":"Different tools expose different layers of system behavior. Tool choice should be driven by the hypothesis being tested.","principle":"Use the least invasive tool capable of answering the current diagnostic question.","rule":"Map each diagnostic action to an explicit information objective.","examples":["Use a stack trace to identify call context before enabling expensive memory instrumentation."],"invalid_examples":["Running every profiler, tracer, sanitizer, and debugger simultaneously without a diagnostic objective."],"problem":"Unstructured tooling creates noise and overhead.","failure":"Evidence volume increases without improving diagnosis.","cause":"Tool selection is not hypothesis-driven.","solution":"Associate each tool invocation with an expected observation and decision criterion.","alternatives":["Diagnostic playbooks","Adaptive investigation trees"],"tradeoffs":"Targeted tooling reduces overhead but may miss unexpected evidence.","constraints":["Instrumentation capabilities differ by runtime"],"edge_cases":["Failures with unknown subsystem ownership"],"security":"Diagnostic tooling must respect access controls and data boundaries.","performance":"Escalate instrumentation only when lower-cost evidence is insufficient.","testing":"Evaluate tool-selection policies on known fault classes.","verification":"Record expected and observed diagnostic outcomes.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Hypothesis model","Tool capability registry"],"relationships":["D25-B38","D25-B49"],"provenance":["Evidence-directed debugging methodology"],"evidence":"Diagnostic tools are most useful when their observations answer explicit causal questions.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B92-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging Automation","subtopic":"Action Planning","concept":"Dry-run remediation","knowledge_type":"method","question":"Why should automated remediation support dry-run behavior?","answer":"A dry-run allows the system to calculate and explain the intended changes without applying them, enabling validation of scope and policy compliance before mutation.","explanation":"Dry-run mode separates planning correctness from execution correctness.","principle":"Planning and mutation should be independently observable whenever feasible.","rule":"Provide a non-mutating preview for consequential corrective actions.","examples":["An agent proposes configuration changes and displays the exact intended diff before approval."],"invalid_examples":["Executing a configuration rewrite while describing it only after completion."],"problem":"Mutation can hide unintended side effects.","failure":"Unexpected state changes occur before review.","cause":"No pre-execution representation of intended action.","solution":"Generate a structured action plan and predicted state delta.","alternatives":["Sandbox execution","Transaction rollback"],"tradeoffs":"Dry-run may not expose effects that only occur during real execution.","constraints":["Some actions cannot be accurately simulated"],"edge_cases":["External side effects","Non-transactional APIs"],"security":"Dry-run output must not disclose protected configuration values.","performance":"Preview generation should be cheaper than full execution when possible.","testing":"Test that dry-run and actual execution produce equivalent intended deltas.","verification":"Compare predicted and observed state changes after execution.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Action model","State model"],"relationships":["D25-B42","D25-B66"],"provenance":["General safe automation methodology"],"evidence":"Explicit action plans make mutation scope reviewable before execution.","confidence":0.95,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B92-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging Automation","subtopic":"Rollback Safety","concept":"Repair rollback criteria","knowledge_type":"governance","question":"When should an automated repair be rolled back?","answer":"Rollback should occur when predefined safety or correctness conditions are violated, when verification fails, or when observed post-change behavior materially diverges from the expected state transition.","explanation":"Rollback criteria must be established before execution so that failure does not depend on an improvised decision after damage occurs.","principle":"Every consequential automated repair should have explicit failure boundaries.","rule":"Define rollback triggers and safe recovery state before executing the repair.","examples":["A configuration patch is reverted when health checks fail and the system cannot reach the expected invariant."],"invalid_examples":["Continuing an automated remediation despite failed verification because the agent believes the change is probably correct."],"problem":"Repairs can create secondary failures.","failure":"A failed repair remains active.","cause":"No predefined rollback condition.","solution":"Attach rollback conditions to the action plan.","alternatives":["Blue-green deployment","Transactional update","Checkpoint restoration"],"tradeoffs":"Rollback itself may have side effects.","constraints":["A recoverable state must exist"],"edge_cases":["Irreversible external actions","Data migrations"],"security":"Rollback controls must themselves require appropriate authorization.","performance":"Health verification should complete within an operationally acceptable window.","testing":"Exercise rollback paths independently from successful repair paths.","verification":"Verify both restored state and absence of the original failure.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["State snapshots","Health checks"],"relationships":["D25-B63","D25-B66","D25-B92-R03"],"provenance":["General resilient remediation methodology"],"evidence":"Predefined rollback criteria reduce uncontrolled continuation after failed remediation.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B92-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging Automation","subtopic":"Human Oversight","concept":"Escalation thresholds","knowledge_type":"governance","question":"How should an autonomous debugger decide when to escalate to a human?","answer":"Escalate when confidence is insufficient, hypotheses conflict, action impact exceeds policy limits, evidence is incomplete, or verification cannot establish a safe outcome.","explanation":"Escalation should be policy-driven rather than dependent on the agent's subjective sense of confidence alone.","principle":"Uncertainty and impact should jointly determine autonomy boundaries.","rule":"Low confidence combined with high impact must always produce escalation.","examples":["An agent identifies two plausible causes for a production data inconsistency and requests human review before modifying records."],"invalid_examples":["Executing a high-impact change because the top-ranked hypothesis has only weak supporting evidence."],"problem":"Agents may overestimate diagnostic certainty.","failure":"Unsafe autonomous action.","cause":"No explicit escalation policy.","solution":"Define confidence, impact, evidence, and reversibility thresholds.","alternatives":["Fully manual investigation","Tiered autonomy"],"tradeoffs":"More escalation reduces automation but increases control.","constraints":["Thresholds must be defined per action class"],"edge_cases":["Emergency incidents","Novel failure modes"],"security":"Security-sensitive actions should use stricter escalation thresholds.","performance":"Low-risk read-only diagnosis can use lower escalation requirements.","testing":"Simulate ambiguous, high-impact, and novel cases.","verification":"Audit whether escalation decisions matched policy.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Risk model","Authorization policy"],"relationships":["D25-B41","D25-B42","D25-B81-R01"],"provenance":["General AI-assisted debugging governance"],"evidence":"Explicit escalation rules prevent confidence estimates from becoming unrestricted execution authority.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B93-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Observability Quality","subtopic":"Telemetry Integrity","concept":"Debugging telemetry validation","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"Why must telemetry itself be treated as potentially faulty during debugging?","answer":"Telemetry can be missing, delayed, sampled, misclassified, duplicated, reordered, or generated with incorrect schema semantics, so diagnostic conclusions must account for telemetry integrity.","explanation":"A debugger that assumes every observation is correct can create false causal narratives from defective instrumentation.","principle":"Observability data is evidence, not ground truth by default.","rule":"Validate critical telemetry semantics before using them as decisive evidence.","examples":["A trace appears to show a long database call, but clock or span instrumentation errors explain the duration."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming absence of a log event proves that the corresponding operation never occurred."],"problem":"Instrumentation failures can resemble application failures.","failure":"Incorrect root cause based on defective telemetry.","cause":"Telemetry is trusted without validation.","solution":"Check schema, timestamps, sampling, propagation, completeness, and collector health.","alternatives":["Direct runtime inspection","Independent instrumentation"],"tradeoffs":"Telemetry validation adds diagnostic overhead.","constraints":["Some historical telemetry cannot be reconstructed"],"edge_cases":["High-cardinality sampling","Clock skew","Dropped spans"],"security":"Telemetry validation must preserve data access controls.","performance":"Use targeted validation for critical signals instead of validating every event equally.","testing":"Inject known telemetry conditions and verify interpretation.","verification":"Cross-check important claims against independent evidence.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Observability model"],"relationships":["D25-B67","D25-B68"],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry semantic conventions and specification"],"evidence":"Telemetry semantics and propagation determine whether traces and events can support reliable diagnosis.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B93-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Observability Quality","subtopic":"Sampling","concept":"Sampling-aware absence reasoning","knowledge_type":"edge_case","question":"Can absence of a sampled trace or log event prove that an operation did not occur?","answer":"No. If telemetry is sampled or selectively collected, absence of an observation cannot generally establish absence of the underlying operation.","explanation":"Sampling changes the relationship between system events and recorded evidence.","principle":"Observed telemetry and system reality are distinct datasets.","rule":"Never infer non-occurrence solely from missing sampled telemetry.","examples":["A missing distributed trace span does not prove the service was never invoked when sampling is enabled."],"invalid_examples":["Concluding that no request reached a service because no corresponding sampled trace exists."],"problem":"Partial telemetry creates false negatives.","failure":"Incorrect causal exclusion.","cause":"Sampling is ignored during reasoning.","solution":"Include collection and sampling state in the evidence model.","alternatives":["Unsampled audit events","Metrics","Direct service logs"],"tradeoffs":"Complete telemetry costs more storage and processing.","constraints":["Sampling policies vary across systems"],"edge_cases":["Tail-based sampling","Adaptive sampling","Collector outages"],"security":"Sensitive audit events may require stricter collection guarantees.","performance":"Sampling can reduce overhead but must be represented in diagnostic metadata.","testing":"Test diagnostic reasoning with intentionally missing observations.","verification":"Cross-check missing evidence against independent signals.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Telemetry collection model"],"relationships":["D25-B67","D25-B81-R02"],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry tracing and telemetry concepts"],"evidence":"Sampling means recorded observations are not necessarily exhaustive.","confidence":0.99,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B93-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Observability Quality","subtopic":"Log Integrity","concept":"Sensitive diagnostic logging","knowledge_type":"security","question":"What security controls are important when collecting debugging logs?","answer":"Sensitive values such as credentials, access tokens, cryptographic keys, and unnecessary personal information should be excluded, masked, sanitized, or otherwise protected from diagnostic output.","explanation":"Debugging increases data collection and can unintentionally expose secrets or sensitive application state.","principle":"Diagnostic usefulness does not override data-protection requirements.","rule":"Collect the minimum diagnostic information required to establish the causal explanation.","examples":["Logging a request identifier and validation result instead of an authorization token."],"invalid_examples":["Writing raw credentials or session tokens into debug logs for convenience."],"problem":"Debug logs can become a secondary data-exposure channel.","failure":"Sensitive information leaks through diagnostics.","cause":"Uncontrolled debug verbosity.","solution":"Apply structured logging, redaction, access controls, and retention policies.","alternatives":["Hashed identifiers","Synthetic reproduction data"],"tradeoffs":"Redaction can reduce diagnostic detail.","constraints":["Legal and organizational data requirements"],"edge_cases":["Incident response requiring sensitive evidence","Forensic acquisition"],"security":"Logs require confidentiality, integrity, and access controls.","performance":"Structured logging should avoid excessive serialization overhead.","testing":"Test redaction against representative secret formats.","verification":"Inspect emitted logs for prohibited sensitive fields.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Logging policy"],"relationships":["D25-B67","D25-B88"],"provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet"],"evidence":"OWASP recommends avoiding direct logging of credentials, tokens, keys, and sensitive data and using sanitization or protection where needed.","confidence":0.99,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B93-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Observability Quality","subtopic":"Correlation","concept":"Cross-signal correlation integrity","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should logs, metrics, traces, and profiles be correlated during debugging?","answer":"Use stable contextual identifiers and compatible timestamps or causal relationships while preserving each signal's semantic limitations.","explanation":"Correlation identifiers help connect evidence from different telemetry systems, but correlation does not automatically prove causality.","principle":"Correlation improves evidence assembly but must not be confused with causal proof.","rule":"Record the relationship between signals explicitly and distinguish observed correlation from inferred causation.","examples":["Use a request or trace identifier to connect an error log to a distributed trace and then verify the suspected causal dependency."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring a service the root cause solely because its trace appears near the failure timestamp."],"problem":"Multiple telemetry streams contain related but non-equivalent information.","failure":"False causal attribution.","cause":"Correlation interpreted as causation.","solution":"Combine cross-signal correlation with controlled diagnostic evidence.","alternatives":["Causal graphs","Differential experiments"],"tradeoffs":"More correlation metadata increases instrumentation complexity.","constraints":["Identifiers must propagate correctly"],"edge_cases":["Asynchronous queues","Batch processing"],"security":"Correlation identifiers should not contain sensitive information.","performance":"Prefer compact identifiers with bounded cardinality.","testing":"Verify propagation and uniqueness across service boundaries.","verification":"Validate correlation against known end-to-end flows.","version":"General","dependencies":["OpenTelemetry concepts"],"prerequisites":["Context propagation"],"relationships":["D25-B38","D25-B68"],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry Context and tracing specifications"],"evidence":"Context propagation enables cross-component correlation but does not itself establish causality.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B93-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Observability Quality","subtopic":"Instrumentation Drift","concept":"Debugging instrumentation regression","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"What happens when instrumentation changes independently of application behavior?","answer":"Diagnostic comparisons can become invalid because changes in logging, tracing, sampling, field names, or metric definitions may alter apparent behavior without changing the underlying application.","explanation":"Instrumentation is part of the diagnostic system and must be versioned and reviewed like production code.","principle":"Observability changes can change the evidence surface without changing the system under observation.","rule":"Include telemetry schema and instrumentation version in diagnostic comparisons.","examples":["A renamed metric creates an apparent drop because dashboards still query the old semantic field."],"invalid_examples":["Comparing pre-release and post-release traces without checking instrumentation changes."],"problem":"Telemetry semantics drift across versions.","failure":"False regression or false improvement.","cause":"Instrumentation changes are excluded from analysis.","solution":"Version schemas and instrumentation configuration.","alternatives":["Compatibility aliases","Schema migration"],"tradeoffs":"Maintaining compatibility increases complexity.","constraints":["Multiple collectors and consumers may exist"],"edge_cases":["Partial rollouts","Mixed-version deployments"],"security":"Telemetry changes must preserve redaction and access-control guarantees.","performance":"Instrumentation changes should be evaluated for runtime overhead.","testing":"Validate telemetry contracts during CI and deployment.","verification":"Compare schema versions before interpreting longitudinal data.","version":"General","dependencies":["Observability schema"],"prerequisites":["Telemetry versioning"],"relationships":["D25-B67","D25-B86"],"provenance":["OpenTelemetry semantic conventions"],"evidence":"Stable semantic conventions are necessary for consistent interpretation of telemetry across implementations and versions.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B94-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Build and Release Debugging","subtopic":"Artifact Integrity","concept":"Binary-source correspondence","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"Why must debuggers verify that symbols and binaries correspond to the failing artifact?","answer":"Incorrect symbol files can produce misleading stack traces, source locations, variables, and call frames, making correct diagnosis impossible or unreliable.","explanation":"Debugging information describes a particular build artifact and must be associated with the exact or compatible binary being investigated.","principle":"Debug symbols are evidence about a specific artifact, not generic source metadata.","rule":"Verify artifact identity before relying on symbol-based diagnostics.","examples":["Match a crash dump's executable build identifier with its symbol package before interpreting stack frames."],"invalid_examples":["Loading symbols from the latest build merely because the source branch name matches."],"problem":"Multiple builds can share source versions but differ in compiler, flags, or generated code.","failure":"Misleading diagnostic locations.","cause":"Artifact identity is not verified.","solution":"Track build identifiers and symbol provenance.","alternatives":["Embedded build IDs","Content hashes"],"tradeoffs":"Artifact metadata adds release-management complexity.","constraints":["Build pipeline must preserve identity metadata"],"edge_cases":["Reproducible builds","Stripped binaries"],"security":"Protect private symbols and source mappings from unauthorized access.","performance":"Symbol resolution can be cached without changing identity validation.","testing":"Test crash-analysis tooling against intentionally mismatched artifacts.","verification":"Confirm symbol and binary identity before diagnosis.","version":"General","dependencies":["Debug symbols"],"prerequisites":["Build provenance"],"relationships":["D25-B22","D25-B25"],"provenance":["GCC Debugging Options; Microsoft debugger symbol documentation"],"evidence":"Compiler and debugger documentation describe the dependency of useful debugging information on appropriate symbols and build artifacts.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B94-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Build and Release Debugging","subtopic":"Optimization","concept":"Optimization-aware debugging","knowledge_type":"edge_case","question":"Why can optimized code make debugger observations appear inconsistent with source code?","answer":"Optimization can remove variables, inline functions, reorder operations, merge code paths, and alter control flow, so debugger state may not map one-to-one with source statements.","explanation":"The optimized binary is the executable artifact; source-level expectations can become approximate when compiler transformations are applied.","principle":"Debug observations must be interpreted in the context of compiler optimization.","rule":"Record compiler optimization and debug-symbol settings when diagnosing native optimized builds.","examples":["A local variable appears unavailable because the optimizer eliminated or transformed its storage."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming every source-level variable must exist at every breakpoint in an optimized build."],"problem":"Source-to-machine-code correspondence becomes nontrivial under optimization.","failure":"Incorrect interpretation of runtime state.","cause":"Optimization effects ignored.","solution":"Use appropriate debug information and understand optimization transformations.","alternatives":["Debug-oriented build","Optimization-preserving diagnostics"],"tradeoffs":"Reduced optimization can improve debuggability but change timing or behavior.","constraints":["Some production failures occur only in optimized builds"],"edge_cases":["Compiler-dependent behavior","Inlining","Dead-code elimination"],"security":"Do not disable security-relevant compiler protections solely for convenience without controlled analysis.","performance":"Production-equivalent optimization may be necessary to reproduce performance-sensitive defects.","testing":"Maintain both debug-friendly and production-equivalent diagnostic configurations.","verification":"Confirm findings against the actual production artifact.","version":"General","dependencies":["Compiler","Debugger"],"prerequisites":["Build configuration knowledge"],"relationships":["D25-B25","D25-B94-R01"],"provenance":["GCC Debugging Options; Microsoft optimized-code debugging documentation"],"evidence":"Compiler and debugger documentation explicitly describe variable elimination, instruction movement, inlining, and optimized-code debugging limitations.","confidence":0.99,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B94-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Build and Release Debugging","subtopic":"Dependency Provenance","concept":"Dependency-induced regression diagnosis","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should a debugger investigate a regression that follows a dependency update?","answer":"Compare dependency graphs, lockfiles, transitive dependencies, runtime behavior, release notes, and vulnerability or compatibility information between the working and failing versions.","explanation":"A direct dependency change can alter many transitive components and runtime behaviors.","principle":"Dependency changes are causal candidates and must be analyzed as graph changes, not only line-level code changes.","rule":"Record direct and transitive dependency differences when local application code is unchanged but behavior changes.","examples":["A lockfile update changes a transitive parser library and introduces a previously absent exception path."],"invalid_examples":["Ignoring dependency changes because no application source files changed."],"problem":"Dependency graphs can change without obvious source changes.","failure":"Regression cause is incorrectly attributed to application code.","cause":"Dependency state omitted from comparison.","solution":"Perform dependency-aware differential analysis.","alternatives":["Dependency pinning","Reproducible environments"],"tradeoffs":"Strict pinning can delay security and compatibility updates.","constraints":["Package ecosystems differ in dependency resolution behavior"],"edge_cases":["Transitive dependency changes","Platform-specific packages"],"security":"Review dependency changes for known vulnerabilities as part of diagnosis.","performance":"Dependency graph analysis should avoid repeated full resolution when lockfiles provide authoritative state.","testing":"Run compatibility and regression suites after dependency changes.","verification":"Identify the exact dependency transition associated with the behavior change.","version":"General","dependencies":["Package manager","Lockfile"],"prerequisites":["Dependency graph"],"relationships":["D25-B27","D25-B86"],"provenance":["GitHub Dependency Review documentation"],"evidence":"Dependency review compares added, removed, and updated dependencies and their security impact.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B94-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Build and Release Debugging","subtopic":"Containerized Systems","concept":"Runtime artifact diagnosis","knowledge_type":"method","question":"What should be inspected when an application repeatedly crashes inside a containerized environment?","answer":"Inspect container state, exit reason, restart count, image identity, command and arguments, environment configuration, mounted resources, logs, and node-level conditions before changing application code.","explanation":"Container orchestration can introduce failure modes independent of application logic.","principle":"Diagnose the execution environment and application together.","rule":"Establish whether the process, container configuration, or orchestration layer is responsible before patching application code.","examples":["A process exits because its configured command is invalid, while the application binary itself is healthy."],"invalid_examples":["Changing application code before inspecting container exit state and startup configuration."],"problem":"Infrastructure failures can present as application crashes.","failure":"Incorrect application-level remediation.","cause":"Environment layer ignored.","solution":"Inspect orchestrator state and runtime metadata first.","alternatives":["Standalone reproduction","Ephemeral debug container"],"tradeoffs":"Environment reproduction may differ from production orchestration.","constraints":["Access to node-level diagnostics may be restricted"],"edge_cases":["CrashLoopBackOff","Init-container failures","Resource limits"],"security":"Debug containers and shells require controlled authorization.","performance":"Resource diagnostics should include CPU and memory limits.","testing":"Reproduce container startup with the same image and configuration.","verification":"Confirm the failing runtime artifact and configuration before code changes.","version":"General","dependencies":["Container runtime","Orchestrator"],"prerequisites":["Container metadata"],"relationships":["D25-B28","D25-B87"],"provenance":["Kubernetes debugging documentation"],"evidence":"Kubernetes documents inspection of Pod state, exit reasons, restart counts, logs, and debug containers for diagnosing crashing workloads.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B94-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Build and Release Debugging","subtopic":"Release Correlation","concept":"Change-event correlation","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should a debugging system use deployment and configuration changes during incident analysis?","answer":"Build a chronological change map linking deployments, configuration changes, feature flags, dependency updates, schema migrations, and observed failure onset, while treating temporal correlation as evidence rather than proof.","explanation":"Changes are valuable diagnostic candidates because they modify the causal environment, but incidents can also coincide with unrelated changes.","principle":"Release history is a hypothesis source, not an automatic root-cause label.","rule":"Compare behavior around relevant changes and test whether the changed component explains the failure mechanism.","examples":["A failure begins after a feature flag rollout, then controlled disabling confirms the affected execution path."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring the most recent deployment responsible without reproducing or correlating its changed behavior."],"problem":"Many changes may occur near an incident.","failure":"Latest-change bias.","cause":"Temporal proximity is treated as causality.","solution":"Combine change correlation with differential diagnosis.","alternatives":["Canary comparison","Version bisect"],"tradeoffs":"More controlled comparisons increase investigation time.","constraints":["Historical environments may be unavailable"],"edge_cases":["Multiple simultaneous deployments","Progressive rollouts"],"security":"Configuration history may contain sensitive values and must be access controlled.","performance":"Change-event correlation should use indexed metadata rather than scanning all historical events.","testing":"Automate regression tests around deployment changes.","verification":"Require behavioral evidence linking the change to the failure.","version":"General","dependencies":["Deployment metadata"],"prerequisites":["Version/change history"],"relationships":["D25-B43","D25-B86"],"provenance":["Google SRE incident and service reliability practices"],"evidence":"SRE guidance treats changes and incident timelines as important inputs to reliability investigation and postmortem analysis.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B95-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Data Debugging","subtopic":"Data Lineage","concept":"End-to-end data provenance debugging","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should a debugger locate the origin of an incorrect value flowing through multiple transformations?","answer":"Trace the value backward through its lineage, transformations, joins, filters, serialization boundaries, caches, and persistence points until the first transformation that violates the expected invariant is identified.","explanation":"The location where an incorrect value is observed is often downstream from the operation that corrupted it.","principle":"For data defects, localize the first invalid transformation rather than the final symptom.","rule":"Track data lineage and validate invariants at transformation boundaries.","examples":["A wrong aggregate is traced back to an earlier deduplication stage that removed valid records."],"invalid_examples":["Fixing the final report calculation when the upstream dataset is already incorrect."],"problem":"Data corruption propagates across pipelines.","failure":"Downstream symptom is mistaken for root cause.","cause":"No lineage-aware analysis.","solution":"Validate intermediate representations and transformation contracts.","alternatives":["Checkpointed datasets","Data quality assertions"],"tradeoffs":"Intermediate validation increases storage and processing costs.","constraints":["Lineage may be incomplete in legacy pipelines"],"edge_cases":["Late-arriving data","Schema evolution","Backfills"],"security":"Lineage metadata must respect data-access boundaries.","performance":"Use sampled or targeted validation for very large datasets.","testing":"Test transformation invariants independently.","verification":"Reconcile source, intermediate, and final records.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Data lineage","Transformation contracts"],"relationships":["D25-B83","D25-B35"],"provenance":["General data-pipeline debugging methodology"],"evidence":"Tracing data through transformation boundaries localizes corruption closer to its causal origin.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B95-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Data Debugging","subtopic":"Schema Evolution","concept":"Schema mismatch diagnosis","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why can schema evolution cause debugging failures even when application logic appears unchanged?","answer":"Producer and consumer versions may interpret fields, defaults, types, nullability, ordering, or encoding differently, creating failures at serialization or semantic interpretation boundaries.","explanation":"Schema compatibility is a behavioral contract between components.","principle":"Data contracts are part of executable system behavior.","rule":"Compare producer and consumer schema versions whenever a data-boundary failure appears.","examples":["A consumer expects a field to be non-null while a newer producer permits null values."],"invalid_examples":["Treating a deserialization error as an isolated parser bug without checking schema compatibility."],"problem":"Distributed components evolve independently.","failure":"Compatibility regression.","cause":"Schema contract drift.","solution":"Inspect schema versions and compatibility guarantees.","alternatives":["Backward-compatible schema evolution","Contract testing"],"tradeoffs":"Strict compatibility constraints can slow evolution.","constraints":["Different systems may use different schema registries"],"edge_cases":["Rolling deployments","Mixed-version consumers"],"security":"Schema changes must not accidentally weaken validation or authorization semantics.","performance":"Compatibility checks should be efficient enough for deployment pipelines.","testing":"Run producer-consumer compatibility tests across supported versions.","verification":"Validate actual serialized payloads against both schemas.","version":"General","dependencies":["Serialization format"],"prerequisites":["Schema contract"],"relationships":["D25-B30","D25-B83"],"provenance":["General distributed data-contract debugging methodology"],"evidence":"Schema boundaries define how independently deployed components interpret shared data.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B95-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Data Debugging","subtopic":"Duplicate Processing","concept":"Exactly-once illusion diagnosis","knowledge_type":"edge_case","question":"Why should debugging avoid assuming that distributed processing is exactly once merely because a system advertises idempotent behavior?","answer":"Delivery, execution, persistence, retries, and acknowledgment are separate stages; duplicates can still occur unless the relevant state transition is designed to tolerate or eliminate repeated effects.","explanation":"A system may provide at-least-once delivery while application-level idempotency makes repeated processing safe.","principle":"Separate delivery semantics from effect semantics.","rule":"Inspect message lifecycle and side-effect handling when duplicate outcomes occur.","examples":["A message is delivered twice but a unique operation key prevents duplicate financial state changes."],"invalid_examples":["Assuming duplicate delivery cannot occur because the message broker is described as reliable."],"problem":"Repeated processing can create duplicate effects.","failure":"Duplicate state mutation.","cause":"Delivery semantics misunderstood.","solution":"Trace message identity, attempts, acknowledgments, and side-effect keys.","alternatives":["Deduplication store","Transactional outbox","Idempotency keys"],"tradeoffs":"Strong deduplication may require persistent coordination.","constraints":["External side effects may not be transactional"],"edge_cases":["Consumer crashes after side effect but before acknowledgment"],"security":"Duplicate security-sensitive actions must be prevented or made idempotent.","performance":"Deduplication state can add storage and lookup overhead.","testing":"Inject duplicate deliveries at multiple lifecycle points.","verification":"Confirm that repeated processing converges to the same intended state.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Message lifecycle knowledge"],"relationships":["D25-B36","D25-B37","D25-B82"],"provenance":["General distributed-systems reliability principles"],"evidence":"At-least-once processing requires application-level handling of repeated delivery where duplicate effects are unsafe.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B95-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Data Debugging","subtopic":"Numeric Data Integrity","concept":"Invariant-based numerical diagnosis","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should numerical data corruption be localized?","answer":"Define domain invariants such as bounds, conservation relationships, monotonicity, dimensional consistency, or expected error tolerance and check them at intermediate stages.","explanation":"Numerical defects can propagate silently because outputs remain syntactically valid.","principle":"Numerical correctness requires semantic invariants in addition to type validity.","rule":"Validate numerical invariants near transformation boundaries.","examples":["A financial total violates conservation between source transactions and aggregated balances."],"invalid_examples":["Accepting a numeric output because it has the correct type and no exception occurred."],"problem":"Floating-point and transformation errors can remain latent.","failure":"Silent numerical drift.","cause":"No semantic numerical checks.","solution":"Add tolerance-aware invariants and intermediate reconciliation.","alternatives":["High-precision reference calculation","Interval arithmetic"],"tradeoffs":"Stricter numerical checks can increase computational cost.","constraints":["Tolerance depends on domain semantics"],"edge_cases":["Overflow","Underflow","Cancellation","NaN propagation"],"security":"Numerical validation is important where values influence authorization, billing, or safety decisions.","performance":"Use appropriate precision and sampling without weakening critical correctness checks.","testing":"Use boundary, adversarial, and property-based numerical tests.","verification":"Compare against independently computed references where feasible.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Numerical domain specification"],"relationships":["D25-B31","D25-B44"],"provenance":["General numerical debugging methodology"],"evidence":"Semantic invariants can detect valid-looking numerical outputs that violate domain relationships.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B95-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Data Debugging","subtopic":"Temporal Data","concept":"Time-domain normalization","knowledge_type":"method","question":"What should be checked when debugging failures involving timestamps?","answer":"Check timezone assumptions, clock source, precision, serialization format, daylight-saving transitions, ordering semantics, and whether timestamps represent event time or processing time.","explanation":"Two components can use syntactically valid timestamps while assigning different meanings to them.","principle":"A timestamp is useful only when its semantic reference frame is known.","rule":"Record timestamp semantics explicitly at system boundaries.","examples":["A stream processor orders events by processing time while the business rule requires event time."],"invalid_examples":["Comparing timestamp strings without checking timezone and precision semantics."],"problem":"Temporal values can be valid but semantically incompatible.","failure":"Incorrect ordering or expiry behavior.","cause":"Timestamp semantics differ between components.","solution":"Normalize representation and explicitly document time semantics.","alternatives":["Monotonic clocks","Event-time windows"],"tradeoffs":"Additional metadata increases complexity.","constraints":["Distributed clocks may not be synchronized"],"edge_cases":["Leap seconds","Clock jumps","DST transitions"],"security":"Time-based authorization must account for trusted clock sources.","performance":"Avoid repeated conversions in hot paths where canonical representations are available.","testing":"Test boundary dates, timezone changes, and clock skew.","verification":"Compare event-time and processing-time behavior explicitly.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Time semantics"],"relationships":["D25-B32","D25-B61"],"provenance":["General temporal-debugging methodology"],"evidence":"Temporal interpretation is a semantic contract rather than merely a string-format concern.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B96-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Concurrency Debugging","subtopic":"Schedule Dependence","concept":"Schedule-sensitive fault classification","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should a debugger classify a failure that appears only under certain thread or task schedules?","answer":"Treat scheduling as a causal variable and compare executions across controlled scheduling conditions while examining shared-state accesses, synchronization, and ordering assumptions.","explanation":"A failure dependent on execution order may not reproduce under ordinary sequential debugging.","principle":"For concurrency faults, execution schedule is part of the input space.","rule":"Preserve scheduling information or manipulate scheduling to expose the violated synchronization assumption.","examples":["A race appears only when two tasks overlap at a specific shared-state transition."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring the bug nonexistent because single-step debugging does not reproduce it."],"problem":"Debugger intervention can change timing.","failure":"Heisenbug remains hidden during diagnosis.","cause":"Schedule sensitivity.","solution":"Use tracing, deterministic scheduling, race detectors, replay, or controlled stress.","alternatives":["Thread sanitizers","Deterministic replay","Fault injection"],"tradeoffs":"Instrumentation can alter scheduling and performance.","constraints":["Exact schedules may be difficult to reproduce"],"edge_cases":["Lock-free algorithms","Priority inversion"],"security":"Concurrency defects in authorization or isolation code require high-priority verification.","performance":"Stress testing should distinguish performance-induced timing from logical races.","testing":"Run repeated and schedule-varied tests.","verification":"Demonstrate the violated happens-before or synchronization condition.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Concurrency model"],"relationships":["D25-B61","D25-B62","D25-B87"],"provenance":["General concurrency debugging methodology; GCC sanitizer documentation"],"evidence":"Thread instrumentation and replay techniques can expose concurrency-dependent failures, but instrumentation itself may affect scheduling.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B96-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Concurrency Debugging","subtopic":"Deadlock Diagnosis","concept":"Wait-for graph closure","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How can a debugger reason about a deadlock involving multiple locks or resources?","answer":"Construct a wait-for graph representing which execution entities hold resources and which resources they await, then identify cycles and validate whether the cycle is reachable under the observed state.","explanation":"A cycle in a wait-for relationship can identify a deadlock candidate, but the graph must reflect actual ownership and waiting semantics.","principle":"Deadlock diagnosis is a state-and-dependency problem.","rule":"Record lock ownership and waiting relationships at the same diagnostic snapshot when possible.","examples":["Thread A holds lock X and waits for Y while Thread B holds Y and waits for X."],"invalid_examples":["Finding two locks in stack traces and declaring deadlock without establishing ownership and waiting relationships."],"problem":"Deadlock information is distributed across execution contexts.","failure":"False deadlock diagnosis.","cause":"Incomplete dependency state.","solution":"Build a wait-for graph from synchronized state.","alternatives":["Lock-order analysis","Runtime deadlock detectors"],"tradeoffs":"Capturing ownership metadata can add runtime overhead.","constraints":["Some lock implementations expose limited introspection"],"edge_cases":["Nested locks","Condition variables","Distributed locks"],"security":"Deadlock can become a denial-of-service vector in critical systems.","performance":"Prefer low-overhead lock telemetry in production.","testing":"Construct known lock cycles and verify detection.","verification":"Confirm all participants and resource ownership involved in the cycle.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Synchronization model"],"relationships":["D25-B13","D25-B61"],"provenance":["General concurrency debugging methodology"],"evidence":"Deadlock requires mutually dependent waits and can be represented through wait-for relationships.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B96-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Concurrency Debugging","subtopic":"Atomicity","concept":"Partial-state observation","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"Why can observing an intermediate shared state create misleading debugging conclusions?","answer":"Another execution context may observe state between multiple logically related updates, causing the debugger to attribute a transient state to corruption even though the state is temporarily valid within a transaction or critical section.","explanation":"Logical atomicity may be broader than individual memory writes.","principle":"Debuggers must distinguish transient states from externally valid states.","rule":"Define the atomicity boundary of each invariant before diagnosing intermediate-state observations.","examples":["A reader observes one field updated before a second field that completes the invariant."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring corruption whenever a multi-field invariant is temporarily incomplete inside a protected transaction."],"problem":"State changes can occur in multiple machine-level steps.","failure":"False-positive corruption diagnosis.","cause":"Atomicity boundary is misunderstood.","solution":"Model transactional or synchronization boundaries explicitly.","alternatives":["Immutable state replacement","Versioned snapshots"],"tradeoffs":"Stronger atomicity can increase synchronization cost.","constraints":["Not all operations can be made atomic"],"edge_cases":["Lock-free structures","Distributed transactions"],"security":"Partial authorization state must never become externally observable if security invariants require atomicity.","performance":"Avoid unnecessary global locks when finer-grained atomicity is sufficient.","testing":"Probe state transitions at concurrency boundaries.","verification":"Check invariants only at defined externally valid states.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Concurrency and state model"],"relationships":["D25-B35","D25-B61"],"provenance":["General concurrent-state debugging methodology"],"evidence":"Correct reasoning about concurrent state requires explicit atomicity and visibility boundaries.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B96-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Concurrency Debugging","subtopic":"Queue and Scheduler Analysis","concept":"Queue starvation diagnosis","knowledge_type":"failure_mode","question":"How can scheduler or queue behavior cause an apparent application logic failure?","answer":"A task may be logically correct but fail to make progress because it is starved by priority, queue saturation, worker exhaustion, unfair scheduling, or blocked dependencies.","explanation":"No-progress conditions can appear as timeouts or missing events even when the task itself has no functional defect.","principle":"Progress is a property that depends on both task logic and scheduling resources.","rule":"When diagnosing timeouts, inspect queue depth, worker availability, scheduling priority, and dependency waits.","examples":["A correctly enqueued job never runs because all workers are occupied by long-running tasks."],"invalid_examples":["Changing job logic when the actual issue is worker starvation."],"problem":"Liveness failures can resemble functional failures.","failure":"Incorrect code-level remediation.","cause":"Scheduler state ignored.","solution":"Correlate task lifecycle with resource and queue state.","alternatives":["Backpressure","Fair scheduling","Separate worker pools"],"tradeoffs":"Isolation improves liveness but increases resource consumption.","constraints":["Worker capacity is finite"],"edge_cases":["Priority inversion","Poison messages","Unbounded queues"],"security":"Queue starvation can be exploited for denial of service.","performance":"Monitor queue latency and service time separately.","testing":"Stress queues under competing workloads.","verification":"Demonstrate that progress resumes when the limiting resource or scheduling condition is corrected.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Scheduler model"],"relationships":["D25-B34","D25-B87"],"provenance":["General scheduler and resource debugging methodology"],"evidence":"Resource saturation and scheduling constraints can prevent otherwise valid tasks from making progress.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B96-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Concurrency Debugging","subtopic":"Race Verification","concept":"Race-fix verification","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"How should a race-condition fix be verified?","answer":"Verify the synchronization invariant under repeated and varied schedules and demonstrate that the shared state remains correct under concurrent access patterns relevant to the defect.","explanation":"A race may disappear in one run while remaining possible under another schedule.","principle":"Concurrency repair requires schedule-diverse verification.","rule":"Do not treat one successful concurrent run as proof that a race is eliminated.","examples":["Run stress and race-detection tests across varied interleavings after introducing synchronization."],"invalid_examples":["Running the repaired program once without a race and declaring the defect permanently fixed."],"problem":"Concurrency defects are probabilistic from an observation perspective.","failure":"False repair confidence.","cause":"Insufficient schedule coverage.","solution":"Combine targeted tests, stress, instrumentation, and invariant checks.","alternatives":["Formal concurrency reasoning","Deterministic scheduler"],"tradeoffs":"Extensive schedule exploration can be expensive.","constraints":["Some interleavings remain untested"],"edge_cases":["Rare hardware-dependent races"],"security":"Race fixes affecting authorization or isolation require stronger evidence.","performance":"Measure synchronization overhead after repair.","testing":"Use repeated schedule variation and race detection.","verification":"Verify the underlying ordering or synchronization invariant, not only absence of symptoms.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Race hypothesis","Concurrency invariant"],"relationships":["D25-B24","D25-B61","D25-B96-R01"],"provenance":["GCC instrumentation documentation; general concurrency verification methodology"],"evidence":"Thread instrumentation can detect data races, but successful repair requires reasoning about the violated synchronization condition.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B97-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security Debugging","subtopic":"Authentication","concept":"Authentication failure localization","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should an authentication failure be localized without weakening security controls?","answer":"Trace the authentication state machine, credential or token validation result, identity resolution, clock assumptions, policy evaluation, and downstream session establishment while preserving security boundaries.","explanation":"Authentication failures can originate in credentials, identity providers, token validation, clocks, policy configuration, or session state.","principle":"Security debugging must preserve the security mechanism while exposing sufficient diagnostic evidence.","rule":"Debug authentication through structured security events rather than logging raw credentials or secrets.","examples":["Record token validation failure category and issuer metadata without logging the token itself."],"invalid_examples":["Printing passwords or bearer tokens to identify why authentication failed."],"problem":"Security diagnostics can accidentally create new vulnerabilities.","failure":"Sensitive credential exposure.","cause":"Unsafe debugging instrumentation.","solution":"Use classified security events and redaction.","alternatives":["Synthetic credentials","Controlled test identities"],"tradeoffs":"Redaction can reduce forensic detail.","constraints":["Security and privacy requirements"],"edge_cases":["Clock skew","Key rotation","Federated identity"],"security":"Never expose authentication secrets in diagnostic output.","performance":"Security event logging should remain bounded under attack traffic.","testing":"Exercise invalid, expired, revoked, and malformed credentials using synthetic data.","verification":"Confirm both failure correctness and absence of sensitive leakage.","version":"General","dependencies":["Authentication system"],"prerequisites":["Authentication state machine"],"relationships":["D25-B88","D25-B93-R03"],"provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet; general authentication debugging methodology"],"evidence":"Security logging guidance emphasizes recording useful security events while excluding authentication secrets.","confidence":0.99,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B97-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security Debugging","subtopic":"Authorization","concept":"Authorization decision tracing","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should an authorization failure be debugged?","answer":"Trace subject identity, requested action, resource classification, policy inputs, policy evaluation result, inherited permissions, deny rules, and enforcement point without exposing unnecessary sensitive data.","explanation":"Authorization is a policy evaluation problem rather than simply a credential problem.","principle":"Debug authorization by reconstructing the policy decision context.","rule":"Capture the decision inputs and resulting policy classification without logging secrets.","examples":["A request is denied because a resource classification changed and the user's role no longer satisfies the policy."],"invalid_examples":["Granting broad temporary permissions just to determine why access was denied."],"problem":"Authorization decisions can depend on many dynamic attributes.","failure":"Debugging changes the security state.","cause":"Unsafe diagnostic workaround.","solution":"Use read-only policy evaluation and structured decision traces.","alternatives":["Policy simulators","Synthetic roles"],"tradeoffs":"Detailed policy traces can expose sensitive authorization structure.","constraints":["Policy data may itself be sensitive"],"edge_cases":["ABAC","RBAC inheritance","Time-based policies"],"security":"Debugging must never bypass the authorization mechanism under investigation.","performance":"Policy traces should be sampled or targeted in high-volume systems.","testing":"Test allow, deny, inheritance, precedence, and boundary cases.","verification":"Confirm the decision matches the intended policy semantics.","version":"General","dependencies":["Authorization policy engine"],"prerequisites":["Policy model"],"relationships":["D25-B88","D25-B97-R01"],"provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet; general authorization debugging methodology"],"evidence":"Security events such as privilege changes and suspicious business-logic activity are appropriate candidates for structured logging.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B97-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security Debugging","subtopic":"Vulnerability Root Cause","concept":"Root cause versus exploit symptom","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"Why should vulnerability debugging distinguish exploit symptoms from the underlying defect?","answer":"An observed crash, unauthorized result, or abnormal response may be only the consequence of a deeper validation, memory-safety, authorization, or state-management defect.","explanation":"Fixing only the observed exploit path can leave equivalent attack paths intact.","principle":"Security repair should target the violated security invariant.","rule":"Generalize security defects from the triggering input to the underlying violated property.","examples":["A malformed input crashes one parser path, but the root defect is insufficient bounds validation across the parser."],"invalid_examples":["Blocking only the exact malicious string while leaving the unsafe parsing condition unchanged."],"problem":"Attack inputs are often one manifestation of a broader weakness.","failure":"Variant exploit remains possible.","cause":"Symptom-specific patch.","solution":"Identify and test the violated security property.","alternatives":["Defense-in-depth","Input validation contracts"],"tradeoffs":"Broader remediation may require more testing.","constraints":["Exploit reproduction must remain controlled"],"edge_cases":["Multiple vulnerable code paths","Shared parsing libraries"],"security":"Use non-destructive validation and isolated reproduction environments.","performance":"Security checks should be measured for acceptable overhead.","testing":"Add regression cases covering the vulnerability class.","verification":"Confirm the vulnerable invariant cannot be violated through equivalent paths.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Security property","Controlled reproduction"],"relationships":["D25-B29","D25-B48","D25-B97-R01"],"provenance":["General secure debugging methodology; GCC sanitizer documentation"],"evidence":"Sanitizers and security diagnostics can identify classes of memory and undefined-behavior defects rather than only individual inputs.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B97-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security Debugging","subtopic":"Logging Integrity","concept":"Tamper-aware diagnostic evidence","knowledge_type":"security","question":"Why should security-relevant debugging evidence protect log integrity?","answer":"If attackers can modify or delete diagnostic records, investigators may lose the evidence needed to reconstruct the incident or may accept manipulated evidence as genuine.","explanation":"Security logs are themselves an attack surface and evidence source.","principle":"Diagnostic evidence requires integrity protection as well as confidentiality.","rule":"Protect security-relevant logs from unauthorized modification and deletion.","examples":["Centralized logs use controlled access and integrity monitoring so incident investigators can detect tampering."],"invalid_examples":["Allowing the same compromised application account to freely rewrite its own security logs."],"problem":"Compromised systems may also compromise local evidence.","failure":"Evidence becomes unreliable.","cause":"Insufficient log integrity controls.","solution":"Use protected collection, restricted modification, and integrity monitoring.","alternatives":["Append-only storage","Remote collection"],"tradeoffs":"Protected logging increases operational complexity and storage cost.","constraints":["Availability of secure logging infrastructure"],"edge_cases":["Offline systems","Collector compromise"],"security":"Protect confidentiality, integrity, and availability of security logs.","performance":"Batch or asynchronous secure logging may reduce application overhead.","testing":"Simulate log deletion or modification attempts and verify detection.","verification":"Check integrity metadata and collection continuity.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Security logging"],"relationships":["D25-B39","D25-B93-R03"],"provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet"],"evidence":"OWASP recommends protecting collected logs against tampering, unauthorized access, modification, and deletion.","confidence":0.99,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B97-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Security Debugging","subtopic":"Dependency Vulnerabilities","concept":"Security regression from dependency changes","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How should a dependency update be evaluated when debugging a security regression?","answer":"Compare dependency versions and transitive changes, identify vulnerability information and behavioral changes, reproduce the security-relevant behavior in a controlled environment, and validate the resulting remediation.","explanation":"Dependency updates can change both security exposure and runtime behavior.","principle":"Dependency state is part of the security attack surface.","rule":"Treat dependency changes as first-class evidence in security regression diagnosis.","examples":["A dependency update introduces a known vulnerable version or changes parsing behavior that exposes an existing application assumption."],"invalid_examples":["Ignoring a changed transitive dependency because only the direct package was intentionally updated."],"problem":"Security behavior can change through indirect dependencies.","failure":"Security regression escapes review.","cause":"Incomplete dependency graph analysis.","solution":"Inspect dependency diffs and security metadata before and after the change.","alternatives":["Pinned dependencies","Automated dependency review"],"tradeoffs":"Strict controls can slow dependency updates.","constraints":["Vulnerability databases may change over time"],"edge_cases":["Newly disclosed vulnerabilities","Platform-specific resolution"],"security":"Prioritize security-relevant dependency changes for explicit review.","performance":"Automated dependency analysis should be integrated into change workflows.","testing":"Run security regression suites after dependency updates.","verification":"Confirm the dependency state and vulnerability status of the deployed artifact.","version":"General","dependencies":["Dependency graph","Vulnerability data"],"prerequisites":["Dependency inventory"],"relationships":["D25-B27","D25-B94-R03"],"provenance":["GitHub Dependency Review documentation"],"evidence":"GitHub dependency review identifies added, removed, and updated dependencies and associated vulnerability information.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B98-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Causal Graphs","concept":"Causal chain normalization","knowledge_type":"representation","question":"How should a complex debugging incident be represented as a causal graph?","answer":"Represent observations, state transitions, contributing conditions, triggering events, intermediate mechanisms, and final effects as distinct nodes connected by explicitly typed relationships.","explanation":"A causal graph prevents a symptom, trigger, and root condition from being collapsed into one label.","principle":"Separate cause categories and causal relationships explicitly.","rule":"Represent symptom, trigger, enabling condition, mechanism, and outcome separately when evidence supports them.","examples":["Configuration drift enables a faulty code path, a request triggers it, a resource exhaustion mechanism occurs, and a timeout becomes the observed symptom."],"invalid_examples":["Labeling the timeout itself as the root cause."],"problem":"Incidents often contain multiple causal layers.","failure":"Root-cause oversimplification.","cause":"Flat incident labels.","solution":"Use typed causal relationships and evidence links.","alternatives":["Fault trees","Bayesian networks","Event graphs"],"tradeoffs":"Graph representations require more structured evidence.","constraints":["Causal relationships may remain uncertain"],"edge_cases":["Multiple independent causes","Feedback loops"],"security":"Causal graphs must respect data-access boundaries.","performance":"Store compact references to evidence rather than duplicating large artifacts.","testing":"Validate graph consistency and detect unsupported edges.","verification":"Require evidence for high-confidence causal relationships.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Evidence model","Causal reasoning"],"relationships":["D25-B40","D25-B81-R01"],"provenance":["General causal-debugging methodology"],"evidence":"Typed causal representations preserve distinctions between symptoms, mechanisms, and contributing conditions.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B98-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Contributing Factors","concept":"Multi-cause incident modeling","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"Can an incident have multiple root causes or contributing causes?","answer":"Yes. Complex failures may require several necessary or interacting conditions, so analysis should distinguish primary mechanism, contributing conditions, triggers, and independent faults.","explanation":"A single-cause narrative can hide systemic conditions that allowed the failure to occur or amplified its impact.","principle":"Root-cause analysis should represent causal multiplicity when supported by evidence.","rule":"Do not force a single-cause explanation when multiple mechanisms are independently evidenced.","examples":["A software defect causes excessive allocation while a deployment configuration removes the normal resource limit, allowing the defect to become an outage."],"invalid_examples":["Calling only the code defect the root cause while ignoring the configuration condition that made the incident severe."],"problem":"Incidents can emerge from interacting conditions.","failure":"Remediation addresses only one component of the causal system.","cause":"Single-cause bias.","solution":"Model independent and interacting contributors explicitly.","alternatives":["Fault-tree analysis","Causal graphs"],"tradeoffs":"Multi-cause analysis is more complex but preserves systemic lessons.","constraints":["Each causal claim requires evidence"],"edge_cases":["Necessary versus sufficient causes","Feedback loops"],"security":"Include security-control failures when they materially contributed to an incident.","performance":"Prioritize contributors by causal impact rather than narrative prominence.","testing":"Test remediation against each important causal contributor.","verification":"Confirm that removing each claimed contributor changes the relevant failure mechanism where feasible.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Causal graph"],"relationships":["D25-B39","D25-B40"],"provenance":["Google SRE incident postmortem practices; general causal analysis"],"evidence":"SRE postmortem examples separate technical causes, contributing circumstances, response factors, and lessons learned.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B98-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Counterfactual Reasoning","concept":"Necessary-condition testing","knowledge_type":"method","question":"How can counterfactual reasoning improve root-cause analysis?","answer":"Ask whether removing or changing a proposed causal condition would prevent the failure while keeping other relevant conditions sufficiently similar.","explanation":"A condition that changes nothing when removed may be correlated rather than causally necessary, although complex systems can contain redundant causes.","principle":"Causal claims should make testable counterfactual predictions.","rule":"For important causal claims, identify what outcome should change if the condition is removed.","examples":["Removing a feature flag eliminates the failing execution path while other conditions remain constant."],"invalid_examples":["Calling a metric a root cause because it rose during the incident without testing whether changing it affects the failure."],"problem":"Correlation can be mistaken for causation.","failure":"Wrong remediation target.","cause":"No counterfactual test.","solution":"Use controlled experiments, replay, or historical differential evidence.","alternatives":["Natural experiments","Differential execution"],"tradeoffs":"Counterfactual experiments may be difficult or unsafe in production.","constraints":["Some causes cannot be safely manipulated"],"edge_cases":["Redundant causes","Interacting causes"],"security":"Counterfactual tests must not intentionally weaken security controls in production.","performance":"Prefer low-cost experiments with high information gain.","testing":"Encode important causal counterfactuals into regression tests where feasible.","verification":"Compare predicted and observed counterfactual outcomes.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Causal hypothesis"],"relationships":["D25-B64","D25-B90","D25-B91-R05"],"provenance":["General causal inference and differential debugging methodology"],"evidence":"Controlled changes provide stronger causal evidence than passive correlation.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B98-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Residual Risk","concept":"Post-fix residual-risk analysis","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"Why should debugging continue after the observed failure disappears?","answer":"A patch can remove the immediate symptom while leaving adjacent failure modes, latent vulnerabilities, performance regressions, or incomplete recovery behavior.","explanation":"Verification should evaluate the causal mechanism and relevant neighboring conditions rather than only checking that the original symptom vanished.","principle":"A successful immediate outcome is necessary but not sufficient for complete closure.","rule":"Document remaining uncertainty and plausible residual failure modes after every significant repair.","examples":["A timeout disappears after increasing a limit, but the underlying leak remains and could reappear under larger load."],"invalid_examples":["Closing an incident solely because the alert stopped firing after a configuration increase."],"problem":"Symptom suppression can hide persistent defects.","failure":"Delayed recurrence.","cause":"Verification scope is too narrow.","solution":"Perform residual-risk analysis and targeted regression testing.","alternatives":["Extended monitoring","Load testing","Fault injection"],"tradeoffs":"Extended validation increases closure time.","constraints":["Some latent risks cannot be completely eliminated"],"edge_cases":["Rare failures","Capacity thresholds"],"security":"Residual security risks require explicit tracking rather than assuming the exploit is permanently closed.","performance":"Evaluate resource trends after symptom resolution.","testing":"Add tests for adjacent boundary conditions.","verification":"Track the original invariant and related system health after repair.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Repair verification"],"relationships":["D25-B48","D25-B66","D25-B90"],"provenance":["Google SRE error-budget and postmortem practices; general repair verification"],"evidence":"SRE practice links significant incidents to explicit corrective actions and follow-up rather than treating symptom recovery as sufficient.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B98-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Root-Cause Analysis","subtopic":"Causal Confidence","concept":"Evidence-backed causal confidence","knowledge_type":"representation","question":"How should a debugging system represent confidence in a root-cause conclusion?","answer":"Represent confidence separately from evidence, link each confidence judgment to supporting and contradicting observations, and make clear whether the conclusion is confirmed, strongly supported, plausible, or unresolved.","explanation":"A numeric score without evidence lineage can create false precision.","principle":"Confidence is meaningful only when traceable to evidence and uncertainty.","rule":"Never represent confidence as proof.","examples":["A diagnosis is marked strongly supported because three independent observations agree and two alternative causes were eliminated."],"invalid_examples":["Assigning 99 percent confidence solely because an AI model selected the hypothesis."],"problem":"Automated systems may express unjustified certainty.","failure":"False diagnostic authority.","cause":"Confidence is detached from evidence quality.","solution":"Use evidence-linked confidence states and explicit uncertainty.","alternatives":["Ordinal confidence categories","Bayesian posterior with assumptions"],"tradeoffs":"Rich evidence models require more storage and reasoning effort.","constraints":["Evidence quality varies"],"edge_cases":["Conflicting telemetry","Novel failures"],"security":"High-impact security actions require stronger evidence regardless of confidence score.","performance":"Confidence recomputation should focus on changed evidence.","testing":"Evaluate calibration on known historical cases where labels are reliable.","verification":"Review whether confidence changes appropriately when counterevidence appears.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Evidence graph"],"relationships":["D25-B41","D25-B81-R01","D25-B98-R03"],"provenance":["General evidence-based diagnostic reasoning"],"evidence":"Traceable supporting and contradicting evidence provides a more defensible basis for diagnostic confidence than unsupported scores.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B99-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Incident Closure","subtopic":"Postmortem Quality","concept":"Blameless technical closure","knowledge_type":"method","question":"What should a technically useful debugging postmortem contain?","answer":"It should preserve the timeline, impact, detection, causal mechanism, contributing conditions, evidence, response actions, verification, unresolved uncertainty, and concrete follow-up actions.","explanation":"A postmortem is most useful when it preserves engineering knowledge rather than merely documenting who responded.","principle":"Closure should create reusable system knowledge.","rule":"Record causal evidence and corrective actions in a structured form that can be retrieved later.","examples":["Timeline plus causal graph plus regression test reference plus owner and verification criteria."],"invalid_examples":["A narrative that says the issue was fixed without recording how the cause was established."],"problem":"Important diagnostic knowledge is often lost after recovery.","failure":"Future incidents repeat the same investigation.","cause":"Postmortem focuses on chronology without causal knowledge.","solution":"Capture structured evidence and durable prevention actions.","alternatives":["Incident knowledge records","Engineering runbooks"],"tradeoffs":"Structured postmortems require more effort.","constraints":["Evidence may be incomplete"],"edge_cases":["Multiple related incidents"],"security":"Postmortems must classify and protect sensitive incident information.","performance":"Index causal and remediation fields for future retrieval.","testing":"Verify that documented regression tests correspond to corrective actions.","verification":"Track action completion and effectiveness.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Incident evidence"],"relationships":["D25-B39","D25-B49"],"provenance":["Google SRE incident postmortem example and error-budget policy"],"evidence":"SRE postmortem practice records timelines, what went well, what went wrong, and corrective lessons.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B99-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Incident Closure","subtopic":"Corrective Actions","concept":"Actionability of remediation items","knowledge_type":"principle","question":"What makes a debugging follow-up action actionable?","answer":"An actionable item identifies the technical change, expected risk reduction, verification condition, ownership, scope, and completion evidence.","explanation":"Vague actions such as improve monitoring do not provide a verifiable closure condition.","principle":"Every corrective action should have an observable completion criterion.","rule":"Do not close a remediation item without evidence that its intended control or fix exists and works as specified.","examples":["Add a bounded retry policy, test duplicate processing, deploy it, and verify duplicate side effects no longer occur."],"invalid_examples":["Create better monitoring."],"problem":"Postmortem actions can become non-verifiable intentions.","failure":"System remains vulnerable to recurrence.","cause":"No acceptance criteria.","solution":"Define implementation and verification conditions.","alternatives":["Automated compliance checks","Regression suites"],"tradeoffs":"Specific acceptance criteria require more planning.","constraints":["Some improvements are difficult to quantify"],"edge_cases":["Organizational process changes"],"security":"Security actions require explicit verification of the security property.","performance":"Include performance impact in acceptance criteria when relevant.","testing":"Associate each action with a test or inspection method.","verification":"Require objective evidence before closure.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Root cause","Risk assessment"],"relationships":["D25-B48","D25-B90"],"provenance":["Google SRE error-budget and postmortem practices"],"evidence":"SRE practices use explicit action items to address root causes and reliability risks.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B99-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Incident Closure","subtopic":"Knowledge Reuse","concept":"Debugging knowledge extraction","knowledge_type":"architecture","question":"How should debugging incidents become reusable knowledge for future diagnosis?","answer":"Extract stable concepts such as symptom patterns, violated invariants, causal signatures, discriminating evidence, remediation patterns, regression tests, and known counterexamples into structured records.","explanation":"Raw incident narratives are difficult to retrieve and reason over consistently.","principle":"Reusable debugging knowledge should be represented at the concept and evidence level.","rule":"Separate incident-specific facts from generalizable diagnostic patterns.","examples":["Extracting a known failure signature and its discriminating diagnostic experiment from one incident without storing confidential incident data."],"invalid_examples":["Training future systems on raw incident logs containing secrets and organization-specific identifiers."],"problem":"Incident data contains both reusable knowledge and sensitive context.","failure":"Knowledge retrieval becomes unsafe or noisy.","cause":"No normalization boundary.","solution":"Anonymize, normalize, classify, and preserve provenance.","alternatives":["Knowledge graph","Case-based reasoning"],"tradeoffs":"Normalization can remove useful context.","constraints":["Sensitive information must remain protected"],"edge_cases":["Unique one-off failures","Novel architectures"],"security":"Remove or protect credentials, personal data, secrets, and sensitive infrastructure details.","performance":"Index stable diagnostic concepts for fast retrieval.","testing":"Evaluate retrieval against known historical fault classes.","verification":"Ensure extracted knowledge remains faithful to the source evidence.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Incident records","Evidence model"],"relationships":["D25-B49","D25-B99-R01"],"provenance":["General debugging knowledge-engineering methodology"],"evidence":"Structured extraction preserves reusable diagnostic relationships while separating them from incident-specific sensitive data.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
