runbook and postmortem methodology"],"evidence":"Operational learning is strongest when incident lessons become concrete, verifiable corrective practices.","confidence":0.96,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B99-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Incident Closure","subtopic":"Closure Criteria","concept":"Technical closure gate","knowledge_type":"governance","question":"What minimum evidence should exist before a significant debugging incident is technically closed?","answer":"The failure mechanism should be documented, the repair or mitigation should be identified, the original failure should be reproduced or otherwise evidenced, post-change verification should be recorded, residual risks should be documented, and follow-up actions should be tracked.","explanation":"Closure is stronger when it proves understanding and remediation rather than only recovery.","principle":"Incident recovery and incident closure are distinct states.","rule":"Do not equate symptom disappearance with verified root-cause closure.","examples":["Service recovers, root cause is confirmed, regression test is added, repair is verified, and remaining capacity risk is tracked."],"invalid_examples":["Closing immediately after the service becomes healthy without understanding why."],"problem":"Operational recovery can occur without causal understanding.","failure":"Repeated incidents and unresolved defects.","cause":"Closure based solely on recovery.","solution":"Use explicit technical closure gates.","alternatives":["Tiered closure states","Formal change review"],"tradeoffs":"Strict closure takes longer but improves organizational learning.","constraints":["Some incidents remain partially understood"],"edge_cases":["Third-party outages","Unreproducible historical failures"],"security":"Security incidents require additional evidence and remediation tracking.","performance":"Include post-fix performance validation where performance was affected.","testing":"Require regression protection for confirmed software defects.","verification":"Record verification evidence and unresolved uncertainty.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Root-cause analysis","Repair verification"],"relationships":["D25-B48","D25-B90","D25-B99-R01"],"provenance":["Google SRE error-budget policy and incident postmortem practices"],"evidence":"SRE practices connect significant incidents with postmortems and explicit corrective actions rather than recovery alone.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B100-R01","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging Synthesis","subtopic":"End-to-End Workflow","concept":"Evidence-first debugging lifecycle","knowledge_type":"architecture","question":"What end-to-end lifecycle should a robust autonomous software debugger follow?","answer":"Observe the failure, preserve evidence, reproduce when feasible, classify the failure, generate competing hypotheses, collect discriminating evidence, localize the violated invariant, propose a bounded repair, validate the repair, assess residual risk, and capture reusable knowledge.","explanation":"The lifecycle integrates local debugging, distributed diagnosis, security controls, automated assistance, and long-term learning.","principle":"Debugging is an evidence-to-verification lifecycle rather than a sequence of ad hoc commands.","rule":"Every major transition should preserve evidence and define its verification objective.","examples":["Incident observation leads to reproducible evidence, causal hypothesis, minimal patch, regression test, post-fix verification, and knowledge extraction."],"invalid_examples":["Jumping directly from an error message to an automated code rewrite."],"problem":"Disconnected debugging activities produce incomplete causal reasoning.","failure":"Plausible but unverified repairs.","cause":"No integrated lifecycle.","solution":"Use explicit diagnostic states and evidence transitions.","alternatives":["Interactive debugging","Human-led incident response"],"tradeoffs":"Structured workflows add process overhead but improve traceability.","constraints":["Not every incident permits complete reproduction"],"edge_cases":["Irreproducible production failures","Third-party failures"],"security":"Authorization boundaries must apply to every mutation stage.","performance":"Escalate diagnostic cost according to uncertainty and impact.","testing":"Validate each lifecycle transition with representative failure classes.","verification":"Require explicit evidence for diagnosis, repair, and closure.","version":"General","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Evidence model","Repair model","Governance model"],"relationships":["D25-B01","D25-B42","D25-B90"],"provenance":["General evidence-driven debugging methodology; Google SRE practices"],"evidence":"Reliable debugging combines observation, diagnosis, remediation, verification, and learning rather than treating them as isolated activities.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B100-R02","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging Synthesis","subtopic":"Evidence Model","concept":"Evidence lineage as a first-class debugging object","knowledge_type":"architecture","question":"Why should evidence lineage be preserved in an autonomous debugging system?","answer":"Evidence lineage allows every important diagnostic conclusion to be traced back to observations, experiments, artifacts, and transformations that produced it.","explanation":"Without lineage, a future debugger cannot distinguish verified facts from inferred conclusions or reproduce the reasoning behind a repair.","principle":"Diagnostic conclusions should be provenance-addressable.","rule":"Every high-impact diagnosis and repair decision must reference its supporting evidence.","examples":["A root-cause record links to the exact trace, reproducer, configuration snapshot, experiment result, and regression test that support it."],"invalid_examples":["Storing only the final root-cause label without supporting evidence references."],"problem":"AI-assisted debugging can otherwise become opaque.","failure":"Unverifiable diagnostic conclusions.","cause":"Evidence is discarded after reasoning.","solution":"Store immutable or versioned evidence references with conclusions.","alternatives":["Event sourcing","Evidence graph"],"tradeoffs":"Evidence retention consumes storage and requires privacy controls.","constraints":["Evidence may expire or become inaccessible"],"edge_cases":["Third-party evidence","Deleted artifacts"],"security":"Evidence access must follow least-privilege rules.","performance":"Use references and hashes instead of duplicating large artifacts.","testing":"Verify that conclusions can be traced to valid evidence references.","verification":"Reject high-impact conclusions with broken or missing provenance.","version":"General","dependencies":["Evidence store"],"prerequisites":["Provenance model"],"relationships":["D25-B49","D25-B98-R05"],"provenance":["General provenance-aware debugging architecture"],"evidence":"Traceability improves verification, reproducibility, and auditability of diagnostic reasoning.","confidence":0.98,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B100-R03","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging Synthesis","subtopic":"Autonomous Repair","concept":"Closed-loop repair verification","knowledge_type":"architecture","question":"What distinguishes closed-loop debugging from one-shot automated code repair?","answer":"Closed-loop debugging observes the post-repair system, verifies the targeted invariant, checks for regressions and residual risk, and updates the diagnostic knowledge base based on the outcome.","explanation":"A generated patch is only a proposed intervention until its effect on the actual system is verified.","principle":"Action completion is not equivalent to outcome success.","rule":"Do not mark an automated repair successful until post-change verification establishes the intended outcome.","examples":["Agent applies a patch, runs the original reproducer, executes regression tests, checks relevant telemetry, and records the verified result."],"invalid_examples":["Marking a patch successful because the source code compiles."],"problem":"Code-level correctness does not guarantee runtime correctness.","failure":"False repair success.","cause":"No outcome verification.","solution":"Close the loop with runtime and regression evidence.","alternatives":["Human code review","Staged deployment"],"tradeoffs":"Closed-loop validation costs more than generation alone.","constraints":["Some outcomes require long observation windows"],"edge_cases":["Delayed failures","External side effects"],"security":"Security fixes require post-change verification of the protected property.","performance":"Include performance regressions in the outcome criteria when relevant.","testing":"Run reproducer and regression suites after repair.","verification":"Require observed evidence that the intended failure mechanism is eliminated.","version":"General","dependencies":["Repair engine","Verification engine"],"prerequisites":["Confirmed diagnosis"],"relationships":["D25-B48","D25-B92","D25-B98-R04"],"provenance":["General autonomous repair and verification methodology"],"evidence":"A repair is stronger when its predicted state transition is compared with observed post-change behavior.","confidence":0.99,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B100-R04","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging Synthesis","subtopic":"Learning","concept":"Outcome-based debugging learning","knowledge_type":"architecture","question":"How should an autonomous debugging system learn from successful and failed investigations?","answer":"Store the initial hypothesis, evidence collected, actions taken, observed outcomes, verification results, false hypotheses, and residual risks so future investigations can retrieve validated patterns without treating every historical conclusion as truth.","explanation":"Learning from debugging requires preserving both successes and failures because discarded hypotheses reveal useful diagnostic boundaries.","principle":"Debugging knowledge should encode outcomes and uncertainty, not only final answers.","rule":"Record failed hypotheses and ineffective actions when they provide reusable diagnostic information.","examples":["A recurring timeout pattern retrieves a prior case showing that queue starvation was ruled out before database latency was confirmed."],"invalid_examples":["Training only on final root-cause labels while discarding rejected hypotheses and evidence."],"problem":"Historical debugging data can teach both useful and misleading patterns.","failure":"Future systems repeat previous diagnostic mistakes.","cause":"Outcome context is discarded.","solution":"Store structured investigation trajectories and verification results.","alternatives":["Case-based reasoning","Retrieval-augmented diagnosis"],"tradeoffs":"Trajectory storage is larger than final-answer storage.","constraints":["Sensitive incident information requires controlled retention"],"edge_cases":["Contradictory historical cases","Changing system architecture"],"security":"Learning datasets must remove or protect secrets and sensitive operational data.","performance":"Retrieve by validated causal signatures and environment compatibility.","testing":"Evaluate retrieval on known cases with changing evidence.","verification":"Separate observed facts from historical inferences before reuse.","version":"General","dependencies":["Debugging knowledge store"],"prerequisites":["Evidence lineage","Outcome model"],"relationships":["D25-B49","D25-B99-R03"],"provenance":["General case-based and evidence-driven debugging methodology"],"evidence":"Preserving diagnostic trajectories enables future systems to reuse both successful reasoning and known dead ends.","confidence":0.97,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; no execution performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}
{"record_id":"D25-B100-R05","dataset_id":"D25","topic":"Debugging Synthesis","subtopic":"Final Completeness","concept":"Debugging capability completeness criteria","knowledge_type":"verification","question":"What criteria indicate that a debugging knowledge system has broad practical coverage?","answer":"Coverage should include deterministic and nondeterministic faults, local and distributed systems, data and state failures, concurrency, resource behavior, build and dependency problems, security failures, observability limitations, repair validation, automation governance, and post-incident learning.","explanation":"A debugging system is incomplete if it handles only code-level exceptions while ignoring environmental, distributed, operational, security, or verification dimensions.","principle":"Debugging capability is multidimensional and must be evaluated by coverage rather than record count.","rule":"Assess dataset completeness by distinct fault classes, diagnostic methods, evidence types, failure modes, repair strategies, and verification requirements.","examples":["A complete knowledge fabric can reason about a parser exception, a race condition, a dependency regression, a distributed timeout, a security policy failure, and an incorrect automated repair."],"invalid_examples":["Declaring a debugging dataset complete because it contains many variants of stack-trace troubleshooting."],"problem":"Large datasets can still have major conceptual gaps.","failure":"High record count creates false completeness.","cause":"Quantity is used as the coverage metric.","solution":"Maintain a coverage matrix and gap analysis across debugging dimensions.","alternatives":["Capability taxonomy","Scenario-based coverage testing"],"tradeoffs":"Coverage analysis requires explicit taxonomy maintenance.","constraints":["New technologies create new failure modes"],"edge_cases":["Novel architectures","Emergent system behavior"],"security":"Coverage must include security-debugging and evidence-protection requirements.","performance":"Coverage should include both correctness and performance diagnosis.","testing":"Use scenario matrices to test retrieval and reasoning coverage.","verification":"Lock only after semantic deduplication, relationship validation, provenance review, and coverage-gap analysis.","version":"General","dependencies":["D25 dataset"],"prerequisites":["All D25 batch records","Coverage taxonomy"],"relationships":["D25-B01","D25-B40","D25-B49","D25-B90"],"provenance":["D25 integrated dataset methodology; Google SRE practices; GCC debugging documentation; OpenTelemetry specifications; OWASP Logging guidance; Kubernetes debugging documentation"],"evidence":"Broad debugging requires code-level, runtime, distributed, operational, security, observability, repair, and learning dimensions.","confidence":0.99,"validation":"Schema and semantic consistency reviewed; final-batch conceptual coverage reviewed; full-file execution not performed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","created_at":"2026-09-19"}

